/* In-course Quiz Centre bank: the same quizzes as the QTI packages (generated by tools/build_qti.js) */
window.COURSE_QUIZBANK = [{"pkg":"End-of-Module Tests","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_5l","title":"Module 1: Module 1 Test","desc":"<p>Module 1: Fundamentals of Information Technology. 40 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>At a careers evening at Coralstone College, a speaker says one word covers computer engineers, computer scientists, software engineers, IT specialists and IS analysts alike. Which term, the BROADEST of these, is she using?</p>","options":["Information Systems","Computing","Software Engineering","Computer Science"],"correct":1,"why":"Computing is the umbrella term covering every discipline the speaker listed. Computer Science is the most tempting distractor, but it is only one discipline within Computing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>At the firm Caribe Devices in Trinidad and Tobago, a specialist designs the circuit board and writes the firmware for a new card-payment terminal to be used in Broad Street shops. This work BEST represents</p>","options":["Computer Engineering","Software Engineering","Information Systems","Information Technology"],"correct":0,"why":"Designing the terminal's hardware and firmware is Computer Engineering. Software Engineering is tempting because firmware is software, but designing the circuit board places the work in CE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Mrs Arrindell of Montego Bay receives an electricity bill three times higher than usual. The clerk had keyed in her meter reading as 2,450 instead of 245. Which limitation of IT does this illustrate?</p>","options":["Garbage in, garbage out","Dependence on power","High cost of hardware","Rapid obsolescence"],"correct":0,"why":"The system processed the wrong input exactly as given and produced a wrong bill: GIGO. Dependence on power is tempting because the electricity company supplies power, but the problem here is bad data, not an outage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Nadia, an IT manager at a bank in Nassau, wants a professional title that shows employers she is a chartered IT practitioner. Which professional organisation awards the status she is looking for?</p>","options":["BCS","AIS","ACM","IEEE"],"correct":0,"why":"BCS, The Chartered Institute for IT, awards Chartered IT Professional (CITP) status, which is what Nadia wants. ACM is tempting as a large computing body, but it is known for the Digital Library and Turing Award, not chartership.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>At Frigate Bay Resort, Anya configures the servers, Wi-Fi and staff accounts, while Ravi studies how bookings are processed and designs reports that help managers decide on room prices. Which statement BEST distinguishes Ravi's discipline from Anya's?</p>","options":["Ravi's discipline is a branch of Computer Engineering; Anya's is a branch of Computer Science.","Ravi works in IS, which focuses on how information and technology serve the resort's processes and decisions; Anya works in IT, which focuses on the technology infrastructure and its users.","Ravi and Anya work in the same discipline under two different names.","Ravi designs computer hardware; Anya designs algorithms."],"correct":1,"why":"Ravi starts from the organisation (IS); Anya starts from the infrastructure (IT). Treating them as one discipline is the commonest misconception, but their focus and roles differ.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>At Frigate Bay Computer Museum, a display card reads: \"This 1965 machine was built with many transistors formed together on small silicon chips, but it had no CPU on a single chip.\" Which generation does the card describe?</p>","options":["Fourth generation: microprocessors","Second generation: discrete transistors","First generation: vacuum tubes","Third generation: integrated circuits"],"correct":3,"why":"Many transistors on one chip is an integrated circuit, which defines the third generation (1964 to 1971). The fourth generation is tempting, but the card says there is no CPU on one chip, and that is what a microprocessor is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>In a Seagrape Bay Secondary School lesson, the teacher shows a video of a Jacquard loom (1804) weaving a pattern controlled by a chain of punched cards, and asks why this loom matters in the history of computing. The BEST answer is that it</p>","options":["was programmed in machine language","used punched cards to store a changeable set of instructions","could add numbers using geared wheels","was the first machine to use binary electronics"],"correct":1,"why":"Swapping the cards in the video changes the pattern: an early stored, changeable set of instructions. Geared addition describes the Pascaline, not the loom.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A technician at the Leeward Motors garage in Gros Islet works with four computer systems in one day. Which of them is the BEST example of an embedded computer?</p>","options":["The controller in a customer's car anti-lock braking system","The airline mainframe that books her holiday flight","The laptop she uses to look up repair manuals","The desktop PC in the garage office"],"correct":0,"why":"The braking controller is built into the car to do one dedicated task: embedded. The laptop and office desktop are microcomputers, general-purpose machines for one user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>For a Flamboyant Heights College open-day poster, a student has four cards: I. TCP/IP adopted by ARPANET  II. World Wide Web created  III. ARPANET launched  IV. Network email sent. In which order should the cards go, earliest first?</p>","options":["III, IV, II, I","IV, III, I, II","III, IV, I, II","III, I, IV, II"],"correct":2,"why":"ARPANET (1969), email (1971), TCP/IP (1983), Web (1989 to 1991). Putting the Web before TCP/IP is the tempting error: the Web runs on an Internet that already existed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A grandmother in Kingstown, St. Vincent, remembers that her mobile phone in the late 1990s was the first one that gave her clear digital calls and let her send SMS text messages. Which mobile generation was that phone?</p>","options":["2G","3G","1G","4G"],"correct":0,"why":"2G (GSM, from 1991) was the first digital mobile standard and brought SMS. 1G was analogue voice only; 3G added mobile data, which her phone is not described as having.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>At the Seaside Pharmacy in Grand Anse, a new clerk finds a box of old till rolls showing amounts such as 12.50, 4.75 and 30.00, with no dates or product names. What the clerk has found is BEST described as</p>","options":["data, because they are raw, unorganised facts with little meaning on their own","information, because the amounts have been processed to give them meaning","knowledge, because the amounts can be used to make decisions","a report, because the amounts were printed by a computer system"],"correct":0,"why":"The amounts are raw, unprocessed and unorganised, with no context, so they are data. Option B is the most tempting distractor, but nothing has been totalled, sorted or put in context, so they are not yet information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>The Grand Anse Sands Hotel asks departing guests to rate their room from 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent) on a tablet in the lobby. The ratings collected are BEST described as</p>","options":["qualitative, because they record opinions","quantitative, because they are numbers","detailed, because every guest is asked","information, because they have been processed"],"correct":0,"why":"The numbers record subjective opinions, so the data is qualitative. Option B is tempting because the values are numbers, but they are not objective measurements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>At Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Barbados, the term attendance report reaches form teachers two weeks after term ends, too late to contact the parents of absent students. Which characteristic of information is MOST affected?</p>","options":["Format","Timeliness","Shareability","Confidentiality"],"correct":1,"why":"Information that arrives after it is needed fails on timeliness. Shareability is tempting because the report is distributed, but it was shared; it was simply late.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>The Roseau Transit Office reviews rainfall records each evening before planning the next day's bus routes. Which statement in its planning notes represents knowledge?</p>","options":["Rainfall last month was double the average.","Rainfall readings: 12, 0, 35, 4.","Rainfall last month was 210 mm.","Heavy rain usually floods the low road, so buses will use the hill route tomorrow."],"correct":3,"why":"Knowledge uses information and experience to make a decision: the office knows heavy rain floods the low road and therefore reroutes the buses. Option A is tempting because it is processed and meaningful, but it is information: no decision is made.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>The San Fernando Catering Company uses an accurate, carefully checked list of last year's cruise-ship arrivals at the Port of Spain cruise port to plan staffing for a new school canteen. The MAIN weakness of this data is that it is</p>","options":["confidential","qualitative","of poor quality","inappropriate for the purpose"],"correct":3,"why":"Accurate data can still be the wrong data for a decision: this is appropriateness. Option C is tempting, but the list is described as accurate, so quality is not the issue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Jaden, a student at Calabash Point Secondary in Sint Maarten, is gathering material for a project on Hurricane Irma in 2017. Which item in his folder is a PRIMARY source?</p>","options":["A textbook chapter on Caribbean history","A magazine review of a documentary about the hurricane","An encyclopaedia entry on Hurricane Irma","A video recorded by a Philipsburg resident during Hurricane Irma"],"correct":3,"why":"The resident's video is original, first-hand evidence made at the time of the storm. The encyclopaedia entry is tempting because it is about the same event, but it summarises other sources, so it is secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>The IT department at Sandbox Tree College in Barbados wants an online space where all its teachers can write and correct the same revision notes together. Which feature MOST clearly makes a wiki a better choice than a blog for this?</p>","options":["A wiki allows readers to post comments.","A wiki is published on the World Wide Web.","Many users can edit the same pages of a wiki.","A wiki contains images and hyperlinks."],"correct":2,"why":"Collaborative editing by many users is the defining feature of a wiki, which is exactly what the teachers need. Option A is tempting, but comments are typical of blogs; on a blog readers comment but cannot edit the post.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Marisol, an Upper Six student in Kingston, is writing a report on the smartphone features released this year. A disadvantage of using a printed library book rather than a manufacturer's web site is that the book</p>","options":["cannot contain diagrams","can be edited by anyone","is likely to be out of date","needs an Internet connection"],"correct":2,"why":"Books take months or years to produce, so for this year's smartphones Marisol's book will have dated. \"Can be edited by anyone\" is tempting because it is a known weakness of some sources, but it applies to wikis, not books.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College student researching coastal erosion in Anguilla can use her library's electronic database of journal articles or a general web search. Which is the MAIN advantage of the database for her?</p>","options":["It is always free to the public.","It contains only primary sources.","Its content is selected and can be searched precisely with filters.","It works without an Internet connection."],"correct":2,"why":"Selected, quality content and precise, filtered searching let her find published research on Anguilla quickly. \"Always free\" is tempting because she uses it through her library at no charge, but many databases require paid subscriptions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>During a tropical storm, residents of Montego Bay share many posts about flooded roads on social media, and a driver uses them to plan his route. Which is the MOST significant disadvantage of relying on these posts?</p>","options":["They can only be read on a CD-ROM.","They may contain rumours that have not been checked.","They cannot include photographs.","They are always out of date."],"correct":1,"why":"Social media lacks editorial checking, so the driver may act on rumours or old photos. \"Always out of date\" is tempting as a general weakness, but social media is actually one of the most current sources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>Nadia, a CAPE student in Gros Islet, is making notes on a general encyclopaedia entry about the Caribbean that covers every island in a few lines each. Which of her note headings names a CHARACTERISTIC of the source rather than a criterion for selecting it?</p>","options":["Completeness","Bias","Cultural context","Breadth of coverage"],"correct":3,"why":"Breadth of coverage describes the source (SO 1.5): the entry covers many islands. Bias, cultural context and completeness are tests for selection (SO 1.6). Completeness is the most tempting distractor because it sounds like depth or breadth, but it judges the source against the user's needs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A student in Roseau revises for a history test by listening to a podcast episode about Caribbean history, streamed through a mobile app on her phone. Which pair correctly identifies its format and medium?</p>","options":["Format: audio; medium: mobile app","Format: mobile app; medium: audio","Format: history; medium: podcast","Format: streaming; medium: Caribbean"],"correct":0,"why":"Audio is how the information is arranged (format); the app is the carrier (medium). Option B reverses them, the most common error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A student finds that a usually trustworthy newspaper gave the wrong date for a hurricane in one article. Which criterion has the article failed?</p>","options":["Breadth of coverage","Accuracy","Reliability","Currency"],"correct":1,"why":"A specific error fails the accuracy criterion. Reliability is tempting, but it describes the newspaper's general trustworthiness, which can remain high even when one article contains a mistake.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>A Coralstone College lecturer tells her class that an article on coral health at Buccoo Reef, Tobago, comes from a refereed journal. Which statement about this article is TRUE?</p>","options":["It was checked only by the publisher’s own editor.","It is certain to be more current than a blog on the lagoon.","It was reviewed by independent experts before publication.","It must be free and available online."],"correct":2,"why":"Refereeing means independent expert review before publication. Editorial checking (option A) is a different, weaker form of quality control; refereed sources are often slower, so less current than blogs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A Belizean business owner uses a US web site to learn about employee leave entitlements. Which criterion is MOST likely to make this source unsuitable?</p>","options":["Cost","Cultural context","Format","Depth"],"correct":1,"why":"Employment law differs between countries, so the source was produced for a different legal and cultural setting. Format, cost and depth are characteristics and do not address the mismatch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A Year 12 student at Mahoe Grove High School is drawing the information processing cycle on a revision poster, using the Seaside Pharmacy's weekly sales as the example. Which sequence should the poster show?</p>","options":["Input, storage, output, processing, with compulsory feedback","Output, processing, input, storage, with feedback to processing","Processing, input, storage, output, with feedback to output","Input, processing, output, storage, with optional feedback to input"],"correct":3,"why":"The pharmacy's sales are entered (input), totalled and sorted (processing), reported (output) and saved (storage), and feedback is optional and loops back to input. The most tempting distractor sends feedback to output, but feedback evaluates the output and returns changes to the input stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>At the Harbour View Supermarket in Willemstad, the cashier passes each item over a scanner that reads its barcode, and the product name and price appear on the screen at once. This is an example of</p>","options":["manual collating","manual data entry","automated data capture","automated dissemination"],"correct":2,"why":"The scanner reads the code directly from the product at the point of sale, which is automated data capture. \"Manual data entry\" is tempting because the cashier is involved, but she does not key or write the data; the device reads it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>The Year 13 form tutor at Sea-Almond Secondary School in Holetown already has each student's average mark in a spreadsheet and now arranges the class list from the highest average to the lowest. Which processing operation is this?</p>","options":["Sorting","Analysing","Collating","Calculating"],"correct":0,"why":"Arranging records in order of a key field (average mark, descending) is sorting. Calculating is tempting because averages are involved, but the tutor already has the averages; the operation described is putting them in order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>The Holetown Clinic keeps its patient folders in a manual filing system, filed alphabetically by surname. Which request would be MOST difficult for the clerk to satisfy?</p>","options":["Finding a folder for a given surname","Adding a new folder in the correct place","Finding all records for patients living in one district","Returning a folder after use"],"correct":2,"why":"District is not the clinic's filing key, so every folder must be checked. The other tasks all use the surname key. This is the main retrieval advantage of an automated system, which can search by any field.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>A marina in Providenciales has replaced its paper berth register with a computerised booking system. Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of the new automated system compared with the old manual one?</p>","options":["It produces more calculation errors","It is slower with large volumes of data","It depends on electricity and working equipment","It allows data to be sorted in any order"],"correct":2,"why":"The marina's new system needs power, equipment and often a network, so it can stop during outages such as after a hurricane, whereas the paper register kept working. \"More calculation errors\" is a misconception: computers calculate consistently if the input and program are correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>A school database stores a student ID such as 004512. Which data type is MOST appropriate, and why?</p>","options":["Character, because each digit is a separate symbol","Visual, because it is printed on an ID card","String, because it is not used in calculations and leading zeros must be kept","Numeric, because it is made up of digits"],"correct":2,"why":"IDs are never used in arithmetic, and storing them as numbers would drop the leading zeros, so a string is correct. Numeric is the tempting distractor because the ID contains only digits, but containing digits is not the test; being used in calculations is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>Calypso Corner music shop in Roseau sells old vinyl records and MP3 downloads, texts customers about new stock and runs its till over fibre broadband. Which of these is an example of an analogue representation?</p>","options":["An MP3 file stored on a customer's phone","The groove on a vinyl record","Light pulses in the shop's fibre-optic cable","A text message sent over a mobile network"],"correct":1,"why":"The vinyl groove is a continuous physical copy of the sound wave, so it is analogue. Fibre-optic pulses are tempting because light is involved, but the pulses are on/off states representing 1s and 0s, which is digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>The computer at the Holetown Marina stores the number of boats in the lagoon as the byte 10010110. How many boats are in the lagoon?</p>","options":["105","86","150","214"],"correct":2,"why":"128 + 16 + 4 + 2 = 150 boats. 105 comes from reading the bits in reverse (01101001), a common error when students write the place values from left to right in the wrong order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>A Flamboyant Heights College student opens a text file in a program that shows each character's ASCII code in denary. The upper-case letter H appears as 72. What code will she see for the upper-case letter L?</p>","options":["104","108","76","74"],"correct":2,"why":"Letters are consecutive and L is four letters after H, so 72 + 4 = 76. 108 is tempting, but it is the code for lower-case l (76 + 32); the question asks for upper case.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>During a steel pan rehearsal at Mahoe Grove High School, the music teacher writes a ♭ symbol before a note on the stave so the band plays it lower. This is an example of</p>","options":["musical notation","scientific notation","Morse Code","mathematical notation"],"correct":0,"why":"The flat sign is a musical symbol that lowers a note by a semitone. Mathematical notation is the tempting distractor because both use symbols, but the stave and the flat sign belong only to music.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>The Coral Reef Bakery in English Harbour has a box between its computer and the telephone line. When the owner emails an order to a supplier, the box changes the data before it travels down the line. Which function is this modem performing?</p>","options":["Storing web pages so that they load faster","Directing data packets between several different networks","Finding web pages that match a user’s keywords","Converting digital data into a signal for transmission and converting received signals back to digital data"],"correct":3,"why":"The bakery’s modem modulates outgoing data and demodulates incoming signals. The most tempting distractor is directing packets between networks, which is the job of a router.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>The Grenville Youth Netball League uses several tools to communicate with its players and coaches. Which pair of the league’s tools are BOTH asynchronous?</p>","options":["A telnet session on the league’s server and a VoIP call","A web-conference team meeting and a mailing list for fixtures","A mailing list for fixtures and a discussion forum for players’ questions","A VoIP call with the head coach and a web-conference team meeting"],"correct":2,"why":"The mailing list and the forum both store messages for players to read later. Option B is tempting because the mailing list is asynchronous, but the web-conference meeting is real-time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>A Form 6 student in Kingston uses an FTP client to copy a large folder of past-paper files from her school’s file server to her laptop so she can revise offline at home. This is called</p>","options":["downloading","indexing","uploading","streaming"],"correct":0,"why":"The files move from the server to her laptop, so she is downloading. Uploading, the tempting distractor, is the opposite direction: from her laptop to the server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>The IT technician at the Bayside Clinic in Montego Bay still uses telnet to log in to a server at the clinic’s branch in Ocho Rios. A consultant advises replacing it with SSH. What is the MAIN reason for this advice?</p>","options":["It needs a graphical interface.","It cannot work over the Internet.","It can only transfer files.","It sends usernames and passwords as plain text."],"correct":3,"why":"Telnet’s plain-text transmission would expose the technician’s login details to anyone intercepting the traffic. \"Can only transfer files\" is a misunderstanding: file transfer is FTP’s job; telnet provides remote command-line login.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>The principal of Hummingbird Ridge High School in Montego Bay must warn parents today about a sudden water shut-off. Many parents have only basic mobile phones without data plans. Which tool is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["A web-conferencing tool","A social media page","A cross-platform messaging tool","SMS"],"correct":3,"why":"SMS works over the cellular network on any phone, so every parent receives the warning. A cross-platform messaging tool is tempting because it is popular, but it needs a smartphone app and Internet access that these parents lack.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_bv","title":"Module 2: Module 2 Test","desc":"<p>Module 2: Information Systems. 45 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Every Monday, the store manager of the Bayview Supermarket in Holetown automatically receives a short list of ONLY the products that sold below their weekly target. Which type of information system is MOST likely producing this report?</p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Office Automation System","Management Information System","Executive Information System"],"correct":2,"why":"A scheduled exception report for a middle manager such as Bayview's store manager is MIS output. The TPS is tempting because it holds the underlying sales data, but it outputs processed transactions and detailed reports, not exception reports for managers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>At the Seaside Pharmacy in Montego Bay, a counter assistant rings up a bottle of sunscreen; the till prints a receipt and the stock file is reduced by one. Which of the following BEST describes the major OUTPUT of this Transaction Processing System?</p>","options":["Expert advice","Processed transactions","Strategic projections","What-if forecasts"],"correct":1,"why":"The receipt and the reduced stock record are processed transactions, the typical TPS output. What-if forecasts are tempting because they also draw on transaction data, but they are produced by a DSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>An architect in Portsmouth uses CAD software on a powerful workstation to design and simulate a hurricane-resistant school building. This is an example of a(n)</p>","options":["Decision Support System","Office Automation System","Knowledge Work System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":2,"why":"A KWS helps knowledge workers create designs and models. OAS is tempting because both support office-based professionals, but an OAS handles documents, email and scheduling, not design and simulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>At Island Breeze Hotel, a laminated card beside each front-desk computer lists the steps staff must follow to check in a guest and to back up the day's bookings. Which component of the information system does this card represent?</p>","options":["Data","Network","Procedures","Software"],"correct":2,"why":"The card gives instructions for people, so it represents procedures. Software is the tempting distractor because it is also a set of instructions, but software instructs the hardware, not the front-desk staff.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A manager at a Kingston bank suddenly asks for the number of loans issued to small businesses in Montego Bay during the last two hurricane seasons. The report produced is BEST described as</p>","options":["an exception report","a summarised report","a detailed report","an ad hoc report"],"correct":3,"why":"A one-off, unscheduled question answered on request produces an ad hoc report. A summarised report is tempting because the answer is a total, but summarised reports are routine and scheduled rather than produced for a sudden, specific question.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>A bank in Barbados processes thousands of cheques daily. Which input method reads the account numbers printed along the bottom of each cheque?</p>","options":["Barcode reading","OCR","OMR","MICR"],"correct":3,"why":"Cheque numbers are printed in magnetic ink and read by MICR. OCR is tempting because it also reads characters, but it reads ordinary print optically, not magnetic ink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>At the Sunset Supermarket in Oranjestad, the till program checks whether a customer’s loyalty points are greater than 500 before it gives a discount. Which part of the CPU carries out this comparison?</p>","options":["Arithmetic logic unit","Program counter","Cache","Control unit"],"correct":0,"why":"The ALU performs logical operations such as comparing the points total with 500. The control unit is tempting because it directs the process, but it fetches, decodes and coordinates rather than carrying out the comparison.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A Calabash Point Secondary student is typing her Communication Studies essay when a power cut switches off her desktop. When the power returns, her unsaved work has gone because it was held in</p>","options":["RAM, which is volatile","ROM, which is non-volatile","the hard disk, which is volatile","cache, which is non-volatile"],"correct":0,"why":"Her unsaved essay sat in RAM, which loses its contents without power. The hard disk option is tempting but wrong: hard disks are non-volatile, which is why saved work survives a power cut.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A developer at a Willemstad software house has finished a booking program for a dive shop in Bonaire and wants to hand over one fast-running executable file. Which translator converts the entire high-level program into that file before it is run?</p>","options":["Interpreter","Assembler","Compiler","Utility"],"correct":2,"why":"A compiler translates the whole program at once into an executable the dive shop can run. An interpreter is the tempting distractor, but it translates and runs one statement at a time and produces no executable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Sensors on a river in Guyana record water levels and display them on a screen at the regional office, where staff decide whether to issue flood warnings. This is BEST described as</p>","options":["a utility program","a control system","bespoke application software","a monitoring system"],"correct":3,"why":"The system records and displays readings while staff decide what to do: monitoring. It is not control because no actuator changes the river or the process automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>The managers of the Harbourside Trading company in St. George’s face several decisions in the weeks after a major hurricane damages its head office. Which of these decisions is MOST likely to be unstructured?</p>","options":["Calculating the turnover tax on a customer's bill","Reordering printer paper when fewer than ten boxes remain","Deciding whether the company should move its head office to another island","Choosing which of two approved couriers to use for a routine delivery"],"correct":2,"why":"Moving Harbourside's head office to another island is novel, uncertain and has no agreed procedure, so it is unstructured. Option D is tempting because it involves a choice, but both couriers are pre-approved and the decision is routine, so it is at most semi-structured.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>Each morning, the shift supervisor at Island Fresh supermarket in San Fernando checks a list of every item sold yesterday and today's stock count for each shelf. This operational information is BEST described as</p>","options":["detailed, internal and current","approximate and gathered only when needed","summarised, external and future-oriented","periodic and compared with the budget"],"correct":0,"why":"The supervisor's item-by-item sales list and shelf counts are detailed, internal and current, which is what operational staff need for daily tasks. Option D is tempting but describes tactical information, such as the store manager's monthly budget report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>A secretary at the Broad Street Law Office in Bridgetown deletes an old letter by dragging its icon onto the recycle bin and watching it disappear at once. This is an example of</p>","options":["a menu-driven interface","a natural language interface","direct manipulation","a command line interface"],"correct":2,"why":"The secretary acts physically on a visible object (the letter's icon) and gets immediate feedback, which is direct manipulation. A menu is tempting because the action happens inside a GUI, but no list of options was selected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>At the Sunset Car Rental in Holetown, the cars respond only to the spoken phrases \"Radio on\" and \"Call office\", while the company website's chatbot answers typed questions such as \"can I drop the car off at the airport?\". Which statement BEST distinguishes the two interfaces?</p>","options":["Speech interfaces need a screen, whereas natural language interfaces do not.","A speech interface understands any sentence, whereas a natural language interface accepts only fixed commands.","Speech interfaces are used only in cars, whereas natural language interfaces are used only on websites.","A speech interface accepts spoken input, whereas a natural language interface accepts everyday sentences that may be typed or spoken."],"correct":3,"why":"The cars use speech (the voice channel); the chatbot uses natural language (everyday sentences, here typed). Option C is tempting because of where Sunset uses each one, but location does not define an interface. Option B reverses the truth: the cars' speech system accepts only fixed phrases.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>An experienced network technician at Sandbox Tree College must apply the same settings to all 60 classroom computers before lessons start tomorrow. Which type of HCI is MOST suitable for this task?</p>","options":["Form-based interface","Menu-driven interface","Command line interface","Direct manipulation interface"],"correct":2,"why":"A command line lets an expert type or script commands to repeat a task quickly with precise control. A menu is tempting because it is easy to use, but stepping through menus 60 times is slow for an expert.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>The Caribbean Credit Union's new teller system must suit staff with 15 years' experience and new hires starting next week. Which pair of HCI evaluation principles is MOST likely to conflict in this design?</p>","options":["Ease of navigation and alignment","Ease of use and ease of learning","Use of colour and ease of navigation","Alignment and use of colour"],"correct":1,"why":"The experienced tellers want fast, efficient interaction (ease of use) such as shortcuts, while the new hires need guided, learnable steps (ease of learning), which can slow the experts down. Alignment and colour are tempting because both are visual, but they usually support each other rather than conflict.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>On the Basseterre Ferry Co. website, the menu appears in a different position on every page and there is no Home link, so tourists booking trips to Nevis keep getting lost. Which principle is MOST affected?</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Ease of navigation","Alignment","Use of colour"],"correct":1,"why":"Tourists cannot reliably find their way or return home, which is navigation. Alignment is tempting because the menu \"moves\", but the main effect on the tourists is getting lost, not an untidy layout.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>At the Island Health Clinic in Gros Islet, four people work with the patient records system every day. Which of them is an end user rather than an IT professional?</p>","options":["The nurse who enters patient notes during appointments","The programmer who writes updates to the system","The help-desk technician who fixes staff log-in problems","The database administrator who backs up the records"],"correct":0,"why":"The nurse uses the system to do her own job. The help-desk technician is tempting because they also \"help users\", but supporting the system is an IT professional role.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>A clerk at a George Town law firm in the Cayman Islands has severe arthritis in both hands and cannot use a mouse or keyboard for long. Which adaptation BEST supports her?</p>","options":["Voice control","A high-contrast colour scheme","Captions on training videos","A language selector"],"correct":0,"why":"Voice control removes the need to use a mouse or keyboard. High contrast is tempting as a common accessibility feature, but it supports vision, not mobility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>An ATM on King Street, Kingston has a raised dot on the number 5 key and offers audio guidance through a headphone jack so that blind customers can withdraw cash. These are examples of</p>","options":["non-visual interfaces","direct manipulation","sensors","cultural adaptation"],"correct":0,"why":"Touch markers and audio let blind customers interact without seeing the screen. Sensors is tempting, but a sensor detects physical changes as input; the dot and audio are tactile and audio interfaces.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>The IT technician at Breadfruit Valley Secondary needs a device that links the school LAN to the Internet service and forwards packets between the two networks using IP addresses. Which device should she install?</p>","options":["Network interface card","Router","Switch","Hub"],"correct":1,"why":"A router connects different networks, here the school LAN and the Internet, and forwards packets by IP address. A switch is tempting but it works within one LAN using MAC addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>Staff at the Island Hotel Group log in to a private web portal to read rosters and policies. Guests and suppliers cannot reach it at all. This portal is</p>","options":["a MAN","an extranet","the Internet","an intranet"],"correct":3,"why":"The portal uses web technologies but is private and staff-only, so it is an intranet. An extranet is tempting, but it would open access to authorised outsiders such as the suppliers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>At Fort Street Jewellers, one PC's cable is cut and only that PC goes offline. A week later the central device fails and every PC loses its connection. Which topology does the shop use?</p>","options":["Star","Ring","Bus","Mesh"],"correct":0,"why":"In a star each PC has its own cable, so one cut affects one PC, but the central switch is a single point of failure. Bus is tempting, but a cut in a bus backbone would stop the whole shop network at once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Two security guards at the Port of Spain cruise port use handheld radios: one presses a button to talk while the other must wait to listen, then they swap. This is an example of</p>","options":["half duplex","modulation","simplex","full duplex"],"correct":0,"why":"Data flows in both directions but only one at a time, so the guards' radios are half duplex. Simplex is tempting, but in simplex the receiver can never reply.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Grand Anse Boatyard must link its office to a workshop that sits beside heavy welding machinery producing strong electrical interference. Which transmission medium is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Unshielded twisted pair","Coaxial cable","Infrared","Fibre-optic cable"],"correct":3,"why":"Fibre carries light, so the welding machinery's electrical interference cannot affect it. Coaxial is tempting because it is shielded, but it still carries electrical signals and is not immune.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>Before a photo leaves a student's phone at Hummingbird Ridge High School, it is compressed to JPEG and encrypted so that only the receiver can read it. At which layer of the OSI model are this encryption and compression performed?</p>","options":["Session","Transport","Application","Presentation"],"correct":3,"why":"Compressing and encrypting the photo changes the format of the data, which is the Presentation layer’s job (translation, encryption and compression). Session is the tempting distractor, but it manages dialogue (opening, managing and closing conversations), not format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>A tourist at the St. George’s cruise pier opens a website hosted in Miami. Routers along the way read each packet’s IP address and choose a path between networks. Which TCP/IP layer is responsible for this logical addressing and routing?</p>","options":["Transport","Internet","Application","Network Access"],"correct":1,"why":"The Internet layer uses IP addresses to route the tourist’s packets between networks. Transport is tempting because it also deals with delivery, but it handles end-to-end delivery between programs using ports, not routing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>In the computer lab at Palmetto Point Secondary, a network device learns the MAC addresses of the 20 connected PCs and forwards each frame only to the intended PC. This device is a</p>","options":["modem","router","hub","switch"],"correct":3,"why":"A switch works at Layer 2 with MAC addresses and forwards each frame to one port, as in the lab. A router is tempting, but it forwards packets between networks using IP addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>Pelican Key Villas are comparing four ways to protect the guest Wi-Fi at their rental units. Which of the following provides the STRONGEST protection for the wireless network?</p>","options":["WPA2 with TKIP","MAC filtering only","Hiding the SSID","WPA3 with a long network key"],"correct":3,"why":"WPA3 uses AES encryption with the SAE handshake, and a long key resists guessing, so the villas’ traffic is protected. MAC filtering is tempting, but MAC addresses can be spoofed and it does not encrypt traffic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>A student is designing a network for Gros Islet Dental Clinic and must protect the reception PC, the dentist's PC and the patient-records server from Internet attacks. Where should the firewall be placed on the diagram?</p>","options":["Between the router (WAN side) and the switch (LAN side)","Between one PC and the switch","Between two PCs","After the wireless access point only"],"correct":0,"why":"Placing the firewall at the WAN–LAN boundary means all traffic entering or leaving the clinic LAN is filtered, protecting every device. Placing it before one PC is tempting but protects only that PC.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>At the Harbour View Insurance office in Roseau, a newly installed PC cannot open any website. Its ipconfig output shows IPv4 Address 169.254.40.12 and no default gateway. What does this MOST likely indicate?</p>","options":["The firewall is blocking web traffic","The DNS server is not responding","The PC has a static IP address set correctly","The PC did not receive an address from a DHCP server"],"correct":3,"why":"A 169.254 address is self-assigned when no DHCP server replies, and the new PC has no gateway. DNS failure is tempting, but a DNS fault would still show a normal address and gateway; the problem here is at the addressing stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>The Sunset Café near the Castries cruise pier gives its Wi-Fi password to every customer. Which setting stops a customer who knows that password from changing the router's configuration?</p>","options":["Router password","Network key","SSID broadcast","AES encryption"],"correct":0,"why":"The router password protects the management settings. The network key is tempting, but it only lets devices join the Wi-Fi, and the café's customers already know it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>A clerk at the Island Hop Travel agency in Paramaribo can ping 8.8.8.8 successfully but cannot open www.example.com by name. The fault is MOST likely with</p>","options":["the Ethernet cable","the router's power supply","the default gateway","DNS name resolution"],"correct":3,"why":"Replies from a public IP prove the clerk's cable, gateway and Internet path work, so only name resolution is failing. The gateway is tempting, but traffic to 8.8.8.8 passes through it successfully.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>In an old lab at Grand Anse Learning Centre, every PC taps into one main cable with a terminator at each end. A chair crushes that cable and every PC loses its connection. Which topology does the lab use?</p>","options":["Star with a switch","Star","Star with a router","Bus"],"correct":3,"why":"All bus devices share one backbone, so the crushed cable stops the whole lab network. Star is tempting because the central device is a single point of failure, but a cable break in a star affects only one device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>The St. Kitts Seaview Hotel must link its reception block to a villa 600 m away, along a route past the laundry's heavy machinery. Which statement BEST compares fibre-optic cable with twisted-pair Ethernet for this link?</p>","options":["Fibre is cheaper and easier to install than twisted pair.","Both media are affected equally by electrical interference.","Fibre carries data further and is immune to electrical interference, whereas twisted pair is limited to about 100 m per run.","Twisted pair is more secure than fibre because it uses electricity."],"correct":2,"why":"Fibre uses light, so it covers the 600 m and ignores the laundry's electrical noise; twisted-pair runs are limited to about 100 m. Option A is tempting because fibre is widely installed, but fibre is generally more expensive and harder to terminate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>The Portsmouth Marina Hotel is listing its security measures for an insurance inspector. Which of the following measures on the list is a LOGICAL access control measure?</p>","options":["A cable lock on a laptop","A locked server cabinet","Read-only access rights for junior clerks","A security guard at the main entrance"],"correct":2,"why":"Access rights are set in the hotel's software and control what clerks can do with data, so they are logical. A security guard is tempting because guards control access, but they control entry to a building, which is physical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>The IT officer at the San Pedro Shipping Agency asks four new clerks to suggest passwords for the cargo database. Which suggestion BEST meets all the characteristics of an effective password?</p>","options":["Belize City","R3d!KiteOver7Bays","84726193","mypassword2024"],"correct":1,"why":"R3d!KiteOver7Bays has well over eight characters, upper and lower case, numbers, a special character in fourth place and nothing personal. \"mypassword2024\" is tempting because it is long and alphanumeric, but it is obvious and has no upper-case letters and no special character.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>To enter the baggage-handling control room at the Hewanorra Airport Ground Services office, a supervisor presents her staff card and then places her finger on a scanner. This is an example of</p>","options":["authorisation without authentication","single-factor authentication using two devices","encryption of the door code","two-factor authentication using something you have and something you are"],"correct":3,"why":"The supervisor's card is something she has and her fingerprint is something she is: two different factors. \"Single-factor using two devices\" is tempting, but what counts is the number of different factor types, not the number of devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>On Monday morning the Montego Bay Car Rentals finds that its bookings file will not open. Which of the following events from the weekend is the MOST likely cause of this data corruption?</p>","options":["A user forgetting a password","A clerk being given read-only access","A sudden power cut while a file is being saved","A file being encrypted with a strong key"],"correct":2,"why":"A save interrupted by a power cut can leave the bookings file partly written and unreadable. Encryption is tempting because ciphertext looks unreadable, but it is deliberate and reversible with the key, so it is not corruption.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>A customer pays the Frederick Street Electronics online store by card. Anyone intercepting the payment on the café Wi-Fi sees only a jumble of characters. This scrambled, unreadable form of the data is called</p>","options":["plaintext","the algorithm","ciphertext","the key"],"correct":2,"why":"Ciphertext is the output of encryption. Plaintext is tempting because it is the other half of the pair, but plaintext is the readable data before encryption.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41","q":"<p>A Lower Sixth student at Bayleaf Hill Secondary says: \"When the island's undersea cable was damaged, the World Wide Web went down, so the Internet and the web must be the same thing.\" Which statement BEST describes the relationship between the Internet and the World Wide Web?</p>","options":["The Internet is a collection of web pages stored on the World Wide Web.","The World Wide Web is a service of linked hypertext documents that runs on the Internet.","The World Wide Web is the hardware that connects networks together.","The Internet and the World Wide Web are two names for the same network."],"correct":1,"why":"The cable is part of the Internet (the network); the web is one service that depends on it, so it fails too, but they are not the same thing. Option A, the most tempting distractor, reverses the relationship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42","q":"<p>Sasha, a student at Coralstone College, is sent the link https://www.example.ag/news/today.html for a class article about the St. John’s cruise season. In this URL, which part is the path?</p>","options":["today.html","/news/","www.example.ag","https"],"correct":1,"why":"/news/ is the folder (path) on the server. today.html is the file name, which candidates often confuse with the path.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43","q":"<p>The English Harbour Snorkel Tours site shows a beach photo, beach.jpg. The owner wants the words \"English Harbour beach\" to appear if the photo cannot load and to be read aloud by screen readers. Which HTML is correct?</p>","options":["img src=\"English Harbour beach\" alt=\"beach.jpg\" (in angle brackets)","img href=\"beach.jpg\" alt=\"English Harbour beach\" (in angle brackets)","img src=\"beach.jpg\" alt=\"English Harbour beach\" (in angle brackets)","img src=\"beach.jpg\" title=\"English Harbour beach\" (in angle brackets)"],"correct":2,"why":"src gives the file and alt gives the text shown if it fails and read by screen readers. Option D uses title, which is only a hover tooltip, not replacement text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44","q":"<p>The Port of Spain Tutoring Centre is building an online registration form. Each student must choose exactly ONE of three subjects: Mathematics, Physics or Information Technology. Which form control is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["A textarea","A password input","Radio buttons with the same name","Checkboxes"],"correct":2,"why":"Radio buttons sharing a name allow only one selection, so each student gets exactly one subject. Checkboxes are tempting but would let a student tick all three.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45","q":"<p>A guest at a San Pedro hotel types www.example.bz into her laptop. Before her browser can request the page, the Domain Name System carries out domain name resolution. This process</p>","options":["converts HTML into XML for transport","registers a new domain name with a registrar","converts the domain name into an IP address","loads the home page of the web site"],"correct":2,"why":"DNS resolution maps the name she typed to the server's IP address so the browser knows where to send its HTTP request. Registering a domain (C) is a different process that the site owner completes long before any guest visits.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_hg","title":"Module 3: Module 3 Test","desc":"<p>Module 3: Information and Problem-Solving. 40 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>At a staff meeting, the manager of the Gros Islet Bakery says: \"We will fix our lost-orders problem by defining it, analysing it, comparing options, choosing one, putting it in place and checking the results.\" Which of the following BEST describes what the manager is planning?</p>","options":["Removing the symptoms of the problem as quickly as possible","Writing a computer program to automate the order book","A process of finding solutions to a problem by following ordered stages","Choosing the cheapest available option"],"correct":2,"why":"The manager describes ordered stages from defining the problem to checking the results, which matches the syllabus description of problem-solving as a process of finding solutions. Writing a program is tempting because this is an IT course, but a program is only one possible solution, and the manager has not chosen one yet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>The committee of the Harbourside Craft Market has agreed a problem statement, found that stock-outs happen because sales are never totalled, and listed its budget limit. Which stage should the committee carry out NEXT?</p>","options":["Define the problem","Identify and evaluate possible solutions","Implement","Select and justify the optimal solution"],"correct":1,"why":"The committee has finished defining and analysing, so it must now identify and evaluate possible solutions. \"Select and justify\" is tempting, but it cannot select the optimal solution until alternatives have been identified and evaluated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>A student at Bayleaf Hill Secondary is writing a problem statement for a Mesopotamia farmer whose sweet peppers are being attacked by whitefly. Which should the statement include? I. Who or what is affected II. How they are affected III. The consequence if the problem is not fixed IV. The pesticide to be used</p>","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","I, II and IV only","I, II, III and IV"],"correct":1,"why":"The farmer's problem statement should say who/what is affected (her sweet peppers), how (whitefly damage) and the consequence. Including IV is tempting, but naming the pesticide puts a solution into the statement and closes off other options such as traps or netting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>The owner of the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet notes that she can spend no more than EC$135 a month and that her two pharmacists have only basic computer skills. During which stage is she identifying these limits?</p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Define the problem","Analyse the problem","Implement"],"correct":2,"why":"The US$50 budget and the pharmacists' skills are constraints, and constraints are identified during analysis. \"Define the problem\" is tempting, but definition states what the problem is and who it affects, not the limits on solutions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Palmetto Point Secondary installs a new library booking system and, three months later, compares the number of lost books with the figure recorded before the change. This activity is part of which stage?</p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Select and justify the optimal solution","Implement","Analyse the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"Comparing results with the original problem after the solution is in use is evaluation and review. \"Implement\" is tempting because the system was installed, but measuring its effect is a separate, later stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>The office at Seagrape Bay Secondary School is creating a database to track daily attendance for every form class. Which item of information is ESSENTIAL?</p>","options":["A teacher's remark that attendance is \"fine this term\", with no daily records","The history of the school's founding","Each student's ID number and the dates they were present or absent","Each student's favourite subject"],"correct":2,"why":"Attendance cannot be recorded without student IDs and dates, so they are essential. The teacher's remark is cosmetic: tempting because it sounds like attendance information, but it masks the missing daily records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A working group planning a new bus route from English Harbour to St. John’s is handed a note on the colours the island's buses were painted in 1985. For this problem, the note is BEST described as information that</p>","options":["must be obtained before the problem can be solved","has no bearing on the problem being solved","looks useful but masks missing information","improves the solution but is not required"],"correct":1,"why":"The 1985 bus colours have no bearing on where the new route should run, so the note is extraneous. Option C describes cosmetic information, the most common confusion: cosmetic information looks necessary or helpful while masking missing facts, but this note does not even look relevant to the route.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A student in St. John’s planning a trip to Jamaica uses a travel website that earns commission from one airline ranks that airline's flights first, describing them as \"the only sensible choice\". This information should be treated with caution mainly because of</p>","options":["bias","currency","completeness","cultural context"],"correct":0,"why":"The site gains from the reader choosing that airline and uses loaded language: bias. Completeness is tempting because other airlines may be downplayed, but the root cause is the commercial motive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>A Coralstone College lecturer tells her class that the marine science article they are using on coral bleaching near Tobago is refereed. Which statement about this article is CORRECT?</p>","options":["It must have been printed on paper.","It must be more current than online news about the coral.","It was reviewed by independent experts before publication.","It contains no errors."],"correct":2,"why":"Refereed means independent expert review before publication. Option D is tempting, but review reduces errors rather than guaranteeing none; the article could also be online, and peer review is slow, so news may be more current.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A family in Barbados uses a 2011 price list to budget for a new refrigerator. The main weakness of this information is its</p>","options":["refereed status","currency","bias","cultural context"],"correct":1,"why":"Prices from 2011 are out of date: a currency problem. Cultural context is tempting, but nothing suggests the list came from another country.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>The Montego Bay Logistics in Kingston has two proposals for its parcel-tracking system: one team will follow Waterfall, the other Agile software development. Which characteristic BEST distinguishes the two approaches?</p>","options":["Waterfall involves the customer continuously, whereas Agile involves the customer only at the end","Waterfall welcomes changing requirements, whereas Agile freezes requirements early","Waterfall completes each stage before the next begins and delivers the system once, whereas Agile delivers working software in short iterations","Waterfall produces no documentation, whereas Agile produces detailed documentation"],"correct":2,"why":"Waterfall is sequential with one final delivery; Agile delivers working software every short iteration. Option A reverses the two models’ customer involvement, a common mix-up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A Tortola bakery owner wants a website but says, \"I can’t describe it, but I’ll know it when I see it.\" Which model is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Prototyping","Iterative/incremental","None, as the requirements are too unclear to begin","Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"A prototype gives the owner something to see and react to, clarifying vague requirements. Incremental (B) is tempting but assumes the core requirements are already known.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>The Caribbean Islands Credit Union is having its member app built in increments: balances first, then transfers, then loans. Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE the credit union may face with this approach?</p>","options":["Members must wait until the end to use any part of the app","The loans increment may not integrate easily with the earlier balances and transfers increments","Members cannot give feedback until the final delivery","Requirements must be completely frozen before any work starts"],"correct":1,"why":"Integration difficulties between increments are a recognised drawback. Options A, D and C describe Waterfall; A is tempting because it sounds like a general weakness, but members can use the balances increment early.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>The owner of the Island Hop Tours in Montego Bay reads that Agile values \"responding to change over following a plan\". For her excursion-app project, this means that</p>","options":["plans are made but adapted as her requirements change","her developers will never make plans","the plan is fixed at the start and followed exactly","she is not allowed to change requirements"],"correct":0,"why":"The Manifesto values responding to change more highly, but plans still exist and are revised each iteration. Option B is the common misconception that Agile has no planning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Developer A builds quick kiosk screens for St. Kitts tourism board and discards them once the board agrees what it wants. Developer B keeps improving a catalogue model for the King George V Street Jewellers until it goes live. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["A’s prototype can be used only in Waterfall projects and B’s only in Agile projects","A built an evolutionary prototype and B built a throwaway prototype","Both are throwaway prototypes, because every prototype is discarded in the end","A built a throwaway prototype, discarded once requirements were clear; B built an evolutionary prototype refined into the final system"],"correct":3,"why":"The difference is what happens to the prototype: discarded (A, throwaway) versus refined into the final system (B, evolutionary). Option B is tempting because it uses the right terms, but reverses them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>The project manager at the Harbourview Clinic in Bridgetown pins the SDLC stages on the office wall, in the order given in the CAPE IT syllabus, so that staff can follow the project. Which list should she use?</p>","options":["Feasibility study, design, analysis, development, review, implementation","Feasibility study, analysis, design, development, implementation, review","Analysis, design, feasibility study, development, implementation, review","Analysis, feasibility study, design, implementation, development, review"],"correct":1,"why":"The syllabus order is feasibility study, analysis, design, development, implementation, review. Option D is tempting because studying the clinic's current system seems like the first step, but the feasibility study must first decide whether the project is worth doing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>A feasibility study finds that the staff of a Curaçao insurance office are strongly opposed to a new claims system, fear for their jobs and may not adapt to it. Using the five feasibility types from the class slides, this finding relates to</p>","options":["economic feasibility","social/behavioural feasibility","technical feasibility","time feasibility"],"correct":1,"why":"Whether users can adapt, including their attitudes and resistance, is social/behavioural feasibility (some textbooks fold it into operational). Economic (A) is tempting because resistance may cost money, but economic feasibility asks whether benefits justify cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>Bridgetown Ferry Line wants its booking system checked against what was required. Its test plans are drawn up from the design specification and handed over with the coded system and the manuals. In which syllabus SDLC stage are the test plans delivered?</p>","options":["Development","Implementation","Review","Feasibility study"],"correct":0,"why":"Test plans are a deliverable of development, alongside the coded system and the user and technical manuals, and they are run in testing, which the syllabus places inside development. Implementation (B) is tempting because acceptance may finish near go-live, but implementation installs and changes over to a system that has already been tested.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>The Island Pharmacy chain keeps its old stock system running in all its branches while only the key parts of the new system, receiving deliveries and reordering, run alongside it until fully tested. Then it switches over completely. Which changeover method is this?</p>","options":["Direct","Parallel","Pilot","Phased"],"correct":2,"why":"Pilot running runs key parts of the new system alongside the old until fully tested, a compromise between direct and parallel. Parallel (B) is the tempting distractor, but parallel running keeps the whole of both systems side by side until the new one is fault free.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>A new IT contractor at the Montego Bay Water Sports shop must add a field for life-jacket size to the booking database without breaking existing reports. Which item would she MOST likely find in the technical manual rather than the user manual?</p>","options":["A list of frequently asked questions from cashiers","A data dictionary describing every field in the database","Instructions for printing a daily sales report","An explanation of the error message \"Invalid PIN\""],"correct":1,"why":"A data dictionary is for developers and IT staff, such as the contractor, who maintain the system. Option D is tempting because error messages sound technical, but explaining them to shop staff is a user manual task.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>Coral Reef Resorts group is replacing its booking system. Its analyst needs answers to the same 12 rating-scale questions from 250 staff working different shifts at its hotels across Jamaica. Which fact-finding tool is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Review of printed material","Structured interview","Observation","Questionnaire"],"correct":3,"why":"A questionnaire reaches many people on different shifts cheaply and gives comparable quantitative answers. A structured interview (B) also asks the same questions but would take far too long for 250 people.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>An analyst spends a morning watching data-entry clerks at the Bridge Street Insurance office in Castries. The clerks work faster and make fewer errors than the office's records show for a normal day. This problem is known as</p>","options":["the Hawthorne effect","scope creep","a low response rate","interviewer bias"],"correct":0,"why":"The Bridge Street Insurance clerks changed their behaviour because they knew they were being watched: the Hawthorne effect. Interviewer bias (D) is tempting but concerns how an interviewer influences answers, not observed behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>At Island Code Studio, one analyst draws a Booking class listing bookingID, pickupDate and calculateCost(). Another draws BOOKING and VEHICLE entities with their attributes and a 1:M relationship. Which statement BEST distinguishes the first model (an object model) from the second (an ER diagram)?</p>","options":["The object model shows tasks against time.","The object model shows only the relationships between entities.","The object model shows data stores and data flows.","The object model shows operations (methods) as well as data."],"correct":3,"why":"The Booking class includes the method calculateCost() alongside its attributes; the ER diagram shows data and relationships only. A describes a DFD, the most tempting distractor because both are modelling tools.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>The project manager for a new stock system at the Barbadian Medical Supplies draws a Gantt chart. Task P (system design) runs from week 4 to week 7 and Task Q (program coding) runs from week 6 to week 9. Which statement is TRUE?</p>","options":["P lasts 4 weeks and the tasks overlap in weeks 6 and 7.","P lasts 4 weeks and the tasks overlap in week 7 only.","P lasts 3 weeks and the tasks overlap in weeks 6 and 7.","P lasts 3 weeks and the tasks do not overlap."],"correct":0,"why":"P: 7 − 4 + 1 = 4 weeks; both bars appear in weeks 6 and 7. C is tempting for candidates who forget to add 1 when calculating duration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Analysts at Caribbean Code Works in Bridgetown, Barbados, need software to draw the DFDs for a new hospital records system and to keep a central data dictionary of every field. Which type of CASE tool would MOST likely provide these facilities?</p>","options":["Upper CASE","A Gantt chart package","A compiler","Lower CASE"],"correct":0,"why":"Upper CASE supports analysis and design, which is where DFDs and the data dictionary are produced. Lower CASE (A) supports coding and testing, so it is the tempting but wrong choice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A systems analyst studying the Seaside Pharmacy in Frigate Bay draws a diagram showing where prescription data comes from, which processes change it, where it is stored and who receives the results. This is a data flow diagram, which is BEST defined as</p>","options":["a chart showing the schedule of tasks in a systems project","a graphical representation of how data moves into, through and out of a system, showing entities, processes, stores and flows","a diagram showing the sequence of steps and decisions in a program","a diagram of the hardware and network links in a system"],"correct":1,"why":"The analyst's diagram models the flow of prescription data, so it is a DFD. Option C is tempting but describes a flowchart, which shows sequence and decisions; a DFD shows neither.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>In a Gane and Sarson DFD for Blue Channel Ferries in San Pedro, a rectangle with rounded corners is divided by a line and contains \"2.0\" and \"Validate booking\". This symbol represents</p>","options":["a data flow","a data store","an external entity","a process"],"correct":3,"why":"Rounded rectangle + number + verb phrase = process: validating the passenger's booking transforms data. A data store (B) is tempting because it also has an identifier, but stores are open-ended rectangles numbered D1, D2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A CAPE student at Bayleaf Hill Secondary has drawn the context level DFD for King George V Street Pharmacy and now draws its level 1 DFD. Which feature will appear on her level 1 DFD that was NOT on the context level DFD?</p>","options":["A process","External entities","Labelled data flows","Data stores"],"correct":3,"why":"Data stores such as the pharmacy's Prescriptions file first appear at level 1. Option A is tempting, but the context level does have a process: exactly one, numbered 0.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29","q":"<p>In a draft payroll DFD for the Blue Water Marine Services in Kralendijk, process 3.0 \"Print payslip\" sends a payslip to Employee, but no data flows into it. This error is called</p>","options":["an illegal connection","a black hole","an unbalanced diagram","a miracle"],"correct":3,"why":"The payslip appears from nothing: outputs with no inputs is a miracle. Option B is the most tempting distractor, but a black hole is the reverse: inputs with no outputs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30","q":"<p>At the Palmetto Point Secondary canteen, a student sends her lunch choice from her phone to the pre-order system. Which label is CORRECT for this data flow on the DFD?</p>","options":["order details","place order","sends order","Record order"],"correct":0,"why":"The student's lunch choice is data, so the flow takes a noun phrase: \"order details\". \"Record order\" (D) is tempting because it looks correct, but it is a verb phrase suitable for a process, not a flow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31","q":"<p>A trainee at the Georgetown Insurance Brokers lists what makes his algorithm for calculating premiums \"well designed\". Which item on his list is NOT a characteristic of a well-designed algorithm in the CAPE syllabus?</p>","options":["It is a general solution","It is written in a high-level programming language","It has a finite number of steps","Its steps are clearly defined and unambiguous"],"correct":1,"why":"The trainee is wrong about C: algorithms are language-independent plans; coding his premium algorithm in a language would produce a program. Option C is tempting if students think long processes are fine, but finiteness is a required characteristic: the algorithm must end.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32","q":"<p>An algorithm for the Bonaire Boat Tours reads: \"1. Get the number of passengers. 2. Multiply 10 by $35. 3. Display the cost.\" Which characteristic does it lack?</p>","options":["Input","Flow of control","General solution","Finite number of steps"],"correct":2,"why":"Step 2 uses a fixed 10 passengers instead of the number obtained, so it works for one case only. Flow of control is tempting, but the steps are in a sensible order; the fault is the fixed value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33","q":"<p>In a flowchart for the Gros Islet Hardware till, one step works out what a customer owes: \"total ← price * quantity\". In which symbol should this step be drawn?</p>","options":["Diamond","Rectangle","Oval","Parallelogram"],"correct":1,"why":"Working out the customer's total is a calculation, which is a process, drawn as a rectangle. The parallelogram is tempting because price and quantity are data, but it is used only when data is input or output, not processed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34","q":"<p>Two clerks at Curaçao Tour Desk follow the same written booking algorithm but read one step differently, so they quote different prices for the same tour. Which representation of an algorithm is MOST likely to cause this problem?</p>","options":["All are equally ambiguous","Flowchart","Natural language","Pseudocode"],"correct":2,"why":"Everyday words can carry more than one meaning, so the two clerks can each interpret a sentence their own way. Pseudocode is tempting for students who find it hard to read, but its structured statements are precise.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35","q":"<p>Before coding a booking system for a guest house in Holetown, the team at the software firm Bay Logic writes and agrees an algorithm. Which statement BEST describes the role of that algorithm?</p>","options":["It is the final executable form of the solution","It replaces the need for a program","It is used only after the program has been tested","It is a plan for the solution that the program is later developed from"],"correct":3,"why":"Bay Logic's algorithm is the plan or blueprint that comes before the booking program. Option A is tempting but describes a program, not an algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36","q":"<p>At San Pedro Tutoring Centre, a teacher's algorithm must input a mark and work out a grade for each of the 25 students in her class, carrying out the same steps every time. Which control structure is needed to handle all 25 students?</p>","options":["Repetition","Selection","Sequence","Assignment"],"correct":0,"why":"Doing the same steps for each of a known number of students is repetition (a FOR loop). Selection is tempting because grading involves decisions, but processing all 25 students is what needs the loop; nothing is chosen between paths at that level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37","q":"<p>A clerk at the Young Street Duty-Free shop uses an algorithm that keeps a running total of the value of every item a cruise passenger buys. Which statement in the algorithm acts as the accumulator?</p>","options":["sum ← sum + price","INPUT price","IF price > 100 THEN","count ← count + 1"],"correct":0,"why":"An accumulator adds a varying value (each price) to a running total. count ← count + 1 is the tempting distractor, but adding a fixed 1 makes it a counter, which counts the items rather than totalling their value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38","q":"<p>A St. John’s radio station uses this algorithm to count down before the morning news: FOR n ← 9 DOWNTO 4 DO OUTPUT n ENDFOR. How many times is the body of the loop executed?</p>","options":["5","4","0","6"],"correct":3,"why":"n takes 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4: six values (9 − 4 + 1). 5 is tempting if you subtract without adding 1; 0 is tempting if you think a loop cannot run from a larger to a smaller number, but DOWNTO counts down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39","q":"<p>A student programmer at the Saba Dive Centre has defined and analysed the dive-booking problem and wants to start typing Python code straight away. According to the stages of program development, which stage should come immediately BEFORE coding?</p>","options":["Documentation","Testing and debugging","Maintenance","Designing and desk-checking the algorithm"],"correct":3,"why":"The algorithm is designed and desk-checked before it is coded: it is the precursor of the program. Testing and debugging is tempting but can only happen once code exists.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40","q":"<p>A programmer at the Roseau Freight Company is rewriting the company's parcel-tracking program in C++, and the manager asks what kind of language C++ is. C++ is BEST classified as which type of programming language?</p>","options":["Markup","Object-oriented","Logic","Functional"],"correct":1,"why":"C++ extends C with classes and objects, so it is classified as object-oriented. It can be used procedurally, but none of the other options fit: logic is Prolog, functional is Haskell, and markup languages such as HTML are not programming languages.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 1","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_q4","title":"Module 1 · Unit 1 quiz: The field of Information Technology","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 1: The field of Information Technology. 59 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (1)","q":"<p>IT captures, stores, processes, retrieves, transmits and presents data. Watch IT at work across the Caribbean and decide which job it is doing at that moment.</p><p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>A utility meter reader's handheld device records the reading from a meter in English Harbour.</b></p>","options":["Capture (input)","Store and retrieve","Transmit","Process","Present (output)"],"correct":0,"why":"Recording the reading brings data into the system: capture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (2)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>A check-in kiosk at Piarco Airport scans a passenger's passport.</b></p>","options":["Transmit","Store and retrieve","Present (output)","Process","Capture (input)"],"correct":4,"why":"Scanning the passport captures data as input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (3)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>The electricity company's billing software multiplies each customer's units used by the tariff to work out the bill.</b></p>","options":["Process","Present (output)","Capture (input)","Transmit","Store and retrieve"],"correct":0,"why":"Calculating turns raw readings into a bill: processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (4)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>The Bay Street Jewellers' till adds up a cruise passenger's purchases and applies a duty-free discount.</b></p>","options":["Present (output)","Store and retrieve","Capture (input)","Transmit","Process"],"correct":4,"why":"Adding and applying a discount is processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (5)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>The Gros Islet clinic keeps patient records on its server and calls one up when the patient returns.</b></p>","options":["Capture (input)","Present (output)","Transmit","Process","Store and retrieve"],"correct":4,"why":"Keeping records and opening them again later is storing and retrieving.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (6)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>Seagrape Bay Secondary School keeps last term's assignments in Google Classroom so students can open them for revision.</b></p>","options":["Process","Present (output)","Capture (input)","Transmit","Store and retrieve"],"correct":4,"why":"The work is stored and retrieved when needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (7)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>A card terminal in a St. John’s shop sends a payment request to the bank's server.</b></p>","options":["Store and retrieve","Process","Present (output)","Capture (input)","Transmit"],"correct":4,"why":"Sending data from one place to another is transmission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (8)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>The San Fernando clinic emails an X-ray image to a specialist in Curaçao.</b></p>","options":["Transmit","Capture (input)","Store and retrieve","Present (output)","Process"],"correct":0,"why":"Sending the image across the network is transmission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (9)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>The Island Courier's website displays the morning headlines on readers' phones.</b></p>","options":["Transmit","Capture (input)","Store and retrieve","Process","Present (output)"],"correct":4,"why":"Showing information to the reader is presenting output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What is IT doing with the data? (10)","q":"<p>Which information-handling job is IT doing here?</p><p><b>Flight information screens at Nassau's airport show departure times and gate numbers.</b></p>","options":["Present (output)","Transmit","Store and retrieve","Capture (input)","Process"],"correct":0,"why":"Displaying information to passengers is presenting output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (1)","q":"<p>IT is used in every sector, and inside each organisation IT work covers infrastructure, software, data and information management, user support and security. Read each situation and choose the BEST answer.</p><p>At the Bayside Insurance office in Bridgetown, Kerwin runs new network cables and replaces a faulty router. Which area of IT work is this?</p>","options":["Data and information management","User support","Infrastructure"],"correct":2,"why":"Networks, cables, routers and servers are infrastructure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (2)","q":"<p>At the Sunset Supermarket in Frigate Bay, the IT officer installs and configures the new version of the accounting package on every office computer. Which area of IT work is this?</p>","options":["Software","Security","Infrastructure"],"correct":0,"why":"Installing and configuring applications is the software part of IT work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (3)","q":"<p>A new receptionist at the Gros Islet Resort cannot print guest invoices, so the IT technician shows her step by step and writes a short guide. Which area of IT work is this?</p>","options":["Security","Software","User support"],"correct":2,"why":"Helping and training users is user support (help desk and training).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (4)","q":"<p>At a law firm on Frederick Street in Port of Spain, the IT team sets up a firewall, enforces strong passwords and encrypts laptops in case they are stolen. Which area of IT work is this?</p>","options":["Infrastructure","User support","Security"],"correct":2,"why":"Protecting systems and data from unauthorised access is security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (5)","q":"<p>At the Seaside Pharmacy in Portsmouth, the IT officer removes duplicate customer records and checks that the stock database is backed up every night. Which area of IT work is this?</p>","options":["Software","User support","Data and information management"],"correct":2,"why":"Keeping data accurate, organised and backed up is data and information management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (6)","q":"<p>Customers in Barbados use a mobile app to transfer money at midnight without visiting a branch. In which sector is IT being used?</p>","options":["Media and communication","Banking and finance","Government"],"correct":1,"why":"Mobile and online banking is IT usage in banking and finance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inside the IT function (7)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma in 2017, relief agencies used satellite phones and social media to locate families in Barbuda. In which sector is IT being used?</p>","options":["Utilities and telecoms","Education","Disaster management"],"correct":2,"why":"Emergency communication and coordination is IT usage in disaster management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Defining the field","q":"<p>At a Sea-Almond Secondary School careers talk, an electricity company officer explains that her department keeps the meters, billing software, servers and network running so that customer data becomes accurate bills and online account information. A student asks what \"the field of IT\" means. Which statement BEST describes it?</p>","options":["Any use of a computer for entertainment such as gaming or streaming.","The use and management of computers, software, networks and telecommunications to capture, process, store, transmit and present information for people and organisations.","The scientific study of algorithms and the theory of computation.","The design of the electronic circuits and processors inside computers."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Entertainment is only one usage. It would not even cover the electricity company officer's billing work, so it is far too narrow to describe a whole field.","Correct. It names the technologies AND the purpose, just like the electricity company officer's work: turning data into information that customers and the company need.","That describes Computer Science, a related field that focuses on theory and algorithms.","That is Computer Engineering, which focuses on hardware and its integration with software."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (1)","q":"<p>A Grade 12 group is preparing a \"Let's Talk IT\" discussion on the scope of IT in Barbados. Sort each statement.</p><p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>Passengers check in for flights at self-service kiosks at GAIA.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":1,"why":"A usage in tourism and transport.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>A wrongly typed meter reading produces an incorrect electricity bill.</b></p>","options":["Usage of IT","Limitation of IT"],"correct":1,"why":"Garbage in, garbage out: the computer cannot judge whether input is correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>Students submit assignments through Microsoft Teams.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":1,"why":"A usage in education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>Card machines stop working during a long island-wide power cut.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":0,"why":"Dependence on power and connectivity is a limitation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>A clinic shares an X-ray image with a specialist in Curaçao.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":1,"why":"A usage in health (telemedicine).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>A laptop bought five years ago can no longer run the latest software.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":0,"why":"Rapid obsolescence is a limitation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>A program cannot decide whether an unusual refund request is fair.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":0,"why":"IT lacks human judgement for situations its rules do not cover.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Usage or limitation? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a usage of IT or a limitation of IT?</p><p><b>Satellite phones and social media help relief agencies coordinate after a hurricane.</b></p>","options":["Limitation of IT","Usage of IT"],"correct":1,"why":"A usage in disaster management.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name that limitation","q":"<p>Each situation below shows a different limitation of IT. Match each limitation to the situation that illustrates it.</p>","left":["Garbage in, garbage out (GIGO)","No human judgement","Dependence on power and connectivity","Cost","Rapid obsolescence","Skills gap","Security and privacy risk","Software error (bug)"],"right":["A stolen, unencrypted laptop exposes a clinic's patient records.","A small guest house in Anguilla cannot afford the software licence, support contract and staff training.","A Montserrat business buys a new server, but no one on staff knows how to configure it.","A loan program rejects a family hit by a hurricane because it cannot weigh their special circumstances.","School laptops bought four years ago can no longer run the new version of the learning software.","A Roseau shop cannot take card payments while the island network is down.","A fault in a booking system sells the same seat on a flight out of Barbados twice.","A clerk keys 9,000 units instead of 900 and a Linden customer receives a huge electricity bill."],"correct":[7,3,5,1,4,2,0,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Build it the software engineering way","q":"<p>The firm IslandPay is building a mobile payment app for customers in Trinidad and Tobago, Grenada and St. Kitts. Put the main software engineering activities in the order the team would first carry them out.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Requirements: meet banks and shop owners to find out exactly what the app must do.","Design: plan the app's structure, screens and data before any coding starts.","Construction: programmers write the code to the team's agreed standards.","Testing: try the app on many phone models and fix the defects found.","Release and maintenance: launch the app, then correct and improve it with regular updates."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the discipline to its focus","q":"<p>Match each computing discipline to its main focus.</p>","left":["Computing","Computer Science","Software Engineering","Computer Engineering","Information Systems","Information Technology"],"right":["The umbrella term for any activity that requires, benefits from or creates computers","Theory of computation, algorithms and making computers solve problems efficiently","Selecting, integrating, managing and supporting technology to meet users' needs","Systematic, disciplined development and maintenance of large, reliable software","Design of computer hardware and its integration with software","How information and technology help organisations reach their goals"],"correct":[0,1,3,4,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (1)","q":"<p>Read each job task and choose the discipline it BEST represents.</p><p>Designing the circuit board and firmware for a new smart water meter.</p>","options":["Information Technology","Computer Engineering","Information Systems"],"correct":1,"why":"Designing hardware and firmware is Computer Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (2)","q":"<p>Proving that a new search algorithm is faster than existing ones.</p>","options":["Software Engineering","Information Technology","Computer Science"],"correct":2,"why":"Analysing algorithms is Computer Science.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (3)","q":"<p>Leading a team that builds, tests and maintains a national tax-filing system over three years.</p>","options":["Computer Science","Software Engineering","Computer Engineering"],"correct":1,"why":"A disciplined, large-scale development process is Software Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (4)","q":"<p>Studying how a hotel handles bookings and recommending a system that gives managers better reports.</p>","options":["Computer Science","Computer Engineering","Information Systems"],"correct":2,"why":"Analysing business processes and information needs is Information Systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (5)","q":"<p>Setting up the Wi-Fi network, user accounts and backups for a school and helping teachers when devices fail.</p>","options":["Information Technology","Computer Engineering","Software Engineering","Computer Science"],"correct":0,"why":"Deploying, administering and supporting infrastructure for users is IT.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline is this? (6)","q":"<p>Deciding whether an airline should invest in a new reservation system to support its expansion strategy.</p>","options":["Information Systems","Information Technology","Computer Engineering"],"correct":0,"why":"Aligning technology investment with business strategy is Information Systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IT or IS?","q":"<p>The Holetown Marina wants to know which services earn the most profit so the owners can plan expansion. A consultant studies how bookings, fuel sales and berth fees are recorded and designs a reporting system for management. This work BEST belongs to which discipline?</p>","options":["Computer Science, because data is being analysed.","Computer Engineering, because a new system is being designed.","Information Technology, because it involves a computer system.","Information Systems, because it focuses on the organisation's processes and information needs for decision-making."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["CS develops algorithms and computing theory. Designing management reports from business processes is IS.","Computer Engineering designs hardware. Nothing here involves designing circuits or devices.","Almost every discipline involves computer systems. The focus here is the business and its decisions, not the infrastructure.","Correct. Studying business processes and designing information for managers is the heart of IS."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the commonalities","q":"<p>A classmate drafts a list of features that ALL five computing disciplines share. Some lines are wrong because they describe only one discipline.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a genuine commonality of all the computing disciplines.</p>","options":["4. They all focus mainly on business strategy.","5. They all expect professionals to act ethically.","3. They all deal with data and information.","7. They all specialise in proving the limits of computation.","1. They all require logical problem-solving.","2. They all design processors and circuit boards.","6. They all involve some programming."],"correct":[1,2,4,6],"why":"Correct. Problem-solving, data, ethics and programming are shared. Circuit design belongs to Computer Engineering, business strategy to Information Systems and the limits of computation to Computer Science."},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (1)","q":"<p>Professional organisations do much more than collect membership fees. Read each situation and decide what the professional body is doing.</p><p>A new router sold in Belmopan works with every brand of phone and laptop because the makers all follow IEEE 802.11.</p>","options":["Supporting members' careers","Accrediting degrees","Setting technical standards"],"correct":2,"why":"IEEE develops technical standards so products from different makers work together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (2)","q":"<p>A software developer in Barbados refuses to hide a known security flaw from a client, pointing to the rules her professional body expects members to follow.</p>","options":["Publishing research","Publishing a code of ethics or conduct","Advising government"],"correct":1,"why":"Codes of ethics and conduct (ACM, IEEE, BCS) guide members' professional behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (3)","q":"<p>BCS examines a university's computing degree and confirms that it meets professional standards.</p>","options":["Accrediting education","Running conferences","Setting technical standards"],"correct":0,"why":"Accrediting degrees is how professional bodies guide and assure the quality of education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (4)","q":"<p>An IT manager in Trinidad and Tobago is awarded Chartered IT Professional (CITP) status after showing her experience and competence.</p>","options":["Developing curricula","Publishing research","Recognising qualified professionals"],"correct":2,"why":"BCS recognises qualified professionals through chartered status.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (5)","q":"<p>A Coralstone College lecturer in Belize reads recent studies on hotel booking systems in the AIS eLibrary and presents her own paper at AMCIS.</p>","options":["Setting technical standards","Accrediting degrees","Publishing research and running conferences"],"correct":2,"why":"AIS publishes Information Systems research and runs conferences such as AMCIS and ICIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (6)","q":"<p>Computing students at a regional university join an IEEE student branch, enter a robotics competition and meet engineers who later offer internships.</p>","options":["Supporting members' careers","Advising government","Publishing a code of ethics"],"correct":0,"why":"Student branches and chapters support members' careers through networking, competitions and mentoring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Professional bodies at work (7)","q":"<p>ACM presents the A.M. Turing Award to a computer scientist whose work changed computing.</p>","options":["Setting electrical safety standards","Accrediting degrees","Recognising achievement through awards"],"correct":2,"why":"ACM's Turing Award, often called the \"Nobel Prize of computing\", recognises outstanding contributions.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the professional organisation","q":"<p>Match each professional organisation to what it is or does.</p>","left":["IEEE","BCS","ACM","AIS","IEEE Computer Society","ACM SIGs"],"right":["The international body for Information Systems academics and professionals","The largest educational and scientific computing society; presents the Turing Award","The Chartered Institute for IT in the UK, awarding Chartered IT Professional status","The branch of IEEE devoted to computing","Develops technical standards such as 802.11 (Wi-Fi) and 802.3 (Ethernet)","Special Interest Groups in areas such as graphics and human-computer interaction"],"correct":[4,2,1,0,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why do standards matter?","q":"<p>A tourist's laptop bought in Canada connects without trouble to the Wi-Fi in a Belize City café whose router was made by a different company in Asia. Which professional organisation's work BEST explains this?</p>","options":["BCS, because it accredits computing degrees.","AIS, because it studies how organisations use information systems.","ACM, because it publishes the Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct.","IEEE, because its 802.11 standard defines how Wi-Fi devices communicate."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Accreditation concerns education quality, not how devices interoperate.","AIS focuses on IS research and teaching, not device standards.","ACM's code guides behaviour. It does not define how wireless devices communicate.","Correct. Shared IEEE standards let equipment from different makers work together."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (1)","q":"<p>A careers fair at Sea-Almond Secondary School in St. Lucia lists these job adverts. Sort each job by the discipline that BEST prepares a candidate for it.</p><p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Embedded systems engineer to design control boards for desalination plant pumps.</b></p>","options":["Computer Engineering","Software Engineering","Information Systems","Information Technology"],"correct":0,"why":"Designing control hardware is Computer Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (2)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Mobile app developer to build and maintain a ferry-booking app for the Castries to Martinique route.</b></p>","options":["Software Engineering","Information Systems","Computer Engineering","Information Technology"],"correct":0,"why":"Building and maintaining software is Software Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (3)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Network administrator for a hotel chain in Rodney Bay.</b></p>","options":["Software Engineering","Information Technology","Information Systems","Computer Engineering"],"correct":1,"why":"Running network infrastructure is IT.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (4)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Business analyst to redesign the claims process at an insurance company in Barbados.</b></p>","options":["Information Systems","Information Technology","Computer Engineering","Software Engineering"],"correct":0,"why":"Analysing business processes is Information Systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (5)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Help desk technician supporting staff at a ministry in Castries.</b></p>","options":["Information Systems","Software Engineering","Computer Engineering","Information Technology"],"correct":3,"why":"User support is a core IT role.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (6)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Hardware engineer to design a sensor that measures water levels in English Harbour.</b></p>","options":["Software Engineering","Information Technology","Computer Engineering","Information Systems"],"correct":2,"why":"Designing physical devices is Computer Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (7)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>IT manager to align technology spending with a bank's five-year strategy.</b></p>","options":["Computer Engineering","Information Technology","Information Systems","Software Engineering"],"correct":2,"why":"Aligning technology with organisational strategy is Information Systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which discipline prepares you for this career? (8)","q":"<p>Which discipline does this career draw on most?</p><p><b>Quality assurance tester for a large accounting software product.</b></p>","options":["Software Engineering","Information Technology","Computer Engineering","Information Systems"],"correct":0,"why":"Testing within a disciplined development process is Software Engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Plan the IT careers research project","q":"<p>You and a partner are completing the CXC careers research activity. Put the steps in the best order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Choose a career and agree on your roles with your partner.","Identify the computing discipline the career draws on most.","Gather information from adverts, websites, professional bodies and a resource person.","Record duties, qualifications, skills and where the job exists in the Caribbean.","Check your sources for reliability and currency.","Present your findings creatively to the class.","Reflect on whether the career suits you and what study it needs."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising a future IT professional (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a peer mentor at Sea-Almond Secondary School. Jaden, a CAPE student, wants a career in computing but is not sure which path to take. Give him the best advice.</p><p>Jaden says: \"I like fixing the school's Wi-Fi and helping teachers with their laptops, but I am not interested in maths proofs or designing chips.\" What do you suggest first?</p>","options":["His interests match Information Technology: infrastructure and supporting users. Suggest he explore IT roles such as network or systems administration.","Tell him Computer Engineering is the best path because all computing jobs are the same."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. You matched his interests to the discipline's focus.","The disciplines differ. CE centres on hardware design, which Jaden says he does not enjoy."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising a future IT professional (step 2)","q":"<p>Jaden asks how he can find out more about real IT jobs in the region. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Research adverts, interview the school's IT officer as a resource person and read the BCS and IEEE websites about the profession.","Just watch one video online and decide from that."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. Multiple sources, including a resource person, give a reliable picture.","One source is not enough. Compare several reliable sources."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising a future IT professional (step 3)","q":"<p>Jaden worries: \"If I go into IT, will a computer just do my job? Computers can do everything now.\" How do you respond?</p>","options":["Explain IT's limitations: systems need people to check data, make judgements, maintain equipment and keep services running during power or network failures, as after Hurricane Irma.","Agree that IT has no limitations, so he should choose another career."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You used the limitations of IT to show why skilled professionals remain essential.","IT has real limitations: GIGO, no human judgement, dependence on power, cost and obsolescence."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Answering a \"describe\" question","q":"<p>Keisha, a CAPE student at Mahoe Grove High School, is practising a Paper 02-style question: \"Describe ONE limitation of Information Technology.\" (2 marks) Her study group writes four answers. Which answer would earn BOTH marks?</p>","options":["IT is expensive.","IT is used in banking, tourism and education.","IT depends on a reliable power supply, so when electricity fails, for example after a hurricane, businesses cannot process card payments or access their records.","Computers can be slow sometimes."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This identifies a limitation but does not describe it. It earns at most one mark.","This describes usage, not a limitation.","Correct. It states the limitation and explains its consequence with an example.","Vague and not a recognised limitation of the field. Speed is usually an advantage of IT."]}]},{"id":"assess_10n","title":"Module 1 · Unit 2 quiz: The history of Information Technology","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 2: The history of Information Technology. 64 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (1)","q":"<p>A Lower Sixth class at Sea-Almond Secondary School is drafting answers to \"Outline the history of computer hardware.\" Judge each point a student wrote as an examiner would.</p><p>Kayla writes: \"Then came transistors.\"</p>","options":["Wrong: incorrect date or order","Strong point","Weak: missing date or effect"],"correct":2,"why":"Not wrong, but thin. Kayla needs a period (late 1950s to mid-1960s) and an effect (smaller, cooler, more reliable).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (2)","q":"<p>Jaden writes: \"About 1971, the microprocessor put a whole CPU on one chip, which led to cheap personal computers.\"</p>","options":["Weak: missing date or effect","Strong point","Wrong: incorrect date or order"],"correct":1,"why":"Date, defining technology and effect in one sentence: a full outline point.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (3)","q":"<p>Amara writes: \"The Internet was invented and everybody started using it.\"</p>","options":["Wrong: incorrect date or order","Strong point","Weak: missing date or effect"],"correct":2,"why":"No date, no technology (ARPANET, packet switching, TCP/IP) and a vague effect. It earns little.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (4)","q":"<p>Shanice writes: \"The 1990s: the microprocessor was invented.\"</p>","options":["Wrong: incorrect date or order","Weak: missing date or effect","Strong point"],"correct":0,"why":"The date is wrong. The first commercial microprocessor, the Intel 4004, came in 1971.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (5)","q":"<p>Devon writes: \"First-generation computers (1940s to mid-1950s) used vacuum tubes, so they filled rooms, used huge power and overheated.\"</p>","options":["Weak: missing date or effect","Strong point","Wrong: incorrect date or order"],"correct":1,"why":"Period, component and effects linked by \"so\": strong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strong or weak outline point? (6)","q":"<p>Luis writes: \"The World Wide Web came first, and then ARPANET joined the networks together.\"</p>","options":["Strong point","Wrong: incorrect date or order","Weak: missing date or effect"],"correct":1,"why":"The order is reversed. ARPANET (1969) came about twenty years before the Web (1989 to 1991).","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"From abacus to microprocessor","q":"<p>The Anna Regina Science Fair team is building a wall display on the history of computing hardware. Put the milestones in order, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The abacus is used to count and calculate with beads.","Pascal builds a geared adding machine, the Pascaline.","Jacquard controls a loom with punched cards.","Babbage designs the Analytical Engine with a mill and a store.","Hollerith tabulates a census with punched-card machines.","ENIAC calculates with thousands of vacuum tubes.","IBM announces the System/360 family built with integrated circuits.","Intel releases the 4004, the first commercial microprocessor."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why were transistor computers better?","q":"<p>At the Montego Bay Computer Museum in Kingston, a guide stands between a 1940s vacuum-tube computer and a 1960 transistor computer. She tells visitors the newer machine broke down far less often and was cheaper to run. Which statement BEST explains why?</p>","options":["The stored-program concept meant computers no longer needed electricity to hold data.","High-level languages such as FORTRAN made the hardware more reliable.","Transistors replaced vacuum tubes, so the machines used less power, produced less heat and had far fewer component failures.","Integrated circuits placed thousands of transistors on one chip, so there were fewer connections to fail."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Both machines in the museum needed electricity. The stored-program concept is about keeping the program in memory, and it began in the first generation.","FORTRAN made programming the 1960 machine easier, but a language does not change how often hardware fails.","Correct. The guide's newer machine changed its switching component; lower power, less heat and fewer failures are the effects.","This describes the third generation (from 1964). The guide's 1960 machine used individual (discrete) transistors wired on circuit boards."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (1)","q":"<p>The Frigate Bay Computer Museum in Basseterre has mixed up its display cards. Sort each card into the generation it describes.</p><p>Which generation?</p><p><b>A room-sized machine needs its own air-conditioned hall because thousands of glowing tubes keep overheating and burning out</b></p>","options":["3rd: integrated circuits","4th: microprocessors","2nd: transistors","1st: vacuum tubes"],"correct":3,"why":"Heat and frequent failures are the classic effects of vacuum tubes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (2)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>Programmers key in binary machine-language codes that work only on that one machine</b></p>","options":["3rd: integrated circuits","4th: microprocessors","2nd: transistors","1st: vacuum tubes"],"correct":3,"why":"Machine language was the only option for first-generation computers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (3)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>A large firm's payroll machine stores data in magnetic core memory and runs programs written in COBOL</b></p>","options":["2nd: transistors","1st: vacuum tubes","4th: microprocessors","3rd: integrated circuits"],"correct":0,"why":"Core memory and early high-level languages arrived with transistors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (4)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>Individual solid-state switches, invented at Bell Labs in 1947, are wired one by one onto circuit boards</b></p>","options":["2nd: transistors","3rd: integrated circuits","1st: vacuum tubes","4th: microprocessors"],"correct":0,"why":"Discrete (separate) transistors define the second generation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (5)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>A university department buys its own cabinet-sized PDP-8 and staff share it from terminals by time-sharing</b></p>","options":["4th: microprocessors","3rd: integrated circuits","1st: vacuum tubes","2nd: transistors"],"correct":1,"why":"ICs made minicomputers and time-sharing operating systems possible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (6)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>A bank upgrades to a bigger model in a compatible family of computers without rewriting its programs</b></p>","options":["4th: microprocessors","3rd: integrated circuits","2nd: transistors","1st: vacuum tubes"],"correct":1,"why":"The IBM System/360 family (1964) was built with integrated circuits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (7)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>The Sunset Reef Hotel in Gros Islet runs its front desk on a small PC with a graphical user interface</b></p>","options":["1st: vacuum tubes","4th: microprocessors","2nd: transistors","3rd: integrated circuits"],"correct":1,"why":"Cheap desktop PCs with GUIs are fourth-generation machines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which generation does it describe? (8)","q":"<p>Which generation?</p><p><b>A whole CPU fits on one chip, so a student carries a laptop to Flamboyant Heights College</b></p>","options":["1st: vacuum tubes","4th: microprocessors","3rd: integrated circuits","2nd: transistors"],"correct":1,"why":"A CPU on one chip is the microprocessor: fourth generation.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the generation to its technology","q":"<p>Match each generation to the description that defines it.</p>","left":["Pre-electronic era","First generation","Second generation","Third generation","Fourth generation","Fifth generation (as often described)"],"right":["Vacuum tubes, magnetic drums and machine language","Gears, punched cards and human operators: the Pascaline and the Analytical Engine design","Transistors, magnetic core memory, FORTRAN and COBOL","Integrated circuits, operating systems, terminals and the minicomputer","A whole CPU on one chip, personal computers and GUIs","Artificial intelligence and parallel processing as goals"],"correct":[1,0,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each system into the category of computer that BEST fits it.</p><p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>Runs a hurricane-track model with billions of calculations for a regional weather service</b></p>","options":["Mainframe","Mobile or embedded","Supercomputer","Minicomputer","Microcomputer"],"correct":2,"why":"One enormous calculation needing maximum speed: a supercomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (2)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>Processes thousands of card payments every second for a large regional bank</b></p>","options":["Supercomputer","Mobile or embedded","Microcomputer","Minicomputer","Mainframe"],"correct":4,"why":"High transaction volume, many users and high reliability: a mainframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (3)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>A 1960s cabinet-sized DEC PDP-8 shared by one university laboratory</b></p>","options":["Microcomputer","Minicomputer","Mainframe","Supercomputer","Mobile or embedded"],"correct":1,"why":"Cheaper than a mainframe and serving one department: a minicomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (4)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>A laptop a Sixth Form student uses to type a research report</b></p>","options":["Mobile or embedded","Mainframe","Microcomputer","Minicomputer","Supercomputer"],"correct":2,"why":"A single-user machine built around a microprocessor: a microcomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (5)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>The chip that controls the wash cycle in a washing machine</b></p>","options":["Microcomputer","Mobile or embedded","Minicomputer","Supercomputer","Mainframe"],"correct":1,"why":"A computer built into a product for one task: embedded.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (6)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>A smartphone used to pay for a taxi at Piarco Airport</b></p>","options":["Mainframe","Supercomputer","Minicomputer","Mobile or embedded","Microcomputer"],"correct":3,"why":"Handheld, battery-powered: mobile.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (7)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>Central airline reservation system handling bookings worldwide</b></p>","options":["Mainframe","Supercomputer","Microcomputer","Mobile or embedded","Minicomputer"],"correct":0,"why":"Huge numbers of simultaneous transactions: a mainframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which category of computer? (8)","q":"<p>Which category is this?</p><p><b>A desktop PC in the Seaside Pharmacy in San Pedro</b></p>","options":["Mainframe","Microcomputer","Mobile or embedded","Supercomputer","Minicomputer"],"correct":1,"why":"A desktop for one user at a time: a microcomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the machine to its category","q":"<p>The Port of Spain Computer Museum is opening a \"Big to Small\" gallery. Match each historic machine or device to the category card that belongs beside it.</p>","left":["CDC 6600 (1964) and Cray-1 (1976)","IBM System/360 (1964)","DEC PDP-8 (1965)","Altair 8800 (1975) and IBM PC (1981)","Motorola DynaTAC (1983), later the smartphone (2007)","Apollo Guidance Computer (1960s), later microcontrollers"],"right":["Mobile device: handheld and battery-powered, like a phone used to pay a taxi at Piarco airport","Minicomputer: cabinet-sized and affordable for one university department","Embedded system: a computer inside another product, like the controller in a home electricity meter","Microcomputer: built around a microprocessor for one user, like an office PC in San Fernando","Mainframe: huge transaction volumes and many users, as in a regional bank","Supercomputer: maximum speed on one huge calculation, the kind used for hurricane-track models"],"correct":[5,4,1,3,0,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Mainframe or supercomputer?","q":"<p>Leeward Islands Bank, with branches in Barbados, St. Vincent and St. Kitts, must process millions of account transactions a day, with almost no downtime and thousands of users connected at once. Which category of computer BEST suits this requirement?</p>","options":["A group of microcomputers, because each teller could keep their own records.","A minicomputer, because it is cheaper and serves a single department.","A mainframe, because it is designed for very high volumes of transactions, many simultaneous users and high reliability.","A supercomputer, because it is the fastest type of computer available."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Separate PCs would give separate, inconsistent records. The bank needs one central, reliable system.","A department-sized machine cannot handle a multi-island bank's workload. The minicomputer is also largely a historical category.","Correct. Volume, reliability and many users are the defining strengths of the mainframe; banks have used mainframes since the 1960s.","Supercomputers are built for raw speed on a single huge calculation, such as weather modelling, not for very large numbers of simultaneous transactions."]},{"t":"match","title":"From plugboards to high-level languages","q":"<p>A Form 6 student at Frangipani Hill Academy is preparing a two-minute talk for Computer Science Week on how programming changed. Put the slides in order, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Ada Lovelace publishes an algorithm for Babbage's Analytical Engine (1843).","ENIAC operators rewire plugboards and set switches to change the task (1940s).","Programmers write binary machine language for one specific processor (late 1940s).","Assembly language uses mnemonics such as LOAD and ADD, converted by an assembler (1950s).","FORTRAN (1957) and COBOL (1959) bring English-like high-level languages translated by compilers.","BASIC (1964) is designed so that students can learn to program easily.","C (1972) is used to write operating systems such as UNIX.","Object-oriented and web languages such as Java and JavaScript appear (1995)."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (1)","q":"<p>A business consultant in Roseau is showing clients how software has changed over the decades. Decide which stage of software history each situation shows.</p><p>In 1975 the Caribbean Mutual Insurance in Trinidad pays a team of programmers to write its own billing program for a leased mainframe.</p>","options":["App or cloud software","Custom-written program","Packaged software"],"correct":1,"why":"Written specially for one organisation: custom software, typical of the 1950s to 1970s.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (2)","q":"<p>In 1995 the Regent Street jeweller buys a spreadsheet package and keeps stock records on one PC without hiring a programmer.</p>","options":["App or cloud software","Custom-written program","Packaged software"],"correct":2,"why":"Bought ready-made for many users: packaged software, which spread with microcomputers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (3)","q":"<p>Today Exuma Island Tours takes bookings through an online service it opens in a phone browser and pays for monthly.</p>","options":["App or cloud software","Packaged software","Custom-written program"],"correct":0,"why":"Delivered over the Internet as a service: cloud software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (4)","q":"<p>In 1985 a clerk at the Kralendijk Shipping Agency types text commands at a prompt to list and copy files in MS-DOS.</p>","options":["Graphical user interface","Command-line interface","Touch interface"],"correct":1,"why":"Typed commands with no icons: a command-line interface.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (5)","q":"<p>In 1998 a receptionist at the Montego Bay Clinic clicks icons and menus with a mouse in Windows 95.</p>","options":["Command-line interface","Touch interface","Graphical user interface"],"correct":2,"why":"Windows, icons, menus and a pointer: a GUI.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (6)","q":"<p>Today a waiter at the English Harbour Grill taps orders into an Android tablet.</p>","options":["Graphical user interface","Command-line interface","Touch interface"],"correct":2,"why":"Tapping the screen with a finger: a touch interface on a mobile operating system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Software eras in Caribbean businesses (7)","q":"<p>A school computer lab in Dominica installs Linux and the Firefox browser free of charge because their source code is publicly available.</p>","options":["Open-source software","Packaged software sold in a box","Custom-written program"],"correct":0,"why":"Freely available source code: open-source software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (1)","q":"<p>Classify each software milestone.</p><p>FORTRAN (1957), used to write scientific calculations</p>","options":["Application package","Operating system","Programming language"],"correct":2,"why":"FORTRAN is a high-level programming language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (2)","q":"<p>UNIX (1969 onward), a multi-user system that manages the computer's resources</p>","options":["Operating system","Application package","Programming language"],"correct":0,"why":"UNIX manages hardware, files and users: an operating system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (3)","q":"<p>VisiCalc (1979), the first electronic spreadsheet</p>","options":["Operating system","Application package","Programming language"],"correct":1,"why":"A spreadsheet is application software for end users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (4)","q":"<p>MS-DOS (1981), shipped with the IBM PC</p>","options":["Operating system","Application package","Programming language"],"correct":0,"why":"DOS stands for Disk Operating System.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (5)","q":"<p>COBOL (1959), designed for business record-keeping</p>","options":["Operating system","Application package","Programming language"],"correct":2,"why":"COBOL is a programming language, even though its purpose is business.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language, operating system or application? (6)","q":"<p>Android (2008), which runs most of the smartphones sold in the Caribbean today</p>","options":["Programming language","Operating system","Application package"],"correct":1,"why":"Android is a mobile operating system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What joined the networks together?","q":"<p>In a Coralstone College lecture, students learn that before the early 1980s a US research network and several other computer networks existed but could not easily exchange data with each other. Which development MOST directly allowed them to interconnect as one Internet?</p>","options":["Email, because it let users on different computers send messages.","The World Wide Web, because it linked pages with hyperlinks.","The Mosaic browser, because it displayed images.","The TCP/IP protocol suite, adopted by ARPANET in 1983, which gave all networks common rules for addressing and transmitting packets."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Email was an early application, but it needed a shared protocol to cross between networks.","The Web (1989 to 1991) runs on top of an Internet that already existed. Hyperlinks connect documents, not networks.","Mosaic (1993) made the Web attractive to the public but did not connect networks.","Correct. TCP/IP is the common language of the Internet; its adoption in 1983 is often called the Internet's birth."]},{"t":"match","title":"Build the Internet timeline","q":"<p>For a Sandbox Tree College open-day poster, put these Internet milestones in order, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Packet switching is proposed as a way to send data across a network.","ARPANET sends its first message between two university sites.","Network email using the @ sign is sent.","ARPANET switches to TCP/IP, and the Internet is born.","Tim Berners-Lee creates the World Wide Web at CERN.","The Mosaic graphical browser brings the Web to the public.","Broadband and social media spread.","Smartphones make the mobile Internet mainstream."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"match","title":"From telegraph to 5G","q":"<p>The Bayleaf Hill Secondary media club is writing a feature called \"How Grenada talks to the world\". Put these telecommunications milestones in order, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Morse sends coded pulses along wires with the electric telegraph (1844).","A lasting transatlantic telegraph cable links Europe and North America (1866).","Bell patents the telephone, carrying voice over copper wires (1876).","Marconi sends a radio signal across the Atlantic (1901).","Telstar relays live television across the Atlantic by satellite (1962).","TAT-8, the first transatlantic fibre-optic cable, carries data as pulses of light (1988).","2G GSM brings digital mobile calls and SMS text messages (1990s).","4G LTE brings mobile broadband and video to smartphones (about 2009 onward).","5G offers very high speed and low latency (2019 onward)."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8","Step 9"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (1)","q":"<p>You are advising the owners of the Pelican Key Guesthouse in Frigate Bay, who have questions about how the island stays connected. Choose the best answer each time.</p><p>The owner asks why guests' video calls to Europe travel mainly by undersea cable rather than by satellite.</p>","options":["Cables can never be damaged by storms","Satellites cannot reach small islands","Fibre gives higher bandwidth and lower latency"],"correct":2,"why":"Fibre carries far more data with much less delay. Satellites can reach islands, and cables can be damaged.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (2)","q":"<p>A guest from Jamaica remembers mobile phones and calls becoming much cheaper in the early 2000s. What MOST caused this?</p>","options":["Liberalisation ended single-provider markets and new operators such as Digicel competed","Governments banned landlines","Satellites replaced all undersea cables"],"correct":0,"why":"Opening markets to competition from around 2000 drove prices down and spread mobile phones.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (3)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma (2017), the owner reads that rebuilding focused on \"resilience\". What does that mean here?</p>","options":["Cheaper call rates for tourists","Stronger towers, more underground fibre and back-up power so services survive storms","Replacing mobile phones with telegraphs"],"correct":1,"why":"Resilience means the network keeps working, or recovers quickly, when disaster strikes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (4)","q":"<p>Most of the guesthouse staff go online with smartphones rather than PCs. Which development made this common across the Caribbean?</p>","options":["Mobile data on 3G and 4G networks","Dial-up modems on landlines","Submarine telegraph cables"],"correct":0,"why":"Mobile data made the smartphone the main Internet device for many households.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (5)","q":"<p>The owner asks what first linked Caribbean islands to Europe and North America for fast messages in the late nineteenth century.</p>","options":["Fibre-optic cables","4G masts","Submarine telegraph cables"],"correct":2,"why":"Telegraph cables on the seabed came first; fibre arrived a century later.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Caribbean connections (6)","q":"<p>The owner asks why satellite links are still kept if cables carry most international traffic.</p>","options":["Because cables only carry voice calls","As a back-up and for remote areas if a cable is cut","Because satellites have lower latency than fibre"],"correct":1,"why":"Satellites give an alternative route. Fibre has lower latency and carries data, not only voice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cable or satellite?","q":"<p>The manager of the Sunset Reef Hotel in Frigate Bay notices that guests' video calls to Europe run smoothly with almost no delay. Which statement BEST explains why fibre-optic submarine cables, rather than satellites, carry most of St. Kitts's international Internet traffic?</p>","options":["Cables are never damaged by storms or ships.","Satellites cannot send signals to small islands.","Fibre carries far more data (higher bandwidth) with much lower delay (latency) than satellite links.","Satellites only carry analogue television signals."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Cables can be cut by anchors or damaged near the shore. That is why networks want more than one cable route.","Satellites can reach the hotel's island; they are used as back-up and for remote areas.","Correct. High bandwidth and low latency are why the hotel's video calls run smoothly over fibre.","Modern satellites carry digital data, including Internet traffic."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the pioneer to the contribution","q":"<p>Match each person to their place in IT history.</p>","left":["Charles Babbage","Ada Lovelace","Herman Hollerith","Grace Hopper","Kilby and Noyce","Cerf and Kahn","Tim Berners-Lee","Samuel Morse"],"right":["Developed early compilers and influenced COBOL","Processed a census using punched-card tabulating machines","Developed the electric telegraph and its code","Created the World Wide Web at CERN","Wrote what is often called the first computer program","Designed the Analytical Engine with a mill and a store","Developed the integrated circuit","Designed TCP/IP, the protocols of the Internet"],"correct":[5,4,1,0,6,7,3,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (1)","q":"<p>The syllabus names four strands. Sort each milestone into its strand.</p><p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>Intel 4004, a CPU on one chip (1971)</b></p>","options":["Internet","Telecommunications","Hardware","Software"],"correct":2,"why":"A physical component: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (2)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>COBOL for business data processing (1959)</b></p>","options":["Hardware","Internet","Telecommunications","Software"],"correct":3,"why":"A programming language: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (3)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>ARPANET switches to TCP/IP (1983)</b></p>","options":["Internet","Hardware","Telecommunications","Software"],"correct":0,"why":"Network protocols: the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (4)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>Bell patents the telephone (1876)</b></p>","options":["Hardware","Software","Internet","Telecommunications"],"correct":3,"why":"Voice over a distance: telecommunications.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (5)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>The IBM System/360 family of compatible mainframes (1964)</b></p>","options":["Software","Telecommunications","Internet","Hardware"],"correct":3,"why":"A family of computers: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (6)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>Windows brings a GUI to most PCs</b></p>","options":["Internet","Telecommunications","Software","Hardware"],"correct":2,"why":"An operating system: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (7)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>The Domain Name System translates names into addresses</b></p>","options":["Hardware","Internet","Software","Telecommunications"],"correct":1,"why":"An Internet naming service.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which strand of IT history? (8)","q":"<p>Hardware, software, Internet or telecommunications?</p><p><b>2G GSM brings digital mobile calls and SMS</b></p>","options":["Telecommunications","Software","Internet","Hardware"],"correct":0,"why":"A mobile network standard: telecommunications.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the errors in the timeline","q":"<p>A classmate drafted this timeline for a revision poster. Some lines contain historical errors.</p><p>Select EVERY line that contains an error.</p>","options":["4. Bell patented the telephone in 1876.","2. The Intel 4004 (1971) was the first commercial microprocessor.","6. ARPANET adopted TCP/IP in 1983.","3. The World Wide Web was created before ARPANET.","5. The IBM PC (1981) was a supercomputer.","1. ENIAC (1945) used thousands of transistors.","7. COBOL was designed for business data processing."],"correct":[3,4,5],"why":"Well spotted. ENIAC used vacuum tubes, the Web (1989 to 1991) came twenty years after ARPANET (1969), and the IBM PC was a microcomputer."},{"t":"mc","title":"Curating a history of IT exhibition (step 1)","q":"<p>You are helping the \"Bits to Broadband\" exhibition at a library in Port of Spain. Make the decisions a careful IT historian would make.</p><p>The organiser has 20 photos of old machines and asks how to arrange the hardware wall. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Arrange them by generation, labelling each with its defining component (tube, transistor, IC, microprocessor) and approximate period.","Arrange them alphabetically by manufacturer so they are easy to find."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Visitors see cause and effect: each component made machines smaller, faster and cheaper.","Alphabetical order hides the story. History needs sequence."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Curating a history of IT exhibition (step 2)","q":"<p>A draft label says: \"The IBM PC (1981) was a powerful mainframe used by banks.\" What do you do?</p>","options":["Correct it: the IBM PC was a microcomputer built around a microprocessor, designed for one user, and it ran MS-DOS.","Leave it. Visitors will not notice the difference."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Right. Categories by size and processing ability matter.","Accuracy is the point of an exhibition, and a CAPE examiner would notice."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Curating a history of IT exhibition (step 3)","q":"<p>The last panel covers telecommunications in Trinidad and Tobago. The organiser wants exact cable names and dates but has no source. What do you write?</p>","options":["Describe the history accurately in general terms (submarine fibre cables, the move to mobile networks and the growth of mobile broadband) and invite the local telecommunications providers to check any specific details before printing.","Make up plausible cable names and dates so the panel looks precise."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You kept to facts you can support and planned to verify details.","Never invent facts about real organisations or infrastructure."]}]},{"id":"assess_185","title":"Module 1 · Unit 3 quiz: Data, information and knowledge","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 3: Data, information and knowledge. 62 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (1)","q":"<p>The Seaview Guesthouse in Kingston keeps records about its guests and bookings. Sort each item.</p><p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>14, 9, 22, 17, 30</b></p>","options":["Information","Data"],"correct":1,"why":"A list of numbers with no labels or context is unprocessed, discrete data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (2)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>Occupancy in March was 82%, the highest this year.</b></p>","options":["Data","Information"],"correct":1,"why":"The figures have been calculated and compared, so they now have meaning: information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (3)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>A pile of handwritten check-in cards.</b></p>","options":["Information","Data"],"correct":1,"why":"The cards are unorganised and unprocessed: data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (4)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>A bar chart showing that most guests book through the website.</b></p>","options":["Data","Information"],"correct":1,"why":"The data has been summarised and presented in a meaningful format: information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (5)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>USA, Canada, Netherlands, USA, Curaçao</b></p>","options":["Information","Data"],"correct":1,"why":"Unprocessed country names with no count or context: data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (6)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>Average length of stay for European guests is 9 nights.</b></p>","options":["Data","Information"],"correct":1,"why":"An average is the result of processing, with a clear context: information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (7)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>Room 12</b></p>","options":["Data","Information"],"correct":0,"why":"A discrete chunk with no context: is it booked, dirty, empty? Data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data or information? (8)","q":"<p>Is this raw data or processed information?</p><p><b>Three rooms need repairs before Friday's cruise-season rush.</b></p>","options":["Data","Information"],"correct":1,"why":"Organised and meaningful to the manager: information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (1)","q":"<p>The Bodden Town Community Clinic is collecting data. Choose the BEST description for each.</p><p>Each patient's blood pressure is measured with a digital monitor.</p>","options":["quantitative","qualitative"],"correct":0,"why":"A measurement with units is objective: quantitative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (2)","q":"<p>Patients write how they felt about the waiting time.</p>","options":["quantitative","qualitative"],"correct":1,"why":"Feelings and opinions are subjective: qualitative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (3)","q":"<p>Patients rate the cleanliness of the clinic from 1 to 5.</p>","options":["qualitative","quantitative"],"correct":0,"why":"The number records an opinion, so it is still qualitative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (4)","q":"<p>The pharmacy system logs every tablet dispensed, with time and batch number.</p>","options":["sampled","detailed"],"correct":1,"why":"Every item is recorded in full: detailed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (5)","q":"<p>A nurse surveys every tenth patient who visits in June.</p>","options":["sampled","detailed"],"correct":0,"why":"Only a selection of patients is recorded: sampled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the data (6)","q":"<p>A cleaner checks the water-cooler temperature at 9 a.m. and 3 p.m. only.</p>","options":["sampled","detailed"],"correct":0,"why":"Two readings a day represent the whole day: sampled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (1)","q":"<p>The Antigua Storm Ready Committee must turn its shelter and storm data into information that different people can use. For each situation, choose the BEST option.</p><p>A fisherman already at sea off English Harbour needs a storm warning within the hour. Which MEDIUM is best?</p>","options":["A printed leaflet left at the fish market","A 20-page PDF on the committee website","A short VHF radio broadcast"],"correct":2,"why":"Radio reaches him at sea in time. The leaflet and the PDF cannot reach him where he is, so the information would fail on medium and timeliness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (2)","q":"<p>The committee wants its board to see how shelter use changed over the last five storms. Which FORMAT shows the trend most clearly?</p>","options":["A list of 2,000 individual sign-in times","A line graph of shelter use per storm","A voice note describing each storm"],"correct":1,"why":"A line graph summarises the data and shows the trend at a glance. A raw list of sign-in times is still data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (3)","q":"<p>A blind resident of All Saints needs to know the nearest shelter. Which choice suits this receiver?</p>","options":["A colour-coded chart emailed as an image","An audio message by phone call","A large colour map on a notice board"],"correct":1,"why":"Format and medium must suit the receiver. Audio by phone is usable; anything purely visual is incomprehensible to this user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (4)","q":"<p>Volunteers add up the cots used at each shelter and divide by the number of nights open. Which processing operation is this?</p>","options":["Sorting","Collecting","Calculating"],"correct":2,"why":"Totals and averages are calculations: processing that turns raw counts into information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (5)","q":"<p>A clerk arranges the list of registered households alphabetically by street name. Which processing operation is this?</p>","options":["Summarising","Calculating","Sorting and organising"],"correct":2,"why":"Putting records into a useful order is sorting and organising.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right format, right medium (6)","q":"<p>The committee stops posting letters about shelter openings and sends the same words as an SMS alert. What has it changed?</p>","options":["The content","The medium","The format"],"correct":1,"why":"The words are the same; only the channel used to deliver them has changed, so this is a change of medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What turned it into information?","q":"<p>The Portsmouth Hardware store records every sale. At the end of the month the manager produces a report stating that \"plywood sales rose by 65% in the week before the hurricane warning\". Which statement BEST explains why the report is information rather than data?</p>","options":["The figures are quantitative, so they are automatically information.","The manager used the report to decide to order more plywood next year.","The sales figures have been processed and compared, giving them context and meaning for the manager.","The figures are stored on a computer rather than on paper."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Quantitative describes a type of data. A raw list of numbers is quantitative but still data.","Using information to make a decision describes knowledge, not the step from data to information.","Correct. Calculating a percentage change and linking it to an event gives the figures meaning: that is processing.","Data can be stored on a computer and still be raw. The medium does not decide whether it is data or information."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the trust characteristic","q":"<p>Match each characteristic of information to the situation that best illustrates it.</p>","left":["Distortion","Disguise","Inconsistency","Incomprehensible","Subject to interpretation","Reliable"],"right":["A chart starts its axis at 95 so a small rise looks huge","A figure of 1,500 becomes 15,000 after it is passed through three WhatsApp groups","Rainfall readings from a calibrated station that match the neighbouring station","The registry and the school database show two different addresses for one student","Two managers read the same survey comments and reach opposite conclusions","A parent receives a report full of error codes and abbreviations"],"correct":[1,0,3,5,4,2]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the timeliness and lifespan problems","q":"<p>The Roseau Ferry Service posts updates for passengers travelling to Guadeloupe and Martinique. Read its notice board.</p><p>Select EVERY line where the information fails because of TIMELINESS or LIFESPAN.</p>","options":["6. Passenger names are shown on a public screen.","1. \"Today's 10:00 sailing is cancelled\" is posted at 10:45.","2. A fare list from three years ago is still displayed beside the ticket desk.","4. Yesterday's sea-state forecast is still pinned up this morning.","5. The notice lists the departure time but not the departure pier.","3. The timetable is printed in English and French."],"correct":[1,2,3],"why":"Correct. Line 1 arrived too late (timeliness); lines 2 and 4 have passed their lifespan. Line 5 is a completeness problem and line 6 a confidentiality problem."},{"t":"match","title":"What makes information worth having?","q":"<p>Match each characteristic of information to the Caribbean situation that best illustrates it.</p>","left":["Value","Relevance","Timeliness","Completeness","Lifespan","Information as a commodity"],"right":["Today's US dollar rate at a Frederick Street exchange bureau is replaced by tomorrow's rate","A cargo manifest at the port lists every container AND its weight for the crane operator","A flight-delay alert reaches the Piarco Airport taxi rank before the passengers land","Leeward Wind Data Co. charges yacht charter firms a monthly fee for detailed wind forecasts","A San Fernando hotel manager plans next week's rosters from expected arrivals, not last year's electricity bills","A tide table helps a Chaguanas fisherman avoid a wasted trip, saving fuel and a day's work"],"correct":[5,4,2,1,0,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (1)","q":"<p>The New Amsterdam Medical Centre is reviewing how it handles patient information. Sort each situation by the characteristic it MOST illustrates.</p><p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>Only the treating doctor may read a patient's test results.</b></p>","options":["Availability","Security","Shareability","Confidentiality"],"correct":3,"why":"A rule about WHO may see sensitive information: confidentiality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (2)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>A receptionist breaks the rules by reading results aloud in the waiting room.</b></p>","options":["Shareability","Security","Confidentiality","Availability"],"correct":2,"why":"Sensitive information reaches unauthorised people: a breach of confidentiality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (3)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>Patient files are encrypted and each nurse logs in with a personal password.</b></p>","options":["Confidentiality","Availability","Security","Shareability"],"correct":2,"why":"Encryption and passwords are controls that protect information: security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (4)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>Access rights let nurses view prescriptions but not change them.</b></p>","options":["Security","Confidentiality","Shareability","Availability"],"correct":0,"why":"Access levels are a security control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (5)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>One vaccination schedule is posted online and every parent can open it at the same time.</b></p>","options":["Confidentiality","Shareability","Availability","Security"],"correct":1,"why":"Many people use the same information without using it up: shareability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (6)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>Two doctors read the same lab report at once, and neither loses it.</b></p>","options":["Security","Availability","Confidentiality","Shareability"],"correct":3,"why":"Sharing information does not use it up: shareability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (7)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>The records system stays online during a power cut because a generator starts.</b></p>","options":["Confidentiality","Security","Availability","Shareability"],"correct":2,"why":"Authorised users can still reach the information when needed: availability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Confidential, secure, shareable or available? (8)","q":"<p>Which characteristic does this situation illustrate?</p><p><b>An emergency doctor can pull up a patient's allergy list at 3 a.m.</b></p>","options":["Security","Shareability","Availability","Confidentiality"],"correct":2,"why":"Information accessible when and where it is needed: availability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (1)","q":"<p>The Regent Street Credit Union is reviewing problems with its information. Sort each problem by the group of characteristics it affects.</p><p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>Two branches show different balances for the same account.</b></p>","options":["Protection and access","Trust and understanding","Usefulness and worth"],"correct":1,"why":"Contradictory records: inconsistency, a trust problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (2)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>A loan report uses so many abbreviations that members cannot follow it.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Protection and access","Usefulness and worth"],"correct":0,"why":"Incomprehensible information cannot be understood.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (3)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>The monthly report reaches the board two weeks after the meeting.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Protection and access","Usefulness and worth"],"correct":2,"why":"Late information fails on timeliness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (4)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>The report on a loan application leaves out the applicant's existing debts.</b></p>","options":["Usefulness and worth","Trust and understanding","Protection and access"],"correct":0,"why":"Missing key facts: completeness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (5)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>A staff member emails member account numbers to a personal address.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Usefulness and worth","Protection and access"],"correct":2,"why":"A breach of confidentiality and security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (6)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>The online banking system is down for two days.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Usefulness and worth","Protection and access"],"correct":2,"why":"Users cannot reach their information: availability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (7)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>Interest-rate figures were mistyped when copied into the newsletter.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Protection and access","Usefulness and worth"],"correct":0,"why":"The meaning was changed in processing: distortion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trust, worth or protection? (8)","q":"<p>Which group of characteristics does this problem affect?</p><p><b>Last year's rates are still on the website.</b></p>","options":["Trust and understanding","Protection and access","Usefulness and worth"],"correct":2,"why":"The information has passed its lifespan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information as a commodity","q":"<p>The Caribbean Market Insights Ltd collects data on shopping habits across Jamaica, Barbados and Guyana, processes it into regional reports and sells the reports to supermarket chains. This BEST illustrates which characteristic of information?</p>","options":["Shareability","Information as a commodity","Lifespan","Availability"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Shareability is the ability to give information to many people without using it up. The key point here is that the reports are sold for money.","Correct. The information is produced and traded for payment, like any other product.","Lifespan is how long information stays valid. Nothing in the scenario concerns how long the reports remain useful.","Availability is about authorised users being able to reach information when they need it, not about trading it."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deciding before the storm (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the operations manager of Island Fresh supermarket in Oistins. A tropical storm watch has been issued for Barbados. Make the best decisions with the data and information you have.</p><p>A staff member forwards a voice note saying \"a Category 4 will hit tomorrow morning, everyone is buying water\". What do you do first?</p>","options":["Check the latest official advisory and its issue time before acting.","Order three containers of water immediately based on the voice note."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. You tested the information for reliability and timeliness before deciding.","Forwarded messages are often distorted. Acting on unreliable information is risky and costly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deciding before the storm (step 2)","q":"<p>The official advisory confirms a storm watch, with arrival in about 48 hours. To plan stock, which data do you use?</p>","options":["Detailed sales data from this store in the 48 hours before previous storm watches.","National average annual sales for all supermarkets in the Caribbean."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Appropriate: relevant, from your own store and the right kind of event.","Accurate perhaps, but inappropriate: wrong population and wrong time scale."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deciding before the storm (step 3)","q":"<p>The report shows water, batteries and canned food sell three to five times faster before storms. You notice last year's battery figures were keyed in twice. What next?</p>","options":["Correct the duplicate entries, recalculate, then decide on order quantities and staff hours.","Ignore it; the numbers are close enough."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You fixed a data-quality problem before turning the information into a decision.","Duplicate data inflates the figures: garbage in, garbage out."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (1)","q":"<p>Mango Scoop ice-cream parlour on Fort Street wants to decide which new flavour to launch for the cruise season. Sort each problem with its data.</p><p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>Some sales were keyed in twice on the till.</b></p>","options":["Appropriateness (is it the right data?)","Data quality (is the data correct?)"],"correct":1,"why":"Duplicates make the data inaccurate: a quality problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>A cashier typed 1,000 cones instead of 100.</b></p>","options":["Data quality (is the data correct?)","Appropriateness (is it the right data?)"],"correct":0,"why":"A data-entry error: the data is inaccurate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>Half the survey forms have no answer to the flavour question.</b></p>","options":["Data quality (is the data correct?)","Appropriateness (is it the right data?)"],"correct":0,"why":"Missing values: the data is incomplete.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>The flavour price list in the system has not been updated for three years.</b></p>","options":["Data quality (is the data correct?)","Appropriateness (is it the right data?)"],"correct":0,"why":"Out-of-date data is a quality problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>The owner studies sales from a parlour in Canada during winter.</b></p>","options":["Appropriateness (is it the right data?)","Data quality (is the data correct?)"],"correct":0,"why":"Accurate, perhaps, but from the wrong place and time: inappropriate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>The owner looks at the shop's electricity bills to choose a flavour.</b></p>","options":["Appropriateness (is it the right data?)","Data quality (is the data correct?)"],"correct":0,"why":"Correct data, but irrelevant to the decision: inappropriate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>Staff, not customers, are asked which flavour customers would buy.</b></p>","options":["Data quality (is the data correct?)","Appropriateness (is it the right data?)"],"correct":1,"why":"Wrong population: the data does not come from the people the decision is about.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality problem or wrong data? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a data-quality problem or an appropriateness problem?</p><p><b>Only customers on one quiet, rainy Monday are surveyed about busy cruise days.</b></p>","options":["Appropriateness (is it the right data?)","Data quality (is the data correct?)"],"correct":0,"why":"The sample is from the wrong time, so it does not represent cruise-day customers.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"From raw readings to a decision","q":"<p>The Botanical Gardens Monitoring Group tests water quality to decide whether to issue a warning. Put the steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Collect water samples and record readings at several points around the pond (data).","Check the readings for errors, missing values and duplicates (data quality).","Process the readings: average them and compare them with safe limits.","Present the results as a short table and map for the committee (information, format and medium).","Interpret the results using past experience of what causes high readings (knowledge).","Decide whether to issue a public warning and act on the decision."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Data, information or knowledge at NMIA?","q":"<p>Each example below comes from an imagined day in operations at Norman Manley International Airport (illustrative only). Match each level to the best example.</p>","left":["Data","Information","Knowledge","Qualitative data","Quantitative data","Sampled data"],"right":["When four flights land within 30 minutes, open two extra immigration desks to avoid long queues","Queue length counted once every 15 minutes","14:05, 14:20, 14:20, 14:35, 15:10","Average wait time measured at 42 minutes","A passenger comment: \"the queue felt endless\"","Four flights are due within 30 minutes of each other this afternoon"],"correct":[2,5,0,4,3,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which statement is knowledge?","q":"<p>A teacher at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Barbados analyses mock-examination results. Which statement represents KNOWLEDGE rather than data or information?</p>","options":["The class average was 59% and four students scored below 50%.","Because students who practise past papers improve most on database questions, the class will do two timed database papers before the exam.","Most students lost marks on database questions.","62, 48, 75, 51, 39, 80"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is processed data with context: information. It describes the situation but does not decide anything.","Correct. Information is combined with experience to make a decision: knowledge.","This is still information: an organised finding. No decision or action has been taken from it.","These are unprocessed, discrete marks with no context: data."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (1)","q":"<p>Students at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Barbados are practising SO 1.3 questions. Judge each answer or question the way an examiner would.</p><p>Question: \"Distinguish between data and information.\" Kemar writes only: \"Data are raw, unprocessed facts.\"</p>","options":["Full marks","Loses marks: only one of the two terms is defined"],"correct":1,"why":"Distinguish needs a difference on the same feature for EACH term. Kemar must also say what information is, with an example.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (2)","q":"<p>Question: \"Give an example of knowledge.\" Aaliyah writes: \"Rainfall in Bridgetown was 40% above average in September.\"</p>","options":["Full marks: this is knowledge","Loses marks: this is information, with no decision"],"correct":1,"why":"The statement is processed and meaningful, but nothing is decided. Adding \"so the council will clear the drains in August\" would make it knowledge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (3)","q":"<p>Question: \"Classify a 1-to-5 hotel room rating.\" Dwayne writes: \"Quantitative, because it is a number.\"</p>","options":["Correct","Incorrect: it records an opinion, so it is qualitative"],"correct":1,"why":"A rating records a subjective judgement. Being written as a number does not make it a measurement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (4)","q":"<p>Question: \"Explain timeliness.\" Shanice writes: \"Information must be available in time for the decision it supports; a storm warning issued after landfall is useless.\"</p>","options":["Earns the marks","Loses marks: timeliness means \"fast\""],"correct":0,"why":"Shanice gives the correct meaning (in time for the decision) and a reason with an example.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (5)","q":"<p>A Paper 02 question begins: \"Identify TWO characteristics of quantitative data.\" What does the command word require?</p>","options":["Simply name the two characteristics","Points for and against, with a conclusion","A detailed reason for each point"],"correct":0,"why":"Identify means name or state; no explanation is needed for the mark.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Would this answer earn the marks? (6)","q":"<p>Question: \"Distinguish between confidentiality and security.\" Ravi writes one sentence saying they both mean \"keeping data safe\".</p>","options":["Loses marks: they are treated as one point","Full marks"],"correct":0,"why":"Confidentiality is the requirement that information be restricted; security is the set of controls that restrict it. Treating them as one loses the distinction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Quality or appropriateness?","q":"<p>Guadeloupe Tourism Survey Team wants to decide which new attraction to build for visitors. It uses an accurate, carefully checked spreadsheet of resident electricity bills for the last five years. Which statement BEST describes the problem with this data?</p>","options":["The data is of poor quality because it contains errors.","The data is qualitative, so it cannot be processed.","The data is inappropriate because it does not relate to visitors' preferences.","The data is incomplete because it covers only five years."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["The scenario says the data is accurate and carefully checked, so quality is not the problem.","Electricity bills are quantitative, and qualitative data can be processed anyway.","Correct. Good-quality data is still useless if it is not relevant to the decision: that is appropriateness.","Five years of bills would not help even if more were added: the issue is what the data is about."]}]},{"id":"assess_1fx","title":"Module 1 · Unit 4 quiz: Types of information sources","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 4: Types of information sources. 73 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>Kayla, a Lower Six student at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Anguilla, has interviewed people about how residents got news during Hurricane Irma in 2017. Sort each note from her research diary.</p><p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>Kayla asks the radio presenter a follow-up question about which stations stayed on air, and he explains straight away.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source"],"correct":0,"why":"Interactive: you can ask follow-up questions and clarify. That is an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>Her grandmother in East End remembers the storm arriving on a different day from the official record.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source"],"correct":1,"why":"Memory errors: recollections change over the years. That is a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>The Sandy Ground shopkeeper describes selling goods by torchlight, something never written down anywhere.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of people as a source","Advantage of people as a source"],"correct":1,"why":"Local, unpublished knowledge that exists only in people's experience: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>A manager she hoped to interview is too busy to meet her before her deadline.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of people as a source","Advantage of people as a source"],"correct":0,"why":"Availability: experts may be busy or unwilling, and interviews take time to arrange. A disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>The shopkeeper insists his shop was the first on the street to reopen, but Kayla has no record to check this.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source"],"correct":1,"why":"Hard to verify and possibly self-serving: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>A Flamboyant Heights College lecturer explains in plain language how radio signals travel, pitched at her level.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source"],"correct":0,"why":"People can explain at a level that suits you: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>The interviews cost Kayla nothing except her time and a few phone calls.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of people as a source","Advantage of people as a source"],"correct":1,"why":"Often free or low-cost: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interviewing people: advantage or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Does this note show an advantage or a disadvantage of people as an information source?</p><p><b>Each person can describe only their own street, so her picture of the whole island is incomplete.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of people as a source","Disadvantage of people as a source"],"correct":1,"why":"Limited to one person's experience, so not representative: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"When is a person the best source?","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet wants to know why customers prefer its delivery service to collecting in store. Which source is MOST likely to give detailed, current insight into this question?</p>","options":["Interviews with a sample of regular customers","A DVD of pharmacy training videos","A printed textbook on retail management","A library catalogue"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The pharmacy's own customers hold this first-hand knowledge, and an interview allows follow-up questions.","A training DVD is fixed content made for staff. It cannot tell you what local customers think.","A textbook gives general principles, not the opinions of this pharmacy's customers, and it may be several years old.","A catalogue only helps you find items held by a library. It does not contain customer opinions."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (1)","q":"<p>A Lower Six class from Flamboyant Heights College spends a morning with Mr. Hodge, a school librarian in St. John's, Antigua. For each situation, choose the best answer about books, catalogues and periodicals.</p><p>Tariq needs a full, structured explanation of how computers developed, and he has a week to read it at home.</p>","options":["Today's newspaper","A supplier catalogue","A textbook from the library"],"correct":2,"why":"Books give depth and a logical structure, with chapters and an index.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (2)","q":"<p>Tariq's library book on smartphones was printed in 2015. What is its main weakness for a report on this year's smartphones?</p>","options":["It cannot contain diagrams","It needs a device to read it","It is likely to be out of date"],"correct":2,"why":"Books take months or years to produce, so in fast-moving IT topics they date quickly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (3)","q":"<p>Shania searches the library catalogue for \"Caribbean history\" and gets a list of titles with shelf numbers. What has the catalogue given her?</p>","options":["The full text of each book","The location of items so she can find them","An expert summary of each book"],"correct":1,"why":"A library catalogue is a finding aid: it describes items and where they are, not their content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (4)","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge compares laptops in a printed supplier catalogue before buying for the library. Which caution should he keep in mind?</p>","options":["The seller wrote it, so it presents the products favourably","It has been checked by independent experts","It reports original research"],"correct":0,"why":"Product catalogues are written by the seller, and printed prices and models date quickly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (5)","q":"<p>Devon needs expert research findings, with references, on solar power in small island states.</p>","options":["A research journal","A computing magazine","A daily newspaper"],"correct":0,"why":"Journals publish research by specialists, often reviewed by experts, with reference lists.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (6)","q":"<p>Aaliyah wants to know what the government announced yesterday about road repairs in All Saints.</p>","options":["A book on the history of Antigua","A national daily newspaper","A research journal"],"correct":1,"why":"Newspapers are the most current print source and cover local events.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Print sources: make the right call (7)","q":"<p>Jaden wants a readable, illustrated review of new gaming consoles before Christmas.</p>","options":["A research journal","A library catalogue","A magazine"],"correct":2,"why":"Magazines offer readable reviews and illustrations for general readers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (1)","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge, a librarian at a St. John's school, is labelling the resources in his collection. Sort each source by its medium.</p><p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A printed copy of the daily newspaper</b></p>","options":["Traditional","Electronic"],"correct":0,"why":"A printed newspaper needs no device: traditional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A retired electricity company engineer who agrees to be interviewed</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":1,"why":"People are a traditional source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A DVD of a documentary on Caribbean history</b></p>","options":["Traditional","Electronic"],"correct":1,"why":"A DVD needs a player or computer: electronic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A subscription database of journal articles</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":0,"why":"Electronic databases are accessed by computer: electronic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A printed supplier catalogue of computer parts</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":1,"why":"A printed catalogue is a traditional source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A wiki written by Form 5 students about local plants</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":0,"why":"A wiki is a collaboratively edited web site: electronic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A printed computing journal on the library shelf</b></p>","options":["Traditional","Electronic"],"correct":0,"why":"A printed journal is traditional. (The same journal online would be electronic.)","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>A tourism board's Instagram account</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":0,"why":"Social media is an electronic source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic? (9)","q":"<p>Is this a traditional or an electronic source?</p><p><b>An encyclopaedia on a CD-ROM</b></p>","options":["Electronic","Traditional"],"correct":0,"why":"The content is the same type as a book, but the medium is a CD-ROM: electronic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>Malik, an Upper Six student in Bridgetown, is using a public library for his Caribbean trade project. Sort each experience he has.</p><p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>A librarian shows Malik how to find three books on Caribbean trade in ten minutes.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a library","Disadvantage of a library"],"correct":0,"why":"Trained librarians help users find and choose sources: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>With his library card, Malik reads articles from an expensive journal subscription for free.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a library","Disadvantage of a library"],"correct":0,"why":"Free or low-cost access to costly sources: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>The only copy of the book Malik needs is out on loan for two weeks.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a library","Disadvantage of a library"],"correct":1,"why":"Limited copies and loan periods: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>The library closes at 5 p.m., but Malik can only study late at night.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of a library","Advantage of a library"],"correct":0,"why":"Opening hours limit access: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>Old local newspapers from the 1990s are kept there and are not available online.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of a library","Advantage of a library"],"correct":1,"why":"Libraries preserve local material: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>Malik's classmate lives far from town and has no transport to reach the building.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of a library","Advantage of a library"],"correct":0,"why":"Users must travel to the building: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>Books are catalogued and shelved by subject, so related items sit together.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a library","Disadvantage of a library"],"correct":0,"why":"Sources are selected and organised: an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Using the library: advantage or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of a library as an information source?</p><p><b>A small island library lost part of its collection when a storm flooded the building.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of a library","Disadvantage of a library"],"correct":1,"why":"Physical collections can be damaged by storms: a disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the source to its description","q":"<p>Match each information source to the description that fits it best.</p>","left":["Journal","Magazine","Newspaper","Catalogue","Library","CD-ROM","DVD"],"right":["Illustrated periodical of features and reviews for a general audience","Organised list describing items so they can be located or chosen","Organised collection of sources, with staff to help users","Periodical of research articles written by specialists for specialists","Daily or weekly report of current local and world events","Read-only optical disc holding about 700 MB of multimedia content","Optical disc of about 4.7 GB, large enough for a full-length film"],"correct":[3,0,4,1,2,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Database or web site?","q":"<p>A student at Sandbox Tree College needs published research articles on renewable energy in small island states. Which statement BEST explains why the library's electronic database is a better choice than a general web search?</p>","options":["The database is free to everyone, while web sites charge a fee.","The database can be used without any Internet connection.","The database is always more up to date than any web site.","The database contains selected material from established publishers and allows precise filtering by subject and date."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["The reverse is usually true: the student reaches the database through the university's paid subscription, while most web sites are free.","Most electronic databases are accessed online, so the student needs a connection just as for a web search.","Web sites can be updated within minutes. Databases are not automatically more current; their strength is quality and searchability.","Correct. The student can filter to published research on small island renewable energy from recent years, instead of wading through adverts and blogs in a web search."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the disadvantages of a wiki","q":"<p>The Statia Heritage Wiki lets anyone edit pages about the history of St. Eustatius. A student lists points about it for an essay.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a DISADVANTAGE of using this wiki as a source.</p>","options":["2. Pages are updated quickly when new archaeological finds are announced.","1. Any visitor can change a page, so incorrect dates may be added.","3. A page could be vandalised and stay wrong until someone notices.","6. Some well-edited pages include references to books and journals.","7. Less popular pages may have been checked by very few people.","4. Many contributors share knowledge that is not written down anywhere else.","5. Contributors are often anonymous, so you cannot check their expertise."],"correct":[1,2,4,6],"why":"Correct. Open editing, vandalism, anonymous authors and uneven checking are the key disadvantages. Fast updates, shared knowledge and references are advantages."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the electronic source to its key disadvantage","q":"<p>Each electronic source has a characteristic weakness. Match them.</p>","left":["CD-ROM","Electronic database","Web site","Blog","Wiki","Social media"],"right":["Often requires a paid subscription or institutional login","Mainly one writer's opinion, with no editorial checking","Content is fixed and cannot be updated once pressed","Rumours and misinformation spread faster than corrections","Anyone can publish, so quality varies widely","Any user may edit a page, so errors or vandalism can appear"],"correct":[2,0,4,1,5,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (1)","q":"<p>For each information need, choose the source that is MOST suitable.</p><p>Today's arrival delays at V. C. Bird International Airport in Antigua.</p>","options":["A computing journal","A book on Caribbean aviation","The airport's official web site"],"correct":2,"why":"Live, official information: the airport's web site.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (2)","q":"<p>Peer-reviewed research on coral reef damage around Saba.</p>","options":["A diver's blog","A scientific journal found through an electronic database","A tourism magazine"],"correct":1,"why":"Expert research is published in journals, best found through a database.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (3)","q":"<p>How small shops in Road Town, Tortola, coped in the week after Hurricane Irma.</p>","options":["A DVD encyclopaedia","A supplier catalogue","Interviews with shop owners"],"correct":2,"why":"First-hand local experience comes from people.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (4)","q":"<p>The specifications and prices of laptops a supplier stocks.</p>","options":["A wiki","The supplier's catalogue","A newspaper editorial"],"correct":1,"why":"A product catalogue lists specifications and prices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (5)","q":"<p>An interactive science programme for a school in the Guyana interior with no Internet.</p>","options":["Social media","An electronic database","A DVD"],"correct":2,"why":"A DVD works offline and carries multimedia.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most suitable source (6)","q":"<p>A detailed, structured explanation of computer history for a beginner.</p>","options":["A daily newspaper","A social media thread","A textbook"],"correct":2,"why":"A book gives depth and a logical structure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (1)","q":"<p>Jaden, a student at Calabash Point Secondary in Sint Maarten, is researching the 2017 hurricane season. Sort each item he collected.</p><p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>A recorded interview with his uncle, who sheltered in Philipsburg during Hurricane Irma</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Primary"],"correct":1,"why":"First-hand account from someone who experienced the event: primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>A textbook chapter on the causes of Atlantic hurricanes</b></p>","options":["Primary","Secondary"],"correct":1,"why":"It explains and summarises other work: secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>Photographs his neighbour took of damage in Simpson Bay the next morning</b></p>","options":["Primary","Secondary"],"correct":0,"why":"Original images captured at the time: primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>A magazine feature reviewing recovery efforts five years later</b></p>","options":["Primary","Secondary"],"correct":1,"why":"It interprets events after the fact: secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>Wind-speed readings recorded by a weather station during the storm</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Primary"],"correct":1,"why":"Raw original data: primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>An encyclopaedia entry on the 2017 Atlantic hurricane season</b></p>","options":["Primary","Secondary"],"correct":1,"why":"An encyclopaedia summarises other sources: secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>A blog post that compares several news reports about the storm</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Primary"],"correct":0,"why":"It analyses and compares other reports: secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a primary or a secondary source?</p><p><b>A resident's live video posted to social media as the eye passed over</b></p>","options":["Primary","Secondary"],"correct":0,"why":"Original eyewitness content: primary, even though it is on social media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (1)","q":"<p>Each card describes a real research need in the region. Use the advantages of each type to decide whether a primary or a secondary source is the better choice.</p><p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>The Portsmouth Water Sports wants to know what its own customers think of evening kayak tours.</b></p>","options":["A secondary source suits it better","A primary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"Only a primary source (its own survey) is specific to its customers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A Lower Six student has one afternoon to get an overview of tourism trends across the Caribbean.</b></p>","options":["A primary source suits it better","A secondary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"Secondary sources are quick and give an overview someone has already put together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A dive shop on Bonaire wants figures no one else has: how many divers saw turtles on its trips this month.</b></p>","options":["A secondary source suits it better","A primary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"Primary data gives unique, current information not available anywhere else.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A Form 4 student needs the causes of hurricanes explained in simple language.</b></p>","options":["A secondary source suits it better","A primary source suits it better"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary sources such as textbooks are easier for a beginner to understand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A researcher wants to control and check exactly how her water samples from Carlisle Bay, Barbados, are collected.</b></p>","options":["A secondary source suits it better","A primary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"With primary data you control and can check the method of collection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A small business in Grenada has no budget for its own survey but needs regional market information.</b></p>","options":["A primary source suits it better","A secondary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"Secondary sources are cheaper than collecting new data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A journalist wants the exact words of a minister's speech, not someone's summary of it.</b></p>","options":["A primary source suits it better","A secondary source suits it better"],"correct":0,"why":"A primary source has no added interpretation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Primary or secondary: which suits the need? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of source suits this need better?</p><p><b>A student new to a topic wants a reading list that will lead her to the original documents.</b></p>","options":["A primary source suits it better","A secondary source suits it better"],"correct":1,"why":"Secondary sources give context and references that lead to primary sources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Classify the journal article","q":"<p>A journal article written by a team at Coralstone College reports the results of their own survey of internet use among 2,000 households in Trinidad and Tobago. As an information source, this article is BEST described as</p>","options":["primary, because it reports original research carried out by the authors","secondary, because it is a printed traditional source","primary, because it was published in the Caribbean","secondary, because all journal articles are secondary sources"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The researchers collected and report their own original data, so the article is a primary source.","Primary or secondary depends on closeness to the original, not on whether the medium is print or electronic.","Where it was published is irrelevant. It is primary because it reports original research.","Journals can carry both types. An article reporting the authors' own new research is primary."]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the sources after an event","q":"<p>A fire breaks out at the Bayside Market in Bridgetown. Put the sources in the order in which information about it would TYPICALLY become available, earliest first.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["A bystander tells the fire service by phone what she can see.","Videos of the smoke are posted on social media.","A local news web site publishes a short online report.","The next printed edition of a newspaper carries a fuller report with quotes.","A monthly magazine runs a feature on fire safety in older Bridgetown buildings.","A fire-safety journal publishes a research article analysing causes of market fires in the region.","A book on the history of Bridgetown includes a paragraph on the fire."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (1)","q":"<p>For each situation in Jamaica and the wider region, decide which group of sources GENERALLY has the advantage.</p><p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>Two days after a hurricane there is no power or Internet in Montego Bay, and a student needs to revise.</b></p>","options":["Electronic sources have the edge","Traditional sources have the edge"],"correct":1,"why":"Traditional sources need no equipment, power or connection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (2)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A student on Carriacou needs to search thousands of articles for the word \"desalination\" in seconds.</b></p>","options":["Traditional sources have the edge","Electronic sources have the edge"],"correct":1,"why":"Electronic sources allow fast keyword searching and filters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (3)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A teacher wants students to watch an animation with narration explaining a process.</b></p>","options":["Traditional sources have the edge","Electronic sources have the edge"],"correct":1,"why":"Electronic sources carry multimedia: audio, video and animation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (4)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>Thirty students in different districts all need the same source at 10 p.m.</b></p>","options":["Electronic sources have the edge","Traditional sources have the edge"],"correct":0,"why":"Electronic sources give many users access at once, 24 hours a day.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (5)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A reader wants an edited, checked account rather than unchecked blog and social media posts.</b></p>","options":["Traditional sources have the edge","Electronic sources have the edge"],"correct":0,"why":"Books, journals and newspapers usually pass through editors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (6)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A family in Martinique wants a source that costs nothing in data charges and needs no device.</b></p>","options":["Electronic sources have the edge","Traditional sources have the edge"],"correct":1,"why":"Traditional sources need no device or data plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (7)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A researcher needs flight information that was updated this morning.</b></p>","options":["Electronic sources have the edge","Traditional sources have the edge"],"correct":0,"why":"Online sources can be updated instantly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or electronic: which has the edge? (8)","q":"<p>In this situation, which group of sources has the edge?</p><p><b>A record must still be readable in 30 years without worrying that its file format will become obsolete.</b></p>","options":["Electronic sources have the edge","Traditional sources have the edge"],"correct":1,"why":"Paper does not depend on a file format or device, although it must be protected from water and fire.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Researching for the BVI tourism project (step 1)","q":"<p>You are helping Amara, an Upper Six student, research the question: \"How did cruise tourism in the British Virgin Islands recover after Hurricane Irma?\" She has two weeks. Choose wisely.</p><p>Amara wants to start. Where should she begin to get an overview quickly?</p>","options":["Read a wiki article and a recent magazine feature for an overview, noting their references.","Copy facts straight from a wiki into her report and finish."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Secondary sources give a quick overview, and references lead to stronger sources.","A wiki is a starting point. Anyone can edit it, so copying it without checking is risky."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Researching for the BVI tourism project (step 2)","q":"<p>She now needs reliable figures on cruise passenger numbers before and after 2017. What next?</p>","options":["Search the school library's electronic database and official statistics web sites for published figures.","Use numbers quoted in social media comments under a cruise video."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. Databases and official sites give selected, checkable data.","Social media posts are unchecked and may repeat rumours or guesses."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Researching for the BVI tourism project (step 3)","q":"<p>Her teacher says the project needs first-hand evidence too. What does she add?</p>","options":["Nothing; secondary sources are always enough.","Interview a tour operator at the Castries cruise pier and a Bridge Street shop owner, recording and noting their answers."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Secondary sources may not answer her specific local question. Primary evidence fills that gap.","Excellent. Primary sources add local, current experience, and recording them reduces the memory and verification problems."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weighing a disadvantage","q":"<p>A teacher in Dominica prefers a printed encyclopaedia to an encyclopaedia on CD-ROM for her classroom. Which reason is a VALID disadvantage of the CD-ROM version in this situation?</p>","options":["The CD-ROM content can be edited by any student.","The CD-ROM needs a constant Internet connection.","The CD-ROM cannot store pictures.","The classroom laptops have no optical drive, so the disc cannot be read."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A pressed CD-ROM is read-only. That describes a wiki, not a CD-ROM.","One advantage of a CD-ROM is that it works offline.","CD-ROMs store multimedia, including images, sound and video. This is not a valid disadvantage.","Correct. CD-ROMs need a drive, and many modern devices lack one."]}]},{"id":"assess_1ms","title":"Module 1 · Unit 5 quiz: Characteristics of sources and criteria for selecting them","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 5: Characteristics of sources and criteria for selecting them. 59 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Available or affordable?","q":"<p>The Bayside Research Group sells a detailed report on tourism in the north-eastern Caribbean. It can be downloaded instantly from its web site, at any hour, for US$350. Which statement BEST identifies the characteristics of this source?</p>","options":["It has low currency because it is a report.","It is highly available but has a high cost.","It is unreliable because it is sold online.","It has low availability because it costs money."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Currency depends on when the content was produced or updated, not on the type of document.","Correct. Instant, round-the-clock download means high availability; US$350 is a high direct cost. The two characteristics are separate.","Reliability is not decided by being online or being sold. Nothing in the stem tells us about its reliability.","Cost and availability are different characteristics. A source can be easy to obtain and still expensive."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (1)","q":"<p>A Grade 12 student at Sea-Almond Secondary School in Dominica is gathering sources. For each source, decide whether its stand-out characteristic is <b>depth</b> (lots of detail on a narrow topic) or <b>breadth</b> (wide coverage, less detail).</p><p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>A 60-page engineering study of one desalination plant's energy use.</b></p>","options":["Depth (narrow and detailed)","Breadth (wide coverage)"],"correct":0,"why":"One plant, many pages of detail: depth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (2)","q":"<p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>A one-page fact sheet listing the capitals, populations and currencies of every CARICOM member.</b></p>","options":["Depth (narrow and detailed)","Breadth (wide coverage)"],"correct":1,"why":"Many countries, little on each: breadth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (3)","q":"<p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>A thesis on the history of a single Roseau street from 1900 to 1950.</b></p>","options":["Breadth (wide coverage)","Depth (narrow and detailed)"],"correct":1,"why":"A narrow topic studied in detail: depth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (4)","q":"<p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>An introductory textbook chapter surveying all major types of computer.</b></p>","options":["Breadth (wide coverage)","Depth (narrow and detailed)"],"correct":0,"why":"It surveys many types briefly: breadth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (5)","q":"<p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>A news round-up summarising ten stories from across the region.</b></p>","options":["Depth (narrow and detailed)","Breadth (wide coverage)"],"correct":1,"why":"Ten stories in brief: breadth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Deep or broad? (6)","q":"<p>Which characteristic stands out?</p><p><b>An interview with an electricity company engineer explaining in detail how one substation was rebuilt after Hurricane Maria.</b></p>","options":["Breadth (wide coverage)","Depth (narrow and detailed)"],"correct":1,"why":"A single event explained thoroughly: depth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (1)","q":"<p>Holetown Marine Survey is sharing its findings in many ways. For each card, decide whether it names the <b>format</b> (how the information is arranged) or the <b>medium</b> (what carries it to the user).</p><p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A table of water temperatures at six dive sites around Holetown.</b></p>","options":["Format (how it is arranged)","Medium (what carries it)"],"correct":0,"why":"A table is a way of arranging information: format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (2)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A printed booklet handed out at the Bridgetown cruise pier.</b></p>","options":["Medium (what carries it)","Format (how it is arranged)"],"correct":0,"why":"Paper is the carrier: medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (3)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A narrated video showing coral damage after Hurricane Elsa.</b></p>","options":["Format (how it is arranged)","Medium (what carries it)"],"correct":0,"why":"Video is the form of the information: format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (4)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>The survey's web site, read on a phone in Speightstown.</b></p>","options":["Medium (what carries it)","Format (how it is arranged)"],"correct":0,"why":"The web site delivers the information: medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (5)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A bar chart comparing fish counts in 2017 and 2025.</b></p>","options":["Format (how it is arranged)","Medium (what carries it)"],"correct":0,"why":"A chart is an arrangement of data: format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (6)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A morning radio broadcast where a survey scientist explains the results.</b></p>","options":["Medium (what carries it)","Format (how it is arranged)"],"correct":0,"why":"The broadcast signal carries the spoken information: medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (7)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>A flash drive of results given to a Hummingbird Ridge High School teacher.</b></p>","options":["Format (how it is arranged)","Medium (what carries it)"],"correct":1,"why":"The flash drive stores and carries the files: medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Format or medium? (8)","q":"<p>Arrangement or carrier?</p><p><b>An audio recording of an interview with a local fisher.</b></p>","options":["Medium (what carries it)","Format (how it is arranged)"],"correct":1,"why":"Audio is the form the information takes: format.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the characteristic to its description","q":"<p>Match each characteristic of an information source (SO 1.5) to the statement that describes it.</p>","left":["Availability","Cost","Currency","Depth","Breadth of coverage","Reliability","Format","Medium"],"right":["How much detail the source gives on its topic","How recently the information was produced or updated","How far it can be trusted to give dependable information consistently","How wide a range of topics, places or periods it covers","What the user must pay in money, time or effort to obtain and use it","The carrier that stores or delivers it, such as paper, DVD or a web site","How the information is arranged, such as text, table or video","Whether, when and where a user can obtain the source"],"correct":[7,4,1,0,3,2,6,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (1)","q":"<p>Each comment comes from a Coralstone College student describing a source. Choose the characteristic the comment is MAINLY about.</p><p>\"The only copy is in the reference section and cannot be taken home.\"</p>","options":["Depth","Cost","Availability"],"correct":2,"why":"Where and how you can get it: availability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (2)","q":"<p>\"The data was last updated in 2009.\"</p>","options":["Breadth of coverage","Reliability","Currency"],"correct":2,"why":"How recent the information is: currency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (3)","q":"<p>\"It covers every island from the Bahamas to Trinidad, but only one paragraph each.\"</p>","options":["Breadth of coverage","Format","Depth"],"correct":0,"why":"Wide coverage across many islands: breadth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (4)","q":"<p>\"It is a podcast episode streamed through a mobile app.\"</p>","options":["Format and medium","Cost","Reliability","Currency"],"correct":0,"why":"Audio (format) delivered through an app (medium).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (5)","q":"<p>\"The statistics office has published this survey every year using the same method, and it matches other sources.\"</p>","options":["Breadth of coverage","Reliability","Availability"],"correct":1,"why":"A consistent, trustworthy track record: reliability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is being described? (6)","q":"<p>\"Downloading the full dataset would use most of my monthly mobile data.\"</p>","options":["Depth","Cost","Currency"],"correct":1,"why":"Mobile data is an indirect cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (1)","q":"<p>Portsmouth Fishers' Co-operative has three sources: (1) an interview with an experienced fisher, (2) a government fisheries report (PDF, 2019) and (3) the fuel dock price board and web page. For each need, use the sources' characteristics to choose the BEST source.</p><p>The treasurer needs today's price of boat fuel for the budget meeting tonight.</p>","options":["Interview with the fisher","Fisheries report (2019)","Fuel dock price board"],"correct":2,"why":"The price board is updated daily: the highest currency for this need, even though it is shallow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (2)","q":"<p>A member wants catch figures for many species across the whole fishery over several years.</p>","options":["Interview with the fisher","Fuel dock price board","Fisheries report (2019)"],"correct":2,"why":"The report is broad and deep, with data tables across the fishery.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (3)","q":"<p>A journalist from a Roseau radio programme wants a first-hand account of how fishing at the reef has changed since the 1990s.</p>","options":["Fuel dock price board","Fisheries report (2019)","Interview with the fisher"],"correct":2,"why":"The fisher gives deep, first-hand experience over decades, though narrow and personal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (4)","q":"<p>The chairperson asks which source is most reliable for official catch statistics.</p>","options":["Fuel dock price board","Fisheries report (2019)","Interview with the fisher"],"correct":1,"why":"The report uses an official method and names its authors: high reliability for statistics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (5)","q":"<p>A member working night shifts wants detailed data tables she can read online at 2 a.m. without paying anything.</p>","options":["Fisheries report (2019)","Interview with the fisher","Fuel dock price board"],"correct":0,"why":"The PDF is available online at any time and is free: high availability, low cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source suits this need? (6)","q":"<p>The secretary notes that the fisher's memory of prices in the 1980s may be inexact. Which characteristic is she questioning?</p>","options":["Reliability","Format","Breadth of coverage"],"correct":0,"why":"Whether the source gives dependable information: reliability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spotting bias","q":"<p>A student is researching whether a new solar farm would benefit Barbados. Which source is MOST likely to be biased?</p>","options":["A peer-reviewed journal article comparing solar output on several Caribbean islands.","An outdated encyclopaedia entry on solar energy.","A national statistics table of electricity consumption by year.","A brochure produced by SunCaribe Ltd, the company bidding to build the solar farm."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Refereed articles can still contain bias, but independent expert review reduces it. The company brochure has a clear commercial motive.","Being outdated is a currency problem, not bias.","Raw statistical tables present figures without argument; they are far less likely to be slanted than a sales brochure.","Correct. The company gains if the project goes ahead, so it has a commercial interest in presenting only the benefits."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the selection problems","q":"<p>A CAPE student at Breadfruit Valley Secondary is writing about mobile data costs on the island and makes these notes about possible sources.</p><p>Select EVERY note that shows a source FAILING one of the selection criteria (bias, accuracy, cultural context, completeness or currency).</p>","options":["4. A 2012 article on smartphone data plans.","2. A survey of 400 residents with its method and questionnaire published.","1. A price comparison written by one mobile operator, which shows its own plans as the cheapest.","6. A post stating that \"data on the island costs nothing\", with no source.","5. A regional table of data prices that lists St. Vincent and St. Kitts but leaves out Barbados.","3. A blog post that gives the price of a data bundle in a currency the island does not use and quotes a European regulator."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,5],"why":"Correct. Note 1 fails bias, 3 fails cultural context, 4 fails currency, 5 fails completeness and 6 fails accuracy. Only the survey with a published method passes."},{"t":"match","title":"Put the refereeing process in order","q":"<p>A lecturer at Coralstone College submits a research article on Internet use among island youth to a refereed journal. Put the stages in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The author submits the article to the journal.","The editor checks that it fits the journal's scope and basic standards.","The editor sends it to at least two, typically three, independent experts in the field (the referees).","The referees assess the methods, evidence and conclusions and write reports.","The author revises the article in response to the referees' comments.","When all the referees agree the revised article is worthy of publication, the editor accepts it and it is published."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (1)","q":"<p>A Flamboyant Heights College student is gathering sources for a report on hurricane-resistant housing in the Caribbean. Sort each source: was it checked by <b>independent experts before publication</b> (refereed) or not (un-refereed)?</p><p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A peer-reviewed engineering journal article on roof failures during Hurricane Irma.</b></p>","options":["Refereed","Un-refereed"],"correct":0,"why":"Peer-reviewed means independent experts checked it before publication: refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (2)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A blog by a San Fernando contractor describing the houses he has built.</b></p>","options":["Un-refereed","Refereed"],"correct":0,"why":"A blog is published without expert review: un-refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (3)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A newspaper feature on rebuilding, checked by the paper's own editor.</b></p>","options":["Un-refereed","Refereed"],"correct":0,"why":"An editor is quality control, but not independent subject experts: un-refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (4)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A conference paper accepted after review by three independent structural engineers.</b></p>","options":["Un-refereed","Refereed"],"correct":1,"why":"Independent expert review before acceptance: refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (5)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A press release from a building-supply company announcing a new shutter.</b></p>","options":["Refereed","Un-refereed"],"correct":1,"why":"Press releases are written by the organisation itself, with no independent review: un-refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (6)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A wiki page on hurricane straps that any user can edit.</b></p>","options":["Refereed","Un-refereed"],"correct":1,"why":"Anyone can change a wiki without expert review: un-refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or un-refereed? (7)","q":"<p>Independent expert review before publication?</p><p><b>A scholarly book on Caribbean architecture from an academic press that sends manuscripts to outside reviewers.</b></p>","options":["Refereed","Un-refereed"],"correct":0,"why":"Outside expert review before publication: refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or not?","q":"<p>A Flamboyant Heights College student must include at least one refereed source in her report on mobile banking in Grenada. She checks how four articles were published. Which article is refereed?</p>","options":["An article that has been shared and linked by many Caribbean web sites.","An article in a local newspaper, checked by the paper's own editor before it was printed.","An article written by a single author who holds a university degree in finance.","An article evaluated by independent experts in banking and IT before it was published, who asked for revisions."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Popularity is not refereeing. Widely shared material can still be wrong.","Editorial checking is quality control, but it is not review by independent experts in the subject. This is the most common misconception.","The author's qualifications are relevant to reliability, but refereeing requires review by OTHER independent experts.","Correct. Independent expert (peer) review before publication defines a refereed source, so this article meets her requirement."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (1)","q":"<p>A CAPE IT student at Palmetto Point Secondary is selecting web sources for a report on coral reefs around Saba. Read each clue and choose what it tells her.</p><p>The address is www.reefscience.edu/reports/saba. Who is most likely hosting the page?</p>","options":["A commercial organisation","A private individual","An academic organisation"],"correct":2,"why":".edu marks an academic organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (2)","q":"<p>The address is www.reefgearshop.com/sale. Who is most likely hosting the page?</p>","options":["An academic organisation","A private individual","A commercial organisation"],"correct":2,"why":".com and a sale page: a commercial organisation that wants to sell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (3)","q":"<p>The address is www.freepages-host.com/~tanya/reef-thoughts.html. Who is most likely hosting the page?</p>","options":["A private individual","A commercial organisation","An academic organisation"],"correct":0,"why":"A personal name after a tilde on a free host: a private individual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (4)","q":"<p>A page on a marine society's site says members' pages are reviewed under the society's publishing policy and offers a \"Contact the Webmaster\" link. What does this suggest?</p>","options":["It must be out of date","It has probably received some organisational approval","It is a private blog"],"correct":1,"why":"An approval policy and a Webmaster contact suggest organisational approval.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (5)","q":"<p>A page on reef diving permits shows \"© 2018\" and no last updated date, but she needs this year's rules.</p>","options":["Doubt its currency and look for a more recent source","Accept it, because it has a copyright sign","Reject it, because it names an author"],"correct":0,"why":"A 2018 copyright with no later update warns that the rules may have changed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the web page clues (6)","q":"<p>A page on coral bleaching links to refereed studies and is linked from a university library guide.</p>","options":["Reliable links in both directions support selecting it","Links prove the page is refereed","Links make no difference to selection"],"correct":0,"why":"Links to reliable pages show knowledge of the literature; links from reliable pages show that authorities trust it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which web page should be selected?","q":"<p>Andre, a Sea-Almond Secondary School student, must select one web source on safe hurricane shutters for a class report. Which page BEST meets the criteria for selecting information from the Internet?</p>","options":["A page with a million views and animated graphics, but no author or date.","A page on a regional engineering institute's .edu site that names its author, shows \"Last updated: March 2026\", offers a Webmaster contact and is linked from a university library guide.","www.shutterdeals.com/sale: no author, no date, and links only to its own shop.","www.freepages-host.com/~andyfixit/shutters.html: a personal page with no contact details."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Popularity and design are not selection criteria. Without an author or date, it cannot be checked.","Correct. An academic host, a named author, a recent update date, a Webmaster contact and links from a reliable page all support selecting it.","A commercial host with no author or date, linking only to itself: bias and currency cannot be checked.","The tilde and free host point to a private individual, with no sign of organisational approval."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (1)","q":"<p>Each statement is about a source on Hurricane Irma recovery. Decide whether it <b>describes</b> the source (a characteristic, SO 1.5) or <b>applies a test</b> to decide whether to select it (a criterion, SO 1.6).</p><p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>The report is a 90-page PDF on a government web site.</b></p>","options":["Criterion (test for selection)","Characteristic (describes the source)"],"correct":1,"why":"It states format and medium: a description.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (2)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>The report costs nothing to download.</b></p>","options":["Characteristic (describes the source)","Criterion (test for selection)"],"correct":0,"why":"It states the cost: a description.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (3)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>The report was written by a construction firm hoping to win rebuilding contracts, so its recommendations may favour that firm.</b></p>","options":["Characteristic (describes the source)","Criterion (test for selection)"],"correct":1,"why":"It tests the source for bias to decide whether to rely on it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (4)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>The report covers every district of Dutch Sint Maarten.</b></p>","options":["Characteristic (describes the source)","Criterion (test for selection)"],"correct":0,"why":"It describes the breadth of coverage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (5)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>The report leaves out the French side, so it is not enough for a whole-island study.</b></p>","options":["Criterion (test for selection)","Characteristic (describes the source)"],"correct":0,"why":"It applies the completeness test against the user's purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (6)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>Its damage figures do not match the official statistics, so they need checking before use.</b></p>","options":["Criterion (test for selection)","Characteristic (describes the source)"],"correct":0,"why":"It applies the accuracy test.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (7)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>It was published in 2018.</b></p>","options":["Characteristic (describes the source)","Criterion (test for selection)"],"correct":0,"why":"It simply states currency: a description.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristic or criterion? (8)","q":"<p>Describing the source, or testing it for selection?</p><p><b>It was not reviewed by independent experts, so it should be balanced with a refereed study.</b></p>","options":["Criterion (test for selection)","Characteristic (describes the source)"],"correct":0,"why":"It applies the refereed or un-refereed criterion.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the criterion to the question it asks","q":"<p>Each selection criterion (SO 1.6) is a test. Match the criterion to the question you would ask when applying it.</p>","left":["Bias","Accuracy","Cultural context","Completeness","Currency","Refereed or un-refereed","Characteristics of Internet information"],"right":["Does the author have an interest that slants the information to one side?","Is the information correct, and can I verify it elsewhere?","Was it checked by independent experts before publication?","Does it contain everything I need, or are important parts missing?","Could anyone have posted, altered or recycled this online content?","Is it recent enough for this particular purpose?","Was it produced for a setting, law and culture like mine?"],"correct":[0,1,6,3,5,2,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selecting sources for a power outage report (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a student intern helping the Mandeville Residents' Association in Jamaica prepare a short report on recent electricity outages in the area, to present at a public meeting. Choose your sources wisely.</p><p>Your first search finds a viral social media post saying \"The power company has had 40 outages this month and is hiding it\". It has thousands of shares. What do you do?</p>","options":["Treat it as an unverified claim and look for an original, accountable source for the outage figures.","Use the figure of 40 outages as the headline of the report because so many people shared it."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Popularity is not accuracy; online content can be posted by anyone without checking.","Sharing is not evidence. The claim fails the accuracy test until it is verified."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selecting sources for a power outage report (step 2)","q":"<p>You find two more sources: the power company's outage notice page with dated entries, and a 2014 overseas magazine article on power grids in the US Midwest. Which do you select?</p>","options":["The magazine article, because it explains grids in detail.","The power company's dated notices, cross-checked with residents' own records of outage dates."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["It is deep, but it fails currency and cultural context: an old article about a different country's grid and climate.","Strong. It is current, specific to the area and can be checked for accuracy against first-hand records."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Selecting sources for a power outage report (step 3)","q":"<p>A board member hands you a leaflet from the PowerSafe Generators Ltd claiming outages will double next year. How do you treat it?</p>","options":["Note the company's commercial interest, treat the prediction as biased, and seek an independent or refereed source on future supply.","Include the prediction as a fact; it comes from a company that knows about power."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You identified bias and balanced it with independent evidence.","The company sells generators, so it gains if people fear outages. That is a bias problem."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet information as a criterion","q":"<p>A web page about Caribbean scholarship deadlines has no author name, no date, several broken links and adverts for loan companies. Which characteristic of information on the Internet does this page MOST clearly warn the student about?</p>","options":["Internet information is refereed because it is published publicly.","Anyone can publish online without review, so authorship, currency and purpose must be checked.","Internet information is always free of cost.","Internet information is always more current than printed information."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Public publication is not peer review. Most web pages are un-refereed.","Correct. No author, no date, broken links and commercial adverts all follow from the lack of gatekeeping online.","Cost is a characteristic, but it is not the warning here, and online information is not always free.","The page has no date and broken links, which suggests it is NOT maintained. Online does not mean current."]}]},{"id":"assess_1z9","title":"Module 1 · Unit 6 quiz: Information processing","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 6: Information processing. 102 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Run the cycle","q":"<p>The Portsmouth Water Sports shop processes its weekly rental bookings. Put the activities in the order they happen in one turn of the information processing cycle.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Staff write each customer booking on a booking slip and key it into the booking system.","The system groups the bookings by equipment type and totals the income for each.","A weekly report and a bar chart are printed for the owner.","The report and the booking records are saved in the shop's files for next month's comparison.","The owner sees paddleboards are always fully booked and asks staff to start recording waiting-list requests too."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (1)","q":"<p>Organisations around the Caribbean process very different kinds of data. Sort each situation by the kind of data being processed.</p><p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>The Bayside Resort in English Harbour prints payslips for 600 staff every fortnight using the same pay calculation.</b></p>","options":["Business data","Scientific data"],"correct":0,"why":"The same processing repeated every pay run, with a large volume of output: business data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (2)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>The electricity company in Grenada produces a bill for every customer account on the island.</b></p>","options":["Scientific data","Business data"],"correct":1,"why":"Thousands of bills from the same calculation: business data needing large volumes of output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (3)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>The Island Savings Bank in Roseau prints a monthly statement for each of its 20,000 account holders.</b></p>","options":["Scientific data","Business data"],"correct":1,"why":"Repeated every month, huge output: business data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (4)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>The Frederick Street Jewellers produces the same daily sales report for each of its three branches.</b></p>","options":["Scientific data","Business data"],"correct":1,"why":"A routine report run again and again: business data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (5)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>Forecasters run computer models on wind and pressure readings to predict where a tropical storm will be in 48 hours.</b></p>","options":["Business data","Scientific data"],"correct":1,"why":"Millions of calculations, and the output is needed fast: scientific data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (6)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>A seismograph network in the Eastern Caribbean records ground movement every second and calculates where an earthquake started.</b></p>","options":["Scientific data","Business data"],"correct":0,"why":"Numerous computations with results needed at once: scientific data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (7)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>Researchers at the Saba Reef Project calculate water-temperature trends from thousands of sensor readings to warn of coral bleaching.</b></p>","options":["Business data","Scientific data"],"correct":1,"why":"Heavy computation on measurements, with a fast warning needed: scientific data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Business or scientific data? (8)","q":"<p>Business data or scientific data?</p><p><b>Students in a Coralstone College physics practical log a temperature every second and calculate the cooling rate as the experiment runs.</b></p>","options":["Business data","Scientific data"],"correct":1,"why":"Many calculations on readings, with results while the experiment is running: scientific data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (1)","q":"<p>The Gouyave Bakery still runs its orders and accounts by hand. Sort each statement about its manual system.</p><p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>A new assistant learns the order book in one morning because the method is so simple.</b></p>","options":["Strength of manual processing","Problem with manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Manual methods are simple and require little training.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>The owner designs her own order sheets and decides how to handle each unusual cake order.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Greater creativity: people shape how the data is handled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>When the bakery opened, a computer system was unaffordable, but a notebook and pen cost almost nothing.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Manual methods suit places where technology is unavailable or unaffordable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>Staff feel involved in every order and are more inclined to work.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"People are often more inclined to work when they handle each step themselves.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>Getting the week's orders ready to be totalled takes the whole of Monday morning.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Slow to get data ready for processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>The order book was soaked when the roof leaked during a storm.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Paper holding the data needs protection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>Each regular customer's name and address is rewritten on every new order sheet.</b></p>","options":["Strength of manual processing","Problem with manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Reusing data is repetitive in a manual system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>To find every order for coconut tarts last month, the owner must read every page.</b></p>","options":["Problem with manual processing","Strength of manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Querying information is difficult by hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual processing: strength or problem? (9)","q":"<p>Is this a strength or a problem of manual processing?</p><p><b>Totals added by hand at the end of a long day often contain mistakes.</b></p>","options":["Strength of manual processing","Problem with manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Manual processing is slow and errors may occur in the output.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Process the sports day results by hand","q":"<p>The network is down on sports day at Frangipani Hill Academy, so the results are processed by hand. Put the scorers' work in the order of the five steps of manual information processing.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Judges at each event write the first, second and third places on paper result cards.","Runners bring the result cards from every event to the scorers' table, where they are grouped by house.","Scorers total each house's points with a calculator and rank the houses from first to last.","The totals are written on a large chart in each house's colour.","The results are announced over the PA system and the chart is pinned on the notice board."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Automated steps: the report card system","q":"<p>Sea-Almond Secondary School now produces report cards with a school management system. Put the steps in the order of automated information processing.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Teachers key each student's exam marks into the system from their laptops.","The system calculates each student's average and grade and ranks every class.","The marks and grades are saved in the student database.","In June, the office recalls each student's saved record to build the report card.","Report cards are printed and published on the parent portal."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the benefit or disadvantage","q":"<p>Each event happened in an organisation in Grenada. Match each benefit or disadvantage of automated processing to the event that shows it.</p>","left":["Efficiency and flexibility","Easy queries and updates","Data reusability","Fast and reliable","Costly to set up","Stops when technology fails","Lost income and revenue","Can endanger human life","Dependence increases laziness"],"right":["A supermarket till totals a full trolley in seconds without a single arithmetic slip","Card machines at a St. George's store go offline on a busy cruise-ship day and shoppers leave without buying","A power cut halts every ticket sale at the Carriacou Ferry Company's computerised desk","A failed smoke detector in a guesthouse stays silent during a kitchen fire","Gouyave Bakery must buy computers, software and training before the new system saves anything","When the till goes down, a cashier who always relies on it cannot add up a simple bill","A student's name, keyed once at enrolment, is used again on every timetable, report card and certificate","Bayside Car Rental changes all its rates for Carnival week with one setting, and every new booking uses them","A clerk at the Island Savings Bank lists every customer in Grenville in seconds and corrects one address in a moment"],"correct":[7,8,6,0,4,2,1,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which benefit of automation?","q":"<p>At Bartica Clinic, a patient's name, address and date of birth are typed once at registration. The same stored details then appear automatically on every prescription, appointment letter and invoice for years. Which benefit of automated processing does this BEST show?</p>","options":["Easy queries and updates","Efficiency and flexibility","Data reusability","Fast and reliable processing"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Queries and updates mean searching for records or changing them. Here the point is that details typed once are used again and again.","Flexibility is about changing how processing is done. The clinic is simply reusing stored details.","Correct. Stored data is used over and over again, so the clinic never rewrites the patient's details, unlike a manual system where reuse is repetitive.","The system is fast, but the scenario stresses the same stored data being used many times."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (1)","q":"<p>Caribbean organisations process data in different ways. For each one, decide whether the system described is fully manual, fully automated, or a mix of both.</p><p>At Gouyave Bakery, the owner writes each order in a notebook, adds the day's totals with a pocket calculator and pins the order list to the wall.</p>","options":["Mixed","Manual","Automated"],"correct":1,"why":"Manual. A handheld calculator is a tool used by a person; every stage is carried out by hand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (2)","q":"<p>At the Bayside Car Rental desk at Grantley Adams International Airport, customers book online, the system calculates the charge, emails a confirmation and saves every booking to a cloud database.</p>","options":["Manual","Mixed","Automated"],"correct":2,"why":"Automated. Input, processing, output and storage are all done by software, with staff supervising.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (3)","q":"<p>A Mathematics teacher at Coralstone College marks tests by hand, then types the scores into a spreadsheet that calculates the class average.</p>","options":["Automated","Mixed","Manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Mixed. Marking and entry are manual; the calculating is automated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (4)","q":"<p>The Frigate Bay Hardware store scans barcodes at the till, but once a month the manager counts the stock in the yard by hand and writes the totals in a ledger.</p>","options":["Manual","Automated","Mixed"],"correct":2,"why":"Mixed. Sales capture is automated; the stock count and ledger are manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (5)","q":"<p>A rain gauge in Jamaica sends a reading every minute to a computer, which plots a rainfall graph and publishes it on a website without anyone touching it.</p>","options":["Mixed","Manual","Automated"],"correct":2,"why":"Automated. A sensor captures the data and software processes and disseminates it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual, automated or mixed system? (6)","q":"<p>At Seaside Pharmacy, a clerk takes the week's raw till entries such as \"Panadol 24s, 1, US$6.50\", groups them by product with pen and paper, and writes out a best-sellers list for the manager.</p>","options":["Mixed","Automated","Manual"],"correct":2,"why":"Manual. The clerk turns raw data into information by hand: grouping, totalling and writing the report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (1)","q":"<p>Manual and automated processing each have strengths. Read each Caribbean situation and decide which approach has the advantage.</p><p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>After a storm knocks out power and the network, a marina in Rodney Bay must still record which boats leave the lagoon.</b></p>","options":["Manual processing","Automated processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Manual processing works without electricity or a network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (2)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>The Island Phone Company must produce 40,000 accurate monthly bills by tomorrow morning.</b></p>","options":["Manual processing","Automated processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Automated processing is very fast with large volumes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (3)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>A small roadside fruit stand in Lelydorp, Suriname, makes about ten sales a day and wants the lowest possible start-up cost.</b></p>","options":["Automated processing","Manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Manual has a low start-up cost: a notebook and a cash box.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (4)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>A savings bank in St. George’s must calculate interest on 15,000 accounts without a single arithmetic slip.</b></p>","options":["Automated processing","Manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Software calculates consistently, provided the input and program are correct.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (5)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>A church secretary receives a donation form with an unusual handwritten note asking to split the gift between two funds.</b></p>","options":["Automated processing","Manual processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Human judgement handles odd cases that a program was never set up for.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (6)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>The Fort Street Boutique wants staff in all three branches to check the same stock record at the same time.</b></p>","options":["Automated processing","Manual processing"],"correct":0,"why":"Automated systems let many users in many places share one record at once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which approach has the edge? (7)","q":"<p>Which approach suits this situation better?</p><p><b>Once its system is set up, the Belmopan Clinic wants the lowest possible cost each time an appointment is recorded.</b></p>","options":["Manual processing","Automated processing"],"correct":1,"why":"Automated processing has a low running cost per transaction once set up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated?","q":"<p>At Frangipani Hill Academy, a form teacher marks the class register on paper each morning. The office then types the absences into a spreadsheet, which counts each student's absences for the term. Which statement BEST describes this system?</p>","options":["It is a fully automated system because a spreadsheet is used.","It is a mixed system: input is manual and the calculating is automated.","It is not information processing because no new data is created.","It is a fully manual system because the register starts on paper."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Using software at one stage does not make the whole system automated. The register is still marked by hand.","Correct. The paper register is manual data collection; the spreadsheet automates the calculation. Many real systems combine both.","Processing does not need new data. Counting absences turns raw marks into information the school can use.","Starting on paper makes the input manual, but the spreadsheet carries out the counting automatically."]},{"t":"match","title":"Collection, capture or entry?","q":"<p>Match each input term to the Jamaican example that BEST illustrates it.</p>","left":["Data collection","Data capture at source","Data entry by keying","Source document","Optical mark recognition (OMR)","Magnetic ink character recognition (MICR)","Sensor"],"right":["A machine reads the shaded bubbles on 400 multiple-choice answer sheets","A census officer interviews a household in Half Way Tree and ticks the answers on a questionnaire","The signed paper time sheet a worker at a Port Antonio marina hands in each Friday","A scanner at a King Street store in Kingston reads each product's barcode as the item is sold","A bank reader picks up the account number printed along the bottom of a cheque","A clerk at Sandbox Tree College types the details from 200 paper application forms into a database","A gauge records wind speed every minute during a tropical storm"],"correct":[1,3,5,2,0,4,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (1)","q":"<p>Clerks at the Gros Islet Ferry Company key passenger data from paper booking forms. Sort each mistake into the type of input error it shows.</p><p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>A passenger wrote her fare as US$35 in untidy handwriting; the clerk read it as US$55 and keyed that.</b></p>","options":["Omission","Transposition error","Duplication","Transcription error"],"correct":3,"why":"Copying the wrong value from the source is a transcription error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (2)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>A clerk copying from a faint carbon copy read the surname \"Hodge\" as \"Hedge\".</b></p>","options":["Omission","Transcription error","Transposition error","Duplication"],"correct":1,"why":"A wrongly copied character is a transcription error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (3)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>A passenger's year of birth, 1954, was typed as 1945.</b></p>","options":["Duplication","Omission","Transposition error","Transcription error"],"correct":2,"why":"Two digits swapped places: a transposition error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (4)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>Ticket number 4217 was keyed as 4271.</b></p>","options":["Duplication","Transcription error","Transposition error","Omission"],"correct":2,"why":"The 1 and the 7 were swapped: transposition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (5)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>The clerk left the phone number field blank for a passenger travelling to Martinique.</b></p>","options":["Transposition error","Duplication","Transcription error","Omission"],"correct":3,"why":"A missing field is an omission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (6)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>A whole booking form slipped behind the desk and was never entered.</b></p>","options":["Transposition error","Omission","Duplication","Transcription error"],"correct":1,"why":"A missing record is also an omission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (7)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>The same family's booking was keyed in twice, so the boat showed four extra passengers.</b></p>","options":["Duplication","Transposition error","Transcription error","Omission"],"correct":0,"why":"Entering the same record twice is duplication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the input error (8)","q":"<p>Which type of input error is this?</p><p><b>A passenger on a slow connection tapped Send twice and the online booking form was submitted two times.</b></p>","options":["Duplication","Transcription error","Omission","Transposition error"],"correct":0,"why":"Two copies of one record: duplication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each input method used by organisations in Belize.</p><p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A nurse at the San Pedro Clinic writes a patient's temperature on a paper chart.</b></p>","options":["Manual input","Automated input"],"correct":0,"why":"A person reads and writes the value: manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (2)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A digital thermometer sends the temperature straight to the patient's electronic record.</b></p>","options":["Manual input","Automated input"],"correct":1,"why":"A device captures the value directly: automated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (3)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A supermarket cashier in Belmopan scans each item's barcode.</b></p>","options":["Automated input","Manual input"],"correct":0,"why":"The scanner reads the code; the cashier only holds the item.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (4)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A market vendor ticks a tally sheet each time she sells a coconut.</b></p>","options":["Automated input","Manual input"],"correct":1,"why":"Tally marks by hand are manual data capture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (5)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>An OMR machine reads the shaded bubbles on 400 multiple-choice answer sheets.</b></p>","options":["Manual input","Automated input"],"correct":1,"why":"Optical mark recognition is automated capture.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (6)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A librarian copies a borrower's name and the book title into a loans book.</b></p>","options":["Automated input","Manual input"],"correct":1,"why":"Handwritten records are manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (7)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A weather station sensor records wind speed every minute during a storm.</b></p>","options":["Manual input","Automated input"],"correct":1,"why":"Sensors capture data automatically without a person.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated input? (8)","q":"<p>Is this input manual or automated?</p><p><b>A student fills in a paper application form for Flamboyant Heights College.</b></p>","options":["Manual input","Automated input"],"correct":0,"why":"A paper source document completed by hand is manual collection.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the processing operation","q":"<p>Match each processing operation to the example that BEST illustrates it.</p>","left":["Collating","Sorting","Calculating","Analysing","Presenting"],"right":["Combining the vote tallies from every polling station into one master sheet","Arranging exam candidates in ascending order of candidate number","Working out each employee's gross pay as hours worked × hourly rate","Comparing three years of tourist arrivals to identify the busiest months","Showing the results as a colour bar chart in a report"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which processing operation?","q":"<p>The Port of Spain Taxi Association receives daily trip sheets from 30 drivers. A clerk places all the sheets for the week into a single folder, arranged by driver number, so that they can be checked together. Which processing operation is the clerk MAINLY carrying out?</p>","options":["Analysing","Collating","Calculating","Dissemination"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Analysing would mean examining the sheets to find patterns, such as the busiest routes. That has not happened yet.","Correct. The main purpose is gathering sheets from many sources into one set so they can be used together.","Nothing is being added or computed yet.","Dissemination is an output activity: distributing information to users."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (1)","q":"<p>Both belong to the output stage, but they are different. Decide whether each activity is about the <b>form</b> of the information (presenting) or about <b>getting it to people</b> (dissemination).</p><p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>The Aruba Youth Football League turns its results table into a colour bar chart.</b></p>","options":["Presenting","Dissemination"],"correct":0,"why":"Choosing a chart is about form: presenting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (2)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>The league table is pinned up in the clubhouse and shared in the parents' WhatsApp group.</b></p>","options":["Dissemination","Presenting"],"correct":0,"why":"Pinning up and sharing gets it to people: dissemination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (3)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>Departure screens at Queen Beatrix International Airport show flight times in large text, with delayed flights highlighted in red.</b></p>","options":["Presenting","Dissemination"],"correct":0,"why":"Text size and colour are about form: presenting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (4)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>An SMS storm alert is sent to every registered phone on the island.</b></p>","options":["Dissemination","Presenting"],"correct":0,"why":"Sending to many receivers is dissemination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (5)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>A teacher at Sea-Almond Secondary School redraws a table of marks as a line graph so parents can see progress over the year.</b></p>","options":["Dissemination","Presenting"],"correct":1,"why":"Changing table to graph is presenting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (6)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>The Harbour View Supermarket emails its weekly specials to 3,000 customers.</b></p>","options":["Presenting","Dissemination"],"correct":1,"why":"Emailing to an audience is automated dissemination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (7)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>A radio announcer reads the hurricane bulletin aloud on the morning show.</b></p>","options":["Dissemination","Presenting"],"correct":0,"why":"Broadcasting the bulletin to listeners is dissemination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Presenting or dissemination? (8)","q":"<p>Form of the information, or distribution?</p><p><b>A report writer uses a table instead of paragraphs so the manager can compare branches at a glance.</b></p>","options":["Presenting","Dissemination"],"correct":0,"why":"Choosing a table is presenting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (1)","q":"<p>Each activity comes from the Charlestown Community Centre's youth sports league. Choose the stage of the information processing cycle it belongs to.</p><p>Coaches fill in a match sheet with each team's goals.</p>","options":["Output","Processing","Input"],"correct":2,"why":"Recording data at source is input (data collection and capture).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (2)","q":"<p>The organiser works out each team's points: 3 for a win, 1 for a draw.</p>","options":["Processing","Storage","Input"],"correct":0,"why":"Calculating is a processing operation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (3)","q":"<p>The league table is ranked from most points to fewest.</p>","options":["Output","Processing","Feedback"],"correct":1,"why":"Ranking is sorting: processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (4)","q":"<p>The league table is posted on the centre's notice board and WhatsApp group.</p>","options":["Storage","Processing","Output"],"correct":2,"why":"Posting and sharing is dissemination: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (5)","q":"<p>Match sheets are kept in a box file for the end-of-season awards.</p>","options":["Storage","Output","Feedback"],"correct":0,"why":"Keeping records for later use is storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage of the cycle? (6)","q":"<p>Coaches complain that goal-scorers' names were missing, so a new column is added to the match sheet.</p>","options":["Storage","Feedback","Output"],"correct":1,"why":"An evaluation that changes what is collected at input is feedback.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (1)","q":"<p>The Pond Island Medical Centre handles patient records. Sort each activity.</p><p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>A clerk files a new patient card in the drawer for surnames beginning with R.</b></p>","options":["Storing","Retrieving","Transmitting"],"correct":0,"why":"Placing a record in its home is storing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (2)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>The system saves each consultation note to the server the moment the doctor clicks Save.</b></p>","options":["Transmitting","Storing","Retrieving"],"correct":1,"why":"Saving to a server is automated storing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (3)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>A nurse searches the database for a patient by date of birth.</b></p>","options":["Storing","Retrieving","Transmitting"],"correct":1,"why":"Finding and bringing back a record is retrieving.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (4)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>A receptionist pulls a patient's folder from the cabinet before an appointment.</b></p>","options":["Retrieving","Storing","Transmitting"],"correct":0,"why":"Taking a stored record out for use is manual retrieval.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (5)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>An X-ray image is emailed securely to a specialist in Curaçao.</b></p>","options":["Retrieving","Transmitting","Storing"],"correct":1,"why":"Sending to another location is transmitting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (6)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>A courier carries a sealed folder of test results to the hospital.</b></p>","options":["Transmitting","Storing","Retrieving"],"correct":0,"why":"Physically moving the record is manual transmission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storing, retrieving or transmitting? (7)","q":"<p>Which activity is this?</p><p><b>A full copy of the database is made every night to a cloud backup.</b></p>","options":["Retrieving","Transmitting","Storing"],"correct":2,"why":"A backup is a stored copy kept for recovery, so this is storing (it also involves sending the copy over a network).","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the feedback","q":"<p>Barbadian Book Fair processes visitor registrations each year. Here is what happens.</p><p>Select EVERY line that describes FEEDBACK.</p>","options":["5. All registrations are saved to the cloud.","3. A chart of visitors per district is emailed to the organising committee.","1. Visitors register on an online form with their name, age group and district.","6. A volunteer spots that one district was misspelt in the data and corrects the stored records before the report is re-run.","4. The committee notices almost no visitors from Holetown and decides to add a \"How did you hear about us?\" question to next year's form.","7. The poster for next year is printed and put up in Bridgetown.","2. The software counts visitors per district and per age group."],"correct":[3,4],"why":"Correct. Line 4 is evaluation leading to a change in what is collected at input; line 6 is an update that corrects the data before it is processed again. Both loop back to input."},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (1)","q":"<p>All of these information systems can be found in Dominica. Sort each one.</p><p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>The Portsmouth Hardware keeps staff attendance in a sign-in notebook at the door.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":1,"why":"Attendance recorded in a notebook is a manual information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>The clerk at the Roseau Freight Company calculates the wages in a ledger book every Friday.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":1,"why":"Payroll in a physical ledger is manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>A church secretary types letters to members on a typewriter.</b></p>","options":["Manual information system","Automated information system"],"correct":0,"why":"Letters by hand or typewriter, not a word processor: manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>At month end, the owner of the Roseau Bakery matches the receipt book against supplier invoices by hand.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":1,"why":"Handwritten reconciliation of sales and purchases is manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>A snackette in Portsmouth rings up sales on a basic cash register and writes the bills by hand.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":1,"why":"Basic cash register and handwritten bills: manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>A customer withdraws cash from an ATM in Roseau and her balance updates at once.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":0,"why":"ATM transactions are an automated information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>A travel agent in Grand Anse books seats on a flight from MBIA in an airline reservation system.</b></p>","options":["Manual information system","Automated information system"],"correct":1,"why":"Airline reservation systems are automated and shared worldwide.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>The Island Bottling Plant's system tracks bottles and caps and raises re-orders when stock runs low.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":0,"why":"Manufacturing inventory control: automated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (9)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>A supermarket's tills scan barcodes and update the stock file with every sale.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":0,"why":"A point-of-sale system is automated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Manual or automated information system? (10)","q":"<p>Is this a manual or an automated information system?</p><p><b>The Bayside Resort pays 600 staff from a payroll database management system.</b></p>","options":["Automated information system","Manual information system"],"correct":0,"why":"A payroll DBMS is automated.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Manual system, automated equivalent","q":"<p>Match each manual information system to the automated system that does the same job.</p>","left":["Card catalogue in drawers","Paper class register","Handwritten receipt book","Bank passbook updated by a teller","Paper seating chart for a flight","Patient folders in a cabinet"],"right":["Electronic register that counts absences and alerts parents","Online library catalogue searchable by author, title or subject","Electronic health record","Online reservation system shared by agents worldwide","Point-of-sale system with barcode scanner","Mobile banking app showing transactions instantly"],"correct":[1,0,4,5,3,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (1)","q":"<p>Each situation comes from the Caribbean. Choose the advantage of automated information systems it shows, or spot the user frustration.</p><p>A grandmother in Willemstad video-calls her grandson studying in Utrecht every Sunday at no extra cost.</p>","options":["New types of jobs","Cost effectiveness and productivity","Communication"],"correct":2,"why":"Communication: video calls are quicker, cheaper and more efficient.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (2)","q":"<p>The Belizean Crafts Co-op website takes orders at 3 a.m. while its Albert Street shop is closed.</p>","options":["New types of jobs","Communication","Availability"],"correct":2,"why":"Availability: the business is open 24×7, anytime and anywhere.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (3)","q":"<p>A Westpunt guesthouse lets guests from Colombia, France and the Netherlands book in their own language.</p>","options":["Globalisation and cultural gap","Availability","Cost effectiveness and productivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Globalisation and cultural gap: linguistic and geographical boundaries are reduced.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (4)","q":"<p>A Coralstone College graduate is hired as a web designer to build online booking pages for local hotels.</p>","options":["Availability","Communication","New types of jobs"],"correct":2,"why":"New types of jobs: web designers, programmers and systems analysts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (5)","q":"<p>The managers of the Harbour View Supermarket use a daily sales dashboard and order only what sells, cutting waste.</p>","options":["New types of jobs","Globalisation and cultural gap","Cost effectiveness and productivity"],"correct":2,"why":"Cost effectiveness and productivity: better information for decision-makers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which advantage of automated information systems? (6)","q":"<p>Staff at the San Fernando Ferry Company say the new booking system freezes at peak times since a second branch joined, and nobody trained them to use it.</p>","options":["Availability","Communication","A user frustration"],"correct":2,"why":"User frustration: lack of training and poor performance as the system spreads.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Open all hours","q":"<p>The Kittitian Crafts Co-op on Fort Street closes at 6 p.m., but since it automated its sales with an online shop, orders arrive at all hours, including 3 a.m. on Sundays, and are confirmed without staff. Which advantage of automated information systems does this BEST illustrate?</p>","options":["Globalisation and cultural gap","Communication","New types of jobs","Availability"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Some buyers may be overseas, but the stem stresses orders at any hour, while the shop is closed.","Communication is about messaging and calls becoming quicker and cheaper. Here the point is taking orders around the clock.","No new job is described; the system works without staff.","Correct. The business is effectively open 24×7, anytime and anywhere."]},{"t":"match","title":"File a new record by hand","q":"<p>A new student joins the class. Put the steps for adding her record to the manual alphabetical filing system in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Collect her details on a blank record card.","Check the card is complete and the spelling of the surname is correct.","Identify the filing key: her surname.","Open the drawer to the divider for the first letter of the surname.","Insert the card in its correct alphabetical position among the existing cards.","Update the box index or count of records."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"ma","title":"Which requests are slow by hand?","q":"<p>The office of Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Basseterre keeps 900 student folders in metal cabinets, filed alphabetically by surname. The principal makes these requests.</p><p>Select EVERY request that would be SLOW to satisfy using this manual file.</p>","options":["5. Count how many students chose IT as their favourite subject.","1. Find the folder for a student named Richardson.","3. Return the Arrindell folder to its place after use.","6. Find the folder for a student named Brooks.","2. List every student who lives in Sandy Point.","4. Re-arrange all 900 folders by date of birth, oldest first."],"correct":[0,4,5],"why":"Correct. Lines 2, 4 and 5 use fields that are not the filing key (district, date of birth, favourite subject), so every folder must be handled. A database could filter, re-sort or count these in seconds."},{"t":"mc","title":"The relief distribution register (step 1)","q":"<p>After a major hurricane, the Roseau Neighbours Relief Group in Dominica is handing out food and water packs from a church hall in Goodwill. You are in charge of the information system. Make the best decisions.</p><p>Day 1. There is no electricity and the mobile network is down. Hundreds of families are queuing. How do you record who has received a pack?</p>","options":["Use a numbered paper register: name, address, household size, signature, and tick when the pack is received.","Wait until a laptop and power are available so everything is recorded properly."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. With no power or network, a well-designed manual system is the only reliable option.","Waiting means no records at all, and families may receive two packs or none."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The relief distribution register (step 2)","q":"<p>Day 4. A generator is running and a donated laptop arrives. 1,200 families are on paper sheets. Donors want a daily count of packs by district, and volunteers keep finding people who were served twice. What now?</p>","options":["Keep counting the paper sheets by hand every evening.","Enter the paper sheets into a spreadsheet, one row per family, and use sort and count functions to report by district and spot duplicates."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Manual counting of 1,200 records daily is slow and error-prone, and duplicates across sheets are hard to see.","Strong choice. Automated sorting and calculating answer both problems in seconds."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The relief distribution register (step 3)","q":"<p>Day 5. The spreadsheet is working. A volunteer suggests throwing the paper sheets away now that the data is on the laptop. What do you decide?</p>","options":["Keep the signed paper sheets filed, and back up the spreadsheet daily to a USB drive kept somewhere else.","Throw them away; one laptop is enough."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. The paper is the source document and a back-up; the USB copy protects the digital data.","If the laptop is lost, stolen or damaged, every record is gone."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Retrieval in a manual system","q":"<p>A manual filing system at the Spanish Town Clinic stores patient folders in alphabetical order of surname. Which request would take LONGEST to satisfy using this system?</p>","options":["Return a folder to its correct place after use.","Find the folder for a patient named Arrindell.","List all patients who were treated for dengue fever last month.","Find the folder for a patient named Richardson."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Re-filing by surname follows the filing key and is quick.","Again, surname is the key; the clerk goes straight to the A divider.","Correct. Diagnosis and date are not the filing key, so every folder must be opened and checked. An automated system could query these fields in seconds.","Surname is the filing key, so this record can be found quickly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Defining feedback","q":"<p>At the Harbour View Supermarket in Kingston, the manager reads the weekly stock report, sees that the bottled-water figure is often wrong, and has staff re-count the shelves and correct the stock records before the next report is run. Which stage of the cycle does this BEST illustrate?</p>","options":["Output, because the manager is reading a printed report.","A compulsory checking stage that every system must carry out before processing begins.","Feedback: the output is evaluated and corrections are looped back to the input stage.","Storage, because the corrected stock records are saved again."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Reading the report is where it starts, but the manager goes further: she evaluates it and sends corrections back. Output is only the report itself.","Feedback is optional; many systems never have it. The manager chose to act after evaluating the output.","Correct. The manager evaluates the report and the corrected counts loop back to input: optional, evaluation or updates, back to input.","The records are saved, but the point of the action is to evaluate the output and correct the data going back in."]}]},{"id":"assess_25t","title":"Module 1 · Unit 7 quiz: Representing data and information","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 7: Representing data and information. 46 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (1)","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in Portsmouth is setting up a new customer system. Sort each item of data into the type it should be represented as.</p><p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>The customer's surname, for example Hodge.</b></p>","options":["String","Aural","Numeric","Visual","Character"],"correct":0,"why":"A name is a sequence of characters with no arithmetic: a string.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (2)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>A Y or N showing whether the customer has health insurance.</b></p>","options":["Visual","Aural","Numeric","String","Character"],"correct":4,"why":"A single symbol is character data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (3)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>The price of a prescription: 24.95</b></p>","options":["Visual","Numeric","Aural","String","Character"],"correct":1,"why":"A price is a quantity that will be totalled, so it is numeric.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (4)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>The customer's phone number: +1 767 555 0188</b></p>","options":["String","Numeric","Character","Aural","Visual"],"correct":0,"why":"It contains digits, but you never calculate with it and it contains + and spaces, so it is a string.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (5)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>A voice message from a doctor confirming a dose.</b></p>","options":["String","Character","Aural","Numeric","Visual"],"correct":2,"why":"Data in the form of sound is aural.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (6)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>A photo of a handwritten prescription.</b></p>","options":["Character","Numeric","String","Visual","Aural"],"correct":3,"why":"An image is visual data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (7)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>The number of tablets in stock: 340</b></p>","options":["Numeric","Character","String","Visual","Aural"],"correct":0,"why":"A count that will go up and down with sales is numeric.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which data type? (8)","q":"<p>Which data type is this?</p><p><b>A single size code: S, M or L.</b></p>","options":["String","Visual","Character","Aural","Numeric"],"correct":2,"why":"One letter is character data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the representation to the job","q":"<p>Canefield Tourism Desk has several pieces of information to publish. Match each representation to the job it does best.</p>","left":["Table","Line graph","Pie chart","Bar chart","Graphic (icon or map)","Text"],"right":["Ferry departure times so travellers can look up an exact time","How sea temperature changed week by week over a year","Showing visitors where the beaches and toilets are, without words","Explaining why a beach is closed and when it will reopen","What share of visitors came from the USA, Europe and the Caribbean","Comparing the number of cruise calls in five different months"],"correct":[0,1,4,5,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital?","q":"<p>A technician at the Mahaut Weather Station replaces an old mercury thermometer with an electronic sensor that sends readings to a computer as a stream of 1s and 0s. Which statement BEST describes the change?</p>","options":["A digital representation has been replaced by an analogue one, because the computer shows more detail.","Aural data has been replaced by visual data.","Nothing has changed: both represent temperature, so both are analogue.","An analogue representation, which can show any value in a range, has been replaced by a digital representation made of discrete values."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This is the wrong way round. The mercury thermometer is the analogue device; streams of 1s and 0s are digital.","Neither device uses sound. The change is from a continuous to a discrete representation, not between senses.","Representing the same quantity does not make them the same kind of signal. How the value is represented is what matters.","Correct. The mercury column rises smoothly through every possible value (analogue); the sensor sends discrete binary values (digital)."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (1)","q":"<p>You are advising several Dominica organisations on how to represent the information they need to share. For each situation, choose the best answer.</p><p>A gate agent at Douglas–Charles Airport must tell passengers spread across a busy lounge, many looking at their phones, that boarding has started. The best representation is...</p>","options":["a table of flight numbers on a notice board","a pie chart of passengers per gate","sound: a spoken announcement over the speakers"],"correct":2,"why":"Sound reaches everyone in the area at once, whatever they are looking at.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (2)","q":"<p>The Mahaut Shutters Ltd wants to show customers, step by step, how to fit hurricane shutters correctly. The best representation is...</p>","options":["a bar chart of shutter sales","a radio bulletin","a short how-to video"],"correct":2,"why":"Video is strongest for demonstrating a process and showing movement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (3)","q":"<p>A visually impaired student at Flamboyant Heights College needs to follow the course web page. Which representation helps most?</p>","options":["A larger table of course dates","A screen-reader voice reading the page aloud","A silent video of the lecturer"],"correct":1,"why":"Sound is the right choice when the receiver cannot see or read the screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (4)","q":"<p>The Portsmouth Youth Club e-mails a 20-minute video of its fundraiser to members who have slow mobile data. What is the main drawback?</p>","options":["Video cannot include sound","The file is very large and needs good bandwidth","Video can only show still images"],"correct":1,"why":"Large file size and the need for bandwidth are the key limitations of video.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (5)","q":"<p>At the Roseau cruise pier, a spoken change of tender time is missed by a passenger standing near a noisy generator. Which weakness of sound does this show?</p>","options":["It can only be understood by experts","It needs very large files","It is temporary and can be drowned out by noise"],"correct":2,"why":"Once spoken it is gone, and background noise can drown it out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, video or something else? (6)","q":"<p>Police use CCTV footage from a King George V Street jewellery store to show how a theft took place. The information is represented as...</p>","options":["a special-purpose notation","video","a table"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving images recorded over time are video, which shows movement and sequence of events.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Students in a Bayleaf Hill Secondary cross-subject project are labelling the notations they use. Answer each question.</p><p>The symbol ♭ on a music sheet tells the player to...</p>","options":["play loudly","raise the note by a semitone","lower the note by a semitone"],"correct":2,"why":"The flat lowers a note by a semitone; the sharp ♯ raises it; f means loud.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>NaCl is an example of...</p>","options":["scientific notation","musical notation","mathematical notation"],"correct":0,"why":"A chemical formula is scientific notation: sodium and chlorine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>In 4/4 time, which note lasts two beats?</p>","options":["minim","crotchet","quaver"],"correct":0,"why":"A minim (half note) lasts 2 beats. A crotchet is 1 beat; a quaver is half a beat.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>A = πr² represents information using...</p>","options":["Morse Code","mathematical notation","a table"],"correct":1,"why":"Symbols such as π, = and the squared sign are mathematical notation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Writing 3.0 × 10⁸ instead of 300 000 000 is mainly done to...</p>","options":["turn it into binary","make the number harder to read","represent a very large number concisely"],"correct":2,"why":"Standard form is a scientific notation for very large or very small numbers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Notations quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>The five horizontal lines that music is written on are called the...</p>","options":["stave","time signature","clef"],"correct":0,"why":"The stave (staff). The clef is a symbol placed on it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (1)","q":"<p>Members of the Canefield Signal Club are training to send and receive Morse Code by lamp and radio. Use the Morse table on the previous screen to answer each question.</p><p>A yacht anchored in Canefield flashes: <b>.... . .-.. .--.</b> What word is it sending?</p>","options":["HELP","HALP","HEAP"],"correct":0,"why":".... = H, . = E, .-.. = L, .--. = P: HELP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (2)","q":"<p>The club wants to flash its initials, GB. Which is the correct Morse?</p>","options":[".-- -...","--. -...","--. ...-"],"correct":1,"why":"G = --. and B = -... ; .-- is W and ...- is V.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (3)","q":"<p>A trainee sends a dot lasting 1 unit. How long should a dash last?</p>","options":["7 units","3 units","1 unit"],"correct":1,"why":"A dash lasts 3 units: three times as long as a dot.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (4)","q":"<p>Between the words of a message such as STORM COMING, the sender should leave a gap of...</p>","options":["3 units","7 units","1 unit"],"correct":1,"why":"1 unit inside a letter, 3 between letters, 7 between words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (5)","q":"<p>A new member asks why E is just a single dot. The best explanation is that...</p>","options":["E is the first vowel in the alphabet","E was the first letter Samuel Morse coded","E is very common, so a short code makes messages faster"],"correct":2,"why":"Common letters were given the shortest codes so messages could be sent faster.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Morse at Canefield (6)","q":"<p>During a power cut a fishing boat sends SOS by flashing a torch, then by tapping on a metal rail. This shows that Morse Code...</p>","options":["only works as electrical pulses on a telegraph wire","is a form of binary place value","can be sent over many media: light, sound, taps or pulses"],"correct":2,"why":"One code, many media: light, sound, electrical pulses or taps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the Morse message","q":"<p>A radio operator at a sailing club in Bequia receives this Morse message during a storm: <b>.-- .. -. -..</b> Using the Morse table, what word was sent?</p>","options":["WINE","AIND","WIND","WAND"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["The last group -.. is D. E is a single dot.","The first group .-- has three elements, so it is W, not A (.-).","Correct. .-- = W, .. = I, -. = N, -.. = D.","The second letter is .. (I), not .- (A). Check each group separately."]},{"t":"match","title":"Read the byte","q":"<p>The computer at the Mahaut Marina stores its daily counts in single bytes. Match each stored pattern or term to what it represents. Write the place values 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 on scrap paper first.</p>","left":["00011001","01100100","10000001","11111111","One byte","One bit"],"right":["Visitors on the dock: 129","Berths occupied: 100","8 bits, giving 256 possible patterns","The largest count one byte can hold: 255","Boats waiting to refuel: 25","A single 0 or 1, the smallest unit of data"],"correct":[4,1,0,3,2,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Convert 26 to binary by division","q":"<p>A Form 6 student is converting the denary number 26 to binary using repeated division by 2. Put the steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["26 ÷ 2 = 13 remainder 0","13 ÷ 2 = 6 remainder 1","6 ÷ 2 = 3 remainder 0","3 ÷ 2 = 1 remainder 1","1 ÷ 2 = 0 remainder 1","Read the remainders from the bottom up: 11010","Pad to 8 bits and check: 00011010 = 16 + 8 + 2 = 26"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Binary conversion","q":"<p>Roseau Parking Authority stores the number of free spaces in a car park in one byte. The byte currently holds <b>00111100</b>. How many spaces are free?</p>","options":["60","30","120","4"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 = 60.","Check the place values: the 1s are under 32, 16, 8 and 4, not 16, 8, 4 and 2.","This would be 01111000. You may have shifted the place values one position to the left.","There are four 1s, but binary is not a count of 1s; you add the place values where each 1 sits."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the true ASCII facts","q":"<p>A telecoms company customer service trainee wrote these notes after a workshop on how text messages are stored. Some notes are wrong.</p><p>Select EVERY statement that is correct.</p>","options":["7. ASCII stores each letter as a pattern of dots and dashes.","2. Lower-case letters have codes 32 lower than their upper-case versions.","3. If the code for E is 69, the code for G is 71.","1. In ASCII, the upper-case letter A has the code 65.","4. Standard ASCII is a 7-bit code with 128 possible codes.","5. The character '5' and the number 5 are stored as the same binary pattern.","6. The space character has its own ASCII code, 32."],"correct":[2,3,4,6],"why":"Correct. A = 65, letters are consecutive, standard ASCII has 128 codes and the space is 32. Lower case is 32 HIGHER, the character '5' is code 53 not 5, and dots and dashes belong to Morse Code."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the code or notation","q":"<p>Each example below represents information using one of the codes or notations in SO 1.8. Match them.</p>","left":["Morse Code","ASCII","Binary","Musical symbols","Scientific notation","Mathematical notation"],"right":["The letter A stored as code 65","A sharp sign ♯ placed before a note on a stave","... --- ... flashed by lamp from a yacht","CO₂ on a report about air quality","x ≥ 18 on an age-check rule","01001101, the base-2 form of 77"],"correct":[2,0,5,1,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each example by the type of signal used to represent the information.</p><p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A singer's voice travelling through the air at a beach concert in Canefield.</b></p>","options":["Analogue","Digital"],"correct":0,"why":"Natural sound is a continuous pressure wave: analogue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (2)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>An MP3 file of the same concert on a phone.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":0,"why":"An MP3 is stored as binary values: digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (3)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A mercury thermometer in a clinic.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":1,"why":"The mercury can stop at any height: continuous, so analogue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (4)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>Light pulses in a fibre-optic broadband cable.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":0,"why":"Light on and off represents 1s and 0s: digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (5)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A vinyl record of an old calypso.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":1,"why":"The groove is a continuous copy of the sound wave: analogue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (6)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A text message sent from a phone.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":0,"why":"Characters are coded (for example in ASCII) and sent as binary: digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (7)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A clock with sweeping hands.</b></p>","options":["Analogue","Digital"],"correct":0,"why":"The hands move continuously through every position: analogue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Analogue or digital signal? (8)","q":"<p>Analogue or digital?</p><p><b>A smartwatch showing 10:47 in numbers.</b></p>","options":["Digital","Analogue"],"correct":0,"why":"Time is shown in discrete steps from stored binary values: digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storm bulletin for Portsmouth (step 1)","q":"<p>You are helping the Portsmouth Community Council prepare information for residents as a tropical storm approaches. Choose the best representation at each step.</p><p>The council has hourly wind-speed readings for the last two days and wants residents to see whether the wind is building. How should the data be represented?</p>","options":["A line graph of wind speed against time, with labelled axes and units.","A pie chart of the wind-speed readings."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. A line graph shows a trend over time at a glance.","A pie chart shows parts of a whole. Wind speeds over time are not parts of a whole."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storm bulletin for Portsmouth (step 2)","q":"<p>Next, residents need the address, opening time and capacity of each of five shelters so they can look up the nearest one. What do you use?</p>","options":["One long paragraph describing all five shelters.","A table with one row per shelter and columns for address, opening time and capacity."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Text is slow to scan for exact details. Residents under pressure need a quick look-up.","Strong choice. A table lets people look up exact values quickly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Storm bulletin for Portsmouth (step 3)","q":"<p>Finally, the council must warn fishermen already at sea and older residents who do not use smartphones. Power may also fail. What do you add?</p>","options":["A colourful infographic posted only on social media.","A sound bulletin: a spoken warning on VHF and local radio, repeated on the hour, plus sirens in the district."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["People at sea and without smartphones would never see it.","Excellent. Sound reaches people who cannot see a screen and works on battery radios."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Working out ASCII codes","q":"<p>A Bayleaf Hill Secondary student is writing a program that converts customers' names to lower case for the King George V Street Print Shop. The notes say that in ASCII the code for the upper-case letter <b>E</b> is 69. What code will the program store for the lower-case letter <b>g</b>?</p>","options":["39","71","103","102"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["You subtracted 32 instead of adding it. Lower-case codes are higher than upper-case codes.","71 is upper-case G (69 + 2). The program needs lower case, which is 32 higher than upper case.","Correct. G = 69 + 2 = 71, and the program stores g as 71 + 32 = 103.","This is 69 + 32 + 1, which gives f. G is two letters after E, not one."]}]},{"id":"assess_2f8","title":"Module 1 · Unit 8 quiz: Tools used in Information Technology","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 8: Tools used in Information Technology. 71 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (1)","q":"<p>You are on duty at the help desk of Montego Bay Connect, a small ISP. Customers call with questions about their connection equipment. Choose the best answer for each call.</p><p>A customer in Ocho Rios asks what happens to the digital data from her computer before it travels down the telephone line as an analogue signal.</p>","options":["The browser encrypts it","The modem demodulates it","The router indexes it","The modem modulates it"],"correct":3,"why":"Digital to a transmittable signal is modulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (2)","q":"<p>A signal arrives on the line at a home in Falmouth and must become digital data the laptop can use. Which process is this?</p>","options":["Demodulation","Ranking","Modulation","Uploading"],"correct":0,"why":"Turning the received signal back into digital data is demodulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (3)","q":"<p>A family in Lucea wants broadband over their existing telephone line and still wants to make ordinary phone calls while online.</p>","options":["Dial-up modem","Network switch","DSL modem","Cable modem"],"correct":2,"why":"A DSL modem gives broadband on a telephone line while voice and data share it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (4)","q":"<p>The Ironshore Beach Bar has no fixed line, but there is good 4G signal on the beach. It needs Internet for its card machine.</p>","options":["Mobile (cellular) modem","Cable modem","Dial-up modem","DSL modem"],"correct":0,"why":"A mobile modem or hotspot connects over 4G/5G where no fixed line exists.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (5)","q":"<p>An office on Broad Street already has a modem, but wants its laptops, printer and phones to share the connection, with data sent to the right device.</p>","options":["A router","An SMS service","A search engine","A second modem"],"correct":0,"why":"A router directs data between devices on the local network and the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Modem check: function, type or router? (6)","q":"<p>During a power cut, a home office in Ocho Rios loses its Internet connection even though the line outside is undamaged. Why?</p>","options":["Modems only work during the day","The ISP deletes the account in a power cut","Dial-up lines are always disconnected at night","The modem needs electrical power to work"],"correct":3,"why":"A disadvantage of the modem: no power, no connection, unless there is a backup supply.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which device?","q":"<p>Ms. Arrindell works from home in Blowing Point, Anguilla. Her computer produces digital data, but the line from the street carries a signal that must be converted before it can be sent and converted back when data arrives. Which device performs this conversion?</p>","options":["A modem","A web browser","A router","A network switch"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Modulation (digital to transmittable signal) and demodulation (back to digital) is exactly what a modem does.","A browser is software for viewing web pages. The question describes a hardware conversion.","A router forwards data between networks and devices. It does not modulate or demodulate the signal on the line, although many home boxes combine a router with a modem.","A switch connects devices within a local network. It does not convert signals for the line to the ISP."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the task to the software tool","q":"<p>Staff at the Seaview Hotel in Holetown need the right software tool for each job. Match each task to the software tool that suits it BEST.</p>","left":["Recalculate the weekly bar budget automatically whenever a drink price changes","Search 4,000 guest records by surname and arrival date","Send the same personalised letter to 200 travel agents using mail merge","Show photos and a video tour of the rooms at a travel fair in Curaçao","Pay the hotel’s electricity bill through its bank’s website","Send a formal quotation with a PDF attachment to a wedding party in Toronto"],"right":["Web browser","Spreadsheet","Database software","Word processor","Presentation software","Email client"],"correct":[1,2,3,4,0,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (1)","q":"<p>The English Harbour Tours uses many communication tools. Sort each use into the correct timing category.</p><p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>A VoIP call to a cruise-line agent in Miami to confirm tomorrow’s tour numbers</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Real-time (synchronous)"],"correct":1,"why":"A call is a live, two-way conversation: real-time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (2)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>An emailed invoice to a travel agency in the Netherlands</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Real-time (synchronous)"],"correct":0,"why":"Email is stored in the mailbox until read: asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (3)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>A weekly web-conference meeting with tour guides on Microsoft Teams</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Real-time (synchronous)"],"correct":1,"why":"Everyone joins the meeting at the same time: real-time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (4)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>An SMS reminder sent to customers the evening before their tour</b></p>","options":["Real-time (synchronous)","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"SMS is store-and-forward; the customer reads it when convenient.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (5)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>A question posted on a travel discussion forum about wheelchair access</b></p>","options":["Real-time (synchronous)","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Forum posts wait for replies, sometimes for days: asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (6)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>A monthly newsletter sent through a mailing list to past customers</b></p>","options":["Real-time (synchronous)","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Mailing-list messages are delivered to inboxes and read later.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (7)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>The IT technician logging in remotely with a telnet-style command session to restart a server</b></p>","options":["Asynchronous","Real-time (synchronous)"],"correct":1,"why":"Commands are typed and answered immediately during the session: real-time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Real-time or asynchronous? (8)","q":"<p>Does this use need both people at the same time?</p><p><b>A post on the company’s Instagram page showing a new snorkelling trip</b></p>","options":["Real-time (synchronous)","Asynchronous"],"correct":1,"why":"Followers see and comment on posts later: asynchronous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (1)","q":"<p>People across Belize and the region use online services every day instead of visiting an office. Sort each activity into the type of online service being used.</p><p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A nurse in San Pedro checks her account balance and pays her credit card at midnight after a late shift</b></p>","options":["E-government","Cloud storage / web-based software","E-commerce","Online banking"],"correct":3,"why":"Managing accounts and payments with the bank online: online banking.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A father in Roseau transfers money to his daughter studying in Jamaica without visiting a branch</b></p>","options":["Cloud storage / web-based software","E-government","Online banking","E-commerce"],"correct":2,"why":"Transferring money through the bank’s website or app: online banking.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>The Broad Street Watch House sells watches to customers in the USA through its web shop</b></p>","options":["Cloud storage / web-based software","Online banking","E-government","E-commerce"],"correct":3,"why":"Buying and selling goods online: e-commerce.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A family orders school shoes from an overseas online store and pays by card</b></p>","options":["Cloud storage / web-based software","E-government","E-commerce","Online banking"],"correct":2,"why":"Online shopping is e-commerce.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A resident downloads, completes and submits a government permit application form online</b></p>","options":["E-commerce","Online banking","Cloud storage / web-based software","E-government"],"correct":3,"why":"Forms, permits and payments to government online: e-government.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A small business pays a government licence fee through an official payment portal</b></p>","options":["E-commerce","E-government","Online banking","Cloud storage / web-based software"],"correct":1,"why":"Paying a government department online is e-government.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>Two Calabash Point Secondary students edit the same report in an online word processor from different homes</b></p>","options":["Cloud storage / web-based software","E-commerce","Online banking","E-government"],"correct":0,"why":"Web-based software that saves to the cloud and allows shared editing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which online service? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of online service is this?</p><p><b>A photographer saves wedding photos to an online storage account so she can open them on any device</b></p>","options":["E-government","Cloud storage / web-based software","E-commerce","Online banking"],"correct":1,"why":"Saving files online to reach them anywhere: cloud storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"How a search engine produces results","q":"<p>A student at Sandbox Tree College searches for \"Caribbean submarine cable outages\". Put the stages in the order the search engine carries them out, starting before the student even types.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Crawlers follow links and visit web pages across the Internet.","The content of each page is stored in the search engine’s index.","The student types keywords into the search box.","The engine matches the keywords against the index and ranks pages by relevance.","An ordered results page with links is displayed."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Justify the tool: alerts after a storm","q":"<p>After a hurricane, the Carriacou Ferry Line must tell passengers that the 7 a.m. crossing from Grand Anse is cancelled. Many passengers use basic phones and mobile data is unreliable across the islands. Which tool and justification is BEST?</p>","options":["A web conference, because passengers can ask questions live","A post on the ferry line’s social media page, because it is free","A mailing list, because it reaches all subscribers","SMS, because it reaches basic phones over the cellular network without a data plan and is delivered when the phone regains signal"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A web conference needs good data and a smartphone or computer, and everyone must join at the same time. It fails both constraints.","Free, but passengers need data and must check the page. Basic phones cannot view it.","Email needs data or Wi-Fi and is read when people check their inbox, which may be too late for a 7 a.m. crossing.","Correct. Store-and-forward delivery and no need for data make SMS the most reliable choice for this audience."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (1)","q":"<p>Groups across the Caribbean use three asynchronous group tools. Sort each situation into the tool being used. Remember: forums and message boards are pulled from a website; mailing lists are pushed to inboxes.</p><p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>Teachers across the region post under topics such as \"Paper 02 tips\", with replies grouped in threads</b></p>","options":["Message board","Mailing list","Discussion forum / board"],"correct":2,"why":"Topics with threaded replies: a discussion forum.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (2)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>A farmer in Guyana searches and finds a two-year-old thread that answers his question about irrigation pumps</b></p>","options":["Message board","Discussion forum / board","Mailing list"],"correct":1,"why":"A permanent, searchable archive of threads is a forum strength.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (3)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>The IT department posts \"Lab B closed Friday\" on the school learning platform’s notice board, newest first</b></p>","options":["Message board","Discussion forum / board","Mailing list"],"correct":0,"why":"A simple shared list of notices: a message board.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (4)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>Residents of an apartment block in Holetown post short notices about lost keys and water shut-offs on a shared online board</b></p>","options":["Message board","Mailing list","Discussion forum / board"],"correct":0,"why":"Short notices everyone reads in one place, with little threading: a message board.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (5)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>The Brades Community Cricket Club in Montserrat emails the season’s fixtures to 400 members at once</b></p>","options":["Discussion forum / board","Message board","Mailing list"],"correct":2,"why":"One message delivered to every subscriber’s inbox: a mailing list.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (6)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>A monthly alumni newsletter arrives in every subscriber’s inbox without them visiting any website</b></p>","options":["Mailing list","Discussion forum / board","Message board"],"correct":0,"why":"Pushed to inboxes: a mailing list (an announcement list).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum, message board or mailing list? (7)","q":"<p>Which group tool is being used?</p><p><b>On a regional teachers’ discussion list, every reply-to-all lands in all 500 members’ inboxes</b></p>","options":["Message board","Mailing list","Discussion forum / board"],"correct":1,"why":"A discussion mailing list: replies go to every subscriber by email, which can cause overload.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the Internet tool","q":"<p>Match each Internet tool from the syllabus to its description.</p>","left":["Search engine","VoIP","SMS","Discussion forum","Mailing list","Telnet","FTP"],"right":["Short text messages of up to 160 characters over the cellular network","Carries voice calls as data packets over an IP network","Crawls and indexes web pages and returns ranked results for keywords","Text-based remote login to run commands on another computer","Uploads and downloads files between a client and a server","Topics with threaded replies kept on a website and searchable later","One message delivered to the email inbox of every subscriber"],"correct":[2,1,0,5,6,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (1)","q":"<p>Each person below is using FTP or telnet. Decide whether they are uploading with FTP, downloading with FTP, or using telnet for remote command-line login.</p><p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>A designer copies the new pages of the San Pedro Marina website from her laptop to the web host</b></p>","options":["Download (FTP: from the server)","Upload (FTP: to the server)","Telnet (remote command-line login)"],"correct":1,"why":"From her computer to the server: upload.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (2)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>A school in Saba sends a 3 GB folder of coursework videos to the exam board’s server</b></p>","options":["Telnet (remote command-line login)","Download (FTP: from the server)","Upload (FTP: to the server)"],"correct":2,"why":"Sending files to a server is uploading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (3)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>A photographer in Kingston sends 2 GB of wedding photos to a print lab’s server in Miami</b></p>","options":["Telnet (remote command-line login)","Upload (FTP: to the server)","Download (FTP: from the server)"],"correct":1,"why":"Files leave her computer for the server: upload.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (4)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>A technician copies a large software update from the supplier’s server to the office PC</b></p>","options":["Telnet (remote command-line login)","Upload (FTP: to the server)","Download (FTP: from the server)"],"correct":2,"why":"From the server to the local computer: download.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (5)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>An architect retrieves a set of building drawings from a partner firm’s server in Curaçao</b></p>","options":["Download (FTP: from the server)","Telnet (remote command-line login)","Upload (FTP: to the server)"],"correct":0,"why":"Bringing files from the server: download.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (6)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>A network technician in Basseterre types commands to check a router at the branch in Frigate Bay without travelling</b></p>","options":["Upload (FTP: to the server)","Telnet (remote command-line login)","Download (FTP: from the server)"],"correct":1,"why":"Text-based remote control of another device: telnet (SSH today, because telnet sends passwords as plain text).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload, download or telnet? (7)","q":"<p>Which direction or tool?</p><p><b>An IT student opens a text session to a mail server to test whether it is responding</b></p>","options":["Upload (FTP: to the server)","Download (FTP: from the server)","Telnet (remote command-line login)"],"correct":2,"why":"Testing a network service with a text session is a common remaining use of telnet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upload or download?","q":"<p>A web designer in Holetown has finished updating the menu pages for the Harbour View Café on her laptop. She now copies the files to the web server so customers can see the new menu. Which statement BEST describes what she is doing?</p>","options":["Using telnet to transfer the files, because telnet copies files","Downloading the files using FTP, from her computer to the server","Uploading the files using FTP, from her computer to the server","Using a mailing list to publish the files to the website"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Telnet gives a remote command-line login. FTP is the file transfer tool.","Downloading is from the server to your computer. The direction here is the opposite.","Correct. Moving files from the local computer to the remote server is uploading.","A mailing list sends email to subscribers. It does not place files on a web server."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (1)","q":"<p>Organisations in Antigua and Barbuda use social media tools for many jobs, but not every job suits them. Sort each use: is social media a good choice, or should another tool be chosen?</p><p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>A Heritage Quay boutique posts photos of new stock the morning cruise ships arrive</b></p>","options":["Poor use: choose another tool","Good use of social media"],"correct":1,"why":"Wide reach and visual content for marketing: a good use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (2)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>The Harbour View Café answers customers’ questions in comments and shares a live video of its new menu</b></p>","options":["Poor use: choose another tool","Good use of social media"],"correct":1,"why":"Two-way interaction and rich media: a strength of social media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (3)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>A community group shares verified shelter locations and safety updates after a hurricane</b></p>","options":["Poor use: choose another tool","Good use of social media"],"correct":1,"why":"Fast, wide reach for public information, as seen after Hurricane Irma; posts must be verified to avoid rumours.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (4)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>A recent graduate connects with employers across the region on LinkedIn</b></p>","options":["Poor use: choose another tool","Good use of social media"],"correct":1,"why":"Professional networking is a key use of social media tools.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (5)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>The Bayside Clinic posts patients’ blood test results so they can see them quickly</b></p>","options":["Good use of social media","Poor use: choose another tool"],"correct":1,"why":"Medical results are confidential. Use a private, secure channel.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (6)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>A school publishes Form 5 students’ exam marks on its public page</b></p>","options":["Poor use: choose another tool","Good use of social media"],"correct":0,"why":"Marks are personal data; posting them publicly breaches privacy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good or poor use of social media? (7)","q":"<p>Does this use suit social media?</p><p><b>A water sports company posts photos of storm damage publicly for its insurance assessor</b></p>","options":["Good use of social media","Poor use: choose another tool"],"correct":1,"why":"Confidential business evidence should go privately, for example by email or web conference.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (1)","q":"<p>For each scenario choose the Internet tool that is MOST appropriate. Think about timing, audience and the type of data.</p><p>A Montserrat school must send a 3 GB folder of video coursework to the exam board’s server.</p>","options":["SMS","Discussion forum","FTP","VoIP"],"correct":2,"why":"Large files to a server: FTP upload (or a secure equivalent).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (2)","q":"<p>A Statia nurse wants to talk face to face with a specialist in Curaçao and show an X-ray image on screen.</p>","options":["Web-conferencing tool","Mailing list","Message board","Search engine"],"correct":0,"why":"Real-time video plus screen sharing: a web-conferencing tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (3)","q":"<p>A Dominica farmers’ group wants questions and answers about crop disease kept and searchable for future members.</p>","options":["VoIP","Telnet","SMS","Discussion forum"],"correct":3,"why":"Threaded, archived and searchable: a discussion forum.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (4)","q":"<p>A student needs to find recent articles on submarine cable damage in the Caribbean.</p>","options":["Telnet","FTP","Mailing list","Search engine"],"correct":3,"why":"Finding information on the web by keyword: a search engine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (5)","q":"<p>A Trinidad and Tobago charity sends a monthly newsletter to 3,000 donors who prefer email.</p>","options":["VoIP","Telnet","Web conference","Mailing list"],"correct":3,"why":"One message pushed to many inboxes: a mailing list.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the most appropriate tool (6)","q":"<p>A family in St. John’s wants to share photos, voice notes and messages daily with relatives in Rotterdam who use iPhones while they use Android.</p>","options":["Telnet","Search engine","FTP","Cross-platform messaging tool"],"correct":3,"why":"Rich media across different operating systems: a cross-platform messaging tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the valid disadvantages","q":"<p>A Form 6 student wrote a list of disadvantages of the tools a business uses. Some are accurate; some are misconceptions.</p><p>Select EVERY line that states an ACCURATE disadvantage.</p>","options":["6. Search engine results can include biased or out-of-date pages.","3. VoIP call quality falls when bandwidth is low or latency is high.","5. Web conferencing requires participants to be available at the same time.","4. A mailing list requires every member to visit a website to read messages.","2. SMS cannot be received on a basic mobile phone.","7. FTP can only download files and cannot upload them.","1. Standard telnet sends usernames and passwords as plain text."],"correct":[0,1,2,6],"why":"Correct. Lines 2, 4 and 7 are misconceptions: SMS works on basic phones, mailing lists push to inboxes, and FTP both uploads and downloads."},{"t":"match","title":"Build the justification chain","q":"<p>Placencia Kayak Tours in Belize must remind guests the evening before of the 8 a.m. start time. Some guests are visitors whose phones have no local data plan. Put the parts of a full-mark justification for SMS in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Need: every guest must receive tomorrow’s start time tonight, including visitors with no local data plan.","Tool: send each guest an SMS reminder.","Feature: SMS travels over the cellular network and works on any mobile phone without data or an app.","Benefit: all guests, including those without data, get the time, so fewer people miss the 8 a.m. launch.","Drawback weighed: each SMS has a small cost, but one short reminder per booking is affordable compared with lost trips."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"The strongest justification","q":"<p>The Sentry Hill Guest House in Saba wants to send booking confirmations, receipts and directions to guests from many countries. Which statement is the STRONGEST justification for using email rather than a web conference?</p>","options":["Email is asynchronous and keeps a written record, so guests in different time zones can read the confirmation when convenient and keep it as proof of booking.","Email is free and modern.","Email is used by most people.","Web conferencing is a real-time tool and email is not."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. It names features (asynchronous, written record) and links each to a benefit for this scenario (time zones, proof of booking).","Vague and partly inaccurate as a reason; no link to the need for a record across time zones.","True, but it gives no feature and no link to the guest house’s need. It would earn little credit.","This states a difference but does not explain why it matters for confirmations and receipts."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (1)","q":"<p>The IT technician at the Bayside Clinic in San Pedro is listing the tools in the building. Sort each into its family.</p><p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>The DSL modem connecting the clinic to its ISP</b></p>","options":["Hardware tool","Software tool","Communication tool"],"correct":0,"why":"Physical equipment that modulates and demodulates: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (2)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A spreadsheet used to track monthly medicine costs</b></p>","options":["Communication tool","Hardware tool","Software tool"],"correct":2,"why":"A program for calculation: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (3)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A VoIP service used to call specialists overseas</b></p>","options":["Communication tool","Software tool","Hardware tool"],"correct":0,"why":"Its main purpose is exchanging voice between people: communication tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (4)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A webcam fitted to the consulting-room computer</b></p>","options":["Software tool","Hardware tool","Communication tool"],"correct":1,"why":"Physical input device: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (5)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A database of patient appointments</b></p>","options":["Communication tool","Software tool","Hardware tool"],"correct":1,"why":"A program for storing and retrieving records: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (6)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>SMS reminders sent to patients the day before appointments</b></p>","options":["Communication tool","Software tool","Hardware tool"],"correct":0,"why":"Messages to people: communication tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (7)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A word processor used to type referral letters</b></p>","options":["Software tool","Communication tool","Hardware tool"],"correct":0,"why":"A program for creating documents: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hardware, software or communication tool? (8)","q":"<p>Which family does this tool belong to?</p><p><b>A web-conferencing tool used for staff training with a hospital in Curaçao</b></p>","options":["Hardware tool","Software tool","Communication tool"],"correct":2,"why":"Real-time meetings between people: communication tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the need to the tool","q":"<p>Match each need of the Kingston Youth Choir to the tool that BEST meets it.</p>","left":["Rehearse live with a soloist in Barbados","Post the new music video for fans worldwide","Send the term newsletter to 250 parents’ inboxes","Upload recordings to the choir’s web server","Text a rain cancellation to parents with basic phones","Keep an archive of members’ questions about costumes"],"right":["Web-conferencing tool","Social media tool","Discussion forum","Mailing list","FTP","SMS"],"correct":[0,1,3,4,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keeping a business connected after a hurricane (step 1)","q":"<p>You advise the Blue Lagoon Water Sports in Grand Anse. A major hurricane has just passed. Make the best tool decisions.</p><p>Power is out across the area and the fixed Internet line is down. The cellular network has weak signal. The owner needs to tell 60 booked customers that all trips this week are cancelled. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Send an SMS to every customer, because it works over the cellular network without data and is delivered when phones regain signal.","Hold a web conference with all 60 customers to explain the situation."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. SMS suits weak signal and any phone.","A web conference needs strong data and everyone online at once. Neither is possible now."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keeping a business connected after a hurricane (step 2)","q":"<p>A week later the generator is running and the 4G mobile modem gives a modest connection. The owner must agree an insurance claim with an assessor in Curaçao, who needs to see photos of damaged boats and ask questions. What now?</p>","options":["Use a web-conferencing tool with audio and screen sharing of the photos, switching video off to save bandwidth, then email the full-resolution photos afterwards.","Post the photos of the damage publicly on social media and ask the assessor to look there."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Real-time discussion where it matters, with sensible bandwidth management and a written record.","Social media is public. Insurance evidence is confidential business information."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Keeping a business connected after a hurricane (step 3)","q":"<p>The repair team wants a simple way to share daily progress photos, voice notes and locations among six staff who use a mix of iPhones and Android phones. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Telnet sessions to each staff member’s phone.","A cross-platform messaging group with clear rules on work-only use."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Telnet is remote command-line login, not a messaging tool, and it sends passwords in plain text.","Right. It works across operating systems and handles photos, voice notes and locations."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Forum or mailing list?","q":"<p>The Trinidadian Cricket Association is deciding whether to use a mailing list or a discussion forum to keep its 400 members informed. Which statement CORRECTLY distinguishes the two tools for the committee?</p>","options":["A mailing list can only be used by businesses, whereas a forum can only be used by schools.","A mailing list pushes each message to subscribers’ email inboxes, whereas a discussion forum keeps threaded posts on a website that members visit.","A discussion forum delivers posts to every member’s inbox, whereas a mailing list stores messages on a website.","A mailing list is real-time, whereas a discussion forum is asynchronous."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Both tools are used by all kinds of organisations, including sports associations.","Correct. Fixtures on a list would arrive in members’ inboxes; on a forum, members must visit the site. The same feature is compared for both.","This reverses the two tools the committee is comparing.","Both are asynchronous: members would read either one later. The difference is how messages reach the members."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 2","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_2q5","title":"Module 2 · Unit 1 quiz: Information systems and their components","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 1: Information systems and their components. 88 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (1)","q":"<p>Around the Caribbean you can see plenty of information technology, but not all of it is part of a working information system. Sort each example.</p><p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>A box of new barcode scanners delivered to the Bayview Supermarket in Belize City, not yet unpacked.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":1,"why":"Scanners in a box are hardware only. No data, users or procedures are involved yet, so this is IT, not an IS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (2)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>Bayview's checkout: cashiers scan items, the program updates stock and the manager receives a daily sales total.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":0,"why":"Hardware, software, data, users and procedures work together to turn sales data into information for the manager: an information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (3)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>A copy of spreadsheet software installed on a laptop at Calabash Point Secondary.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":1,"why":"One piece of software on one device is a tool. Nobody is yet using it, with data, for a purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (4)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>The Harbourview school office records fee payments in that spreadsheet, following a set routine, and the bursar receives a termly list of unpaid fees.</b></p>","options":["Information technology (a tool on its own)","Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)"],"correct":1,"why":"Now the tool is used by people, following procedures, to turn payment data into information: an information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (5)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>A Wi-Fi router mounted on the wall of a King Street jewellery shop.</b></p>","options":["Information technology (a tool on its own)","Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)"],"correct":0,"why":"A router is network hardware. On its own it is IT; it becomes part of an IS only when it carries data for users and a purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (6)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>A Port of Spain bank's ATM service: customers withdraw cash, accounts update instantly, and branch managers see daily withdrawal totals.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":0,"why":"Hardware, software, data, procedures, users and a network all work together to support the bank: an information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (7)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>A receipt printer sitting in a storeroom of a shop in Gros Islet.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":1,"why":"Unused hardware is information technology, not an information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information technology or information system? (8)","q":"<p>Is this just information technology, or a whole information system?</p><p><b>An utility's billing set-up: meter readers enter readings, software calculates each bill, staff follow billing procedures and customers receive statements.</b></p>","options":["Information system (tools, data, people and procedures working for a purpose)","Information technology (a tool on its own)"],"correct":0,"why":"All six components work together to turn meter readings into bills and information: an information system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Defining an information system","q":"<p>Kayla tells her class at Mahoe Grove High School in Belize that Bayview Supermarket's \"information system\" is simply the new computers it bought. Her teacher says she is wrong. Which statement BEST describes the supermarket's information system?</p>","options":["An organised combination of hardware, software, data, procedures, users and network that converts data into information to support an organisation.","The computers, printers and cables owned by an organisation.","The field of study that includes Computer Science and Software Engineering.","A computer program that stores data in tables."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Bayview's system is its tills, programs, sales data, cashiers' routines, staff and store network working together to turn sales data into information for the store.","This is Kayla's mistake. Bayview's computers and cables are only hardware (and part of the network). Without software, data, procedures and users there is no system producing information.","This confuses Bayview's working system with the discipline of Information Systems that you met in Module 1.","That describes one piece of Bayview's software. An IS also needs hardware, people, procedures and a purpose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (1)","q":"<p>Information systems are at work across Trinidad and Tobago and the region. Decide whether each one mainly supports business operations or managerial decision making.</p><p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>Tills at the Bayview Supermarket in San Fernando record every sale and update stock.</b></p>","options":["Supports managerial decision making","Supports business operations"],"correct":1,"why":"Recording daily transactions keeps the business running: a TPS, on the operations side.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (2)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>At the Island Spring Water plant in Couva, sensors check each bottle's fill level and the computer adjusts the filling machine.</b></p>","options":["Supports managerial decision making","Supports business operations"],"correct":1,"why":"Controlling a physical process automatically: a process control system, on the operations side.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (3)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>Staff of Caribe Marts group on three islands edit one shared price list and meet by video every Monday.</b></p>","options":["Supports business operations","Supports managerial decision making"],"correct":0,"why":"Teams communicating and sharing work: an enterprise collaboration system, on the operations side.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (4)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>A teller at a Kingston bank records a customer's deposit.</b></p>","options":["Supports business operations","Supports managerial decision making"],"correct":0,"why":"A routine transaction: TPS, operations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (5)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>The Bayview store manager receives a weekly sales report by department every Monday.</b></p>","options":["Supports managerial decision making","Supports business operations"],"correct":0,"why":"Routine reports for a middle manager: an MIS, supporting decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (6)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>The revenue manager of the Island Breeze Hotel tests what-if room rates for next season.</b></p>","options":["Supports managerial decision making","Supports business operations"],"correct":0,"why":"Models and what-if analysis: a DSS, supporting decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (7)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>The Caribe Marts CEO checks a dashboard of group profit and drills down to one store.</b></p>","options":["Supports business operations","Supports managerial decision making"],"correct":1,"why":"Summaries and drill-down for an executive: an EIS, supporting decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operations or decisions? (8)","q":"<p>What is this system mainly for?</p><p><b>A list of only the accounts more than 60 days overdue goes to the credit manager of a Chaguanas furniture store.</b></p>","options":["Supports business operations","Supports managerial decision making"],"correct":1,"why":"An exception report for a manager to act on: MIS, supporting decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (1)","q":"<p>The Frigate Bay Hardware store in Basseterre uses both a TPS and an MIS. Sort each activity or output.</p><p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>Scanning a customer's bag of cement and printing the receipt.</b></p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Management Information System"],"correct":0,"why":"Recording a single sale and producing a receipt is a routine transaction: TPS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (2)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>A weekly report of total sales per department for the store manager.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":0,"why":"Totals produced on a schedule for a middle manager are MIS output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (3)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>Recording a delivery of 200 sheets of plywood into stock.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":1,"why":"A delivery is a business event that updates records: a transaction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (4)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>A list of only the items whose stock is below the reorder level.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":0,"why":"This is an exception report, a classic MIS output for middle managers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (5)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>A clerk entering a customer's credit payment against their account.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":1,"why":"Payments are transactions recorded at the operational level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (6)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>A monthly comparison of this year's paint sales with last year's.</b></p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Management Information System"],"correct":1,"why":"Summarising and comparing over time to control the business is MIS work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (7)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>Updating stock levels each time an item is sold.</b></p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Management Information System"],"correct":0,"why":"Updating records after each sale is processing of transactions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (8)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>A report showing overtime hours by department for the month.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":0,"why":"A summarised report for managers to control staffing costs: MIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (9)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>Raising a purchase order to a supplier in Trinidad for 50 bags of cement.</b></p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Management Information System"],"correct":0,"why":"A purchase order is one of the daily transactions a TPS records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TPS or MIS? (10)","q":"<p>Is this handled by the TPS or the MIS?</p><p><b>Running the payroll that calculates each worker's pay and prints payslips.</b></p>","options":["Management Information System","Transaction Processing System"],"correct":1,"why":"Payroll is a routine, high-volume, efficiency-oriented transaction process: TPS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (1)","q":"<p>Staff at offices and firms across Barbados and the region use computers all day. Decide whether each person is using an Office Automation System or a Knowledge Work System.</p><p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>The secretary at Calabash Point Secondary drafts letters to parents about the sports day and mail-merges the addresses.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge Work System (KWS)","Office Automation System (OAS)"],"correct":1,"why":"A data worker producing and sending documents: OAS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (2)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>An architect at a firm in Freeport, Grand Bahama, uses CAD to design a hurricane-resistant roof for a new house.</b></p>","options":["Office Automation System (OAS)","Knowledge Work System (KWS)"],"correct":1,"why":"A knowledge worker creating a new design with specialised software: KWS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (3)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>An administrative assistant at the Island Breeze Hotel books the conference room on a shared calendar and emails the agenda.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge Work System (KWS)","Office Automation System (OAS)"],"correct":1,"why":"Scheduling and communication for an office worker: OAS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (4)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>An engineer simulates wind loads on a proposed jetty for the Coral Tours company before building starts.</b></p>","options":["Office Automation System (OAS)","Knowledge Work System (KWS)"],"correct":1,"why":"Modelling and simulation by a professional engineer: KWS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (5)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>A clerk at the Grand Anse Hardware store scans supplier invoices into a document management system so they can be found later.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge Work System (KWS)","Office Automation System (OAS)"],"correct":1,"why":"Document imaging and management for clerical staff: OAS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (6)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>A scientist at a marine research station on Saba models how rising sea temperatures affect coral reefs.</b></p>","options":["Office Automation System (OAS)","Knowledge Work System (KWS)"],"correct":1,"why":"Scientific analysis and modelling that creates new knowledge: KWS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (7)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>The office manager of a company sets up a video conference between its Grenada and Carriacou offices.</b></p>","options":["Office Automation System (OAS)","Knowledge Work System (KWS)"],"correct":0,"why":"Video conferencing is an office communication tool: OAS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OAS or KWS: data worker or knowledge worker? (8)","q":"<p>Which knowledge-level system is this person using?</p><p><b>A designer at a Georgetown agency uses a high-end graphics workstation to create a 3D animation for a tourism advert.</b></p>","options":["Office Automation System (OAS)","Knowledge Work System (KWS)"],"correct":1,"why":"A professional creating new graphics on a specialised workstation: KWS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (1)","q":"<p>You are shadowing managers at Caribe Marts group, which runs supermarkets in Jamaica, the Cayman Islands and St. Kitts. For each situation, decide which system is being used.</p><p>A logistics analyst changes the expected fuel price in a model to see how delivery costs to the Cayman Islands store would change.</p>","options":["DSS","EIS","MIS"],"correct":0,"why":"Changing an input to see the effect is what-if analysis with a model: DSS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (2)","q":"<p>The CEO opens one screen of colour charts showing group profit for all three islands beside regional visitor-arrival figures.</p>","options":["TPS","DSS","EIS"],"correct":2,"why":"Highly summarised, graphical information with external data for a senior executive: EIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (3)","q":"<p>The CEO sees that Guadeloupe's profit has fallen, taps the figure and moves down to one store, then to its fresh-produce department.</p>","options":["DSS","EIS","MIS"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving from a summary to the detail behind it is drill-down, a key EIS feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (4)","q":"<p>The Basseterre store manager receives the same fixed-format weekly sales summary every Monday morning.</p>","options":["EIS","MIS","DSS"],"correct":1,"why":"Routine, scheduled, structured reports for a middle manager come from an MIS, not a DSS or EIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (5)","q":"<p>The marketing manager tests three possible Sunday opening times to compare forecast sales and staff costs for each.</p>","options":["TPS","DSS","EIS"],"correct":1,"why":"Testing options with forecasts for a semi-structured decision is DSS work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"DSS or EIS? Spot the clues (6)","q":"<p>The board is deciding whether to open in Curaçao within five years and wants trends in the economy, competitors and tourism alongside group results.</p>","options":["MIS","EIS","TPS"],"correct":1,"why":"An unstructured, long-term, strategic decision using external data is supported by an EIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the system to the situation","q":"<p>Match each type of information system to the situation it fits best.</p>","left":["Transaction Processing System","Management Information System","Office Automation System","Knowledge Work System","Decision Support System","Executive Information System"],"right":["A cashier at a Portsmouth supermarket scans goods and prints a receipt","A branch manager receives a weekly sales summary and a list of low-stock items","A hotel revenue manager tests what-if room-rate scenarios","A CEO views a group dashboard and drills down to a failing store","An engineer models a hurricane-resistant roof in CAD","A school secretary drafts letters and books meetings on a shared calendar"],"correct":[0,1,5,4,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Caribe Marts group is building a data warehouse for its supermarkets in Jamaica, the Cayman Islands and St. Kitts. Choose the BEST answer for each step.</p><p>Caribe Marts wants one central place holding five years of sales, loyalty and supplier data integrated from all its systems. What does it need?</p>","options":["One more till","An exception report","A data warehouse"],"correct":2,"why":"A central repository of integrated current and historical data: a data warehouse.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Sales records are copied out of the tills on all three islands. Which ETL step is this?</p>","options":["Transform","Load","Extract"],"correct":2,"why":"Copying data out of the source systems is Extract.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Prices are converted to one currency and duplicate customer records are merged. Which ETL step is this?</p>","options":["Load","Extract","Transform"],"correct":2,"why":"Cleaning and converting into one consistent form is Transform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>The cleaned data is written into the warehouse every night. Which ETL step is this?</p>","options":["Transform","Load","Extract"],"correct":1,"why":"Writing the data into the warehouse is Load, the final step.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>The marketing team gets its own smaller store holding only sales and loyalty data. What is this?</p>","options":["A data mart","An operational data store","A transaction processing system"],"correct":0,"why":"A data mart serves one subject area for one group of users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data warehouse quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>The duty manager in Kingston needs an instant snapshot of today's sales so far, without waiting for tonight's load.</p>","options":["A detailed report printed last month","An operational data store","A data mart of last year's sales"],"correct":1,"why":"An operational data store gives an instant snapshot of current operational data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Inside an expert system","q":"<p>A plant-health service is building an expert system for farmers in Dominica and St. Kitts, and a clinic on Saba uses one for nurses. Match each part or person to what it does.</p>","left":["Knowledge base","Inference engine","User interface","Knowledge engineer","Domain expert","Non-expert user"],"right":["A clinic nurse on Saba who enters a patient's symptoms to get advice on referral","Interviews experienced agronomists and turns their know-how into rules","Stores the IF ... THEN rules about banana and citrus diseases collected from plant scientists","Asks the farmer questions on a phone screen and shows the advice with an explanation","A plant pathologist whose years of experience are captured in the system","Applies the stored rules to the farmer's answers to reach a diagnosis"],"correct":[2,5,3,1,4,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert system or not?","q":"<p>The Leeward Agri-Help service lets farmers in Dominica describe the colour, shape and spread of spots on their banana leaves. The system asks follow-up questions, names the most likely disease and recommends a treatment. Which type of information system is this?</p>","options":["Knowledge Work System","Decision Support System","Transaction Processing System","Expert system"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A KWS helps professionals create designs and models. The farmer is not creating new knowledge; the system is supplying expert knowledge.","A DSS supports a manager exploring options with models and what-if analysis. Here the system gives a specific expert diagnosis to a non-expert.","No business transaction is recorded. The system reasons from symptoms to a diagnosis.","Correct. It uses stored expert knowledge (rules about plant diseases) to give a diagnosis and advice to a non-expert user."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Shania is shadowing staff at Caribe Marts group for a week. Answer each question about what goes into and comes out of their systems.</p><p>The barcode, quantity and price of each item a customer buys is the major input to the...</p>","options":["KWS","EIS","TPS"],"correct":2,"why":"Individual transaction data is the input to the TPS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>The major output of the TPS is best described as...</p>","options":["processed transactions","forecasts","dashboards"],"correct":0,"why":"A TPS outputs processed transactions: receipts, updated stock and account records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>The MIS takes as its main input...</p>","options":["the CEO's opinions","summarised transaction data from the TPS","design specifications"],"correct":1,"why":"The output of the TPS becomes the input of the MIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>The expert system used by the pharmacy counter receives symptoms from the user and also needs...</p>","options":["the payroll file","a knowledge base of rules","a CAD drawing"],"correct":1,"why":"The inference engine applies the knowledge base to the user's facts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A forecast of next quarter's sales under three price scenarios is the output of a...</p>","options":["TPS","OAS","DSS"],"correct":2,"why":"What-if forecasts are DSS output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Aggregated group figures plus external tourism statistics are the input to the...</p>","options":["TPS","OAS","EIS"],"correct":2,"why":"An EIS combines summarised internal data with external data for executives.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Inputs and outputs quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>The system found in every Caribe Marts store, the most widely used of all, built to record each sale quickly and cheaply and to feed data to the other systems, is the...</p>","options":["TPS","DSS","EIS"],"correct":0,"why":"The TPS is the most widely used IS: efficiency oriented, operational, and the source of data for the MIS, DSS and EIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (1)","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet prints several reports. Sort each into the correct type.</p><p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>Every prescription filled on Monday, listed one by one with time and pharmacist.</b></p>","options":["Detailed","Exception","Ad hoc","Summarised"],"correct":0,"why":"Every individual record, line by line: a detailed report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>Total sales by product category for October.</b></p>","options":["Summarised","Detailed","Exception","Ad hoc"],"correct":0,"why":"Totals for a period condense many records: summarised.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>Only the medicines that will expire within 30 days.</b></p>","options":["Summarised","Ad hoc","Detailed","Exception"],"correct":3,"why":"Only items meeting a warning condition: exception.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>The owner asks today: \"How many flu vaccines did we give to over-65s since August?\"</b></p>","options":["Detailed","Summarised","Exception","Ad hoc"],"correct":3,"why":"A one-off question answered on request: ad hoc.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>Average daily takings per weekday for the quarter.</b></p>","options":["Summarised","Exception","Ad hoc","Detailed"],"correct":0,"why":"Averages across many records: summarised.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>Only the suppliers whose deliveries arrived more than 5 days late.</b></p>","options":["Exception","Ad hoc","Summarised","Detailed"],"correct":0,"why":"Only cases outside the normal limit: exception.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>A list of every item sold at the counter during the evening shift.</b></p>","options":["Summarised","Detailed","Exception","Ad hoc"],"correct":1,"why":"Each transaction listed: detailed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of report? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of report is this?</p><p><b>A one-time comparison of sunscreen sales in cruise and non-cruise weeks, requested for a meeting.</b></p>","options":["Summarised","Exception","Detailed","Ad hoc"],"correct":3,"why":"Unscheduled, on request, for a specific question: ad hoc.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Management by exception","q":"<p>The manager of the Harbour Fuel depot in Castries has no time to read long printouts. She wants to see ONLY the storage tanks whose fuel level has dropped below 20 per cent of capacity. Which type of report should the system produce?</p>","options":["Ad hoc report","Summarised report","Exception report","Detailed report"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Ad hoc reports answer one-off questions. This is a standing condition she wants checked routinely.","A summarised report would total or average all tanks. She wants only the tanks below the limit.","Correct. An exception report shows only records outside a set limit (below 20 per cent), so problems stand out.","A detailed report lists every tank and reading, the long printout she wants to avoid."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the knowledge-level and management systems","q":"<p>The Sunrise Medical Centre in West Bay, Grand Cayman, lists the computer tasks done in a typical day.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is NOT a Transaction Processing System task.</p>","options":["7. A clerk books a new appointment for a patient.","4. The pharmacy till updates stock when a prescription is dispensed.","2. A nurse enters symptoms and the system suggests likely conditions and when to refer.","3. The practice manager receives a weekly summary of appointments per doctor.","5. The director views a dashboard comparing the centre with regional health trends.","6. The secretary drafts referral letters and books meetings in a shared calendar.","1. The receptionist records a patient's payment and prints a receipt."],"correct":[2,3,4,5],"why":"Correct. Line 2 is an expert system, line 3 an MIS, line 5 an EIS and line 6 an OAS. Payments, stock updates and bookings (lines 1, 4 and 7) are routine transactions."},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right system (step 1)","q":"<p>You are an IT consultant for the Coral Tours company, which runs boat and island tours from Gros Islet and Castries. Recommend the right system at each step.</p><p>Bookings are written in a notebook and payments are often lost. The owner wants every booking and payment recorded accurately as it happens. What do you recommend first?</p>","options":["A transaction processing system for bookings and payments.","An executive information system with a dashboard for the owner."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Right. Routine, high-volume events need a TPS, and every other system will depend on its data.","An EIS needs reliable data underneath it. Without a TPS there is nothing accurate to summarise."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right system (step 2)","q":"<p>The TPS is running. The operations manager now wants a weekly view of bookings per tour and a list of only the tours that ran below half capacity. What do you add?</p>","options":["A detailed report listing every booking, so the manager can count them himself.","An MIS that produces a summarised weekly report and an exception report of under-filled tours."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A detailed report hides the pattern in thousands of lines. He needs totals and exceptions.","Good. Summarised and exception reports for a middle manager are MIS output."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right system (step 3)","q":"<p>The owner is thinking of adding a weekly tour to Martinique and wants to test prices and expected demand under different fuel costs before deciding. What fits best?</p>","options":["A decision support system with what-if models using booking history and external data such as fuel prices.","An office automation system so the secretary can type a proposal."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. A semi-structured decision with what-if analysis is exactly what a DSS supports.","An OAS produces documents. It cannot model demand or test prices."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (1)","q":"<p>A student describing the information system at the Bayview Supermarket in San Fernando used words from several component lists. Sort each item into the syllabus component (SO 2.2) that an examiner would mark.</p><p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The tills and the back-office server</b></p>","options":["Network","Procedures","Users","Software","Data","Hardware"],"correct":5,"why":"Physical devices: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (2)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The point-of-sale program on every till</b></p>","options":["Users","Procedures","Hardware","Network","Software","Data"],"correct":4,"why":"Instructions for the machine: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (3)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The stock database on the server (the student wrote \"databases\")</b></p>","options":["Software","Hardware","Users","Data","Network","Procedures"],"correct":3,"why":"A database is organised stored data. The syllabus word is data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (4)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>Every product code, price and quantity sold</b></p>","options":["Network","Procedures","Data","Hardware","Users","Software"],"correct":2,"why":"Raw facts captured and stored: data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (5)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The Monday steps for running the low-stock report, specific to the question \"what must we reorder?\"</b></p>","options":["Software","Users","Hardware","Network","Data","Procedures"],"correct":5,"why":"Instructions for people, specific to one question: procedures.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (6)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>Cashiers trained to use the tills (end users)</b></p>","options":["Hardware","Users","Network","Software","Data","Procedures"],"correct":1,"why":"End users are users in the syllabus list.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (7)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The IT manager who sets up staff accounts (an IT professional)</b></p>","options":["Data","Procedures","Users","Software","Hardware","Network"],"correct":2,"why":"IT professionals are also users in the syllabus list (SO 2.8).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Translate to the syllabus six (8)","q":"<p>Which syllabus component is this?</p><p><b>The store network linking the tills to the server</b></p>","options":["Procedures","Hardware","Software","Network","Users","Data"],"correct":3,"why":"Communication links that let data be shared: network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which list does the examiner want?","q":"<p>A Paper 02 question asks Jaden, a Calabash Point Secondary student, to identify the six components of the information system at the Island Breeze Hotel. He writes: hardware, software, databases, network, procedures, IT professionals. Which change gives the syllabus answer?</p>","options":["Replace \"databases\" with \"data\" and \"IT professionals\" with \"users\"","Add \"reports\", because the hotel's system produces them","Nothing: any recognised list of components earns full marks","Replace \"network\" with \"the Internet\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The SO 2.2 list is hardware, software, data, procedures, users and network. Jaden's databases are a way of storing data, and IT professionals are one kind of user.","Reports are an output of the system, not a component. Adding them would lose a mark.","Examiners mark against the syllabus terms, so use the six syllabus words.","The syllabus word is network; the Internet is only one example of one."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (1)","q":"<p>Things are going wrong at businesses around the Caribbean. For each case, choose the relationship between components that explains what happened.</p><p>New tills arrive at the Bay Street Spirits shop in Nassau, but the point-of-sale program has not been installed, so no sales can be recorded. This shows that...</p>","options":["users need a network to follow procedures","hardware needs software to instruct it","data is the same as information"],"correct":1,"why":"Without software the hardware has no instructions, so it cannot process a single sale.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (2)","q":"<p>At the Seaside Pharmacy the program works perfectly, but a new assistant does not know the refund steps and records a refund as a sale. This shows that...</p>","options":["software instructs the machine, but procedures instruct the people","the hardware is faulty","the network is too slow"],"correct":0,"why":"Good software is not enough: users need procedures to operate it correctly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (3)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma, a Road Town shop's computers and software were undamaged, but card payments could not be approved for weeks. The broken link was...</p>","options":["software","procedures","the network"],"correct":2,"why":"The network connects the shop's hardware to the bank. Without it, the whole system stopped: it is only as strong as its weakest component.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (4)","q":"<p>A cashier at the Bayview Supermarket types 100 instead of 10 for a delivery, and the stock report is now wrong. Which relationship explains this?</p>","options":["data entered by users is processed by software into information, so wrong data gives wrong information","procedures replace software","hardware depends on the network"],"correct":0,"why":"Data is the bridge between people and machine: garbage in, garbage out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (5)","q":"<p>Front-desk staff at the Island Breeze Hotel in Holetown cannot see bookings made at the group's Oistins hotel. The component that should connect them is the...</p>","options":["hardware","network","procedures"],"correct":1,"why":"The network links hardware in different places so users can share data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which link in the chain? (6)","q":"<p>The Calabash Point Secondary office follows a written backup routine every Friday. When a hard drive fails, the student records are restored. This shows that...</p>","options":["software replaces procedures","the network stores all the data","procedures guide users to protect the data stored on hardware"],"correct":2,"why":"Users following a procedure protected the data held on hardware: the components support each other.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Follow one sale through the components","q":"<p>A visitor from a cruise ship pays for a bottle of mango liqueur at the Fort Street Spirits shop. Put the steps in order to show the components working together.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The cashier (user) follows the sales procedure and scans the bottle's barcode with the scanner (hardware).","The barcode number is captured as data.","The point-of-sale program (software) looks up the price and adds it to the bill.","The card payment is sent over the network to the bank for approval.","The software records the processed transaction and reduces the stock level stored on the server.","The printer (hardware) outputs the receipt, which the cashier hands to the customer."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the component","q":"<p>At the Seaside Pharmacy in English Harbour, match each item to the component of the information system it belongs to.</p>","left":["Hardware","Software","Data","Procedures","Users","Network"],"right":["Patient names, drug codes, doses and stock quantities","The pharmacist, the counter assistant and the owner","The pharmacy management program and the Windows operating system","The till, the label printer and the dispensing workstation","The written steps for checking a prescription and backing up each night","The router and cables linking the counter to the back office and the Internet"],"correct":[3,2,0,4,1,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"When one component fails","q":"<p>At Island Breeze Hotel, a new reservation program was installed on new computers, but staff were never given instructions on how to cancel a booking. Guests are now being double-booked. Which component of the information system has been neglected?</p>","options":["Network","Hardware","Software","Procedures"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Nothing suggests a communication fault. The missing piece is the instructions for staff.","The computers are new and working. The problem is what people do with them.","The program itself works; staff simply do not know the correct steps to follow.","Correct. Procedures are the instructions that tell users how to operate the system. Without them, users make errors even with good hardware and software."]}]},{"id":"assess_35j","title":"Module 2 · Unit 2 quiz: Hardware and software","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 2: Hardware and software. 110 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (1)","q":"<p>The Grand Anse Realty in St. George’s is setting up a company email system: a mail server running Microsoft Exchange, with Outlook on every PC. Sort each item into the component of the IT system it belongs to.</p><p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The new mail server in the back office</b></p>","options":["IT professionals","Hardware","End users","Network","Software"],"correct":1,"why":"A physical device you can touch: hardware.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (2)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The cables running from each desk to the back office</b></p>","options":["End users","IT professionals","Hardware","Network","Software"],"correct":2,"why":"Cables are physical: hardware. The network is the sharing they make possible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (3)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>Microsoft Exchange running on the mail server</b></p>","options":["End users","Network","Hardware","Software","IT professionals"],"correct":3,"why":"The program that stores and routes mail: software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (4)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>Outlook installed on each agent’s PC</b></p>","options":["End users","IT professionals","Hardware","Network","Software"],"correct":4,"why":"The email client is application software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (5)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The office network that lets every PC share the server and the printer</b></p>","options":["Network","IT professionals","End users","Hardware","Software"],"correct":0,"why":"Interconnected computers sharing resources: the network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (6)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The Internet link that carries mail to and from overseas clients</b></p>","options":["IT professionals","Network","Hardware","Software","End users"],"correct":1,"why":"It connects the office to other networks: network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (7)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>A letting agent, trained last week, who emails viewing times to tenants</b></p>","options":["End users","Network","IT professionals","Hardware","Software"],"correct":0,"why":"She uses the system for her own work: an end user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (8)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The receptionist who books meetings in the shared calendar</b></p>","options":["Hardware","Software","Network","IT professionals","End users"],"correct":4,"why":"Trained to use the system’s capabilities: an end user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (9)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>The IT manager who creates mailboxes for new staff</b></p>","options":["Network","Hardware","IT professionals","Software","End users"],"correct":2,"why":"Running and supporting the system: an IT professional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Five components of an IT system (10)","q":"<p>Which component of the IT system is this?</p><p><b>A database administrator (DBA) who looks after the tenant database</b></p>","options":["Software","Network","Hardware","IT professionals","End users"],"correct":3,"why":"DBAs are IT professionals.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Follow the data through the hardware","q":"<p>A cashier at the Seaside Pharmacy in English Harbour scans a bottle of sunscreen. Put the steps in order to show how the input, processing, storage and output hardware interact.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The barcode reader captures the product code from the bottle (input).","The reader turns the code into electrical signals and sends them to the processor.","The processor retrieves the price and description of the sunscreen from storage.","The processor adds the item to the bill and calculates the new total (processing).","The new total appears on the customer display (output).","At the end of the sale, the processor saves the reduced stock level back to storage."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Choose the input device","q":"<p>Organisations across the Caribbean need to capture data quickly and accurately. Match each input device or method to the situation it suits best.</p>","left":["MICR","OMR","OCR","Barcode reader","Biometric scanner","Sensor","Contactless card reader","Graphics tablet and stylus","Magnetic-stripe reader"],"right":["A bank in Barbados reads the account numbers printed in magnetic ink along the bottom of cheques","A weather station in Roseau measures wind speed as a hurricane approaches","Staff at the Portsmouth Marina clock in with a fingerprint so no one can sign in for a friend","An illustrator at the Canefield Design Studio draws a Carnival poster by hand with a pen on a pressure-sensitive pad","Island Lotto counts the shaded boxes on thousands of slips each night","Immigration at Douglas–Charles Airport turns the printed text on a passport page into editable data","A cashier at the Seaside Pharmacy in Mahaut captures each product code at the till","The front desk at the Coral Reef Hotel swipes a guest’s plastic key card to read the data on its stripe","A tourist on King George V Street pays by tapping a bank card at the shop counter"],"correct":[0,4,5,6,2,1,8,3,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (1)","q":"<p>The Bayside Insurance Agency in Charlestown, Nevis, is listing the hardware in its office. Sort each device by its main function.</p><p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Barcode reader at the reception desk</b></p>","options":["Output","Input","Storage"],"correct":1,"why":"It captures codes directly into the computer: input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (2)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Laser printer for policy documents</b></p>","options":["Storage","Input","Output"],"correct":2,"why":"It produces hard copy: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (3)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Solid-state drive in the manager’s laptop</b></p>","options":["Input","Storage","Output"],"correct":1,"why":"It keeps files and programs permanently: secondary storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (4)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Webcam used for online meetings</b></p>","options":["Storage","Output","Input"],"correct":2,"why":"It captures images: input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (5)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Multimedia projector in the boardroom</b></p>","options":["Storage","Input","Output"],"correct":2,"why":"It displays soft-copy output on a large screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (6)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>External hard drive used for nightly backups</b></p>","options":["Storage","Output","Input"],"correct":0,"why":"It stores copies of data: secondary storage (and a peripheral).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (7)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Fingerprint scanner on the staff door</b></p>","options":["Output","Input","Storage"],"correct":1,"why":"It captures biometric data: input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (8)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>Speakers on the reception computer</b></p>","options":["Input","Storage","Output"],"correct":2,"why":"They turn signals into sound: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, output or storage? (9)","q":"<p>What is the main function of this device?</p><p><b>USB flash drive</b></p>","options":["Storage","Output","Input"],"correct":0,"why":"A portable solid-state storage device.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the output device to the job","q":"<p>Each business below needs output of a particular kind. Match the output device to the job it suits best.</p>","left":["Laser printer","Inkjet printer","Dot-matrix printer","Thermal printer","Plotter / large-format printer","3D printer","Actuator","Speech-generating device","Television"],"right":["A signal from the computer opens the barrier at the Young Street car park","An architect in St. George’s prints full-size A0 building plans after a hurricane","A student who cannot speak taps words on a tablet and the device says them aloud in class","A Flamboyant Heights College engineering student builds a plastic model of a new jetty, layer by layer","A family in Grand Anse prints a few colour photos from a birthday party at home","A check-in desk at MBIA prints boarding passes without ink","A large screen in the lobby of Gouyave Suites shows the day’s tours to every guest in the room","Grenville Law Chambers prints 300 pages of contracts a day, quickly and quietly","A freight office at the St. George’s cruise port prints invoices with two carbon copies in one pass"],"correct":[7,4,8,5,1,3,0,2,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"The right printer for the job","q":"<p>The Sunset Supermarket in Frigate Bay needs printers at its eight checkouts. Receipts must print quickly and quietly, and the store wants to avoid buying ink or toner. Which printer is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Laser printer","Inkjet printer","Thermal printer","Dot-matrix printer"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Laser printers are fast and quiet but need toner and are bulky and costly for every checkout.","Inkjets need ink cartridges and are slower; the store wanted to avoid ink.","Correct. Thermal printers heat special paper, so they need no ink or toner, and they are fast and quiet: ideal for receipts.","Dot-matrix printers use an inked ribbon and are noisy, so they fail two of the requirements."]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (1)","q":"<p>A technician at the Island Tech Repairs in Frigate Bay is explaining computer parts to customers. Sort each description by the processing device it describes.</p><p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Executes the instructions of the accounting program and controls the other components</b></p>","options":["Motherboard","CPU","GPU"],"correct":1,"why":"General instructions and control: the CPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (2)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Draws the 3D scenes of a racing game sixty times a second using many small cores in parallel</b></p>","options":["GPU","CPU","Motherboard"],"correct":0,"why":"Parallel graphics work: the GPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (3)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>The main circuit board with the processor socket and the RAM slots</b></p>","options":["GPU","CPU","Motherboard"],"correct":2,"why":"It holds and connects the parts: the motherboard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (4)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Decides which file to retrieve from the SSD and compares two totals in a spreadsheet</b></p>","options":["CPU","Motherboard","GPU"],"correct":0,"why":"Controlling retrieval and comparing values: the CPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (5)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Renders the effects on a high-definition Carnival video while it is edited</b></p>","options":["Motherboard","CPU","GPU"],"correct":2,"why":"Rendering images and video: the GPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (6)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Its circuits and buses carry data between the processor, RAM, storage and ports</b></p>","options":["CPU","Motherboard","GPU"],"correct":1,"why":"The connecting board: the motherboard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (7)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>A customer’s laptop will not take more memory because there are no free slots on it</b></p>","options":["CPU","GPU","Motherboard"],"correct":2,"why":"RAM fits into slots on the motherboard.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"CPU, GPU or motherboard? (8)","q":"<p>Which processing device is this?</p><p><b>Contains the control unit, the arithmetic logic unit and registers</b></p>","options":["GPU","CPU","Motherboard"],"correct":1,"why":"These are the parts of the CPU.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Run the machine cycle","q":"<p>A student at Frangipani Hill Academy runs a program that adds two exam scores. Put the steps the CPU follows for ONE instruction in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The program counter holds the address of the next instruction.","The instruction is fetched from main memory into the CPU.","The program counter is increased to point to the following instruction.","The control unit decodes the instruction to work out what must be done.","The ALU executes the instruction by adding the two values.","The result is stored in a register (the accumulator) or written back to memory."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Inside the CPU","q":"<p>A Flamboyant Heights College student opens a spreadsheet of cruise-passenger numbers for the St. George’s cruise port and asks it to total a column. Match each part of the processor to what it does during that job.</p>","left":["Control unit","Arithmetic logic unit (ALU)","Program counter (register)","Accumulator (register)","Address bus","Data bus","Cache"],"right":["Keeps the instructions used again and again close to the CPU so it does not wait for RAM","Fetches the \"add\" instruction from memory, decodes it and signals the other parts when to act","Adds the passenger numbers and checks whether the total is greater than the pier’s limit","Carries the passenger figure itself between RAM and the CPU","Holds the address of the next instruction to fetch","Holds the running total between additions","Carries the memory location of the cell value the CPU wants"],"correct":[1,2,4,5,6,3,0]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the storage to the need","q":"<p>Match each storage device to the situation it suits best.</p>","left":["RAM","ROM","Cache","Magnetic tape","Solid-state drive (SSD)","Blu-ray disc","Cloud storage"],"right":["Holds the spreadsheet you are editing right now","Keeps the start-up instructions when the computer is switched off","Gives the CPU the most frequently used instructions at top speed","Cheap, very large backups that are rarely read, accessed sequentially","Fast, silent, shock-resistant storage inside a laptop","Distributing a high-definition film on a physical disc","Off-site copies of records reachable from anywhere after a hurricane"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (1)","q":"<p>Businesses and students around the Caribbean are making storage decisions. Read each situation and choose the BEST answer.</p><p>A laptop at the Harbour View Accounting switches off during a power cut. Which work survives?</p>","options":["Unsaved work held in RAM","Files already saved on the internal SSD","Both the saved and the unsaved work"],"correct":1,"why":"RAM is temporary and volatile; the SSD stores files permanently.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (2)","q":"<p>A photographer in Port of Spain wants to carry finished photos to a client’s office in her pocket.</p>","options":["The laptop’s internal hard drive","A removable USB flash drive","RAM"],"correct":1,"why":"Removable storage is the medium you take out and carry.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (3)","q":"<p>The Holetown Marina office needs an extra 4 TB drive in its own case that can be unplugged each night and locked in a safe.</p>","options":["An external hard drive","Cache","ROM"],"correct":0,"why":"A drive in its own case, connected by cable: external storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (4)","q":"<p>A terminal at the Port Information Desk has no storage of its own. It shows only what a central server sends and forgets every setting when it restarts. What is it?</p>","options":["A multi-user operating system","A dumb terminal","An embedded control system"],"correct":1,"why":"Without storage, a computer is a dumb terminal that saves no settings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (5)","q":"<p>While a spreadsheet of cruise-passenger figures is being totalled, where are the figures held for the processor?</p>","options":["An optical disc","A flash memory card","Primary storage (RAM)"],"correct":2,"why":"RAM stores and retrieves data during processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where should the data be kept? (6)","q":"<p>A Frigate Bay student copies her CAPE notes to a USB drive every Friday in case her laptop fails. What is the USB drive doing?</p>","options":["Acting as an input device","Serving as a backup","Acting as primary storage"],"correct":1,"why":"Flash cards and USB drives often serve as backup.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (1)","q":"<p>A technician is setting up computers at the Frigate Bay Freight Services in Basseterre. Read each situation and choose the BEST answer.</p><p>A handheld barcode scanner is plugged into the warehouse computer by USB. What is it?</p>","options":["Part of the core","An input peripheral","A storage peripheral"],"correct":1,"why":"It is connected to and controlled by the computer, outside the CPU and memory, and it captures data: an input peripheral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (2)","q":"<p>The RAM chips on the motherboard hold the stock program while it runs. Are they a peripheral?</p>","options":["No, main memory is part of the core","Yes, a storage peripheral","Yes, an output peripheral"],"correct":0,"why":"The core is the CPU and main memory, so RAM is not a peripheral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (3)","q":"<p>An external hard drive is attached each evening to back up the delivery records. What kind of peripheral is it?</p>","options":["Storage peripheral","Communication peripheral","Output peripheral"],"correct":0,"why":"It keeps copies of data outside the core: a storage peripheral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (4)","q":"<p>A Wi-Fi dongle lets an old desktop join the office network. What kind of peripheral is it?</p>","options":["Input peripheral","Not a peripheral","Communication peripheral"],"correct":2,"why":"A network adapter adds a way to communicate: a communication peripheral.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (5)","q":"<p>A new label printer prints nonsense until the manufacturer’s file is installed. What was missing?</p>","options":["A device driver","A faster CPU","More RAM"],"correct":0,"why":"A device driver lets the operating system communicate with that particular printer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Peripheral or core? (6)","q":"<p>A new mouse works the moment it is plugged in because Windows finds and loads its driver by itself. What is this called?</p>","options":["Plug and play","Spooling","Booting"],"correct":0,"why":"Plug and play: the OS detects the device and loads its driver automatically.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which is not a peripheral?","q":"<p>A technician at the Marsh Harbour Medical Centre is listing the peripheral devices attached to a reception computer. Which item should NOT be on the list?</p>","options":["An external hard drive for backups","A wireless printer","The arithmetic logic unit","A USB fingerprint scanner"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This is a storage peripheral.","A printer is an output peripheral, even when it connects by Wi-Fi.","Correct. The ALU is part of the CPU, the core of the computer, so it is not a peripheral.","This is an input peripheral: it is connected to and controlled by the computer but sits outside the core."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (1)","q":"<p>Read each description and choose the BEST classification.</p><p>A spreadsheet program used by a Guyana rice farmer for budgets and by a Barbados teacher for marks.</p>","options":["Special-purpose application","Utility","General-purpose application"],"correct":2,"why":"Many users, many tasks: general-purpose application software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (2)","q":"<p>A point-of-sale package sold to hundreds of supermarkets across the region.</p>","options":["Special-purpose application","Operating system","Bespoke application"],"correct":0,"why":"One kind of task, sold to many businesses: special-purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (3)","q":"<p>A booking system written only for the Saba Dive Centre by a hired programmer.</p>","options":["Language translator","Special-purpose application","Bespoke application"],"correct":2,"why":"Written for one organisation’s exact needs: bespoke.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (4)","q":"<p>Android, running on a student’s phone and managing its memory, apps and touchscreen.</p>","options":["Operating system","Application software","Embedded system"],"correct":0,"why":"It manages the device’s resources: an operating system (system software).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (5)","q":"<p>A program that scans the hard disk and reorganises fragmented files.</p>","options":["Compiler","Utility","General-purpose application"],"correct":1,"why":"It maintains the computer: a utility (disk defragmenter).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of software? (6)","q":"<p>The chip and program inside a washing machine that runs the wash cycle.</p>","options":["Embedded system","Operating system","Bespoke application"],"correct":0,"why":"A dedicated computer built into a larger device: an embedded control system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (1)","q":"<p>Application software reaches users in different ways and comes from different sources. Read each Caribbean situation and choose the BEST answer.</p><p>A new laptop bought on Young Street already has a calculator and a photo viewer the first time it is switched on.</p>","options":["Bundled","Published separately","Bespoke"],"correct":0,"why":"Supplied with the operating system or the hardware: bundled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (2)","q":"<p>The Georgetown Community Library buys a library management system from a vendor and downloads it on its own.</p>","options":["Embedded","Bundled","Published separately"],"correct":2,"why":"Bought and installed on its own: published separately.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (3)","q":"<p>The Frigate Bay Realty pays for a licence to use Microsoft Office; the company that makes it keeps its source code private.</p>","options":["University project","Proprietary","Open-source"],"correct":1,"why":"Owned by a company, licence sold, code private: proprietary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (4)","q":"<p>A teacher in Paramaribo installs LibreOffice free of charge; its source code is published so anyone can study and change it.</p>","options":["Bundled utility","Open-source","Proprietary"],"correct":1,"why":"Published source code anyone may change: open-source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (5)","q":"<p>Computing students at Sandbox Tree College build a bus-timetable app as their final-year project and share it with classmates.</p>","options":["Proprietary","Operating system","University project"],"correct":2,"why":"Built by university students for study: a university project.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Where does the app come from? (6)","q":"<p>A student copies a flight simulator onto a computer that has no operating system installed. What happens?</p>","options":["It runs faster, with nothing else to slow it down","It turns into system software","It cannot run, because application software needs system software"],"correct":2,"why":"Application software cannot run without system software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (1)","q":"<p>Organisations around the region are choosing software. Sort each description by whether it fits proprietary or open-source software, as described in the extension table.</p><p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>The Montego Bay Realty pays a yearly licence fee for Microsoft Office</b></p>","options":["Proprietary","Open-source"],"correct":0,"why":"Bought from the company that owns it: proprietary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (2)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A school lab in San Ignacio installs LibreOffice on every computer at no licence cost</b></p>","options":["Proprietary","Open-source"],"correct":1,"why":"In most cases free of charge: open-source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (3)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A Coralstone College student downloads the source code of a program and changes it for her project</b></p>","options":["Open-source","Proprietary"],"correct":0,"why":"Source code provided and editable: open-source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (4)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>The maker of the AccountsPro package keeps its code secret, so users cannot edit it</b></p>","options":["Open-source","Proprietary"],"correct":1,"why":"Source code not provided, uneditable: proprietary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (5)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A firm relies on regular bug fixes and a support line from the developer of its software</b></p>","options":["Open-source","Proprietary"],"correct":1,"why":"Regular fixes and developer support are listed as a proprietary strength.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (6)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A small project is left without fixes after its only volunteer programmer moves on</b></p>","options":["Proprietary","Open-source"],"correct":1,"why":"Contributions may be unsupported if the programmer has left: an open-source risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (7)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A designer in Frigate Bay buys an Apple Mac running macOS</b></p>","options":["Proprietary","Open-source"],"correct":0,"why":"macOS is proprietary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proprietary or open-source? (8)","q":"<p>Proprietary or open-source?</p><p><b>A server at the Island Web Hosting runs Red Hat Enterprise Linux</b></p>","options":["Open-source","Proprietary"],"correct":0,"why":"Red Hat is listed as open-source (businesses pay for support).","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the operating system at work","q":"<p>A Form 6 student in Mandeville describes an evening on her laptop. Some events are handled by the operating system; others are done by application software.</p><p>Select EVERY line that describes a function of the OPERATING SYSTEM.</p>","options":["6. She moves her essay from Downloads into a folder called CAPE.","1. When she logs in, the laptop checks her password and opens her own account.","3. With music, a browser and her essay all open, each program gets turns on the processor.","7. RAM is nearly full, so part of the drive is used as virtual memory.","5. She builds a formula to average her test marks.","4. She plugs in a new USB mouse and it works straight away after a driver loads.","2. She types her Communication Studies essay and checks the spelling."],"correct":[0,1,2,3,5],"why":"Correct. Security, process management, device management, file management and memory management are all OS functions. Typing an essay and building a formula are tasks done in application software."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the operating system type","q":"<p>Different jobs need different kinds of operating system. Match each type of OS to the Caribbean situation it suits.</p>","left":["Single-user, multitasking","Multi-user","Mobile","Real-time","Batch","Multi-programming"],"right":["A reporter at the Bridgetown Courier writes a story, plays music and browses the web on her own laptop at the same time","Forty terminals at the Caribbean Shipping Registry share one central server at the same time","An aircraft landing at Grantley Adams International Airport needs its flight systems to respond to sensor input within milliseconds","Island Water Billing Office prints thousands of customer bills overnight with no one at the keyboard","A taxi driver at the Bridgetown cruise terminal uses a touchscreen phone that must save battery all day","A computer at the Island Water Billing Office holds the meter-reading program and the report program in memory together, and the CPU switches to one whenever the other waits for the disk"],"correct":[0,1,4,2,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (1)","q":"<p>Programmers around the region are translating their code. Sort each situation by the translator being described.</p><p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>A developer in Trinidad turns her whole C payroll program into an executable file to sell to clients</b></p>","options":["Assembler","Interpreter","Compiler"],"correct":2,"why":"Whole program translated at once into an executable: a compiler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (2)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>A Form 6 student’s Python program stops at line 14 and highlights a misspelt variable</b></p>","options":["Compiler","Interpreter","Assembler"],"correct":1,"why":"Stopping at the first error, line by line: an interpreter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (3)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>An engineer converts LOAD, ADD and STORE mnemonics for the chip in a water pump</b></p>","options":["Compiler","Assembler","Interpreter"],"correct":1,"why":"Mnemonics into machine code: an assembler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (4)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>The finished program runs quickly on clinic computers that have no translator installed</b></p>","options":["Assembler","Interpreter","Compiler"],"correct":2,"why":"A compiled executable does not need the compiler at run time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (5)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>Each time the script runs, every statement is translated again, so it runs more slowly</b></p>","options":["Assembler","Compiler","Interpreter"],"correct":2,"why":"Retranslating on every run is the interpreter’s drawback.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (6)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>After translation, a list of three syntax errors appears and no executable is produced until they are fixed</b></p>","options":["Compiler","Assembler","Interpreter"],"correct":0,"why":"Errors reported at the end of whole-program translation: a compiler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (7)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>One low-level instruction becomes exactly one machine-code instruction for a hardware driver</b></p>","options":["Assembler","Interpreter","Compiler"],"correct":0,"why":"One-to-one translation of assembly language: an assembler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which translator fits? (8)","q":"<p>Which language translator is at work here?</p><p><b>A teacher at Mahoe Grove High School demonstrates code line by line, running each statement as it is translated</b></p>","options":["Interpreter","Assembler","Compiler"],"correct":0,"why":"Translate and execute one statement at a time: an interpreter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Compiler or interpreter?","q":"<p>A software developer in Trinidad has finished a payroll program and wants to give clients a file that runs quickly without the source code or any translator being installed on their computers. Which translator should she use?</p>","options":["A compiler, because it translates the whole program into a stand-alone executable","A utility, because it optimises the program for speed","An assembler, because it converts high-level code into machine code","An interpreter, because it translates one line at a time"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. A compiler produces an executable that runs fast and does not need the source code or the compiler at run time.","Utilities maintain the computer; they do not translate programs.","An assembler translates assembly language, not a high-level language.","An interpreter must be present every time the program runs, and the source code must be supplied. It suits testing, not this requirement."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the utility to the problem","q":"<p>The IT support desk at the Gros Islet Marina Office receives these complaints. Match each problem to the utility that solves it.</p>","left":["Disk defragmenter","Backup utility","File compression","Antivirus","Disk clean-up","Encryption utility","Registry cleaner","Disk partitioning","Formatting"],"right":["A 40 MB folder of photos is too large to email to a client","Staff need a copy of the berth bookings in case the office floods","Files on the old hard disk load slowly because their parts are scattered","A brand-new drive must have a file system set up before any files can be saved on it","A new 1 TB drive should be split into one section for programs and another for berth records","The drive is full of temporary Internet files","A manager’s flash drive with client records could be lost","Settings entries left behind by programs uninstalled years ago still clutter the Windows registry","A downloaded attachment may contain malicious software"],"correct":[2,1,0,8,5,6,7,4,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (1)","q":"<p>All of these are system software. Sort each into its group.</p><p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Allocates RAM to each running program</b></p>","options":["Utility","Operating system","Language translator"],"correct":1,"why":"Memory management is an OS function.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (2)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Converts a whole C program into an executable file</b></p>","options":["Utility","Operating system","Language translator"],"correct":2,"why":"That is a compiler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (3)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Copies the hard disk to the cloud every night</b></p>","options":["Utility","Language translator","Operating system"],"correct":0,"why":"A backup utility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (4)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Schedules turns on the CPU for several programs</b></p>","options":["Utility","Operating system","Language translator"],"correct":1,"why":"Process management: OS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (5)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Turns LOAD and ADD mnemonics into machine code</b></p>","options":["Utility","Language translator","Operating system"],"correct":1,"why":"An assembler.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (6)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Reorganises scattered file fragments on an HDD</b></p>","options":["Utility","Language translator","Operating system"],"correct":0,"why":"A disk defragmenter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (7)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Runs a Python program one statement at a time</b></p>","options":["Operating system","Utility","Language translator"],"correct":2,"why":"An interpreter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (8)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Checks user names and passwords at log-in</b></p>","options":["Language translator","Operating system","Utility"],"correct":1,"why":"Security is an OS function.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (9)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Shrinks a folder into a ZIP file</b></p>","options":["Utility","Operating system","Language translator"],"correct":0,"why":"A compression utility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (10)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Splits a new drive into separate sections for programs and data</b></p>","options":["Utility","Operating system","Language translator"],"correct":0,"why":"A disk partitioning utility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Operating system, translator or utility? (11)","q":"<p>Which kind of system software is this?</p><p><b>Manages the laptop’s Wi-Fi connection so programs can reach the Internet</b></p>","options":["Utility","Language translator","Operating system"],"correct":2,"why":"Managing network connections is an OS function.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (1)","q":"<p>Embedded systems are working all over the islands. Decide whether each one only watches and warns, or acts on the process by itself.</p><p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>A weather station in Barbados logs wind speed and air pressure and displays them for forecasters as a storm approaches</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":1,"why":"It records and displays readings; people decide what to do: monitoring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (2)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>The air conditioner at the Coral Reef Hotel switches its compressor on and off to keep a room at 22 °C</b></p>","options":["Monitoring system","Control system"],"correct":1,"why":"It drives an actuator using feedback: control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (3)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>A patient monitor at a Kingstown hospital shows heart rate and sounds an alarm for the nurse</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":1,"why":"It alerts a person, who acts: monitoring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (4)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>Automatic irrigation on a farm in St. Kitts opens a valve when soil moisture falls too low</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":0,"why":"The valve is an actuator, so the system changes the process: control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (5)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>A smart electricity meter records a home’s usage every half hour for the bill</b></p>","options":["Monitoring system","Control system"],"correct":0,"why":"It records usage only: monitoring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (6)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>A pump keeps a hillside water tank in Portsmouth at the right level, switching on when the float sensor reads low</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":0,"why":"Sensor, comparison, pump (actuator) and feedback: control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (7)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>A vaccine fridge sends a text to the pharmacist if it rises above 8 °C</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":1,"why":"A text to a person is an alert, not an actuator: monitoring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control system? (8)","q":"<p>Does the computer act on the process?</p><p><b>Traffic lights on the Bridge Street change when sensors detect a queue of waiting cars</b></p>","options":["Control system","Monitoring system"],"correct":0,"why":"The lights act on the traffic automatically: control.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Close the control loop","q":"<p>A guest at the Coral Reef Hotel in English Harbour sets the room air conditioner to 22 °C. The room is at 27 °C. Put the steps of the embedded control system in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The temperature sensor measures the room temperature as an analogue signal.","The analogue-to-digital converter (ADC) changes the reading into digital data.","The microcontroller compares the reading (27 °C) with the set point (22 °C).","The microcontroller sends a signal to switch the compressor (actuator) on.","The compressor cools the room.","The sensor feeds the new temperature back, and the compressor switches off when the room reaches 22 °C."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Monitoring or control?","q":"<p>A pharmacy in Castries keeps vaccines in a refrigerator fitted with a temperature sensor. The system records the temperature every five minutes and sends a text message to the pharmacist if it rises above 8 °C. The pharmacist then moves the vaccines. Which BEST describes this system?</p>","options":["Application software, because the pharmacist uses it","A control system, because it uses a sensor","A control system, because it sends a text message as output","A monitoring system, because it records readings and alerts a person, who takes the action"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This is an embedded system dedicated to one function inside a device.","Both monitoring and control systems use sensors. The test is whether the computer acts on the process.","A text alert is output to a person, not a signal to an actuator, so the process is not controlled.","Correct. The computer measures, records and alerts, but it does not change the temperature itself."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (1)","q":"<p>Embedded systems are everywhere in Antigua's shops, hotels and homes. Read each situation and choose the BEST answer.</p><p>A microwave in an English Harbour kitchen has a chip that reads the keypad and runs the timer. Is it an embedded system?</p>","options":["No: it has no keyboard or monitor","Yes: a programmable computer inside a device that is not a general-purpose computer","No: embedded systems must be connected to the Internet"],"correct":1,"why":"A dedicated, programmable computer inside an appliance: an embedded system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (2)","q":"<p>A student’s laptop runs a word processor, games and a web browser. What is it?</p>","options":["An embedded system","A monitoring system","A general-purpose computer"],"correct":2,"why":"It can run many kinds of program: general-purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (3)","q":"<p>A card terminal in a Market Street shop has not had a firmware update since it was installed six years ago. Why is this a risk?</p>","options":["Embedded systems are hard to update, so known vulnerabilities linger","Old terminals print receipts more slowly","The terminal will refuse cards after five years"],"correct":0,"why":"Without updates, known weaknesses stay open while the terminal is connected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (4)","q":"<p>In the Target breach, how was data from over 40 million cards stolen?</p>","options":["Through malicious software on connected point-of-sale systems","By stealing cards from customers’ wallets","By guessing PINs at cash machines"],"correct":0,"why":"Attackers infected the connected POS tills, which captured card data at payment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (5)","q":"<p>The Coral Reef Hotel has its door locks, tills and guest Wi-Fi all on one network. What is the BEST first step?</p>","options":["Separate the tills and locks from guest Wi-Fi and change default passwords","Connect more devices so they can share updates","Switch off the antivirus on the office PCs"],"correct":0,"why":"Separating networks and changing defaults limits who can reach the embedded devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Embedded systems: spot the risk (6)","q":"<p>A smart TV in an English Harbour hotel lobby no longer receives updates from its maker. What should the hotel do?</p>","options":["Nothing: TVs do not contain computers","Keep it off the main network or replace it, because its flaws will not be fixed","Connect it to the payment network so it is monitored"],"correct":1,"why":"An unpatched connected device is a lingering vulnerability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Why the tills stay vulnerable","q":"<p>The manager of the Sunset Supermarket in Holetown reads that over 40 million credit cards were stolen in the Target breach through point-of-sale systems. She asks why embedded tills like hers can stay vulnerable for years. Which answer is BEST?</p>","options":["They are general-purpose computers, so they run any software an attacker sends","Being embedded means they cannot be reached over a network","They have no processor, so they cannot run security checks","They are hard to update, so known vulnerabilities linger while they stay connected"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Tills are embedded systems built for one job, not general-purpose computers.","The opposite: once connected, they can be reached, which is exactly how the Target tills were infected.","Every embedded system has a programmable processor; that is what makes it a computer.","Correct. Fixed firmware, no easy update route and long service lives mean weaknesses stay open on connected tills."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Equip a new office in Montego Bay (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising Harbour View Accounting, a new four-person firm in Montego Bay. It prepares clients’ accounts and payroll and must keep records safe through the hurricane season. Make the best choices.</p><p>The owner asks what software the firm needs to prepare accounts and payroll for many small businesses. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Hire a programmer to write bespoke accounting software from scratch.","A special-purpose accounting and payroll package already used by many firms, plus a general-purpose office suite."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Bespoke software is expensive and slow to build, and a standard task like payroll is already well served by special-purpose packages.","Good. Proven, affordable and ready now, with an office suite for letters and spreadsheets."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Equip a new office in Montego Bay (step 2)","q":"<p>Next, hardware. Staff open large spreadsheets and the accounting package at the same time, and the old office laptops freeze. What do you prioritise?</p>","options":["Keep the old laptops but buy larger monitors.","Laptops with a multi-core processor, at least 16 GB of RAM and an SSD."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A bigger screen is output; it does nothing for processing power or memory.","Strong choice. More cores and RAM support multitasking, and an SSD loads files quickly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Equip a new office in Montego Bay (step 3)","q":"<p>Finally, the owner remembers Hurricane Beryl. How should client records be protected?</p>","options":["Use a backup utility to copy records nightly to cloud storage, keep an encrypted external drive off-site, and keep antivirus up to date.","Save everything on one desktop hard disk in the office; it has never failed."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Several copies, on different media, with one off-site, and protection against malicious software.","One copy in one building is one disaster away from total loss."]}]},{"id":"assess_3cl","title":"Module 2 · Unit 3 quiz: Data for decisions and types of HCI","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 3: Data for decisions and types of HCI. 49 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (1)","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in Portsmouth collects data every day. For each situation, choose the BEST answer.</p><p>The till records \"Panadol 24-pack, 1 unit, 10:14\". On its own, this record is...</p>","options":["information","a decision","data"],"correct":2,"why":"A single raw, unprocessed fact with no context or meaning is data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (2)","q":"<p>The owner reads \"Sales of sunscreen rose 35% in the week the number of cruise ships at the St. John’s cruise port doubled\". This is...</p>","options":["information","a transaction","data"],"correct":0,"why":"The sales data has been processed, compared and given meaning for the owner: information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (3)","q":"<p>The manager compares this month's overtime at each branch with the budget and cuts extra shifts at the branch that is over budget. Information is being used to...</p>","options":["control","plan","record and comply"],"correct":0,"why":"Comparing actual with planned figures and taking corrective action is control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (4)","q":"<p>The owners study a three-year sales forecast and set a goal of opening a branch in Roseau. Information is being used to...</p>","options":["coordinate","plan","control"],"correct":1,"why":"Setting goals and deciding where resources will go is planning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (5)","q":"<p>The sales system tells the stockroom which medicines the counter is running short of before the evening shift starts. Information is being used to...</p>","options":["plan","record and comply","coordinate"],"correct":2,"why":"Making departments work together (sales telling the stockroom what to restock) is coordination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (6)","q":"<p>The pharmacy keeps every receipt for seven years so that the tax inspector can check its turnover. Information is being used to...</p>","options":["decide","record and comply","control"],"correct":1,"why":"Keeping evidence of transactions for auditors and tax authorities is recording and compliance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Data, information and why they matter (7)","q":"<p>A 900-page list of every sale for five years is sent to the owner the night before she must decide on the new branch. Why does it have little value to her?</p>","options":["It was produced by a computer","It is not at the right level of detail, or in time, for that person","It cost nothing to collect"],"correct":1,"why":"Information is a resource with value only when it reaches the right person, at the right time, at the right level of detail. A transaction list is far too detailed for an owner.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level is this decision?","q":"<p>The department head of the Bayside Hotel in Negril compares occupancy figures for the last three months and decides how many extra housekeeping staff to hire for the coming winter tourist season. At which decision-making level is this decision MOST likely made?</p>","options":["Tactical","Strategic","Transactional","Operational"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. A middle manager (department head) is allocating resources for the medium term (one season) to carry out the hotel's plans.","Strategic decisions set long-term direction, such as building a new wing. Hiring seasonal staff for one department is a medium-term resource decision.","There is no \"transactional\" decision-making level in the syllabus. Transactions are recorded at the operational level, but the three levels are strategic, tactical and operational.","Operational decisions are day to day, such as which room an attendant cleans first. Planning a season's staffing is longer term than that."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (1)","q":"<p>The Grenadine Ferries company runs boats between St. Vincent, Bequia and Union Island. Sort each piece of information to the level that would MOST use it.</p><p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A ten-year forecast of regional tourist arrivals from a tourism board report.</b></p>","options":["Tactical","Strategic","Operational"],"correct":1,"why":"External, future-oriented and highly summarised: strategic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (2)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>Today's passenger list for the 09:00 sailing to Bequia.</b></p>","options":["Strategic","Tactical","Operational"],"correct":2,"why":"Detailed, current and needed now for one sailing: operational.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (3)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A monthly report comparing fuel spending on each route with the budget.</b></p>","options":["Tactical","Strategic","Operational"],"correct":0,"why":"Periodic, summarised and compared with budget: tactical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (4)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A competitor's plans to launch a fast catamaran service in the region.</b></p>","options":["Operational","Strategic","Tactical"],"correct":1,"why":"External information about the environment that may change long-term direction: strategic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (5)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>The number of life jackets checked on board this morning.</b></p>","options":["Strategic","Tactical","Operational"],"correct":2,"why":"Detailed, internal and for a daily task: operational.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (6)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A quarterly exception report listing routes with fewer than 50% of seats sold.</b></p>","options":["Tactical","Operational","Strategic"],"correct":0,"why":"An exception report for a manager to adjust schedules and resources: tactical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (7)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A crew roster for this weekend's sailings.</b></p>","options":["Operational","Tactical","Strategic"],"correct":0,"why":"Short-term scheduling of staff: operational.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which level needs this information? (8)","q":"<p>Which decision-making level needs this information?</p><p><b>A summary of five years of profits by route, shown as a trend chart.</b></p>","options":["Strategic","Operational","Tactical"],"correct":0,"why":"Highly summarised, long time span, used to decide which routes to grow or drop: strategic.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the decision to its type","q":"<p>Match each decision with the description that classifies it correctly.</p>","left":["Calculate a cashier's weekly pay from hours worked","Choose one of three quotes to supply school laptops","Decide whether a hotel damaged by a hurricane should rebuild or relocate","Approve a loan only if the credit score is above a set limit","Plan extra staff for days when four cruise ships dock at once","Decide whether a Barbados company should expand into Guyana"],"right":["Structured: a clear rule that can be automated","Unstructured: novel, uncertain, no agreed procedure","Unstructured: a one-off strategic choice relying on insight","Semi-structured: prices can be compared, but reliability needs judgement","Structured: a fixed formula always gives the same answer","Semi-structured: past data helps, but managers must judge"],"correct":[4,3,1,0,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information for a hurricane season (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the information systems adviser to the Sunrise Wholesale company in Port of Spain. The managing director wants better decisions before, during and after hurricane season. Choose the best advice.</p><p>The managing director asks for \"one big report\" with every transaction from the last five years so she can decide whether to build a second warehouse in Chaguanas. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Print every transaction so she has all the detail.","Give her a summarised trend report of five years of sales, combined with external storm-risk and population forecasts."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Detailed transaction lists suit the operational level. Too much detail hides the trends a strategic decision needs.","Good. A strategic, unstructured decision needs summarised, external, future-oriented information."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information for a hurricane season (step 2)","q":"<p>The purchasing manager now wants to decide how many generators to pre-order for the season. What should the system give him?</p>","options":["A monthly summary of last year's generator sales, an exception report on items that sold out, and a what-if tool to test order sizes.","Nothing: let the system order a fixed number automatically every year."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. A semi-structured tactical decision is supported by periodic summaries, exception reports and what-if analysis, with judgement on top.","Demand varies with each season's forecast. This decision needs data plus judgement, so it is not fully structured."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information for a hurricane season (step 3)","q":"<p>Warehouse clerks must decide, many times a day, when to reorder batteries and tarpaulins. How should these decisions be handled?</p>","options":["Set reorder levels in the stock system so it raises orders automatically, using accurate, real-time stock counts.","Ask the managing director to approve each reorder personally."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. A structured, operational decision with a clear rule can be automated using detailed, current internal data.","Routine structured decisions belong at the operational level. Sending them to the top wastes strategic time and slows restocking."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Command line or menu?","q":"<p>A network technician at the Kralendijk Clinic types \"ping 192.168.1.1\" at a prompt to test a printer's connection. A receptionist at the same clinic uses a screen that shows \"1. Book appointment 2. Cancel appointment 3. Print list\" and presses 1. Which statement BEST distinguishes the two interfaces?</p>","options":["The technician must recall and type commands in exact syntax, whereas the receptionist selects from options the computer displays.","Both use a natural language interface because they use English words.","The technician's interface is text based, whereas the receptionist's is not.","The technician uses a GUI, whereas the receptionist uses a command line."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Recall versus recognition is the key difference between a command line and a menu interface, and both sides are on the same feature.","Natural language means the computer interprets everyday sentences. \"ping 192.168.1.1\" is a strict command, not everyday language.","Both screens could be entirely text. Being text based does not separate them; how the user gives instructions does.","Neither is a GUI: there are no windows, icons or pointer described. The receptionist chooses from numbered options, which is a menu."]},{"t":"match","title":"Strengths and weaknesses of interfaces","q":"<p>The Sea Link Freight office in Kingstown uses several interfaces. Match each thing that happened to the interface and the advantage or disadvantage that explains it.</p>","left":["The network administrator updates 40 office computers in one minute by running a saved script of commands.","A new trainee cannot remember what to type at the prompt and gets \"syntax error\" three times.","A walk-in customer picks \"Track a parcel\" from four numbered options on the kiosk with no training at all.","A clerk must step through five sub-menus every time she needs \"Reprint customs invoice\".","Customs clerks key shipment weight, origin and value into labelled boxes that reject letters typed into number fields.","Visitors who speak Spanish, Dutch and French all understand the parcel picture on the self-service screen.","Staff could not tell what the \"validate address\" picture meant until a word was added beneath it.","The oldest office computers run the new windowed system slowly, while the text-only system ran quickly."],"right":["Icons (part of a GUI): language independent","Icons (part of a GUI): need some text backup for abstract functions","Menu: slow to use in large systems","Command line: quick to use, sophisticated and extensible for experts","Command line: difficult to learn and difficult for novices","Forms: suit all users, and validation reduces data-entry errors","GUI: needs more memory and processing power than a command line","Menu: easy to use and easy to learn, because options are recognised, not recalled"],"correct":[3,4,7,2,5,0,1,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (1)","q":"<p>Each card describes a system in Belize. Sort it to the type of HCI it MAINLY uses.</p><p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A technician types \"ipconfig /all\" at a prompt to check a computer's network settings.</b></p>","options":["GUI","Menu","Command line","Forms"],"correct":2,"why":"Typed commands in fixed syntax: command line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (2)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>An ATM shows \"1. Withdraw 2. Balance 3. Transfer\" and the customer presses a button beside one.</b></p>","options":["Forms","GUI","Command line","Menu"],"correct":3,"why":"The customer selects from a list of displayed options: menu.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (3)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A Coralstone College applicant enters name, date of birth and programme in labelled boxes, then clicks Submit.</b></p>","options":["GUI","Forms","Menu","Command line"],"correct":1,"why":"Labelled fields for data entry: forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (4)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A student double-clicks a folder icon in a window and opens a document with the mouse pointer.</b></p>","options":["Menu","GUI","Forms","Command line"],"correct":1,"why":"Windows, icons and a pointer: GUI.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (5)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A phone line says \"Press 1 for billing, press 2 for faults\".</b></p>","options":["GUI","Forms","Command line","Menu"],"correct":3,"why":"A telephone menu of numbered options.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (6)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A hotel check-in screen with fields for passport number, arrival date and room type chosen from a drop-down list.</b></p>","options":["Menu","Forms","GUI","Command line"],"correct":1,"why":"Several fields to complete, with validation and a drop-down list: forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (7)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A server administrator runs a saved script of commands to back up files every night.</b></p>","options":["GUI","Forms","Command line","Menu"],"correct":2,"why":"Scripts of typed commands are a feature of the command line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the interface type (part 1) (8)","q":"<p>Which type of HCI is this?</p><p><b>A desktop where several windows are open, with a taskbar of icons along the bottom.</b></p>","options":["Menu","GUI","Forms","Command line"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiple windows and icons: GUI.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (1)","q":"<p>The right interface depends on the user and the task. For each user, choose the interface that suits them BEST.</p><p>An expert network technician at the Island Internet company must apply the same settings to 30 routers tonight.</p>","options":["menu","command line","speech"],"correct":1,"why":"An expert who knows the commands gains speed and can script the repeated task: command line.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (2)","q":"<p>Cruise passengers who speak many different languages use an information kiosk at the Harbour Mall near the cruise pier.</p>","options":["natural language typed in English","command line","GUI with icons and short text labels"],"correct":2,"why":"Icons are language independent and easy for novices; short labels back up the less obvious ones.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (3)","q":"<p>First-time applicants and experienced admissions clerks both enter applicant details at Coralstone College.</p>","options":["command line","forms","direct manipulation"],"correct":1,"why":"Forms copy familiar paper forms and suit all users, experts and novices, for data entry.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (4)","q":"<p>A mechanic at the Fort Street Garage is lying under a car with oily hands and needs to look up a part number.</p>","options":["command line","forms","speech"],"correct":2,"why":"Speech is hands-free and eyes-free, so it suits a user whose hands are busy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (5)","q":"<p>Waiters at a busy, noisy restaurant in San Fernando keep finding that their spoken orders are misheard by the ordering system.</p>","options":["a touchscreen menu of dishes","a command line","speech with a louder voice"],"correct":0,"why":"Background noise is a key weakness of speech. A menu lets staff recognise and tap dishes quickly with no recall needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (6)","q":"<p>A designer at the Island Ads agency wants to resize and position a logo on a poster and see the result as she drags it.</p>","options":["menu","direct manipulation","forms"],"correct":1,"why":"Rapid, reversible actions on a visible object with immediate feedback: direct manipulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (7)","q":"<p>Customers of the Island Power company want to report an outage in their own words without learning any menus or commands.</p>","options":["command line","forms","natural language"],"correct":2,"why":"Natural language lets users communicate in everyday sentences, with nothing new to learn.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the right interface for the user (8)","q":"<p>A student with a visual impairment at the Speightstown Library needs web pages read aloud to her.</p>","options":["GUI","speech (a web reader)","direct manipulation on a touchscreen"],"correct":1,"why":"Speech interfaces such as web readers and page scanners support users who cannot see the screen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (1)","q":"<p>Each situation is set in the Caribbean. Choose the type of HCI it MAINLY demonstrates.</p><p>A visitor drags a pin on a map app from Bridge Street to Gros Islet Beach and the route redraws instantly.</p>","options":["command line","forms","direct manipulation"],"correct":2,"why":"Acting on a visible object with immediate feedback is direct manipulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (2)","q":"<p>A driver in Kingston, Jamaica says \"Call Mum\" and the car dials the number.</p>","options":["speech","menu","forms"],"correct":0,"why":"Spoken input processed by speech recognition: speech.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (3)","q":"<p>A customer types \"I want to change my delivery address to Portsmouth\" into a courier's chat window.</p>","options":["natural language","speech","command line"],"correct":0,"why":"Typed everyday sentences that the system interprets: natural language. It is typed, so not speech.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (4)","q":"<p>A Trinidad bank's online loan application asks for income, employer and loan amount in separate boxes.</p>","options":["natural language","GUI","forms"],"correct":2,"why":"Labelled fields for structured data entry: forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (5)","q":"<p>A student pinches a photo on a tablet to enlarge it.</p>","options":["direct manipulation","menu","speech"],"correct":0,"why":"Pinch to zoom acts directly on the object: direct manipulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which HCI type? (6)","q":"<p>A Guyanese technician types \"shutdown /r /t 0\" to restart a server.</p>","options":["menu","command line","natural language"],"correct":1,"why":"An exact command with switches typed at a prompt: command line.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the HCI type to its defining feature","q":"<p>Match each type of HCI to the feature that distinguishes it from the others.</p>","left":["Command line","Menu","Forms","Natural language","GUI","Speech","Direct manipulation"],"right":["User enters data into labelled fields, often with validation","Physical actions on visible objects with immediate, reversible feedback","User recognises and selects one option from a displayed list","Spoken input processed by speech recognition","Windows, icons, menus and a pointer","User communicates in everyday sentences that the computer interprets","User recalls and types instructions in an exact syntax at a prompt"],"correct":[6,2,0,5,4,3,1]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the hands-free interfaces","q":"<p>Coastline Motors in Paramaribo is advertising a new car. Its brochure lists these dashboard features. A driver wants to use features safely <b>while the car is moving</b>.</p><p>Select EVERY line that describes a speech or natural language interface.</p>","options":["4. Scroll through Trip, Fuel and Tyre pressure using arrows on the steering wheel.","7. Tap the phone icon on the home screen to open your contacts.","3. Ask \"Where is the nearest pharmacy that is still open?\" and hear the answer read aloud.","2. Pinch the map on the touchscreen to zoom in on Paramaribo.","6. Say \"Play the radio\" to switch on the radio.","5. Type a destination into the Street and Town boxes on the screen.","1. Say \"Call home\" and the car dials your saved home number."],"correct":[2,4,6],"why":"Correct. Lines 1 and 6 are spoken commands (speech), and line 3 is a spoken everyday question (speech and natural language). Line 2 is direct manipulation, line 4 a menu, line 5 forms and line 7 GUI."},{"t":"mc","title":"Speech or natural language?","q":"<p>A tourist types \"what time does the last ferry to Carriacou leave tonight\" into a chat window on the Grand Anse Ferries website and receives a correct answer. Which type of HCI is MAINLY demonstrated?</p>","options":["Natural language, because the system interprets an everyday sentence","Speech, because the tourist is asking a question","Command line, because the tourist typed text","Forms, because the text was entered on a website"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Natural language is defined by the form of the input (everyday sentences), not by whether it is typed or spoken.","Speech requires spoken input processed by speech recognition. This question was typed.","A command line requires exact syntax. The tourist wrote a free everyday sentence without punctuation, and the system still worked out the meaning.","A form has several labelled fields for data entry. A single free-text chat question is not a form."]},{"t":"match","title":"Follow the data up the pyramid","q":"<p>At Island Fresh supermarket, one sale becomes part of a strategic decision. Put the stages in order, from the first event to the final decision.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["A cashier scans a case of bottled water at the Grand Anse till.","The stock system records the sale and updates the detailed stock level (operational information).","The stock level falls below the reorder point, so the system raises an order automatically (structured decision).","Each week, sales are summarised by department and compared with budget in a report for the store manager (tactical information).","The manager adjusts next quarter's drinks budget using the report and her judgement (semi-structured decision).","Five years of summarised sales are combined with external tourism forecasts in a trend chart for the owners (strategic information).","The owners decide whether to open a new store in another territory (unstructured decision)."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information at the strategic level","q":"<p>The owners of Island Fresh supermarket chain are deciding whether to open a store in Curaçao within the next five years. Their adviser is preparing a briefing pack for them. Which of the following BEST describes the information the pack should contain?</p>","options":["Periodic summaries of internal performance compared with budget","Highly summarised, largely external and future-oriented, often uncertain","Lists of individual transactions produced in real time","Detailed, internal, current and highly precise"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This describes tactical information, such as the store manager's monthly exception report. Tempting, because it is summarised, but it lacks the external, long-term focus the owners need for the Curaçao decision.","Correct. The Curaçao decision looks years ahead at the whole business and its environment, so the owners need summarised, external, forecast information (such as Curaçao population and tourism trends) and must accept uncertainty.","Real-time transaction lists help supervisors control today's trading, not owners planning a new store years ahead.","This describes operational information, such as today's till receipts in Oranjestad. It would not help the owners judge a five-year move into Curaçao."]}]},{"id":"assess_3mm","title":"Module 2 · Unit 4 quiz: Evaluating HCI, users and adaptive interfaces","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 4: Evaluating HCI, users and adaptive interfaces. 73 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (1)","q":"<p>Each situation below comes from a workplace or public service in the Caribbean. Decide which evaluation principle the evidence is mainly about, or which HCI type fits the user best.</p><p>At Holetown Grill, an experienced server orders two johnnycakes and a drink in four taps during the cruise-ship rush. Which principle does this show?</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Ease of use","Alignment"],"correct":1,"why":"Few steps for someone who already knows the system is ease of use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (2)","q":"<p>A tourist uses a bill-payment kiosk in a Castries supermarket for the first time and finishes without help because every screen shows one prompt and four labelled buttons. Which principle does this show?</p>","options":["Ease of navigation","Use of colour","Ease of learning"],"correct":2,"why":"A first-time user succeeding without training is ease of learning (recognition rather than recall).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (3)","q":"<p>Clerks at a Portsmouth insurance office often delete records by accident because there is no confirmation box and no Undo. Which principle is weak?</p>","options":["Alignment","Ease of use","Ease of learning"],"correct":1,"why":"Error prevention and recovery are guidelines for ease of use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (4)","q":"<p>A once-a-year online tax form shows \"dd/mm/yyyy\" inside the date box and a tooltip beside every field. Which principle do these features mainly improve?</p>","options":["Ease of use for experts","Alignment","Ease of learning"],"correct":2,"why":"Built-in help and examples help occasional users learn the form.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (5)","q":"<p>A telecoms company network technician configures routers all day and wants the fastest possible interface. Which HCI type suits her best?</p>","options":["Menu with large buttons","Speech interface","Command line"],"correct":2,"why":"A command line is very easy to use (fast and powerful) for a trained expert, even though it is hard to learn.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it fast or learn it fast? (6)","q":"<p>A voice assistant at a San Pedro hotel desk keeps mishearing guests with strong accents in a noisy lobby. Which principle is mainly reduced?</p>","options":["Use of colour","Ease of use","Alignment"],"correct":1,"why":"Misunderstood speech and background noise reduce ease of use of speech interfaces.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use or learning?","q":"<p>A network administrator at the San Pedro Data Centre can restart services across twenty servers with one typed command, but new interns need weeks of practice before they can use the command line. Which evaluation statement is MOST accurate?</p>","options":["The interface scores high on ease of use for experts but low on ease of learning.","The interface scores high on alignment because commands are typed in a line.","The interface scores poorly on ease of navigation because it has no colour.","The interface scores high on ease of learning but low on ease of use."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Speed for a trained user is ease of use; the weeks interns need shows poor ease of learning.","Alignment is about arranging screen elements in an orderly layout, not about typing on a line.","Navigation is about moving between screens or sections, and colour is a separate principle. Neither is the main point here.","This is reversed. Weeks of practice means it is hard to learn; one command for twenty servers means it is efficient to use."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (1)","q":"<p>An evaluation team has listed problems found in interfaces around Trinidad and Tobago. Sort each problem by the evaluation principle it MAINLY breaks.</p><p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Cashiers must re-type the same customer number on three separate screens for every sale.</b></p>","options":["Ease of learning","Alignment","Use of colour","Ease of use","Ease of navigation"],"correct":3,"why":"Extra repeated steps for a trained user are an ease-of-use problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (2)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>A new parking app uses unlabelled icons that first-time visitors cannot work out.</b></p>","options":["Ease of navigation","Ease of use","Alignment","Use of colour","Ease of learning"],"correct":4,"why":"First-time users cannot recognise what to do: a learnability problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (3)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>A ferry booking site has no Back button on the payment page, so users restart from the home page.</b></p>","options":["Ease of use","Ease of navigation","Alignment","Use of colour","Ease of learning"],"correct":1,"why":"Users cannot get back: a navigation problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (4)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Form labels and boxes start at different points across the screen, so the eye zigzags.</b></p>","options":["Ease of use","Ease of navigation","Alignment","Use of colour","Ease of learning"],"correct":2,"why":"Elements are not lined up on shared edges: an alignment problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (5)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Light grey text on a white background is almost unreadable in sunlight.</b></p>","options":["Ease of use","Ease of learning","Ease of navigation","Alignment","Use of colour"],"correct":4,"why":"Poor contrast is a use-of-colour problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (6)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Error messages are shown only by turning a field red, with no text.</b></p>","options":["Ease of navigation","Ease of use","Ease of learning","Alignment","Use of colour"],"correct":4,"why":"Relying on colour alone excludes colour-blind users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (7)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>The help page is six sub-menus deep and has no search box.</b></p>","options":["Alignment","Ease of use","Use of colour","Ease of navigation","Ease of learning"],"correct":3,"why":"Deep hierarchy and no search make it hard to find content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (8)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>There is no on-screen help, no tooltips and no examples in the fields for a once-a-year tax form.</b></p>","options":["Ease of learning","Use of colour","Ease of navigation","Alignment","Ease of use"],"correct":0,"why":"Occasional users need built-in help to learn the form.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (9)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Deleting a record has no confirmation and no Undo, so experienced clerks often lose data.</b></p>","options":["Use of colour","Alignment","Ease of use","Ease of learning","Ease of navigation"],"correct":2,"why":"Poor error prevention and recovery reduce ease of use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which principle is broken? (10)","q":"<p>Which principle does this problem mainly break?</p><p><b>Buttons on the same screen are placed randomly, some top-left and some bottom-right.</b></p>","options":["Ease of use","Alignment","Ease of learning","Use of colour","Ease of navigation"],"correct":1,"why":"Inconsistent placement of buttons is an alignment issue.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the evaluation process","q":"<p>Your class is evaluating the HCI of a self-service kiosk for a presentation. Put the steps of a systematic evaluation in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Identify the users, their task and the setting.","Identify the type or types of HCI in use.","Apply each criterion: ease of use, learning, navigation, alignment, colour.","Rate each criterion and record evidence.","Weigh the criteria according to the context.","Give an overall judgement and recommend improvements."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (1)","q":"<p>An evaluation team is visiting workplaces and testing devices around the Caribbean. For each one, choose the evaluation point that matters MOST in that setting or on that device.</p><p>Servers at an English Harbour seafood restaurant enter orders on a touchscreen POS during a full lunchtime rush.</p>","options":["Command options for experts","Ease of use: few taps and large picture buttons","A dark theme for low light"],"correct":1,"why":"Under pressure, speed (few taps, large targets) matters most in a restaurant.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (2)","q":"<p>Nurses at a Portsmouth clinic record medication doses on a form.</p>","options":["Hidden gestures to save space","Bright branding colours","Error prevention: validation, confirmation and aligned fields"],"correct":2,"why":"A wrong entry could harm a patient, so error prevention and clear alignment outweigh speed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (3)","q":"<p>A sound engineer in a Belize City recording studio edits a track late at night.</p>","options":["Ease of use for experts: drag-to-edit, shortcuts and a dark theme","Step-by-step wizards for every edit","A language selector"],"correct":0,"why":"Trained engineers want speed and direct manipulation; dark themes suit low light.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (4)","q":"<p>A technician in a school laboratory works with gloves on and must follow a test procedure.</p>","options":["Small icons to fit more on screen","Large touch targets and navigation that follows the procedure","Typed commands"],"correct":1,"why":"Gloved hands need large targets, and steps should follow the lab procedure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (5)","q":"<p>A family plays on a game console from the sofa, choosing options with a D-pad controller.</p>","options":["Long text-heavy menus","A command line","Short visual menus and text readable from a distance"],"correct":2,"why":"Controller navigation through long lists is slow, so menus are short and readable from the sofa.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (6)","q":"<p>A cruise visitor opens the Bus KIN website on a phone in bright sunlight at the pier.</p>","options":["High contrast and a layout that adapts to a small screen","Keyboard shortcuts","A recording-studio dark theme"],"correct":0,"why":"Outdoor glare tests contrast, and a web page must adapt to a phone screen for an untrained visitor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting and device check (7)","q":"<p>A Grade 1 child uses a tablet reading game at a Castries primary school.</p>","options":["Deep menus","Long typed answers","Touch GUI with large targets that is easy to learn"],"correct":2,"why":"Tablets are very learnable through direct manipulation, as long as touch targets are large.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion matters most here? (1)","q":"<p>For each setting, choose the evaluation principle that should carry the MOST weight.</p><p>A self-service ticket kiosk at the cruise pier used once by each visitor.</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Alignment","Ease of use for experts"],"correct":0,"why":"One-time public users have no training, so learnability decides success.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion matters most here? (2)","q":"<p>A pharmacy dispensing screen where a wrong dose could harm a patient.</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Use of colour for branding","Ease of use with error prevention"],"correct":2,"why":"Error prevention (confirmation, validation) is part of ease of use and is critical here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion matters most here? (3)","q":"<p>A security guard switching between 24 camera feeds during an incident.</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Ease of navigation","Use of colour"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving instantly between views is navigation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion matters most here? (4)","q":"<p>A long online scholarship application form with 30 fields.</p>","options":["Speech","Use of colour","Alignment"],"correct":2,"why":"Aligned fields and grouped sections make long forms easier to complete accurately.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion matters most here? (5)","q":"<p>A traffic dashboard where operators must spot red, amber and green road status at a glance.</p>","options":["Ease of learning","Alignment","Ease of navigation","Use of colour"],"correct":3,"why":"Colour carries the status here, so contrast, consistency and backup labels are essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the true evaluation points","q":"<p>A student evaluated the Frederick Street Jewels website for cruise passengers shopping on their phones. Some lines are proper evaluation points; others are just description or opinion.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a genuine evaluation point (criterion + evidence + effect on the user).</p>","options":["4. I like the website because it looks expensive.","1. The navigation bar stays at the top of every page, so shoppers always know how to return to the catalogue.","2. The website has a picture of a diamond ring.","3. Gold text on a white background has low contrast, so shoppers outdoors on the pier struggle to read prices.","6. The website was made in 2024.","5. The checkout form aligns all labels and fields in one column, so users complete it quickly with fewer missed fields.","7. Product categories use clear words and icons, so first-time visitors can find watches without help, which aids ease of learning."],"correct":[1,3,5,6],"why":"Correct. Each chosen line names a criterion, gives evidence and states the effect on users. The others are description or personal opinion."},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (1)","q":"<p>An IT professional works in one particular IT job role with the comprehensive skills for it. Anyone else using a system is an end user, even if they are \"good with computers\". Sort each person by the role they are playing in the situation described.</p><p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>Rhea, a trained network engineer, keeps the firewall and Wi-Fi running at a Grace Bay hotel in Providenciales.</b></p>","options":["End user","IT professional"],"correct":1,"why":"A defined IT job role with specialist training: IT professional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (2)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>The same web designer who builds hotel websites books a doctor's appointment on the Island Health portal.</b></p>","options":["IT professional","End user"],"correct":1,"why":"Here he is using someone else's system for a personal task, so he is an end user (probably a novice on that portal).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (3)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>Jerome, a teenager who games and builds PCs as a hobby, uses the school's learning platform to submit homework.</b></p>","options":["IT professional","End user"],"correct":1,"why":"Being good with computers is not a profession. He is using the system for his own task: end user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (4)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>A database administrator at a Linden credit union sets up nightly backups of member records.</b></p>","options":["IT professional","End user"],"correct":0,"why":"Protecting data is the DBA's professional role.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (5)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>A cashier at a Regent Street jewellery store processes sales on the POS.</b></p>","options":["End user","IT professional"],"correct":0,"why":"She uses the system to do her job: end user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (6)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>A systems analyst with a degree in information systems writes the requirements for a new ferry booking system.</b></p>","options":["IT professional","End user"],"correct":0,"why":"Special education and one particular IT role: IT professional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (7)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>A help-desk technician resets a teacher's password at Calabash Point Secondary.</b></p>","options":["End user","IT professional"],"correct":1,"why":"Supporting other users' systems is an IT professional role.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"In this role: IT professional or end user? (8)","q":"<p>In this situation, is the person acting as an IT professional or an end user?</p><p><b>A nurse who is very confident with apps enters patient notes on the clinic's records system.</b></p>","options":["End user","IT professional"],"correct":0,"why":"Confidence does not make her an IT professional; she is an (expert) end user.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the IT role to the task","q":"<p>Every task below comes from an organisation in Grenada. Match each IT professional to the task they would carry out.</p>","left":["Database Administrator","Applications Developer","Information Security Specialist","Information Systems Manager","IT Consultant","IT Technical Support Officer","Network Engineer","Systems Analyst","Systems Developer","Web Designer","Full-stack Web Developer"],"right":["Makes sure the Island Health Clinic's patient records load quickly, cannot be altered without authority and are backed up nightly.","Plans how a jewellery store's product pages look and how shoppers move through the site.","Turns the analyst's specification into working code for a mobile banking app.","Answers a call from a teacher whose printer will not work and fixes it in person.","Traces how a phishing attacker got into Bayside Shipping's email and closes the gap.","Approves the purchase of new servers, sets the backup policy and leads the technicians and DBAs.","Is brought in from outside to recommend a booking system to a Grand Anse villa-rental company and train its staff.","Writes diagnostic programs to find why the stock system keeps crashing, fixes it and recommends upgrades.","Keeps a St. George’s store's computer, voice and firewall networks up during cruise season.","Builds both the shopping cart in the browser and its link to the stock database on the server.","Designs and costs a new tracking system and acts as the go-between for managers and programmers."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,6,3,8,10,7,1,9]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which IT role?","q":"<p>The San Fernando Logistics needs someone to work out what a new tracking system must do, estimate what it will cost, and act as the go-between for the managers and the programmers. Which IT professional should it hire?</p>","options":["IT consultant","Information systems manager","Applications developer","Systems analyst"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A consultant advises on using IT to meet business objectives, but designing and costing a specific solution and liaising with developers is the analyst's core role.","IS managers oversee installation, backups, purchasing and staff; they do not normally design individual solutions.","Applications developers turn requirements into code; they work to the analyst's specification rather than producing it.","Correct. Systems analysts design and cost IT solutions and liaise between client and developers."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (1)","q":"<p>All of these people are end users. Sort each one by whether they are acting as an expert user or a novice user of the system described, remembering that expertise is relative to each system.</p><p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>An accounts clerk at Bayside Shipping enters invoices with keyboard shortcuts after eight years on the same package.</b></p>","options":["Expert user","Novice user"],"correct":0,"why":"Rich interaction knowledge and shortcuts: expert user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (2)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A first-year Sandbox Tree College student logs into the student portal for the first time.</b></p>","options":["Novice user","Expert user"],"correct":0,"why":"New to the system and naïve about it: novice user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (3)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A grandmother in Dominica tries online banking for the first time and needs prompts on every screen.</b></p>","options":["Novice user","Expert user"],"correct":0,"why":"Little experience and needs guidance: novice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (4)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A Piarco airport check-in agent customises her screen layout and fixes minor glitches herself.</b></p>","options":["Novice user","Expert user"],"correct":1,"why":"Customising and solving minor problems are signs of an expert user.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (5)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A telecoms company router expert opens the Island Health patient system for the first time.</b></p>","options":["Expert user","Novice user"],"correct":1,"why":"Expertise is relative to the system: on this system he is a novice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (6)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A nurse with ten years on the records system knows the clinic's procedures and the software's shortcuts.</b></p>","options":["Expert user","Novice user"],"correct":0,"why":"Task, domain and interaction knowledge together: expert.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Expert or novice end user? (7)","q":"<p>Is this end user an expert or a novice on this system?</p><p><b>A cruise visitor using a bus route app once to reach Gros Islet.</b></p>","options":["Novice user","Expert user"],"correct":0,"why":"Occasional, one-time use: novice.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the person to the user role","q":"<p>Match each person to the user role that best describes them.</p>","left":["Keisha writes and tests code for a new hotel reservation system.","Marco manages servers, accounts and Wi-Fi at a Frigate Bay school.","Ana has used the airline check-in system daily for ten years and uses only keyboard shortcuts.","Mr. Richardson opens the government tax portal for the first time.","Devon studies a pharmacy's workflow and writes the requirements for a new stock system.","Priya answers staff calls when their printers and email stop working."],"right":["IT professional: programmer","IT professional: network administrator","IT professional: technical support","End user: expert user","IT professional: systems analyst","End user: novice user"],"correct":[0,1,3,5,4,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Designing for both kinds of user","q":"<p>The Caribbean Credit Union is redesigning its teller software. Tellers range from new hires to staff with 15 years' experience. Which design BEST serves both groups?</p>","options":["A step-by-step wizard for every transaction that cannot be skipped.","A command-line interface only, because it is fastest.","A speech interface so tellers can talk to the system.","A menu-driven GUI with clear labels and help, plus keyboard shortcuts and customisable screens."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Good for novices, but experts would be slowed by forced steps on every transaction.","Fast for experts, but new hires would need long training and make many errors.","A busy banking hall is noisy and transactions are confidential, so speech is a poor main interface here.","Correct. Menus and help support novices; shortcuts and customisation give experts speed."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (1)","q":"<p>Places around Dominica are adapting their systems for different users. Sort each adaptation into the kind of interface it is: a non-visual interface, a sensor, or a built-in accessibility feature.</p><p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>A telephone line at an utility office says \"Press 1 or say 'billing'\".</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface","Sensor"],"correct":1,"why":"A spoken voice menu works without a screen: non-visual (audio).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>A Braille display lets a blind clerk at a Roseau law firm read emails by touch.</b></p>","options":["Sensor","Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface"],"correct":2,"why":"Tactile output without a screen: non-visual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>The raised dot on the 5 key of an ATM on Bridge Street.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Sensor","Non-visual interface"],"correct":2,"why":"A tactile marker lets users find keys without seeing: non-visual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>Motion detectors open the doors automatically for travellers with luggage at Piarco Airport.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface","Sensor"],"correct":2,"why":"It detects a physical change (movement) as input: sensor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>A smartwatch accelerometer detects that an older resident has fallen and alerts family.</b></p>","options":["Sensor","Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface"],"correct":0,"why":"An accelerometer detects movement: sensor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>An eye-tracking camera lets a person with paralysis move a pointer by looking.</b></p>","options":["Non-visual interface","Accessibility feature","Sensor"],"correct":2,"why":"It senses eye movement as input: sensor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>A student switches on high-contrast mode and larger text in the phone's settings.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Sensor","Non-visual interface"],"correct":0,"why":"Built-in settings for different abilities: accessibility features.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>Sticky keys let a user with one working hand press Ctrl+S one key at a time.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface","Sensor"],"correct":0,"why":"A built-in operating-system setting: accessibility feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual, sensor or accessibility feature? (9)","q":"<p>What kind of adaptive interface is this?</p><p><b>Automatic captions are turned on for a recorded Flamboyant Heights College lecture.</b></p>","options":["Accessibility feature","Non-visual interface","Sensor"],"correct":0,"why":"Captions are a built-in accessibility feature for deaf users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (1)","q":"<p>A Guyana government services portal is adding features. Sort each adaptation by the user characteristic it MAINLY addresses.</p><p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>Picture-based buttons with sound for a children's library catalogue.</b></p>","options":["Education","Cultural differences","Age","Disability"],"correct":2,"why":"Designed for young children who may not read well.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (2)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>A \"large mode\" with bigger text and slower screen time-outs for retirees.</b></p>","options":["Cultural differences","Disability","Age","Education"],"correct":2,"why":"Addresses age-related changes in eyesight and speed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (3)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>Replacing jargon such as \"authenticate credentials\" with \"sign in\".</b></p>","options":["Education","Cultural differences","Disability","Age"],"correct":0,"why":"Plain language helps users with lower literacy or technical knowledge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (4)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>A step-by-step wizard with examples for users who have never filled an online form.</b></p>","options":["Disability","Age","Cultural differences","Education"],"correct":3,"why":"Supports users with limited computer education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (5)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>Alternative text on every image so screen readers can describe them.</b></p>","options":["Education","Age","Cultural differences","Disability"],"correct":3,"why":"Supports blind and low-vision users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (6)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>Captions on all video guides.</b></p>","options":["Education","Disability","Age","Cultural differences"],"correct":1,"why":"Supports deaf and hard-of-hearing users.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (7)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>A language selector offering English, Dutch, Spanish and French.</b></p>","options":["Education","Disability","Age","Cultural differences"],"correct":3,"why":"Supports speakers of different languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic does this adaptation serve? (8)","q":"<p>Which user characteristic is this adaptation mainly for?</p><p><b>Showing dates as \"3 Apr 2026\" instead of 03/04/2026.</b></p>","options":["Cultural differences","Age","Education","Disability"],"correct":0,"why":"Avoids confusion between different cultural date formats.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Non-visual or sensor?","q":"<p>The Gros Islet Hotel in Castries installs new room controls so that guests of all abilities, including blind guests, can use them. Which feature is a NON-VISUAL interface?</p>","options":["A touchscreen panel with large, high-contrast icons.","A website for booking spa treatments with alternative text on images.","A voice assistant that answers \"What time is breakfast?\" aloud and a light switch with a raised tactile marker.","A TV menu with a colour-blind-friendly palette."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This is still a visual interface, though an accessible one.","Alt text supports screen readers, but the website itself is a visual GUI; the screen reader is the non-visual layer.","Correct. Spoken output and touch markers allow interaction without seeing a screen.","The user still needs to see the menu, so it is visual."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Adapting a public kiosk (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising Island Health Services, which is placing self check-in kiosks in its clinics in Basseterre and Frigate Bay. Patients include older residents, visitors who speak little English, and people with disabilities.</p><p>The vendor's default kiosk has small text, English only and a long typed form. What do you recommend first?</p>","options":["Study the actual patients: their ages, languages, literacy and disabilities, then adapt the interface.","Install it as it is; patients will learn."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Adaptation starts with knowing the users' characteristics.","Ignoring user characteristics excludes many patients."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Adapting a public kiosk (step 2)","q":"<p>Your survey finds many older patients and many Spanish and French speakers. What changes do you make to the screen?</p>","options":["Keep English only but add bright colours to make it friendlier.","Large high-contrast buttons, a language choice on the first screen, and touch-to-select menus instead of typing."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Colour does not solve a language barrier and may reduce contrast.","Strong. You addressed age, culture and education together."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Adapting a public kiosk (step 3)","q":"<p>A blind patient and a wheelchair user report problems. What do you add?</p>","options":["A sign saying \"Ask staff for help if you cannot use the kiosk\".","Audio guidance through a headphone jack with a tactile keypad, and a lower, tiltable screen within reach of a seated user."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This removes independence and privacy; the interface itself should adapt.","Excellent. Non-visual output, tactile input and physical access address both disabilities."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Colour, culture and accessibility","q":"<p>A Barbados tourism app shows hotel availability with green for \"available\" and red for \"fully booked\", with no other marker. Which evaluation is MOST complete?</p>","options":["It is fine, because red and green are universally understood.","It relies on colour alone, which fails users with red-green colour-vision deficiency, and colour meanings vary across cultures; adding icons and text labels such as \"Full\" fixes both.","It is an alignment problem because the colours are not lined up.","It should use more colours so each hotel has its own."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Colour meanings vary by culture, and red-green colour blindness is common.","Correct. It names the principle, two user groups affected, and a specific adaptation.","This is a use-of-colour and accessibility issue, not alignment.","More colours add clutter and do not fix the reliance on colour."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the feature to the user need","q":"<p>Match each adaptation or interface to the user it helps most.</p>","left":["Screen reader","Captions and transcripts","Eye-tracking sensor","Sticky keys","Vibration alert","Language selector and local date formats","Large-text mode with slower time-outs"],"right":["A blind accountant who needs on-screen text read aloud","A deaf student watching a recorded lecture","A person with paralysis who controls a pointer with eye movement","A visitor from Curaçao who prefers Dutch or Papiamento","A user who can press only one key at a time","A hard-of-hearing worker who needs to notice a new message","An older user with weaker eyesight and slower reactions"],"correct":[0,1,2,4,5,3,6]}]},{"id":"assess_3vk","title":"Module 2 · Unit 5 quiz: Networks: types, components and communication","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 5: Networks: types, components and communication. 68 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the network function to the situation","q":"<p>The Calabash Point Secondary IT club is making a poster on why organisations across Trinidad and Tobago build networks. Match each network function to the real-life situation that shows it.</p>","left":["Hardware sharing","Data and file sharing","Communication","Centralised management","Remote access","Software sharing"],"right":["Coralstone College lecturers hold a live online class with students in Tobago","Every pharmacist at the Seaside Pharmacy in Couva reads the same stock file on the server","One network-licensed accounting package serves every office PC at Bayside Traders","A Piarco airport manager checks flight data from home while a storm keeps her off the roads","The school technician pushes security updates and backups to every lab PC overnight","Thirty Academy lab PCs print to one laser printer instead of thirty separate printers"],"correct":[5,1,0,4,3,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hub or switch?","q":"<p>The Grand Anse Shipping office replaces the central device in its LAN. Staff notice that the network is faster and that a computer no longer receives data meant for other computers. Which change was MOST likely made?</p>","options":["A hub was replaced by a switch.","A switch was replaced by a hub.","A modem was replaced by a router.","A NIC was replaced by an access point."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. A switch forwards data only to the port of the destination MAC address, which cuts unnecessary traffic and improves security.","The opposite. A hub broadcasts data to every port, so every computer would receive everything.","A modem converts signals for the Internet line; it is not the central device that links PCs in a LAN.","A NIC sits in each computer and an access point provides wireless access; neither decides which computer receives data inside a wired LAN."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the device to its function","q":"<p>You are labelling the equipment cupboard in the Sea-Almond Secondary School computer lab. Match each device to what it does.</p>","left":["Network interface card (NIC)","Hub","Switch","Router","Modem","Wireless access point"],"right":["Forwards packets between different networks using IP addresses","Sends data only to the device with the matching MAC address","Converts digital signals to analogue and back again","Sends incoming data out of every port to all devices","Connects one computer to the network and holds its MAC address","Lets laptops and phones join the wired LAN by radio"],"correct":[4,3,1,0,2,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (1)","q":"<p>The Caribbean Cruise Services company in St. John’s runs several systems. Sort each one.</p><p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>Tourists anywhere in the world view the public tour timetable.</b></p>","options":["Extranet","Intranet","Internet"],"correct":2,"why":"Open to anyone: the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>Staff log in to read the employee handbook and apply for leave.</b></p>","options":["Extranet","Internet","Intranet"],"correct":2,"why":"Staff only: an intranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>Approved bus companies log in to see tomorrow's passenger numbers.</b></p>","options":["Internet","Extranet","Intranet"],"correct":1,"why":"Selected outside partners: an extranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>Managers share internal sales reports in a private web portal.</b></p>","options":["Internet","Extranet","Intranet"],"correct":2,"why":"Internal and private: an intranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>A travel agent in Barbados logs in to book group tours directly.</b></p>","options":["Extranet","Intranet","Internet"],"correct":0,"why":"An authorised outside partner: an extranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of network is being used?</p><p><b>The company posts a promotional video that anyone can watch.</b></p>","options":["Intranet","Extranet","Internet"],"correct":2,"why":"Public: the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Mobile networks and VPNs: match the feature","q":"<p>A technician from a BVI mobile provider is training new staff after Hurricane Irma repairs. Match each term to the situation that shows it.</p>","left":["Cell","Base station","Hand-over","2G","3G","4G (LTE)","5G","VPN"],"right":["A tourist streams a cruise ship webcam on her phone at broadband speed","A phone that can browse basic websites and make simple video calls","An encrypted tunnel from a home laptop to the office server over the Internet","Very high speed and very low delay for thousands of connected devices at once","An old handset that can make digital voice calls and send SMS texts only","The tower with antennas on a hill above Road Town that sends and receives phone signals","The area around Cane Garden Bay served by one base station","A taxi passenger's call moves from the Road Town tower to the Sea Cows Bay tower without dropping"],"correct":[6,5,7,4,1,0,3,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (1)","q":"<p>Read each situation and choose the type of network that BEST fits.</p><p>Twenty computers in one office at Sandbox Tree College share a printer.</p>","options":["MAN","LAN","WAN"],"correct":1,"why":"One building: a LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (2)","q":"<p>A bank links its branches in Jamaica, Curaçao and Trinidad.</p>","options":["MAN","WAN","LAN"],"correct":1,"why":"Several countries: a WAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (3)","q":"<p>A teacher at home opens the school server through an encrypted tunnel over the Internet.</p>","options":["VPN","Extranet","Intranet"],"correct":0,"why":"Encrypted remote access over a public network: a VPN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (4)","q":"<p>A taxi driver on the English Harbour road streams music through cell towers.</p>","options":["Intranet","LAN","Mobile network"],"correct":2,"why":"Cells and base stations: a mobile network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (5)","q":"<p>A fibre network connects government offices across the Castries area.</p>","options":["MAN","VPN","LAN"],"correct":0,"why":"A town or city: a MAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name that network (6)","q":"<p>Approved suppliers log in to a supermarket's private ordering portal.</p>","options":["Extranet","Intranet","Internet"],"correct":0,"why":"Trusted outsiders: an extranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (1)","q":"<p>You are auditing networks for small and large organisations around the Caribbean. Sort each description into the configuration it shows.</p><p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>Hummingbird Ridge High School students log in with school accounts that one administrator creates and controls.</b></p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client-server"],"correct":1,"why":"Central control of accounts by an administrator: client-server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (2)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>Three laptops at the Guavaberry Café share one printer with no dedicated server.</b></p>","options":["Client-server","Peer-to-peer"],"correct":1,"why":"Equal computers sharing directly with no server: peer-to-peer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (3)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>The Dominica Credit Union backs up every member record from one central machine each night.</b></p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client-server"],"correct":1,"why":"Central backups from a server: client-server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (4)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>At a tiny boutique in George Town, each PC's user decides which folders to share.</b></p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client-server"],"correct":0,"why":"Each user controls their own machine: peer-to-peer, with weaker security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (5)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>When the main server at the Frigate Bay Clinic failed, nobody could open patient files.</b></p>","options":["Client-server","Peer-to-peer"],"correct":0,"why":"A server failure stopping everyone is the classic client-server weakness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (6)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>Two freelance designers in English Harbour copy files directly between their computers to avoid buying a server.</b></p>","options":["Client-server","Peer-to-peer"],"correct":1,"why":"Low cost and no server: peer-to-peer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (7)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>Island Breeze Hotels group buys a network operating system and hires a skilled administrator.</b></p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client-server"],"correct":1,"why":"Higher cost for central management: client-server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Client-server or peer-to-peer? (8)","q":"<p>Which configuration does this describe?</p><p><b>Files at the Cayman Brac Dive Shop are scattered across four PCs, so backups are hard to organise.</b></p>","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client-server"],"correct":0,"why":"Scattered files and difficult backups: peer-to-peer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a configuration","q":"<p>The Dominica Credit Union has 45 staff, stores confidential member records and needs daily backups and controlled user permissions. Which configuration is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Peer-to-peer, because every user can manage their own files.","Client-server, because it never stops working if the server fails.","Client-server, because a server allows central control of security, permissions and backups.","Peer-to-peer, because it is cheaper to install."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Letting each user control their own files is exactly what makes P2P risky for confidential data.","The right configuration but the wrong reason: a server failure stops services for everyone.","Correct. Central administration suits a larger organisation with confidential records.","Cost is not the priority here. P2P gives weak central control over confidential data and backups."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (1)","q":"<p>You are a network consultant taking calls from clients across the Caribbean. For each situation, choose the BEST answer.</p><p>A cruise terminal office at the Bridgetown Port will keep adding check-in desks and wants one broken cable to affect only one desk.</p>","options":["Bus","Star","Ring"],"correct":1,"why":"Star: each desk has its own cable to the switch, so one fault affects one desk and expansion is easy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (2)","q":"<p>A provider links towers across the island and wants traffic to take another route if hurricane damage cuts one link.</p>","options":["Partial mesh","Bus","Star"],"correct":0,"why":"Mesh gives alternative routes, so one cut link does not isolate a district.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (3)","q":"<p>A one-day event in Frigate Bay needs four laptops linked with the least cable and lowest cost, accepting that a backbone break stops everything.</p>","options":["Star","Full mesh","Bus"],"correct":2,"why":"Bus uses the least cable and is cheapest for a few devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (4)","q":"<p>A network must give orderly data flow with no collisions under heavy load, accepting that one failed node could stop the loop.</p>","options":["Ring","Mesh","Star"],"correct":0,"why":"Ring: data passes in order around the loop with no collisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (5)","q":"<p>The Caribbean Union Bank wants a full mesh linking its 5 branches. How many links are needed?</p>","options":["20","5","10"],"correct":2,"why":"n(n-1)/2 = 5 × 4 ÷ 2 = 10. Twenty forgets to divide by 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Recommend the topology (6)","q":"<p>Every PC in the Georgetown Law Office star LAN lost its connection at the same moment. What MOST likely failed?</p>","options":["One PC's cable","The central switch","A terminator"],"correct":1,"why":"The central switch is the single point of failure in a star.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the topology to the statement","q":"<p>A technician at the Gros Islet Tech Hub describes five networks. Match each statement to the topology.</p>","left":["Bus","Star","Ring","Full mesh","Hybrid (tree)"],"right":["Several star networks joined to one backbone","Every node is linked directly to every other node","One backbone cable with a terminator at each end","Each computer has its own cable to a central switch","Data passes from node to node around a closed loop"],"correct":[2,3,4,1,0]},{"t":"match","title":"Pick the right medium for the job","q":"<p>A St. George's installation company has nine jobs this week. Match each transmission medium or service to the job it suits best.</p>","left":["Twisted pair (Cat6)","Coaxial cable","Fibre-optic cable","Microwave","Satellite","Infrared","Bluetooth","Wi-Fi","Hotspot"],"right":["Free public wireless Internet for travellers in the Maurice Bishop International Airport departure lounge","Staff laptops connecting by radio to an access point at the Bayside Traders office","Wireless earbuds paired to a student's phone","A hotel room TV remote that works only when pointed at the set","Internet for a yacht crossing open sea far from any cell tower","A line-of-sight dish link between two buildings on facing hills","A submarine link carrying Internet traffic between Grenada and neighbouring islands","Bringing cable TV and cable broadband into a home in Grand Anse","Connecting 25 classroom PCs to a switch in the same Bayleaf Hill Secondary lab"],"correct":[8,7,6,5,4,3,2,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (1)","q":"<p>Saba Eco Lodge is choosing transmission media. Sort each statement into the medium type it describes.</p><p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Pulses of light travel through glass strands.</b></p>","options":["Wired (guided)","Wireless (unguided)"],"correct":0,"why":"Fibre-optic cable is a wired, guided medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Guests move freely around the lodge with their phones online.</b></p>","options":["Wired (guided)","Wireless (unguided)"],"correct":1,"why":"Mobility is the key advantage of wireless.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Copper pairs are twisted to reduce interference.</b></p>","options":["Wired (guided)","Wireless (unguided)"],"correct":0,"why":"Twisted pair is a wired medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Signals are relayed through a satellite for a remote site.</b></p>","options":["Wireless (unguided)","Wired (guided)"],"correct":0,"why":"Satellite links are wireless.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Someone must physically connect to the cable to intercept data.</b></p>","options":["Wireless (unguided)","Wired (guided)"],"correct":1,"why":"Physical access is needed, which makes wired media more secure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Thick stone walls weaken the signal.</b></p>","options":["Wireless (unguided)","Wired (guided)"],"correct":0,"why":"Wireless signals are affected by walls and distance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>A central copper core is wrapped in a metal shield.</b></p>","options":["Wired (guided)","Wireless (unguided)"],"correct":0,"why":"That is coaxial cable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe wired or wireless transmission?</p><p><b>Point-to-point dishes need a clear line of sight.</b></p>","options":["Wireless (unguided)","Wired (guided)"],"correct":0,"why":"Microwave links are wireless.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the good security practice","q":"<p>The St. George’s Dental Clinic wrote this list of network practices. Some protect the network; some put it at risk.</p><p>Select EVERY line that IMPROVES network security.</p>","options":["2. The Wi-Fi network is open with no password so patients can connect easily.","1. A hardware firewall sits between the clinic LAN and the Internet.","5. Staff working from home connect through an encrypted VPN.","3. Each receptionist has their own user ID and a strong password.","7. Everyone shares one login so nobody forgets it.","6. The server is kept in a locked room.","4. The router still uses the default admin password from the box."],"correct":[1,2,3,5],"why":"Correct. Firewall, individual logins, VPN and physical security all protect the network. Open Wi-Fi, default passwords and shared logins are risks."},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting up the café network (step 1)","q":"<p>You are advising the Boardwalk Bean Café in Bridgetown, which wants to offer a free customer hotspot and also runs its till and stock system on the same premises. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The owner plans to connect the tills, the office PC and customers' phones all to one open Wi-Fi network. What do you advise first?</p>","options":["Separate the networks: a password-protected staff network for the tills and office, and a separate guest hotspot.","Keep one open network; it is simpler for everybody."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Customers should never share a network with the business systems.","An open shared network lets any customer reach the tills and see unencrypted traffic."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting up the café network (step 2)","q":"<p>The router connects the café to the Internet. Which protection do you switch on at the router?</p>","options":["The router's firewall, with rules that block unrequested incoming connections, and a new strong admin password.","Nothing. A firewall slows things down."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong choice. The firewall filters traffic and the new password stops intruders changing settings.","Without a firewall the internal network is exposed to the Internet."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Setting up the café network (step 3)","q":"<p>The manager wants to check stock figures from home at night. How should she connect?</p>","options":["Through a VPN that encrypts her connection to the café network.","Open the stock system directly to the Internet with no login so it is quick."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. A VPN gives secure remote access over the public Internet.","This exposes confidential business data to anyone online."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (1)","q":"<p>Follow a contract sent by the Gros Islet Realty to a client in Curaçao, then answer each question about the communication.</p><p>Ana at the Gros Islet Realty emails a sales contract from her laptop. In this communication her laptop is the...</p>","options":["Sender","Transmission medium","Receiver"],"correct":0,"why":"The device that creates and sends the message is the sender.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (2)","q":"<p>The client's computer in Curaçao accepts the email and opens the contract. It is the...</p>","options":["Sender","Receiver","Modem"],"correct":1,"why":"The device that accepts and interprets the message is the receiver.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (3)","q":"<p>A home modem in Gros Islet varies the frequency of a carrier wave so digital data can travel along the telephone line. This is...</p>","options":["Modulation","Bandwidth","Demodulation"],"correct":0,"why":"Changing a carrier wave to carry the data is modulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (4)","q":"<p>At the far end, the modem recovers the digital data from the analogue wave. This is...</p>","options":["Modulation","Simplex","Demodulation"],"correct":2,"why":"Recovering the data at the receiver is demodulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (5)","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College student downloads a 40 MB lecture video on a 10 Mbps link. What is the minimum time?</p>","options":["400 seconds","4 seconds","32 seconds"],"correct":2,"why":"40 MB × 8 = 320 megabits; 320 ÷ 10 = 32 seconds. Four seconds forgets to convert bytes to bits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (6)","q":"<p>The English Harbour Tours office upgrades its link from 10 Mbps to 100 Mbps so more staff can stream at once. What has increased?</p>","options":["Modulation","Topology","Bandwidth"],"correct":2,"why":"Bandwidth is the channel's capacity in bits per second.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sender, receiver, modulation or bandwidth? (7)","q":"<p>Ana's laptop and the client's computer both follow the same agreed rules so the email can be understood. These rules are a...</p>","options":["Protocol","Carrier wave","Terminator"],"correct":0,"why":"Agreed rules for communication are protocols.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which communication mode? (1)","q":"<p>Choose the communication mode for each situation.</p><p>A Guyanese radio station broadcasts the news to listeners.</p>","options":["Half duplex","Full duplex","Simplex"],"correct":2,"why":"Listeners cannot reply on the same channel: simplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which communication mode? (2)","q":"<p>Port crew use two-way radios: one speaks while the other waits.</p>","options":["Full duplex","Simplex","Half duplex"],"correct":2,"why":"Both directions but one at a time: half duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which communication mode? (3)","q":"<p>A student in Saba talks to her teacher on a phone call; both can speak at once.</p>","options":["Simplex","Full duplex","Half duplex"],"correct":1,"why":"Simultaneous two-way: full duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which communication mode? (4)","q":"<p>A keyboard sends key presses to the computer.</p>","options":["Full duplex","Half duplex","Simplex"],"correct":2,"why":"One direction only: simplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which communication mode? (5)","q":"<p>A videoconference between campuses in Barbados and Jamaica.</p>","options":["Simplex","Half duplex","Full duplex"],"correct":2,"why":"Participants see and hear each other at the same time: full duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"The journey of an email over a phone line","q":"<p>A student at home in Gros Islet sends an assignment by email through a modem on a telephone line. Put the stages in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The sender's computer produces the email as digital data.","The sender's modem modulates the digital data onto an analogue carrier wave.","The analogue signal travels along the transmission medium.","The receiving modem demodulates the signal back into digital data.","The receiver's computer accepts and interprets the digital data."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (1)","q":"<p>The human resources office of a regional company based in The Valley, Anguilla, is reviewing how staff work and meet. Sort each situation.</p><p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>A claims officer at the Anguilla Insurance Agency works from home three days a week, logging in over a VPN.</b></p>","options":["Videoconferencing","Teleconferencing","Telecommuting"],"correct":2,"why":"Working away from the office through the network: telecommuting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (2)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>Managers in St. Kitts, Antigua and Guyana dial in to an audio-only conference call.</b></p>","options":["Teleconferencing","Telecommuting","Videoconferencing"],"correct":0,"why":"A meeting by audio link: teleconferencing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (3)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>A guest lecturer in Curaçao teaches a Sandbox Tree College class live; students see and hear her.</b></p>","options":["Videoconferencing","Telecommuting","Teleconferencing"],"correct":0,"why":"Live video and audio between locations: videoconferencing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (4)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>After Hurricane Irma closed its office, staff at a firm in The Valley kept working from home by email and VPN.</b></p>","options":["Videoconferencing","Telecommuting","Teleconferencing"],"correct":1,"why":"Continuing normal work from home: telecommuting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (5)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>Hotel managers on two islands switch on webcams and share screens in their weekly meeting.</b></p>","options":["Videoconferencing","Telecommuting","Teleconferencing"],"correct":0,"why":"Webcams and live pictures: videoconferencing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (6)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>A speakerphone in the boardroom links three branch heads by voice only.</b></p>","options":["Teleconferencing","Videoconferencing","Telecommuting"],"correct":0,"why":"Voice only: teleconferencing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (7)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>Needs webcams, microphones, software and high bandwidth in full duplex.</b></p>","options":["Teleconferencing","Videoconferencing","Telecommuting"],"correct":1,"why":"Those are the requirements for videoconferencing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Telecommuting, teleconferencing or videoconferencing? (8)","q":"<p>Which network application is this?</p><p><b>Replaces the daily drive through Kingston traffic with a network connection.</b></p>","options":["Teleconferencing","Telecommuting","Videoconferencing"],"correct":1,"why":"Replacing the commute: telecommuting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Working from a distance","q":"<p>Marisol works for the Cayman Islands Insurance Agency. Three days a week she works at home, logging in to the office system over a VPN and sending reports by email. This arrangement is BEST described as</p>","options":["an extranet","telecommuting","teleconferencing","videoconferencing"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An extranet gives outside partners access. Marisol is an employee working remotely.","Correct. Working away from the office and staying connected by network is telecommuting.","Teleconferencing is a meeting by telephone or audio link, not a way of working.","Videoconferencing is a live video meeting. Marisol is doing her normal work from home."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bandwidth and modulation","q":"<p>At Breadfruit Valley Secondary, video lessons freeze whenever a whole class streams at once, although the network cabling and devices are working correctly. Which network characteristic is MOST likely insufficient?</p>","options":["Simplex transmission","Topology","Modulation","Bandwidth"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Streaming to students is effectively one-way already; the mode is not the cause.","The devices and cabling work correctly. The issue appears only under heavy load.","Modulation is how data is placed on a carrier wave. It does not set how many users the link can serve.","Correct. Bandwidth is the channel's capacity; many simultaneous video streams can exceed it."]}]},{"id":"assess_45k","title":"Module 2 · Unit 6 quiz: Networking standards and designing simple networks","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 6: Networking standards and designing simple networks. 68 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (1)","q":"<p>Mr. Hodge, a network technician at Breadfruit Valley Secondary, is explaining to a Form 6 class why networks need standards and layered models. Answer each of his questions.</p><p>A student's Dell laptop connects without any problem to the Huawei Wi-Fi router in the school library. Which idea makes this possible?</p>","options":["Hiding the SSID","Encapsulation","Interoperability through agreed standards"],"correct":2,"why":"Both makers follow the same published standards (such as Wi-Fi, IEEE 802.11), so their equipment works together: interoperability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (2)","q":"<p>The computer lab moves from Ethernet cables to Wi-Fi, yet every web browser still uses HTTP exactly as before. Which benefit of a layered model does this show?</p>","options":["Wi-Fi removes the need for protocols","Every layer must be rewritten when one changes","Each layer can change without rewriting the others"],"correct":2,"why":"Only the lowest layer changed; HTTP at the top was untouched. Layers are independent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (3)","q":"<p>When the Internet drops in the staff room, Mr. Hodge checks the cable first, then the IP settings, then the browser. Which benefit of layering is he using?</p>","options":["It makes the connection faster","It divides the problem so each layer can be checked on its own","It encrypts the traffic"],"correct":1,"why":"Layering divides communication into separate jobs, so a technician can troubleshoot one layer at a time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (4)","q":"<p>HTTP sets out exactly how a browser at the Bay Times newsroom requests a page and how the server must reply. HTTP is best described as a...</p>","options":["network diagram","protocol","MAC address"],"correct":1,"why":"A protocol is a set of rules for formatting, sending, receiving and acknowledging data. HTTP is a protocol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (5)","q":"<p>Ethernet was formally agreed and published by the IEEE, so any manufacturer can build network cards that work with everyone else’s. This makes Ethernet a...</p>","options":["standard","firewall","session"],"correct":0,"why":"A standard is a protocol or specification that has been formally agreed and published.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standards and layers in action (6)","q":"<p>As an email travels down the layers on a teacher’s laptop in Port of Spain, each layer adds its own header to the data. What is this process called?</p>","options":["Routing","Compression","Encapsulation"],"correct":2,"why":"Adding a header at each layer on the way down is encapsulation; removing them on the way up is de-encapsulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (1)","q":"<p>Follow one day of network activity across St. Kitts. For each event, choose the OSI layer that is doing the job.</p><p>A reporter at the Bay Times on Fort Street in Basseterre presses Send, and her email program hands the story to SMTP.</p>","options":["Network","Session","Application"],"correct":2,"why":"SMTP is an Application-layer protocol: it provides the email service to the user’s program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (2)","q":"<p>Before a Coralstone College student’s photo is sent, it is compressed to JPEG and encrypted with TLS.</p>","options":["Presentation","Session","Data Link"],"correct":0,"why":"Compression and encryption change the format of the data: Presentation layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (3)","q":"<p>A 600 MB video upload from Frigate Bay drops halfway when the Wi-Fi fails, then resumes from the last checkpoint instead of starting again.</p>","options":["Session","Physical","Transport"],"correct":0,"why":"Checkpoints (synchronisation) and managing the conversation are Session-layer jobs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (4)","q":"<p>A banking app at the Basseterre Credit Union splits data into numbered segments and sends them to port 443 using TCP.</p>","options":["Network","Transport","Application"],"correct":1,"why":"Segments, sequence numbers, port numbers and TCP all belong to the Transport layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (5)","q":"<p>A router at Caribbean Connect ISP reads the destination IP address of each packet and forwards it towards a server in Miami.</p>","options":["Data Link","Network","Transport"],"correct":1,"why":"IP addressing and routing of packets happen at the Network layer, where routers work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (6)","q":"<p>A switch in the Palmetto Point Secondary computer lab reads the MAC address on a frame and sends it out of one port only.</p>","options":["Physical","Data Link","Network"],"correct":1,"why":"Frames and MAC addresses belong to the Data Link layer, where switches work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which OSI layer is at work? (7)","q":"<p>Pulses of light carry 1s and 0s along the undersea fibre-optic cable that links the island to the rest of the world.</p>","options":["Data Link","Presentation","Physical"],"correct":2,"why":"Transmitting raw bits as signals on a medium is the Physical layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Stack the OSI layers","q":"<p>Build the OSI model from the <b>top</b> (Layer 7, nearest the user) to the <b>bottom</b> (Layer 1, nearest the cable).</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Application: network services for user programs (HTTP, SMTP, DNS)","Presentation: translation, encryption and compression","Session: opens, manages and closes conversations","Transport: end-to-end delivery with TCP or UDP","Network: IP addressing and routing of packets","Data Link: frames and MAC addresses on the local network","Physical: bits as signals on cables or radio waves"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the OSI layer to its job","q":"<p>Match each OSI layer to the function it performs.</p>","left":["Application","Presentation","Session","Transport","Network","Data Link","Physical"],"right":["Delivers frames using MAC addresses and detects errors","Translates, encrypts and compresses data","Provides email, web and file-transfer services to user programs","Sends bits as electrical, light or radio signals","Splits data into segments and uses port numbers for end-to-end delivery","Sets up, manages and ends a conversation, with checkpoints","Adds IP addresses and routes packets between networks"],"correct":[2,1,5,4,6,0,3]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the protocol to the job","q":"<p>The office at the Gros Islet Hardware uses many protocols every day. Match each protocol to the job it does there.</p>","left":["HTTPS","FTP","SMTP","IMAP","POP3","DNS","DHCP","SSH","UDP"],"right":["Downloads the clerk's email to one PC, usually removing it from the server","Uploads the updated shop website files to the web host","Loads the supplier's online catalogue securely, with the padlock showing in the browser","Turns the supplier's domain name into an IP address","Gives a new till PC its IP address, subnet mask and default gateway automatically","Sends the manager's order email to a supplier in Miami","Keeps the shop's email in step on the manager's phone and laptop","Lets the technician log in to the server remotely over an encrypted session","Carries the live video call with head office, without resending lost data"],"correct":[2,1,5,6,0,3,4,7,8]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (1)","q":"<p>The IT technician at Kralendijk Hardware is labelling a poster for staff training. Sort each protocol or job into its TCP/IP layer.</p><p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>HTTPS</b></p>","options":["Network Access","Transport","Application","Internet"],"correct":2,"why":"HTTPS delivers secure web pages to the browser: Application layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (2)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>DHCP gives a new laptop its IP address</b></p>","options":["Transport","Internet","Network Access","Application"],"correct":3,"why":"DHCP is an Application-layer service, even though it hands out IP settings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (3)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>IMAP</b></p>","options":["Transport","Network Access","Internet","Application"],"correct":3,"why":"IMAP retrieves and synchronises email: Application layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (4)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>UDP</b></p>","options":["Internet","Application","Network Access","Transport"],"correct":3,"why":"UDP is the connectionless Transport protocol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (5)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>Port numbers direct data to the right program</b></p>","options":["Transport","Network Access","Application","Internet"],"correct":0,"why":"Port numbers are a Transport-layer feature (port 443 for HTTPS).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (6)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>TCP three-way handshake</b></p>","options":["Transport","Application","Internet","Network Access"],"correct":0,"why":"TCP sets up a reliable connection: Transport layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (7)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>IPv6</b></p>","options":["Application","Transport","Network Access","Internet"],"correct":3,"why":"IP, version 4 or 6, is the Internet-layer protocol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (8)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>Routers choose a path for each packet</b></p>","options":["Network Access","Transport","Application","Internet"],"correct":3,"why":"Routing by IP address is the Internet layer’s job.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (9)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>Ethernet (IEEE 802.3)</b></p>","options":["Application","Internet","Network Access","Transport"],"correct":2,"why":"Ethernet frames and cabling belong to Network Access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which TCP/IP layer? (10)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer does this belong to?</p><p><b>Wi-Fi radio signals (IEEE 802.11)</b></p>","options":["Application","Internet","Transport","Network Access"],"correct":3,"why":"Wi-Fi moves frames over radio on the local network: Network Access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the transport protocol","q":"<p>The Arima Sports Network streams live football matches online. The engineers accept that a few frames of video may be lost, but they cannot accept delays caused by resending data. Which protocol, at which layer, should carry the video stream?</p>","options":["FTP at the Application layer","IP at the Network layer","TCP at the Transport layer","UDP at the Transport layer"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["FTP transfers complete files reliably. It is not used for live streaming.","IP routes packets for every stream, but it does not decide whether lost data is resent. That decision is made at the Transport layer.","TCP is the right layer but the wrong protocol: it resends lost segments, which causes the very delays the engineers want to avoid.","Correct. UDP is connectionless and does not resend lost data, so it keeps live video flowing with minimal delay."]},{"t":"match","title":"Wrap the email: encapsulation","q":"<p>A lecturer at Flamboyant Heights College emails an assignment from her laptop on a wired connection. Put the encapsulation steps in order, from the moment she clicks Send to the moment the data leaves the laptop.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Application: the email program formats the message and hands it to SMTP (data).","Transport: TCP adds a header with port numbers and sequence numbers, making a segment.","Internet: IP adds the source and destination IP addresses, making a packet.","Network Access: Ethernet adds MAC addresses and an error-checking trailer, making a frame.","The frame is transmitted as bits (electrical signals) along the Cat6 Ethernet cable."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (1)","q":"<p>Kezia is making a revision poster that compares the two networking models for her Form 6 class at Breadfruit Valley Secondary. Sort each statement onto the right part of her poster.</p><p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Has seven layers</b></p>","options":["Both models","OSI model only","TCP/IP model only"],"correct":1,"why":"OSI has seven layers; TCP/IP has four.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (2)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Has separate Presentation and Session layers</b></p>","options":["Both models","OSI model only","TCP/IP model only"],"correct":1,"why":"Only OSI separates them; TCP/IP puts their jobs inside one Application layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (3)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Has separate Data Link and Physical layers</b></p>","options":["Both models","OSI model only","TCP/IP model only"],"correct":1,"why":"OSI separates them; TCP/IP combines them as Network Access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (4)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Designed by ISO as a general reference model</b></p>","options":["Both models","OSI model only","TCP/IP model only"],"correct":1,"why":"The OSI model was designed by ISO as a reference model before protocols were fixed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (5)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Has four layers</b></p>","options":["TCP/IP model only","OSI model only","Both models"],"correct":0,"why":"TCP/IP: Application, Transport, Internet, Network Access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (6)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Combines Data Link and Physical into a Network Access layer</b></p>","options":["Both models","TCP/IP model only","OSI model only"],"correct":1,"why":"Network Access is the TCP/IP layer that covers OSI Layers 1 and 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (7)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Grew from working ARPANET protocols and is the model the Internet runs on</b></p>","options":["Both models","OSI model only","TCP/IP model only"],"correct":2,"why":"TCP/IP is the practical model of the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (8)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Is layered, with each layer serving the layer above</b></p>","options":["OSI model only","TCP/IP model only","Both models"],"correct":2,"why":"Both models are layered.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (9)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Has a Transport layer that uses TCP or UDP</b></p>","options":["Both models","TCP/IP model only","OSI model only"],"correct":0,"why":"Both models have a Transport layer with TCP and UDP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"OSI, TCP/IP or both? (10)","q":"<p>Which model does this statement describe?</p><p><b>Uses encapsulation as data moves down the layers</b></p>","options":["TCP/IP model only","OSI model only","Both models"],"correct":2,"why":"Encapsulation happens in both models.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Kezia is revising for Paper 01 in the Mahoe Grove High School library. Answer each question she is stuck on.</p><p>Which OSI layer is responsible for compression and encryption?</p>","options":["Transport","Session","Presentation"],"correct":2,"why":"Presentation handles format: translation, encryption and compression.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>A switch forwards frames using...</p>","options":["port numbers","IP addresses","MAC addresses"],"correct":2,"why":"Switches are Layer 2 devices and read MAC addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Which TCP/IP layer corresponds to OSI Layers 5, 6 and 7?</p>","options":["Transport","Network Access","Application"],"correct":2,"why":"The single TCP/IP Application layer covers Application, Presentation and Session.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>The PDU at the OSI Network layer is called a...</p>","options":["packet","segment","frame"],"correct":0,"why":"Network = packet. Transport = segment. Data Link = frame.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Which protocol keeps email synchronised on the server across several devices?</p>","options":["SMTP","POP3","IMAP"],"correct":2,"why":"IMAP keeps mail on the server and syncs it; POP3 downloads to one device; SMTP sends.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Networking standards quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Which protocol automatically assigns an IP address to a phone joining a network?</p>","options":["DHCP","DNS","FTP"],"correct":0,"why":"DHCP assigns IP settings automatically. DNS converts names to IP addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the shape to the device","q":"<p>Rhea, a Form 6 student, is drawing a network diagram for a Virgin Gorda marina office. Match each standard shape or line style on her diagram to what it represents.</p>","left":["Cloud","Cylinder (or circle) with arrows","Rectangular box with arrows","Brick wall","Box with antennas","Solid line","Dashed or zig-zag line","Dashed box drawn around the office devices","Key (legend)"],"right":["Router","Wireless access point","Wireless link","The WAN or Internet supplied by the ISP","Switch","The LAN boundary","Firewall","Explains the symbols and line styles used","Ethernet cable (wired link)"],"correct":[3,0,4,6,1,8,2,5,7]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the component to its role","q":"<p>The English Harbour Dental Clinic is planning its first network. Match each component in the plan to its role.</p>","left":["Router","Switch","Hub","Firewall","Ethernet cable (Cat6)","SSID","WAN"],"right":["Connects the clinic LAN to the ISP and the Internet using IP addresses","Sends each frame only to the port of the destination device","The name the wireless network broadcasts","The wide area network, shown as a cloud on the diagram","Allows or blocks traffic between the Internet and the LAN using rules","Twisted-pair wired link with RJ45 connectors, up to 100 m","Repeats every signal out of all ports"],"correct":[0,1,6,4,5,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hub or switch?","q":"<p>The Oranjestad Print Shop has eight PCs connected to a hub. Staff complain that the network slows down whenever several people print large files, and the owner worries that any PC can capture the accounts clerk's data. Which change BEST solves both problems?</p>","options":["Use longer Ethernet cables","Replace the hub with a switch","Change the SSID of the Wi-Fi network","Replace the hub with a second hub"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Cable length does not change how a hub broadcasts. Runs beyond 100 m would actually make performance worse.","Correct. A switch sends each frame only to the destination port, so bandwidth is not shared by every device and other PCs do not receive the clerk’s traffic.","The PCs are wired to a hub. The SSID only names the wireless network and does not affect wired traffic.","A second hub still repeats every signal to every port, so both the slowdown and the privacy problem remain."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the design errors","q":"<p>A Form 6 student submitted this written description of her network diagram for the Cayman Islands Dive Centre. Some statements show design mistakes.</p><p>Select EVERY line that contains a design error.</p>","options":["5. The wireless access point is labelled \"SSID: DiveCentre, WPA3 with AES\".","8. The two offices are joined by a hub so that all traffic reaches every PC.","4. A 140 m Cat5e cable runs from the switch to the boathouse PC.","3. All five PCs connect to a central switch with Cat6 cable (star topology).","1. The Internet is drawn as a cloud labelled \"WAN\".","7. A dashed box around the office devices is labelled \"LAN\".","2. The firewall is placed after the switch, between the switch and one PC only.","6. The router is labelled \"admin password: admin (factory default)\"."],"correct":[1,2,6,7],"why":"Correct. The firewall must sit between the WAN and the whole LAN; copper Ethernet runs must stay within 100 m (use fibre or another switch); the default router password must be changed; and a switch, not a hub, should be used."},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (1)","q":"<p>Business owners across the island bring their Wi-Fi questions to the Kingston Tech Clinic. Choose the best answer for each one.</p><p>The Montego Bay Bakery has turned on MAC filtering and says it no longer needs Wi-Fi encryption. Why is this unsafe?</p>","options":["MAC filtering changes the SSID every day","MAC addresses are sent unencrypted and can be copied (spoofed), and filtering does not scramble traffic","MAC filtering makes the network too slow"],"correct":1,"why":"MAC filtering is only an extra layer: an attacker can spoof an allowed address, and without WPA2/WPA3 the traffic is readable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (2)","q":"<p>The router at the Negril Beach Bar offers \"WPA2-TKIP\" or \"WPA2-AES\". Which should the owner choose?</p>","options":["WPA2-AES","Neither: switch to WEP","WPA2-TKIP"],"correct":0,"why":"AES is the strong cipher; TKIP is outdated and WEP is broken.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (3)","q":"<p>An attacker records the WPA2 handshake outside the Ocho Rios Dive Shop and tries millions of password guesses at home. Which WPA3 feature stops this?</p>","options":["A hidden SSID","SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals)","MAC filtering"],"correct":1,"why":"SAE replaces the WPA2 handshake, so every guess must be a live attempt against the network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (4)","q":"<p>The Port Antonio Guesthouse has older tablets that cannot use WPA3 and new phones that can. Which router setting lets both connect securely?</p>","options":["WEP","WPA2/WPA3 transitional (mixed) mode","An open network with no key"],"correct":1,"why":"Transitional mode lets WPA3 devices use WPA3 while older devices still use WPA2 with AES.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (5)","q":"<p>The Mandeville Pharmacy uses WPA3. The owner asks what \"AES\" on the settings page means.</p>","options":["The symmetric encryption algorithm used inside WPA3 (and WPA2)","Another name for the network key","A list of allowed MAC addresses"],"correct":0,"why":"WPA2 and WPA3 are security protocols; AES is the symmetric encryption algorithm they use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wi-Fi security clinic (6)","q":"<p>An attacker discovers the Wi-Fi key of Frederick Street Boutique, which uses WPA3. Can she decrypt traffic she captured there last month?</p>","options":["Only if MAC filtering was off","No, because WPA3 provides forward secrecy","Yes, all of it"],"correct":1,"why":"Forward secrecy means a key discovered later cannot unlock traffic captured earlier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (1)","q":"<p>The Saba Island Library is securing its network. Sort each measure: does it mainly protect the <b>wired</b> network or the <b>wireless</b> network?</p><p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Lock the switch and router in a cabinet</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":1,"why":"Physical security of cabled equipment is a wired measure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (2)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Disable unused wall sockets at the switch</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":1,"why":"Disabling unused ports stops visitors plugging into the wired LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (3)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Port security: allow only known MAC addresses on each switch port</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":1,"why":"Switch port security controls cabled connections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (4)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Run cables inside trunking and ceilings</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":1,"why":"Protecting cables from tapping or damage is wired security.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (5)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Turn on WPA3</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":0,"why":"WPA3 encrypts and authenticates Wi-Fi connections.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (6)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Choose AES rather than TKIP on the access point</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":0,"why":"AES is the encryption cipher used by WPA2/WPA3 Wi-Fi.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (7)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Use a long network key and change it when staff leave</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":0,"why":"The network key controls who joins the Wi-Fi.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Wired or wireless security? (8)","q":"<p>Which part of the network does this measure mainly protect?</p><p><b>Rename the default SSID and avoid personal details</b></p>","options":["Wireless security","Wired security"],"correct":0,"why":"The SSID is the wireless network name.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"WPA2 or WPA3?","q":"<p>Statia Fuel Depot uses WPA2-Personal with an eight-character network key. An IT consultant recommends moving to WPA3-Personal. Which statement BEST explains the security benefit?</p>","options":["WPA3 replaces AES with a stronger cipher called TKIP.","WPA3 hides the SSID so attackers cannot find the network.","WPA3 automatically blocks all devices whose MAC address is not on a list.","WPA3 uses SAE, so an attacker cannot capture the handshake and guess the key offline."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["The reverse: TKIP is an outdated cipher. WPA3 still uses AES.","WPA3 does not hide the SSID, and hiding the SSID is not real security anyway.","That describes MAC filtering, a separate setting. It is not what WPA3 adds.","Correct. SAE forces every guess to be a live attempt against the network and adds forward secrecy, which protects even a modest key far better."]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the design process","q":"<p>Put the steps for designing a simple network in a sensible order, first to last.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Identify the users, devices and requirements (wired, wireless, guest access).","Choose the devices and media: router, firewall, switch, access point, Ethernet cables.","Draw the diagram with standard shapes, WAN cloud at the top and LAN boundary marked.","Label every device and connection and add a key.","Annotate security: SSID, WPA3/AES, network key, MAC filtering, router password, wired measures.","Check the design: all devices connected, firewall between WAN and LAN, cable runs under 100 m."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Design the network for Bay View Guesthouse (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the IT consultant for the Bay View Guesthouse near Gros Islet. The owner, Mr. Arrindell, needs a network for the reception PC, the office PC, a card machine and Wi-Fi for staff and guests.</p><p>Mr. Arrindell has an old 8-port hub and asks if he can use it to connect the reception and office PCs. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Use a switch instead: it sends frames only to the destination port, which is faster and keeps traffic private.","Use the hub; it does the same job and saves money."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. A switch is the correct central device for a modern star LAN.","A hub repeats every frame to every port, wasting bandwidth and exposing data."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Design the network for Bay View Guesthouse (step 2)","q":"<p>Next, he wants guests and staff to share one Wi-Fi network called \"Arrindell-Family-Home\" with no password, \"so guests don't complain\". What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Keep one open network but hide the SSID so only people who know the name can join.","Two SSIDs without personal details: a staff network with WPA3 (AES) and a strong network key, and a separate guest network with its own key, isolated from the staff LAN."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Hiding an SSID is not security, and an open network sends everything unencrypted.","Excellent. Separation, encryption and a sensible SSID protect the business."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Design the network for Bay View Guesthouse (step 3)","q":"<p>Finally, you log in to the router and find the administrator password is still \"admin\", and there is no firewall rule set. What do you do?</p>","options":["Leave it: guests only use the Wi-Fi, they will not try to log in to the router.","Change the router password to a strong one, enable the firewall to block unrequested inbound traffic, turn off remote administration, and add MAC filtering on the staff SSID."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Default router passwords are published online. Anyone on the network could change the settings.","Excellent. You closed the most common attack routes."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading a design","q":"<p>In a network diagram for Dominica Coffee Exporters, the designer draws a brick-wall shape between the router and the switch. What does this shape represent, and why is it placed there?</p>","options":["An Ethernet patch panel, placed there to organise cables","A wireless access point, placed there to broadcast the SSID","A hub, placed there to repeat signals to every PC","A firewall, placed there so all traffic between the WAN and the LAN is filtered"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A patch panel is not one of the standard shapes in the syllabus, and the brick wall is a security symbol.","An access point is drawn with antennas, and it connects to the switch on the LAN side.","A hub is drawn as a box with arrows, and it would not be placed between the router and the switch for protection.","Correct. The brick wall is the standard firewall symbol. Placing it at the WAN–LAN boundary means every packet entering or leaving the LAN is checked against its rules."]}]},{"id":"assess_4fo","title":"Module 2 · Unit 7 quiz: Configuring, troubleshooting and comparing components","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 7: Configuring, troubleshooting and comparing components. 71 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (1)","q":"<p>A technician is turning the network design for the Seaside Pharmacy in Frigate Bay into a working network. Sort each job she does into the physical half or the logical half of configuration.</p><p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She runs an Ethernet cable from the dispensary PC to a LAN port on the router.</b></p>","options":["Physical (devices and cables)","Logical (settings)"],"correct":0,"why":"Connecting a cable as the diagram shows is physical work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She plugs the cable from the ISP's fibre terminal into the router's WAN port.</b></p>","options":["Physical (devices and cables)","Logical (settings)"],"correct":0,"why":"Choosing and connecting the correct port is part of the physical set-up.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She types a new SSID, SP-Staff, into the router's management page.</b></p>","options":["Physical (devices and cables)","Logical (settings)"],"correct":1,"why":"Entering a setting in the management page is logical configuration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She chooses WPA3-Personal with AES in the wireless settings.</b></p>","options":["Logical (settings)","Physical (devices and cables)"],"correct":0,"why":"Security modes are settings, so this is logical configuration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She places the point-of-sale terminal and the printer exactly where the diagram shows them.</b></p>","options":["Logical (settings)","Physical (devices and cables)"],"correct":1,"why":"Placing devices to match the design is physical work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She switches on the router's firewall and saves the settings.</b></p>","options":["Logical (settings)","Physical (devices and cables)"],"correct":0,"why":"Turning on the firewall is done in the settings: logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She sets the DHCP range to 192.168.1.100 to 192.168.1.199 and reserves an address for the printer.</b></p>","options":["Logical (settings)","Physical (devices and cables)"],"correct":0,"why":"IP addressing is part of the logical half.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical step? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical configuration step?</p><p><b>She mounts the router in a locked cabinet behind the counter.</b></p>","options":["Physical (devices and cables)","Logical (settings)"],"correct":0,"why":"Positioning and securing the hardware is physical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (1)","q":"<p>A technician is describing the networks she has set up around the island. Sort each description into the topology it belongs to.</p><p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>A terminator is fitted at each end of the main cable.</b></p>","options":["Ring","Bus","Star"],"correct":1,"why":"Terminators at both ends of a single backbone are the signature of a bus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (2)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>Each PC is cabled to the next PC, and the last PC is cabled back to the first.</b></p>","options":["Star","Bus","Ring"],"correct":2,"why":"A closed loop of node-to-node links is a ring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (3)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>Every device has its own Ethernet cable running to one central hub.</b></p>","options":["Bus","Ring","Star"],"correct":2,"why":"All cables meeting at a central device makes a star.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (4)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>Data passes from node to node in one direction until it reaches its destination.</b></p>","options":["Ring","Star","Bus"],"correct":0,"why":"Data travelling round a loop through each node is a ring.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (5)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>If the central hub loses power, every device is cut off.</b></p>","options":["Star","Ring","Bus"],"correct":0,"why":"The central device is the single point of failure in a star.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (6)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>It uses the least cable because all devices share one backbone.</b></p>","options":["Star","Ring","Bus"],"correct":2,"why":"One shared backbone means least cable: a bus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (7)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>A damaged cable at one desk disconnects only that desk.</b></p>","options":["Ring","Star","Bus"],"correct":1,"why":"Separate cables isolate faults in a star.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Bus, ring or star? (8)","q":"<p>Which topology is being described?</p><p><b>A single break in the backbone cable stops the whole network.</b></p>","options":["Ring","Bus","Star"],"correct":1,"why":"Every device depends on the backbone in a bus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hub or router?","q":"<p>The San Pedro Tours office in Belize City has four PCs cabled to a hub. Staff can share files with each other, but none of the PCs can reach the Internet. The ISP's fibre terminal is working. Which device must be added and configured so that the LAN can reach the WAN?</p>","options":["A terminator at each end of the hub","A router, with its WAN port connected to the fibre terminal and a LAN port connected to the hub","A second hub, connected to the first hub","Longer Ethernet cables, so the PCs can reach the fibre terminal"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Terminators belong on a bus backbone. A hub in a star topology has no terminators.","Correct. A router forwards traffic between the LAN and the WAN, acting as the default gateway for the PCs.","A hub only repeats signals within the LAN. Adding another hub gives more ports but still no route to the Internet.","Cable length is not the problem. The PCs need a device that joins two different networks."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (1)","q":"<p>You are helping the Montego Bay Tours office on King Street, Kingston, wire up its new network. For each situation, choose the best device, port, cable or term.</p><p>The cable from the ISP's fibre terminal must go into the new router. Which port should it use?</p>","options":["the WAN (Internet) port","any free hub port","a LAN port"],"correct":0,"why":"The WAN port joins the router to the ISP; LAN ports are for local devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (2)","q":"<p>Six desk PCs need wired connections, but the router has only four LAN ports. What extends the star?</p>","options":["a terminator on the router","a second ISP line","a hub or switch cabled to one of the router's LAN ports"],"correct":2,"why":"A hub or switch gives more ports and connects to the router through one LAN port.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (3)","q":"<p>The manager does not want every booking PC to receive every other PC's traffic. Which central device is better?</p>","options":["a switch, because it sends data only to the intended port","a hub, because it repeats data to every port","a longer Ethernet cable"],"correct":0,"why":"A switch learns MAC addresses and forwards data only to the right port; a hub repeats to all.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (4)","q":"<p>The PC in the back store room would need a 130-metre cable run to reach the hub. What is the problem?</p>","options":["the PC needs its own WAN port","Ethernet cannot connect PCs to hubs","Ethernet runs should not exceed about 100 metres"],"correct":2,"why":"A single twisted-pair Ethernet run is limited to about 100 metres.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (5)","q":"<p>The network links only the PCs and printer inside the Albert Street office. What type of network is it?</p>","options":["a LAN","a ring","a WAN"],"correct":0,"why":"A network inside one building, owned by the organisation, is a LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pick the right device, port or cable (6)","q":"<p>ipconfig on a booking PC shows its default gateway as 192.168.1.1. Which device has that address?</p>","options":["the printer","the router","the ISP's fibre terminal"],"correct":1,"why":"The router's LAN address is the default gateway for every device on the LAN.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Configure the router in order","q":"<p>Kemar is configuring a new router for his family's snack shop on Broad Street. Put his steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Connect the ISP terminal to the WAN port and a laptop to a LAN port.","Power on and wait for steady power and Internet status lights.","Open the router's management page by typing its LAN IP address in a browser.","Log in and change the default router password.","Enter the WAN connection type supplied by the ISP.","Rename the SSID and set WPA3 with AES and a strong network key.","Enable the firewall, save the settings and reboot.","Test every device and document the settings."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (1)","q":"<p>The technician for the Bayside Family Clinic in Portsmouth is tightening security on the clinic's wired network. Sort each measure she takes.</p><p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>The router blocks all unrequested inbound connections from the Internet for every device on the LAN.</b></p>","options":["Software (host) firewall","Hardware (router) firewall","Other wired security measure"],"correct":1,"why":"Protecting the whole LAN at the boundary is the job of the hardware firewall in the router.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>Remote management of the router from the WAN is switched off on the router.</b></p>","options":["Other wired security measure","Hardware (router) firewall","Software (host) firewall"],"correct":1,"why":"This is part of configuring the router's firewall so outsiders cannot reach the management page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>Windows Defender Firewall on the reception PC blocks a games program from accepting incoming connections.</b></p>","options":["Software (host) firewall","Hardware (router) firewall","Other wired security measure"],"correct":0,"why":"A firewall installed on one computer, controlling its programs, is a software (host) firewall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>An allow rule on the records PC lets the records program receive data on TCP port 5050 from LAN addresses only.</b></p>","options":["Other wired security measure","Hardware (router) firewall","Software (host) firewall"],"correct":2,"why":"A rule for one program on one computer belongs to that host's software firewall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>The router and hub are moved into a locked cupboard.</b></p>","options":["Hardware (router) firewall","Other wired security measure","Software (host) firewall"],"correct":1,"why":"Locking equipment away is physical security, not a firewall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>The unused network socket in the waiting room is disconnected at the hub.</b></p>","options":["Software (host) firewall","Other wired security measure","Hardware (router) firewall"],"correct":1,"why":"Disabling unused ports stops a visitor plugging in a laptop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>The router's firmware is updated to the latest version.</b></p>","options":["Software (host) firewall","Hardware (router) firewall","Other wired security measure"],"correct":2,"why":"Up-to-date firmware closes known weaknesses in the router's own software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Firewall or other wired measure? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of security measure is this?</p><p><b>Only the clinic's own PCs, listed by MAC address, can receive an address on the wired ports.</b></p>","options":["Hardware (router) firewall","Other wired security measure","Software (host) firewall"],"correct":1,"why":"MAC filtering on wired ports admits only listed devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the security setting to its job","q":"<p>The Carriacou Ferry Desk office is securing its network. Match each setting to what it does.</p>","left":["Router password","Network key","AES","WPA3","MAC filtering","Firewall","SSID"],"right":["Allows or blocks traffic between LAN and WAN according to rules","Encrypts wireless data so intercepted frames cannot be read","Wireless security standard that resists offline password guessing","The name a wireless network broadcasts to nearby devices","Admits only devices whose hardware address is on an approved list","The passphrase a device must enter to join the Wi-Fi","Stops unauthorised people changing the router's settings"],"correct":[6,5,1,2,4,0,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Configure the clinic network (step 1)","q":"<p>You are configuring the network for the Bayside Family Clinic in San Fernando from the designer's diagram: a star LAN, a router to the ISP, staff Wi-Fi and a waiting-room guest Wi-Fi. Make the best decisions.</p><p>You have cabled everything. The router came with the admin login <i>admin / admin</i>. What do you do first in the management page?</p>","options":["Leave it; nobody outside will know the address of the management page.","Change the router password to a long, unique one."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Anyone on the Wi-Fi can type the gateway address. Default credentials are easily found online.","Right. Default admin passwords are publicly listed; changing it first protects every other setting."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Configure the clinic network (step 2)","q":"<p>Now the wireless settings. A few older tablets support only WPA2. Which security setting do you choose?</p>","options":["WPA2/WPA3 transitional mode with AES and a 16-character network key, with a plan to replace the old tablets.","Open network with no key, so the old tablets can connect easily."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good balance: modern devices use WPA3, old ones still work, and AES protects the data.","An open network sends patient data unencrypted to anyone in range."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Configure the clinic network (step 3)","q":"<p>Patients want Wi-Fi in the waiting room. What do you set up?</p>","options":["A separate guest SSID, isolated from the LAN, with its own key, and the firewall left on.","Give patients the staff network key."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Guests get Internet access but cannot reach the clinic's records.","Patients would be on the same LAN as the records server."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the problem lines","q":"<p>A Grade 12 student at Breadfruit Valley Secondary cannot get online in the library. Her teacher ran some commands and pasted the output.</p><p>Select EVERY line that shows a problem.</p>","options":["2. Subnet Mask . . . . : 255.255.0.0","3. Default Gateway . . : (blank)","4. Physical Address . : 5C-26-0A-91-44-B3","5. ping 127.0.0.1 : Reply from 127.0.0.1: bytes=32 time<1ms","7. DHCP Enabled . . . : Yes","1. IPv4 Address . . . : 169.254.88.17(Preferred)","6. ping 192.168.10.1 : Destination host unreachable."],"correct":[1,5,6],"why":"Correct. The 169.254 address and blank gateway show DHCP failed, so the gateway cannot be reached. The loopback reply shows the PC's own network software is fine."},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (1)","q":"<p>You are the technician for the Island Hop Travel agency in Bridgetown. For each piece of evidence, choose the most likely cause.</p><p>ping 8.8.8.8 gets 4 replies, but ping www.example.com says \"could not find host\".</p>","options":["a broken Ethernet cable","a DNS problem","the router has no power"],"correct":1,"why":"The Internet path works by IP address, so name resolution (DNS) is failing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (2)","q":"<p>ipconfig shows \"Media disconnected\" for the Ethernet adapter.</p>","options":["no physical link: cable or port","wrong DNS server","firewall blocking ping"],"correct":0,"why":"Media disconnected means the network card sees no link at all: check the cable and port.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (3)","q":"<p>All PCs are offline and the router's Internet light is red; LAN lights are normal.</p>","options":["a fault on the WAN side: ISP line or WAN settings","MAC filtering on one laptop","a problem with one PC"],"correct":0,"why":"The LAN is working; the link to the ISP (WAN) is down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (4)","q":"<p>Wired speed test gives 95 Mbps; Wi-Fi in the back office gives 12 Mbps.</p>","options":["weak Wi-Fi signal or interference in that room","the ISP is throttling","DNS failure"],"correct":0,"why":"The line is fine when cabled, so the wireless link is the bottleneck.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (5)","q":"<p>A new staff laptop sees the SSID and accepts the network key but never connects; other devices are fine.</p>","options":["the gateway is wrong for everyone","MAC filtering has not been updated with its address","the hub is broken"],"correct":1,"why":"Only the new device is refused: its MAC address is probably missing from the allow list.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Diagnose from the evidence (6)","q":"<p>ping to a colleague's PC times out, but she can browse the web normally.</p>","options":["the router has failed","her PC has no IP address","her software firewall blocks incoming ping"],"correct":2,"why":"Her connection works, so her host firewall is most likely blocking inbound ICMP echo requests.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Troubleshoot in a logical order","q":"<p>After hurricane-season repairs, a classroom PC at the Gros Islet Learning Centre cannot reach any website. Put the troubleshooting steps in a logical order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Find out whether other devices in the room are affected.","Check the Ethernet cable at both ends and look at the link lights.","Run ipconfig to check the IP address, gateway and DNS server.","Ping the default gateway.","Ping a public IP address such as 8.8.8.8.","Run nslookup on a website name to test DNS.","Apply the fix, retest, and record the fault and solution."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interpreting nslookup","q":"<p>A user at the Saba Dive Shop reports \"the Internet is down\". The technician finds that ping 8.8.8.8 receives 4 replies with 0% loss, but nslookup www.example.com returns \"DNS request timed out\". Which action is MOST appropriate next?</p>","options":["Call the ISP to report that the line is down","Turn off the firewall permanently","Change the DNS server setting, then test again with nslookup","Replace the Ethernet cable"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["The line is working: replies came back from a public IP address.","Nothing points to the firewall, and it should never be left off.","Correct. The Internet path works by IP address but names are not being resolved, so the DNS server setting is the fault.","The pings to 8.8.8.8 prove the physical path to the Internet works, so the cable is not the fault."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which upgrade helps?","q":"<p>A teacher's PC at the San Pedro Learning Centre has a fast new SSD, but it freezes whenever several programs are open and the drive light flashes constantly. The CPU is only moderately busy. Which upgrade is MOST likely to improve performance?</p>","options":["A faster Internet connection","A second, larger SSD","A larger monitor","More RAM"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["The freezing happens with local programs, so the network is not the cause.","Storage is not the bottleneck; a larger drive does not stop the swapping.","An output device does not affect processing speed.","Correct. The flashing drive light shows constant swapping between RAM and virtual memory; more RAM reduces it."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the bottleneck (1)","q":"<p>Staff at the English Harbour Learning Centre have logged performance complaints. For each one, choose the best explanation.</p><p>The video-editing PC has a new SSD and 32 GB of RAM, but renders still crawl and Task Manager shows the old processor at 100%.</p>","options":["The monitor is too small.","The SSD is too fast for the RAM.","The CPU is the bottleneck: it sets the speed limit."],"correct":2,"why":"Storage and RAM are feeding the CPU well; the processor itself limits how fast work can be done.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the bottleneck (2)","q":"<p>The office PC has 4 GB of RAM. With the accounts package, email and a browser open it slows badly, and the drive light never stops flashing.</p>","options":["RAM is full, so the PC keeps swapping to virtual memory.","The keyboard is faulty.","The Internet connection is slow."],"correct":0,"why":"Constant drive activity with many programs open shows swapping between RAM and virtual memory; more RAM would reduce it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the bottleneck (3)","q":"<p>The centre bought faster PCs, but web pages load no faster over its old, slow office connection.</p>","options":["Upgrading one part leaves the outdated part limiting the whole system.","The new PCs are inefficient.","Web pages do not use the network."],"correct":0,"why":"A system performs only as well as its parts work together; the slow connection still limits web browsing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the bottleneck (4)","q":"<p>The payroll program prints correct payslips, but it takes an hour and uses all the PC's memory.</p>","options":["It has a portability problem.","It is efficient because the output is right.","It is accurate but inefficient: the input-output ratio is adverse."],"correct":2,"why":"Correct output does not make a system efficient if it uses far too much time and memory to produce it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the bottleneck (5)","q":"<p>Receptionists needed nine clicks to book a class; the redesigned screen needs only three.</p>","options":["Storage capacity has improved.","The CPU has been upgraded.","Usability has improved: the goal is reached with fewer steps."],"correct":2,"why":"Usability efficiency means reaching goals with as few resources (time, steps, effort) as possible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (1)","q":"<p>The St. Kitts Seaview Hotel is choosing new equipment. Sort each manager's comment by the feature it compares.</p><p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>The SSD opens the booking system in seconds; the old hard drive took a minute.</b></p>","options":["Storage","Transmission","Portability","Speed","Maintainability","Efficiency"],"correct":3,"why":"How quickly a task is done is speed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (2)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>The laptops use a third of the electricity of the old desktops.</b></p>","options":["Maintainability","Storage","Portability","Transmission","Efficiency","Speed"],"correct":4,"why":"Useful work for less power is efficiency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (3)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>Housekeeping staff can carry the tablets from room to room.</b></p>","options":["Speed","Transmission","Storage","Maintainability","Efficiency","Portability"],"correct":5,"why":"Ease of carrying and using elsewhere is portability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (4)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>The desktop's RAM can be upgraded in five minutes; the sealed laptop cannot.</b></p>","options":["Maintainability","Storage","Transmission","Portability","Speed","Efficiency"],"correct":0,"why":"Ease of repair and upgrade is maintainability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (5)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>The new server holds 8 TB of guest records; the old one held 500 GB.</b></p>","options":["Efficiency","Transmission","Storage","Portability","Maintainability","Speed"],"correct":2,"why":"Capacity to hold data is storage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (6)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>Fibre between the two buildings is unaffected by the kitchen's electrical equipment, unlike the copper cable.</b></p>","options":["Transmission","Portability","Efficiency","Storage","Maintainability","Speed"],"correct":0,"why":"Comparing how media carry data is transmission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (7)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>The booking software receives regular updates and has a clear manual.</b></p>","options":["Maintainability","Transmission","Portability","Efficiency","Storage","Speed"],"correct":0,"why":"Updates and documentation make software maintainable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which feature is being compared? (8)","q":"<p>Which SO 2.15 feature does this comment compare?</p><p><b>Wi-Fi lets guests connect anywhere in the lobby, but cabled PCs at reception get a steadier signal.</b></p>","options":["Efficiency","Maintainability","Transmission","Portability","Storage","Speed"],"correct":2,"why":"Wired versus wireless links is a transmission comparison.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the trade-off to the example","q":"<p>The ICT department at Frangipani Hill Academy is reviewing its equipment and software. Match each idea to the situation that shows it.</p>","left":["Flexibility versus optimisation","Hardware portability","Replace faulty parts","Prevent breakdowns","Cope with change","Trained maintainers","Manuals and support equipment"],"right":["Fans are cleaned and updates installed each term, before anything fails","The technical guide, diagnostic tools and a shelf of spare parts in the server room","A dead power supply in a lab desktop is swapped, and the working drive, RAM and CPU are kept","The timetable software is updated so it runs on the new operating system","Teachers choose the lightest laptop with a 12-hour battery for a field trip to Saba","A web-based register runs on every school device, but a program written for one graphics card renders video faster","A technical support officer who can isolate a lab fault in minutes"],"correct":[5,4,2,0,3,6,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (1)","q":"<p>You are advising the Statia Harbour Office on how to store and send its data. For each need, choose the best option.</p><p>The office must keep ten years of shipping records as a cheap archive that is only ever read from start to finish.</p>","options":["A flash drive kept in a desk drawer","Tape, because sequential access is enough and it is cheap for large backups","RAM, because it is fastest"],"correct":1,"why":"Tapes allow only sequential access, but that suits a large, cheap archive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (2)","q":"<p>The live berth-booking database must jump straight to any vessel's record.</p>","options":["Disk, because it allows direct (random) access","An optical disc read in sequence","Tape"],"correct":0,"why":"Disks allow any file organisation, including direct access to a record.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (3)","q":"<p>Records must survive if the building is badly damaged in a hurricane.</p>","options":["More RAM","An off-site cloud copy","A second hard drive in the same office"],"correct":1,"why":"Only an off-site copy is safe from a local disaster; RAM is volatile.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (4)","q":"<p>The office PC loads programs slowly from its spinning hard disk. Which storage would load them much faster?</p>","options":["A DVD","A tape drive","A solid-state drive (SSD)"],"correct":2,"why":"An SSD has no moving parts, so access is much faster than an HDD.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (5)","q":"<p>The harbour radio link lets each side transmit, but only one side at a time.</p>","options":["Half-duplex","Synchronous","Full-duplex"],"correct":0,"why":"Both directions, one at a time, is half-duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (6)","q":"<p>Video calls with the Willemstad office need both sides sending at the same time.</p>","options":["Full-duplex (duplex)","Half-duplex","Asynchronous"],"correct":0,"why":"Both directions at once is full-duplex.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (7)","q":"<p>Large blocks of cargo data are sent in a steady stream timed by a shared clock.</p>","options":["Asynchronous","Synchronous","Half-duplex"],"correct":1,"why":"Blocks in a steady stream timed by a clock is synchronous transmission; asynchronous sends a character at a time with start and stop bits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the storage and the channel (8)","q":"<p>Cargo records sent from Statia arrive as gibberish in Curaçao because the two systems interpret the bits differently.</p>","options":["No common data encoding standard was used.","The disk is too small.","The cable is too short."],"correct":0,"why":"Sender and receiver must follow the same standards, such as data encoding, to interpret the bits the same way.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing transmission media","q":"<p>The Statia Harbour Office must link two buildings 400 metres apart. The cable route passes beside heavy electrical machinery, and data must be hard to intercept. Which transmission medium BEST meets ALL these needs?</p>","options":["A hub in each building","Wi-Fi access points in each building","Cat6 twisted-pair Ethernet cable","Fibre-optic cable"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A hub is a connecting device, not a transmission medium.","Wi-Fi range over 400 metres outdoors is poor, and wireless signals are easier to intercept.","A single Ethernet run is limited to about 100 metres and is affected by electrical interference.","Correct. Fibre carries light over kilometres, is immune to electrical interference and is very hard to tap."]},{"t":"match","title":"Command, setting or check: what does it tell you?","q":"<p>Match each troubleshooting tool or check to what it tells you.</p>","left":["ipconfig","ping","nslookup","Speed test","Status lights","Firewall configuration","169.254.x.x address"],"right":["Actual download and upload speeds, latency and jitter","Whether rules are blocking a program, port or address","The PC's IP address, subnet mask, default gateway and DNS server","The PC did not receive an address from a DHCP server","Whether a domain name can be translated into an IP address","Whether a device replies, and how long the reply takes","Whether a device has power and a link on each port"],"correct":[2,5,4,0,6,1,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Comparing storage for a business","q":"<p>Dominica Craft Co-op keeps 2 TB of product photos on one office hard disk drive. The manager wants faster access and wants the photos to survive if the office is damaged in a hurricane. Which combination BEST meets both needs?</p>","options":["Move working files to an SSD and keep a synchronised cloud backup","Copy the photos onto flash drives kept in the office desk","Keep the hard disk drive and print the photos as backup","Add more RAM to the office PC"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The SSD gives faster access and the cloud copy is stored off-site, so it survives local damage.","Flash drives in the same office would be lost with the building, and 2 TB would need many drives.","Printing does not speed up access and is not a practical backup of digital files.","RAM can make the PC faster, but it is volatile and does not protect data from loss."]}]},{"id":"assess_4ty","title":"Module 2 · Unit 8 quiz: Security mechanisms and security terms","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 8: Security mechanisms and security terms. 110 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (1)","q":"<p>The IT officer at the Bayview Credit Union in New Amsterdam has noted how access control works during a normal week. Sort each event into the part of access control it shows.</p><p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>The manager decides that tellers may record deposits but may not approve loans.</b></p>","options":["Authentication","Authorisation","Accountability","Identification"],"correct":1,"why":"Deciding who is approved and exactly what they may use is authorisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (2)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>A teller types her user ID, TELLER07, at the log-in screen.</b></p>","options":["Authentication","Authorisation","Accountability","Identification"],"correct":3,"why":"Stating who you are with a user ID is identification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (3)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>The system checks her password and the code on her hardware token.</b></p>","options":["Authentication","Accountability","Authorisation","Identification"],"correct":0,"why":"Verifying the claimed identity is authentication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (4)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>An auditor's report lists every change TELLER07 made to member accounts last month.</b></p>","options":["Authentication","Accountability","Identification","Authorisation"],"correct":1,"why":"Tracing actions to an individual for reporting and auditing is accountability.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (5)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>A member gives her account number at the counter so the teller can find her record.</b></p>","options":["Authorisation","Identification","Authentication","Accountability"],"correct":1,"why":"An account number is a form of username: it identifies the member.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (6)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>The member's fingerprint is compared with her enrolled template to confirm she is the account holder.</b></p>","options":["Authorisation","Authentication","Accountability","Identification"],"correct":1,"why":"Checking that she really is the person she claims to be is authentication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (7)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>Only the IT officer is approved to create new user accounts.</b></p>","options":["Authorisation","Authentication","Accountability","Identification"],"correct":0,"why":"Approving who may use which resource is authorisation (policy definition).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which part of access control? (8)","q":"<p>Which of the four parts of access control is this?</p><p><b>The log shows that the shared \"frontdesk\" account deleted a record, so nobody can be held responsible.</b></p>","options":["Identification","Authorisation","Accountability","Authentication"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a failure of accountability: the action cannot be traced to one person.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (1)","q":"<p>Access control is everywhere, from a car door in San Pedro to a business computer in Belize City. Judge each situation.</p><p>Kezia locks her car outside the Belize City market and takes the key with her. In access-control terms, the lock and key...</p>","options":["encrypt the car","record who used the car","protect a resource so that only those allowed can use it"],"correct":2,"why":"A lock and key is an everyday access control: only the key holder can open or start the car.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (2)","q":"<p>At a Frederick Street cash machine, Jaden's bank card works only after he types four secret digits. This everyday access control is a...</p>","options":["digital certificate","username","personal identification number (PIN)"],"correct":2,"why":"A PIN controls use of a bank or credit card.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (3)","q":"<p>The Island Fresh Supermarket locks its cash office and puts passwords on its stock database. The cash office is a...</p>","options":["hardware token","physical asset","computing resource"],"correct":1,"why":"Buildings and rooms are physical assets; computer systems and data are computing resources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (4)","q":"<p>Every night at 2 a.m. a backup program at the supermarket logs in to copy the stock file. Why does the IT definition of access control say \"who or what\"?</p>","options":["Users may be people or computer processes","Backups are not resources","Only machines need access control"],"correct":0,"why":"Users may be people or computer processes, and both must be controlled.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (5)","q":"<p>A Grenadian bank gives staff a small device that shows a new six-digit code every minute, typed in when logging in. This technology is a...</p>","options":["biometric","hardware token","digital certificate"],"correct":1,"why":"Hardware tokens are one of the access-control technologies businesses use, with passwords, biometrics and certificates.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (6)","q":"<p>Before the new till system goes live, the supermarket manager sets the rule \"cashiers may record sales; only supervisors may approve refunds\". Which phase is this?</p>","options":["Accountability","Policy enforcement","Policy definition"],"correct":2,"why":"Deciding in advance who may do what is policy definition (authorisation).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Access control all around us (7)","q":"<p>Next morning, the till refuses a cashier's attempt to approve a refund. Which phase is this?</p>","options":["Encryption","Policy enforcement","Policy definition"],"correct":1,"why":"Granting or rejecting a request using the authorisations already defined is policy enforcement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who changed the price?","q":"<p>At the Sunrise Hardware store in Holetown, all six cashiers log in to the till system with one shared account, \"till1\". A generator was sold at a heavily discounted price, and the store cannot tell which cashier entered it. Which part of access control is MOST clearly missing?</p>","options":["Authorisation","Encryption","Physical access control","Accountability"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Rights for the account were set; the till accepted the price because the account was allowed to enter prices. The gap is tracing who used it.","Encryption keeps data unreadable to outsiders; it does not record who made a change.","The cashiers are allowed to be at the till. The problem is not entry to the shop but tracing the action to a person.","Correct. Accountability traces actions and changes to an individual. A shared account makes that impossible, so each cashier needs a unique user ID."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (1)","q":"<p>The Grand Anse Marina Hotel is reviewing its security. Sort each measure by what it protects.</p><p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>A security guard checks staff ID badges at the back entrance.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":1,"why":"A guard controls entry to the building, so this is physical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>Front-desk staff can view bookings but cannot change room rates.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"Access rights set in software decide what each user can do with data: logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>The server cabinet is kept in a locked room.</b></p>","options":["Physical access control","Logical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"A locked room protects the hardware itself: physical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>The booking system locks an account after five wrong passwords.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"The software is controlling log-in attempts: logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>A swipe card is needed to open the staff-only corridor.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":1,"why":"The card opens a door, so it controls physical entry.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>Guest credit-card numbers are stored in encrypted form.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"Encryption protects the data itself through software: logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>Each laptop is chained to the desk with a cable lock.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":1,"why":"A cable lock stops the hardware from being carried away: physical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>The manager logs in to the accounts PC with a fingerprint.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"Here the fingerprint opens a computer system, not a door, so it is logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (9)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>The barrier at the staff parking lot lifts only for registered staff cards.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":1,"why":"Parking lots, like buildings and IT rooms, are protected by physical access controls.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical or logical? (10)","q":"<p>Is this a physical or a logical access control measure?</p><p><b>Each employee has a unique username and password for the hotel network.</b></p>","options":["Logical access control","Physical access control"],"correct":0,"why":"A unique username and password limits connections to the network and its data: logical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (1)","q":"<p>Staff at the Princess Port Hotel in Bridgetown and other Barbadian organisations are sorting out who controls access and how users are identified. Answer each question.</p><p>The barrier at the hotel's staff parking lot needs reprogramming so that new staff cards open it. Who is MOST likely responsible?</p>","options":["The facilities manager","The computer-system manager","The reservation clerk"],"correct":0,"why":"Parking lots, buildings and rooms are physical access, usually the facilities manager's responsibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (2)","q":"<p>The hotel's IT manager decides that housekeeping supervisors can view room status but not guests' payment details. She is using...</p>","options":["a hardware token","physical access control","logical access control"],"correct":2,"why":"Computer-system managers use logical access controls to decide who enters a system and which tasks they can perform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (3)","q":"<p>The IT manager sets every hotel PC to lock after five idle minutes and blocks software installation. This shows that computer-system managers can also...</p>","options":["influence how staff use the system","repair door locks","issue parking permits"],"correct":0,"why":"Logical access settings shape how staff use the system, not just who gets in.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (4)","q":"<p>A caller to the Bayview Credit Union gives her account number so the clerk can find her record. The account number is acting as a...</p>","options":["username (identifier)","passphrase","biometric"],"correct":0,"why":"A username may take the form of a user ID, an account number or a PIN.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (5)","q":"<p>An employee of a Speightstown firm scans his card at a reader to lift the car-park barrier. Which identification method is this?</p>","options":["Username","Smart card","Keystroke dynamics"],"correct":1,"why":"Smart cards are swiped or scanned through a reader to grant entry to parking facilities, buildings and rooms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (6)","q":"<p>A Holetown laboratory recognises staff at the door from the pattern of their iris. Which identification method is this?</p>","options":["Smart card","Username","Biometrics"],"correct":2,"why":"Biometrics recognises a person through physical or behavioural traits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Who controls access, and how? (7)","q":"<p>A Mahoe Grove High School student types her username at the network log-in. Why must the system still ask for a password?</p>","options":["The username only claims an identity, which must then be authenticated","The username is encrypted","Usernames change every day"],"correct":0,"why":"Identification claims who you are; authentication proves it.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"How a key card opens the door","q":"<p>At the San Pedro Shipping Agency in Belize City, Andre arrives for the night shift and uses his key card to enter the records room. Put the steps in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Andre takes out his key card, which holds a pre-recorded code.","He swipes the card through the slot in the reader beside the door.","The reader reads the code electronically and passes it to the electronic controls.","The controls check whether the code matches the code required for this door at this time.","The code matches, so the lock releases and the portal (the door) opens.","The system logs Andre's card number and the time he entered."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Which portable key?","q":"<p>Businesses across Dominica use different portable keys. Match each type to the situation that describes it.</p>","left":["Swipe / key card","Proximity card","Magnetic stripe","Smart card","Long-range proximity tag","Ordinary metal key"],"right":["Opens the lock but cannot be cancelled, time-limited or show who entered","A clerk at the Portsmouth Shipping Agency slides her wallet-size card through a groove in the reader","A Canefield hotel guest holds the room card near the reader without inserting it","A bank card whose data is held on the dark band on the back, swiped at a King George V Street shop","A tag on a car windscreen opens the gate of a Mahaut office car park as the car approaches","A staff card with a chip whose memory stores access codes plus the holder's photo and details"],"correct":[1,2,3,5,4,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (1)","q":"<p>The Sunset Reef Resort at Dickenson Bay, Antigua, is replacing metal keys with key cards. The manager has listed what she has noticed in the first month. Sort each observation.</p><p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>A housekeeper reports her card missing at 9 a.m.; the IT officer cancels it in two minutes without changing any locks.</b></p>","options":["Drawback of key cards","Benefit of key cards"],"correct":1,"why":"Lost cards can be cancelled instantly in software, unlike a lost metal key.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>A delivery driver walks into the staff corridor right behind a waiter before the door closes.</b></p>","options":["Benefit of key cards","Drawback of key cards"],"correct":1,"why":"This is tailgating: the card opened the door for one person, but two got in.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>The log shows exactly which card opened the server room at 11:42 p.m. on Saturday.</b></p>","options":["Benefit of key cards","Drawback of key cards"],"correct":0,"why":"Each card has a unique code, so the system keeps an audit log of entries.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>A guest lends her room card to a friend, who uses the pool gate without being registered.</b></p>","options":["Benefit of key cards","Drawback of key cards"],"correct":1,"why":"A card proves possession, not identity. Whoever holds it gets in.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>Guest cards stop working automatically at noon on the check-out date.</b></p>","options":["Drawback of key cards","Benefit of key cards"],"correct":1,"why":"Access can be restricted by time, so old guests cannot return.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>During a power cut, the card readers on the side doors stop working.</b></p>","options":["Drawback of key cards","Benefit of key cards"],"correct":0,"why":"Readers and controllers need electricity, unlike a simple key lock.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>The cleaners' cards open offices only between 6 p.m. and 10 p.m.</b></p>","options":["Drawback of key cards","Benefit of key cards"],"correct":1,"why":"Access can be limited by time and by door for each card.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards at the resort: benefit or drawback? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a benefit or a drawback of using key cards?</p><p><b>A magnetic stripe card stops working after being left next to a phone speaker.</b></p>","options":["Drawback of key cards","Benefit of key cards"],"correct":0,"why":"Magnetic stripes can be wiped by magnets and wear out with swiping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Key cards: what do they really prove?","q":"<p>At Basseterre Data Centre, staff open the server-room door with a proximity key card. One afternoon an unauthorised visitor enters using a card that an employee left on a café table in Fort Street. Which statement BEST explains the weakness this incident exposes?</p>","options":["Proximity cards can be read only when the power is off.","Key cards are a logical access control and cannot protect rooms.","The card must have been encrypted incorrectly.","A key card authenticates possession of the card, not the identity of the person holding it."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Not so. Readers need power to work; the incident has nothing to do with a power cut.","A key card on a door is a physical access control. It does protect rooms; it simply cannot tell who is holding it.","Nothing suggests a technical fault. The card worked exactly as designed for whoever held it.","Correct. Key cards check something the user has. Anyone holding a valid card is treated as authorised."]},{"t":"match","title":"Nine ways to recognise a person","q":"<p>Organisations across the Caribbean use all nine types of biometric. Match each type to the situation where it is being used.</p>","left":["Fingerprint","Palm print","Hand geometry","Retina scan","Iris scan","Facial recognition","Voice pattern","Keystroke dynamics","Signature dynamics"],"right":["A worker at the San Pedro Construction Ltd. clocks in by pressing a fingertip on the reader","A teacher unlocks her laptop by looking into its camera","Leeward Data Vault in Curaçao reads the blood-vessel pattern at the back of the eye","An online system flags a log-in because the dwell and flight times of the typing are unusual for that user","A caller to a Jamaican telephone-banking line is checked by the way she speaks","A courier tablet in San Pedro records the speed, direction and pressure as a customer signs","A Belize City gym scans the lines and creases of a member's whole palm at the turnstile","A Barbados laboratory scans the patterned ring around each worker's pupil at its door","A Trinidad factory terminal measures the length, width and thickness of a worker's fingers"],"correct":[0,6,8,2,7,1,4,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>Organisations in Guyana have reported what happened after they installed biometric systems. Sort each report.</p><p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>At Linden Construction Ltd., nobody can clock in for an absent friend, because each worker must place their own finger on the reader.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of biometrics","Advantage of biometrics"],"correct":1,"why":"Presence: the person must be physically present to authenticate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>Staff at a Georgetown insurance office no longer call the help desk about forgotten passwords.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of biometrics","Advantage of biometrics"],"correct":1,"why":"Memory: there is nothing to remember.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>An intruder cannot copy a manager's iris pattern the way he once copied a door key.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of biometrics","Disadvantage of biometrics"],"correct":0,"why":"Imitation: biometrics are hard to fake.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>An employee who left her staff badge at home still gets into the office with her fingerprint.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of biometrics","Disadvantage of biometrics"],"correct":0,"why":"Convenience: lost IDs are not a problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>The fingerprint reader rejects a mason whose fingertips are worn from years of laying blocks.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of biometrics","Advantage of biometrics"],"correct":0,"why":"Changing traits: physical characteristics may change, causing false rejection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>A clerk with limited hand movement cannot use the hand-geometry terminal.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of biometrics","Disadvantage of biometrics"],"correct":1,"why":"Accessibility: physically disabled users may struggle with fingerprint, hand-geometry or signature systems.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>The Island Credit bank finds voice recognition works well by phone but fails in its noisy branch lobby.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of biometrics","Advantage of biometrics"],"correct":0,"why":"Suitability: techniques differ in effectiveness for different uses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>At shift change, a queue forms because the face scanner at a cruise-pier tour office takes several seconds per person.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of biometrics","Advantage of biometrics"],"correct":0,"why":"Time and cost: response time may be too slow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics: advantage or disadvantage? (9)","q":"<p>Does this report show an advantage or a disadvantage of biometrics?</p><p><b>The Seaside Pharmacy decides retina scanners are too expensive for its two-door shop.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of biometrics","Disadvantage of biometrics"],"correct":1,"why":"Time and cost: the required devices can be expensive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (1)","q":"<p>Organisations across the region are trying out biometric devices. Judge each situation using what you know about biometrics.</p><p>At Linden Construction Ltd., a mason with a freshly cut finger is refused by the fingerprint time clock, even though he is enrolled. This is a...</p>","options":["false rejection","tailgating incident","false acceptance"],"correct":0,"why":"A genuine, enrolled user being refused is a false rejection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (2)","q":"<p>A Jamaican bank lets callers into telephone banking by checking the pitch and tone of their speech. Which biometric is this?</p>","options":["Hand geometry","Voice recognition","Iris scan"],"correct":1,"why":"Pitch, tone and speech patterns are measured by voice recognition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (3)","q":"<p>A Roseau gym's fingerprint database is hacked. Why is this worse than a hacked password file?</p>","options":["A fingerprint cannot be changed like a password can","Fingerprints are cheaper to store","Fingerprints are always encrypted"],"correct":0,"why":"A stolen password can be reset, but a person cannot get new fingerprints.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (4)","q":"<p>A Trinidad factory switched from paper sign-in sheets to a fingerprint clock. What problem does this mainly solve?</p>","options":["Forgotten passwords on the stock system","Power cuts during saving","Workers clocking in for absent friends"],"correct":2,"why":"A fingerprint cannot be lent, so one worker cannot clock in for another (\"buddy punching\").","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (5)","q":"<p>Signing on a pad at a Curaçao courier office measures how a person writes. This kind of biometric is...</p>","options":["not biometric at all","behavioural","physical"],"correct":1,"why":"Signature and typing rhythm measure how a person behaves, so they are behavioural biometrics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Biometrics on the job (6)","q":"<p>An enrolled worker at the San Fernando Shipping Agency places her finger on the reader. What does the system compare her sample with?</p>","options":["Her password","Her key card code","Her stored template"],"correct":2,"why":"The sample is converted into a template and compared with the template stored at enrolment.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the mechanism to the situation","q":"<p>Match each security mechanism to the Caribbean situation it suits best.</p>","left":["Fingerprint time clock","Proximity key card","Cable lock","Visitor log and ID badges","Voice recognition","CCTV camera"],"right":["A Guyanese factory wants to stop workers clocking in for absent friends","A Gros Islet hotel needs room access that expires at check-out","Laptops on a public counter at a Barbados library must not be carried away","A Castries office wants a record of every outsider who enters","A Jamaican bank checks callers on its telephone banking line","A shop in Rodney Bay wants evidence of who approached the server cupboard"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the effective passwords","q":"<p>The IT officer at the San Fernando Shipping Agency is auditing staff passwords. Apply every rule for an effective password: at least eight alphanumeric characters; uppercase and lowercase letters plus numbers; a special character within the first 7 characters; nothing personal and no simple patterns such as qwerty123.</p><p>Select EVERY password that meets all the rules for an effective password.</p>","options":["7. Blue7Kite!","8. Anne#0412","2. Mango#4BreakfastDaily","5. t7R$v2Lq9Xm4","3. Kareem1998!","4. qwerty123","1. password123","9. Gr8!TideRises","6. TRINIDAD#1"],"correct":[2,3,7],"why":"Correct. \"Mango#4BreakfastDaily\", \"t7R$v2Lq9Xm4\" and \"Gr8!TideRises\" each have at least eight characters, upper and lower case, numbers, a special character within the first 7 characters, and nothing personal. \"Blue7Kite!\" fails because its \"!\" is the tenth character, and \"Anne#0412\" hides a name and a birth date."},{"t":"mc","title":"Password clinic (1)","q":"<p>Students in a Coralstone College computer lab have asked you to check their password habits. Answer each question.</p><p>Aaliyah uses \"aaliyah05\" because it is easy to remember. Which characteristic does it fail MOST clearly?</p>","options":["It is alphanumeric","It is obvious","It is too long"],"correct":1,"why":"It contains her own name, which anyone who knows her could guess. Being alphanumeric is a strength, not a failure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password clinic (2)","q":"<p>Marco changes \"sunsetbeach\" to \"SunsetBeach\". Which characteristic has he added?</p>","options":["Alphanumeric","Length","Mixed case"],"correct":2,"why":"He added capital letters, so the password now uses mixed case. It still has no digits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password clinic (3)","q":"<p>Keisha's password is 10 mixed-case alphanumeric characters. What happens to the number of possible combinations if she adds one more such character?</p>","options":["It is multiplied by 62","It increases by 62","It stays the same"],"correct":0,"why":"Each extra position multiplies the possibilities by the number of characters available, so length is very powerful.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password clinic (4)","q":"<p>Shania writes her school password on a sticky note on her monitor. The best advice is to...</p>","options":["use a memorable passphrase and remove the note","make it shorter so she remembers it","share it with her best friend as a backup"],"correct":0,"why":"A passphrase is long but memorable, so she no longer needs the note.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password clinic (5)","q":"<p>Why does the school system show dots instead of the letters as you type?</p>","options":["Masking stops onlookers reading the password","To encrypt the password","To save screen space"],"correct":0,"why":"Masking prevents shoulder-surfing. Encryption is a different process that scrambles stored or transmitted data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (1)","q":"<p>The IT officer at the Bayview Credit Union in St. George’s is reviewing staff habits and the log-in system. Judge each situation.</p><p>A loan officer has used the same password since she joined the credit union six years ago. This is called a...</p>","options":["passphrase","static password","one-time code"],"correct":1,"why":"A static password is seldom, if ever, changed, so an attacker who learns it can use it for a long time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (2)","q":"<p>The credit union now makes staff change passwords every 90 days. The MAIN benefit is that...</p>","options":["passwords no longer need to be secret","passwords become easier to remember","an attacker has less time to use a stolen password before it changes"],"correct":2,"why":"A shorter lifespan reduces an attacker's opportunity to compromise and use the password.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (3)","q":"<p>A teller wants to use her work password on a cooking forum and for her personal e-mail. Following good practice, she...</p>","options":["should write it down to avoid mixing them up","may reuse it anywhere if it is strong","should not reuse her work password; reuse is only for non-critical resources"],"correct":2,"why":"Only reuse a password for resources considered non-critical. A work password protecting member data is critical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (4)","q":"<p>Someone tries 40 different passwords on the manager's user ID in one minute. Which system feature guards against this?</p>","options":["Masking the password with dots","Disabling the user ID after consecutive failed logons","Encrypting the screen"],"correct":1,"why":"Lockout after consecutive failed logons guards against repeated password guesses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (5)","q":"<p>A cashier changes from \"Sun7!\" to the passphrase \"Sunrise over San Fernando at 7!\". Compared with the short password, the passphrase is...</p>","options":["generally harder to guess, because it is longer and has several words","the same, because both contain a number","weaker, because it contains spaces"],"correct":0,"why":"A passphrase is longer and usually has more than one word, so it is harder to guess and somewhat more secure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (6)","q":"<p>Passwords are the oldest and most common way to authenticate staff at the credit union. They are also usually considered...</p>","options":["the weakest method","impossible to share","the most expensive method"],"correct":0,"why":"Passwords can be guessed, shared, observed or forgotten, so they are the weakest common method.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Password and log-in policy review (7)","q":"<p>The credit union's loan system holds sensitive member data. Which log-in follows the guidance for sensitive or critical systems?</p>","options":["A password only","A password plus a code from a hardware token","A password plus a passphrase"],"correct":1,"why":"Use at least two of the three factors for sensitive or critical systems. A password and a passphrase are both things you know, so they count as one factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (1)","q":"<p>Staff and customers across Jamaica prove who they are in many ways. Sort each authentication method into its factor.</p><p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A customer types a 4-digit PIN at a bank machine on King Street.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge: something you know","Ownership: something you have","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":0,"why":"A PIN is a secret the user knows.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (2)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A telephone company technician taps a contactless badge to enter the exchange building.</b></p>","options":["Ownership: something you have","Knowledge: something you know","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":0,"why":"The badge is a physical token the technician holds.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (3)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A teacher unlocks her laptop by looking at its camera (face recognition).</b></p>","options":["Ownership: something you have","Characteristics: something you are","Knowledge: something you know"],"correct":1,"why":"Her face is a characteristic of who she is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (4)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>An online banking user enters a one-time code sent by text to his registered phone.</b></p>","options":["Ownership: something you have","Knowledge: something you know","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":0,"why":"The code proves he has the phone, so it is a possession factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (5)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A caller answers \"What was the name of your first school?\"</b></p>","options":["Characteristics: something you are","Ownership: something you have","Knowledge: something you know"],"correct":2,"why":"A security question tests something the user knows.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (6)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>An NMIA worker places her finger on a reader to enter the baggage area.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge: something you know","Characteristics: something you are","Ownership: something you have"],"correct":1,"why":"A fingerprint is an inherence factor: something she is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (7)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A student types his password into the school network.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge: something you know","Ownership: something you have","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":0,"why":"A password is the classic knowledge factor.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (8)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A Jamaican bank checks the caller's voice before discussing the account.</b></p>","options":["Ownership: something you have","Knowledge: something you know","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":2,"why":"Voice recognition measures a characteristic of the person.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (9)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A manager at the Bayview Credit Union types her passphrase \"Kites over Hellshire at 6!\".</b></p>","options":["Ownership: something you have","Characteristics: something you are","Knowledge: something you know"],"correct":2,"why":"A passphrase, like a password, is something you know.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Know, have or are? (10)","q":"<p>Which authentication factor is being used?</p><p><b>A courier in Spanish Town signs on a pad that checks the speed and pressure of her signature.</b></p>","options":["Knowledge: something you know","Ownership: something you have","Characteristics: something you are"],"correct":2,"why":"A signature measured as it is written is a characteristic unique to the person.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Getting into the payroll system","q":"<p>Mrs. Richardson, the payroll officer at King Street Trading Company, arrives at work and needs to update staff salaries. Put the security checks she passes in order, from the street to the data.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["She shows her staff ID badge to the security guard at the building entrance.","She presents her key card to open the accounts office door.","She types her user ID at the payroll PC (identification).","She enters her password and a code from her phone (authentication).","The system grants her read and write rights to the salary records only (authorisation).","Her changes are saved and recorded in the audit trail under her user ID (accountability)."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Is it really two-factor?","q":"<p>The Anguilla Island Bank wants to introduce two-factor authentication for online banking. Which of the following log-in designs is a TRUE example of two-factor authentication?</p>","options":["A password followed by a one-time code sent to the customer's registered phone","A password followed by a four-digit PIN","A password followed by a security question about the customer's first school","A username followed by a password"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The password is something you know and the phone is something you have: two different factors.","Both are something the user knows, so this is still a single factor, however many secrets are asked for.","Both are knowledge factors. Answers to such questions can often be found on social media.","The username only identifies the customer; the password is the only authentication factor."]},{"t":"match","title":"Encryption in action","q":"<p>A customer at the Young Street Electronics online store pays for a phone by card. Match each encryption term to what happens in this purchase.</p>","left":["Plaintext","Ciphertext","Key","Decryption","Asymmetric encryption","Symmetric encryption","Data at rest","Encryption algorithm","With the correct key","Without the correct key"],"right":["The store's public key encrypts; only its private key can decrypt (HTTPS padlock)","The store's server turning the scrambled card number back into readable form","The store encrypts its own backup drive with one key that both locks and unlocks it","The eavesdropper would need enormous computing resources, so decryption is usually infeasible","The secret value the algorithm uses, without which the data cannot be unscrambled","Customer records stored on an encrypted laptop at the store's Grand Anse office","The store's server decrypts the payment with relatively little computational effort","The scrambled characters an eavesdropper on the café Wi-Fi would see","The card number as the customer types it, readable by anyone","The procedure that encodes the typed card number into scrambled form"],"correct":[8,7,4,1,0,2,5,9,6,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"What encryption does and does not do","q":"<p>A laptop holding the Saba Dive Centre's customer list is stolen from a car. The hard drive was fully encrypted and the thief does not have the key. Which statement BEST describes the effect of the encryption?</p>","options":["It prevented the laptop from being stolen.","The thief has the data but cannot read it, because it is stored as ciphertext.","It allows the dive centre to track the laptop's location.","The data has been corrupted and can no longer be used by anyone."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Encryption is not a physical control; it cannot stop theft of the device.","Correct. Encryption protects the confidentiality of data even after physical controls fail.","Tracking is a separate feature. Encryption only scrambles data.","Encrypted data is not corrupted. The dive centre, which holds the key, can decrypt its backup copies normally."]},{"t":"match","title":"Which integrity mechanism?","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in San Pedro wants its stock and patient data to stay accurate. Match each integrity mechanism to the situation where it is working.</p>","left":["Range check (validation)","Presence check (validation)","Verification","Access rights","Audit trail","Backup","Checksum"],"right":["A new customer record cannot be saved until a phone number is entered","A price list downloaded from the Curaçao supplier is checked to confirm no bits changed in transit","Cashiers can view prices but only the manager can change them","The stock system refuses a delivery of 500 boxes of insulin because the limit is 200","Last night's copy of the stock database is restored after the file is damaged","The log shows which clerk altered the price of a medicine last Tuesday","A pharmacist compares the quantities on screen with the supplier's delivery note"],"correct":[3,0,6,2,5,4,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (1)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma, the Road Town Water Board records office is reviewing what went wrong and what protects its data. Sort each item.</p><p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>The power failed while the billing file was being saved.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":1,"why":"An interrupted save leaves a partly written, corrupted file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>A UPS keeps the server running long enough to shut down safely.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":0,"why":"The UPS prevents corruption from sudden power loss.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>Rainwater leaked onto an external hard drive.</b></p>","options":["Cause of data corruption","Safeguard for data integrity"],"correct":0,"why":"Physical damage can make stored data unreadable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>A range check rejects any meter reading below zero.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":0,"why":"Validation stops unreasonable data entering the system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>A file was copied over an unstable network link and some bits changed.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":1,"why":"Transmission errors alter data in transit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>Every night a backup is copied to a server off the island.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":0,"why":"An off-site backup allows correct data to be restored.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>A clerk pulled out a flash drive while files were still being written.</b></p>","options":["Cause of data corruption","Safeguard for data integrity"],"correct":0,"why":"Improper removal interrupts writing and corrupts files.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Corruption cause or integrity safeguard? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a cause of data corruption or a safeguard for data integrity?</p><p><b>A checksum is compared after each file transfer.</b></p>","options":["Safeguard for data integrity","Cause of data corruption"],"correct":0,"why":"Checksums detect data changed in transmission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (1)","q":"<p>The Leeward Medical Laboratory in Kingstown has listed incidents that better access control could prevent. Sort each one by the purpose of access control it relates to.</p><p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A visitor photographs a patient's results on an unattended screen.</b></p>","options":["Preventing data corruption","Data security","Data integrity"],"correct":1,"why":"Disclosure to an unauthorised person is a data security failure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (2)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A former employee whose account was never disabled logs in and deletes a folder of reports.</b></p>","options":["Preventing data corruption","Data security","Data integrity"],"correct":1,"why":"Unauthorised deletion is a data security failure; disabling leavers' accounts prevents it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (3)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A courier reads a printed list of test results left in an unlocked room.</b></p>","options":["Data integrity","Data security","Preventing data corruption"],"correct":1,"why":"Disclosure to an unauthorised person: physical access control would have prevented it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (4)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A receptionist with full rights accidentally changes a glucose result from 5.2 to 52.</b></p>","options":["Data integrity","Data security","Preventing data corruption"],"correct":0,"why":"Only authorised users should modify data; read-only rights for reception would keep results accurate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (5)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>Lab technicians enter results, but only the senior scientist may amend a result after it is approved.</b></p>","options":["Data security","Preventing data corruption","Data integrity"],"correct":2,"why":"Restricting who may modify data keeps it accurate and consistent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (6)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>Two staff update the same patient record in different ways, and nobody can tell which is correct.</b></p>","options":["Data security","Preventing data corruption","Data integrity"],"correct":2,"why":"Data should be accurate and consistent; limiting who may modify records protects integrity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (7)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A hacker guesses a weak password and plants a virus that scrambles the results database.</b></p>","options":["Data integrity","Preventing data corruption","Data security"],"correct":1,"why":"Malicious access led to corrupted data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security, integrity or corruption? (8)","q":"<p>Which purpose of access control does this incident relate to?</p><p><b>A visitor plugs an infected USB drive into an unlocked lab PC, and files are damaged.</b></p>","options":["Data security","Data integrity","Preventing data corruption"],"correct":2,"why":"Physical and logical access controls reduce vulnerability to viruses that corrupt data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Security terms","q":"<p>SO 2.17 asks you to explain security terms. Match each term to its meaning.</p>","left":["Data security","Password","Authentication","Encryption","Data corruption","Data integrity","Authorisation","Accountability"],"right":["A secret string of characters used to prove identity to a system","Tracing actions and changes in a system to an individual user","Verifying that a user is who they claim to be","The accuracy, completeness and consistency of data over its life","Granting a verified user the access rights for their role","Protecting data from unauthorised access, change, disclosure or loss","Converting plaintext into ciphertext using an algorithm and a key","Unintended alteration of data that makes it incorrect or unreadable"],"correct":[5,0,2,6,7,3,4,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Securing the clinic (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the IT consultant for the Harbourview Medical Clinic on Hincks Street, Bridgetown. It has a small server, six PCs and paper-free patient records. Make the best security decisions.</p><p>The server sits under the reception desk, where visitors wait. The owner asks what to do first.</p>","options":["Leave it there; a strong password on the server is enough.","Move the server into a locked room opened only by staff key cards, with a UPS."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A password does nothing if someone unplugs the server and carries it away.","Good. Physical access control and power protection come first: anyone at reception could otherwise reach the hardware."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Securing the clinic (step 2)","q":"<p>Staff all log in with the shared account \"clinic\" and password \"harbour1\". What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Keep the shared account but change the password to \"Harbour2\".","Give each person a unique user ID, require effective passwords, and set access rights by role."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["The password is still obvious, and a shared account means nobody can be held responsible for changes.","Excellent. Unique IDs make the audit trail meaningful, and role-based rights limit what each user can see."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Securing the clinic (step 3)","q":"<p>The clinic backs up to a USB drive kept in the same room as the server. Patient forms often contain impossible dates of birth. What next?</p>","options":["Nothing more is needed; the server room is locked.","Encrypt the backups, store a copy off-site, and add validation checks to the patient form."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["One hurricane, fire or burglary could destroy both server and backup, and invalid data will keep entering the system.","Strong. Encryption protects the backup if lost, off-site storage protects against fire or hurricane, and validation protects integrity."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Security or integrity?","q":"<p>The Statia Fuel Depot's database is fully protected by key cards, passwords and encryption, and no outsider has ever accessed it. However, managers find that several delivery quantities were entered with an extra zero. Which concept has been compromised?</p>","options":["Data security, because the data is incorrect","Encryption, because the data was scrambled","Authentication, because the staff were not verified","Data integrity, because the data is not accurate"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Security is about protecting data from unauthorised access or loss. The access controls worked; the problem is accuracy.","Nothing was scrambled. The values were entered wrongly by authorised staff.","The staff were authorised users who logged in correctly. Their input was simply wrong.","Correct. Integrity is about accuracy and consistency. A range check or verification step would help."]}]},{"id":"assess_57l","title":"Module 2 · Unit 9 quiz: The Internet, the Web and HTML","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 9: The Internet, the Web and HTML. 113 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (1)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma in 2017, a class surveyed how people in The Valley, Anguilla, got back online. Sort each statement: does it describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>Technicians repair the cables and routers that link homes in Sandy Ground to the rest of the world.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":0,"why":"Cables and routers are network infrastructure: the Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>A student clicks from a news page about Irma to a weather report stored on another server.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving between linked hypertext documents is using the web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>A family in Blowing Point makes a video call to relatives in Curaçao.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":0,"why":"Video calling is a different service carried by the Internet, not the web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>Documents are joined to one another by hyperlinks and viewed in a browser.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":1,"why":"Interconnected hypertext documents viewed in a browser are the web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>Data travels between networks using TCP/IP.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":0,"why":"TCP/IP is the Internet's main set of protocols.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>Pages are requested from web servers using HTTP.</b></p>","options":["World Wide Web","Internet"],"correct":0,"why":"HTTP is the protocol of the web.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>Its forerunner, ARPANET, dates from 1969.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":0,"why":"The Internet is the older system; the web was built on top of it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Internet or World Wide Web? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe the Internet or the World Wide Web?</p><p><b>It was proposed by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN in 1989.</b></p>","options":["Internet","World Wide Web"],"correct":1,"why":"Berners-Lee proposed the web, which runs on the existing Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"From defence network to World Wide Web","q":"<p>The Lower Sixth class is building a timeline wall for a \"Let's Talk IT\" session at Seagrape Bay Secondary School. Put the events in the order they happened.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The US Department of Defense funds ARPANET (1969) to link research computers.","Networks around the world are joined using the TCP/IP protocols, forming the Internet.","At CERN in 1989, Sir Tim Berners-Lee proposes a hypertext system so researchers can locate and share data easily.","The first web server and browser link hypertext documents across the Internet using TCP/IP.","Early web pages carry mainly text, joined by hyperlinks.","Hyperlinks expand to video, sound, interactive programs, conferencing and online gaming."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Dante, a Lower Sixth student from San Pedro, is revising hypertext with his study group. Answer each question about what they see.</p><p>Dante reads the printed manual for his family's car from page 1 to the last page, in the order the author chose. He is processing the information...</p>","options":["through hyperlinks","as hypertext","linearly"],"correct":2,"why":"Reading from start to finish in the author's order is linear.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Belize City Motors puts the same manual online as short topic pages, and Dante clicks straight to \"Warning lights\". This is...</p>","options":["a hypertext approach: the user chooses the order and selection of topics","a linear approach","a home page"],"correct":0,"why":"Hypertext lets the user, not the author, choose the order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>On a Flamboyant Heights College course page, Aaliyah moves her pointer over a photo of the library and the arrow becomes a small pointing hand. The photo is...</p>","options":["a hyperlink","a broken image","a plug-in"],"correct":0,"why":"Images can be links; the hand pointer shows a link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Aaliyah clicks a link on a St. John’s page and a document loads from a server in Europe, though she never sees where it came from. Why does hypertext suit networks so well?</p>","options":["The user only needs to activate the link, not know where the document is stored","All the documents are on one computer","The user must type the server's IP address first"],"correct":0,"why":"The location is largely unseen: the user just activates the link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A link on a Belize tourism page jumps to the \"Beaches\" heading lower down the same page. A hyperlink can point to...</p>","options":["another place in the same document or a different document","only an image","only another web site"],"correct":0,"why":"Links can target the same document or a different one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>Kemar says: \"The web and the Internet are the same thing.\" The best correction is...</p>","options":["The web is the cables and the Internet is the pages","The web is a service, a subset, accessed on the Internet","The Internet is a service that runs on the web"],"correct":1,"why":"The WWW is one service on the Internet, not the whole Internet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Hypertext and hyperlinks quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>A gamer in San Pedro clicks a link that opens an online game in her browser. This shows that hyperlinks...</p>","options":["have expanded to video, sound, interactive programs and gaming","still carry text only","no longer use the Internet"],"correct":0,"why":"The original web was text-only; links now reach any form of information.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the web term","q":"<p>A class is mapping the web for the Gros Islet Guest House. Match each term to the description that fits it best.</p>","left":["Internet","World Wide Web","Web browser","Hyperlink","Web page","Web site","Home page"],"right":["Software that requests, renders and displays web pages","A service of interconnected hypertext documents that runs on the network","The main entry page that loads when only the domain name is typed","A single hypertext document with its own URL","The global network of interconnected networks that carries the data","Clickable text or image that loads another document","A collection of related pages under one domain name"],"correct":[4,1,0,5,3,6,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (1)","q":"<p>A class is auditing web pages used around the Caribbean. Sort each page: is it static or dynamic?</p><p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>Grand Anse Guest House \"Our history\" page shows the same text to every visitor all year.</b></p>","options":["Static page","Dynamic page"],"correct":0,"why":"Same content every time it is viewed: static.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>The Bequia Ferries page shows today's departure times, updated as each boat leaves.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic page","Static page"],"correct":0,"why":"Content that changes each time it is accessed: dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>A student logs in to the Sixth Form portal and sees her own name and her own assignments.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic page","Static page"],"correct":0,"why":"Built for each visitor from stored data: dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>The Montego Bay Bakery page lists its address and opening hours; the owner edits it twice a year.</b></p>","options":["Static page","Dynamic page"],"correct":0,"why":"It changes only when the author edits the file: static.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>A weather page for Bridgetown shows a new temperature every time it is refreshed.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic page","Static page"],"correct":0,"why":"New data on each visit: dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>A Flamboyant Heights College student's one-page CV, unchanged since she uploaded it.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic page","Static page"],"correct":1,"why":"A stored file sent exactly as written: static.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>An online shop page shows a different basket total for every customer.</b></p>","options":["Static page","Dynamic page"],"correct":1,"why":"Different content for each visitor: dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic page? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a static or a dynamic web page?</p><p><b>The menu of a King George V Street café, saved once as an HTML file.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic page","Static page"],"correct":1,"why":"Same content each time: static.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Kiara, a Lower Sixth student at Sea-Almond Secondary School, is helping the Seaside Pharmacy in San Fernando understand its new web site. Answer each question.</p><p>She types https://www.seasidepharmacy.tt/services/refills.html. Which part tells the browser to use secure, encrypted rules?</p>","options":["refills.html","https://","www.seasidepharmacy.tt"],"correct":1,"why":"https:// is the protocol (scheme). The s means HTTP secured with encryption.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>In the same URL, /services/ is the...</p>","options":["path (folder on the server)","top-level domain","domain name"],"correct":0,"why":"The path names the folder on the server that holds the file.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>The .sx at the end of the domain name shows...</p>","options":["the protocol","the country-code top-level domain for Sint Maarten","the file type of the page"],"correct":1,"why":".sx is the TLD for Sint Maarten, like .jm for Jamaica and .tt for Trinidad and Tobago.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>A customer mistypes the file name as refils.html. The domain works, but the server cannot find that file. Which status code comes back?</p>","options":["200 OK","500 server error","404 Not Found"],"correct":2,"why":"404 means the server was reached but no file exists at that address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>The refill form sends a customer's name and prescription number. Which HTTP method sends the data in the body of the request rather than in the URL?</p>","options":["GET","POST","DNS"],"correct":1,"why":"POST sends data in the request body. GET adds it to the URL, which is fine for searches but not private data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>The pharmacy's server treats every request as brand new, with no memory of the customer's last click. HTTP is therefore...</p>","options":["case-sensitive","encrypted","stateless"],"correct":2,"why":"HTTP is stateless: each request is handled on its own.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTTP and URL quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>The browser asks the server for the page and the server replies with a status code and the file. This model is called...</p>","options":["request-response","store and forward","peer-to-peer"],"correct":0,"why":"HTTP works on a request-response model between client (browser) and server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading a URL","q":"<p>A student at Sandbox Tree College types https://www.coralreeftours.bb/trips/snorkel.html into a browser. Which part of this URL identifies the SERVER that holds the page?</p>","options":["https://","www.coralreeftours.bb","snorkel.html","/trips/"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is the protocol. It says how to communicate (secure HTTP), not which server to contact.","Correct. This host name (the domain name coralreeftours.bb with the www prefix) identifies the server; it is later resolved to an IP address.","This is the file name of the page on the server. It identifies the document, not the machine.","This is the path: a folder on the server, not the server itself."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (1)","q":"<p>Kiara, a Lower Sixth student, is explaining to the owner of the Seaside Pharmacy in San Fernando how his new web site works. Sort each description.</p><p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Stores the pharmacy's pages so any device on the Internet can reach them.</b></p>","options":["Web browser","Web server"],"correct":1,"why":"Storing pages and making them available is the server's job.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>The app on a customer's phone that retrieves the refill page and renders it on screen.</b></p>","options":["Web server","Web browser"],"correct":1,"why":"Retrieving and rendering pages is what a browser does.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Receives an HTTP request for /index.html and sends back the file.</b></p>","options":["Web browser","Web server"],"correct":1,"why":"The server answers HTTP requests.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Shows the URL of the current page in its address bar.</b></p>","options":["Web server","Web browser"],"correct":1,"why":"The address bar is part of the browser.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Lets a Flamboyant Heights College student go back and forward through the pages she has traversed.</b></p>","options":["Web browser","Web server"],"correct":0,"why":"Traversing information resources is a browser function.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>A computer in a data centre that hosts the web sites of several Caribbean businesses.</b></p>","options":["Web server","Web browser"],"correct":0,"why":"A computer that hosts sites is a web server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Opens an HTML file saved in the student's own Documents folder.</b></p>","options":["Web server","Web browser"],"correct":1,"why":"Browsers can also open files in the local file system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Web server or web browser? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe a web server or a web browser?</p><p><b>Holds the pages of a school intranet so staff on the private network can view them.</b></p>","options":["Web browser","Web server"],"correct":1,"why":"Servers can serve private networks too.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the URL protocol","q":"<p>Your teacher is building a links page for her Sixth Form class. Match each URL prefix to the task it is used for.</p>","left":["http:","https:","file:","ftp:","mailto:","news:","telnet:"],"right":["Click \"Email us\" to open a new message to Grenada Kayak Tours","Open a practice web page saved on your own C: drive","Use your computer as a terminal on a remote computer","Join a Usenet newsgroup where comments are organised by topic","Request an ordinary HTML document from a WWW server, without encryption","Log in to an online banking page so the password is encrypted","Download a timetable stored on an anonymous file-transfer server"],"correct":[4,5,1,6,0,3,2]},{"t":"ma","title":"Check the markup facts","q":"<p>Tyrese, a Flamboyant Heights College student, wrote these revision cards on HTML, markup and plug-ins for his study group in Arima. Some contain errors.</p><p>Select EVERY card that is correct.</p>","options":["Card G: A plug-in is a helper application the browser calls for a file with a particular extension, such as a video clip.","Card C: HTML is a programming language because it uses loops to display tags.","Card B: A markup language describes the content and structure of a document.","Card D: Standard tags let any browser on any platform display a page in much the same way.","Card F: Extra paragraphs may be added after the closing html tag.","Card A: A web page is a text file written in HyperText Markup Language.","Card E: The root element of an HTML document is the body element.","Card H: The opening html tag tells any device reading the file that it is written in HTML."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"why":"Correct. HTML is a markup language of standard tags; html is the root element; nothing follows the closing html tag; plug-ins are helper applications."},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (1)","q":"<p>Island Data Services in Oranjestad builds both web pages and data feeds. Sort each statement.</p><p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Its main purpose is to display content in a browser.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":0,"why":"Displaying content is the role of HTML.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (2)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>The author invents tags such as cruiseship and arrivaltime.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":1,"why":"User-defined tags are what make XML extensible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (3)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Uses a fixed set of tags such as p, h1 and table.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":0,"why":"HTML tags are predefined.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (4)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Used to send port schedules from one computer system to another.</b></p>","options":["XML","HTML"],"correct":0,"why":"Transporting data between systems is a typical XML use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (5)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>A file fails if a single tag is left unclosed.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":1,"why":"XML is strict: it must be well-formed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (6)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Browsers will usually still show the page even if a closing tag is missing.</b></p>","options":["XML","HTML"],"correct":1,"why":"Browsers are tolerant of HTML errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (7)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Tags are case-sensitive, so Status and status are different.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":1,"why":"XML is case-sensitive; HTML is not.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (8)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Describes how a heading, a list and an image are laid out on a page.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":0,"why":"Structuring and presenting a page is HTML.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (9)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Island Data Services uses it to create a new markup language for cruise schedules, with rules that are enforced.</b></p>","options":["HTML","XML"],"correct":1,"why":"XML is a language for creating markup languages whose rules are enforced.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (10)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>Supports many rules but has no mechanism to enforce them, so the browser does its best with mistakes.</b></p>","options":["XML","HTML"],"correct":1,"why":"HTML rules are not enforced; browsers tolerate errors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"HTML or XML? (11)","q":"<p>Does this describe HTML or XML?</p><p><b>A cruise schedule file that a port officer and a computer program can both read easily.</b></p>","options":["XML","HTML"],"correct":0,"why":"XML documents are designed to be both human-readable and machine-readable.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Resolve the domain name","q":"<p>A visitor at the Belize City cruise pier types the address of the Albert Street Jewels web site into her phone. Put the steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["She types the URL and the browser extracts the domain name.","The browser checks its cache and finds no stored IP address.","The request goes to the ISP's DNS resolver.","The resolver asks a root server, which refers it to the .bz TLD server.","The TLD server refers the resolver to the domain's authoritative name server.","The authoritative server returns the IP address, which is passed back to the browser.","The browser sends an HTTP request to that IP address and the page is returned."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"What DNS really does","q":"<p>Students at Coralstone College can reach a web site by typing its IP address directly, but typing its domain name gives an error. Which component is MOST likely failing?</p>","options":["The Domain Name System (DNS) resolution","The HTTP protocol on the web server","The browser's ability to follow hyperlinks","The HTML code of the home page"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The server is reachable by IP, so the network and server work. Only the name-to-IP translation is failing.","If HTTP were failing, the page would not load by IP address either.","No link is involved here; the students type the address directly.","Faulty HTML would affect how the page displays, not whether it can be reached by name."]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Malik works part-time at the Holetown Computer Repairs. Help him answer customers' questions about addresses and names.</p><p>Malik reads three addresses from customers' laptops. Which one is a valid IPv4 address?</p>","options":["10.4.7","205.239.115.148","192.168.1.256"],"correct":1,"why":"Four numbers from 0 to 255, separated by three dots. 256 is too high, and 10.4.7 has only three numbers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>A customer in Speightstown notices her home connection gets a different IP address every time it reconnects. She has a...</p>","options":["dynamic IP address","domain name","static IP address"],"correct":0,"why":"An address that changes each time you log on is dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>The Fort Street Jewels web server must keep the same address so its domain name always points to it. It needs a...</p>","options":["static IP address","new domain suffix","dynamic IP address"],"correct":0,"why":"A static address is always the same.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Who assigns a home in Oistins its IP address on the Internet?</p>","options":["the web browser","the home page","its Internet Service Provider (ISP)"],"correct":2,"why":"The ISP assigns the static or dynamic address.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>A business in Port of Spain uses a name ending in .tt. This country-code suffix mainly helps identify...</p>","options":["that the page is static","that the site is non-profit","the location and audience of the site"],"correct":2,"why":"Country codes such as .tt and .sx point to location and audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>A Barbados animal-rescue charity registers a name ending in .org. This suffix is often used by...</p>","options":["non-profit organisations","web browsers","commercial shops"],"correct":0,"why":".org is often used by non-profit organisations; .com by commercial sites.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>In https://www.anguillakayaks.com/tours, which part is the domain name, strictly speaking?</p>","options":["www.anguillakayaks.com/tours","https://www","anguillakayaks.com"],"correct":2,"why":"The domain name starts after // and www is not part of it; it ends at the single slash.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"IP address and domain name quick-fire (8)","q":"<p>Why do customers type anguillakayaks.com instead of the server's IP address?</p>","options":["Domain names are easier to remember than numbers","Browsers cannot use IP addresses","IP addresses are secret"],"correct":0,"why":"Names are easier to recall; DNS turns them back into IP addresses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Static or dynamic IP address?","q":"<p>The Gros Islet Ferries company runs its booking web site on its own server in Rodney Bay. Customers reach it by domain name, and the owner wants that name to keep pointing to the server without anyone updating records. Which type of IP address should the ISP assign to the server?</p>","options":["A static IP address","A dynamic IP address","No IP address, because customers use the domain name","A private address beginning 192.168"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. A static address is always the same, so the domain name keeps resolving to the right server.","A dynamic address can change every time the server reconnects, so the name could end up pointing to the wrong place.","Every computer on the Internet needs an IP address; DNS translates the domain name into it.","Private addresses are used inside local networks and cannot be reached directly from the open Internet."]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (1)","q":"<p>Kiara in Frigate Bay is sending a large photo to her teacher at Seagrape Bay Secondary School. Sort each job: is it done by TCP or by IP?</p><p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Checks that every packet of Kiara's photo arrived at her teacher's computer.</b></p>","options":["IP","TCP"],"correct":1,"why":"Verifying delivery is TCP's job.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (2)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Moves each packet from node to node across the network towards its destination.</b></p>","options":["TCP","IP"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving packets between nodes is IP's job.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (3)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Asks for packet 7 to be sent again because it never arrived.</b></p>","options":["IP","TCP"],"correct":1,"why":"Re-requesting missing packets is part of verifying delivery: TCP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (4)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Labels each packet with the sender's and receiver's IP addresses.</b></p>","options":["IP","TCP"],"correct":0,"why":"Addressing packets so they can be routed is IP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (5)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Puts the packets back in the correct order before the photo is opened.</b></p>","options":["TCP","IP"],"correct":0,"why":"Reassembling in order is TCP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (6)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Lets routers between Jamaica and Miami pass each packet on to the next node.</b></p>","options":["TCP","IP"],"correct":1,"why":"Forwarding packets between nodes is IP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (7)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Verifies that a downloaded lecture file is complete before it is saved.</b></p>","options":["TCP","IP"],"correct":0,"why":"Verified delivery is TCP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"TCP or IP? (8)","q":"<p>Is this the job of TCP or IP?</p><p><b>Gives each device a numeric address so packets can be routed to it.</b></p>","options":["TCP","IP"],"correct":1,"why":"IP addresses belong to the Internet Protocol.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the correct HTML statements","q":"<p>Jaden, a Lower Sixth student in Kingston, wrote these notes about HTML structure for his study group. Some are wrong.</p><p>Select EVERY statement that is correct.</p>","options":["1. The title element belongs inside the head section and appears on the browser tab.","7. The last element opened should be the first one closed.","3. The html element is the root element that contains the head and the body.","5. Visible content such as headings and paragraphs belongs in the head section.","2. Attributes are written inside the end tag of an element.","4. The br and img elements are empty elements with no closing tag.","6. An attribute is written as a name=\"value\" pair, for example width=\"300\"."],"correct":[0,1,2,5,6],"why":"Correct. Title in the head; html is the root; br and img are empty; attributes are name=\"value\" pairs in the start tag; nest last-in, first-out."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the formatting element","q":"<p>Aiden is coding the Caroni Swamp Clean-up notice for his community group. Match each thing he wants on the page to the HTML element that does it.</p>","left":["The warning \"No glass on the beach\" must stand out as important","The name of the volunteer boat, Island Spirit, in italics","The 2 in CO2, written lower and smaller, in a note on emissions","The 2 in \"10 squared tonnes of rubbish\", written raised","The clean-up date highlighted so it catches the eye","The insurance conditions at the bottom in smaller text","A dividing line between the \"When\" and \"What to bring\" sections","A new line for \"Meet at the bridge\" inside the same paragraph"],"right":["br","i","sup","strong","sub","small","mark","hr"],"correct":[3,1,4,2,6,5,7,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Aaliyah is building a page for the Saba Hiking Club. Choose the HTML she needs each time.</p><p>The largest, most important heading on the page uses...</p>","options":["h1","h6","head"],"correct":0,"why":"h1 is the top-level heading. The head element is the non-visible section of the document.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>To force a new line inside a paragraph without starting a new paragraph, she uses...</p>","options":["p","br","hr"],"correct":1,"why":"br is a line break. hr draws a horizontal line; p starts a new paragraph.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A note to other developers that will not appear on the page is written as...</p>","options":["a comment opened with an exclamation mark and two dashes","a title element","a small element"],"correct":0,"why":"Comments open with less-than, exclamation mark, two dashes, and are not displayed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>To make one heading dark green using inline styling, she adds...</p>","options":["style=\"color: darkgreen;\"","colour=\"darkgreen\"","style=\"text-color = darkgreen\""],"correct":0,"why":"The style attribute takes property: value pairs, and the property is spelt color.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>To centre a paragraph, the style property is...</p>","options":["text-align","align-text","center-text"],"correct":0,"why":"text-align: center; aligns text horizontally.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Tag quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>The text shown on the browser tab comes from the...</p>","options":["title element in the head","h1 element","first paragraph"],"correct":0,"why":"The title element in the head sets the tab text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Shanice is building a hurricane-preparedness page for the Bridgetown Neighbourhood Watch. Choose the HTML she needs each time.</p><p>The steps to take before a storm (secure shutters, charge phones, fill water tanks) must be done in order. Which list?</p>","options":["ul","dl","ol"],"correct":2,"why":"An ordered list (ol) numbers the steps because order matters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>The hurricane-kit items (water, torch, batteries) can appear in any order. Which list?</p>","options":["ol","ul","dl"],"correct":1,"why":"An unordered list (ul) gives bullets when order does not matter.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>A glossary of storm terms (watch, warning, storm surge) with a meaning for each needs...</p>","options":["ol with li","ul with li","dl with dt and dd"],"correct":2,"why":"A description list pairs each term (dt) with its description (dd).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>Each bullet or numbered step inside ul or ol is marked with...</p>","options":["td","li","dt"],"correct":1,"why":"li is the list item element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>She wants the steps lettered A, B, C instead of 1, 2, 3. She adds to the ol start tag...</p>","options":["type=\"A\"","start=\"A\"","style=\"A\""],"correct":0,"why":"The type attribute sets the numbering style; start sets the first number.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>She adds a photo of the hurricane shelter. Which attribute names the image file?</p>","options":["src","href","alt"],"correct":0,"why":"src (source) gives the file name, path or URL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (7)","q":"<p>A blind resident uses a screen reader. Which attribute gives the description it reads aloud?</p>","options":["width","title","alt"],"correct":2,"why":"alt text is read by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Lists and images quick-fire (8)","q":"<p>Which statement about the img element is true?</p>","options":["It is an empty element with no end tag.","It must be closed with an end tag.","It stores the picture inside the HTML file."],"correct":0,"why":"img is empty. The browser makes a separate HTTP request for the file named in src.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Images that fail to load","q":"<p>A page on the Anguilla Boat Tours site shows the words \"Catamaran at Shoal Bay\" where a photo should be. Which statement BEST explains this?</p>","options":["The width attribute was set too small for the picture to appear.","The alt attribute was left out, so the browser printed the file name.","The img element needs a closing tag to display the picture.","The src path is wrong or the file is missing, so the browser displays the alt text instead."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A small width would show a small picture, not replace it with text.","The text shown is a description, which is exactly what alt holds. Leaving out alt would not create this text.","img is an empty element with no closing tag; that is not the problem.","Correct. Alt text is shown when the image file cannot be loaded."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the table errors","q":"<p>Devon, a student in Belize City, coded a ferry timetable for San Pedro Ferries. He wants three columns (Day, Depart, Fare) and a final row saying \"No sailings on public holidays\" across the full width. His teacher describes each line of his code.</p><p>Select EVERY line that contains an error.</p>","options":["8. The code ends with a table end tag.","1. The table starts with a table start tag that has border=\"1\".","7. The heading cells are written with td and given the attribute header=\"yes\".","5. The Tuesday row is a tr containing only two td cells, although the table has three columns.","2. A caption element comes straight after the table start tag.","3. The header row is a tr containing three th cells: Day, Depart, Fare.","6. The last row is a tr with one td that has colspan=\"3\".","4. The Monday data is written as three td cells placed directly inside table, with no tr around them."],"correct":[2,3,7],"why":"Correct. Every cell must sit inside a tr, every row needs the same number of cells (or a colspan to make up the gap), and header cells use th."},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (1)","q":"<p>Mr. Richardson's IT class at a school in Basseterre, St. Kitts, is revising HTML vocabulary. Sort each item.</p><p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>td</b></p>","options":["Attribute","Element"],"correct":1,"why":"td is the table data element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (2)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>colspan</b></p>","options":["Attribute","Element"],"correct":0,"why":"colspan is an attribute of a cell that sets how many columns it spans.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (3)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>href</b></p>","options":["Element","Attribute"],"correct":1,"why":"href is the attribute that holds a link's destination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (4)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>li</b></p>","options":["Attribute","Element"],"correct":1,"why":"li is the list item element.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (5)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>alt</b></p>","options":["Element","Attribute"],"correct":1,"why":"alt is the alternative-text attribute of img.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (6)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>caption</b></p>","options":["Element","Attribute"],"correct":0,"why":"caption is an element that gives a table its title.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (7)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>style</b></p>","options":["Attribute","Element"],"correct":0,"why":"style is the attribute used for inline styling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Element or attribute? (8)","q":"<p>Is this an element or an attribute?</p><p><b>th</b></p>","options":["Element","Attribute"],"correct":0,"why":"th is the table header cell element.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the form control","q":"<p>Dominica Eco-Lodge wants an online booking form. Match each piece of information to the best control.</p>","left":["Guest's full name","Account password","Room type: single, double or family (choose one)","Extras: breakfast, airport pick-up, guided hike (choose any)","Country of residence from a long list","Special requests in several sentences","Send the booking to the server"],"right":["input type=\"submit\"","textarea","select with option elements","input type=\"checkbox\"","input type=\"radio\" with a shared name","input type=\"password\"","input type=\"text\""],"correct":[6,5,4,3,2,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (1)","q":"<p>The Bridge Street Craft Market is adding links across its small web site. Sort each link by type.</p><p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>The Products page links to contact.html, stored in the same folder.</b></p>","options":["Relative link","Absolute link","Email link","Bookmark link"],"correct":0,"why":"A file name on the same site with no protocol is a relative link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>The Contact page links to https://www.example.org/library on another site.</b></p>","options":["Relative link","Email link","Absolute link","Bookmark link"],"correct":2,"why":"A full URL with protocol and domain name is an absolute link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>A \"Back to top\" link at the foot of a long page uses href=\"#top\".</b></p>","options":["Relative link","Bookmark link","Email link","Absolute link"],"correct":1,"why":"# followed by an id jumps within the same page: a bookmark link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>An \"Email the manager\" link uses href=\"mailto:manager@example.sx\".</b></p>","options":["Email link","Bookmark link","Absolute link","Relative link"],"correct":0,"why":"mailto: opens the visitor's email program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>The menu jumps to the \"Opening hours\" section further down the same page.</b></p>","options":["Relative link","Bookmark link","Email link","Absolute link"],"correct":1,"why":"Moving to a section of the same page needs a bookmark link to an id.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>Every page has a navigation bar with href=\"index.html\" to return to the home page.</b></p>","options":["Absolute link","Bookmark link","Email link","Relative link"],"correct":3,"why":"index.html on the same site is a relative link; it keeps working if the folder moves to a new server.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>A link to a weather service uses a full address beginning https://.</b></p>","options":["Relative link","Absolute link","Email link","Bookmark link"],"correct":1,"why":"Full URLs to other sites are absolute links, often opened with target=\"_blank\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of link? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of hyperlink is this?</p><p><b>Clicking \"Ask about custom orders\" opens a new message addressed to orders@example.sx.</b></p>","options":["Email link","Relative link","Bookmark link","Absolute link"],"correct":0,"why":"Opening an email program addressed to someone is a mailto: link.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right link","q":"<p>The Bonaire Dive Centre has a long single page. The owner wants a link at the top that jumps straight to the \"Safety rules\" section further down the same page. Which code achieves this?</p>","options":["Use href=\"mailto:safety\"","Give the section heading id=\"safety\", then link to it with href=\"#safety\"","Add target=\"_blank\" to the safety heading","Use href=\"safety.html\" pointing to the section"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["mailto opens an email program; it does not move within a page.","Correct. A bookmark link uses # followed by the id of the target element on the same page.","target controls where a linked page opens. It does not create a link or a destination.","That links to a separate file called safety.html, not to a section of the current page."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build the Montego Bay Youth Football page (step 1)","q":"<p>The Montego Bay Youth Football Club asks you to build its first web page before the new season. Make the best decisions.</p><p>You open a blank file in a text editor. What do you write first?</p>","options":["The DOCTYPE declaration, then html containing a head (with a title) and a body.","Start straight away with the fixtures table; structure can be added later."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. A correct document structure is the foundation of every page.","Without html, head and body the document is not properly structured, and there is no page title."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build the Montego Bay Youth Football page (step 2)","q":"<p>The coach wants the weekly fixtures (date, opponent, venue) on the page. How do you present them?</p>","options":["One long paragraph with all the matches separated by commas.","A table with a header row of th cells and one tr per match with td cells."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Players cannot scan a paragraph quickly. Structured data belongs in a table.","Strong choice. Data with the same fields for each match suits rows and columns."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build the Montego Bay Youth Football page (step 3)","q":"<p>Parents must register players online, choosing one age group (U10, U12 or U14). Which form control do you use for the age group?</p>","options":["Radio buttons sharing the same name, plus a submit button, inside a form with method=\"post\".","Checkboxes, so parents can tick as many age groups as they like."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. One choice from a few options is a radio group; post keeps personal data out of the URL.","A player belongs to one age group only. Checkboxes allow several selections."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build the Montego Bay Youth Football page (step 4)","q":"<p>Finally, the club wants its logo at the top and a link to the league's web site. What do you write?</p>","options":["An img with src and alt=\"Montego Bay Youth Football logo\", and an a element with the league's full URL in href and target=\"_blank\".","An img with only src, and the league's name typed in bold with no a element."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. alt makes the logo accessible, and an absolute link opens the external site in a new tab.","Without alt the logo is inaccessible, and bold text is not a hyperlink."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 3","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_5g4","title":"Module 3 · Unit 1 quiz: Problem-solving and its stages","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 1: Problem-solving and its stages. 67 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (1)","q":"<p>Each card comes from a problem in a Caribbean community. Decide whether it describes the current state (how things are now), the desired state (how the people affected want things to be) or only a symptom (a visible sign of the problem).</p><p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>The Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet tracks insulin stock on paper and only reorders when the shelf is empty</b></p>","options":["Symptom","Current state","Desired state"],"correct":1,"why":"This is how things work at the pharmacy now: the current state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (2)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Seaside Pharmacy always has insulin in stock when a patient arrives with a prescription</b></p>","options":["Desired state","Current state","Symptom"],"correct":0,"why":"This is how the pharmacy wants things to be: the desired state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (3)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Patients leave Seaside Pharmacy angry and empty-handed twice a week</b></p>","options":["Desired state","Symptom","Current state"],"correct":1,"why":"Angry, turned-away patients are a visible sign that something is wrong. They point to the problem but are not the problem itself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (4)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Ms. Ollivierre's tomato plants in Mesopotamia are infested with whitefly and are producing a third less fruit</b></p>","options":["Symptom","Current state","Desired state"],"correct":1,"why":"This describes her situation now: the current state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (5)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Ms. Ollivierre harvests a full crop and meets every weekly order from the Calliaqua restaurants</b></p>","options":["Current state","Desired state","Symptom"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the outcome she wants: the desired state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (6)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Yellow, curling patches appear on Ms. Ollivierre's tomato leaves</b></p>","options":["Current state","Symptom","Desired state"],"correct":1,"why":"Curling, yellow leaves are what you can see. They are a symptom; the whitefly infestation and its effect on her harvest are the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (7)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Bookings at the Carenage Water Taxi are written in one paper diary shared by two staff</b></p>","options":["Desired state","Current state","Symptom"],"correct":1,"why":"This is how bookings are handled now: the current state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (8)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Every cruise passenger booked with Carenage Water Taxi is collected on time at the St. George’s cruise port</b></p>","options":["Desired state","Symptom","Current state"],"correct":0,"why":"This is what the company wants to achieve: the desired state.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Current state, desired state or symptom? (9)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem does this card describe?</p><p><b>Long queues and loud complaints build up at the water taxi desk on cruise-ship days</b></p>","options":["Desired state","Symptom","Current state"],"correct":1,"why":"Queues and complaints are visible signs. The underlying problem is the double-booking caused by the paper diary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (1)","q":"<p>Business owners and clubs around the Caribbean are tackling problems. Decide whether each approach follows a structured problem-solving process or is unstructured trial and error.</p><p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>The owner of the Belmopan Bakery buys a new oven the day sales fall, without checking why customers stopped coming</b></p>","options":["Structured problem-solving process","Trial and error"],"correct":1,"why":"This jumps straight to the first idea without defining or analysing the problem: trial and error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (2)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>The IT club at Seagrape Bay Secondary School writes down exactly who is affected by the slow printer queue, and how, before suggesting any fixes</b></p>","options":["Trial and error","Structured problem-solving process"],"correct":1,"why":"Defining the problem before acting is the first stage of a structured process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (3)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>Arima homework club compares three ways to speed up its laptops before choosing one</b></p>","options":["Structured problem-solving process","Trial and error"],"correct":0,"why":"Generating and comparing several options is part of a structured process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (4)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>A Bridge Street shop owner keeps buying faster routers because the card machine fails, but never checks what is causing the failures</b></p>","options":["Structured problem-solving process","Trial and error"],"correct":1,"why":"This treats the symptom again and again without finding the cause: trial and error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (5)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>Three months after installing a booking system, the Carenage Water Taxi counts how many double bookings still happen</b></p>","options":["Trial and error","Structured problem-solving process"],"correct":1,"why":"Checking the outcome against the original problem is evaluation and review, the last stage of the process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (6)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>The Harbourview Grill in Kingston tries one ordering app, then another, then another, with no record of why each failed</b></p>","options":["Trial and error","Structured problem-solving process"],"correct":0,"why":"With no analysis or review, each attempt is a guess and money is wasted: trial and error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (7)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>The manager of the Seaside Pharmacy explains to the owner, with reasons, why an inventory app was chosen over a larger weekly order</b></p>","options":["Structured problem-solving process","Trial and error"],"correct":0,"why":"A choice justified with reasons is a feature of a structured process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Structured process or trial and error? (8)","q":"<p>Which approach is this?</p><p><b>A farmer sprays whatever chemical the farm shop has in stock and never checks whether the damage has stopped</b></p>","options":["Trial and error","Structured problem-solving process"],"correct":0,"why":"No definition, no comparison and no review: this is trial and error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What makes it a process?","q":"<p>Stock keeps running out on cruise days at two Bay Street souvenir shops. Shop A's owner buys a new till at once. Shop B's owner defines the problem, finds its causes, compares three options, chooses one with reasons, installs it and checks the results. Why is Shop B's approach called a problem-solving process?</p>","options":["It required Shop B's owner to write a computer program.","It started by buying the best technology available.","It follows an ordered set of stages, from understanding the problem to checking that the chosen solution worked.","It produces one correct answer, like a mathematics problem."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Shop B did not write any program. Problem-solving is a general process; programming is only one possible solution.","That describes Shop A. Buying technology first skips defining and analysing the problem, which is the trial-and-error approach the process avoids.","Correct. Shop B moved through ordered stages, each building on the last and ending with evaluation and review. Shop A jumped straight to a solution.","Shop B compared three options, which shows that real problems usually have several possible solutions. The process helps choose the optimal one, not find a single \"correct\" answer."]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the stages in order","q":"<p>Arrange the six stages of the problem-solving process in the order given in the CAPE syllabus.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Define the problem","Analyse the problem","Identify and evaluate possible solutions","Select and justify the optimal solution","Implement","Evaluate and review"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the true problem statements","q":"<p>A class at Hummingbird Ridge High School drafted problem statements for different scenarios. Some are proper problem statements; others are symptoms on their own or solutions in disguise.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a complete problem statement (who/what affected, how, and consequence) and does not name a solution.</p>","options":["1. Ms. Ollivierre's tomato crop in Mesopotamia is infested with whitefly, cutting her harvest by a third; if it continues she will lose her restaurant contracts.","6. Cruise passengers booked with the Carenage Water Taxi are left waiting because boats are double-booked, about five groups a week; if this continues the company will lose customers and gain bad reviews.","2. The farmer needs to buy a drone to spray her field.","3. Customers at the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet are turned away twice a week because stock levels are unknown, and if this continues patients will miss medication and the pharmacy will lose business.","5. Leaves are curling.","4. The school needs a new website."],"correct":[0,1,3],"why":"Correct. Lines 1, 3 and 6 each name who is affected, how, and the consequence, without naming a fix. Lines 2 and 4 are solutions in disguise; line 5 is only a symptom."},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (1)","q":"<p>Each phrase comes from a problem statement written for a business or person. Sort it into the part of the statement it provides.</p><p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>Ms. Ollivierre's tomato and sweet pepper crops in Mesopotamia</b></p>","options":["Who/what is affected","How they are affected","Consequence if not fixed"],"correct":0,"why":"This names who and what is affected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (2)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>whitefly damage has reduced the harvest by one third</b></p>","options":["How they are affected","Who/what is affected","Consequence if not fixed"],"correct":0,"why":"This describes how the crops are affected, with a measurement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (3)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>she will lose her two restaurant contracts and her main income</b></p>","options":["Who/what is affected","Consequence if not fixed","How they are affected"],"correct":1,"why":"This is what happens if nothing is done: the consequence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (4)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>cruise passengers booked with San Pedro Water Taxi</b></p>","options":["How they are affected","Who/what is affected","Consequence if not fixed"],"correct":1,"why":"These are the people affected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (5)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>boats are double-booked, leaving about five groups a week waiting at the pier</b></p>","options":["Consequence if not fixed","Who/what is affected","How they are affected"],"correct":2,"why":"This is how they are affected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (6)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>the company will lose customers to competitors and receive poor reviews</b></p>","options":["How they are affected","Consequence if not fixed","Who/what is affected"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the consequence of leaving the problem unsolved.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (7)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>students in the San Fernando homework club</b></p>","options":["Consequence if not fixed","Who/what is affected","How they are affected"],"correct":1,"why":"This identifies who is affected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (8)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>the shared laptops take 15 minutes to start, cutting study time</b></p>","options":["How they are affected","Who/what is affected","Consequence if not fixed"],"correct":0,"why":"This explains how the students are affected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Take a problem statement apart (9)","q":"<p>Which part of the problem statement is this?</p><p><b>students will miss assignment deadlines and their grades may fall</b></p>","options":["Who/what is affected","Consequence if not fixed","How they are affected"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the consequence if nothing changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this?","q":"<p>Ms. Ollivierre records which plants are infested each week, notices that the worst damage is next to a patch of weeds, and lists her budget limit and the restaurants' requirement for low-residue produce. Which stage of the problem-solving process is she carrying out?</p>","options":["Identify and evaluate possible solutions","Analyse the problem","Evaluate and review","Define the problem"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["She has not suggested any solutions yet. She is still investigating the problem.","Correct. Gathering facts, finding causes (the weeds) and listing constraints (budget, buyer requirements) are analysis activities.","Evaluate and review happens after a solution has been implemented. No solution has been tried here.","Definition states what the problem is, who is affected and the consequence. Investigating causes and constraints goes deeper, which is analysis."]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the evaluation criterion","q":"<p>Problem-solvers around the Caribbean are evaluating possible solutions. Match each criterion to the comment that judges an option against it.</p>","left":["Effectiveness","Cost","Time","Feasibility","Impact and risks","Sustainability"],"right":["Chemical spray could leave residue that the Calliaqua restaurants refuse to buy","Insect-proof netting would keep almost all whitefly off Ms. Gumbs's tomatoes","The inventory app for the Seaside Pharmacy costs US$35 a month, inside its US$50 monthly budget","The Harbourside Craft Market vendors have only basic phone skills, so a complex stock system may be too hard for them to use","A shared spreadsheet could be running at the Carenage Water Taxi by tomorrow; a custom app would take six months","Removing the weeds that host whitefly keeps pest numbers down season after season"],"correct":[1,2,4,3,0,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the stage to the activity","q":"<p>The Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet keeps running out of insulin. Match each stage to what the pharmacy does in that stage.</p>","left":["Define the problem","Analyse the problem","Identify and evaluate possible solutions","Select and justify the optimal solution","Implement","Evaluate and review"],"right":["After three months, check how many patients were turned away and gather staff feedback.","Compare a stock spreadsheet, an inventory app with low-stock alerts and a larger weekly order, listing pros and cons.","Write: patients are turned away twice a week because stock is tracked on paper; if this continues, patients miss doses and the pharmacy loses trust.","Find that reorders are late because nobody sees stock levels until the shelf is empty, and note a budget limit.","Install the app, enter current stock and train the two pharmacists to use it.","Choose the inventory app because its alerts tackle the cause at an affordable cost, unlike the larger order, which ties up cash."],"correct":[2,3,1,5,4,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weigh it up and justify (1)","q":"<p>The East End Bakery loses customer cake orders written on scraps of paper. The owner has a budget of US$600 and is comparing a shared spreadsheet, an online order app and a custom-built system. Use a weighted scoring matrix and strong justification to help her choose.</p><p>Cost has a weight of 3. The shared spreadsheet scores 5 out of 5 for cost. What is its weighted score for cost?</p>","options":["8","5","15"],"correct":2,"why":"Weighted score = weight × score, so 3 × 5 = 15. Adding the weight and score (8) is a common slip.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weigh it up and justify (2)","q":"<p>The weighted totals are: shared spreadsheet 38, custom-built system 31, online order app 44. Which option does the matrix select?</p>","options":["The custom-built system, because custom is always best","The online order app, because it has the highest weighted total","The shared spreadsheet, because it is the cheapest"],"correct":1,"why":"The option with the highest weighted total gives the best overall balance across the criteria, so the app is selected.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weigh it up and justify (3)","q":"<p>Which of these is the STRONGEST justification for choosing the online order app?</p>","options":["The app is the best choice because it is modern.","The app is the best choice because it is cheap.","Unlike the spreadsheet, the app sends every order to one list so none are lost, and unlike the custom system it fits the US$600 budget."],"correct":2,"why":"A strong justification links the choice to the cause (lost paper orders), the constraint (budget) and compares it with each alternative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weigh it up and justify (4)","q":"<p>The owner's nephew says, \"Pick the spreadsheet because it is free.\" What is wrong with this as a justification?</p>","options":["It mentions cost, which is never a criterion","Nothing: the cheapest option is always optimal","It gives one reason only and does not compare the options against the requirements"],"correct":2,"why":"Cost is a valid criterion, but one reason with no comparison is weak. The spreadsheet scored lower overall because it does not stop orders being missed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Weigh it up and justify (5)","q":"<p>A weakness of the app is that the two counter staff need training. What should a strong justification do with this weakness?</p>","options":["Choose a different option immediately","Admit it and explain how it will be managed, such as a short training session before launch","Hide it so the choice looks better"],"correct":1,"why":"Admitting a weakness and saying how it will be managed makes a justification more convincing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Help the farmer through the process (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a student volunteer helping the farmer Mr. Desmond Brooks, who grows callaloo and cabbage near Christiana, Jamaica. Caterpillars are eating his crop. Guide him through the problem-solving process.</p><p>Mr. Brooks says, \"Just tell me which poison to buy.\" What do you do first?</p>","options":["Help him write a problem statement: who and what is affected, how, and what happens if nothing is done.","Recommend the strongest pesticide in the farm shop straight away."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Defining the problem first keeps every option open and focuses on the real issue.","That skips defining and analysing. You might treat the wrong pest or cause new problems."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Help the farmer through the process (step 2)","q":"<p>The statement is agreed: caterpillars have destroyed about 40% of his cabbage in a month, and if this continues he will lose his market income. What next?</p>","options":["Order insect netting online now, because netting always works.","Analyse: check when and where damage occurs, identify the caterpillar, and list his budget and time limits."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Choosing a solution before analysing ignores cost, plot size and whether netting even suits his crop.","Right. Analysis finds causes, requirements and constraints."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Help the farmer through the process (step 3)","q":"<p>You find that damage is worst after moths are seen at dusk, and his budget is small. You list hand-picking and a biological spray, netting, and chemical spray. How do you choose?</p>","options":["Evaluate each option against cost, effectiveness, time and safety, then select the best and justify it by comparing it with the others.","Pick whichever option his neighbour used last year."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. That is identifying, evaluating, selecting and justifying.","A choice without evaluation cannot be justified. The neighbour's situation may be different."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Help the farmer through the process (step 4)","q":"<p>He chooses hand-picking plus a biological spray and puts it into action. A month later, what should happen?</p>","options":["Evaluate and review: compare the damage now with the 40% in the problem statement and decide whether to adjust.","Nothing; once a solution is implemented the process is finished."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. Evaluating against the original problem statement shows whether the gap has closed.","Without review, nobody knows whether the solution worked or needs changing."]},{"t":"match","title":"Implement step by step","q":"<p>The Carenage Water Taxi company in St. George's has selected a subscription online booking system. Put its implementation tasks in a sensible order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Plan the tasks: list what must be done and by whom, and set the switch-over date","Gather the resources: pay the subscription and collect the boat list and timetables","Carry out the set-up: enter every boat and timetable into the system","Prepare the people: train both booking staff to take and check bookings","Switch over from the paper diary on the first day of the month","Monitor progress: record every booking and complaint from day one"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the problem-solving term","q":"<p>Match each term from this unit to its meaning, using the farmer and crop pest case.</p>","left":["Symptom","Root cause","Requirement","Constraint","Optimal solution","Feedback"],"right":["The farmer can spend no more than about EC$1,350","The produce must stay low in chemical residue for the restaurants","Tomato leaves are curling and turning yellow","Integrated control, the best overall fit to the requirements and constraints","Review findings showing one bed still infested, so netting is added there","Whitefly breeding on the weeds beside the plot"],"correct":[2,5,1,0,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>The Arima homework club has slow shared laptops. Name the stage for each action.</p><p>The club leader writes: \"Students wait 15 minutes for laptops to start, losing study time; if this continues, they will miss deadlines.\"</p>","options":["implement","analyse the problem","define the problem"],"correct":2,"why":"A statement of who, how and consequence is the problem definition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>A volunteer discovers the laptops have nearly full hard drives and old start-up programs.</p>","options":["analyse the problem","select and justify","evaluate and review"],"correct":0,"why":"Finding the causes is analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>The leader lists three options: clean up the laptops, upgrade the memory, or buy new laptops, with pros and cons of each.</p>","options":["implement","identify and evaluate possible solutions","define the problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Listing and judging alternatives is identifying and evaluating possible solutions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>She chooses the clean-up plus memory upgrade because it fixes the cause for a fraction of the price of new laptops.</p>","options":["analyse the problem","evaluate and review","select and justify the optimal solution"],"correct":2,"why":"Choosing with reasons compared to alternatives is selecting and justifying.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>Volunteers remove unused programs and fit the new memory on Saturday.</p>","options":["define the problem","identify possible solutions","implement"],"correct":2,"why":"Putting the solution into action is implementation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Stage quick-fire (6)","q":"<p>A month later, she times start-up again and asks students whether they finish more work.</p>","options":["implement","evaluate and review","analyse the problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Measuring results against the original problem is evaluation and review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (1)","q":"<p>Students often confuse the first two stages with the last one. Sort each activity from the Seaside Pharmacy case into the correct stage.</p><p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Writing that patients are turned away twice a week and will miss doses if this continues</b></p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Define the problem","Analyse the problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Who, how and consequence: this is the problem definition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (2)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Discovering that stock is only counted when a shelf is empty</b></p>","options":["Define the problem","Evaluate and review","Analyse the problem"],"correct":2,"why":"This finds a cause, so it is analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (3)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Listing the budget limit and the need for the system to work offline during power cuts</b></p>","options":["Analyse the problem","Define the problem","Evaluate and review"],"correct":0,"why":"Constraints and requirements are identified during analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (4)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Counting how many patients were turned away in the three months after the app was installed</b></p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Analyse the problem","Define the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"Measuring results after implementation is evaluation and review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (5)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Asking pharmacists whether the low-stock alerts are useful after using them</b></p>","options":["Define the problem","Analyse the problem","Evaluate and review"],"correct":2,"why":"Feedback on a solution in use is part of evaluation and review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (6)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Agreeing with the owner on exactly which problem is being solved</b></p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Analyse the problem","Define the problem"],"correct":2,"why":"Agreeing the problem is part of defining it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (7)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Breaking the problem into ordering, recording and supplier parts</b></p>","options":["Define the problem","Evaluate and review","Analyse the problem"],"correct":2,"why":"Decomposing the problem is analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Define, analyse or evaluate and review? (8)","q":"<p>Which stage does this activity belong to?</p><p><b>Deciding to return to analysis because shortages still happen with one supplier</b></p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Analyse the problem","Define the problem"],"correct":0,"why":"A review that sends you back to an earlier stage is evaluation and review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (1)","q":"<p>Sixth Form students at Calabash Point Secondary are choosing a real problem to investigate as practice for Paper 03. Decide whether each choice is suitable or unsuitable. A suitable problem is real, can be investigated, has an information component and is bounded.</p><p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>Late-fee records at the Harbourside Community Library are kept on index cards and are often lost</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":1,"why":"Real, local, bounded to one process and full of information to collect: a suitable choice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>Crime in the Caribbean</b></p>","options":["Suitable problem to research","Unsuitable problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Far too broad. It is not bounded, and one student cannot investigate it properly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>An aunt's hair salon in New Amsterdam double-books clients because appointments are written in a notebook</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":1,"why":"A real problem the student can observe, with a clear information component (appointments): suitable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>\"The school needs a new canteen building\"</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":0,"why":"This names a solution, not a problem, and a building has little information component for an IT-based solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>The Frangipani Hill Academy sports club loses track of who has paid fees because payments are written on loose sheets</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":1,"why":"Bounded, real and information-based: a suitable choice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>Climate change around the world</b></p>","options":["Suitable problem to research","Unsuitable problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Global and unbounded. Choose one local business process instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>A business in Jamaica that the student has never visited and cannot contact</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":0,"why":"The student cannot gather facts, interview people or observe the problem, so it cannot be properly researched.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Good problem to research? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a suitable problem to research?</p><p><b>The Frigate Bay Taxi Association cannot see which drivers are free when a cruise ship docks</b></p>","options":["Unsuitable problem","Suitable problem to research"],"correct":1,"why":"Real, local, bounded and about information (driver availability): suitable.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the command word","q":"<p>A Paper 02 question is set on Harbourside Craft Market, whose vendors run out of popular items on cruise-ship days. Match each command word to the answer that does exactly what it asks.</p>","left":["Explain","Describe","Identify","Evaluate","Justify"],"right":["A shared sales spreadsheet; a point-of-sale app.","In the analyse stage the student interviews vendors, finds that sales are never totalled, and lists the budget and skills limits.","Problem-solving is a process because the committee follows ordered stages, so the fix tackles the real cause instead of a guess.","The app is optimal because, unlike the spreadsheet, it does not rely on vendors typing in every sale, and it fits the budget.","The app records every sale automatically, but it has a monthly fee and vendors need training."],"correct":[2,1,0,4,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Optimal does not mean cheapest","q":"<p>The Saba Dive Centre must replace its paper booking forms. Option X is free but does not prevent double bookings. Option Y costs US$40 a month, prevents double bookings and works on phones. Option Z is custom-built for US$12,000, far above the centre's budget. Which is the BEST justification for selecting Option Y as the optimal solution?</p>","options":["Z is the best because it is custom-built for the centre.","Unlike X, Y removes the cause of double bookings, and unlike Z it fits within the budget, so it best meets the requirements and constraints.","Y is the most modern technology available.","X is free, so it must be the optimal solution."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Z might fit the requirements, but it breaks the budget constraint, so it cannot be optimal here.","Correct. A justification compares the chosen option with each alternative against the requirements and constraints.","Being modern is not a criterion from the analysis. Justification must refer to the problem's requirements and constraints.","X is cheapest, but it fails the main requirement. The optimal solution balances all criteria, not cost alone."]}]},{"id":"assess_5qt","title":"Module 3 · Unit 2 quiz: Information for solving real-life problems","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 2: Information for solving real-life problems. 78 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (1)","q":"<p>Every problem is a gap between a present situation and a goal situation, and information is needed at every stage of closing it. Read each short Caribbean situation and choose the best answer.</p><p>Kezia, a Breadfruit Valley Secondary student, has failed her last two CAPE IT Unit 1 tests and wants a Grade II in the final exam. \"She has failed her last two tests\" describes her...</p>","options":["Solution","Goal situation","Physical resource","Present situation"],"correct":3,"why":"Where she is now is the present situation. The Grade II she wants is the goal situation; problem solving closes the gap between them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (2)","q":"<p>For the same student, \"a Grade II in the final CAPE IT Unit 1 exam\" describes her...</p>","options":["Goal situation","Present situation","Information resource","Research"],"correct":0,"why":"Where she wants to be is the goal situation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (3)","q":"<p>A farmer in Guyana whose bora bean plants are being eaten asks an agricultural officer to identify the insect and count the damaged plants per row. At which stage of problem solving is this information mainly used?</p>","options":["Evaluate and review","Select and justify the best solution","Implement the solution","Define and analyse the problem"],"correct":3,"why":"Identifying the pest and measuring the damage tells the farmer exactly what is wrong and how serious it is: defining and analysing the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (4)","q":"<p>Three months after moving its shelves to make space for a cold drinks fridge, the Belmopan Minimart compares its new monthly sales with last year's figures. At which stage is this information used?</p>","options":["Define the problem","Evaluate and review","Identify possible solutions","Analyse the problem"],"correct":1,"why":"Checking new figures after the change shows whether the solution worked: evaluate and review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (5)","q":"<p>A clinic in Port-au-Prince has enough nurses, beds and medicine, but patients wait for hours because staff cannot see which patients have appointments at which times. What does the clinic mainly lack?</p>","options":["Goal situation","Physical resources","Information resources","Equipment"],"correct":2,"why":"The materials, people and equipment are there. What is missing is knowledge of who is booked when: an information resource.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (6)","q":"<p>A Noord family reads its electricity bills, sees that usage jumped after their old fridge began to leak, and uses those figures to decide to replace the fridge. Which step of \"understand → inform → solve\" is the decision to replace the fridge?</p>","options":["Understand","Inform","Solve","None of these"],"correct":2,"why":"Using the information to choose a response is the solve step. Reading the bills helped them understand; the figures informed the decision.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Closing the gap with information (7)","q":"<p>Before her first evening lecture at Coralstone College, Nadia checks the campus map to find room B12. Which everyday research habit is this?</p>","options":["Find a contact","Find the time","Find a place","Find a cure"],"correct":2,"why":"Locating a room is \"find a place\", one of the simple research habits we use every day.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Information for a personal decision","q":"<p>Marcus, a student in Port of Spain, wants to buy his first used car with money saved from a summer job. Which item of information is MOST necessary to solve his problem?</p>","options":["The colours each model is available in","The year the car manufacturer was founded","Which cars his favourite celebrity drives","The total amount of money he has available, including insurance and registration costs"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Colour may matter to Marcus personally, but it concerns appearance, not whether the car solves his transport problem.","This is unrelated to his decision. It would not change which car he buys.","This is not relevant information for a budget-limited purchase.","Correct. Without knowing what he can afford in total, he cannot narrow the choice at all. This information is necessary for the solution."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (1)","q":"<p>Like the guava jelly exporter, many organisations think they are short of materials, people or equipment when they are really short of information. Sort each Caribbean situation by what is REALLY missing.</p><p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>A jelly exporter's buyers return from the markets with too little fruit, although many farmers have ripe guavas they would sell. The exporter does not know which farmers they are.</b></p>","options":["Information gap","Physical resource gap"],"correct":0,"why":"The guavas exist. The exporter does not know where to find them: an information gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (2)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>Farmers with ripe guavas let the fruit rot because they do not know the exporter wants to buy it.</b></p>","options":["Information gap","Physical resource gap"],"correct":0,"why":"The producers lack information about the buyer. An information gap on the other side.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (3)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>A San Pedro restaurant says it \"cannot get\" local fish, while fishermen land good catches at Oistins and Speightstown on days the restaurant does not know about.</b></p>","options":["Physical resource gap","Information gap"],"correct":1,"why":"The fish is there. The restaurant does not know which boats land where and when: an information gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (4)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>Parents on Saba miss free vaccination days because the dates are not announced, although the clinic has the vaccines in stock.</b></p>","options":["Information gap","Physical resource gap"],"correct":0,"why":"The vaccines are in stock. The missing piece is the dates: an information gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (5)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>After Hurricane Irma, two classrooms at a primary school in The Valley, Anguilla, have no roof.</b></p>","options":["Information gap","Physical resource gap"],"correct":1,"why":"The school needs roofing materials and builders: a physical resource gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (6)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>The only oven at the Sweet Hands Bakery in Salem has broken, and no spare parts are on the island.</b></p>","options":["Physical resource gap","Information gap"],"correct":0,"why":"Knowing more will not bake the bread. The bakery needs a working oven: a physical resource gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Physical gap or information gap? (7)","q":"<p>Is the real gap in physical resources or in information?</p><p><b>A St. Kitts fishing co-operative knows exactly where the deep-water fish are, but its boats' engines are too small to reach them safely.</b></p>","options":["Information gap","Physical resource gap"],"correct":1,"why":"The co-operative has the information. It lacks bigger engines: a physical resource gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each problem into the syllabus category it BEST fits.</p><p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>A student must choose between two scholarship offers.</b></p>","options":["Social","Scientific","Commercial","Personal"],"correct":3,"why":"One person decides and lives with the result: personal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>A Jan Thiel restaurant needs to cut its food waste costs.</b></p>","options":["Commercial","Personal","Scientific","Social"],"correct":0,"why":"A business trying to protect its profit: commercial.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>Researchers want to know why sargassum arrivals have increased.</b></p>","options":["Scientific","Social","Commercial","Personal"],"correct":0,"why":"Investigated through measurement and data: scientific.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>A community wants to reduce youth unemployment in its district.</b></p>","options":["Scientific","Personal","Commercial","Social"],"correct":3,"why":"It affects society and needs collective action: social.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>A family wants to lower its monthly electricity bill.</b></p>","options":["Scientific","Commercial","Personal","Social"],"correct":2,"why":"A household decision: personal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>A car rental firm near Grantley Adams International Airport must decide how many vehicles to keep for the high season.</b></p>","options":["Commercial","Scientific","Social","Personal"],"correct":0,"why":"A trading business planning stock and demand: commercial.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>Scientists test whether a new crop variety survives drought in Guyana.</b></p>","options":["Social","Scientific","Commercial","Personal"],"correct":1,"why":"Controlled experiments and measurements: scientific.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of problem? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a personal, commercial, scientific or social problem?</p><p><b>Flooding regularly blocks roads in a low-lying neighbourhood during rainy season.</b></p>","options":["Personal","Scientific","Social","Commercial"],"correct":2,"why":"A shared community and government problem: social.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (1)","q":"<p>The Lagoon Water Taxi wants to launch an online booking system so tourists can book rides across the lagoon. Sort each item of information.</p><p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>The departure times and number of seats on each boat.</b></p>","options":["Extraneous","Desirable","Cosmetic","Essential"],"correct":3,"why":"Without times and seats, no booking can be taken. Essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (2)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>The ticket price for each route.</b></p>","options":["Desirable","Extraneous","Cosmetic","Essential"],"correct":3,"why":"Customers cannot pay and the business cannot charge without prices. Essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (3)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>Which hotels most customers stay at, so pick-up points can be suggested.</b></p>","options":["Essential","Cosmetic","Desirable","Extraneous"],"correct":2,"why":"Useful for a better service, but bookings work without it. Desirable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (4)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>Customer reviews from previous trips.</b></p>","options":["Extraneous","Cosmetic","Desirable","Essential"],"correct":2,"why":"Helpful for marketing and improvement, but not needed to take a booking. Desirable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (5)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>The history of how the lagoon was formed.</b></p>","options":["Extraneous","Cosmetic","Desirable","Essential"],"correct":0,"why":"Interesting, but irrelevant to booking a ride. Extraneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (6)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>The owner's favourite cricket team.</b></p>","options":["Essential","Extraneous","Desirable","Cosmetic"],"correct":1,"why":"No bearing on the problem at all. Extraneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (7)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>A staff member's assurance that \"the boats are never full\", given instead of actual seat counts.</b></p>","options":["Desirable","Essential","Cosmetic","Extraneous"],"correct":2,"why":"It looks like capacity information but masks the missing seat figures, so it cannot guide bookings. Cosmetic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Essential, desirable, extraneous or cosmetic? (8)","q":"<p>How important is this information to the booking solution?</p><p><b>A note that \"tourists love us online\", with no figures on how many people would actually book.</b></p>","options":["Essential","Cosmetic","Extraneous","Desirable"],"correct":1,"why":"It sounds like demand information but masks the missing booking figures. Cosmetic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (1)","q":"<p>Each item of information is paired with a problem. Choose the category it belongs to FOR THAT PROBLEM.</p><p>The tide times for Prince Rupert Bay, Dominica, for a fisherman deciding when to launch his boat.</p>","options":["Desirable","Essential","Extraneous","Cosmetic"],"correct":1,"why":"Launching safely depends on the tide. Essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (2)","q":"<p>The tide times for Prince Rupert Bay, for a student choosing a laptop.</p>","options":["Extraneous","Desirable","Essential","Cosmetic"],"correct":0,"why":"Tides have nothing to do with choosing a laptop. Extraneous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (3)","q":"<p>Customers' birthdays, for a Paramaribo bakery deciding how many loaves to bake tomorrow.</p>","options":["Essential","Extraneous","Cosmetic","Desirable"],"correct":1,"why":"Birthdays do not predict daily bread demand. Extraneous for this problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (4)","q":"<p>Customers' birthdays, for the same bakery planning a birthday-cake promotion.</p>","options":["Cosmetic","Essential","Extraneous","Desirable"],"correct":1,"why":"Now the promotion cannot work without them. Essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (5)","q":"<p>A manager's statement that \"dengue is under control\", sent to the health department without any case figures.</p>","options":["Cosmetic","Essential","Desirable","Extraneous"],"correct":0,"why":"It sounds like useful information but masks the missing case numbers, so it cannot guide a decision. Cosmetic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Same information, different problem (6)","q":"<p>Last year's exam results, for a teacher planning revision for this year's CAPE IT class.</p>","options":["Desirable","Essential","Extraneous","Cosmetic"],"correct":0,"why":"They help target weak areas, but revision can be planned without them. Desirable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Cosmetic or desirable?","q":"<p>The St. Vincent Tours company is building a website to sell island tours. Which of the following is BEST described as cosmetic information for this problem?</p>","options":["Reviews from previous customers","The price and duration of each tour","The number of goats on a neighbouring island","A partner's assurance that \"cruise passengers will definitely buy online\", with no figures on how many actually book tours online"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Reviews are content that can persuade customers and improve the service. They are desirable, not cosmetic.","Without prices and durations no tour can be sold. This is essential.","This is irrelevant to selling tours, so it is extraneous, not cosmetic.","Correct. It looks like demand information, but it masks the missing booking figures and cannot guide the decision. Cosmetic."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (1)","q":"<p>Use common sense first, then the validity questions. For each Caribbean situation, choose the best answer.</p><p>A flyer on Broad Street claims that a new app \"doubles your phone's battery life overnight\". Before checking anything else, what is the sensible first reaction?</p>","options":["Accept it, because it is printed","Accept it, because the app is free","Take it with a pinch of salt: it fails common sense"],"correct":2,"why":"An unbelievable claim fails the common-sense test first. Then check it against the concrete criteria.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (2)","q":"<p>A tour company's web page about diving off Saba lists only the good points of its own trips and none of the risks or costs.</p>","options":["Cultural context","Completeness","Bias","Accuracy","Currency"],"correct":2,"why":"The source favours a particular point (its own trips): it is subjective, so bias is the question it fails.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (3)","q":"<p>A report on school attendance in Belize is full of typing errors, and its total does not match its own table.</p>","options":["Bias","Currency","Cultural context","Completeness","Accuracy"],"correct":4,"why":"Is it correct as presented, without typographical errors? No: accuracy fails.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (4)","q":"<p>A guide to \"Caribbean eating habits\" treats the whole region as one, and a health team wants to use it for a campaign in one Curaçao neighbourhood.</p>","options":["Cultural context","Bias","Accuracy","Completeness","Currency"],"correct":0,"why":"The guide is generalised, not specific to the community in the problem: a cultural context question.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (5)","q":"<p>A budget guide for Flamboyant Heights College students leaves out accommodation costs and is written in jargon most students cannot follow.</p>","options":["Currency","Completeness","Cultural context","Bias","Accuracy"],"correct":1,"why":"Vital pieces are missing and it is hard to understand: both are completeness questions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Run the validity questions (6)","q":"<p>A list of mobile data prices on the island was last checked in 2017, and a student wants to use it to compare plans today.</p>","options":["Currency","Bias","Cultural context","Completeness","Accuracy"],"correct":0,"why":"How current is it? Not current enough for today's prices: currency fails.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the criterion to the warning sign","q":"<p>Match each criterion for accepting or rejecting information to the warning sign that points to it.</p>","left":["Bias","Accuracy","Completeness","Currency","Cultural context","Un-refereed source"],"right":["A guide to mobile data plans was published six years ago.","A budget lists tuition but leaves out accommodation and flights.","A health claim appears in an anonymous blog post with no expert review.","A table of school results has totals that do not add up.","A hotel's own website says it is \"the best hotel in the Caribbean\".","Advice on winter heating costs is used to plan a budget in St. Lucia."],"correct":[4,3,1,0,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which criterion fails?","q":"<p>A Barbados student researching how to reduce flooding in Bridgetown finds a detailed, well-referenced report written in 2024 by engineers about flood control in the Netherlands, where many areas lie below sea level and rivers are the main flood source. Which criterion gives the STRONGEST reason to be cautious about using it directly?</p>","options":["Cultural context","Accuracy","Bias","Currency"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The report is accurate and current, but it was written for a very different geography, climate and system, so it may not apply to Bridgetown without adaptation.","The report is described as detailed and well-referenced, so there is no evidence of errors.","Nothing suggests the engineers are promoting a product or a one-sided view.","A 2024 report is reasonably current. Age is not the main problem here."]},{"t":"match","title":"How an article becomes refereed","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College lecturer writes a research paper on sargassum and submits it to a peer-reviewed journal. Put the stages of the refereeing process in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The author submits the research paper to the journal.","The editor checks that the paper fits the journal and meets basic standards.","The editor sends the paper to at least two, typically three, independent experts in marine science.","The experts review the methods, data and conclusions and report their comments.","The author revises the paper to answer the reviewers' comments.","When all the referees agree that the revised paper is worthy of publication, the editor accepts it and it is published."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (1)","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College student researching how rising sea temperatures affect coral near Tobago sorts the publications she has found by their level of scholarship.</p><p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A refereed marine biology journal article with its methods, data tables and a reference list.</b></p>","options":["Scholarly publication","General audience"],"correct":0,"why":"Written for specialists, refereed and in depth: scholarly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (2)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A local newspaper feature in which divers describe the bleaching they have seen.</b></p>","options":["General audience","Scholarly publication"],"correct":0,"why":"Newspapers are written for the public: general audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (3)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>An academic book from a university press that sends manuscripts to outside experts.</b></p>","options":["General audience","Scholarly publication"],"correct":1,"why":"A reputable academic publisher with expert review: scholarly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (4)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A tourism magazine article titled \"Tobago's ten best dive sites\".</b></p>","options":["General audience","Scholarly publication"],"correct":0,"why":"Written to interest tourists, not to analyse coral science: general audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (5)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A conference paper presented to coral reef scientists, with a full bibliography.</b></p>","options":["Scholarly publication","General audience"],"correct":0,"why":"Prepared for specialists and fully referenced: scholarly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (6)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A popular science website that sums up a study in simple words, with no references.</b></p>","options":["General audience","Scholarly publication"],"correct":0,"why":"Easier to understand, but it lacks in-depth analysis: general audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Scholarly or general audience? (7)","q":"<p>Who is the intended audience?</p><p><b>A blog post by a Saba dive shop owner about what he saw on Sunday's dive.</b></p>","options":["Scholarly publication","General audience"],"correct":1,"why":"A personal account for the public: general audience, and un-refereed.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Where to look for relevance","q":"<p>A CAPE IT student at Mahoe Grove High School is researching online shopping for small Guyanese businesses. Match each item to what she should examine or conclude.</p>","left":["A book on Caribbean e-commerce","A journal article on mobile payments","A newspaper report on a new online shop in Georgetown","The author's expertise","The author's standing in the field","The author's affiliation"],"right":["Its title, preface and table of contents","The institution the author works with, such as a reputable university","Written for a general audience, so it may not be specific enough","Whether others writing on the subject cite the author","Biographical notes: is the author an expert, scholar or practitioner?","Its title and abstract"],"correct":[0,5,2,4,3,1]},{"t":"ma","title":"Is this author an authority?","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College student is deciding whether to use a book on cybersecurity for small Caribbean businesses. She notes these details from its cover, preface and back pages.</p><p>Select EVERY line that shows the author has authority or knows the literature.</p>","options":["2. The author has published three earlier studies on network security.","8. It is written in a chatty, friendly style.","3. Other researchers writing on Caribbean cybersecurity cite the author's work.","5. Each chapter ends with references, and there is a full bibliography.","4. The book has a glossy cover and a quote from a famous actor on the back.","1. The author lectures in computer security at a regional university.","7. The book is the most-liked title on a social media reading list.","6. The author explains that experts still disagree about one encryption policy and sets out both views."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"why":"Correct. Affiliation, other publications and being cited show authority; references, a bibliography and acknowledging a controversy show familiarity with the literature. Covers, popularity and style show neither."},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (1)","q":"<p>Each item gives a research topic and a source. Decide whether the source is fit for THAT research.</p><p>Topic: which laptops Palmetto Point Secondary should buy this year. Source: a Computer Science book on hardware published in 1980.</p>","options":["Timely: accept","Likely outdated: reject for this research"],"correct":1,"why":"Computing has changed completely since 1980, so the book is outdated for a purchase today.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (2)","q":"<p>Topic: the history of computing in Caribbean schools. Source: the same 1980 hardware book.</p>","options":["Historically relevant: accept","Likely outdated: reject"],"correct":0,"why":"For historical research, an old book can be extremely relevant evidence of its time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (3)","q":"<p>Topic: how doctors treated yellow fever in the early twentieth century. Source: a Medicine book published in 1925.</p>","options":["Likely outdated: reject","Historically relevant: accept"],"correct":1,"why":"The research is historical, so a 1925 book is a valuable source, even though it is outdated for treating patients today.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (4)","q":"<p>Topic: comparing views on the current telecommunications regulatory framework in Jamaica. Source: opinion columns by an operator, a consumer group and a commentator.</p>","options":["Reject: they are subjective","Accept: this research must rely on subjective views"],"correct":1,"why":"Comparing views requires subjective publications. Label each view and balance them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (5)","q":"<p>Topic: whether well water in Belmopan is safe to drink. Source: a resident's strong opinion that \"it tastes fine\".</p>","options":["Rely on objective information, such as laboratory test results","Accept the opinion as the main evidence"],"correct":0,"why":"Water safety is an empirical question, so it needs objective measurements, not opinions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Timely and objective enough? (6)","q":"<p>Topic: whether a new treatment for coral disease works near Saba. Source: a refereed article reporting the measured results of a controlled experiment.</p>","options":["Reject: scientific articles are too technical","Accept: an empirical question answered with objective evidence"],"correct":1,"why":"An empirical scientific question should rely primarily on objective, refereed results like these.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Subjective sources: accept or reject?","q":"<p>Kayla, a CAPE IT student in Kingston, is researching how residents, mobile operators and consumer groups view the current telecommunications regulatory framework. A friend tells her to reject every opinion piece she has found because it is subjective. Which response is BEST?</p>","options":["Replace them with a Computer Science textbook from 1980, because textbooks are objective.","Reject them all: subjective information must always be rejected.","Keep only the opinion piece she agrees with, because it is the most convincing.","Keep the opinion pieces: research comparing views must rely on subjective publications, so she should label each view and balance them."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["The book is off topic and outdated, and it cannot tell her what groups think about regulation today.","This is the misconception the friend holds. Whether subjective information is useful depends on the research topic.","Choosing only the view she agrees with would bring her own bias into the research. She needs a range of views.","Correct. Subjective information is not automatically rejected; Kayla's topic is the views themselves, so she needs them, balanced against one another."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the warning signs","q":"<p>A CAPE student is researching whether a herbal drink cures diabetes for a health awareness project. She finds this web page.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a good reason to reject or doubt this information.</p>","options":["2. It says \"Doctors don't want you to know this miracle cure!\"","4. The page is written in English.","1. The page has no author name and no \"About us\" information.","6. It quotes no research and links to no studies.","3. A \"Buy now\" button sells the drink at the bottom of the page.","7. It has a photo of a plant.","5. There is no date showing when it was written or updated."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,6],"why":"Correct. No author (authority), loaded language (bias), a sales motive (bias), no date (currency) and no evidence or review (accuracy, un-refereed). Language and a photo are not reasons to reject."},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (1)","q":"<p>A CAPE IT student in Warrens is collecting web pages on hurricane-resistant roofing. Sort each page by who is most likely to have published it.</p><p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.windloadstudies.edu/reports/roofs-2025</b></p>","options":["Commercial organisation","Academic organisation","Private individual"],"correct":1,"why":".edu marks an academic organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (2)","q":"<p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.coastalengineering.ac.uk/research/hurricanes</b></p>","options":["Private individual","Commercial organisation","Academic organisation"],"correct":2,"why":".ac before a country code (.uk) marks an academic organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (3)","q":"<p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.roofstrongsupplies.com/hurricane-straps</b></p>","options":["Academic organisation","Commercial organisation","Private individual"],"correct":1,"why":".com and a product page: a commercial organisation that wants to sell.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (4)","q":"<p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.islandhardware.lc/specials</b></p>","options":["Academic organisation","Private individual","Commercial organisation"],"correct":2,"why":"A business using St. Lucia's country code, with a \"specials\" page: commercial.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (5)","q":"<p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.freepages-host.com/~kevinroofs/tips.html</b></p>","options":["Academic organisation","Commercial organisation","Private individual"],"correct":2,"why":"A tilde and a personal name on a free hosting site: a private individual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Decode the URL (6)","q":"<p>Who is hosting this page?</p><p><b>www.windloadstudies.edu/~mbrown/my-roof-tips.html</b></p>","options":["Academic organisation","Commercial organisation","Private individual"],"correct":2,"why":"The host is academic, but the tilde shows a personal page published by an individual, which the university may not have checked.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Good signs on a web page","q":"<p>A Coralstone College student finds a web page on hurricane planning for small businesses. These are the details she notes about it.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is a GOOD sign that the page can be accepted.</p>","options":["7. The page uses bright colours and moving banners.","2. A footer note says all pages are reviewed under the institute's web publishing policy.","3. A \"Contact the Webmaster\" link appears at the bottom of the page.","4. It shows the author's name, \"© 2026\" and \"Last updated: 10 June 2026\".","6. A university library guide on hurricane planning links to this page.","5. It links to refereed studies and to the regional disaster management agency.","8. A visitor counter shows 250,000 visits.","9. It also links to a site selling \"hurricane-proof\" miracle paint.","1. The URL is www.caribbeanresilience.edu/business/hurricane-plan."],"correct":[1,2,3,4,5,8],"why":"Correct. An academic host, an approval policy, a Webmaster contact, the author, © and update date, and reliable links in both directions are all good signs. Design, visit counts and links to unreliable sites are not."},{"t":"mc","title":"Links to and from a page","q":"<p>Marlon, a CAPE IT student in Grand Anse, has two undated web pages on making drinking water safe after a storm. Page X is linked from the regional public health agency's website and from a university library guide. Page Y is linked only from a few anonymous forums. Why does this make Page X more acceptable?</p>","options":["Reliable pages link to it, and an authoritative publisher is unlikely to undermine its authority by linking to unreliable pages.","Search engines rank pages by the number of links, so Page X must be correct.","Any page that is linked to has been refereed.","Links show that Page X has been updated recently."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Links from reliable pages, such as the health agency and the library guide, suggest that other authorities trust Page X.","Ranking and link counts do not prove accuracy. What matters is that the linking pages are reliable.","Links are not refereeing. No independent experts reviewed Page X before publication just because others link to it.","Links say nothing about the update date. Both pages are undated, so currency still needs checking."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The sargassum report (step 1)","q":"<p>You are an intern at an environmental consultancy in Bridgetown. Your manager asks you to gather information for a report to beach-front hotels on how to deal with sargassum seaweed. Make good information decisions.</p><p>Before searching, your manager asks what you will gather first. What do you say?</p>","options":["First list the information we NEED (essential): arrival forecasts, volumes, removal methods and costs, and health guidance. Then search for it.","Collect everything online about seaweed and sort it out later."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Identifying essential information first focuses the search.","This leads to information overload, with extraneous material burying what matters."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The sargassum report (step 2)","q":"<p>You find a viral video claiming that sargassum \"can be burned safely on the beach\". It has 2 million views but no named expert. What do you do?</p>","options":["Treat it as un-refereed and unverified; check health guidance and refereed research before using any of it.","Include it in the report because so many people have watched it."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Popularity is not accuracy, and safety advice needs reliable evidence.","Views measure popularity, not accuracy. This could cause real harm."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The sargassum report (step 3)","q":"<p>You find a refereed study from 2012 and a Caribbean government advisory from this year that partly disagree on the best removal method. Which do you rely on?</p>","options":["Use both: note the study's age, prefer the current regional advisory for local practice, and explain the difference to the hotels.","Use only the 2012 study, because refereed sources are always right."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong judgement. You weighed refereed status against currency and cultural context.","Refereed sources are more reliable, but a 2012 study may be out of date. Currency matters too."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The sargassum report (step 4)","q":"<p>Your draft report is ready. A colleague suggests adding a two-page history of the Sargasso Sea's discovery and a line saying \"hotels report that sargassum is no longer a serious problem\", although no hotel figures were collected. What do you do?</p>","options":["Leave out the history as extraneous; treat the \"no longer a serious problem\" line as cosmetic, because it hides missing hotel figures, so collect the figures or remove it.","Add both: the history fills pages and the line sounds reassuring."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You kept the report focused on what the hotels need and refused a claim with nothing behind it.","Extraneous material and a cosmetic claim now hide the missing figures the hotels need."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the problem to its essential information","q":"<p>Match each real-life problem to the information that is ESSENTIAL for solving it.</p>","left":["Personal: a family budgeting for school fees","Commercial: a Bay Street shop rostering staff","Scientific: testing beach water safety","Social: planning hurricane shelters","Commercial: setting a restaurant's menu prices","Scientific: forecasting a storm's track"],"right":["Ingredient costs, overheads and competitors' prices","Air pressure, wind speed and satellite readings","Number and location of residents without safe housing","Household income, monthly expenses and the fee amounts and due dates","Bacteria counts from samples, with dates, locations and safe limits","Cruise ship arrival schedule and past sales on similar days"],"correct":[3,5,4,2,0,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Refereed or not?","q":"<p>Jada, a CAPE IT student in Scarborough, Tobago, is researching the health effects of rotting sargassum. She finds a refereed journal article and a popular blog post on the same topic. Which of the following BEST explains why the journal article is generally considered more reliable?</p>","options":["It has been evaluated by independent experts in the field before publication.","It is always more up to date than a blog post.","It has been read and shared by more people.","It is printed on paper rather than published online."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Independent expert review before publication is the defining feature of a refereed source, so errors in the sargassum article are more likely to have been caught.","The opposite is often true: peer review takes time, so the blog post on sargassum could be more current.","Popularity is not a measure of reliability, and a popular blog post is often shared far more widely than a journal article.","Jada may well have found the journal article online. Many refereed journals are published online; the medium does not make a source refereed."]}]},{"id":"assess_5z3","title":"Module 3 · Unit 3 quiz: Software development models","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 3: Software development models. 63 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (1)","q":"<p>Windward Customs Brokers in Roseau is having its declarations software built with the Waterfall model. Sort each thing that happens on the project as an <b>advantage</b> or a <b>disadvantage</b> of Waterfall.</p><p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>Before work starts, the developer gives the brokers a fixed price and a delivery date that both sides can rely on.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":1,"why":"Detailed up-front planning makes cost and time easy to estimate, which suits a fixed-price contract.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (2)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>A new programmer joins halfway through and gets up to speed by reading the signed-off design document.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":1,"why":"Thorough documentation at every stage helps staff changes and later maintenance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (3)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>The manager checks progress each month against clear milestones: analysis signed off, design signed off, coding complete.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":1,"why":"Clear stages and deliverables make Waterfall simple to manage and progress easy to measure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (4)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>The brokers are busy with cruise season and only need to attend meetings at the start and the end.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of Waterfall","Disadvantage of Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"Waterfall needs little customer time during the project, an advantage when users are not available.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (5)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>In month five the owner asks for a new report, but the design is already signed off, so two stages must be redone.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"Waterfall is inflexible: changes after sign-off are costly because earlier stages must be repeated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (6)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>Staff see working screens for the first time only in month eight, near the end of the project.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"Working software appears late in Waterfall, so users cannot react to it early.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (7)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>During final testing, the team finds that a field required by Customs was missed during analysis.</b></p>","options":["Advantage of Waterfall","Disadvantage of Waterfall"],"correct":1,"why":"Testing comes late, so requirement errors surface late, when they are expensive to fix.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Waterfall: strengths and weaknesses (8)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage of Waterfall?</p><p><b>At handover the owner admits he did not really know what he needed at the start, and the system misses some needs.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage of Waterfall","Advantage of Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"Waterfall assumes users know all their requirements at the start; if they do not, the whole investment is at risk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Is Waterfall a good fit?","q":"<p>Four organisations in Trinidad and Tobago each want new software and are asking a Port of Spain developer which model to follow. For which project would the Waterfall model be MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Leeward Ferries’ ticket-pricing module, built to a fixed government fare formula, under a fixed-price contract that requires full documentation","A Frederick Street tourism kiosk whose client says, \"I will know what I want when I see it\"","A San Fernando start-up’s website that must go live in two weeks and then grow feature by feature","A Chaguaramas youth group’s social app, whose features will be decided by watching how early users react"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Leeward Ferries has stable, fully known requirements (a fixed fare formula), a fixed price and a need for documentation: the classic conditions for Waterfall.","That phrase is the signal for prototyping: the kiosk client needs to see a model to clarify requirements.","Going live early and then growing gradually describes incremental or Agile development.","The youth group’s requirements are unknown and will emerge from user feedback, which suits prototyping or Agile, not Waterfall."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (1)","q":"<p>Each event happened on a Caribbean prototyping project. Decide what each one shows.</p><p>At Harbour Gems Jewellers, the owner sees a clickable catalogue on Friday and asks why it cannot go live with real stock on Monday.</p>","options":["Advantage: lower risk of building the wrong system","Disadvantage: users mistake the prototype for the finished system","Advantage: users feel ownership"],"correct":1,"why":"The prototype has no stock database or security behind it. Users thinking it is finished is a classic prototyping disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (2)","q":"<p>Volunteers for the Island Relief Network try a registration screen and immediately notice there is no field for the number of children in a household.</p>","options":["Disadvantage: hard to plan and cost","Advantage: missing functions are spotted early","Disadvantage: documentation is weak"],"correct":1,"why":"Users evaluating a working model find missing functions early, before the full system is built.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (3)","q":"<p>After eleven rounds of changes, the developer of the Bright Smile Dental Clinic booking system finds the code so tangled that a small fix breaks two other screens.</p>","options":["Disadvantage: poorly structured code that is hard to maintain","Advantage: vague requirements become clear","Advantage: users are involved early"],"correct":0,"why":"Constant quick changes to an evolutionary prototype can leave the code poorly structured and hard to maintain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (4)","q":"<p>St. Kitts tourism board could not describe its kiosk, but after trying a model for one week it produced a clear list of the six screens it needs.</p>","options":["Disadvantage: needs a lot of user time","Disadvantage: users may stop giving feedback","Advantage: vague requirements are clarified"],"correct":2,"why":"\"I will know it when I see it\" became a concrete list: clarifying requirements is the main advantage of prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (5)","q":"<p>Sea Breeze Marina asks how many weeks the berth-booking prototype will take. The developer honestly cannot say how many feedback cycles will be needed.</p>","options":["Advantage: lower risk","Advantage: high user acceptance","Disadvantage: difficult to plan and cost"],"correct":2,"why":"No one knows in advance how many build-evaluate-refine cycles are needed, so prototyping is hard to plan and cost.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping pros and cons in action (6)","q":"<p>A Bridgetown insurer’s busy claims staff miss three feedback sessions in a row during the tourist season.</p>","options":["Advantage: users feel ownership","Disadvantage: testing comes too late","Disadvantage: prototyping needs a lot of user time"],"correct":2,"why":"Prototyping depends on users trying each version; busy users who stop giving feedback stall the process.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the prototyping cycle","q":"<p>The Bright Smile Dental Clinic is having an online appointment booking system built using evolutionary prototyping. Put the stages in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Identify the basic, outline requirements with the receptionist and dentists.","Quickly build an initial prototype of the booking screens.","Staff and a few patients use the prototype and give feedback.","Refine the prototype using the feedback, then test it again with users.","When users are satisfied, complete, test and implement the final system."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (1)","q":"<p>Each event comes from a Caribbean project. Does it show <b>incremental</b> development (adding a new working part) or <b>iterative</b> development (repeating the cycle to refine what already exists)?</p><p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>Harbour Water Taxi Co-operative’s system goes live with seat booking; two months later online card payment is added.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":1,"why":"A new working part (payment) is delivered on top of what exists: incremental.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (2)","q":"<p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>The Caribbean Islands Credit Union releases transfers as a new section of its app, after balances went live in June.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":1,"why":"Transfers are a new increment adding functionality: incremental.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (3)","q":"<p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>The Guyana licensing system delivers vehicle licences first; business licences follow as a separate module.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":1,"why":"Delivering the system module by module is incremental development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (4)","q":"<p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>After members complain the balance screen is slow, the credit union team redesigns and rebuilds that same screen in the next cycle.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":0,"why":"Revisiting and improving an existing part through another cycle is iterative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (5)","q":"<p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>The water taxi team repeats analysis, design, build and review on the booking screen three times, improving it each time from passenger feedback.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":0,"why":"Repeating the whole cycle to refine the same product is iterative development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Iterative or incremental? (6)","q":"<p>Adding a part or refining by repeating?</p><p><b>A Bayleaf Hill Secondary club app’s sign-up form is reworked in a second cycle because students found the layout confusing.</b></p>","options":["Iterative: repeating to refine","Incremental: adding a part"],"correct":0,"why":"The same form is refined in a repeated cycle: iterative.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the model to its defining feature","q":"<p>Match each model or term to the description that defines it.</p>","left":["Waterfall","Evolutionary prototype","Throwaway prototype","Incremental development","Iterative development","Agile software development"],"right":["Each stage is completed and signed off before the next begins","A rough version is refined with user feedback until it becomes the final system","A quick model built only to clarify requirements, then discarded","The development cycle is repeated to revise and improve the product","The system is delivered in working parts, each adding functionality","Short time-boxed cycles, close customer collaboration and welcoming change"],"correct":[0,1,2,4,3,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Agile practices at Island Hop Tours","q":"<p>The Island Hop Tours in Montego Bay is building its excursion app with Agile (Scrum). Match each Agile term to what is happening on the project.</p>","left":["User story","Product backlog","Sprint","Stand-up meeting","Product owner","Working software over comprehensive documentation","Responding to change over following a plan"],"right":["When a competitor launches a new feature, the next sprint’s plan is changed to answer it.","The owner, who meets the team every two weeks, sets priorities and reviews what each sprint produced.","The team writes a one-page note on the payment feature instead of a 40-page specification, and spends the time testing it.","A fixed two-week period at the end of which visitors can actually pay for tours in the app.","Every morning at 8:30 the three developers spend ten minutes on progress, plans and problems.","The prioritised list of every feature still to build, with WhatsApp booking just moved to the top.","\"As a cruise visitor, I want to see tours that fit my six hours in port, so that I am back on the ship in time.\""],"correct":[6,5,3,4,1,2,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (1)","q":"<p>Each event happened on a Caribbean project using iterative/incremental development or Agile. Decide which advantage or disadvantage it shows.</p><p>Members of the Caribbean Islands Credit Union can check balances on the app in month two, long before transfers and loans are ready.</p>","options":["Incremental disadvantage: integration problems","Incremental advantage: early delivery of core functions","Agile disadvantage: needs a committed customer representative"],"correct":1,"why":"The core increment is in use early, so the credit union benefits sooner: the key incremental advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (2)","q":"<p>When the loans module of the credit union app is added, it uses a different member-number format from the balances module and the two will not link.</p>","options":["Incremental disadvantage: later increments do not integrate cleanly","Incremental advantage: easier to test small parts","Agile advantage: flexibility"],"correct":0,"why":"Later increments that do not fit earlier ones are a recognised incremental disadvantage, which is why good up-front design is needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (3)","q":"<p>A bug in the payment increment of the Harbour Water Taxi system is found and fixed in one afternoon because only that small part changed.</p>","options":["Incremental disadvantage: total cost can be higher","Agile disadvantage: light documentation","Incremental advantage: small increments are easier to test and debug"],"correct":2,"why":"Testing a small increment is easier than testing a whole system at once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (4)","q":"<p>The Island Hop Tours owner moves WhatsApp booking to the top of the backlog, and it is working two weeks later.</p>","options":["Agile advantage: changes are built into the next iteration at low cost","Incremental disadvantage: hard to define the full scope","Agile disadvantage: scope creep"],"correct":0,"why":"Agile welcomes change; a new priority is simply built in the next sprint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (5)","q":"<p>The Daily Island Deals website asks for a final price for its Agile project, but the developer can only quote per sprint because the scope keeps changing.</p>","options":["Agile disadvantage: final cost and date are hard to predict","Agile advantage: frequent delivery","Incremental advantage: lower risk"],"correct":0,"why":"Because scope changes, the total cost of an Agile project is hard to predict, so it suits fixed-price contracts poorly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (6)","q":"<p>The manager acting as product owner for a Barbados hotel app is called away for a month, and the team cannot get priorities approved.</p>","options":["Agile advantage: high customer satisfaction","Agile disadvantage: needs a committed customer representative","Incremental advantage: feedback improves the next iteration"],"correct":1,"why":"Agile depends on an available customer representative; without one the process breaks down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Incremental and Agile trade-offs (7)","q":"<p>A year after the tour app launches, a new developer struggles to maintain it because only short notes were ever written.</p>","options":["Agile disadvantage: light documentation makes maintenance and handover harder","Incremental disadvantage: more management effort","Agile advantage: good team communication"],"correct":0,"why":"Agile’s \"just enough\" documentation can make maintenance and handover to new staff difficult.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Agile misconceptions","q":"<p>The owner of the Island Hop Tours in Montego Bay hears four claims about the Agile approach her developers want to use for her excursion app. Which claim about Agile software development is ACCURATE?</p>","options":["The team will value working software more than comprehensive documentation, but will still document where it adds value","She will receive the complete app only at the end of the project","Agile works best if she stays away from the developers until the end","The team will do no planning, so it can respond to change"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The Agile Manifesto ranks working software above comprehensive documentation; it does not abolish documentation, so her app will still have the notes it needs.","This describes Waterfall. With Agile she will receive working software at the end of each short iteration.","The reverse. Agile depends on her close, continuous collaboration as the customer.","Not so. Her team will plan at the start of every sprint. Agile values responding to change over following a fixed plan, not instead of planning."]},{"t":"match","title":"Waterfall in sequence","q":"<p>Saba Island Utility Board is building a meter-billing system using the Waterfall model. Put the stages in the order Waterfall requires.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Feasibility study: decide whether the project is worthwhile and possible.","Analysis: gather and document all the requirements, then sign them off.","Design: produce the full design of data, processes and interfaces.","Development: write and test the program code.","Implementation: install the system, convert data and train users.","Review: evaluate and maintain the system in use."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each statement to the model it describes BEST.</p><p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>Requirements are frozen after analysis and signed off.</b></p>","options":["Agile","Iterative/incremental","Prototyping","Waterfall"],"correct":3,"why":"Freezing requirements early is the defining feature of Waterfall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (2)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>Users try a working model and say, \"Move that button, add a search box.\"</b></p>","options":["Waterfall","Agile","Iterative/incremental","Prototyping"],"correct":3,"why":"Users evaluating a model to refine requirements is prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (3)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>The core module goes live first; payments are added in the next release.</b></p>","options":["Iterative/incremental","Waterfall","Prototyping","Agile"],"correct":0,"why":"Delivering in working parts that add functionality is incremental development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (4)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>A product owner reprioritises the backlog before every two-week sprint.</b></p>","options":["Iterative/incremental","Prototyping","Agile","Waterfall"],"correct":2,"why":"Product owners, backlogs and sprints are Agile practices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (5)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>Testing begins only after all the code is written.</b></p>","options":["Prototyping","Iterative/incremental","Waterfall","Agile"],"correct":2,"why":"Late testing of the whole system is a feature (and weakness) of Waterfall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (6)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>A quick model is built just to find out what users want, then discarded.</b></p>","options":["Iterative/incremental","Waterfall","Prototyping","Agile"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a throwaway prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (7)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>The team holds a brief stand-up meeting every morning.</b></p>","options":["Iterative/incremental","Waterfall","Agile","Prototyping"],"correct":2,"why":"Daily stand-ups are a typical Agile practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model has this feature? (8)","q":"<p>Which model is this?</p><p><b>Each cycle revisits the design and improves what was built before.</b></p>","options":["Iterative/incremental","Waterfall","Prototyping","Agile"],"correct":0,"why":"Repeating and refining through cycles is iterative development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (1)","q":"<p>For each project, choose the most suitable development model.</p><p>A Barbados bank must replace its interest-calculation engine. The rules are set by regulation and every stage must be documented for auditors.</p>","options":["Waterfall","Agile","Prototyping"],"correct":0,"why":"Stable, regulated requirements and heavy documentation needs point to Waterfall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (2)","q":"<p>A Saba dive shop owner wants a booking website but says, \"Show me something and I will tell you what I like.\"</p>","options":["Prototyping","Waterfall","Iterative/incremental"],"correct":0,"why":"Vague requirements that become clear on seeing a model: prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (3)","q":"<p>A Guyana ministry needs a large licensing system. Vehicle licences are urgent; business and firearm licences can follow later.</p>","options":["Prototyping","Waterfall","Iterative/incremental"],"correct":2,"why":"The urgent core can be delivered first and other modules added later: incremental.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (4)","q":"<p>A Curaçao music-streaming start-up changes priorities every month based on user data. The founder works alongside the developers daily.</p>","options":["Agile","Throwaway prototype only","Waterfall"],"correct":0,"why":"Constant change plus a customer working daily with the team: Agile.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (5)","q":"<p>A Dominica school wants a simple exam-mark system. Requirements are clear, small and unlikely to change; the teacher has little time for meetings.</p>","options":["Agile","Prototyping","Waterfall"],"correct":2,"why":"Clear, stable requirements and little user time favour Waterfall.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits best? (6)","q":"<p>An Anguillan insurer’s staff cannot agree what the claims screens should look like, but once they agree, the system must be built under a fixed-price contract with full documentation for the regulator.</p>","options":["Throwaway prototype, then Waterfall","Waterfall only, with no prototype","Agile only"],"correct":0,"why":"Models can be combined: a throwaway prototype settles the unclear screens, then plan-driven Waterfall builds the documented, fixed-price system.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build an E-F-C justification","q":"<p>A student is recommending a model for Caribbean Supermarkets chain in Jamaica, whose failing stock control must be fixed first. Put the sentences of the answer in the order that follows the E-F-C pattern: recommendation, <b>E</b>vidence, <b>F</b>eature, <b>C</b>onsequence, then a drawback and how to manage it.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["I recommend iterative/incremental development.","Evidence: the scenario says stock control is failing and must be fixed first, and the system is large, covering stock, tills, online orders and rostering.","Feature: incremental development splits a large system into modules and delivers the highest-priority module first as a working increment.","Consequence: stock control can go live as increment 1 within months, so the chain stops losing money on stock while tills and online orders are still being built.","Drawback: later increments may not integrate cleanly with stock control, so the overall design should be planned before increment 1 is built."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising Harbour Bites (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior developer advising the Harbour Bites restaurant group, which has restaurants in Kingston and Montego Bay. Choose wisely.</p><p>The owner, Mr. Richardson, wants an online ordering app. He says: \"I don't really know what it should look like. I'll know when I see it. And I need customers ordering before carnival season in April.\" Which approach do you propose first?</p>","options":["Write a complete, signed-off requirements specification and then follow Waterfall.","Build a quick prototype of the ordering screens for him and some customers to try."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["He cannot state his requirements, so a specification written now would be guesswork, and nothing would be ready by April.","Good. His requirements are vague, and a prototype will make them concrete quickly."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising Harbour Bites (step 2)","q":"<p>The prototype works well. Customers love the menu screen. Mr. Richardson now wants ordering, card payment, delivery tracking and a loyalty scheme, but April is close. How do you plan the build?</p>","options":["Build all four features together and release everything at once.","Deliver in increments: menu and ordering before April, then payment, then tracking and loyalty."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["One big release risks missing April entirely and delays all the benefits.","Strong. The core goes live in time, and later features follow in planned increments."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advising Harbour Bites (step 3)","q":"<p>After launch, Mr. Richardson is in the office every day and keeps changing priorities as customer feedback arrives. How should the team continue?</p>","options":["Work in two-week Agile iterations, with Mr. Richardson as product owner reprioritising the backlog.","Freeze all remaining requirements now and refuse further changes until the end."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. He is available, priorities change often, and Agile handles both.","Freezing requirements ignores real feedback and the customer’s availability."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the scenario clue to the model","q":"<p>Each clue comes from an exam-style scenario. Match it to the model it points to.</p>","left":["\"The rules are fixed by law and auditors must approve each stage.\"","\"The client cannot explain what she wants, but will recognise it on screen.\"","\"The booking module must be live next month; reports can wait.\"","\"The founder sits with the developers and priorities change weekly.\""],"right":["Waterfall","Agile","Prototyping","Iterative/incremental"],"correct":[0,2,3,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Justify the choice","q":"<p>Statia Fuel Terminal needs control software for a fuel pump safety system. Requirements are fully defined by an international safety standard and the regulator must approve the design before any code is written. Which is the BEST justification for using the Waterfall model?</p>","options":["The requirements are stable and fully defined, and Waterfall requires the design to be completed and approved before development, matching the regulator’s demand","Agile delivers working software quickly, which is important for safety systems","Waterfall lets users test an early prototype of the pump controls","Waterfall is the oldest model, so it is the most reliable"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. It uses evidence (stable, fully defined requirements; approval before coding) and links it to a Waterfall feature (stage sign-off).","This argues for Agile, not Waterfall, and speed is not the key concern for a safety system.","User testing of a prototype describes prototyping, not Waterfall.","Age is not a justification. Justify from the features of the model and the scenario."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>Each statement refers to a model named in brackets. Sort it as an advantage or a disadvantage of that model.</p><p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Waterfall) Cost and time are easy to estimate at the start.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Up-front planning makes Waterfall predictable, an advantage for fixed budgets.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Waterfall) Users see working software only near the end.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Late visibility means errors in requirements are found late.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Prototyping) Users may mistake the prototype for the finished system.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Unrealistic expectations are a known disadvantage of prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Prototyping) Vague requirements become clear when users try a model.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Clarifying requirements is the main advantage of prototyping.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Incremental) The core system is delivered and used early.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Early delivery of core functions is the key advantage of incremental development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Incremental) Later increments may not integrate cleanly with earlier ones.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Integration problems are a common disadvantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Agile) The final cost and end date are hard to predict.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Because scope changes, Agile is hard to cost in advance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (8)","q":"<p>Advantage or disadvantage?</p><p><b>(Agile) Changes in requirements can be added in the next iteration.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Flexibility to change is Agile’s chief advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the accurate statements","q":"<p>A classmate wrote these revision notes on software development models. Some contain errors.</p><p>Select EVERY line that is accurate.</p>","options":["1. In Waterfall, each stage is completed before the next one starts.","6. Agile depends on close collaboration with the customer throughout.","7. Iterative development means repeating the development cycle to refine the product.","4. Incremental development delivers the system in working parts.","3. A throwaway prototype is discarded once the requirements are clear.","2. Agile projects produce no documentation at all.","5. Waterfall is best when the client keeps changing their mind."],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4],"why":"Correct. Line 2 is wrong because Agile values working software over comprehensive documentation but still documents where useful. Line 5 is wrong because changing requirements are Waterfall’s weakness."},{"t":"match","title":"Command words in action","q":"<p>A Paper 02 question is about the Leeward Ferries company in St. John’s, which is choosing a model for its new booking system. Match each command word to the student answer that does exactly what that command word asks.</p>","left":["Distinguish","Describe","State an advantage","Justify","Evaluate"],"right":["\"The fare rules are set by the government and will not change (evidence), Waterfall fixes requirements early (feature), so the system can be costed and documented accurately (consequence).\"","\"Cost can be estimated at the start, so the ferry company can agree a fixed-price contract.\"","\"Waterfall is linear and sequential: each stage is completed and signed off before the next begins, and requirements are fixed at the start.\"","\"Waterfall suits the fixed fare rules, but staff will see nothing until the end; showing them screen designs at the design stage would reduce that risk.\"","\"In Waterfall Leeward Ferries would receive the system once, at the end, whereas in Agile it would receive working software every two weeks.\""],"correct":[4,2,1,0,3]}]},{"id":"assess_6ck","title":"Module 3 · Unit 4 quiz: The system development life cycle and software engineering","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 4: The system development life cycle and software engineering. 102 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Put the nine phases in order","q":"<p>The Bayside Bakery in New Amsterdam is replacing its handwritten order book with a computerised ordering system. Put the nine phases from the class slides in the order the project passes through them.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Problem definition: interview the bakers about the order book, agree the aims and produce the System Proposal.","Feasibility study: test technical, economic, operational, social/behavioural and time feasibility; the Feasibility Report gives a go/no-go decision.","Project planning: set roles, milestones and resources in the Project Management Plan and a Gantt chart.","Analysis: gather facts and write functional and non-functional requirements in the Requirements Specification.","Design: produce the logical and physical designs and a prototype in the Design Specification.","Development: code the system and produce test plans and user and technical manuals.","Testing: verify and validate the system and write the Test Report.","Implementation: install, convert the order data, train staff and change over to the new system.","Evaluation and maintenance: evaluate the system and maintain it until it is decommissioned."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7","Step 8","Step 9"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (1)","q":"<p>The Bayside Bakery project in New Amsterdam is described below using the nine phases from the class slides. For each activity, choose where it sits in the six CAPE syllabus stages (your exam framework).</p><p>The analyst interviews the bakers and the owner about the order book and writes a System Proposal stating the problem, objectives, terms of reference, constraints and expected benefits (problem definition).</p>","options":["Review","Feasibility study","Analysis"],"correct":1,"why":"Problem definition is the opening part of the syllabus feasibility study stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (2)","q":"<p>After the \"go\" decision, a Project Management Plan and a Gantt chart are drawn up (project planning).</p>","options":["Review","Between the feasibility study and analysis","Implementation"],"correct":1,"why":"Project planning follows the go/no-go decision of the feasibility study and leads into analysis; the plan then controls every later stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (3)","q":"<p>The Requirements Specification lists what the system must do and how fast and securely it must do it (analysis).</p>","options":["Design","Analysis","Feasibility study"],"correct":1,"why":"Specifying requirements is the analysis stage in both models.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (4)","q":"<p>Testers run black box and regression tests and write the Test Report (testing).</p>","options":["Design","Review","Development"],"correct":2,"why":"In the syllabus six, testing is part of development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (5)","q":"<p>Order records are converted, staff are trained and the new system goes live (implementation).</p>","options":["Development","Review","Implementation"],"correct":2,"why":"Install, convert, train and change over: implementation in both models.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Nine phases, six stages (6)","q":"<p>A year after go-live, a developer fixes a fault and adds a gluten-free option, recorded in a Maintenance Report (evaluation and maintenance).</p>","options":["Review","Development","Implementation"],"correct":0,"why":"Evaluation and maintenance correspond to the syllabus review stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (1)","q":"<p>The Cornerstone Code in Belize City is building a fee-payment system for a secondary school. A trainee has written notes on how different people approached the job. Sort each note: is it a software engineering practice, or an informal \"just code it\" approach?</p><p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>The team writes down and agrees the school's requirements with the bursar before any code is written.</b></p>","options":["Software engineering practice","Informal trial and error"],"correct":0,"why":"Formal, agreed requirements are a core software engineering practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (2)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>Every programmer follows the company coding standards: meaningful variable names, comments and separate modules.</b></p>","options":["Informal trial and error","Software engineering practice"],"correct":1,"why":"Coding standards make the code maintainable: software engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (3)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>Each change is saved in version control so that an earlier working version can be restored.</b></p>","options":["Informal trial and error","Software engineering practice"],"correct":1,"why":"Version control is a disciplined engineering practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (4)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>Testers follow a test plan with normal, boundary and erroneous fee amounts and record the actual results.</b></p>","options":["Software engineering practice","Informal trial and error"],"correct":0,"why":"Planned testing against a test plan is software engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (5)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>A technical manual is written so that another developer can maintain the system next year.</b></p>","options":["Software engineering practice","Informal trial and error"],"correct":0,"why":"Documentation for maintenance is part of the engineering discipline.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (6)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>A student programmer starts typing code on her laptop the same day she hears about the project.</b></p>","options":["Software engineering practice","Informal trial and error"],"correct":1,"why":"Coding with no requirements or design is trial and error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (7)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>The program is \"tested\" by trying a few payments to see whether anything crashes.</b></p>","options":["Software engineering practice","Informal trial and error"],"correct":1,"why":"Random checks with no planned data or expected results are not systematic testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineered or improvised? (8)","q":"<p>Software engineering practice or informal trial and error?</p><p><b>Nobody records why the code was written that way, so only the original programmer can fix it.</b></p>","options":["Informal trial and error","Software engineering practice"],"correct":0,"why":"Undocumented code makes the organisation depend on one person: the opposite of engineering.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the engineering goal","q":"<p>Each situation shows one goal of software engineering being achieved in a Caribbean project. Match each situation to the goal it shows.</p>","left":["Island Hardware stock system calculates the correct reorder quantity every day without failing.","Two years later, a new contractor adds a field to the school fee system in one afternoon, guided by clear code and the technical manual.","New cashiers at the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet learn the till system in a single morning.","Saba Dive Centre booking app runs smoothly on old phones and slow island Internet connections.","Leeward Credit Union system goes live on the planned date and within its approved budget."],"right":["Delivery on time and within budget","Efficiency","Usability","Maintainability","Correctness and reliability"],"correct":[4,3,2,1,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Engineering or just programming?","q":"<p>The Cornerstone Code in Bridgetown is building a school fee-payment system. Instead of letting one programmer \"just code it\", the firm documents requirements, designs the database, tests against a test plan and writes manuals. Which statement BEST describes software engineering, the approach the firm is using?</p>","options":["Repairing computer hardware so that software can run correctly.","Choosing which software package an organisation should buy.","Writing program code in a high-level programming language.","The application of a systematic, disciplined approach to developing, operating and maintaining software."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Nothing in the scenario involves hardware repair, and hardware repair is not software engineering.","Cornerstone Code is building a system, not choosing a package. Selecting packages is not the definition of software engineering.","That is programming, which is only one of the activities Cornerstone Code carries out. Its requirements, design, test plan and manuals go well beyond coding.","Correct. Cornerstone Code plans, specifies, tests and documents across the whole life of the system, not just the coding."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (1)","q":"<p>The Carenage Water Taxi company is considering an online ticketing system. Sort each finding from its feasibility study into the type of feasibility it concerns.</p><p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>The ticket booth has no Internet connection and the nearest fibre line is 200 metres away.</b></p>","options":["Operational","Technical","Social/behavioural","Time","Economic"],"correct":1,"why":"Whether existing technology can handle it is technical feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>No current employee knows how to configure a payment gateway.</b></p>","options":["Time","Economic","Social/behavioural","Operational","Technical"],"correct":4,"why":"Whether existing staff can handle the technology is technical feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>The system will cost about USD 18,000 but should save USD 9,000 a year in cash-handling costs.</b></p>","options":["Time","Technical","Operational","Economic","Social/behavioural"],"correct":3,"why":"Do benefits justify cost? A cost/benefit comparison is economic feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>Online sales are expected to increase bookings from cruise passengers.</b></p>","options":["Time","Social/behavioural","Economic","Technical","Operational"],"correct":2,"why":"A tangible benefit counted in the cost/benefit analysis: economic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>Tickets would still be checked at the boarding gate exactly as now, so the boarding procedure barely changes.</b></p>","options":["Technical","Social/behavioural","Time","Operational","Economic"],"correct":3,"why":"Does it fit existing procedures? That is operational feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>The new process removes the need to count cash at the end of each shift, which fits how managers already close the day.</b></p>","options":["Technical","Economic","Social/behavioural","Time","Operational"],"correct":4,"why":"Fit with existing business procedures is operational feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>Long-serving boat captains dislike using smartphones and may refuse to scan e-tickets.</b></p>","options":["Time","Social/behavioural","Technical","Economic","Operational"],"correct":1,"why":"Can users adapt? Attitudes and resistance to change are social/behavioural feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>Booth staff worry that online tickets will cost them their jobs.</b></p>","options":["Social/behavioural","Economic","Time","Operational","Technical"],"correct":0,"why":"Fear of job losses affects whether users will adapt: social/behavioural.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (9)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>The owners want it live before the cruise season starts in eleven weeks.</b></p>","options":["Operational","Social/behavioural","Technical","Time","Economic"],"correct":3,"why":"Can it meet the deadline? That is time (schedule) feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of feasibility? (10)","q":"<p>Which type of feasibility does this finding address?</p><p><b>The developer estimates fourteen weeks for the full build and testing.</b></p>","options":["Social/behavioural","Time","Economic","Technical","Operational"],"correct":1,"why":"Comparing time needed with time available is time feasibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"What belongs in the system proposal?","q":"<p>A junior analyst at the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet has drafted a list of sections for the system proposal, the deliverable of the problem definition phase, for a new stock-control system.</p><p>Select EVERY line that belongs in the system proposal.</p>","options":["1. Problem definition: paper stock cards mean medicines run out unnoticed.","7. Expected benefits: less expired stock and fewer emergency orders.","5. Constraints: a budget of USD 15,000, and it must run on the existing back-office PC.","6. Step-by-step instructions telling cashiers how to print a receipt.","8. A Gantt chart showing the start and end of every development activity.","4. Terms of reference: requested by the owner; covers stock control only, not the tills.","2. Objective: no medicine runs out without a reorder being raised.","3. The complete program code for the stock module."],"correct":[0,1,2,5,6],"why":"Correct. The system proposal sets out the problem definition, objectives, terms of reference, constraints and expected benefits. Program code belongs to development, receipt instructions to the user manual, and the Gantt chart to project planning."},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (1)","q":"<p>The Seaview Medical Centre is replacing its paper appointment diaries. The practice manager has a pile of notes. Decide whether each belongs in the documents of the feasibility study stage, the <b>system proposal or feasibility report</b> (should we do it?), or in the <b>project management plan</b> (after the \"go\" decision: how, who and when?).</p><p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Doctors are double-booked because receptionists keep three separate paper diaries.</b></p>","options":["Project management plan","System proposal or feasibility report"],"correct":1,"why":"The problem definition is set out in the system proposal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (2)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Option 1: buy a clinic package. Option 2: commission a custom system.</b></p>","options":["Project management plan","System proposal or feasibility report"],"correct":1,"why":"Alternative solutions are compared in the feasibility report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (3)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Set-up will cost about USD 12,000 and save about 15 receptionist hours a week.</b></p>","options":["System proposal or feasibility report","Project management plan"],"correct":0,"why":"Cost/benefit findings (economic feasibility) belong in the feasibility report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (4)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Recommendation: buy the package, because it is cheaper and can be live sooner.</b></p>","options":["System proposal or feasibility report","Project management plan"],"correct":0,"why":"A justified go/no-go recommendation ends the feasibility report.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (5)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Design: 4 weeks; development: 8 weeks; implementation: 3 weeks.</b></p>","options":["System proposal or feasibility report","Project management plan"],"correct":1,"why":"Timescales for each remaining stage are in the project plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (6)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Milestone: the test plan must be approved before coding ends.</b></p>","options":["Project management plan","System proposal or feasibility report"],"correct":0,"why":"Milestones are part of the project plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (7)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>The IT contractor builds the database; the practice manager signs off the screen layouts.</b></p>","options":["Project management plan","System proposal or feasibility report"],"correct":0,"why":"Responsibilities are set out in the project plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort it: proposal or project plan? (8)","q":"<p>Proposal/feasibility report or project plan?</p><p><b>Risk: the contractor may be away in August, so a backup developer is named.</b></p>","options":["System proposal or feasibility report","Project management plan"],"correct":1,"why":"Risks and how they will be handled are in the project plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Inside the project management plan","q":"<p>The project manager for the Seaview Medical Centre appointment system is writing the project management plan. Match each entry to the area of the plan, or the chart, it belongs to.</p>","left":["Two label printers will be ordered from an approved supplier by 1 March.","If the contractor is away in August, a named backup developer takes over.","A request to add online payments must be approved by the practice manager before any work starts.","Progress is reported to the doctors at a short meeting every Friday.","Each screen layout is checked against the requirements before it is signed off.","A bar for each activity shows its start and end dates."],"right":["Change","Communication","Risk","Quality","Procurement","Gantt chart"],"correct":[4,2,0,1,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Proposal or plan?","q":"<p>The manager of the Harbour Gems Jewellers asks for a document showing each task in the new sales-system project, who is responsible for it, its start and finish dates, and the budget allocated. Which deliverable is she asking for?</p>","options":["The system proposal","The conversion plan","The project plan","The technical manual"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["The proposal recommends WHETHER to proceed. Detailed tasks, responsibilities and dates come after approval.","A conversion plan covers changing over from the old system to the new one, not the whole project schedule.","Correct. Tasks, responsibilities, timescales, milestones and resources are the contents of the project plan.","The technical manual documents how the finished system works for IT staff."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (1)","q":"<p>Here are lines from the requirements specification for the Seaview Medical Centre appointment system. Sort each requirement.</p><p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>The system books an appointment with a chosen doctor at a chosen time.</b></p>","options":["Non-functional requirement","Functional requirement"],"correct":1,"why":"Booking is an action the system performs: functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (2)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>The system sends an SMS reminder to the patient the day before the appointment.</b></p>","options":["Functional requirement","Non-functional requirement"],"correct":0,"why":"Sending a reminder is something the system does: functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (3)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>The system prints a daily appointment list for each doctor.</b></p>","options":["Non-functional requirement","Functional requirement"],"correct":1,"why":"Producing an output is a function: functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (4)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>The system cancels a booking and frees the slot for another patient.</b></p>","options":["Non-functional requirement","Functional requirement"],"correct":1,"why":"Cancelling is an action: functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (5)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>Only clinic staff with a password can view patient details.</b></p>","options":["Functional requirement","Non-functional requirement"],"correct":1,"why":"A security quality: non-functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (6)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>A new receptionist can book an appointment after ten minutes of training.</b></p>","options":["Non-functional requirement","Functional requirement"],"correct":0,"why":"A usability quality: non-functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (7)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>A booking is confirmed within five seconds, even at the busy Monday-morning peak.</b></p>","options":["Non-functional requirement","Functional requirement"],"correct":0,"why":"A performance quality: non-functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Functional or non-functional? (8)","q":"<p>Functional (what it does) or non-functional (a quality it must have)?</p><p><b>Online booking is available 24 hours a day, including weekends.</b></p>","options":["Functional requirement","Non-functional requirement"],"correct":1,"why":"An availability quality: non-functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the design item to its purpose","q":"<p>The designer of the Harbour Ferry Line booking system has produced the items below. Match each design item to the part of the system it specifies.</p>","left":["An ER diagram linking Passengers, Bookings and Sailings","A drawing of the booking screen showing its fields and buttons","The rule \"a sailing date cannot be in the past\"","A flowchart for \"check seats available\"","A layout of the printed boarding pass","Ticket clerks can sell tickets, but only the manager can change fares","A data dictionary entry: BookingID, text, 6 characters, must be unique","A decision table showing which fare applies to children, seniors and residents"],"right":["Data storage: tables, keys and relationships","Input validation","Processing logic","Security and access levels","Output design","User interface and input screen layout","Description of every data item","Processing rules with several conditions"],"correct":[0,5,1,2,4,3,6,7]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (1)","q":"<p>The design team for the Harbour Ferry Line booking system has produced the items below. Decide whether each is part of the logical design, the physical design or a prototype.</p><p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>Each booking must link one passenger to one sailing.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Logical design","Physical design"],"correct":1,"why":"A statement about the data needed, with no technology chosen: logical design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (2)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>The system needs to record each passenger's name, phone number and nationality.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Logical design","Physical design"],"correct":1,"why":"What data is needed, not how it is stored: logical design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (3)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>A process \"check seats available\" takes a sailing and returns the number of free seats.</b></p>","options":["Prototype","Physical design","Logical design"],"correct":2,"why":"A process described without any language or platform: logical design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (4)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>The Bookings table has a six-character text field, BookingID, as its primary key.</b></p>","options":["Physical design","Logical design","Prototype"],"correct":0,"why":"Exact field names and data types are the blueprint: physical design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (5)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>The system will be a web app on a cloud server, written in Java.</b></p>","options":["Physical design","Prototype","Logical design"],"correct":0,"why":"Platform and programming language are physical design decisions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (6)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>The booking form uses a drop-down list for sailing times and a date picker.</b></p>","options":["Physical design","Logical design","Prototype"],"correct":0,"why":"The actual form layout is part of the physical design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (7)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>Ticket clerks click through a model of the booking screens and ask for a larger \"Pay\" button.</b></p>","options":["Physical design","Logical design","Prototype"],"correct":2,"why":"Users inspecting a working model before development: prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (8)","q":"<p>Logical design, physical design or prototype?</p><p><b>A mock-up of the boarding pass screen is shown to the operations manager for approval before coding.</b></p>","options":["Physical design","Prototype","Logical design"],"correct":1,"why":"A model shown to the customer before development: prototype.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (1)","q":"<p>The Harbour View Guesthouse booking system accepts stays of <b>1 to 14 nights</b>, entered as a whole number. You are completing the test plan. Classify each test value.</p><p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>A guest books 5 nights.</b></p>","options":["Boundary","Normal","Erroneous"],"correct":1,"why":"5 is a typical valid value: normal data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (2)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>A cruise-ship crew member books 10 nights.</b></p>","options":["Boundary","Erroneous","Normal"],"correct":2,"why":"10 is valid and not at an edge: normal data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (3)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>A guest books 1 night.</b></p>","options":["Boundary","Normal","Erroneous"],"correct":0,"why":"1 is the lowest valid value: boundary data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (4)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>A guest books 14 nights.</b></p>","options":["Erroneous","Normal","Boundary"],"correct":2,"why":"14 is the highest valid value: boundary data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (5)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>A guest tries to book 15 nights.</b></p>","options":["Erroneous","Boundary","Normal"],"correct":0,"why":"15 is just outside the range and should be rejected: erroneous data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (6)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>The receptionist enters 0 nights by mistake.</b></p>","options":["Erroneous","Boundary","Normal"],"correct":0,"why":"0 is outside the valid range, so it is erroneous, not boundary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (7)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>The receptionist types \"two\" instead of a number.</b></p>","options":["Erroneous","Normal","Boundary"],"correct":0,"why":"Wrong data type: erroneous data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Normal, boundary or erroneous? (8)","q":"<p>What type of test data is this?</p><p><b>The receptionist enters -3 nights.</b></p>","options":["Normal","Erroneous","Boundary"],"correct":1,"why":"A negative value is invalid: erroneous data.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the deliverable to its stage","q":"<p>Match each deliverable to the SDLC stage that produces it (or, for the manuals, where it is mainly completed).</p>","left":["System proposal and feasibility report","Requirements specification (functional and non-functional requirements)","Design specification: ER diagrams, screen layouts, data dictionary","Coded system, test plans, test report, user and technical manuals","Conversion plan and the new system in live use","Evaluation and maintenance reports"],"right":["Development","Implementation","Review","Feasibility study","Analysis","Design"],"correct":[3,4,5,0,1,2]},{"t":"match","title":"Order the testing","q":"<p>Developers of the Anguilla Ferry Pass app are building and testing it against the test plan. Put the development steps in the order they are carried out.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Build the database from the ER diagram and write the program modules, following coding standards.","Test the \"buy ticket\" module on its own with normal, boundary and erroneous data.","Test the \"buy ticket\" and payment modules together to check that they pass data correctly.","Test the whole app on several phone models.","Ferry company staff carry out acceptance testing to confirm the app meets their requirements."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (1)","q":"<p>The Saba Dive Centre is developing a booking system. Choose the SDLC stage in which each activity takes place.</p><p>The owner is told the project would cost more than three years of the savings it brings, and is advised not to proceed.</p>","options":["Analysis","Review","Feasibility study"],"correct":2,"why":"Deciding whether the project is worth it is the feasibility study (economic feasibility).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (2)","q":"<p>An analyst interviews dive instructors to find out why bookings are lost.</p>","options":["Feasibility study","Analysis","Design"],"correct":1,"why":"Investigating the current system in detail is analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (3)","q":"<p>Screen layouts for the booking form are drawn and the database tables are specified.</p>","options":["Design","Development","Analysis"],"correct":0,"why":"Deciding how the system will look and store data is design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (4)","q":"<p>Programmers enter a booking for 0 divers to check that it is rejected.</p>","options":["Development","Implementation","Design"],"correct":0,"why":"Carrying out tests from the test plan happens during development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (5)","q":"<p>Last year's bookings are transferred into the new database and staff attend a training session.</p>","options":["Implementation","Review","Development"],"correct":0,"why":"Data conversion and training are part of implementation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which stage is this? (6)","q":"<p>Six months after go-live, the owner checks whether lost bookings have fallen as the objectives promised.</p>","options":["Review","Analysis","Implementation"],"correct":0,"why":"Evaluating the system against its objectives is the review stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name that test","q":"<p>Testers are checking the Anguilla Ferry Pass app before it goes live. Match each activity to the type of testing it shows.</p>","left":["A tester reads the fare-calculation code and chooses data that makes every IF branch run at least once.","A tester who has never seen the code enters a journey and checks the fare shown against the expected result.","The \"buy ticket\" module is tested on its own before it is joined to anything else.","The \"buy ticket\" and payment modules are tested together to check the fare is passed across correctly.","Ferry company staff use the app for a week and sign to confirm it meets their requirements.","After a bug in the receipt module is fixed, all the earlier tests are run again."],"right":["Regression testing","Integration testing","Unit testing","Black box testing","Acceptance testing","White box testing"],"correct":[5,3,2,1,4,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (1)","q":"<p>The King Street Jewellers in Kingston is checking its new sales system. Decide whether each check is <b>verification</b> (are we building the product right?) or <b>validation</b> (are we building the right product?).</p><p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>Checking that the turnover tax on each sale is calculated exactly as the design specification states.</b></p>","options":["Validation","Verification"],"correct":1,"why":"Checking against the specification: verification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (2)","q":"<p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>A code review confirms the receipt module follows the agreed design and coding standards.</b></p>","options":["Validation","Verification"],"correct":1,"why":"Is it built right? Verification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (3)","q":"<p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>Black box tests show every report matches the expected results in the test plan.</b></p>","options":["Verification","Validation"],"correct":0,"why":"Matching the planned expected results: verification.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (4)","q":"<p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>The owner tries the system and finds it cannot record layaway payments, which the shop relies on.</b></p>","options":["Validation","Verification"],"correct":0,"why":"Is it the right product for the business? Validation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (5)","q":"<p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>Sales staff confirm they can now serve cruise passengers faster, as the system proposal promised.</b></p>","options":["Validation","Verification"],"correct":0,"why":"Checking against the real need in the proposal: validation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Verification or validation? (6)","q":"<p>Verification or validation?</p><p><b>The manager checks that the stock report shows the information she actually needs to reorder.</b></p>","options":["Validation","Verification"],"correct":0,"why":"Does it meet the user's real need? Validation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Right product or product right?","q":"<p>Harbour Gems Jewellers' new sales system passes every test in its test plan: totals, receipts and reports all match the design specification. In acceptance testing, however, staff find it cannot record layaway payments, which many customers use. Which statement BEST describes the situation?</p>","options":["The system has failed regression testing.","The system has been verified but not validated.","The system has failed white box testing of its code.","The system has been validated but not verified."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Regression testing re-runs earlier tests after a change. Nothing was changed here, and every planned test passed.","Correct. It was built right (it matches the specification), but it is not yet the right product, because it misses a real business need.","The code passed every test. The problem is a missing requirement, not faulty code.","This is reversed. Matching the specification is verification; failing the users' real need is a validation failure."]},{"t":"ma","title":"What belongs in the conversion plan?","q":"<p>The Leeward Credit Union in Brades is drafting the conversion plan for its new member-accounts system. An assistant has listed possible contents.</p><p>Select EVERY line that belongs in the conversion plan.</p>","options":["7. Fallback: keep the old system ready to switch back if serious errors appear.","2. Timetable: data conversion over the long weekend; go-live on Tuesday.","6. Commented program code for the interest-calculation module.","4. A cost-benefit analysis comparing a package with a custom system.","3. Every account balance to be checked against the old system before branches open.","1. Changeover method: parallel running for one month, because account balances are critical.","5. Teller training sessions in the week before go-live."],"correct":[0,1,4,5,6],"why":"Correct. The conversion plan covers the changeover method and reason, timetable, data conversion and checking, training and fallback. Cost-benefit analysis belongs in the system proposal; program code belongs in the technical manual."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the changeover to the situation","q":"<p>Match each Caribbean scenario to the changeover method being used.</p>","left":["A supermarket chain switches off its old tills on Sunday night and opens on Monday with only the new ones.","A Barbados payroll office runs the old and new payroll systems for two months and compares every payslip.","A Jamaican hotel group replaces the reservations module first, then housekeeping, then billing.","A pharmacy chain keeps its old system running while the new system's key stock and prescription functions run alongside it until fully tested, then switches over completely."],"right":["Pilot running","Direct changeover","Parallel running","Phased implementation"],"correct":[1,2,3,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (1)","q":"<p>You are assembling the documentation for the University Book Corner's new sales system. Decide which manual each item belongs in.</p><p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>Step-by-step instructions, with screenshots, for processing a sale.</b></p>","options":["Technical manual","User manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Task instructions for operators belong in the user manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (2)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>What to do when the message \"Card declined\" appears.</b></p>","options":["Technical manual","User manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Error messages and responses help end users: user manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (3)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>A list of frequently asked questions from cashiers.</b></p>","options":["User manual","Technical manual"],"correct":0,"why":"FAQs support users: user manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (4)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>The data dictionary listing each field, its data type and length.</b></p>","options":["User manual","Technical manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Database detail is for developers: technical manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (5)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>Commented program listings for the discount module.</b></p>","options":["User manual","Technical manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Code is for maintainers: technical manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (6)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>Installation steps and the minimum server specification.</b></p>","options":["Technical manual","User manual"],"correct":0,"why":"Installation and hardware requirements are technical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (7)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>The completed test plan with expected and actual results.</b></p>","options":["User manual","Technical manual"],"correct":1,"why":"Test evidence helps maintainers: technical manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"User manual or technical manual? (8)","q":"<p>Which manual should contain this?</p><p><b>A plain-language glossary of terms such as \"void\" and \"refund\".</b></p>","options":["Technical manual","User manual"],"correct":1,"why":"A plain glossary helps users: user manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the SDLC in order","q":"<p>Arrange the six SDLC stages, as named in the CAPE syllabus, in the order a project passes through them.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Feasibility study: decide whether the system is worth developing and can be developed.","Analysis: investigate the current system and define the requirements.","Design: specify inputs, outputs, data, processes and interface.","Development: build the system and test it against the test plan.","Implementation: install, convert data, train users and change over.","Review: evaluate the working system against its objectives."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Testing or review?","q":"<p>Five months after the Statia Port Authority began using a new cargo-tracking system, a team compares the number of lost container records with the target in the original system proposal and surveys staff about the system. This activity belongs to which SDLC stage?</p>","options":["Implementation, because the system is new","Development, because the system is being tested","Review, because the live system is being evaluated against its objectives","Analysis, because staff are being surveyed"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Implementation ends once the changeover is complete. Evaluation months later is review.","Testing in development happens before go-live and checks the system against the test plan. This system has been live for five months.","Correct. Comparing actual performance with the proposal's objectives after real use is the review stage.","Surveys can be used in analysis, but here the purpose is to evaluate a system already in use against its objectives."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Steer the project (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the systems analyst for Island Wheels Car Rentals, which has branches at the airport and in Bridgetown and wants to replace its paper booking cards with a computerised system. Make the key decisions.</p><p>The owner says: \"My nephew can code. Let him start building tomorrow.\" What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Define the problem and carry out a feasibility study first, presenting a system proposal and feasibility report that compare options.","Agree: starting to code early saves time."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. A feasibility study checks technical, economic, operational, social/behavioural and time feasibility before money is committed.","Coding without a feasibility study or requirements risks building the wrong system."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Steer the project (step 2)","q":"<p>The proposal is approved. Development is finished and tested. Bookings are critical, and the owner cannot afford lost reservations during the tourist season. Which changeover method do you put in the conversion plan?</p>","options":["Phase it in by location: the Bridgetown branch first, keeping backups of the paper cards, then NMIA.","Direct changeover at both branches on the first day of the season."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Sensible. Phasing by location means problems are found at the smaller branch while the busy airport branch continues safely.","Direct is the riskiest method, and the timing is the worst possible."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Steer the project (step 3)","q":"<p>The system is live at both branches. Four months later, the owner asks whether the project was a success. What do you do?</p>","options":["Carry out a review: compare results with the proposal's objectives, survey staff and customers, and produce an evaluation report.","Tell him it is working, so nothing more is needed."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Review closes the cycle and identifies improvements.","Without review, hidden problems and missed objectives go unnoticed."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the changeover","q":"<p>The Caribbean Mutual Bank is replacing its customer-account system. Accuracy is critical, the bank must be able to prove every balance is correct, and cost is a lesser concern. Which changeover method is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Pilot running","Phased implementation","Direct changeover","Parallel running"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Pilot running tries only key parts of the new system alongside the old, so not every balance would be compared. It is a compromise, but the bank needs full proof.","Phased reduces risk by introducing parts one at a time, but it does not let every balance be checked against the old system.","Direct offers no fallback and no way of comparing results. It is too risky for critical financial data.","Correct. Running both systems lets the bank compare every output and fall back on the old system, which justifies the extra cost."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (1)","q":"<p>Apply the class scenarios. For each situation, choose the BEST option and read the feedback for the justification. (Extension: beyond the CXC Unit 1 syllabus, except the changeover question.)</p><p>Scenario 1: The Sunset Boutique on Albert Street needs a payroll system for its eight staff within a month and has a very limited budget.</p>","options":["Ready-made package","Joint application development","In-house development"],"correct":0,"why":"A ready-made package is cheapest and fastest, and payroll is a common need, so having no competitive advantage hardly matters. The trade-off is vendor dependence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (2)","q":"<p>Scenario 2: The Windward Commercial Bank in Castries needs software that matches its unique loan products, and it has a strong in-house IT team.</p>","options":["In-house development","Ready-made package","End user development"],"correct":0,"why":"In-house development fits unique needs fully, and the bank has the IT staff to build and maintain it. It is costly, but a package would not fit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (3)","q":"<p>Scenario 3: Accounts staff at the Island Builders' Supply want to automate a spreadsheet process. No programmers are available, so they will build it themselves. What is this approach?</p>","options":["In-house development","End user development","Ready-made package"],"correct":1,"why":"Staff who are not programmers building their own solution with a spreadsheet is end user development. It can suit a small, low-risk task.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (4)","q":"<p>Scenario 3 (continued): What is the MAIN risk of the Island Builders' Supply staff building it themselves?</p>","options":["Spreadsheet errors that go unnoticed, and a system that is hard to maintain","Vendor dependence","Very high development cost"],"correct":0,"why":"End user development is prone to errors and hard to maintain: it is rarely tested or documented, so mistakes slip through and nobody else can fix it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (5)","q":"<p>Scenario 4: On the Coastline Tours booking project, users and developers keep misunderstanding each other's requirements, and the project is slipping. Which approach could fix this?</p>","options":["End user development","Joint application development","Ready-made package"],"correct":1,"why":"Joint application development brings programmers and users together, so there is less miscommunication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (6)","q":"<p>Scenario 4 (continued): Which phase does joint application development MAINLY affect on the Coastline Tours project?</p>","options":["Implementation","Evaluation and maintenance","Analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"Requirements are gathered and agreed in analysis, which is where the misunderstandings arise.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the way to obtain it (7)","q":"<p>Scenario 6: The Bayview General Hospital in Castries is replacing its patient records system. A fault could harm patients, and results must be checked against the old system.</p>","options":["Phased by location","Parallel running","Direct changeover"],"correct":1,"why":"Parallel running keeps old and new side by side until fault free so results can be compared; patient safety justifies the cost. Pilot running is a defensible cheaper compromise; direct is far too risky.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_6u3","title":"Module 3 · Unit 5 quiz: Tools used in the SDLC","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 5: Tools used in the SDLC. 118 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Five managers","q":"<p>Bayside Freight is replacing its cargo-tracking system. The analyst needs detailed information about the needs of the company's five department managers, including the reasons behind their current procedures. Which fact-finding tool is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Observation","Questionnaires","Interviews","Review of printed material"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Observation shows what people do, but not the reasons behind policies or what managers want from a new system.","Questionnaires suit large groups and mostly closed questions. With only five managers you lose the chance to probe reasons.","Correct. Five people is a small group, and interviews allow in-depth questions and follow-up about reasons and procedures.","Documents show the data in use, but they cannot explain why procedures exist or what managers need."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (1)","q":"<p>The Carenage Water Taxi company in Grenada is replacing its paper ticketing. Sort each situation by the fact-finding tool that suits it BEST.</p><p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Find out the owner's goals and budget for the new system.</b></p>","options":["Observation","Focus group","Interview","Questionnaire","Review of printed material"],"correct":2,"why":"One key decision-maker with detailed, personal goals: interview.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (2)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Collect satisfaction ratings from 300 regular passengers.</b></p>","options":["Interview","Review of printed material","Focus group","Questionnaire","Observation"],"correct":3,"why":"Many people, the same rating questions: questionnaire.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (3)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>See how ticket sellers really handle a queue when a cruise ship docks.</b></p>","options":["Review of printed material","Observation","Questionnaire","Focus group","Interview"],"correct":1,"why":"Real day-to-day work under pressure is best seen by observation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (4)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Identify every field on the current paper ticket and daily sales sheet.</b></p>","options":["Interview","Review of printed material","Focus group","Questionnaire","Observation"],"correct":1,"why":"The data items are written on existing documents: review of printed material.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (5)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Ask the two senior captains why boarding procedures are done in a certain order.</b></p>","options":["Questionnaire","Interview","Observation","Focus group","Review of printed material"],"correct":1,"why":"A small number of experts and the reasons behind procedures: interview.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (6)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Count how often 40 part-time staff use each feature, using rating scales.</b></p>","options":["Review of printed material","Interview","Observation","Questionnaire","Focus group"],"correct":3,"why":"A large group answering quantitative questions: questionnaire.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (7)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Time how long it takes to issue one ticket at the St. George's kiosk.</b></p>","options":["Focus group","Observation","Review of printed material","Interview","Questionnaire"],"correct":1,"why":"Timing a task as it happens is observation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (8)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Read last year's procedures manual and monthly sales reports.</b></p>","options":["Interview","Observation","Focus group","Questionnaire","Review of printed material"],"correct":4,"why":"Studying existing manuals and reports is review of printed material.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (9)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Bring eight ticket sellers, captains and office staff together to discuss and agree what the new booking screen should show.</b></p>","options":["Interview","Focus group","Observation","Questionnaire","Review of printed material"],"correct":1,"why":"A guided group discussion in which users build on each other's ideas: focus group.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which fact-finding tool? (10)","q":"<p>Which tool fits this situation best?</p><p><b>Gather six regular passengers to react together to a proposed mobile ticket and talk through their concerns.</b></p>","options":["Observation","Questionnaire","Focus group","Review of printed material","Interview"],"correct":2,"why":"A small group reacting to a proposal through discussion: focus group.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (1)","q":"<p>Sunset Supermarkets chain, with branches in Port of Spain, San Fernando and Arima, is planning a new stock system. Its analyst must plan interviews, design a questionnaire, run a focus group, observe staff and write the requirements. Judge each decision.</p><p>The analyst asks every branch manager the same ten prepared questions in the same order, so their answers can be compared. What type of interview is this?</p>","options":["Unstructured","Participant observation","Semi-structured","Structured"],"correct":3,"why":"The same prepared questions in the same order for everyone is a structured interview; answers are easy to compare.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (2)","q":"<p>With the owner, the analyst prepares a few questions but follows up on anything interesting she says about opening a branch in St. Kitts. What type of interview is this?</p>","options":["Unstructured","Semi-structured","Closed","Structured"],"correct":1,"why":"Prepared questions plus freedom to follow up is semi-structured, the most common type in practice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (3)","q":"<p>The questionnaire asks cashiers: \"Rate the speed of the current till system from 1 to 5.\" What kind of question is this?</p>","options":["A leading question","An open question giving qualitative data","A follow-up question","A closed question giving quantitative data"],"correct":3,"why":"A rating scale offers fixed answers, so it is a closed question producing numbers that are quick to total and chart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (4)","q":"<p>A draft question reads: \"Don't you agree the old system is far too slow?\" What is wrong with it?</p>","options":["It is too short to be useful","It is an open question","It is a leading question that pushes respondents towards one answer","It produces quantitative data"],"correct":2,"why":"The wording suggests the \"right\" answer, so results would be biased. Use neutral wording.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (5)","q":"<p>Before sending the questionnaire to 150 staff, the analyst tries it on five cashiers in San Fernando. Why?</p>","options":["To guarantee a 100% response rate","To create the Hawthorne effect","To replace the interviews","To pilot it and find confusing questions"],"correct":3,"why":"Piloting on a few users finds misunderstood questions before the full survey goes out, when they can no longer be changed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (6)","q":"<p>Shelf-packers in Arima worked unusually fast on the one day the analyst watched them. What should the analyst do?</p>","options":["Stop fact-finding at that branch","Accept the timings as typical","Switch to a questionnaire only","Observe over several days and compare with the stock-system logs"],"correct":3,"why":"This is the Hawthorne effect. Several visits let staff get used to the analyst, and logs cross-check the timings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (7)","q":"<p>Managers say a stock count takes 20 minutes, but observation shows it takes an hour. What is the analyst doing by comparing the two results?</p>","options":["Reviewing printed material","Piloting a questionnaire","Building a prototype","Cross-checking results from combined tools"],"correct":3,"why":"Combining tools and comparing their results lets one tool expose the weakness of another.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (8)","q":"<p>In a focus group of eight Sunset cashiers, the head cashier does most of the talking and the others simply agree with her. Which limitation of focus groups is this?</p>","options":["One confident member dominating, so quieter views are lost","A leading question","The Hawthorne effect","A low response rate"],"correct":0,"why":"Focus groups can be dominated by one or two confident members. A skilled facilitator invites each person to speak.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (9)","q":"<p>The requirements specification says: \"The system must print a reorder list when any item falls below its reorder level.\" What kind of requirement is this?</p>","options":["Non-functional (security)","Functional","A test plan","Non-functional (performance)"],"correct":1,"why":"It states what the system does, so it is a functional requirement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Fact-finding fine print (10)","q":"<p>The specification also says: \"Stock look-ups must return a result in under two seconds at every till.\" What kind of requirement is this?</p>","options":["Non-functional (usability)","Non-functional (availability)","Non-functional (performance)","Functional"],"correct":2,"why":"It states how well (how fast) the system must work: a non-functional performance requirement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The analyst at Sunrise Medical Centre (step 1)","q":"<p>You are the systems analyst for the Sunrise Medical Centre in West Bay, which wants a new appointment system. Choose your fact-finding tools well.</p><p>The centre has 3 senior doctors, 12 receptionists and nurses, and about 2,000 registered patients. You start with the doctors, who set clinic policy. What do you do?</p>","options":["Send the doctors a ten-question tick-box questionnaire.","Interview each of the three doctors, with prepared questions and follow-ups."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["With only three experts, a questionnaire wastes the chance to ask follow-up questions about policy.","Good. Three key decision-makers: interviews give depth and let you probe their reasons."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The analyst at Sunrise Medical Centre (step 2)","q":"<p>Next, you want patients' views on waiting times and preferred booking methods. What do you use?</p>","options":["A short anonymous online and paper questionnaire with mostly closed questions.","Interview patients one by one in the waiting room until you have spoken to most of them."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Right. Many patients, the same questions, quantitative answers that can be totalled.","Interviewing a large proportion of 2,000 patients would take months and cost far too much."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The analyst at Sunrise Medical Centre (step 3)","q":"<p>Receptionists say booking is \"quick and easy\", but patients complain about long queues. How do you find out what really happens?</p>","options":["Accept the receptionists' answer; they know the job best.","Observe the front desk over several mornings, timing each booking, and compare with the appointment logs."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["What people say and what they do can differ. You need first-hand evidence.","Excellent. Observation shows real behaviour; several visits reduce the Hawthorne effect, and logs cross-check."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (1)","q":"<p>Analysts across the Caribbean are modelling new systems. Read what each one is drawing and sort it by the type of model.</p><p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For Belmopan Clinic: rectangles PATIENT and DOCTOR joined by a diamond \"is seen by\", with M and 1 at the ends.</b></p>","options":["ER diagram","Process model","Data flow diagram","Object model"],"correct":0,"why":"Entities, a relationship and cardinality: an ER diagram.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (2)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For Breadfruit Valley Secondary: STUDENT with StudentID, Name and DateOfBirth, linked to COURSE in a many-to-many (M:N) relationship.</b></p>","options":["Object model","Data flow diagram","Process model","ER diagram"],"correct":3,"why":"Stored data, attributes and cardinality with no processes: an ER diagram.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (3)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For the Island Pizza in Punta Cana: order details flowing from the CUSTOMER into \"Process order\" and on to the Orders data store.</b></p>","options":["ER diagram","Process model","Data flow diagram","Object model"],"correct":2,"why":"Data moving from an external entity, through a process, into a data store: a DFD.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (4)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For a water utility: meter readings moving from the meter reader, through \"Calculate bill\", into the Accounts data store.</b></p>","options":["Object model","Process model","ER diagram","Data flow diagram"],"correct":3,"why":"The focus is how data moves between entities, processes and stores: a DFD.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (5)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For Belize City Car Rentals: receive request, check availability, confirm booking, take deposit, issue confirmation, drawn in order to spot slow manual steps.</b></p>","options":["Object model","Process model","Data flow diagram","ER diagram"],"correct":1,"why":"A sequence of activities used to find bottlenecks: a process model.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (6)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For Harbourside Hotel: each check-in activity from guest arrival to key issued, drawn in sequence to find a duplicated step.</b></p>","options":["Process model","ER diagram","Object model","Data flow diagram"],"correct":0,"why":"Activities and their order: a process model.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (7)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>A Booking class listing bookingID and pickupDate, then the method calculateCost(), linked to a Vehicle class.</b></p>","options":["ER diagram","Data flow diagram","Process model","Object model"],"correct":3,"why":"Attributes plus methods in a class: an object model.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model is the analyst drawing? (8)","q":"<p>Which type of model is this?</p><p><b>For a Java ticketing app at Island Link Ferries: classes Passenger and Ticket, each with attributes and operations such as issueTicket().</b></p>","options":["Object model","Data flow diagram","Process model","ER diagram"],"correct":0,"why":"Classes with data and behaviour for an object-oriented program: an object model.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the tool to its purpose","q":"<p>Match each SDLC tool to what it is used for.</p>","left":["ER diagram","Data flow diagram","Object model","Process model","Gantt chart","Decision table","Prototype"],"right":["Shows entities, their attributes and the relationships between them","Shows every combination of conditions and the actions for each","Shows the sequence of activities that turn inputs into outputs","Shows project tasks against time, with durations and overlaps","Shows how data moves between entities, processes and data stores","Shows classes with attributes and methods, and how they relate","A working model users can try before the full system is built"],"correct":[0,4,5,2,3,1,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (1)","q":"<p>Use the Island Link Ferries decision tree from the notes (Under 12? then Return? leading to four leaves), and decide which tool suits each Caribbean situation.</p><p>A 9-year-old buys a single (not return) Island Link Ferries ticket to Bequia. Following the tree, which leaf is reached?</p>","options":["Adult fare + 10% discount","Adult fare","Child fare","Child fare + 10% discount"],"correct":2,"why":"Under 12: Y, then Return: N, leads to the Child fare leaf.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (2)","q":"<p>A 40-year-old buys a return ticket. Following the tree, what is charged?</p>","options":["Adult fare","Adult fare + 10% discount","Child fare","Child fare + 10% discount"],"correct":1,"why":"Under 12: N, then Return: Y, leads to Adult fare + 10% discount.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (3)","q":"<p>The board of Northstar Credit Union, none of whom are technical, must approve the loan rules at a meeting. Which tool should the analyst present?</p>","options":["Decision tree","Decision table","ER diagram","Gantt chart"],"correct":0,"why":"A tree is visual and easy for non-technical readers to follow branch by branch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (4)","q":"<p>Before coding, a programmer must prove that all 32 combinations of five loan conditions are handled. Which tool should be used?</p>","options":["Decision tree","Prototype","Decision table","Process model"],"correct":2,"why":"Completeness is easy to check in a table: count the 2 to the power 5 = 32 columns.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (5)","q":"<p>At Wellcare Pharmacy, staff ask questions in a fixed order: prescription first, then insurance, then age. Which tool shows that order?</p>","options":["Questionnaire","Decision table","Data dictionary","Decision tree"],"correct":3,"why":"A tree shows the order of conditions from left to right; a table does not.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (6)","q":"<p>A tree for two yes/no conditions has four leaves. How many rules would the equivalent decision table have?</p>","options":["8","16","4","2"],"correct":2,"why":"Each leaf matches one rule: 2 to the power 2 = 4.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Table or tree? (7)","q":"<p>A customs duty rule at a Kingstown port agent has six conditions. What happens if it is drawn as a tree?</p>","options":["It loses the order of conditions","It stays compact","It becomes very wide and cluttered","It can no longer show actions"],"correct":2,"why":"With many conditions a tree becomes cluttered (64 leaves here); a table stays compact.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the decision table (1)","q":"<p>The Island Hardware uses this delivery policy table. C1 Order over $200? C2 Customer in Freeport or Lucaya? Actions: A1 Free delivery; A2 Charge $15 delivery; A3 Add 5% loyalty discount.<br>R1 (Y,Y): A1, A3 · R2 (Y,N): A1 · R3 (N,Y): A2, A3 · R4 (N,N): A2.</p><p>How many rules does a table with these 2 conditions need?</p>","options":["4","8","6","2"],"correct":0,"why":"2 to the power 2 = 4 rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the decision table (2)","q":"<p>A customer in Freeport orders $250 of goods. Which rule applies?</p>","options":["R2","R4","R1","R3"],"correct":2,"why":"Over $200 (Y) and in Freeport (Y): Rule 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the decision table (3)","q":"<p>A customer in St. George’s orders $120. What happens?</p>","options":["$15 delivery and 5% discount","$15 delivery only","Free delivery and 5% discount","Free delivery only"],"correct":1,"why":"Not over $200 (N), not in Grand Anse/Gouyave (N): Rule 4, $15 delivery only.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the decision table (4)","q":"<p>Which condition alone decides whether delivery is free?</p>","options":["C2 Location","Neither","C1 Order over $200","Both together"],"correct":2,"why":"Free delivery appears in R1 and R2, exactly where C1 is Y, whatever C2 says.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the decision table (5)","q":"<p>If a third condition (Paid online?) were added, how many rules would be needed?</p>","options":["6","16","8","9"],"correct":2,"why":"2 to the power 3 = 8 rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a decision table","q":"<p>Put the steps for building a limited-entry decision table in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Identify and list all the conditions in the condition stub.","Identify and list all the possible actions in the action stub.","Calculate the number of rules using 2 to the power of the number of conditions.","Fill in the Y/N condition entries using a halving pattern so every combination appears once.","For each rule, mark an X against the action(s) that apply.","Simplify by merging rules with identical actions, using a dash for conditions that do not matter.","Check the table against the original policy with the users."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (1)","q":"<p>Designers at the Island Code Studio in Holetown are specifying a booking system for the Harbourside Hotel in Bridgetown. Sort each piece of design work by the tool being used.</p><p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>GuestID: text, 8 characters, unique, must start with \"HH\". The same kind of entry is recorded for every field.</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Structured English","Data dictionary","Decision tree","Decision table"],"correct":2,"why":"Name, type, size and validation for each data item: a data dictionary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (2)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>One shared entry for RoomRate that the form designer and the programmer both check, so the screens and the code agree.</b></p>","options":["Structured English","Decision tree","Pseudocode","Data dictionary","Decision table"],"correct":3,"why":"A central record that keeps the team consistent: a data dictionary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (3)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>FOR EACH booking / IF guest is a loyalty member THEN apply member rate OTHERWISE apply standard rate, written for the hotel manager to approve.</b></p>","options":["Data dictionary","Decision table","Structured English","Pseudocode","Decision tree"],"correct":2,"why":"Plain English with capitalised keywords and no variables, for users to check: structured English.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (4)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>Indented plain-English steps with keywords in capitals and no variables, shown to the front-desk staff so they can confirm the check-out process.</b></p>","options":["Data dictionary","Decision tree","Decision table","Structured English","Pseudocode"],"correct":3,"why":"Readable by users, limited keywords, no programming syntax: structured English.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (5)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>INPUT nights / IF nights > 7 THEN rate ← weeklyRate ELSE rate ← nightlyRate ENDIF / OUTPUT rate</b></p>","options":["Decision tree","Pseudocode","Decision table","Structured English","Data dictionary"],"correct":1,"why":"Variables and assignment arrows written for the programmer: pseudocode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (6)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>total ← 0 / FOR each item on the bill: total ← total + price / OUTPUT total</b></p>","options":["Decision table","Data dictionary","Decision tree","Pseudocode","Structured English"],"correct":3,"why":"A loop with variables and assignment: pseudocode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (7)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>A grid with Y/N entries for \"Member?\" and \"Weekend?\", four rules, and X marks against the rate to charge.</b></p>","options":["Decision tree","Pseudocode","Decision table","Structured English","Data dictionary"],"correct":2,"why":"Conditions, rules and X marks in a grid: a decision table.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which design tool is this? (8)","q":"<p>Which design tool is this?</p><p><b>A branching diagram from a root: \"Member?\" then \"Weekend?\", with the rate at each leaf, shown to the hotel board.</b></p>","options":["Decision tree","Structured English","Pseudocode","Decision table","Data dictionary"],"correct":0,"why":"Conditions at branch points and actions at the leaves: a decision tree.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (1)","q":"<p>The Hilltop Bakery in Paramaribo is developing an online ordering system. Its Gantt chart shows:</p><div><table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Weeks</th></tr><tr><td>A Feasibility study</td><td>1 to 2</td></tr><tr><td>B Analysis</td><td>2 to 5</td></tr><tr><td>C Design</td><td>5 to 7</td></tr><tr><td>D Development</td><td>7 to 12</td></tr><tr><td>E Testing</td><td>11 to 13</td></tr><tr><td>F Implementation</td><td>14 to 15</td></tr></table></div><p>How long does Analysis (weeks 2 to 5) last?</p>","options":["4 weeks","2 weeks","5 weeks","3 weeks"],"correct":0,"why":"5 − 2 + 1 = 4 weeks: weeks 2, 3, 4 and 5.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (2)","q":"<p>How long does Development (weeks 7 to 12) last?</p>","options":["7 weeks","12 weeks","6 weeks","5 weeks"],"correct":2,"why":"12 − 7 + 1 = 6 weeks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (3)","q":"<p>Which two tasks overlap in week 5?</p>","options":["A and B","B and C","C and D","No tasks overlap in week 5"],"correct":1,"why":"Analysis ends in week 5 and Design starts in week 5, so both run that week.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (4)","q":"<p>In which weeks do Development and Testing overlap?</p>","options":["Weeks 11 and 12","Week 11 only","They do not overlap","Weeks 12 and 13"],"correct":0,"why":"Testing starts in week 11 and Development ends in week 12: both bars appear in weeks 11 and 12.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (5)","q":"<p>Testing ends in week 13 and Implementation starts in week 14. How are these two tasks scheduled?</p>","options":["Sequential (one after the other)","Concurrent (overlapping)","Duplicated","Unscheduled"],"correct":0,"why":"There is no shared week, so Implementation follows Testing: sequential tasks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (6)","q":"<p>What is the total length of the project?</p>","options":["12 weeks","15 weeks","13 weeks","20 weeks"],"correct":1,"why":"Week 1 to week 15 = 15 weeks. The durations add to 20 weeks, but overlaps shorten the project.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (7)","q":"<p>Development falls two weeks behind during Carnival. What should the project manager do with the chart?</p>","options":["Replace it with a decision table","Delete the Testing bar","Update the chart and reschedule the later tasks","Nothing: a Gantt chart is fixed at the feasibility stage"],"correct":2,"why":"A Gantt chart is a monitoring tool that must be updated whenever the schedule changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (8)","q":"<p>Every Monday the bakery owner, the programmer and the web designer check the same chart and can all see when each task starts and ends. Which strength of a Gantt chart does this show?</p>","options":["It generates the program code","It lists every combination of conditions","It removes the need for testing","It keeps everyone on the same page about the schedule"],"correct":3,"why":"Showing each activity's start and end to the whole team keeps everyone on the same page.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the Hilltop Gantt chart (9)","q":"<p>Caribbean Code Works in Barbados later plans a hospital system with 250 tasks. What weakness of a Gantt chart is it likely to meet?</p>","options":["The chart grows complex and cluttered on a large project","The chart can show only one task at a time","The chart can only be drawn by hand","The chart cannot show start and end weeks"],"correct":0,"why":"A Gantt chart grows complex on large projects; hundreds of bars become hard to read.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reading the Harbourside Gantt chart","q":"<p>The project manager for the Harbourside Hotel in St. George's is checking the Gantt chart for its new online booking system. Development runs from week 6 to week 10 and Testing runs from week 9 to week 11. Which statement is correct?</p>","options":["Development lasts 5 weeks and does not overlap Testing.","Testing lasts 2 weeks and starts after Development ends.","Development lasts 5 weeks and overlaps Testing for 2 weeks.","Development lasts 4 weeks and overlaps Testing for 1 week."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["At Harbourside, Testing begins in week 9, before Development ends in week 10, so they overlap.","Testing runs weeks 9 to 11 (3 weeks) and starts before Development finishes.","Correct. 10 − 6 + 1 = 5 weeks; both Harbourside tasks run in weeks 9 and 10.","This subtracts without adding 1. Count weeks 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10: five weeks. Weeks 9 and 10 are both shared."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (1)","q":"<p>The Island Code Studio in Grand Anse is building a guest-booking system for a Gouyave guest house. Sort each tool or feature it uses into upper CASE, lower CASE or integrated CASE.</p><p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The analysts draw the DFDs and the tool flags a process that has no output.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Integrated CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":2,"why":"Drawing and checking DFDs supports analysis: upper CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (2)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The data dictionary records that GuestID is 8 characters on every screen.</b></p>","options":["Integrated CASE","Lower CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":2,"why":"A data dictionary supports analysis and design: upper CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (3)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The designers lay out the check-in screen and the nightly occupancy report in the package.</b></p>","options":["Integrated CASE","Upper CASE","Lower CASE"],"correct":1,"why":"Screen and report design belong to the design stage: upper CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (4)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The ER diagram is drawn and checked for missing relationships before the database is designed.</b></p>","options":["Integrated CASE","Upper CASE","Lower CASE"],"correct":1,"why":"ER diagramming supports analysis and design: upper CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (5)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The tool generates the SQL table definitions and program code from the approved ERD.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Upper CASE","Integrated CASE"],"correct":0,"why":"A code generator supports development: lower CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (6)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>A programmer steps through the code in a debugger to find why the invoice total is wrong.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Integrated CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":0,"why":"Debugging belongs to development and testing: lower CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (7)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>An automated testing tool runs 200 test bookings overnight and reports failures.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Integrated CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":0,"why":"Testing tools are lower CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (8)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>After go-live, a documentation generator updates the technical manual whenever the code is changed.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Upper CASE","Integrated CASE"],"correct":0,"why":"Supporting maintenance with generated documentation is lower CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (9)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>The analysts model the system in Visible Analyst, like an architect drawing plans before building starts.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Upper CASE","Integrated CASE"],"correct":1,"why":"Visible Analyst is an example upper-CASE tool for the early phases.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (10)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>Every change to the booking code is saved to Git so the team can roll back a bad change.</b></p>","options":["Integrated CASE","Upper CASE","Lower CASE"],"correct":2,"why":"Git supports development and maintenance: lower CASE, like the site crew building the walls.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (11)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>One package links the planning schedule, the diagrams, the code and the test results, from planning through maintenance.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Upper CASE","Integrated CASE"],"correct":2,"why":"Covering the whole life cycle with linked tools is integrated CASE, like one project manager overseeing every stage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (12)","q":"<p>Upper, lower or integrated CASE?</p><p><b>Renaming a field in the ERD automatically updates the data dictionary, the generated code and the manual.</b></p>","options":["Lower CASE","Integrated CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":1,"why":"A change flowing through every phase's tools is the mark of integrated CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which kind of CASE tool?","q":"<p>At the Island Code Studio in St. George's, analysts finish the ER diagram and data dictionary for a Grand Anse hotel's guest-booking system. The team's software then automatically produces the database tables and program code from them. This feature is BEST described as</p>","options":["a lower-CASE code generator","a process model","a prototype","an upper-CASE diagramming tool"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Generating program code supports the development stage, which is lower CASE.","A process model is a diagram of activities, not software that writes code.","A prototype is a working model for users to try, not a tool that converts designs to code.","Upper CASE covers drawing the ERD and data dictionary, but producing code is a later-stage feature."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the CASE tool family","q":"<p>The Island Code Studio in Frigate Bay is choosing CASE tools for a ticket-booking system for the Sandy Point Water Taxi. Match each CASE category or type to the tool, use case or analogy that belongs to it.</p>","left":["Diagramming and modelling","UI prototyping","Code and version control","Testing","Project management","Upper CASE","Lower CASE","Integrated CASE"],"right":["Like one project manager overseeing every stage: links tools across the whole life cycle","Visible Analyst or Lucidchart: draw the DFD of how booking data moves and is stored","Figma or Balsamiq: clickable booking screens for passengers to try before coding","Visual Studio and Git: review, merge and roll back every change to the booking code","Selenium or JUnit: rerun automated regression tests after each build","Microsoft Project or Jira: a Gantt schedule of tasks, milestones and deadlines","Like an architect's drawing tools: supports planning, analysis and design","Like a site crew that builds and checks the walls: supports implementation, testing and maintenance"],"correct":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (1)","q":"<p><b>Scenario:</b> the Island Code Studio in Holetown is building a ticket-booking system for the Oistins Water Taxi. The team needs to <b>draw data flow diagrams</b>, <b>prototype screens</b> and <b>track code changes</b>. For each task, choose the CASE category, then the SDLC phase it supports.</p><p>Task 1: the analysts must draw DFDs showing how booking data moves from passengers into the Bookings data store. Which CASE category do they need?</p>","options":["Testing","Code and version control","Diagramming and modelling","UI prototyping"],"correct":2,"why":"Drawing DFDs and ERDs is diagramming and modelling (Visible Analyst, Visio, Lucidchart, Enterprise Architect).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (2)","q":"<p>Task 1 again: in which SDLC phase are these DFDs mainly drawn?</p>","options":["Analysis","Testing","Implementation","Evaluation and maintenance"],"correct":0,"why":"DFDs document how data moves and is stored, which is analysis work.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (3)","q":"<p>Task 2: the designers must build clickable ticket-booking screens that captains and passengers can try before any code is written. Which CASE category?</p>","options":["Project management","Diagramming and modelling","Testing","UI prototyping"],"correct":3,"why":"Clickable prototype screens come from UI prototyping tools such as Figma or Balsamiq.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (4)","q":"<p>Task 2 again: which SDLC phase does the prototype mainly support?</p>","options":["Problem definition","Testing","Design","Development"],"correct":2,"why":"Users check layouts in the design phase, before development starts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (5)","q":"<p>Task 3: three programmers edit the same booking code and must be able to review, merge and roll back each other's changes. Which CASE category?</p>","options":["Diagramming and modelling","Code and version control","UI prototyping","Project management"],"correct":1,"why":"Tracking every code change so it can be reviewed, merged and rolled back is code and version control (Visual Studio, Git).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (6)","q":"<p>Task 3 again: in which SDLC phase is the code tracked?</p>","options":["Development","Feasibility study","Analysis","Design"],"correct":0,"why":"Code is written and changed during development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (7)","q":"<p>Which example tools suit tasks 1, 2 and 3, in that order?</p>","options":["Git, Visible Analyst, Figma","Jira, Selenium, Balsamiq","Figma, JUnit, Microsoft Project","Visible Analyst, Figma, Git"],"correct":3,"why":"Visible Analyst draws the DFDs, Figma builds the prototype screens and Git tracks the code changes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (8)","q":"<p>The tools for tasks 1 and 2 (diagramming and UI prototyping) belong mainly to which type of CASE?</p>","options":["Neither type","Lower CASE","Integrated CASE","Upper CASE"],"correct":3,"why":"Diagramming and prototyping support the early phases (analysis and design): upper CASE.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (9)","q":"<p>Later, the team wants regression tests to rerun automatically after every build. Which category and phase?</p>","options":["Version control such as Git, in analysis","Project management such as Jira, in the planning phase","UI prototyping such as Balsamiq, in design","Testing tools such as Selenium, in the testing phase"],"correct":3,"why":"Automated regression tests after each build belong to testing tools in the testing phase.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right CASE tool (10)","q":"<p>The diagramming tool flags a DFD process with no output weeks before any code is written. Which benefit of CASE tools does this show?</p>","options":["Code no longer needs testing","Licences become cheaper","Users no longer need training","Flaws are found early"],"correct":3,"why":"Automatic checking finds flaws early, when they are cheap to fix. CASE tools also make work faster, cut errors and keep documentation consistent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping the student portal (step 1)","q":"<p>You are an analyst at Seaview Systems, building a new student portal for Sandbox Tree College. Use prototyping well.</p><p>Students cannot say clearly what they want on the timetable and grades pages. \"We'll know it when we see it,\" one tells you. What do you do?</p>","options":["Build a clickable prototype of the two pages and let a group of students try it.","Code the full portal from your own best guess, then show students the finished product."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. Users who are unsure of their requirements react quickly to something they can see and try.","Without early feedback, errors in requirements are found only after coding, when they are expensive to fix."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping the student portal (step 2)","q":"<p>Students like the prototype and ask for a mobile-friendly layout and a hurricane-closure notice banner. The prototype was built quickly in a design tool, with no security or database behind it. How should you treat it?</p>","options":["As an evolutionary prototype: put the design-tool mock-up live as the final portal straight away.","As a throwaway prototype: use it to fix the requirements, then build the real portal properly with security."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Evolutionary prototypes are refined into the final system, but this quick mock-up skipped security and performance, a known limitation of fast prototypes.","Right. A quick mock-up with no security is a throwaway prototype; the real system is built properly afterwards."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Prototyping the student portal (step 3)","q":"<p>The registrar sees the prototype at a demonstration and announces that the portal will launch next week. What do you do?</p>","options":["Explain that it is only a model: the real portal still has to be built and tested. Agree a realistic schedule.","Say nothing and accept the launch date to keep the registrar happy."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. Managing expectations deals with the classic prototype limitation: users mistaking it for the finished system.","Users may think a prototype is the finished system. Staying silent creates false expectations."]},{"t":"match","title":"Flowchart symbols in action","q":"<p>Airport Road Parking, near Lynden Pindling International Airport, needs a program to calculate parking fees. Match each design or development tool to the matching part of the fee program.</p>","left":["Terminator (rounded rectangle)","Input/output (parallelogram)","Process (rectangle)","Decision (diamond)","Pseudocode","Programming language","Platform (a language-choice factor)"],"right":["Python source code typed into an IDE to build the fee program","Choose JavaScript because the fee calculator must run in customers' web browsers","fee ← hours × 5","IF hours > 24 THEN fee ← 100 ELSE fee ← hours × 5 ENDIF","Is hours greater than 24?","Read the number of hours parked from the ticket","Start and Stop of the parking-fee algorithm"],"correct":[6,5,2,4,3,0,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (1)","q":"<p>The Island Code Studio in Montego Bay is developing and testing a ticket-booking system for the Ocho Rios Water Taxi. Choose the right development or testing tool for each situation.</p><p>The finished booking program must run fast on the Kingston kiosk, and the source code must not be handed over. Which translator should the team use?</p>","options":["Interpreter","Debugger","Compiler","Data dictionary"],"correct":2,"why":"A compiler produces a fast executable file in one go, so the source code need not be shipped.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (2)","q":"<p>A trainee programmer wants the program to stop at the first error so she can fix lines one at a time. Which translator suits her?</p>","options":["Version control","Compiler","Interpreter","Code generator"],"correct":2,"why":"An interpreter runs one statement at a time and stops at the first error, which helps while testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (3)","q":"<p>The team must create the bookings tables and list all of Saturday's bookings. Which language is designed for this?</p>","options":["C++","SQL","Structured English","Java"],"correct":1,"why":"SQL is the database language for creating tables and querying data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (4)","q":"<p>The passenger app must run on many models of Android phone. Which of the named languages suits it best?</p>","options":["Java","C","SQL","Structured English"],"correct":0,"why":"Java is object-oriented and runs on many platforms; it is widely used for Android apps.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (5)","q":"<p>A tester reads the fare code and designs tests so that both branches of IF age < 12 are run. Which kind of testing is this?</p>","options":["Black box","Regression","White box","Acceptance"],"correct":2,"why":"Seeing the code and testing every path is white box testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (6)","q":"<p>Without seeing any code, a tester books a return ticket for a 30-year-old and checks the fare against the specification. Which kind of testing?</p>","options":["White box","Unit","Black box","Integration"],"correct":2,"why":"Inputs and outputs checked against the specification, with the code hidden: black box testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (7)","q":"<p>The fare routine is tested on its own before it is joined to the payment module. Which kind of testing?</p>","options":["Integration","Acceptance","Regression","Unit"],"correct":3,"why":"Testing one module on its own is unit testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (8)","q":"<p>The booking module and the payment module are now tested together to check the fare passes correctly. Which kind of testing?</p>","options":["Integration","Unit","Regression","White box"],"correct":0,"why":"Checking that modules work together is integration testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (9)","q":"<p>Captains and office staff try the system with a week of real bookings before they sign it off. Which kind of testing?</p>","options":["Unit","White box","Acceptance","Regression"],"correct":2,"why":"Users testing with real data before sign-off is acceptance testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (10)","q":"<p>After a payment bug is fixed, Selenium re-runs every earlier booking test. Which kind of testing?</p>","options":["Unit","Acceptance","Integration","Regression"],"correct":3,"why":"Re-running earlier tests after a change is regression testing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build it and prove it (11)","q":"<p>Every screen matches the design specification, but pier staff say the system does not fit how they actually sell tickets. Which question has the project failed?</p>","options":["Validation: are we building the right product?","Integration: do the modules work together?","Translation: is the code compiled?","Verification: are we building the product right?"],"correct":0,"why":"The system meets the specification (verification passed) but not users' real needs, so validation failed.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the design-stage tools","q":"<p>The Island Code Studio is planning a new stock system for a Young Street jewellery shop. Its project log lists these activities.</p><p>Select EVERY activity that uses a tool mainly associated with the DESIGN stage.</p>","options":["9. Recording the type, size and validation rule of every field in a data dictionary.","8. Running a focus group with six sales staff about the new till.","10. Writing the reorder rule in structured English for the owner to check.","5. Building a clickable prototype of the stock-entry screens.","1. Writing pseudocode for the reorder-level routine.","3. Drawing a flowchart of the stock-checking algorithm.","2. Handing out a questionnaire to 60 sales staff.","4. Sitting in the shop for a day observing how sales are recorded.","6. Interviewing the owner about her budget.","7. Producing a decision table for the discount rules to guide the programmers."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,5,9],"why":"Correct. Pseudocode, flowcharts, interface prototypes, decision tables, the data dictionary and structured English all specify the new system: design tools. The questionnaire, observation, interview and focus group are fact-finding tools used in analysis."},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each tool by the SDLC stage in which it is MAINLY used.</p><p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Interviews with department managers</b></p>","options":["Design","Analysis","Development"],"correct":1,"why":"Fact-finding about the current system and requirements: analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (2)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Review of existing invoices and reports</b></p>","options":["Development","Analysis","Design"],"correct":1,"why":"Investigating printed material is fact-finding: analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (3)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Observation of staff at work</b></p>","options":["Design","Development","Analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"Observation gathers facts about the current system: analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (4)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Pseudocode for each module</b></p>","options":["Development","Design","Analysis"],"correct":1,"why":"Pseudocode specifies program logic: design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (5)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Flowchart of the login algorithm</b></p>","options":["Development","Design","Analysis"],"correct":1,"why":"A flowchart represents the logic to be programmed: design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (6)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Writing source code in Python</b></p>","options":["Analysis","Design","Development"],"correct":2,"why":"Using a programming language builds the system: development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (7)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Lower-CASE code generator</b></p>","options":["Development","Design","Analysis"],"correct":0,"why":"Generating code supports development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (8)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Debugger in an IDE</b></p>","options":["Analysis","Design","Development"],"correct":2,"why":"Finding and fixing errors in code: development.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (9)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Focus group with eight users from different departments</b></p>","options":["Design","Analysis","Development"],"correct":1,"why":"A guided group discussion gathers requirements: analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (10)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Structured English for the discount rule</b></p>","options":["Design","Analysis","Development"],"correct":0,"why":"Plain-English logic that specifies the solution: design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (11)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Data dictionary entry for every field</b></p>","options":["Design","Development","Analysis"],"correct":0,"why":"Defining each data item for the new system: design.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which SDLC stage? (12)","q":"<p>Which stage is this tool mainly used in?</p><p><b>Compiler translating the finished source code</b></p>","options":["Analysis","Development","Design"],"correct":1,"why":"Translating source code into machine code: development.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the limitation","q":"<p>Match each tool to the limitation that belongs to it.</p>","left":["Observation","Questionnaire","Interview","Review of printed material","Decision table","CASE tools","Prototype"],"right":["Documents may be out of date and not match current practice","Users may mistake it for the finished system","Time-consuming and costly, so only a few people can be included","Grows rapidly as 2 to the power n when conditions are added","Low response rate and no chance to ask follow-up questions","People may change their behaviour because they know they are being watched","Expensive licences and staff training"],"correct":[5,4,2,0,3,6,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Counting rules","q":"<p>An analyst for the Northstar Credit Union records the loan policy in a limited-entry decision table. The conditions are: Is the applicant employed? Is the loan under $5,000? Does the applicant have a savings account? Has the applicant missed a payment before? How many rules will the complete table contain?</p>","options":["8","32","4","16"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Eight is 2 to the power 3; there are four conditions here.","Thirty-two would need five yes/no conditions.","Four is the number of conditions, not the number of rules.","Correct. 2 to the power 4 = 16 rules."]}]},{"id":"assess_71v","title":"Module 3 · Unit 6 quiz: Data flow diagrams","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 6: Data flow diagrams. 56 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (1)","q":"<p>The Breadfruit Valley Secondary canteen is moving to phone pre-orders, and an analyst is drawing a DFD. For each situation, decide what the DFD can and cannot do for her.</p><p>The canteen supervisor wants to know whether the kitchen actually receives the list of orders. Would a DFD show this?</p>","options":["Yes: it shows data flowing to Kitchen Staff","No: a DFD only shows hardware"],"correct":0,"why":"A DFD shows where data goes, so a \"preparation list\" flow to Kitchen Staff answers her question.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (2)","q":"<p>The principal asks what time the order cut-off is each morning. Would the DFD show this?</p>","options":["Yes: DFDs show timing","No: a DFD does not show timing or the order of steps"],"correct":1,"why":"Timing and sequence are not shown on a DFD; it shows only what data moves and where.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (3)","q":"<p>The IT technician wants to know whether students order from Android phones or iPhones. Would the DFD show this?</p>","options":["No: a DFD does not show hardware, programs or devices","Yes: devices are drawn as entities"],"correct":0,"why":"DFDs show data, not hardware. The student is the entity, whatever phone she uses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (4)","q":"<p>The analyst draws the current paper system and asks the supervisor, \"Is this how orders really move?\" Why is she drawing this DFD?</p>","options":["To test the finished program","To choose the server hardware","To understand the current system and check it with users"],"correct":2,"why":"Understanding the current system and communicating with users are two key purposes of a DFD.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (5)","q":"<p>On the draft, order records are written to the Orders store but no process ever reads them. What is the DFD helping the analyst to do?</p>","options":["Show the canteen timetable","Show the decisions a student makes","Find problems in the system"],"correct":2,"why":"Data that goes nowhere shows up clearly on a DFD; finding problems is one reason analysts draw them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (6)","q":"<p>A student either pays by card or uses lunch credit. Would the DFD show this yes/no choice as a branch?</p>","options":["Yes, with a decision diamond","No: a DFD does not show decisions or loops"],"correct":1,"why":"Decisions belong on a flowchart. The DFD shows only the data that flows, such as \"payment details\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"What can a DFD tell you? (7)","q":"<p>For the new system, the analyst wants to plan which files the canteen will need and which reports the bursar will receive. Can a DFD of the proposed system help?</p>","options":["No: DFDs only describe old systems","Yes: it shows the data stores and outputs needed"],"correct":1,"why":"Planning the new system is a key purpose: the DFD shows what data must be captured, stored and output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which symbol?","q":"<p>In a Gane and Sarson DFD for Island Wheels Car Rentals, the analyst draws an open-ended rectangle labelled \"D2 | Rental agreements\". What does this symbol represent?</p>","options":["A process that produces rental agreements","A data store holding rental agreement records for later use","A data flow carrying rental agreements to the customer","An external entity that receives rental agreements"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A process is a rounded rectangle with a number such as 2.0 and a verb-phrase name, for example \"Produce agreement\".","Correct. The open-ended rectangle with a D-number is a data store: the car rental firm's agreements are kept at rest for later use.","A data flow is a labelled arrow. It shows data moving, not data being kept.","An external entity is a square representing a person or organisation outside the system, such as Customer."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (1)","q":"<p>An analyst is modelling the Wellcare Pharmacy prescription system in Georgetown. Decide how each item would appear on the DFD.</p><p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Doctor: sends a prescription to the pharmacy system</b></p>","options":["Not drawn as an external entity","Source and sink","Source only","Sink only"],"correct":2,"why":"The doctor only sends data in, so this external entity is a source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (2)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Supplier: receives purchase orders from the system</b></p>","options":["Sink only","Not drawn as an external entity","Source and sink","Source only"],"correct":0,"why":"The supplier only receives data out, so it is a sink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (3)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Pharmacy Owner: receives a monthly sales report</b></p>","options":["Source and sink","Not drawn as an external entity","Sink only","Source only"],"correct":2,"why":"The owner only receives a report: a sink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (4)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Patient: hands in a prescription and receives a medicine label and receipt</b></p>","options":["Sink only","Source and sink","Not drawn as an external entity","Source only"],"correct":1,"why":"The patient sends data in and receives data out: both a source and a sink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (5)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Health Insurer: receives claims and sends back claim approvals</b></p>","options":["Sink only","Source only","Not drawn as an external entity","Source and sink"],"correct":3,"why":"Data flows both ways, so the insurer is a source and a sink.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (6)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>\"Record prescription\": the work the system does with each prescription</b></p>","options":["Source only","Not drawn as an external entity","Sink only","Source and sink"],"correct":1,"why":"This is an action inside the system: a process, not an entity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (7)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>The Prescriptions file on the pharmacy computer</b></p>","options":["Not drawn as an external entity","Sink only","Source and sink","Source only"],"correct":0,"why":"Data held inside the system is a data store, not an external entity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Source, sink or both? (8)","q":"<p>How should this be drawn?</p><p><b>Mrs Richardson, a regular customer</b></p>","options":["Source and sink","Source only","Not drawn as an external entity","Sink only"],"correct":2,"why":"Entities are named by role, not by a person's name. Draw the entity \"Patient\" instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (1)","q":"<p>An analyst has listed the parts of the Breadfruit Valley Secondary canteen pre-order system. Sort each one into the correct Gane and Sarson symbol.</p><p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Student</b></p>","options":["Data flow","External entity","Process","Data store"],"correct":1,"why":"A student is a person outside the system who sends orders in: an external entity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (2)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Kitchen Staff</b></p>","options":["Data store","External entity","Data flow","Process"],"correct":1,"why":"Kitchen staff receive the preparation list: an external entity (a sink).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (3)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Record order</b></p>","options":["Data store","Data flow","Process","External entity"],"correct":2,"why":"Verb + noun, and it transforms order details into a stored record: a process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (4)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Produce preparation list</b></p>","options":["External entity","Data store","Data flow","Process"],"correct":3,"why":"An action that turns stored orders into a list: a process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (5)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Orders</b></p>","options":["Data store","External entity","Process","Data flow"],"correct":0,"why":"A plural noun for data held for later: a data store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (6)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>Menu</b></p>","options":["Data store","Data flow","External entity","Process"],"correct":0,"why":"The menu items and prices are kept for later use: a data store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (7)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>order details</b></p>","options":["Data store","Data flow","External entity","Process"],"correct":1,"why":"Data moving from the student into the system: a data flow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the canteen components (8)","q":"<p>Which DFD symbol should represent this?</p><p><b>sales report</b></p>","options":["External entity","Data store","Process","Data flow"],"correct":3,"why":"Data moving out of the system to the bursar: a data flow.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the symbol to its description","q":"<p>Match each Gane and Sarson DFD term to its correct description.</p>","left":["External entity","Process","Data store","Data flow","Source","Sink","D1","2.0"],"right":["An identifier given to a process on a level 1 DFD","A square for a person or organisation outside the system that sends or receives data","A rounded rectangle for an activity that transforms input data into output data","An identifier given to a data store","An external entity that receives data from the system","An open-ended rectangle for data held for later use","A labelled arrow showing data moving in one direction","An external entity that supplies data to the system"],"correct":[1,2,5,6,7,4,3,0]},{"t":"match","title":"Trace the shuttle context flows","q":"<p>Sunset Shuttle in English Harbour takes online bookings for rides from V.C. Bird International Airport (V.C. Bird Airport) to hotels. Passengers send a ride request and receive a booking confirmation. The system sends a payment request to the card payment provider, which returns an authorisation code. The dispatcher enters the driver roster, each driver receives a pickup list, and the owner receives a weekly earnings report. Match each data flow on the context diagram to its direction.</p>","left":["ride request","booking confirmation","payment request","authorisation code","driver roster","pickup list","earnings report"],"right":["From Passenger to process 0","From Card Payment Provider to process 0","From process 0 to Driver","From process 0 to Passenger","From Dispatcher to process 0","From process 0 to Card Payment Provider","From process 0 to Owner"],"correct":[0,3,5,1,4,2,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Steps to build a context diagram","q":"<p>Put the steps for drawing a context level DFD from a scenario into a sensible order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Read the scenario and agree on the system boundary.","Identify the external entities that send data to or receive data from the system.","Draw one process in the centre, numbered 0 and named after the whole system.","Draw each external entity around the process.","Draw a data flow arrow for each item of data exchanged, in the correct direction.","Label every flow with a noun phrase, then check there are no stores and no entity-to-entity flows."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"What belongs on a context diagram?","q":"<p>A student draws a context level DFD for the Wellcare Pharmacy prescription system. Which feature shows that the diagram is INCORRECT for this level?</p>","options":["An external entity \"Health Insurer\" receiving a \"claim\" data flow","A single process numbered 0 labelled \"Prescription System\"","An external entity \"Doctor\" sending a \"prescription\" data flow to the process","A data store labelled \"D1 | Prescriptions\" connected to the central process"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This is correct: a sink receiving a labelled noun flow from the system.","This is exactly what a context diagram should have: one process for the whole pharmacy system.","This is correct: a source sending a labelled noun flow into the system.","Correct. The pharmacy's Prescriptions store is inside the system, so it is hidden at context level and first appears at level 1."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (1)","q":"<p>You are reviewing a trainee's level 1 DFD for the Blue Water Marine Services payroll in Kralendijk. Answer each review question.</p><p>Process 3.0 \"Calculate pay\" reads \"hours worked\" from D1 Timesheets but receives nothing from D2 Employees. What should you ask?</p>","options":["Where is the decision diamond?","Why is the process not numbered 0?","Where does the pay rate come from?"],"correct":2,"why":"Pay cannot be calculated from hours alone. Add \"pay rate\" read from D2 Employees.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (2)","q":"<p>The context diagram has four external flows. The draft shows timesheet, new employee details and payslip, but not payment details to the Bank. Is the level 1 balanced?</p>","options":["Yes: three flows is enough","No: \"payment details\" to Bank is missing"],"correct":1,"why":"Every external flow on the context diagram must appear on level 1, attached to the same entity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (3)","q":"<p>The Supervisor's timesheet goes to process 1.0, which writes \"hours worked\" to D1 Timesheets. Is this legal?</p>","options":["Yes: a process sits between the entity and the store","No: timesheets cannot be stored"],"correct":0,"why":"Correct. The entity never touches the store directly; process 1.0 handles the data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (4)","q":"<p>D2 Employees is written to by process 2.0 but no process ever reads from it. What does this suggest?</p>","options":["Nothing: stores are only ever written to","Something is missing: a store should be both written to and read from"],"correct":1,"why":"A store that is never read is a warning sign. Here, 3.0 should read \"pay rate\" from D2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (5)","q":"<p>Which is the best name for the process that turns new employee details from the HR Officer into a stored record?</p>","options":["employee details","Update employee details","Employee file"],"correct":1,"why":"A process takes a verb phrase. \"Employee file\" sounds like a store and \"employee details\" like a flow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Check the payroll level 1 (6)","q":"<p>Which data store should hold each crew member's pay rate?</p>","options":["D2 Employees","D1 Timesheets","Bank"],"correct":0,"why":"Pay rates are standing employee data, so they belong in D2 Employees. The Bank is an external entity, not a store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (1)","q":"<p>Read each description of a DFD and decide which level it describes.</p><p>One process numbered 0 labelled \"Ferry Booking System\" surrounded by four entities.</p>","options":["Context level","Level 1"],"correct":0,"why":"A single process for the whole system is the context level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (2)","q":"<p>Processes 1.0 \"Check availability\", 2.0 \"Take payment\" and 3.0 \"Issue e-ticket\" with a store D1 Sailings.</p>","options":["Context level","Level 1"],"correct":1,"why":"Several numbered processes and a data store: level 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (3)","q":"<p>Shows the system boundary and nothing inside it.</p>","options":["Level 1","Context level"],"correct":1,"why":"The context diagram treats the system as one box: it defines the boundary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (4)","q":"<p>Shows data being written to and read from data stores.</p>","options":["Level 1","Context level"],"correct":0,"why":"Stores first appear at level 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (5)","q":"<p>Must contain the same external flows as the diagram above it.</p>","options":["Level 1","Context level"],"correct":0,"why":"Level 1 must balance with the context diagram.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Context level or level 1? (6)","q":"<p>Used early to agree the scope of the project with managers.</p>","options":["Context level","Level 1"],"correct":0,"why":"The overview of the context diagram suits scope discussions.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the broken rules","q":"<p>A Grade 12 group at Frangipani Hill Academy drew a level 1 DFD for the Harbourside Community Library loan system. Their teacher read out the description below.</p><p>Select EVERY line that describes a DFD error.</p>","options":["6. A data flow from process 1.0 to Member has no label.","4. Process 3.0 \"Send reminder\" reads overdue loans from D1 and sends a reminder notice to Member.","2. An arrow goes directly from Member to D2 Members, labelled \"membership form\".","1. Process 1.0 \"Issue loan\" receives a loan request from Member and writes a loan record to D1 Loans.","7. A data flow from Librarian to process 4.0 is labelled \"update catalogue\".","3. Process 2.0 is labelled \"Fines\".","5. An arrow from D1 Loans to D3 Books is labelled \"book status\"."],"correct":[0,2,4,5,6],"why":"Correct. Line 2 links an entity directly to a store; line 3 names a process with a noun; line 5 links two stores; line 6 omits a label; line 7 labels a flow with a verb. Lines 1 and 4 are correct."},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (1)","q":"<p>Each card describes part of a faulty DFD for the Saba Dive Centre booking system. Sort it by the type of error.</p><p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>Process 2.0 \"Check equipment\" receives a gear request but sends nothing out.</b></p>","options":["Illegal direct connection","Labelling error","Black hole or miracle"],"correct":2,"why":"Inputs only: a black hole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (2)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>Process 4.0 \"Print certificate\" sends a certificate to Diver but has no input.</b></p>","options":["Labelling error","Black hole or miracle","Illegal direct connection"],"correct":1,"why":"Outputs only: a miracle.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (3)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>Diver sends \"booking form\" straight into D1 Bookings.</b></p>","options":["Black hole or miracle","Labelling error","Illegal direct connection"],"correct":2,"why":"Entity to store with no process between them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (4)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>D1 Bookings sends \"dive list\" straight to D2 Boats.</b></p>","options":["Labelling error","Black hole or miracle","Illegal direct connection"],"correct":2,"why":"Store to store is illegal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (5)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>Diver sends \"medical form\" directly to Instructor.</b></p>","options":["Illegal direct connection","Labelling error","Black hole or miracle"],"correct":0,"why":"Entity to entity is outside the system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (6)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>A flow from Diver to process 1.0 is labelled \"book dive\".</b></p>","options":["Illegal direct connection","Labelling error","Black hole or miracle"],"correct":1,"why":"A verb phrase on a flow; rename it \"booking request\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (7)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>Process 3.0 is labelled \"Payments\".</b></p>","options":["Illegal direct connection","Black hole or miracle","Labelling error"],"correct":2,"why":"A noun on a process; rename it \"Record payment\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the error (8)","q":"<p>Which type of error is this?</p><p><b>An arrow from process 3.0 to Diver has no label.</b></p>","options":["Labelling error","Illegal direct connection","Black hole or miracle"],"correct":0,"why":"Label omitted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Black hole or miracle?","q":"<p>On a level 1 DFD for the Anguilla Water Taxi company, process 3.0 \"Calculate fare\" receives \"trip details\" from process 2.0 and \"fare rates\" from D2 Fares. No arrow leaves process 3.0. Which error is this?</p>","options":["An illegal connection, because a process cannot read from a data store","A miracle, because the process produces a fare from nothing","A labelling error, because \"Calculate fare\" is a noun","A black hole, because the process has inputs but no output"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Processes may read from stores; that is exactly how stores are meant to be used.","A miracle has outputs but no inputs. This process has two inputs and no outputs, the opposite pattern.","\"Calculate fare\" is a verb phrase, which is the correct way to name a process.","Correct. Data enters \"Calculate fare\" and disappears. The calculated fare must go somewhere, such as to the customer or to a store."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Which sentences earn marks?","q":"<p>A Lower Six student at Frangipani Hill Academy wrote a description of the context level DFD for the Hummingbird Ridge High School canteen pre-order system. Act as the marker.</p><p>Select EVERY sentence that would earn a mark.</p>","options":["7. The external entity Bursar receives the \"sales report\" from process 0.","6. Menu details go between the Canteen Supervisor and the system.","4. Kitchen Staff is a sink that receives the \"preparation list\" from process 0.","1. The context level DFD has a single process, 0 \"Canteen Pre-order System\".","2. The student orders food from the canteen.","5. Data store D1 Orders holds every order that is placed.","3. The external entity Student sends the data flow \"order details\" to process 0."],"correct":[0,2,3,6],"why":"Correct. Lines 1, 3, 4 and 7 name the symbols and give labelled flows with direction. Line 2 describes an event, not a data flow; line 5 adds a store at context level; line 6 gives no direction."},{"t":"mc","title":"Reviewing a ferry DFD (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a junior analyst working with Blue Channel Ferries in Grand Anse. Your senior analyst asks you to review a draft level 1 DFD before it goes to the manager.</p><p>The draft shows the Passenger sending \"booking request\" in an arrow that goes straight into D1 Bookings. What do you recommend?</p>","options":["Insert process 1.0 \"Record booking\" between Passenger and D1, so the request is checked and then written to the store.","Leave it. Passengers type their own bookings, so they really do write to the file."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. An entity cannot write directly to a store; a process must handle the data.","On a DFD, entities never touch stores directly, whatever the user interface looks like."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reviewing a ferry DFD (step 2)","q":"<p>Next you notice process 2.0 is labelled \"Payment\" and an arrow from 2.0 to Card Payment Provider is labelled \"send money\". What do you change?</p>","options":["Rename the process \"Take payment\" and the flow \"payment request\".","Rename the process \"Payments\" and leave the flow as it is."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Processes take verb phrases; flows take noun phrases.","A plural noun is still a noun. And \"send money\" remains a verb on a flow."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reviewing a ferry DFD (step 3)","q":"<p>Finally, process 3.0 \"Produce manifest\" sends a \"passenger manifest\" to the Immigration Officer, but nothing flows into it. The context diagram has exactly six external flows. What do you do?</p>","options":["Delete process 3.0 because it has an error.","Call it a miracle and add an input: \"passenger list\" read from D1 Bookings. Then check that all six context flows still appear."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Deleting it removes the manifest output, so the level 1 no longer balances with the context diagram.","Excellent. You fixed the miracle and checked balancing."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which label is correct?","q":"<p>At City Taxi Dispatch, callers phone in trip requests, a process matches each request to the nearest free driver, and driver details are kept in a store. The analyst is labelling the DFD. Which set of labels follows the Gane and Sarson naming rules?</p>","options":["Process: \"Driver assignment\"; data flow: \"request trip\"; data store: \"Drivers\"","Process: \"Assign driver\"; data flow: \"sends request\"; data store: \"Store drivers\"","Process: \"Drivers\"; data flow: \"trip request\"; data store: \"Assign driver\"","Process: \"Assign driver\"; data flow: \"trip request\"; data store: \"Drivers\""],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["The process is a noun and the flow is a verb phrase: two labelling errors.","The flow is a verb and the store is named like a process.","The process and store names have been swapped.","Correct. A verb phrase for the matching process and nouns for the caller's trip request and the Drivers store."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the error to its fix","q":"<p>Match each DFD error to the correction an analyst should make.</p>","left":["Black hole","Miracle","Entity connected directly to a store","Store connected directly to a store","Label omitted","Flow labelled with a verb","Process labelled with a noun"],"right":["Insert a process that reads one store and writes the other","Rename the process with a verb phrase, e.g. \"Calculate pay\"","Insert a process between the entity and the store","Add an input data flow to the process","Add an output data flow from the process","Rename the flow with a noun phrase, e.g. \"payment details\"","Write a noun phrase naming the data on the arrow"],"correct":[4,3,2,0,6,5,1]}]},{"id":"assess_793","title":"Module 3 · Unit 7 quiz: Algorithms: design and representation","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 7: Algorithms: design and representation. 61 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (1)","q":"<p>These examples come from homes, businesses and schools around the Caribbean. Decide whether each one is an <b>algorithm</b> (a plan of finite, clear, ordered steps), a <b>program</b> (a plan already coded so a computer can execute it) or <b>not an algorithm</b> at all.</p><p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>A numbered recipe card at Warrens Bakery: \"1. Weigh 500 g of flour. 2. Add 10 g of baking powder. ... 6. Bake for 20 minutes at 180 °C.\"</b></p>","options":["Program (coded)","Algorithm (a plan)","Not an algorithm"],"correct":1,"why":"Finite, precise steps in order that produce a result: an algorithm, even though it is not about computers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (2)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>Written directions for a taxi driver: \"1. Leave the airport and turn onto the coast road. 2. Cross the harbour bridge. 3. Stop at Lagoon Hotel.\"</b></p>","options":["Not an algorithm","Algorithm (a plan)","Program (coded)"],"correct":1,"why":"Clear, ordered steps with a definite end: an everyday algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (3)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>Pseudocode on a whiteboard at Flamboyant Heights College: INPUT hours, charge ← hours * 2, OUTPUT charge.</b></p>","options":["Not an algorithm","Algorithm (a plan)","Program (coded)"],"correct":1,"why":"Pseudocode is one way of writing an algorithm. It is not coded in a real language, so a computer cannot run it yet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (4)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>A Python file on the Sea-Almond Secondary School server that calculates each student's average mark when it is run.</b></p>","options":["Program (coded)","Not an algorithm","Algorithm (a plan)"],"correct":0,"why":"It is coded in a programming language and can be executed: a program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (5)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>The app on the Carenage Water Taxi tablet that works out a group's fare when the crew taps \"Calculate\".</b></p>","options":["Algorithm (a plan)","Not an algorithm","Program (coded)"],"correct":2,"why":"The fare algorithm has been coded and runs on a device: a program.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (6)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>Unity Credit Union's withdrawal check, written in Java and running on the credit union's computers.</b></p>","options":["Program (coded)","Algorithm (a plan)","Not an algorithm"],"correct":0,"why":"Coded in Java and executing: a program. Its plan, the steps before coding, was the algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (7)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>A note on a fridge in Georgetown: \"Do something nice for dinner.\"</b></p>","options":["Program (coded)","Not an algorithm","Algorithm (a plan)"],"correct":1,"why":"Vague and not a sequence of steps, so it is not an algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Algorithm, program or neither? (8)","q":"<p>Algorithm, program or not an algorithm?</p><p><b>A clean-up volunteer's instruction after Hurricane Irma: \"Keep sweeping the street\", with no point at which to stop.</b></p>","options":["Not an algorithm","Program (coded)","Algorithm (a plan)"],"correct":0,"why":"It never ends, so it is not finite and does not qualify as an algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (1)","q":"<p>The StormGuard Shutters is using an algorithm as a problem-solving strategy to quote hurricane shutters at $120 per square metre. For each action, choose the stage of problem solving it belongs to.</p><p>The manager asks: \"What will customers tell us about their windows, and what result do we need to give them?\"</p>","options":["Understand the problem","Carry out the plan","Plan the solution"],"correct":0,"why":"Working out the data given and the result required is understanding the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (2)","q":"<p>A clerk writes: \"1. Get the width. 2. Get the height. 3. Multiply the width by the height to find the area. 4. Multiply the area by 120 to find the price. 5. Display the price.\"</p>","options":["Carry out the plan","Understand the problem","Plan the solution"],"correct":2,"why":"Writing the ordered steps is planning the solution: this is the algorithm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (3)","q":"<p>A worker follows the written steps for a 2 m by 1.5 m window in Marsh Harbour and tells the customer the price is $360.</p>","options":["Carry out the plan","Understand the problem","Plan the solution"],"correct":0,"why":"Following the steps with real data to get the result is carrying out the plan (2 × 1.5 = 3 m², × 120 = $360).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (4)","q":"<p>Before writing any steps, the team notes that every window has a width and a height in metres and that the rate is fixed per square metre.</p>","options":["Carry out the plan","Understand the problem","Plan the solution"],"correct":1,"why":"Identifying the data available is part of understanding the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (5)","q":"<p>The staff check that the area is worked out before the price, and move a step that was in the wrong place.</p>","options":["Understand the problem","Carry out the plan","Plan the solution"],"correct":2,"why":"Putting the steps in the right order is part of planning the solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Understand, plan or carry out? (6)","q":"<p>A programmer codes the agreed steps into the shop's quoting app so that the computer produces each quote.</p>","options":["Plan the solution","Understand the problem","Carry out the plan"],"correct":2,"why":"Coding the plan so a computer follows it is one way of carrying out the plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Defining an algorithm","q":"<p>Before planning a program that works out each student's average mark, an IT class at Palmetto Point Secondary is asked to agree a definition of \"algorithm\". Four students offer answers. Which is the BEST definition?</p>","options":["A program written in a high-level language such as Python","A diagram that uses standard symbols to show a process","A finite sequence of clear, unambiguous steps that, when followed in order, solves a problem","Any list of instructions given to a computer"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["That is a program. The average-mark algorithm is the language-independent plan the program will be coded from.","That describes a flowchart, which is one way the class could represent the algorithm, not the algorithm itself.","Correct. This student includes all the key ideas: finite, clear and unambiguous, ordered, and solving a problem, exactly what the average-mark plan needs.","Too vague. The instructions must be finite, unambiguous and ordered, and must solve a problem."]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (1)","q":"<p>Students at Bayleaf Hill Secondary made these claims about why algorithms matter. Sort each claim.</p><p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>An algorithm lets the team agree on the logic before anyone writes code.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":1,"why":"Valid. It is a shared plan for communication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (2)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>An algorithm only works in one programming language.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":0,"why":"Misconception. Algorithms are language-independent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (3)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>Logic errors can be found by working through the steps by hand.</b></p>","options":["Valid importance","Misconception"],"correct":0,"why":"Valid. Early error detection is cheaper than fixing code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (4)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>Once you have an algorithm, the computer can run it immediately without coding.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":0,"why":"Misconception. An algorithm must be coded in a programming language before a computer can execute it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (5)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>A written algorithm documents how the solution works for future maintenance.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":1,"why":"Valid. It helps other programmers understand and change the solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (6)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>Algorithms are only needed for very large programs.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":0,"why":"Misconception. Every program, however small, needs a clear plan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (7)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>A general algorithm can be reused for similar problems.</b></p>","options":["Misconception","Valid importance"],"correct":1,"why":"Valid. Reuse saves time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"True importance or misconception? (8)","q":"<p>Is this a valid reason why algorithms are important, or a misconception?</p><p><b>Algorithms make programs slower because of the extra planning.</b></p>","options":["Valid importance","Misconception"],"correct":1,"why":"Misconception. Planning happens before coding and does not slow the program down; it usually saves development time.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (1)","q":"<p>Each algorithm below was written by a student for a Caribbean business. Choose the characteristic of a well-designed algorithm that it BEST fails to show.</p><p>Frigate Bay Car Rentals: \"1. Multiply 3 days by $45. 2. Display $135.\"</p>","options":["Flow of control","General solution","Finite number of steps"],"correct":1,"why":"It works only for a 3-day rental at $45, so it is not a general solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (2)","q":"<p>Roseau Bakery: \"1. Bake the bread until it looks right. 2. Remove from oven.\"</p>","options":["Finite number of steps","General solution","Clearly defined and unambiguous"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Until it looks right\" can be read in many ways. It should state a time or temperature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (3)","q":"<p>Montego Bay Gym: \"1. Display the member's fee. 2. Get the number of months. 3. Calculate fee as months times 50.\"</p>","options":["Finite number of steps","General solution","Flow of control"],"correct":2,"why":"The fee is displayed before it is calculated. The steps are in the wrong order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (4)","q":"<p>Grand Anse Fishing: \"1. Cast the net. 2. Go back to step 1.\"</p>","options":["Finite number of steps","General solution","Clearly defined and unambiguous"],"correct":0,"why":"There is no condition for stopping, so it never ends.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (5)","q":"<p>King George V Street Jewellers: \"1. Get the price. 2. Apply a reasonable discount. 3. Display the new price.\"</p>","options":["Finite number of steps","Flow of control","Clearly defined and unambiguous"],"correct":2,"why":"\"A reasonable discount\" is vague. A precise rule, such as 10% off, is needed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which characteristic is missing? (6)","q":"<p>Saba Dive Centre: \"1. Get the number of divers. 2. If there are more than 6, book two boats. 3. ???\"</p>","options":["General solution","Finite number of steps","Flow of control","Clearly defined and unambiguous"],"correct":2,"why":"There is no next step for 6 or fewer divers, so control has nowhere to go: a dead end in the flow of control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the ambiguity","q":"<p>A programmer at the Anguilla Ferry Service writes the step \"If the boat is nearly full, stop selling tickets.\" Which characteristic of a well-designed algorithm does this step violate?</p>","options":["Clearly defined and unambiguous","Flow of control","Finite number of steps","General solution"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. \"Nearly full\" has no precise meaning. It should state an exact condition, such as \"if 120 tickets have been sold\".","The order is not the problem. A computer could not decide what \"nearly\" means.","The step does not cause endless repetition. The problem lies in its wording.","The step could apply to any sailing; the issue is that the condition is vague."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Find the faulty steps","q":"<p>Dominica Eco-Lodge wants an algorithm that works out the total cost of a stay for any guest. A trainee wrote this draft.</p><p>Select EVERY step that breaks a characteristic of a well-designed algorithm.</p>","options":["5. Display the total cost.","6. Go back to step 5.","4. Add a small cleaning fee to the room cost.","3. Get the nightly room rate.","1. Get the number of nights.","2. Multiply 4 nights by $90 to find the room cost."],"correct":[1,2,5],"why":"Correct. Step 2 uses fixed values (not general) and comes before the rate is obtained in step 3 (flow of control); step 4 is ambiguous (\"small\"); step 6 makes the algorithm loop for ever (not finite). Steps 1, 3 and 5 are acceptable on their own."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the symbol to its job","q":"<p>Match each flowchart symbol to what it is used for.</p>","left":["Rounded rectangle (oval)","Parallelogram","Rectangle","Diamond","Arrow","Small labelled circle"],"right":["Start or stop the algorithm","Input data or output a result","Connect parts of the flowchart without long lines","Carry out a calculation or action","Ask a Yes/No question and branch","Show the direction of flow of control"],"correct":[0,1,3,4,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (1)","q":"<p>Students at Calabash Point Secondary drew flowcharts for businesses around the Caribbean. Sort each feature: does it follow the rules for a well-formed flowchart, or break one?</p><p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Jan Thiel Car Wash: one Start at the top, and both branches of the decision rejoin and flow to a single Stop.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":1,"why":"One Start, every path reaches Stop: a well-formed, finite flowchart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (2)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Wellcare Pharmacy: the diamond \"Prescription?\" has two exits labelled Yes and No.</b></p>","options":["Follows the rules","Breaks a rule"],"correct":0,"why":"A decision has one line in and two labelled lines out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (3)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Palm Beach Bar: the diamond \"Age >= 18?\" has two exits, but neither is labelled.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":0,"why":"Decision exits must be labelled Yes and No (or True and False); otherwise the reader cannot tell which path is which.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (4)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Salem Bakery: the No path from \"Enough flour?\" ends in an arrow pointing to nothing.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":0,"why":"Every path must reach Stop. This is a dead end in the flow of control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (5)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Town Library: two flow lines would cross, so the student uses connector circles labelled A instead.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":1,"why":"Connectors avoid crossing or very long lines.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (6)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Island Link Ferry: the process box \"fare = adults * 10\" has two arrows leaving it to different boxes.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":0,"why":"A process has one line in and one line out. Only a decision has two exits.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (7)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Dickenson Bay Hotel: the flowchart has two Start symbols, one for guests and one for staff.</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":0,"why":"A flowchart has exactly one Start.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rule kept or broken? (8)","q":"<p>Does this feature follow the flowchart rules?</p><p><b>Calabash Point Secondary tuck shop: the flow runs top to bottom and each symbol holds short, precise text such as \"Input price\".</b></p>","options":["Breaks a rule","Follows the rules"],"correct":1,"why":"Top-to-bottom flow and precise text keep the flowchart clear and unambiguous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the right symbol","q":"<p>In a flowchart for Sea Breeze Marina, the step \"Display the berth fee\" should be drawn in which symbol?</p>","options":["A rectangle","A parallelogram","A rounded rectangle","A diamond"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Rectangles are for processes such as calculations. Displaying a result is output.","Correct. Displaying (outputting) a result uses the input/output symbol, the parallelogram.","Rounded rectangles (terminals) mark only the start and stop.","Diamonds are for decisions with Yes/No exits. Displaying a fee is not a question."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (1)","q":"<p>A Montego Bay software team has left notes from a planning meeting. Sort each description or fragment by the representation it belongs to.</p><p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>\"3. Multiply the number of tickets by the price to find the cost.\"</b></p>","options":["Flowchart","Pseudocode","Natural language"],"correct":2,"why":"A full English sentence describing a step: natural language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (2)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>cost ← tickets * price</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Natural language","Flowchart"],"correct":0,"why":"An assignment statement with a variable and operator: pseudocode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (3)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>A parallelogram containing \"Input tickets\".</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Natural language","Flowchart"],"correct":2,"why":"A standard symbol: flowchart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (4)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>IF tickets > 10 THEN discount ← 5 ENDIF</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Flowchart","Natural language"],"correct":0,"why":"Keywords IF, THEN, ENDIF: pseudocode.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (5)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>A diamond with arrows labelled Yes and No.</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Natural language","Flowchart"],"correct":2,"why":"The decision symbol: flowchart.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (6)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>Most easily understood by a client with no technical training.</b></p>","options":["Pseudocode","Natural language","Flowchart"],"correct":1,"why":"Everyday sentences need no training to read.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (7)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>Converts almost line by line into program code.</b></p>","options":["Flowchart","Natural language","Pseudocode"],"correct":2,"why":"Pseudocode is closest to real code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which representation is it? (8)","q":"<p>Natural language, flowchart or pseudocode?</p><p><b>Shows the flow of control visually with arrows.</b></p>","options":["Flowchart","Pseudocode","Natural language"],"correct":0,"why":"Flow lines make flowcharts the most visual.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build the pseudocode","q":"<p>The Island Treasures Souvenirs shop needs pseudocode that works out what a customer pays for any number of identical T-shirts. Put the lines in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["START","INPUT price","INPUT quantity","amount ← price * quantity","OUTPUT amount","STOP"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Pseudocode to flowchart","q":"<p>Each pseudocode statement below comes from an algorithm for Saba Hiking Trail permit desk. Match it to the flowchart symbol that would represent it.</p>","left":["START","INPUT hikers","fee ← hikers * 15","IF hikers > 8 THEN","OUTPUT fee"],"right":["Terminal (rounded rectangle)","Decision (diamond)","Process (rectangle)","Input/output (parallelogram) reading data","Input/output (parallelogram) showing a result"],"correct":[0,3,2,1,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Best representation for the audience","q":"<p>The Barbados Bus Co-operative wants to show its board of directors, none of whom are programmers, the decision steps for approving a refund. Which representation is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A flowchart, because it shows the decision points and paths visually without needing programming knowledge","Program code, because it is the most precise","Any representation, because they all communicate equally well","Pseudocode, because it converts easily into a program"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Decisions appear as diamonds with labelled Yes/No paths, which a non-technical board can follow easily.","Code is precise but unreadable for non-programmers, and it is not one of the three ways of representing an algorithm.","They represent the same logic, but they do not suit every audience equally.","Ease of coding matters to programmers, not to this audience. Non-programmers find pseudocode keywords harder to follow."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the electricity bill-checker project (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a student intern with the Kittitian software firm Harbour Code, building a simple app that helps households in Basseterre estimate their electricity bill from meter readings. Make good design decisions.</p><p>Your team leader asks how you will begin. A fellow intern says, \"Let's just open the editor and start coding.\" What do you do?</p>","options":["Write an algorithm first, so the team agrees on the steps before coding.","Start coding straight away and fix problems as they appear."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good. The algorithm is the plan for the solution and lets errors be found early.","\"Code and fix\" wastes time and makes logic errors more likely."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the electricity bill-checker project (step 2)","q":"<p>Your first draft says: \"Get this month's reading. Subtract a reasonable previous reading. Multiply by the rate.\" What do you change?</p>","options":["Replace \"a reasonable previous reading\" with \"the previous month's reading\", and state the exact rate per unit.","Nothing. The programmer will know what \"reasonable\" means."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. Each step is now clearly defined and unambiguous.","A computer cannot interpret \"reasonable\". The step is ambiguous."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the electricity bill-checker project (step 3)","q":"<p>The client, a manager with no programming background, wants to check the logic before you code. How do you present the algorithm to her?</p>","options":["As detailed pseudocode only, since that is what the programmers need.","As a flowchart using standard symbols, with the pseudocode kept for the programmers."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Pseudocode is hard for non-technical readers to check.","Excellent. You matched the representation to each audience."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength or limitation? (1)","q":"<p>For each statement, choose the representation it BEST describes.</p><p>Its biggest weakness is that the same words can be understood in different ways.</p>","options":["Flowchart","Pseudocode","Natural language"],"correct":2,"why":"Ambiguity is the classic weakness of natural language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength or limitation? (2)","q":"<p>It is slow to modify because shapes and lines have to be redrawn.</p>","options":["Flowchart","Natural language","Pseudocode"],"correct":0,"why":"Editing a diagram is harder than editing text.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength or limitation? (3)","q":"<p>There is no single universal standard, so styles vary between textbooks.</p>","options":["Natural language","Flowchart","Pseudocode"],"correct":2,"why":"Pseudocode conventions vary; the key is to be consistent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength or limitation? (4)","q":"<p>Decision points and both of their paths can be seen at a glance.</p>","options":["Flowchart","Natural language","Pseudocode"],"correct":0,"why":"Diamonds with labelled exits make branches visible.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Strength or limitation? (5)","q":"<p>A programmer can convert it almost line by line into a program.</p>","options":["Natural language","Pseudocode","Flowchart"],"correct":1,"why":"Pseudocode statements map closely onto program statements.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Flowchart rules","q":"<p>A student's flowchart for the Statia Dive Shop has a diamond containing \"Certified diver?\" with only one arrow leaving it. What is wrong?</p>","options":["The question should be in a parallelogram","Diamonds may only be used at the start of a flowchart","A diamond should contain a calculation, not a question","The decision needs two labelled exits, Yes and No, so the flow of control is clear for both answers"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Parallelograms are for input and output, not decisions.","Decisions can appear anywhere they are needed.","The opposite: diamonds contain questions. Calculations go in rectangles.","Correct. With only one exit, there is no path for the other answer, so the flow of control has a dead end."]}]},{"id":"assess_7hy","title":"Module 3 · Unit 8 quiz: Developing algorithms and programming","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 8: Developing algorithms and programming. 62 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (1)","q":"<p>The Carenage Water Taxi is writing an algorithm for its new ticket kiosk on the Port of Spain boardwalk. Sort each line of pseudocode by the kind of statement it is.</p><p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>INPUT adults (the kiosk asks how many adults are travelling)</b></p>","options":["Output","Input","Processing"],"correct":1,"why":"INPUT brings data from the user into the variable adults.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (2)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>READ children</b></p>","options":["Processing","Input","Output"],"correct":1,"why":"READ is another input keyword: it stores data in children.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (3)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>fare ← adults * 15 + children * 8</b></p>","options":["Processing","Input","Output"],"correct":0,"why":"A calculation assigned to fare with the arrow: processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (4)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>OUTPUT \"Fare due: US$\", fare</b></p>","options":["Input","Output","Processing"],"correct":1,"why":"OUTPUT sends the result to the screen, even though it names a variable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (5)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>GET paymentMethod (cash or card)</b></p>","options":["Input","Processing","Output"],"correct":0,"why":"GET accepts data from the customer: input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (6)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>change ← tendered - fare</b></p>","options":["Processing","Input","Output"],"correct":0,"why":"Working out the change and storing it is an assignment: processing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (7)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>ticketCount ← ticketCount + 1</b></p>","options":["Input","Output","Processing"],"correct":2,"why":"Updating a variable with the arrow is processing (here, a counter).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (8)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>PRINT \"Ticket number \", ticketCount</b></p>","options":["Output","Input","Processing"],"correct":0,"why":"PRINT produces a result on the receipt: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Input, processing or output? (9)","q":"<p>What kind of statement is this?</p><p><b>DISPLAY \"Thank you for sailing with us\"</b></p>","options":["Output","Processing","Input"],"correct":0,"why":"DISPLAY shows a message: output.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the flowchart symbol","q":"<p>You are drawing a flowchart for Sea Breeze Marina, which charges boats a berthing fee. Match each flowchart symbol or term to the job it does in the marina's chart.</p>","left":["Oval (terminator)","Parallelogram","Rectangle","Diamond","Flow line (arrow)","Connector (small circle)","Nesting"],"right":["INPUT boatLength, or OUTPUT \"Your fee is \", fee","START at the top of the chart and STOP at the bottom","A decision placed inside a loop that checks every boat in turn","\"Is boatLength > 20?\" with a Yes exit and a No exit","Shows the direction in which control passes to the next step","Joins two parts of the long chart that run onto a second page","fee ← boatLength * nights * 2.50"],"correct":[1,0,6,3,4,5,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which statement is processing?","q":"<p>Sea Breeze Marina uses an algorithm to charge boats for berthing. Which ONE of these lines is a <b>processing</b> statement?</p>","options":["fee ← boatLength * nights * 2.50","INPUT boatLength","OUTPUT \"Your fee is \", fee","READ nights"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. It calculates a value and assigns it to fee: an assignment, which is processing.","This accepts data into the variable boatLength. It is an input statement.","This displays a result. It is an output statement, even though it mentions a variable.","READ is another keyword for input: it brings data into the algorithm."]},{"t":"match","title":"Put the island tour algorithm in sequence","q":"<p>The King George V Street Island Tours sells tours to cruise passengers at the Roseau cruise port: US$45 per adult, US$25 per child and a US$10 booking fee per group. A new clerk has jumbled the lines of the booking algorithm. Put them back in a working sequence.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["START","INPUT adults, children","adultCost ← adults * 45 and childCost ← children * 25","cost ← adultCost + childCost + 10","OUTPUT \"Total cost: US$\", cost","STOP"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (1)","q":"<p>The FreshMart Supermarket is automating its checkout. Sort each description by the control structure it needs MOST.</p><p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Scan every item in the customer's basket until there are no more items.</b></p>","options":["Sequence","Selection","Repetition"],"correct":2,"why":"Doing the same steps for each item until none are left is repetition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (2)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Give a 5% discount only if the customer shows a loyalty card.</b></p>","options":["Repetition","Sequence","Selection"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Only if\" signals a condition: selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (3)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Input the amount tendered, calculate the change, then print the change.</b></p>","options":["Repetition","Sequence","Selection"],"correct":1,"why":"Three steps, once each, in order: sequence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (4)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>If the change is negative, display \"Insufficient payment\"; otherwise print the receipt.</b></p>","options":["Selection","Repetition","Sequence"],"correct":0,"why":"Two alternative paths chosen by a condition: IF-THEN-ELSE selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (5)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Print the store name, the date and the cashier number at the top of the receipt.</b></p>","options":["Sequence","Repetition","Selection"],"correct":0,"why":"Simple statements carried out one after the other: sequence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (6)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Keep asking for the PIN while the PIN entered is wrong and fewer than three attempts have been made.</b></p>","options":["Sequence","Repetition","Selection"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Keep asking while\" is a WHILE loop: repetition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (7)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Add up the prices of the 12 items in a box of mangoes.</b></p>","options":["Selection","Sequence","Repetition"],"correct":2,"why":"A fixed number of passes (12) suits a FOR loop: repetition with an accumulator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sequence, selection or repetition? (8)","q":"<p>Which control structure does this describe?</p><p><b>Charge a bag fee when the customer asks for a plastic bag.</b></p>","options":["Repetition","Sequence","Selection"],"correct":2,"why":"The fee depends on a condition: selection.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (1)","q":"<p>Each condition below comes from a Caribbean system. Do the arithmetic first (precedence), then decide TRUE or FALSE, then choose the result.</p><p>An airline at GAIA uses: IF weight > 23 THEN charge an excess fee. A passenger's bag weighs exactly 23 kg. Is a fee charged?</p>","options":["Yes","No"],"correct":1,"why":"23 > 23 is FALSE, so no fee. With >= 23 the 23 kg bag would be charged: always check the boundary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (2)","q":"<p>Bright Minds Tutoring Centre grades with: IF mark >= 70 THEN grade ← \"A\" ELSE IF mark >= 55 THEN grade ← \"B\" ELSE grade ← \"C\". A student scores 80. What grade?</p>","options":["B","A","C"],"correct":1,"why":"80 >= 70 is TRUE at the first test, so grade is A and the other tests are skipped. This is why the highest boundary is tested first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (3)","q":"<p>A King Street shop algorithm sets a ← 6 and b ← 4, then tests IF a + b * 3 > 20. Is the condition TRUE or FALSE?</p>","options":["TRUE","FALSE"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply first: 4 * 3 = 12; 6 + 12 = 18; 18 > 20 is FALSE. Adding first, (6 + 4) * 3 = 30, is the classic error.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (4)","q":"<p>Sea Breeze Marina gives a discount IF length > 20 OR nights >= 5. A boat is 18 m long and stays 5 nights. Discount given?</p>","options":["No","Yes"],"correct":1,"why":"length > 20 is FALSE but nights >= 5 is TRUE. OR needs only one TRUE condition, so the discount is given.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (5)","q":"<p>FreshMart Supermarket gives a discount IF total > 100 AND card = TRUE. A customer spends US$120 but has no loyalty card. Discount given?</p>","options":["No","Yes"],"correct":0,"why":"total > 100 is TRUE but card = TRUE is FALSE. AND needs both conditions TRUE, so no discount.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (6)","q":"<p>A Bridgetown bus company marks a bus as an express IF busNumber MOD 3 = 0. Is bus 14 an express?</p>","options":["No","Yes"],"correct":0,"why":"14 MOD 3 = 2 (14 = 4 × 3 + 2). 2 = 0 is FALSE, so bus 14 is not an express.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the condition (7)","q":"<p>A tour bus works out seats ← 20 − 8 / 4 * 2 + 1. What value is stored in seats?</p>","options":["17","13","7"],"correct":0,"why":"8 / 4 = 2, then 2 * 2 = 4 (left to right), then 20 − 4 + 1 = 17. Working strictly left to right gives the wrong answer 7.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which branch runs?","q":"<p>A Sixth Form student at Palmetto Point Secondary is desk-checking this loyalty-points rule for the Grand Anse Bakery before coding it.<br><br>x ← 5<br>y ← 3<br>IF x * 2 - y &gt;= 7 THEN<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;z ← x + y * 2<br>ELSE<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;z ← (x + y) * 2<br>ENDIF<br>OUTPUT z<br><br>What is output?</p>","options":["4","16","7","11"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This comes from 5 * (2 − 3) style errors. Multiplication happens before subtraction: 10 − 3 = 7.","That is (5 + 3) * 2, the ELSE branch. The condition 5 * 2 − 3 = 7, and 7 >= 7 is TRUE, so the THEN branch runs.","7 is the value of the condition x * 2 − y, not z. The condition only decides which branch runs.","Correct. 5 * 2 − 3 = 7; 7 >= 7 is TRUE; THEN branch: 3 * 2 = 6 first, then 5 + 6 = 11."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the loop to its behaviour","q":"<p>Match each loop or loop term to the description that fits it.</p>","left":["FOR loop","WHILE loop","REPEAT-UNTIL loop","Sentinel value","Iteration","Infinite loop","FOR i ← 10 DOWNTO 1"],"right":["A counting-down loop that runs ten times","A dummy value such as -1 entered only to signal the end of the data","A loop whose condition never changes, so it never ends","Tests the condition after each pass and always runs at least once","Tests the condition before each pass and may run zero times","Repeats a fixed number of times controlled by a counter","One pass through the body of a loop"],"correct":[5,4,3,1,6,2,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (1)","q":"<p>For each loop, choose how many times its body runs.</p><p>FOR k ← 1 TO 12 DO ... ENDFOR</p>","options":["13","11","12"],"correct":2,"why":"k takes 1 to 12 inclusive: 12 − 1 + 1 = 12 passes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (2)","q":"<p>FOR k ← 10 DOWNTO 3 DO ... ENDFOR</p>","options":["0 (because 10 is bigger than 3)","7","8"],"correct":2,"why":"10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3: eight passes. Counting down is allowed with DOWNTO.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (3)","q":"<p>FOR k ← 0 TO 20 STEP 5 DO ... ENDFOR</p>","options":["5","4","20"],"correct":0,"why":"0, 5, 10, 15, 20: five passes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (4)","q":"<p>n ← 10. WHILE n < 5 DO n ← n + 1 ENDWHILE</p>","options":["1","5","0"],"correct":2,"why":"10 < 5 is FALSE at the first test, so a WHILE loop body never runs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (5)","q":"<p>n ← 10. REPEAT n ← n + 1 UNTIL n > 5</p>","options":["1","0","Infinite"],"correct":0,"why":"REPEAT runs the body once (n becomes 11), then 11 > 5 is TRUE, so it stops.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many times does it loop? (6)","q":"<p>n ← 1. WHILE n < 50 DO n ← n * 3 ENDWHILE</p>","options":["4","5","3"],"correct":0,"why":"n goes 1 → 3 → 9 → 27 → 81: four passes. 81 < 50 is FALSE, so it stops.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (1)","q":"<p>A Sixth Form student at Mahoe Grove High School is dry-running algorithms for the Rainwatch Weather Club before they are coded. For each item, choose the value or answer the trace table should show.</p><p>total ← 0. FOR k ← 5 DOWNTO 1: total ← total + k * 2. ENDFOR. What is the FIRST value of k in the trace table?</p>","options":["1","0","5"],"correct":2,"why":"DOWNTO starts at the first number, 5, and counts down to 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (2)","q":"<p>Same algorithm. What is total after the pass where k = 3?</p>","options":["24","30","18"],"correct":0,"why":"k = 5 adds 10 (10), k = 4 adds 8 (18), k = 3 adds 6 (24). 30 is the final total after k = 1.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (3)","q":"<p>p ← 10. FOR i ← 4 DOWNTO 1: IF p − i * 2 > 4 THEN p ← p − i ELSE p ← p + 2 * i − 1 ENDIF. ENDFOR. On the first pass (i = 4), which branch runs?</p>","options":["THEN","ELSE"],"correct":1,"why":"i * 2 = 8 first, then 10 − 8 = 2. 2 > 4 is FALSE, so the ELSE branch runs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (4)","q":"<p>Same algorithm. What is p after the first pass?</p>","options":["6","17","16"],"correct":1,"why":"ELSE: 10 + 2 * 4 − 1 = 10 + 8 − 1 = 17. 16 comes from 2 * (i − 1); 6 comes from wrongly taking the THEN branch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (5)","q":"<p>c ← 0. FOR n ← 1 TO 10: IF n MOD 3 = 0 THEN c ← c + 1 ENDIF. ENDFOR. OUTPUT c. What is output?</p>","options":["18","3","10"],"correct":1,"why":"n MOD 3 = 0 is TRUE for 3, 6 and 9, so c becomes 3. 18 adds the numbers (accumulator) instead of counting them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (6)","q":"<p>What is the FUNCTION of the algorithm in the previous item?</p>","options":["It counts the multiples of 3 from 1 to 10","It adds up the numbers from 1 to 10","It outputs every number from 1 to 10"],"correct":0,"why":"The function is the purpose in plain words: it counts how many numbers from 1 to 10 are divisible by 3.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Complete the trace (7)","q":"<p>Rainfall of 12, 0 and 8 mm is entered into: total ← 0. FOR d ← 1 TO 3: INPUT r; total ← total + r. ENDFOR. OUTPUT total. How many values appear in the Output column?</p>","options":["3","1"],"correct":1,"why":"OUTPUT is after ENDFOR, outside the loop, so it runs once and prints 20.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Trace the countdown","q":"<p>Peak Fitness Club's app works out a \"workout score\" during a short countdown routine. What is output by this algorithm?<br><br>s ← 0<br>FOR j ← 6 DOWNTO 2 STEP -2 DO<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;s ← s + j * j<br>ENDFOR<br>OUTPUT s</p>","options":["24","56","90","4"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is (6 + 4 + 2) * 2. The loop squares j (j * j), it does not double the sum.","Correct. j takes 6, 4, 2: 36 + 16 + 4 = 56.","This adds every value from 6 down to 2 (36 + 25 + 16 + 9 + 4). STEP -2 skips 5 and 3.","This is only the last pass. s is an accumulator, so it keeps adding on every pass."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Find the bugs in the rainfall algorithm","q":"<p>A Sixth Form student at Seagrape Bay Secondary School wrote this algorithm for the Frigate Bay Weather Club. It should read the rainfall for 7 days and output the total and the number of days with more than 10 mm. It contains errors.</p><p>Select EVERY line that contains an error.</p>","options":["5.     total ← total + rain","10. ENDFOR","8.     ENDIF","4.     INPUT rain","3.     total ← 0","6.     IF rain > 10 THEN","7.         wetDays ← wetDays + rain","2. FOR day ← 1 TO 7 DO","9.     OUTPUT total, wetDays","1. wetDays ← 0"],"correct":[4,6,8],"why":"Well found. Line 3 resets the accumulator every pass (move total ← 0 above the loop); line 7 adds rain to a counter (should be wetDays + 1); line 9 outputs inside the loop (move it after ENDFOR)."},{"t":"match","title":"Assemble the bus fare algorithm","q":"<p>The Kingston–Spanish Town bus line wants an algorithm that reads the fares for a fixed 20 passengers and outputs the total. Put the lines in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["total ← 0","FOR p ← 1 TO 20 DO","    INPUT fare","    total ← total + fare","ENDFOR","OUTPUT \"Total fares: \", total"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Put program development in order","q":"<p>The Saba Dive Centre wants a program to book dives. Put the stages its programmer follows in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Define the problem: write a clear statement of what the booking program must do.","Analyse the problem: identify inputs (diver, date, site), processing and outputs.","Design the algorithm in pseudocode and desk-check it with a trace table.","Code the algorithm in a programming language.","Test and debug the program with normal, boundary and erroneous data.","Document the program and maintain it after it goes into use."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each description or language into its programming paradigm.</p><p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>A program written as step-by-step instructions grouped into procedures.</b></p>","options":["Functional","Declarative / logic","Procedural","Object-oriented"],"correct":2,"why":"Step-by-step \"how\" instructions in procedures: procedural.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (2)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>A class called Booking has attributes (name, date) and methods (confirm, cancel).</b></p>","options":["Object-oriented","Procedural","Functional","Declarative / logic"],"correct":0,"why":"Classes, attributes and methods: object-oriented.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (3)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Facts and rules are stated; the system answers queries such as \"Who is the parent of Ana?\"</b></p>","options":["Declarative / logic","Procedural","Functional","Object-oriented"],"correct":0,"why":"Facts, rules and queries: logic programming (declarative).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (4)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Programs are built from functions that return values without changing any variables.</b></p>","options":["Procedural","Functional","Object-oriented","Declarative / logic"],"correct":1,"why":"Pure functions and no changing state: functional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (5)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Pascal</b></p>","options":["Functional","Object-oriented","Procedural","Declarative / logic"],"correct":2,"why":"Pascal is a classic procedural (structured) language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (6)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>C++</b></p>","options":["Functional","Declarative / logic","Object-oriented","Procedural"],"correct":2,"why":"C++ is classed as object-oriented (\"C with classes\"), though it can also be used procedurally.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (7)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Prolog</b></p>","options":["Procedural","Declarative / logic","Functional","Object-oriented"],"correct":1,"why":"Prolog is the standard example of a logic language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (8)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Haskell</b></p>","options":["Functional","Procedural","Declarative / logic","Object-oriented"],"correct":0,"why":"Haskell is a purely functional language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (9)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>Java</b></p>","options":["Functional","Declarative / logic","Procedural","Object-oriented"],"correct":3,"why":"Java is object-oriented: nearly everything is written inside classes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paradigm? (10)","q":"<p>Which paradigm does this belong to?</p><p><b>A Sales object inherits features from a Transaction class.</b></p>","options":["Functional","Procedural","Object-oriented","Declarative / logic"],"correct":2,"why":"Inheritance is an OOP feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the term to its meaning","q":"<p>Match each programming term from SO 3.12 to its meaning.</p>","left":["Algorithm","Program","Programming paradigm","Compiler","Interpreter","Logic error","Syntax error"],"right":["A rule of the language's grammar is broken","A style or approach to organising a program","Translates the whole source program into machine code at once","Translates and executes the program one statement at a time","A language-independent plan of steps to solve a problem","Instructions in a programming language that a computer can execute","The program runs but gives a wrong result"],"correct":[4,5,1,2,3,6,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Building the canteen ordering program (step 1)","q":"<p>The Seagrape Bay Secondary School canteen wants a small program to total each student's lunch order and count orders during the lunch break. You are the student programmer. Make the best decisions.</p><p>The canteen manager is in a hurry: \"Just start typing the code now, we need it by Friday.\" What do you do first?</p>","options":["Start coding immediately and fix problems as they appear.","Define and analyse the problem, then write and desk-check an algorithm before coding."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Skipping design usually costs more time, because logic errors are found late.","Good. The algorithm is the precursor to the program; logic errors are cheapest to fix on paper."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Building the canteen ordering program (step 2)","q":"<p>Orders arrive one at a time and nobody knows how many students will buy lunch. The cashier types 0 after the last order. Which loop do you design?</p>","options":["A WHILE loop with 0 as a sentinel value, with one INPUT before the loop and another at the end of the loop body.","FOR order ← 1 TO 200, because the school has about 200 students."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. An unknown number of items with a stop value calls for a condition-controlled loop and a sentinel.","A FOR loop needs a known, fixed count. Here the number of orders is unknown."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Building the canteen ordering program (step 3)","q":"<p>You trace your algorithm with the test data 12, 8, 0. The trace table shows a final total of 8 instead of 20. What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["total ← 0 has been placed inside the loop, so the accumulator is reset on every pass.","The trace table must be wrong; the computer will get it right."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Exactly. Move the initialisation above the loop.","A computer follows the same logic. If the trace is wrong, the program will be wrong too."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Building the canteen ordering program (step 4)","q":"<p>The algorithm now traces correctly. The manager asks which language to use; the IT teacher suggests an object-oriented language so that a MenuItem class can be reused later. Which do you choose?</p>","options":["HTML, because the menu will appear on a screen.","C++ or Java, both object-oriented languages."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["HTML is a markup language; it has no selection, loops or objects.","Sound choice. Both support classes and objects, and the algorithm translates directly into them."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paradigm in practice","q":"<p>A developer at the Statia Shipping Agency writes: <i>parent(maria, john). parent(john, ana). grandparent(X, Z) :- parent(X, Y), parent(Y, Z).</i> She then asks the system who is Ana's grandparent, without writing any steps for how to search. Which paradigm is she using?</p>","options":["Functional","Declarative (logic)","Procedural","Object-oriented"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Functional programs are built from functions returning values. Facts, rules and queries signal logic programming.","Correct. She states facts and a rule (what is true) and the system works out the answer: logic programming, as in Prolog.","Procedural code gives step-by-step instructions (loops, assignments). Here no steps are given.","There are no classes, objects or methods; only facts and a rule."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 01 Specimen and Mock Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_7o8","title":"Paper 01 · 2020 specimen","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 01 · 2020 specimen</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><p>Original: CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 specimen paper (2020).</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options lettered (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Start the optional timer to practise under exam conditions.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following refers to the transmitting and receiving of data, from voice to video across a distance via a communication channel?</p>","options":["Protocol","Modem","Network","Telecommunications"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Telecommunications is the sending and receiving of data of any kind (voice, text, video) over a distance through a communication channel. A network (C) is tempting, but a network is the set of linked devices that uses telecommunications; it is not the act of transmitting itself. A protocol is a set of rules and a modem is a single device.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following sequences lists the data storage devices in order of the increasing size of the device?</p>","options":["Floppy disk, CDROM, USB flash drive, hard disk","CDROM, USB flash drive, floppy disk, hard disk","USB flash drive, CDROM, hard disk, floppy disk","Hard disk, floppy disk, USB flash drive, CDROM"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. \"Size\" here means storage capacity: a floppy disk holds about 1.44 MB, a CD-ROM about 700 MB, a USB flash drive several gigabytes and a hard disk hundreds of gigabytes to terabytes. Option B is the most tempting because the CD-ROM is physically larger, but it puts the floppy disk (the smallest capacity) third, so the order is wrong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>One characteristic that books traditionally have as an information source is that they usually</p>","options":["are easily updated","provide information on the most current topics","provide an in-depth examination of the topic","take a short time to publish"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Books are known for depth: they treat a topic in detail and at length. Option B is the trap; books take a long time to write and publish, so they are usually less current than newspapers, journals or websites. Options A and D are also the opposite of what books are like.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Items 4–5 refer to the following options.</b></p><p>(A) data<br>(B) wisdom<br>(C) information<br>(D) knowledge</p><p>Match EACH item below with ONE of the options above. Each option may be used once, more than once or not at all.</p></blockquote><p>The temperatures at three different locations across the country were recorded. The temperatures recorded are an example of</p>","options":["data","wisdom","information","knowledge"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Raw, unprocessed readings such as individual temperatures are data. They become information (C) only when processed, for example averaged or compared, so that they carry meaning. That processing has not happened yet in this item.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Susan decided to take along an umbrella with her after learning of the daily average temperature. This is an example of</p>","options":["data","wisdom","information","knowledge"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Knowledge is the use of information to make a decision. Susan applied what she learnt to decide to take an umbrella. Information (C) is the tempting choice, because the daily average temperature is information, but the item asks about her decision, which is knowledge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>All of the following are real time communication tools used in information technology EXCEPT</p>","options":["Mailing list","Internet relay chat","Video conferencing","Voice over Internet Protocol"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A mailing list sends messages to subscribers’ inboxes to be read later, so it is asynchronous, not real time. Internet Relay Chat (B) may look old-fashioned, but it is live text chat, so it is a real-time tool, as are video conferencing and VoIP.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The IEEE/ACM sub-disciplines for computing include all the following EXCEPT</p>","options":["information systems","artificial technology","computer engineering","software engineering"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The computing sub-disciplines are computer science, computer engineering, software engineering, information systems and information technology. \"Artificial technology\" is not a discipline; it is a distractor built to sound like artificial intelligence. Information systems (A) is a genuine sub-discipline, even though it sounds more like business.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Miss Jane prepared a practice exam for her class on her personal computer and would like to make this file available on the Internet for her students to download. Which of the following tools should she use to make this file available online?</p>","options":["FTP","Telnet","Newsgroup","Internet Relay Chat"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is used to upload files to a server so that others can download them. Telnet (B) is tempting because it also connects to a remote computer, but it gives a text-based remote login session; it is not a file upload and download tool.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A large tanker, carrying toxic waste, overturns on a major highway. The Office of Disaster Management needs to inform drivers and the surrounding communities of the disaster. Which of the following information sources is the MOST appropriate to use for sending out the information?</p>","options":["Website","Television","Newspaper","Social media"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Social media spreads an urgent message almost instantly, reaches people on their phones (including drivers already on the road) and is easily shared onward. Television (B) is the strongest distractor, but people must already be watching, and drivers cannot watch television. A newspaper is far too slow, and a website relies on people deciding to visit it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 10 refers to the following diagram.</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 130\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:500px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"30\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"30\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\"/><rect x=\"290\" y=\"30\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\"/><rect x=\"430\" y=\"30\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"50\"/><polyline points=\"143.7,53 150,50 143.7,47\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"15.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"110\" y=\"15.5\">Encoded</tspan><tspan x=\"110\" y=\"28.5\">data</tspan></text><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"50\"/><polyline points=\"238.7,53 245,50 238.7,47\"/><line x1=\"258\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"50\"/><polyline points=\"283.7,53 290,50 283.7,47\"/><text x=\"232\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"232\" y=\"38\">Signal</tspan></text><polyline points=\"262,40 270,22 266,22 276,4\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"296\" y=\"10\">Noise</tspan></text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"430\" y2=\"50\"/><polyline points=\"423.7,53 430,50 423.7,47\"/><text x=\"390\" y=\"15.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"390\" y=\"15.5\">Decoder</tspan><tspan x=\"390\" y=\"28.5\">data</tspan></text><text x=\"40\" y=\"83.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"83.5\">Network</tspan><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"96.5\">PC</tspan></text><text x=\"180\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"180\" y=\"84\">X</tspan></text><text x=\"320\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"320\" y=\"84\">Z</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"83.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"460\" y=\"83.5\">Remote</tspan><tspan x=\"460\" y=\"96.5\">server</tspan></text><path d=\"M212,80 q0,8 10,8 h26 q8,0 8,8 q0,-8 8,-8 h26 q10,0 10,-8\"/><text x=\"256\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"256\" y=\"112\">Y</tspan></text></svg><figcaption>Item 10: data passes from the network PC through X, across Y (where noise enters the signal), through Z to the remote server.</figcaption></figure></blockquote><p>What does X represent?</p>","options":["Telnet","Channel","Receiver","Transmitter"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The printed CXC key is C (receiver), but X is the device that takes the encoded data from the network PC and sends it out as a signal, so X is the <b>transmitter</b> (D). Y is the channel, where noise enters, and Z, which hands the data on to the remote server, is the receiver. Remember the order sender → transmitter → channel → receiver → destination.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>As one of his references, a student used a text book that was written in 1982. Which of the following characteristics of information sources can be challenged by his tutor?</p>","options":["Cost","Reliability","Coverage","Currency"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Currency is how up to date a source is, and a textbook from 1982 is badly out of date for most IT topics. Reliability (B) is tempting, but a published textbook from a reputable author may still be reliable; the problem the date points to is its age.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>What is the name of the first electronic computer?</p>","options":["Abacus","ENIAC","ARPANET","Calculator"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. ENIAC (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer, 1946) is generally named as the first general-purpose electronic computer. The abacus (A) is an early calculating aid, but it is mechanical (beads), not electronic. ARPANET was the forerunner of the Internet, not a computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following sequences represents the CORRECT timeline of invention for the following hardware technologies?<br>I. Transistors<br>II. Vacuum tubes<br>III. Integrated circuits</p>","options":["Transistors, Integrated circuits, Vacuum tubes","Integrated circuits, Vacuum tubes, Transistors","Vacuum tubes, Integrated circuits, Transistors","Vacuum tubes, Transistors, Integrated circuits"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The computer generations run: first generation, vacuum tubes (1940s); second, transistors (late 1950s); third, integrated circuits (1960s). Option C starts correctly with vacuum tubes but swaps the next two; integrated circuits are made of many transistors, so transistors had to come first.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>What is software engineering?</p>","options":["A discipline which is concerned with all aspects of software programming.","A discipline which is concerned with all aspects of software production.","A discipline which is concerned with all aspects of software integration.","A discipline which is concerned with all aspects of software modification."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Software engineering covers every stage of producing software, from specification and design through coding and testing to maintenance. Programming (A) is the tempting choice, but programming is only one part of production; software engineering is wider than writing code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The decimal equivalent of the ASCII representation for ‘E’ is 69. What is the ASCII decimal representation for ‘A’?</p>","options":["1","74","65","75"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Capital letters have consecutive ASCII codes, so A is four places before E: 69 − 4 = 65. Option B (74) comes from counting forwards instead of backwards (69 + 5), and option A (1) is the letter’s position in the alphabet, not its ASCII code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 16 refers to the text below.</b></p><p>http://www.cxc.org</p></blockquote><p>The text is an example of</p>","options":["IP","URL","HTML","HTTP"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is the full address of a resource on the web. HTTP (D) is tempting because \"http\" appears at the start, but that is only the protocol part of the URL; the whole text, protocol plus domain name, is the URL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following components are NOT a part of an information system?</p>","options":["Data","Reports","Software","Procedures"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The components of an information system are hardware, software, data, procedures, users (people) and the network. Reports are an output the system produces, not one of its components. Procedures (D) can look less technical, but they are a component: the rules people follow to use the system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following types of HCI is typically used by customers to order food through a drive-through lane at a fast food restaurant?</p>","options":["Menu-driven","Command-line","Graphical user","Direct"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. At a drive-through the customer chooses from a fixed list of options on the menu board, which is how a menu-driven interface works. A graphical user interface (C) is tempting if you picture pictures on the board, but a GUI uses windows, icons and a pointer, which the customer does not use.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>To convert a document from text into a coded text, ensuring data security, it is BEST to use</p>","options":["encryption","biometrics","access logs","file transfer protocol"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Encryption scrambles readable text (plaintext) into coded text (ciphertext) that only someone with the key can decode. Biometrics (B) is also a security measure, but it controls who gets in by checking a fingerprint or face; it does not change the document into code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The physical layout of a network is called a(n)</p>","options":["design","topology","presentation","implementation"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Topology is the arrangement of the devices and connections in a network, for example bus, ring or star. \"Design\" (A) is too general; a network design includes the topology but also addressing, security and hardware choices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Reports are produced monthly to show the total number of students registered per course as well as total fees paid. Year-to-date totals are also provided. The type of system that would MOST likely provide this information is a/an</p>","options":["knowledge work system","office automation system","transaction processing system","management information system"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A management information system produces regular, scheduled summary reports (monthly totals, year-to-date figures) for middle managers. A transaction processing system (C) is the tempting choice because it records each registration and payment, but it handles the day-to-day transactions; summarising them into routine reports is the job of the MIS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 22 refers to the following scenario.</b></p><p>SME College used protocols to create a network service on which it provides a set of services for its students. However, a neighbouring school has made a special request to be able to log on remotely so they can have access to some of the services.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following terms is used to describe connectivity that has been extended to another entity?</p>","options":["Internet","Intranet","Extranet","Ethernet"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. An extranet is a private network (often an intranet) that gives controlled access to selected outside parties, here the neighbouring school. Intranet (B) is the trap: the college’s own private network for its students is the intranet, but once it is opened to another organisation it becomes an extranet.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following protocols forms part of a URL?</p>","options":["TCP/IP","HTTP","SMTP","TCP"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A URL begins with its protocol, as in <b>http</b>://www.cxc.org. TCP/IP (A) is tempting because it carries all Internet traffic, but it works underneath and never appears in the address. SMTP is for sending e-mail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 24 refers to the following scenario.</b></p><p>A financial company has branches in five different locations throughout the country. At present, each location has a small, internal network for the purpose of sharing resources. Management wants each location to benefit efficiently from the resources available at all other locations.</p></blockquote><p>What is the name given to the network at each branch?</p>","options":["MAN","VPN","WAN","LAN"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The printed CXC key is A (MAN), but a small internal network inside one building or branch is a <b>LAN</b> (D). A MAN covers a city or town, and linking all five branches across the country would create a WAN (C). The scenario asks about the network at each branch, not the network management wants to build, so D is the correct answer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>What type of user interface is commonly utilized by mobile phones?</p>","options":["Graphic","Menu-driven","Command-line","Natural language"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Smartphones use a graphical user interface: icons, windows and touch, which is a form of pointing. Natural language (D) is tempting because phones have voice assistants, but these are an extra feature; the main interface people use is graphical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>When Janis, a new computer user, installed a program and attempted to use it, she was confused because all she saw on the screen was a green bar with 5 evenly spaced words. When she clicked on each of them, a list of additional words was displayed.<br><br>Which HCI is Janis MOST likely using?</p>","options":["Graphic user","Menu-driven","Event-driven","Command-line"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A bar of words, each opening a list of further choices, is a menu bar with drop-down menus, so the interface is menu-driven. Graphical user interface (A) is tempting because she clicks with a mouse, but there are no icons or windows described, only lists of words to choose from. \"Event-driven\" describes a programming approach, not a type of HCI.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>To access information on the Internet, it would be MOST appropriate to use</p>","options":["FTP","Telnet","E-mail","Browsers"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A web browser retrieves and displays web pages, which is how most information on the Internet is accessed. FTP (A) can fetch files from the Internet, but it is for transferring files, not for viewing and moving between web pages of information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Ron opened an email and downloaded a document. When he opened the document, the contents were illegible. The document was</p>","options":["encrypted","corrupted","protected","authenticated"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A document that downloads but shows unreadable, garbled contents has most likely been damaged in transfer, that is, corrupted. Encrypted (A) is the strongest distractor, because an encrypted file also looks like nonsense; however, Ron was not expecting a secured file, and an encrypted file would normally ask for a password or key rather than simply open as gibberish. A protected document would refuse to open or edit, and authenticated describes a confirmed identity, not a file’s contents.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following is NOT an example of wireless telecommunication transmission media?</p>","options":["Radio","Satellite","Microwave","Fibre optic cable"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Fibre optic cable is wired (bounded) media: light travels along a physical glass or plastic cable. Satellite (B) may seem like a \"system\" rather than a medium, but satellite links send signals through the air as microwaves, so they are wireless, as are radio and microwave.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following statements about web pages are TRUE?<br>I. They are written using HTTP.<br>II. Hyperlinks connect web pages to form a website.<br>III. They can contain multimedia content.</p>","options":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Statements II and III are true. Statement I is false: web pages are written in HTML; HTTP is the protocol used to transfer them from the server to the browser. That is why D (all three) is wrong, even though it is tempting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following could correctly represent “read name” as part of a flow chart?</p>","options":["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 150 64\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:150px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><polyline points=\"75,0 75,12\"/><polygon points=\"75,12 113,32 75,52 37,32\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"75\" y=\"26\">read</tspan><tspan x=\"75\" y=\"38\">name</tspan></text><polyline points=\"75,52 75,64\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 150 64\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:150px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"32\" rx=\"44\" ry=\"14\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"75\" y=\"32\">read name</tspan></text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 150 64\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:150px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><polyline points=\"75,0 75,12\"/><rect x=\"25\" y=\"18\" width=\"100\" height=\"28\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"75\" y=\"32\">read name</tspan></text><polyline points=\"75,52 75,64\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 150 64\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:150px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><polyline points=\"75,0 75,12\"/><polygon points=\"33,18 127,18 117,46 23,46\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"75\" y=\"32\">read name</tspan></text><polyline points=\"75,52 75,64\"/></svg>"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Input and output are shown with a parallelogram, so \"read name\" goes in a parallelogram. The rectangle (C) is the most tempting, but a rectangle is for a process such as a calculation; the diamond (A) is for a decision and the oval (B) is for start or stop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The top-level data flow diagram is called the</p>","options":["data diagram","context diagram","data store diagram","data process diagram"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The top-level (level 0) DFD is the context diagram: the whole system as one process, with the external entities and data flows around it. \"Data process diagram\" (D) sounds plausible because the context diagram shows one process, but it is not a recognised name. The more detailed diagram below it is the level 1 DFD.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The review stage in problem-solving</p>","options":["involves defining a problem","focuses on collecting data from users","involves finding the cost of implementing the system","examines the system to see if any changes are required"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Review comes after implementation: the solution is evaluated to see whether it works as intended and whether changes are needed. Option B (collecting data from users) is tempting because reviewing may involve user feedback, but collecting data is the focus of the analysis stage, not the purpose of the review.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following statement(s) is/are TRUE about algorithms?<br>I. Clearly defined and unambiguous<br>II. Infinite number of steps<br>III. Problem-solving strategy</p>","options":["II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An algorithm is a problem-solving strategy (a plan for a solution) whose steps are clearly defined and unambiguous. Statement II is false: an algorithm must finish in a <b>finite</b> number of steps. So any option containing II, including D, is wrong.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 35 refers to the table below.</b></p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Task No.</th><th>Task Name</th><th>Mon</th><th>Tue</th><th>Wed</th><th>Thur</th><th>Fri</th><th>Mon</th><th>Tue</th><th>Wed</th><th>Thur</th><th>Fri</th><th>Mon</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Discuss project</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Assign tasks</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Gather data</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sort relevant data</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Compile information</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"background:#F5C542;min-width:2.2em\" aria-label=\"scheduled\">&nbsp;</td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td><td style=\"min-width:2.2em\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Shaded cells show the days on which each task is scheduled (two working weeks plus the following Monday).</p></blockquote><p>Above is a copy of a Gantt chart used by a group of students to guide them in completing a project. How many TOTAL days will it take to complete tasks 2, 3 and 4?</p>","options":["4","5","6","11"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The printed CXC key is A (4), but the chart as shown gives 5. Reading the chart, task 2 runs on Tuesday of week 1, task 3 from Tuesday to Friday of week 1, and task 4 from Thursday of week 1 to Monday of week 2. Tasks 2 to 4 therefore take Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday and Monday: <b>5</b> working days from start to finish (B). Four days is only the length of task 3 on its own. When reading a Gantt chart, count from the first shaded cell of the earliest task to the last shaded cell of the latest task, because tasks can overlap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 36 refers to the following diagram.</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 160\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:380px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><text x=\"40\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"12\">I</tspan></text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"4\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"18\"/><polyline points=\"57,11.7 60,18 63,11.7\"/><rect x=\"48\" y=\"18\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"42\"/><polyline points=\"57,35.7 60,42 63,35.7\"/><rect x=\"48\" y=\"42\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"66\"/><polyline points=\"57,59.7 60,66 63,59.7\"/><rect x=\"48\" y=\"66\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"12\">II</tspan></text><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"4\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"28\"/><polyline points=\"187,21.7 190,28 193,21.7\"/><polygon points=\"190,28 212,48 190,68 168,48\"/><polyline points=\"168,48 150,48 150,70\"/><polyline points=\"212,48 230,48 230,70\"/><rect x=\"138\" y=\"70\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"70\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><polyline points=\"150,80 150,100 230,100 230,80\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"122\"/><polyline points=\"187,115.7 190,122 193,115.7\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"290\" y=\"12\">III</tspan></text><line x1=\"320\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"82\"/><polyline points=\"317,75.7 320,82 323,75.7\"/><polygon points=\"320,82 342,104 320,126 298,104\"/><polyline points=\"342,104 350,104 350,64\"/><rect x=\"338\" y=\"54\" width=\"24\" height=\"10\" rx=\"0\"/><polyline points=\"350,54 350,34\"/><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"326.3,31 320,34 326.3,37\"/><line x1=\"320\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"150\"/><polyline points=\"317,143.7 320,150 323,143.7\"/></svg><figcaption>Item 36: I is three process boxes in a straight line; II is a decision whose two branches each pass through a process box and then rejoin; III is a decision whose branch passes through a process box and loops back above the decision.</figcaption></figure></blockquote><p>Which of the following flowchart diagrams can be used to represent an IF-THEN-ELSE control structure?</p>","options":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"why":"CXC Key: C. Note: the printed key looks doubtful. Only diagram II is a true IF-THEN-ELSE: one decision, a separate action on each branch, and the branches rejoin. Diagram III uses a decision box, but its branch loops back above the decision, so it shows repetition (a loop), and diagram I is plain sequence. None of the options says \"II only\"; C is the CXC answer because both II and III contain a decision (selection) symbol. Learn the shapes: two branches that rejoin = selection; a line going back up = loop.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following DFDs depicts the correct flow of data as it relates to student data being stored in a student information system?</p>","options":["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 470 50\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:470px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"12\" width=\"70\" height=\"28\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"26\">Student</tspan></text><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"26\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"26\"/><polyline points=\"143.7,29 150,26 143.7,23\"/><text x=\"112\" y=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"112\" y=\"16\">Name, id</tspan></text><polyline points=\"260,14 150,14 150,38 260,38\"/><polyline points=\"166,14 166,38\"/><text x=\"213\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"213\" y=\"26\">Student file</tspan></text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 470 66\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:470px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"20\" width=\"70\" height=\"28\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"34\">Student</tspan></text><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"138.7,37 145,34 138.7,31\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"110\" y=\"24\">Name, id</tspan></text><rect x=\"145\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\"/><polyline points=\"145,20 225,20\"/><text x=\"185\" y=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"10\"><tspan x=\"185\" y=\"13\">I</tspan></text><text x=\"185\" y=\"29\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"185\" y=\"29\">Accept</tspan><tspan x=\"185\" y=\"41\">student</tspan><tspan x=\"185\" y=\"53\">info</tspan></text><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"305\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"298.7,37 305,34 298.7,31\"/><text x=\"265\" y=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"265\" y=\"10\">Student</tspan><tspan x=\"265\" y=\"22\">details</tspan></text><polyline points=\"415,22 305,22 305,46 415,46\"/><polyline points=\"321,22 321,46\"/><text x=\"368\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"368\" y=\"34\">Student file</tspan></text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 470 60\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:470px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"18\" width=\"70\" height=\"28\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"40\" y=\"32\">Student</tspan></text><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"32\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"32\"/><polyline points=\"138.7,35 145,32 138.7,29\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"110\" y=\"22\">Name, id</tspan></text><rect x=\"145\" y=\"8\" width=\"70\" height=\"48\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"180\" y=\"32\"></tspan></text><text x=\"180\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"180\" y=\"20\">data</tspan><tspan x=\"180\" y=\"32\">entry</tspan><tspan x=\"180\" y=\"44\">clerk</tspan></text><line x1=\"215\" y1=\"32\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"32\"/><polyline points=\"288.7,35 295,32 288.7,29\"/><text x=\"255\" y=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"255\" y=\"8\">Student</tspan><tspan x=\"255\" y=\"20\">details</tspan></text><polyline points=\"405,20 295,20 295,44 405,44\"/><polyline points=\"311,20 311,44\"/><text x=\"358\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"358\" y=\"32\">Student file</tspan></text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 66\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><polyline points=\"105,22 5,22 5,46 105,46\"/><polyline points=\"21,22 21,46\"/><text x=\"63\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"63\" y=\"34\">Student file</tspan></text><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"163.7,37 170,34 163.7,31\"/><text x=\"137\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"137\" y=\"24\">Name, id</tspan></text><rect x=\"170\" y=\"20\" width=\"70\" height=\"28\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"205\" y=\"34\">Student</tspan></text><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"293.7,37 300,34 293.7,31\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"270\" y=\"24\">Name, id</tspan></text><rect x=\"300\" y=\"10\" width=\"70\" height=\"48\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"335\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"335\" y=\"34\"></tspan></text><text x=\"335\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"335\" y=\"22\">data</tspan><tspan x=\"335\" y=\"34\">entry</tspan><tspan x=\"335\" y=\"46\">clerk</tspan></text><line x1=\"370\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"34\"/><polyline points=\"433.7,37 440,34 433.7,31\"/><text x=\"405\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"405\" y=\"24\">Name, id</tspan></text><rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"90\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\"/><polyline points=\"440,20 530,20\"/><text x=\"485\" y=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"10\"><tspan x=\"485\" y=\"13\">I</tspan></text><text x=\"485\" y=\"29\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"485\" y=\"29\">Accept</tspan><tspan x=\"485\" y=\"41\">student</tspan><tspan x=\"485\" y=\"53\">info</tspan></text></svg>"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. In a DFD, data must pass through a process before it reaches a data store: Student (external entity) → Accept student info (process) → Student file (data store). Option C is tempting because it has something in the middle, but \"data entry clerk\" is a person (an entity), not a process, and an entity must not write straight to a data store. Option A also links an entity directly to a store.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 38 refers to the following statement.</b></p><span style=\"font-family:monospace;display:block;line-height:1.45\">FOR J FROM 20 to 15 DO<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;WRITE J<br>END FOR</span></blockquote><p>What values would be printed for J?</p>","options":["20, 19, 18, 17, 16, 15","15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20","20, 19, 18, 17, 16","19, 18, 17, 16"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The loop starts at 20 and counts down to 15, and both end values are included, so it writes 20, 19, 18, 17, 16, 15. Option C misses the final value 15; the loop runs while J has not gone past 15, so 15 is still printed. (Strictly, many programming languages need \"STEP −1\" to count down; without it the loop would not run at all. CXC expects you to read the loop as counting down.)","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Into which of the following paradigms are the programming languages C++ classified?</p>","options":["Declarative","Functional","Procedural","Object-oriented"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. C++ was designed as \"C with classes\" and is classified as an object-oriented language. Procedural (C) is tempting because C++ grew out of C, a procedural language, and can still be written procedurally, but its defining paradigm is object-oriented.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 40 refers to the flowchart below.</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 310\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:340px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"26\" rx=\"13\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"19\">START</tspan></text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"32\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"46\"/><polyline points=\"137,39.7 140,46 143,39.7\"/><polygon points=\"93,46 195,46 187,82 85,82\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"57.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"57.5\">Read Num1</tspan><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"70.5\">Read Num2</tspan></text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"94\"/><polyline points=\"137,87.7 140,94 143,87.7\"/><polygon points=\"140,94 200,118 140,142 80,118\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"118\">Num1&gt;Num2</tspan></text><text x=\"150\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"start\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"150\" y=\"150\">Y</tspan></text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"160\"/><polyline points=\"137,153.7 140,160 143,153.7\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"160\" width=\"108\" height=\"22\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"171\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"171\">Num1 = Num2</tspan></text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"182\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"196\"/><polyline points=\"137,189.7 140,196 143,189.7\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"196\" width=\"108\" height=\"22\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"207\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"207\">Num2 = Num1</tspan></text><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"218\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"232\"/><polyline points=\"137,225.7 140,232 143,225.7\"/><polygon points=\"84,232 204,232 196,256 76,256\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"244\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"244\">Print Num1, Num2</tspan></text><polyline points=\"140,256 140,270\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"110\">N</tspan></text><polyline points=\"200,118 270,118\"/><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"152\"/><polyline points=\"267,145.7 270,152 273,145.7\"/><polygon points=\"228,152 320,152 312,188 220,188\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"163.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"270\" y=\"163.5\">Print Num2,</tspan><tspan x=\"270\" y=\"176.5\">Num1</tspan></text><polyline points=\"270,188 270,270\"/><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"270\"/><polyline points=\"146.3,267 140,270 146.3,273\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"270\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"280\"/><polyline points=\"137,273.7 140,280 143,273.7\"/><rect x=\"100\" y=\"280\" width=\"80\" height=\"26\" rx=\"13\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"293\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"140\" y=\"293\">STOP</tspan></text></svg><figcaption>Item 40 flowchart.</figcaption></figure></blockquote><p>Which of the following is the correct pseudocode for the algorithm?</p>","options":["<span style=\"font-family:monospace;display:block;line-height:1.45\">READ Num1,Num2<br>If Num1 > Num2 Then<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num1=Num2<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num2=Num1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num1,Num2<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num2, Num1<br>Endif</span>","<span style=\"font-family:monospace;display:block;line-height:1.45\">READ Num1,Num2<br>If Num1 < Num2 Then<br>Print Num2, Num1<br>Else<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num1=Num2<br>Num2=Num1<br>Print Num1,Num2<br>Endif</span>","<span style=\"font-family:monospace;display:block;line-height:1.45\">READ Num1,Num2<br>If Num1 > Num2 Then<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num2,Num1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num1=Num2<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num2=Num1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num2, Num1<br>Endif</span>","<span style=\"font-family:monospace;display:block;line-height:1.45\">READ Num1,Num2<br>If Num1 > Num2 Then<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num1=Num2<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Num2=Num1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num1,Num2<br>ELSE<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Print Num2, Num1<br>Endif</span>"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. On the Y (true) branch the flowchart does Num1 = Num2, then Num2 = Num1, then prints Num1, Num2; on the N (false) branch it only prints Num2, Num1. Option D matches this exactly with IF–ELSE. Option A is tempting because its first lines match, but it has no ELSE, so both print statements run when the condition is true and nothing runs when it is false. Option B uses &lt; instead of &gt;, which wrongly sends the case where the numbers are equal to the swap branch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>To gather information from 700 users about the usefulness of a particular system, which of the following tools would be MOST efficient?</p>","options":["Investigation","Observation","Questionnaires","Interviews"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Questionnaires can be sent to all 700 users at once and the answers gathered and analysed quickly. Interviews (D) give richer detail but would take far too long with 700 people, and observation shows what users do rather than what they think of the system’s usefulness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which line of the following algorithm illustrates the control structure of iteration?</p>","options":["read num","if 0 &lt; num &lt; 10 then<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;num = num + 1","while 0 &lt; num &lt; 10 do","print num"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The printed CXC key is B, but iteration means repetition, and the only line that repeats is the <b>while</b> loop (C): \"while 0 &lt; num &lt; 10 do\" keeps running its body as long as the condition is true. The \"if ... then\" line (B) is selection; it tests the condition once and runs its statement at most once.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 43 refers to the diagram below.</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 250\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:300px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"22\" rx=\"38\" ry=\"15\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"22\">Start</tspan></text><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"58\"/><polyline points=\"77,51.7 80,58 83,51.7\"/><polygon points=\"48,59 120,59 112,89 40,89\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"74\">Input X</tspan></text><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"89\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"104\"/><polyline points=\"77,97.7 80,104 83,97.7\"/><polygon points=\"80,104 114,136 80,168 46,136\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"129.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"129.5\">IS</tspan><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"142.5\">X&gt;100</tspan></text><line x1=\"114\" y1=\"136\" x2=\"158\" y2=\"136\"/><polyline points=\"151.7,139 158,136 151.7,133\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"130\" y=\"126\">Y</tspan></text><polygon points=\"165,119 267,119 259,153 157,153\"/><text x=\"212\" y=\"129.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"212\" y=\"129.5\">Print</tspan><tspan x=\"212\" y=\"142.5\">(‘Error’)</tspan></text><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"168\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"196\"/><polyline points=\"77,189.7 80,196 83,189.7\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"start\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"90\" y=\"182\">N</tspan></text><polygon points=\"48,197 120,197 112,227 40,227\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"212\">Print X</tspan></text><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"212\" x2=\"158\" y2=\"212\"/><polyline points=\"151.7,215 158,212 151.7,209\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"212\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"16\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"212\">Stop</tspan></text></svg><figcaption>Item 43 flowchart.</figcaption></figure></blockquote><p>From the flow chart above, what action takes place if X &gt; 100?</p>","options":["An error message is printed.","Nothing takes place.","X is printed.","The flow chart is terminated."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. When X &gt; 100 the decision follows the Y (yes) branch to Print (‘Error’), so an error message is displayed. Option C is the trap: X is printed only on the N (no) branch, when X is 100 or less.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Items 44–45 refer to the following diagram.</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 390\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:400px;height:auto;display:block\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"inherit\" role=\"img\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"6\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"21\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"21\">Start</tspan></text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"52\"/><polyline points=\"197,45.7 200,52 203,45.7\"/><rect x=\"165\" y=\"52\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"65.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"65.5\">Input</tspan><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"78.5\">A, B</tspan></text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"108\"/><polyline points=\"197,101.7 200,108 203,101.7\"/><polygon points=\"200,108 240,138 200,168 160,138\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"131.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"131.5\">Is</tspan><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"144.5\">A &gt; B ?</tspan></text><polyline points=\"160,138 80,138 80,170\"/><text x=\"118\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"118\" y=\"128\">N</tspan></text><polyline points=\"240,138 320,138 320,170\"/><text x=\"282\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"282\" y=\"128\">Y</tspan></text><rect x=\"25\" y=\"170\" width=\"110\" height=\"26\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"183\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"80\" y=\"183\">B = B + A × 2</tspan></text><rect x=\"265\" y=\"170\" width=\"110\" height=\"26\" rx=\"0\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"183\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"320\" y=\"183\">A = A + B × 2</tspan></text><polyline points=\"80,196 80,228 320,228 320,196\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"228\" r=\"3.5\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"231\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"256\"/><polyline points=\"197,249.7 200,256 203,249.7\"/><polygon points=\"113,256 305,256 287,316 95,316\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"271\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"271\">OUTPUT</tspan><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"286\">‘The value of A is, ’ A</tspan><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"301\">‘The value of B is, ’ B</tspan></text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"316\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"340\"/><polyline points=\"197,333.7 200,340 203,333.7\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"340\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-weight=\"bold\"><tspan x=\"200\" y=\"355\">End</tspan></text></svg><figcaption>Items 44–45 flowchart.</figcaption></figure></blockquote><p>What is the final value of A and B when A = 4, B = 3 is the input?</p>","options":["A = 4, B = 1","A = 10, B = 3","A = 10, B = 6","A = 10, B = 11"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A = 4 is greater than B = 3, so the Y branch runs: A = A + B × 2 = 4 + 6 = 10. B is not changed on this branch, so it stays 3. Option D (B = 11) comes from also running the N branch with the new A (3 + 4 × 2 = 11), but only one branch of a decision is taken.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>What control structures are used in the data flow diagrams shown?<br>I. Loop<br>II. Sequence<br>III. Selection</p>","options":["I only","II only","I and III only","II and III only"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The steps run in order (input, decide, calculate, output), which is sequence, and the \"Is A &gt; B?\" decision chooses between two branches, which is selection. There is no loop (I) because no line goes back to an earlier step, so C is wrong. (The item says \"data flow diagrams\", but the diagram is a flowchart; CXC’s wording is kept.)","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_7ui","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 1","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 1</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1–15 Module 1, 16–30 Module 2, 31–45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Coralstone College open day features a computer engineer, a computer scientist, a software engineer, an IT specialist and an IS analyst. A visitor asks what all five have in common. Which answer is a genuine commonality?</p>","options":["Logical problem-solving","Designing processors","Aligning technology with business strategy","Proving the limits of computation"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. All five use logical problem-solving. Each other option is the specialism of one discipline (CE, IS and CS respectively).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Harbourview Credit Union in Roseau uses software to approve loans automatically. A member whose home was damaged in a hurricane is rejected, although staff believe she will repay. Which limitation of IT does this show?</p>","options":["It cannot exercise human judgement in situations its programs do not cover.","It depends on a reliable power supply.","It processes large volumes of applications quickly.","It becomes obsolete quickly."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The program applies its rules but cannot weigh the member's special circumstances: a lack of human judgement. Dependence on power is a real limitation, but nothing failed here; option C is an advantage of IT, not a limitation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Form 6 student is writing a label for a photo of ENIAC (1945) in a Seagrape Bay Secondary School class display. Which characteristic should she list as MOST closely associated with this first-generation computer?</p>","options":["Magnetic core memory and COBOL","Graphical user interfaces","High heat output and frequent component failure","Very large-scale integration"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. ENIAC's thousands of vacuum tubes produced great heat and burned out often. Core memory and COBOL belong to the second generation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In 1982 the owner of a hardware store in Bridgetown, Barbados wants to keep her accounts on a computer, but she cannot program and has no access to a mainframe. Which development in software history MOST allowed users like her to use computers?</p>","options":["Plugboard programming","Packaged application software such as spreadsheets and word processors","Machine language","Batch processing on mainframes"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Packages such as VisiCalc and WordStar let the store owner work on a PC without writing programs. Batch processing still required specialists and a mainframe.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Sorting a cupboard at the Linden Community Centre, a volunteer finds index cards on which \"Blue\", \"17\" and \"Regent Street\" are each written on a separate card, with nothing linking them. In the sense used in the CAPE IT syllabus, these cards hold</p>","options":["information, because the items can be compared","information, because each card has been written down","knowledge, because a volunteer can use them to decide","discrete data"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The items are separate, unrelated chunks with no connection or context: discrete data. B and A are tempting, but writing items down or being able to compare them does not process them; nothing has been organised or put in context. D confuses possible use with knowledge, which requires information used to make a decision.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Trinidadian Road Survey Team counts vehicles on the San Fernando hill for 10 minutes in every hour instead of continuously. The data collected is BEST described as</p>","options":["detailed and qualitative","detailed and quantitative","sampled and quantitative","sampled and qualitative"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Counting is an objective measurement (quantitative), and only selected periods are recorded (sampled). B is tempting because counts are quantitative, but the data is not recorded continuously.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Shania, a student at Sea-Almond Secondary School, types \"Caribbean history\" into her school library's catalogue and gets a list of titles, authors and shelf numbers. The catalogue she used is BEST described as</p>","options":["a periodical containing research articles","a primary source of information about an event","an organised list that describes items and shows where they can be found","a collection of web pages edited by many users"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The catalogue is a finding aid that describes items and their location so Shania can fetch them. B describes a journal; C describes a wiki; B is wrong because a catalogue does not report an event.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Kayla, a Lower Six student, interviews her grandmother in Sandy Ground, Anguilla, about how the family got news during Hurricane Irma in 2017. Which pair gives one advantage and one disadvantage of this PERSON as an information source?</p>","options":["Always unbiased; always available","Follow-up questions can be asked; memories may be inaccurate","Large storage capacity; read-only","Permanent record; needs electricity"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Kayla can ask follow-up questions (advantage), but her grandmother's memories of 2017 may have faded or changed (disadvantage). A is wrong because people can be biased and are not always available; C describes a CD-ROM.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>For her project on the environment, a Breadfruit Valley Secondary student finds a specialist journal article that studies mangrove loss in one lagoon over twenty years with extensive data. Which characteristics BEST describe it?</p>","options":["Narrow breadth, little depth","Wide breadth, little depth","Narrow breadth, great depth","Wide breadth, great depth"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. One lagoon is a narrow coverage; twenty years of extensive data is great depth. Option B describes an encyclopaedia-style overview, the opposite.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A student writing a newspaper-style article on household electricity bills in The Bahamas finds a source on tariffs that is available free online, is easy to search, and was published eight years ago. When selecting it to quote CURRENT tariffs, the student should be MOST concerned about its</p>","options":["cost","availability","currency","format"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Tariffs change, so eight-year-old figures fail the currency criterion. Availability and cost are favourable here and are not the concern.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>At Sea-Almond Secondary School, the head of Mathematics reviews the term mark sheet and asks teachers to record marks for each question, not just totals, from next term. This is an example of which stage of the information processing cycle?</p>","options":["Output","Processing","Feedback","Storage"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Evaluating the output and changing what is collected at input is feedback. Output is tempting because a mark sheet is involved, but the action described is the evaluation and update that loops back to input.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The owner of the Sunset Travel Agency in Gros Islet is comparing the systems used by businesses she deals with. Which of these is an example of an AUTOMATED information system?</p>","options":["A handwritten ledger in a corner shop","An airline reservation system shared by agents and websites","A card catalogue in a school library","A paper register marked each morning by a form teacher"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A reservation system is computer-based and shared over networks, so the agency can book seats instantly. The card catalogue is a common distractor because libraries can be automated, but a card catalogue itself is manual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Bayside Resort in Grace Bay plans to replace its paper payroll ledger with a payroll database. The finance manager warns that new computers, software and staff training must all be paid for before any savings are seen. Which disadvantage of automated processing is she describing?</p>","options":["Stored data cannot be reused","Dependence on technology increases laziness","Failure can endanger human life","It is costly to set up"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Paying for hardware, software and training before any benefit is the cost of setting up. Option B is tempting because staff will rely on the system, but the manager's warning is about money spent at the start, and option A reverses a benefit: automated data is reusable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Island Link Ferry Company stores the number of passengers on its smallest water taxi in a 6-bit field. What is the largest passenger count the field can hold?</p>","options":["64","36","32","63"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 6 bits give 2⁶ = 64 patterns, numbered 0 to 63, so the largest passenger count is 63 (111111). 64 is the number of patterns, not the largest value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Harbour View Hotel wants to show its board what share of guests came from each region last year. Which representation is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A paragraph of text","An audio recording","A line graph","A pie chart"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A pie chart shows parts of a whole, which is exactly what \"share of guests\" means. A line graph is tempting, but it is used for change over time, not proportions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The CEO of Caribe Marts group opens a single screen of charts comparing group sales in Antigua, Barbuda and St. Kitts with regional visitor arrivals, then taps one island's figure to see its individual stores. Which type of information system is she using?</p>","options":["Transaction Processing System","Executive Information System","Management Information System","Expert system"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A senior executive viewing a graphical summary of internal and external data, with drill-down to individual stores, is using an EIS. C is tempting because an MIS also summarises, but it serves middle managers with routine internal reports.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Bayview Supermarket in Holetown offers credit accounts to local restaurants. At month end its system lists ONLY those customers whose accounts are more than 60 days overdue. Which type of report is this?</p>","options":["Exception","Detailed","Summarised","Ad hoc"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Only records meeting a condition outside normal limits are shown, which defines an exception report. B is tempting because each customer is listed individually, but a detailed report would list every account, not only overdue ones.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The office computer at the Harbour Gems Jewellers has an old hard disk. A technician runs a program that gathers the scattered parts of each file into one continuous area so files load faster. This program is BEST classified as</p>","options":["an operating system","application software","a language translator","a utility program"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The technician’s program is a disk defragmenter: it performs one maintenance task, so it is a utility. It is often bundled with the operating system, which makes B tempting, but it is not the OS itself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>An engineer at Caribbean Pump Works in Trinidad writes the code for a water-pump controller using mnemonics such as LOAD, ADD and STORE, then runs an assembler on it. The assembler</p>","options":["loads the operating system when the controller starts","translates a high-level program one statement at a time","copies the code files to a backup device","translates the assembly language mnemonics into machine code"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. An assembler translates the engineer’s low-level assembly language into machine code. A describes an interpreter, a common confusion because both are translators.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The managing director of Windward Air, based at Piarco Airport, studies a chart of passenger numbers over ten years together with regional tourism forecasts. At which decision-making level is this information MOST likely used?</p>","options":["Tactical","Strategic","Operational","Transactional"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Long-term, summarised, external and future-oriented information supports strategic decisions. A is tempting because charts and summaries also appear at the tactical level, but ten-year trends and external forecasts point to top management.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Frigate Bay Marina replaces its command line booking system with a graphical user interface after newly hired dock staff struggle to use it. Which disadvantage of the command line BEST explains this change?</p>","options":["Users must remember commands and their exact syntax.","It cannot be used to automate tasks.","It needs more memory and processing power.","It is slower for expert users."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The new dock staff are novices, and recalling exact commands makes a CLI hard for them. A and D are the reverse of the truth: a CLI uses fewer resources and is faster for experts; B is wrong because scripts automate tasks.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet redesigns its customer registration form so all labels line up on one edge and all input boxes sit in a single column. This design follows the principle of</p>","options":["ease of navigation","use of colour","alignment","ease of learning"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Placing elements on shared lines is alignment. Ease of learning (D) may improve as a result, but the principle being applied is alignment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Saba Dive Shop's booking website shows full dates in red and available dates in green, with no other indicator. Which customers are MOST disadvantaged when they try to book a dive?</p>","options":["Users with limited mobility","Users with red-green colour-vision deficiency","Users with hearing impairments","Expert users"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. They cannot tell the two colours apart, so the meaning is lost. Hearing impairment (B) is not relevant because the information is visual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>An accountant at the Portsmouth Marine Supplies uses her home Internet connection to reach the office server. Her data is encrypted in a \"tunnel\" before it leaves her laptop. What is she using?</p>","options":["A peer-to-peer network","A metropolitan area network (MAN)","A virtual private network (VPN)","A public Wi-Fi hotspot"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. An encrypted tunnel over a public network such as the Internet is a VPN. B covers a city, C is a public place offering Wi-Fi (and would be riskier without a VPN) and D is a configuration of equal computers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Frigate Bay Tours office upgrades its Internet connection from 20 Mbps to 200 Mbps so that all staff can stream training videos at once. Which statement about the characteristic that has improved is correct?</p>","options":["It is the layout of nodes in a network","It is measured in bits per second for digital channels","It is the direction in which data flows","It is the process of varying a carrier wave"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The office has increased its bandwidth, the channel's capacity, which is measured in bits per second. A is topology, B is communication mode and D is modulation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Ms. Richardson, a teacher at Hummingbird Ridge High School, finishes a report and clicks Send in her email program. Which protocol carries the email from her computer to the school’s mail server?</p>","options":["POP3","IMAP","SMTP","FTP"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. SMTP sends email from Ms. Richardson’s client to the mail server. POP3 and IMAP are tempting but they retrieve email from the server; FTP transfers files.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The administrator at the English Harbour Car Rental office discovers that the router still uses its factory login, \"admin\". Which security measure should be applied FIRST to stop staff and customers changing the router settings?</p>","options":["Enabling MAC filtering","Changing the router password","Renaming the SSID","Switching from WPA2 to WPA3"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The router (administrator) password protects the settings page, so changing \"admin\" closes the gap at the car rental office. WPA3 is tempting, but it protects wireless traffic, not access to the router configuration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A technician at the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet wants data sent over the staff Wi-Fi to be scrambled, so that a customer capturing the signal in the shop cannot read it. Which setting is the encryption algorithm that does this?</p>","options":["DHCP","MAC filtering","SSID","AES"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. AES is the encryption algorithm used by WPA2 and WPA3 to scramble the pharmacy's wireless data. MAC filtering is tempting because it is also a security setting, but it controls which devices may join; it does not encrypt data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The manager of the Ambergris Caye Ferry Desk notes that the RAM in the office desktop can be upgraded in five minutes, but the RAM in a sealed laptop cannot be changed at all. Which feature of IS components is she MOST directly comparing?</p>","options":["Maintainability","Transmission","Storage","Efficiency"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Ease of repair and upgrade is maintainability. Storage is tempting because RAM holds data, but the manager is comparing how easily the part can be changed, not its capacity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>When teachers at Bayleaf Hill Secondary in Port of Spain enter end-of-term marks, the grade system rejects any mark below 0 or above 100. This check mainly helps to maintain</p>","options":["authentication","data integrity","encryption","data security"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The 0 to 100 rule is a range check, a form of validation, which keeps data accurate and so protects data integrity. Data security is tempting, but security concerns unauthorised access or loss, not whether entered values are reasonable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Grade 12 class at Calabash Point Secondary is reviewing four lines written about local problems around the Caribbean. Which line describes only a SYMPTOM rather than stating the problem?</p>","options":["Whitefly is cutting the farmer's harvest by a third, and she will lose her contracts if it continues.","Double bookings leave five groups a week waiting, which will cost the company customers.","Tomato leaves are curling and turning yellow.","Patients are turned away twice a week because stock levels are unknown, so they may miss medication."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Curling, yellow leaves are a visible sign only; they do not say who is affected, the cause, or the consequence. B, C and D each contain the parts of a problem statement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Six months after installing a new fuel-ordering system, the Sea Breeze Marina finds boats still waiting for fuel on busy weekends, so the manager goes back to analyse the causes again. Why is evaluation and review said to make problem-solving a cycle?</p>","options":["It repeats the implementation stage exactly.","It must be completed before the problem is defined.","Its feedback may send the problem-solver back to an earlier stage.","It replaces the need to analyse the problem."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The marina's review showed the problem was not fully solved, and that feedback sent the manager back to analysis. B reverses the order of the stages: evaluation and review comes last, not before definition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Curaçao hotel is deciding whether to build an online booking system. A manager's claim that \"guests prefer to book online\", with no booking figures behind it, is BEST classified as</p>","options":["essential information","extraneous information","desirable information","cosmetic information"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. It looks like useful demand information but masks the missing booking figures, so it cannot guide the decision. Desirable is tempting, but desirable information genuinely helps the decision; this claim only appears to.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A sales report given to the manager of the Island Hardware chain in St. Lucia shows figures for only three of its four branches. The report is MOST weakened by a lack of</p>","options":["completeness","currency","cultural context","bias"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A branch is missing, so the information is incomplete. Accuracy may come to mind, but the figures that are present may be correct; the problem is the gap.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Sandbox Tree College student needs evidence on how sea temperature affects coral near Tobago. She has a tourism magazine feature, a dive shop's blog, a local newspaper report and an article in a scholarly marine science journal. Applying the level of scholarship criterion, which should carry the MOST weight?</p>","options":["All four equally, because they cover the same topic","The journal article, because it is written for specialists and gives in-depth analysis","The magazine feature, because it is the easiest to understand","The dive shop blog, because the owner dives there every week"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Scholarly publications are written for specialists and give in-depth analysis, so they should carry the main weight in research that needs evidence. Option C is tempting because general-audience material is easier to read, but it may lack in-depth analysis.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Nevis Ferry Line wants its booking system to go live within six weeks with seat booking only, adding online payment and a loyalty scheme later. Which model BEST suits this?</p>","options":["A single big-bang release","Throwaway prototyping","Waterfall","Iterative/incremental"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Delivering the core (seat booking) first and adding parts later is incremental development. Throwaway prototyping (C) clarifies requirements but does not deliver a working system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The Port of Spain Water Taxi booking system was delivered as seat booking, then payments, then loyalty points, and its booking screen was reworked over several cycles. Which statement CORRECTLY distinguishes iterative from incremental development?</p>","options":["Iterative and incremental development are identical terms","Iterative development delivers the system once, whereas incremental development never delivers a complete system","Iterative development is used only for hardware, whereas incremental development is used only for software","Iterative development repeats the cycle to refine the product, whereas incremental development delivers the system in parts that add functionality"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Iterative = repeating and refining; incremental = adding working parts. Option A is a common misconception because the two are often used together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Portsmouth Water Taxi compares the USD 18,000 cost of an online ticketing system with the USD 9,000 a year it should save in cash handling. This cost-benefit analysis is MOST closely associated with which type of feasibility?</p>","options":["Economic","Operational","Schedule","Technical"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Asking whether benefits justify cost is economic feasibility. Operational (C) asks whether the system fits existing procedures, such as the boarding routine, not money.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Sunrise Canteen app at a Roseau secondary school lets students order meal vouchers, entered as a whole number from 1 to 50. Which set contains ONLY boundary test data?</p>","options":["25 and 30","\"ten\" and -3","0 and 51","1 and 50"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. 1 and 50 are at the edges of the valid range. C is tempting, but 0 and 51 lie just outside the range, so they are erroneous data the system should reject.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Guadeloupe Ferry Service sets fares using five yes/no conditions: resident? under 12? over 65? return trip? peak season? The analyst records the rule in a limited-entry decision table. How many rules does the complete table contain?</p>","options":["25","10","32","16"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Rules = 2 to the power 5 = 32. B (25) is tempting for candidates who square the number of conditions instead of raising 2 to that power.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Before any code is written for a Port of Spain supermarket's checkout system, a developer at the Trinidad Web Works writes \"IF total > 500 THEN apply discount ENDIF\" and draws a matching diagram of diamonds and boxes. In which stage of the SDLC are these tools MAINLY being used?</p>","options":["Implementation","Feasibility study","Design","Review"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The developer's pseudocode and flowchart represent the logic of the solution before it is coded, which is design. Implementation (A) is tempting but concerns installing the finished system and changeover.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The context diagram for the Blue Channel Ferries booking system shows six external data flows. The analyst now draws the level 1 DFD and must make sure it is balanced. A level 1 DFD is balanced with its context level DFD when it</p>","options":["has no processes that read from data stores","has the same number of data stores as entities","has exactly the same number of processes","has the same external entities and the same input and output data flows"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. All six ferry flows must reappear with the same entities. C is tempting, but the context level has one process and level 1 has several, so the numbers are never the same.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>At Frigate Bay Hardware, process 2.0 \"Check stock\" receives an item code from Customer and reads stock levels from D1 Stock, but the draft DFD shows no outgoing data flow. What error is this?</p>","options":["Process labelled with a noun","Miracle","Flow labelled with a verb","Black hole"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The stock check result disappears: inputs but no outputs is a black hole. A (miracle) is the reverse pattern and is the most common confusion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Frangipani Hill Academy marks program must add a student's two coursework marks, mark1 and mark2, and store the result in a variable called total. Which pseudocode statement does this correctly?</p>","options":["IF total THEN mark1 + mark2","INPUT mark1 + mark2","total ← mark1 + mark2","OUTPUT total ← mark1"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The assignment arrow stores the value on the right in the variable on the left. Option B is tempting because it mentions both marks, but INPUT reads data; it does not calculate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The Jamaican courier firm Island Parcels gives its drivers the instruction \"If the parcel is heavy, charge extra.\" Which change would make the step clearly defined and unambiguous?</p>","options":["If the parcel seems heavy to the driver, charge a fair amount extra.","If the parcel weighs more than 5 kg, add $10 to the charge.","Charge extra for some parcels.","If the parcel is very heavy, charge a lot extra."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. An exact weight and an exact amount give one meaning only. Option D is tempting because \"very\" sounds more precise, but it is still vague.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_80s","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 2","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 2</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1–15 Module 1, 16–30 Module 2, 31–45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A cruise passenger's tablet bought in Germany connects instantly to the free network at the Castries cruise pier, even though the pier's access points come from a different manufacturer. Both follow IEEE 802.11. This standard defines</p>","options":["the rules of the A.M. Turing Award","wired Ethernet networking","Wi-Fi wireless networking","Chartered IT Professional status"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. IEEE 802.11 defines Wi-Fi, which lets the tablet and the pier's access points work together. Wired Ethernet is the most tempting distractor, but that is IEEE 802.3; the Turing Award belongs to ACM and CITP to BCS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Darnell, a Computer Science student from Dominica, is told by his lecturer to join the Association for Computing Machinery (ACM) as a student member. ACM is BEST described as</p>","options":["an educational and scientific computing society that publishes research and promotes ethics","an organisation for Information Systems academics only","a body that sets electrical safety standards for power utilities","a UK chartered institute that licenses IT professionals by law"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. ACM is the largest educational and scientific computing society, with a Digital Library and Code of Ethics that would serve Darnell well. Option B describes AIS more closely, and option D confuses ACM with BCS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>In 1965 a university physics laboratory buys a cabinet-sized DEC PDP-8. It costs far less than a mainframe and is shared only by staff in that one department. The PDP-8 is BEST classified as a</p>","options":["microcomputer","supercomputer","mainframe","minicomputer"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A cabinet-sized, relatively cheap machine serving one department is the classic minicomputer. It predates the microprocessor, so it is not a microcomputer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>An engineering student at Flamboyant Heights College reads that the Intel 4004 (1971) held a complete central processing unit on one small piece of silicon. Which development does this describe?</p>","options":["The transistor","Magnetic core memory","The vacuum tube","The microprocessor"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A CPU on one chip is a microprocessor. The transistor is a single switch, the building block of chips.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Island Pulse news website in Jamaica starts a graph's vertical axis at 98 so that a 2% rise in supermarket prices looks like a dramatic jump. This is an example of information that is</p>","options":["disguised","incomplete","inconsistent","incomprehensible"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The figures are accurate but Island Pulse presents them misleadingly, hiding their true size, so the information is disguised. Inconsistency (B) would require two sources that contradict each other.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Caribbean Credit Check Ltd collects repayment histories and charges banks in Barbados and St. Kitts a fee for each credit report it supplies. Which of the following BEST explains why this information is a commodity?</p>","options":["It can be shared with many banks without being used up.","It is bought, sold and traded for value.","It loses its usefulness as repayment histories change.","It can be presented as a table or as a chart."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The company sells the reports for a fee: a commodity is something traded for value. B describes shareability, the most tempting distractor because many banks receive reports; C describes lifespan; D describes format.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Breadfruit Valley Secondary in the Guyana interior has no Internet access. Which of the following is an ADVANTAGE of a DVD of science lessons as an information source for this school?</p>","options":["Its content updates automatically.","It needs no player or computer.","Users can edit its content.","It can deliver video and audio offline."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A DVD stores multimedia and works without the Internet, which suits the school. A is wrong because disc content is fixed; B is tempting, but the school still needs a DVD player or computer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Mr. Richardson, the owner of a small electronics shop on Bridge Street, wants a readable, illustrated review of the latest laptops before he orders stock. Which source is MOST suitable for him?</p>","options":["Raw sales data from a supplier","A research journal on processor design","A technology magazine","A library catalogue"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Magazines are written for general readers, with reviews and illustrations. B is tempting because it is expert, but journals are technical and written for specialists, not a shop owner choosing stock.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Breadfruit Valley Secondary student finds a page claiming to be the official exam timetable. It has no author's name and was changed twice last week. Which characteristic of information on the Internet MOST affects whether she should select it?</p>","options":["Online information is never biased.","All online content is peer reviewed.","Content can be published by anyone and changed or removed at any time.","Online content is always more accurate than print."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The anonymous, frequently changed page shows the lack of gatekeeping and changeable content that define Internet information, so she should confirm the timetable with the school. The other options are common misconceptions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Harbourview Planning Group is preparing a public briefing on a hypothetical extension to the cruise pier in Kingston. Which source is MOST likely to show bias on the benefits of the extension?</p>","options":["A press release from the company contracted to build the extension","A statistics table of cruise passenger arrivals by year","A dictionary definition of a cruise pier","A refereed study of cruise tourism’s economic effects"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The building contractor gains from the project going ahead, so it has a clear interest. The refereed study (D) is tempting because it is on the same topic, but independent expert review reduces bias.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Dominica Carnival Committee has finished processing the parade results. Volunteers now post the results on the committee's website, send them by SMS to every troupe leader and pin a printed copy at the Carnival Village. Which activity is being carried out?</p>","options":["Collating the scores from several judges","Capturing data at the point where it occurs","Dissemination of information to its users","Storing the results for later retrieval"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Posting, texting and pinning up the results distributes information to its audience, which is dissemination (part of output). Option A describes collating, which is often confused with dissemination because both involve moving data around, but collating brings data together rather than sending it out.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Bayside Property Agency in Bridgetown keeps 10,000 paper property records filed by owner surname and is considering moving them to a searchable database. Which statement about manual and automated retrieval is CORRECT?</p>","options":["Manual retrieval is equally fast for any field in the record.","Manual retrieval is fast only by the filing key, while automated retrieval can search by any field.","Automated retrieval requires records to be physically re-sorted first.","Automated retrieval can only be performed by one user at a time."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The agency's paper file has one fixed order, so only the surname key gives fast retrieval; a database can query any field, such as street or price. Option C is a misconception carried over from paper systems: software sorts or filters without moving stored data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Every month the Island Savings Bank in Georgetown runs the same interest calculation and prints statements for 20,000 account holders. Which statement BEST describes the data the bank is processing?</p>","options":["Scientific data: it requires numerous computations on each reading","Business data: it is repeatedly processed and needs large volumes of output","Scientific data: its output must be generated within seconds","Business data: it needs few repetitions but very fast output"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Statements are produced by the same processing every month, in large volumes, which is the mark of business data. Option A is tempting because interest involves calculation, but scientific data needs numerous complex computations and fast output, as in storm forecasting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A cashier at the Grand Anse Mini Mart sells 37 bottles of water in one morning, and the till stores this count as an 8-bit binary number. Which pattern is stored?</p>","options":["00110101","00100101","00100111","10100100"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. 37 = 32 + 4 + 1, giving 00100101. 10100100 is the correct bits written in reverse, the result of reading division remainders from the top down.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A telecoms company customer service trainee is writing notes on how a phone stores the text of an SMS message. Which statement about ASCII in the notes is correct?</p>","options":["Lower-case letters have codes 32 lower than their upper-case versions.","The character '9' is stored as the binary number 00001001.","The upper-case letters A to Z have consecutive codes from 65 to 90.","Each character is represented by a sequence of dots and dashes."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A to Z run consecutively from 65 to 90. Lower case is 32 higher, not lower; dots and dashes are Morse; and the character '9' has code 57, not the value 9.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A nurse at a clinic on Saba enters a patient's fever, rash and joint pain into an expert system. The system checks its stored IF ... THEN rules against these symptoms and suggests a blood test for dengue. Which part of the expert system applied the rules to the symptoms?</p>","options":["knowledge base","inference engine","user interface","database management system"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The inference engine applied the rules to the nurse's facts to reach the suggestion. A is tempting, but the knowledge base only stores the rules; it does not apply them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the Harbour Hardware store in Nassau, every sale recorded at the tills during the week is later totalled by department in a report for the store manager. Which statement BEST describes the relationship between the TPS and the MIS here?</p>","options":["The MIS records transactions that the TPS later summarises.","The TPS and MIS use completely separate data.","The output of the TPS becomes the input of the MIS.","The MIS replaces the TPS once reports are needed."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The MIS totals the sales that the store's TPS has already captured and processed at the tills. A is tempting but reverses the roles: the TPS records transactions and the MIS summarises them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A clerk at the Bridgetown Shipping Agency has email, a spreadsheet and a payroll package open at once on her Windows laptop. Which of these tasks is carried out by the OPERATING SYSTEM?</p>","options":["Producing a newsletter layout","Allocating memory to each of the programs that are running","Calculating staff pay and deductions","Converting assembly language into machine code"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Giving each of the clerk’s open programs its own memory is memory management, a core OS function. D is tempting because it is system software, but converting assembly language is the job of an assembler, a language translator.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Nevis Ferry Link, which carries passengers and cargo between Basseterre and Charlestown, is deciding whether to commission bespoke ticketing software or buy an off-the-shelf package. What is the MAIN advantage of the bespoke option?</p>","options":["It is cheaper to obtain","It is designed to meet the ferry company’s exact requirements","It has been tested by thousands of users","It is available immediately"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Bespoke software would be written for the ferry company’s own mix of passengers, cargo and day trips. A, B and D are all advantages of off-the-shelf software, which is why they tempt candidates who reverse the comparison.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the San Pedro Hardware store, the stock system automatically reorders plywood whenever fewer than 40 sheets remain, and it makes the same choice every time. Which feature of a structured decision does this show?</p>","options":["It is made only once, in response to a new situation.","It relies on incomplete and uncertain external information.","It follows a well-defined procedure and can often be automated.","It depends mainly on the intuition of senior managers."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The plywood reorder follows a fixed rule (below 40 sheets), so the stock system can make it automatically. A, C and D describe unstructured decisions; A is tempting because the rule is clear, but structured decisions like this are repetitive, not one-off.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The online registration screen of Breadfruit Valley Secondary contains boxes labelled Name, Date of Birth and Address, and a drop-down list for Form Level. Which type of HCI is this?</p>","options":["Natural language","Menu-driven","Command line","Form-based"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Labelled fields for data entry define a form-based interface. B is tempting because of the drop-down list, but that is only one control inside a form whose purpose is collecting several items of data.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Flamboyant Heights College is adding menus, step-by-step wizards and tooltips to its student portal for students logging in during their first week. Which group of users will MOST benefit?</p>","options":["Network administrators","Novice users","Programmers","Expert users"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Guidance features suit users who are new or occasional, like first-week students. Expert users (B) find forced wizards slow and prefer shortcuts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Frigate Bay Logistics plans a new parcel-tracking system and hires Devon, a systems analyst. Which statement BEST describes what Devon will do?</p>","options":["Use keyboard shortcuts to process parcel transactions quickly","Repair printers and reset passwords for warehouse staff","Enter customer orders into the sales system","Study the company's problem and specify what the new system must do"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Analysing requirements is the analyst's role. Option B is tempting because it is also an IT professional's job, but it describes technical support.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A student in Gros Islet connects her home computer to the Internet over a telephone line. A device converts the computer's digital data into analogue signals for the line and back again. The device is needed because it</p>","options":["increases the bandwidth of the line","sends data only to the correct MAC address","converts between digital and analogue signals","filters unauthorised traffic"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The student's modem modulates digital data onto an analogue carrier for the telephone line and demodulates incoming signals. A describes a firewall, C a switch; a modem cannot increase the line's bandwidth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Leeward Wholesale in St. George's lets its approved shop customers on Carriacou and Petite Martinique log in to its private network to check stock and place orders. This arrangement is</p>","options":["a MAN","a LAN","an extranet","an intranet"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Giving authorised outsiders, the shop customers, access to part of a private network is an extranet. A (intranet) is tempting but an intranet is restricted to the wholesaler's own staff.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A technician at Caribbean Connect ISP in Kingston captures traffic on the office LAN and sees units of data carrying source and destination MAC addresses and an error-checking trailer. At the Data Link layer of the OSI model, this protocol data unit (PDU) is called a</p>","options":["segment","frame","packet","bit"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Units with MAC addresses and a trailer are frames, the Data Link PDU. Packet is the most tempting distractor, but packets carry IP addresses and belong to the Network layer.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A lecturer at Flamboyant Heights College asks her class to compare the two networking models in one line of a revision table. Which statement BEST distinguishes the OSI model from the TCP/IP model?</p>","options":["The OSI model has no Transport layer, whereas the TCP/IP model does.","The OSI model has seven layers, whereas the TCP/IP model has four.","The OSI model is used by the Internet, whereas the TCP/IP model is only theoretical.","The OSI model combines the Data Link and Physical layers, whereas the TCP/IP model separates them."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. OSI has seven layers and TCP/IP four, a correct contrast on the same feature for the revision table. Option D is tempting but reversed: TCP/IP combines Data Link and Physical into Network Access.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Kemar is setting up a new router for his family's snack shop on Bridge Street, Castries. The cable from the ISP's modem should be connected to the router's</p>","options":["USB port","LAN port","WAN port","console port"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The WAN port connects Kemar's router to the wide area network (the ISP). A LAN port is tempting because it is the same shape, but LAN ports are for the shop's local devices.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the Frigate Bay Tours office, a speed test on Wi-Fi gives 15 Mbps, while the same laptop cabled to the router gives 92 Mbps on a 100 Mbps plan. What should the technician conclude?</p>","options":["The Ethernet cable is faulty.","The wireless link is the bottleneck.","The DNS server is slow.","The ISP is delivering far less than the plan."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The office's wired result is close to the plan, so the line is fine and the Wi-Fi link is limiting speed. Blaming the ISP is tempting, but the wired test proves the ISP is delivering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Bayside Credit Union in English Harbour must choose between swipe cards and fingerprint readers for its vault-room door. Which statement BEST compares the two options?</p>","options":["A swipe card cannot be cancelled if lost, whereas a biometric can be reset.","A swipe card checks something the user has, whereas a biometric checks something the user is.","A swipe card is a logical control, whereas a biometric is always a physical control.","Swipe cards cannot record entries, whereas biometrics always can."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Cards prove possession; biometrics prove a physical or behavioural characteristic. Option A is tempting but reversed: a lost card is easily cancelled, whereas a stolen biometric template cannot be changed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Ambergris Caye Ferry Desk must choose between a free paper sign-up sheet that keeps getting lost, a mid-priced online booking tool that meets all its requirements, and the newest system, priced far above its budget. The \"optimal solution\" is the one that</p>","options":["uses the most recent technology","was suggested first by the person who owns the problem","costs the least to implement","gives the best overall fit to the requirements and constraints"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Optimal means the best balance of criteria for this problem, which here is the mid-priced tool that meets the requirements within budget. A is a common misconception: the free sign-up sheet is cheapest but keeps getting lost, so it fails to solve the problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Sixth Form student studying Gros Islet Mini Mart, where shelves are often empty because stock is counted by hand, writes: \"The problem is that the shop needs a barcode scanner.\" What is the MAIN weakness of this problem statement?</p>","options":["It mentions technology.","It is written in the present tense.","It names a solution instead of describing the problem.","It is too long."],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A problem statement must be solution-neutral; naming a scanner closes off other options. A is tempting, but technology may appear in a statement as long as the statement describes the problem rather than the fix.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Four groups bring problems to a community IT fair at Coralstone College. Which group is dealing with a scientific problem?</p>","options":["A student deciding which laptop to buy for school","A Grand Anse restaurant choosing prices for a new menu","A dive team measuring sea temperatures to explain coral bleaching near Tobago","A community council planning a programme to reduce youth crime"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The dive team investigates through measurement and observation. Option D is tempting because it uses data, but it is a social problem owned by the community.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>As a hurricane approaches St. Kitts, a forwarded voice note falsely claiming that \"all the shelters are already full\" reaches thousands of phones within an hour. Which characteristic of information on the Internet MOST explains this?</p>","options":["Content can be easily copied and shared by anyone.","Information online is always refereed.","Online information always shows its publication date.","Search engines rank pages by accuracy."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Easy copying and sharing lets unverified claims spread rapidly. Option D is a common misconception: ranking reflects popularity and other factors, not accuracy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A CAPE student in St. John’s finds dengue prevention advice at the address www.healthtips-host.com/~rjames/dengue.html. The page has no Webmaster contact and no update date, and no reliable site links to it. Which conclusion is MOST justified?</p>","options":["It must be current, because web pages are updated every day.","It is an academic page, because the address contains a tilde.","It is refereed, because it has been published on the Internet.","It is probably a private individual's page without organisational approval, so it should be verified before use."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The tilde and personal name point to a private individual, and there is no sign of organisational approval, currency or links from reliable pages. Option B reverses the clue: a tilde usually marks a personal page, not an academic one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Sales staff at the Broad Street Jewellers try a clickable model of a tablet catalogue with cruise passengers over a weekend and report two missing functions. Which ADVANTAGE of the prototyping model does this show?</p>","options":["User involvement is no longer needed","Thorough documentation is produced at every stage","Final costs become easy to predict at the start","Users identify missing functions and usability problems early"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The staff evaluated a working model early, so problems surfaced early. Option C is tempting, but prototyping is hard to cost because the number of cycles is unknown.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Anguilla Port Authority used Waterfall for its cargo manifest system. During final testing, staff discover that a field required by Customs was missed at the analysis stage. This illustrates which DISADVANTAGE of Waterfall?</p>","options":["Errors in the requirements may not be discovered until testing, when they are costly to fix","It is difficult to understand and manage","It delivers working software too often","It cannot be used for large projects"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Testing comes late, so the missed Customs field surfaced late and is expensive to fix. Option B is wrong: Waterfall is one of the simplest models to understand and manage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Trinidad payroll department runs its old and new systems together for three pay periods. The MAIN disadvantage of this approach is that</p>","options":["old and new modules must exchange data during the changeover","staff must carry out every task twice, increasing cost and workload","there is no fallback if the new system fails","only one department can use the new system"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Parallel running doubles the workload. A describes direct changeover; D describes a problem of phased implementation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Albert Street Code tells a client: \"Our programmers write the code, but our process also covers specification, design, testing, documentation and maintenance.\" Which statement BEST distinguishes software engineering from programming?</p>","options":["Software engineering is a systematic, disciplined approach covering specification, design, testing, documentation and maintenance, of which programming is one part.","Software engineering applies only to hardware design.","Programming produces documentation, whereas software engineering produces code.","Software engineering uses only high-level languages."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. As Albert Street Code explains, programming is one activity within the wider discipline of software engineering. C reverses the relationship: software engineering includes both code and documentation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>At Guyana Rice Exporters in Georgetown, an analyst studies the company's procedures manual and order forms. Staff later explain that half of those procedures were dropped when a new manager arrived. Which limitation of reviewing printed material does this illustrate?</p>","options":["It requires the analyst to schedule meetings with every user.","Staff change their behaviour when documents are read.","It produces only quantitative data from rating scales.","Documents may be out of date and not reflect current practice."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The Guyana Rice Exporters manual describes procedures no longer followed, so the documents are out of date. B confuses this tool with the Hawthorne effect of observation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An analyst at the Dominica Credit Union in Roseau must present the loan-approval rules to the board, whose members have no technical training. She decides to use a decision tree instead of a decision table. Which advantage of the tree supports her decision?</p>","options":["It shows the data stores used by each process.","It is easier for non-technical users to follow visually.","It makes it easier to check that all 2 to the power n rules are present.","It always uses less space when there are many conditions."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A tree shows the logic as branches that the board members can trace without learning a Y/N/X grid. C is the most tempting distractor, but completeness is easier to check in a table, where the columns can be counted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An analyst at Blue Channel Ferries in San Pedro is naming the process that sends each passenger a ticket after the card payment is authorised. Which of the following is a correctly named PROCESS?</p>","options":["Issue e-ticket","Ticket","Booking request","Bookings"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A process is named with a verb phrase describing the work done. \"Booking request\" (C) is a noun phrase suitable for a data flow, not a process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Lower Six student drawing the level 1 DFD for the Island Wheels Car Rentals must add the file that holds rental agreements. Which statement about this data store in Gane and Sarson notation is TRUE?</p>","options":["It may receive data directly from the Customer entity","It is drawn as an open-ended rectangle and identified as D1, D2 or D3","It must also appear on the context level DFD","It is drawn as a square and named with a verb"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The Rental Agreements store is an open-ended rectangle with a D-identifier. C is tempting, but stores are hidden inside process 0 at context level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An algorithm for the Trinidad Taxi Co. contains the step \"Repeat: drive to the next stop.\" with no stopping condition. This step violates which characteristic?</p>","options":["General solution","Flow of control","Clearly defined and unambiguous","Finite number of steps"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. With no stopping condition the algorithm never terminates. Option B is tempting, but the order of steps is clear; the problem is that it never ends.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A CAPE student in Georgetown, Guyana, is planning an algorithm that prices produce for a market stall and lists ways she could represent it. Which list contains ONLY ways of representing algorithms named in the CAPE IT syllabus?</p>","options":["Program code, flowcharts, decision tables","Flowcharts, data flow diagrams, pseudocode","Pseudocode, Gantt charts, natural language","Natural language, flowcharts, pseudocode"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The syllabus names natural language, flowcharts and pseudocode. Option B is tempting because DFDs are diagrams, but a DFD models the flow of data through a system, not the steps of an algorithm.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_872","title":"Mock Paper 01 · Set 3","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Grade One · Mock Paper 01 · Set 3</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes · 45 items</p><ol><li>Answer <b>ALL</b> items. Each item has four options (A), (B), (C), (D).</li><li>Items 1–15 Module 1, 16–30 Module 2, 31–45 Module 3.</li><li>Submit to see your score, the key and an explanation for every item.</li></ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<p>For a Caribbean government's finance ministry, a team uses requirements gathering, design, version control, testing and planned maintenance to build a national tax-filing system over three years. This approach BEST describes</p>","options":["Information Technology","Software Engineering","Computer Science","Computer Engineering"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A disciplined process for large, reliable software is Software Engineering. Computer Science is tempting because programming is involved, but CS focuses on theory and algorithms rather than the development process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>An employee at a Kingston hotel installs servers, configures the Wi-Fi network, manages user accounts and helps staff when devices fail. This role is MOST closely related to</p>","options":["Computer Engineering","Information Systems","Information Technology","Computer Science"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Deploying, administering and supporting infrastructure for users is Information Technology. Information Systems is tempting because the setting is an organisation, but IS focuses on processes and decisions rather than running the infrastructure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>At the Harbour View Internet Café in St. George’s, a tourist tells the owner, \"The Internet and the Web are just two names for the same thing.\" Which statement CORRECTLY distinguishes the two?</p>","options":["The Internet is a global network of networks; the Web is a service of linked pages that runs on it.","They are two names for the same thing.","The Web was created before the Internet.","The Web is the physical network; the Internet is a collection of pages."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The Internet (network, TCP/IP) existed before the Web (1989 to 1991), which is one service running on it, so the tourist is wrong. Option D reverses the two.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Engineers planning a new telecommunications link between St. Lucia and a neighbouring island choose fibre-optic cable rather than copper wire. They choose fibre MAINLY because fibre</p>","options":["cannot be damaged by ships' anchors","is cheaper to lay on the seabed","works without electricity at either end","carries much more data over long distances with less signal loss"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Light in glass fibre offers far higher bandwidth and lower loss than electrical signals in copper. Fibre cables can still be cut by anchors.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>At Sandy Point Medical Centre, patients' test results may be seen only by authorised doctors, so the records are encrypted and protected by personal passwords. Which pair of characteristics of information is MOST concerned here?</p>","options":["Format and medium","Relevance and value","Confidentiality and security","Timeliness and lifespan"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. The rule that only authorised doctors may see results is confidentiality; encryption and passwords are the security controls that enforce it. Timeliness and lifespan concern when information is useful, not who may see it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>At Montego Bay Learning Centre, the exam registration system and the school office hold different dates of birth for the same student. This problem is BEST described as</p>","options":["a lack of shareability","distortion of information","information being subject to interpretation","inconsistency of information"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Two records of the same fact contradict each other: inconsistency. Distortion (B) is tempting, but it refers to meaning being changed as information is processed or passed on, not to two stored versions disagreeing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Lower Six student finds a journal article in which a team of researchers in Jamaica reviews and compares the findings of twenty earlier studies on mobile banking. As a source, this article is</p>","options":["a secondary source, because it analyses other people's research","a primary source, because it contains data","a primary source, because it was published in a journal","a traditional source, because all journals are printed"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A review article interprets and summarises earlier studies, so it is secondary. C is tempting, but the journal medium does not decide; an article reporting the team's own new survey would be primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<p>During a storm a resident films live video of floodwater on Broad Street and posts it on social media. A week later a blogger writes a post comparing several such videos. Which statement about these sources is CORRECT?</p>","options":["Both are secondary because all social media and blogs are secondary.","Whether a source is primary or secondary depends on its closeness to the original event, not its medium.","The blog post is primary because it is the most recent source.","A source cannot be both electronic and primary, so the video must be secondary."],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The two classifications are independent: the live video is electronic and primary, while the blog post comparing videos is electronic and secondary. A is tempting, but a resident's own footage filmed during the event is first-hand, whatever the platform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Flamboyant Heights College student studying Internet access across the whole of Dominica finds a report that gives results for Roseau and Portsmouth but omits all other districts. For his study, the report fails mainly on</p>","options":["bias","currency","format","completeness"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Important parts needed for the purpose are missing, which is completeness. Bias would require a one-sided slant from an interest; nothing suggests that here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Leon, a Flamboyant Heights College student, finds a 300-page report on hurricane damage to Broad Street shops, but it has no figures for Holetown, which his project needs. Which statement correctly applies depth and completeness to this report?</p>","options":["The report has great depth, but it is incomplete for Leon because the Holetown figures he needs are missing.","Both mean the number of pages, so a 300-page report is complete.","Depth applies only to print and completeness only to web sites, so neither applies.","Depth is a criterion and completeness a characteristic, so the report fails on depth."],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Depth is a characteristic (SO 1.5): the report is very detailed. Completeness is a criterion judged against the user's purpose (SO 1.6): it lacks what Leon needs. Option D reverses the two.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Head office of Sunrise Pharmacies chain in Castries receives each of its three branches' daily sales sheets and places them together in one folder for the week. The operation carried out is</p>","options":["retrieving","calculating","presenting","collating"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. Bringing data from several branches together into one set is collating. Retrieving is tempting, but nothing is being taken out of storage; new data is being brought together.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Every Friday afternoon, the manager of the Seaside Pharmacy branch in Gros Islet emails the weekly sales report to head office in Castries. In the information processing cycle, this is an example of</p>","options":["collating","transmitting","data capture","calculating"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Sending information electronically from one place to another is transmitting (part of storing, retrieving and transmitting). Collating is tempting because head office may later combine the branch reports, but the act described is sending.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A Form 6 student at Calabash Point Secondary is making a revision card headed \"Ways of representing DATA (SO 1.8)\". Which of the following lists contains ONLY items that belong on that card?</p>","options":["Morse Code, ASCII, tables","Text, tables, video","Character, string, numeric","Graphs, charts, signals"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Character, string and numeric (with aural and visual) are the data representations. Text, tables, video, graphs, charts and signals are ways of representing information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A ship in Grand Anse flashes a lamp: short, short, short, long, long, long, short, short, short. The message is being represented using</p>","options":["Morse Code","musical notation","binary place values","ASCII"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Short and long flashes are dots and dashes, here ... --- ... (SOS) in Morse Code. Binary also uses two states, but binary uses 0s and 1s with place values, not dots and dashes for letters.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The Island Yacht Club sends one email to a single club address, and the message is automatically delivered to the inboxes of all 300 members. Which tool is the club using?</p>","options":["A mailing list","SMS","A web-conferencing tool","A discussion forum"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. A mailing list delivers one message to every subscriber’s inbox. Option D, a discussion forum, is the most common confusion, but forum posts stay on a website that members must visit.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Caribe Marts group is training new staff in Basseterre on who uses each of its information systems. Which pairing of system and typical users should the trainer present as correct?</p>","options":["TPS: senior executives","EIS: data entry clerks","OAS: engineers and scientists","DSS: middle managers and analysts"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A DSS supports managers and analysts making semi-structured decisions. C is tempting because engineers work in offices, but engineers and scientists are knowledge workers who use a KWS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Calabash Point Secondary installs new computers and a new registration program, but forgets to connect the office computers to the server holding student records, so staff cannot open any student file. Which component of the information system is missing?</p>","options":["Software","Network","Procedures","Hardware"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The network connects hardware so that data can be shared. D is tempting because cables and routers are physical, but the question is about the missing communication link, which is the network component.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Portsmouth Market wants to print customer receipts quickly and quietly without buying ink or toner. Which printer is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Dot-matrix","Thermal","Laser","Inkjet"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Thermal printers use heat-sensitive paper and no ink. A laser printer is fast and quiet but needs toner.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the Coral Reef Hotel in Grand Anse, the air-conditioning system keeps a guest’s room at 22 °C. The compressor that switches on to cool the room when it gets too warm acts as the</p>","options":["actuator","analogue-to-digital converter","microprocessor","sensor"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The compressor is an actuator: it turns the processor’s signal into physical action. The sensor is tempting because it is also part of the loop, but it measures the room temperature rather than changing it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At the Sea View Hotel in Basseterre, the head of the restaurant department uses last quarter's sales figures and her own experience to set the next quarter's overtime budget. This decision is BEST classified as</p>","options":["semi-structured and tactical","structured and operational","structured and strategic","unstructured and strategic"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Data (sales figures) plus judgement (experience) makes it semi-structured, and a department head setting a quarterly budget works at the tactical level. B is tempting because a budget involves calculation, but the judgement element means it is not fully structured.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Driving along Bridge Street on a hot afternoon, the owner of the Bridge Street Bakery says \"Turn on the air conditioning\" and the car responds. Which pair of HCI types does this interaction involve?</p>","options":["Speech and natural language","Direct manipulation and GUI","GUI and forms","Command line and menu"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. The input is spoken (speech) and in an everyday sentence (natural language). D is tempting because the instruction is a command, but a command line requires typed input in exact syntax at a prompt.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A taxi driver leaves the shaded rank at Norman Manley International Airport and his phone screen automatically becomes brighter in the midday sun. This is achieved through</p>","options":["a screen reader","a natural language interface","a light sensor","a command line"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. A light sensor detects ambient light and adjusts the display. A screen reader (A) converts text to speech and has nothing to do with brightness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A Sint Maarten government website offers English, Dutch and Spanish versions and writes dates as \"3 Apr 2026\" so that residents and visitors from different countries read them correctly. This adapts the interface mainly for</p>","options":["cultural differences","disability","age","education"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Language and date formats differ across cultures. Education (D) is tempting because language relates to reading, but the adaptation targets different languages and conventions, not literacy level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The Caribbean Union Bank wants a full mesh network directly linking its 6 offices in Antigua, Curaçao, Aruba, Jamaica, Barbados and Trinidad. How many links are required?</p>","options":["15","6","30","12"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. n(n-1)/2 = 6 × 5 ÷ 2 = 15 links for the bank's six offices. D (30) forgets to divide by 2 and counts each link twice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Managers of Coral Reef Resorts in Jamaica and Barbados hold a weekly live meeting over the network in which they see and hear each other at the same time. This is</p>","options":["telecommuting","teleconferencing","videoconferencing","simplex transmission"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Live video and audio between the two resorts is videoconferencing. B (teleconferencing) is tempting but typically uses an audio link only, so the managers would not see each other.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A Form 6 student at Calabash Point Secondary is labelling a TCP/IP poster for the computer lab and must pair each layer with one protocol. Which pair correctly matches a TCP/IP layer with a protocol used at that layer?</p>","options":["Network Access: Ethernet","Internet: TCP","Transport: IP","Application: UDP"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Ethernet operates at the Network Access layer, so this label is correct. \"Internet: TCP\" is tempting because of the name TCP/IP, but TCP is a Transport protocol and IP is the Internet protocol.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The owner of the Grand Anse Surf Shop sees \"WPA3 (AES)\" on her new router’s settings page and asks what AES means. AES, as used in WPA2 and WPA3, is BEST described as</p>","options":["a symmetric encryption algorithm","a method of filtering MAC addresses","a wireless network name","a cable standard for Ethernet"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) is the symmetric encryption algorithm that scrambles the surf shop’s Wi-Fi traffic inside WPA2 and WPA3. MAC filtering is tempting because it is another Wi-Fi setting, but it controls access, not encryption.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At Saba Dive Shop, the booking PC cannot get online. A technician pings the default gateway, 192.168.1.1, and receives \"Request timed out\" four times. Which conclusion is MOST reasonable?</p>","options":["The Internet speed is below the plan.","The website being visited is offline.","The DNS server address is wrong.","There is a problem between the PC and the router, or the router is blocking ping."],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The gateway is the shop's router, so no reply points to the PC-to-router link or a rule blocking ping. DNS is tempting, but pinging an IP address does not use DNS.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The administrator at the Bayside Family Clinic in Gros Islet wants only the clinic's registered laptops to join the staff Wi-Fi, even if a patient learns the network key. Which setting should be added?</p>","options":["Hiding the SSID","A longer DHCP range","A speed test","MAC filtering"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. MAC filtering admits only devices whose hardware addresses are on the clinic's allow list. Hiding the SSID is tempting, but a patient who knows the name and key can still join.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>After Hurricane Irma, the Harbour Insurance Agency in Road Town began keeping its backup drives in a fire-proof safe in a separate building in East End. This is BEST described as</p>","options":["encryption","logical access control","authentication","a physical security measure"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A fire-proof safe in another building physically protects the agency's backup drives. Logical access control is tempting because backups hold data, but the safe protects the drives themselves, not access through software.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A consultant is helping the Kingston Print Shop deal with a growing backlog of print jobs. Which of her activities is MOST closely associated with the \"select and justify the optimal solution\" stage?</p>","options":["Listing three possible job-tracking systems with their costs","Explaining why one system is better than the others in terms of cost and effectiveness","Interviewing staff to find the cause of the backlog","Training staff on the new job-tracking system"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Justifying means giving reasons for a choice by comparing it with the alternatives, as the consultant does when she explains why one system beats the others. A is tempting, but listing the systems with costs belongs to identifying and evaluating possible solutions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Statia Dive Centre in Oranjestad sets up its chosen online booking system, enters its boats and timetables, and trains both staff members to use it. Which stage of the problem-solving process is this?</p>","options":["Implement the solution","Select and justify the optimal solution","Analyse the problem","Evaluate and review"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Setting up the chosen system, entering data and training staff put the solution into action, which is implementation. D is tempting because it comes next, but evaluation and review happens after the system is running, when results are compared with the problem statement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A student uses a health website from another country to plan a nutrition campaign in Jamaica, but the foods and eating habits described are uncommon there. Which criterion is the student MOST likely to have overlooked?</p>","options":["Currency","Cultural context","Accuracy","Refereed status"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. The information was produced for a different society with different foods and habits. Accuracy is tempting, but the information may be correct for its original setting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Mr. Lake, a CAPE IT teacher in Belize City, writes four revision flashcards on categories of information for his class. Which flashcard correctly matches the category with its definition?</p>","options":["Cosmetic: information that is irrelevant to the problem","Desirable: information that improves the solution but is not strictly required","Essential: information that improves the solution but is not required","Desirable: information without which the problem cannot be solved"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. Desirable information adds value but the problem can still be solved without it. Option D describes essential information and Option A describes extraneous information.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Leeward Islands Pension Fund in Basseterre needs software to calculate pensions set by law. The requirements are fully known and stable, and an external auditor must review the documents from every stage. Which model is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["Evolutionary prototyping","Agile software development","Waterfall","Iterative development with weekly changes"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Stable, fully known requirements and formal stage documentation suit Waterfall. Agile (B) is tempting as a modern model, but it values working software over comprehensive documentation and expects change.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A team building an app for the Saba Dive Centre works in two-week cycles, meets briefly every morning and has a customer representative who reorders a prioritised list of features before each cycle. Which model is being used?</p>","options":["Throwaway prototyping","Waterfall","Agile software development","Plan-driven incremental"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Time-boxed sprints, daily stand-ups and a product owner managing a backlog are Agile practices. Plan-driven incremental (D) is tempting because it also uses cycles, but it lacks the continuous customer collaboration and daily stand-ups.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>San Pedro Hardware has compared an online-ordering package with a custom web store and must now give the owner a document recommending whether, and how, to proceed. Which deliverable is this?</p>","options":["Technical manual","Conversion plan","Test plan","System proposal"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. The feasibility study produces the system proposal recommending whether to proceed. A conversion plan (A) belongs to implementation, after the web store is built.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Four months after the Island Wheels Car Rentals booking system went live, the analyst counts double bookings and compares the figure with the target in the system proposal. Which BEST describes the SDLC stage she is carrying out?</p>","options":["Training users and converting data to the new format","Testing each program module with normal, boundary and erroneous data","Evaluating the live system against the objectives set in the system proposal","Investigating how the current manual system works"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Review evaluates the system after go-live against its objectives. A is testing during development, which happens before go-live.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The analyst for the Saba Dive Centre needs to understand why the owner schedules dive trips the way she does, and wants to respond at once to anything unclear she says. Which fact-finding tool gives the BEST opportunity to ask follow-up questions?</p>","options":["Observation","Questionnaire","Interview","Review of printed material"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. In an interview with the owner, the analyst can respond to each answer immediately with further questions about her reasons. Observation (C) involves the analyst on site, but the analyst is watching rather than questioning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Librarians and students at Mahoe Grove High School cannot clearly describe what they want the search screens of a proposed library system to look like, though they say they will \"know it when they see it\". Which tool would BEST help the analyst?</p>","options":["An ER diagram","A decision table","A Gantt chart","A prototype"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. A prototype lets users see and try the screens and react, which clarifies unclear requirements. An ER diagram (D) models stored data, not what the screens look like.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An analyst drawing a level 1 DFD for the Island Hardware store checks every arrow before showing the diagram to the owner. Which of the following data flows is ILLEGAL on the DFD?</p>","options":["From external entity Customer to process 1.0","From external entity Customer to data store D1","From process 1.0 to data store D1","From data store D2 to process 3.0"],"correct":1,"why":"Key: B. A customer cannot write straight into the store; a process must be in between. Options C and D are legal because a process is at one end.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>On the context level DFD for the Portsmouth Marine Services payroll in Roseau, the Bank receives payment details from the system each fortnight but sends nothing back. The Bank is BEST described as</p>","options":["a sink","a data store","a process","a source"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. An external entity that only receives data is a sink. D is tempting because the Bank is an external entity, but a source sends data into the system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A student's algorithm for the Gros Islet Laundromat gets the load weight, calculates the price and then displays it, and it is always clear which step comes next. It shows \"flow of control from one process to another\", which means that</p>","options":["every step must be a calculation","the algorithm must contain at least one decision","the steps are carried out in a clear, logical order with no doubt about which step comes next","the algorithm can be used by only one person at a time"],"correct":2,"why":"Key: C. Flow of control is the order of execution and the passing of control from step to step. Option B is tempting because decisions affect the flow, but a simple sequence with no decision still has a flow of control.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<p>An analyst at the Bridgetown firm CodeWave must present a refund algorithm with several decisions to a client in Barbados and is choosing between a flowchart and pseudocode. Which is an ADVANTAGE of the flowchart over pseudocode?</p>","options":["It shows the flow of control and decision paths visually","It converts more directly into program code","It is quicker to edit","It is more compact for very large algorithms"],"correct":0,"why":"Key: A. Arrows and diamonds make each refund decision and its paths visible at a glance. Option B is tempting, but pseudocode, not a flowchart, is closest to program code.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A Sixth Form student at Hummingbird Ridge High School is desk-checking a discount rule for Montego Bay Bakery: a ← 8; b ← 2; IF a - b * 3 > 3 THEN OUTPUT a / b ELSE OUTPUT a + b ENDIF. What is output?</p>","options":["18","4","6","10"],"correct":3,"why":"Key: D. b * 3 = 6 first, then 8 − 6 = 2; 2 > 3 is FALSE, so the ELSE branch outputs 8 + 2 = 10. 4 is tempting if you work left to right: (8 − 2) * 3 = 18 > 3 would wrongly select the THEN branch and output 8 / 2.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 02 Specimen and Mock Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_879","title":"Paper 02 · 2020 specimen","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Paper 02 · Specimen paper (2020)</b></p><ol><li>This paper consists of THREE sections and SIX questions: Section I (Module 1), Section II (Module 2) and Section III (Module 3).</li><li>Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 15 marks.</li><li>Time: 2 hours 30 minutes. Plan about 25 minutes per question.</li><li>Label every part (a), (b), (i), (ii) and use the command word: <i>state</i> and <i>identify</i> need a short answer; <i>describe</i>, <i>explain</i>, <i>distinguish</i> and <i>justify</i> need developed points.</li><li>Where a question asks for a diagram (network, DFD, flowchart or sketch), draw it on paper and describe it in your answer box: name each symbol or device, its label and what it connects to.</li></ol><p>Transcribed for class practice from the CXC specimen paper (Copyright © 2019 Caribbean Examinations Council). Rubrics and model answers are built from the official key and mark scheme; where the scheme contains a slip, the teacher tip explains it.</p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q1: data, information sources and processing","q":"<ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between 'data' and 'information'. Give ONE example of EACH. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The manager of the Nutritional Centre in the Health Ministry would like to conduct research on the eating habits of students in schools.<ol type=\"i\"><li>State TWO sources of information that the manager can use to assist with her research and provide ONE characteristic of each source. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe TWO characteristics that the manager can use in selecting information corresponding to this research. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Describe FOUR steps that the manager would take to present the findings and carry out the recommendations of this research, using the automated information processing cycle. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Data versus information</b> (3 marks): Distinction and examples are correct (1 + 1 + 1).</li><li><b>(b)(i) Sources and characteristics</b> (4 marks): Two sources each with a matching characteristic (2 + 2).</li><li><b>(b)(ii) Selecting information</b> (4 marks): Two characteristics described and applied (2 + 2).</li><li><b>(c) Automated information processing cycle</b> (4 marks): Four stages described in context (1 + 1 + 1 + 1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Data</b> is a collection of raw, unprocessed facts and figures that have no meaning on their own, whereas <b>information</b> is data that has been processed (organised, sorted or calculated) so that it conveys meaning and is useful. Example of data: the numbers 3, 0, 5, 2 written on survey forms for \"servings of fruit eaten this week\". Example of information: a report stating that most Form 2 students eat fewer than two servings of fruit a week.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Journals</b> (for example a refereed public-health journal): reliable and authoritative because articles are peer-reviewed, and current. <b>People</b> (students and canteen staff, through interviews or questionnaires): readily available and highly relevant as a primary source, although answers may be less reliable.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> <b>Relevance/appropriateness:</b> the manager should select information that applies to the country and to the age group being studied; a study of adults in another region would not reflect Caribbean secondary students. <b>Amount of depth required:</b> she must decide how much detail is needed, for example whether students will be interviewed in depth, whether only a sample of schools is used, or whether an island-wide survey is required; this determines which sources are suitable.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Input:</b> data from the surveys, questionnaires and interviews is captured or entered into a computer, for example through an online form. <b>Processing:</b> the data is collated and analysed, for example sorted by age and school and averages calculated. <b>Storage:</b> the data and results are stored in a database for later retrieval and comparison with future studies. <b>Output:</b> the findings are presented to the Ministry and schools using charts, graphs and a report, and the recommendations (such as healthier canteen menus) are disseminated. <b>Feedback:</b> the effect of the recommendations is evaluated in a follow-up survey, and the results loop back to the input stage to update the findings.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>From the mark scheme: in (c) a description is expected; if the stages are only stated, a maximum of 2 marks is awarded. Common errors: giving \"a computer\" as an example of data; naming sources without a characteristic; repeating the same characteristic for both sources; answering (b)(ii) with characteristics of sources (cost, format) instead of how to <b>select</b> information for this research. (The printed paper contains a stray line under (b)(i), \"Describe FOUR characteristics common to all of the information sources…\", which is a typesetting slip and is not part of the question.)</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q2: representing information and Internet research tools","q":"<ol type=\"a\"><li>Data and information can be represented by various methods. For each of the following outputs, draw a labelled sketch to illustrate the representation. <ol type=\"i\"><li>Graphical</li><li>Analogue</li></ol><i>(4 marks)</i> [Space is provided in the booklet for two sketches.]</li><li>A friend doing research on professional careers is told to use the Internet to find sources of information. The Internet has several research tools.<ol type=\"i\"><li>State, with reasons, THREE Internet research tools that your friend could use. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Suggest THREE fields that are suitable for a career in Information Technology. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend TWO professional organisations that the friend may find useful. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p><p>Where a part asks you to draw, sketch it on paper first, then describe it here in words: name each symbol or device, its label, and what it connects to (for example \"Arrow labelled <i>hours worked</i> from Payroll Clerk to process 0\").</p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Labelled sketches</b> (4 marks): Two sketches with labels (2 x 1 sketch, 2 x 1 label).</li><li><b>(b)(i) Internet research tools with reasons</b> (6 marks): Three tools each with a reason (3 x 2).</li><li><b>(b)(ii) IT career fields</b> (3 marks): Three valid fields (3 x 1).</li><li><b>(b)(iii) Professional organisations</b> (2 marks): Two correct organisations (2 x 1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: a labelled bar chart of cruise ship calls per month. Right: an analogue sound wave from a siren.\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"135\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"25\"/></g><g fill=\"#F5C542\"><rect x=\"42\" y=\"70\" width=\"22\" height=\"65\"/><rect x=\"78\" y=\"45\" width=\"22\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"114\" y=\"95\" width=\"22\" height=\"40\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"53\" y=\"149\">Jan</text><text x=\"89\" y=\"149\">Feb</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"149\">Mar</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"16\">Cruise ship calls per month</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"85\" transform=\"rotate(-90 12 85)\">Ships</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"16\">Siren sound (analogue signal)</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"149\">Time</text></g><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/><path d=\"M195 80 C 205 30, 215 30, 225 80 S 245 130, 255 80 S 275 30, 285 80 S 305 130, 315 80 S 335 30, 345 80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4FD18B\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg><figcaption>(i) Graphical: a labelled bar chart. (ii) Analogue: a continuously varying wave, such as the sound of a siren.</figcaption></figure><p><b>(i) Graphical:</b> [Sketch] a bar chart titled \"Cruise ship calls per month\" with the x-axis labelled with months (Jan, Feb, Mar) and the y-axis labelled \"Ships\"; bar heights show the values. <b>(ii) Analogue:</b> [Sketch] a continuous wave labelled \"Siren sound\" plotted against a time axis, showing a signal that varies smoothly rather than in steps (a needle dial or a ringing bell is also acceptable).</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Search engine</b> (e.g. Google): finds current web pages on IT careers quickly using keywords, so the friend can compare many sources. <b>Social media tools</b> (e.g. professional networking groups or discussion forums): give timely, first-hand accounts from people working in IT careers and allow questions. <b>Web-conferencing or cross-platform messaging tools</b> (e.g. Zoom, Teams, WhatsApp): allow a live interview with an IT professional, which is interactive and appropriate for primary research.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> Computer Science; Software Engineering; Information Systems (Computer Engineering is also acceptable).</p><p><b>(b)(iii)</b> The <b>BCS</b> (British Computer Society, the Chartered Institute for IT) and the <b>IEEE</b> (Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers); the ACM and AIS are also acceptable.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Mark scheme: (a) sketch 2 x 1 and labels 2 x 1; (b)(i) 1 mark per tool and 1 per reason. Common errors: an unlabelled sketch; drawing a digital square wave for \"analogue\"; giving web sites (e.g. a named careers site) instead of <b>tools</b>; reasons such as \"it is good\"; careers such as \"doctor\" or job titles instead of the syllabus fields; inventing organisation names. SO 1.2 (history) is listed for this question but is not examined in the specimen; expect it in other papers.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q3: users, HCI and remote access","q":"<p>An information system can be described as a collection of components used to produce quality information.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Explain TWO important roles of users as components of an information system. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Define the term 'human computer interaction or interface (HCI)'. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Describe THREE types of HCI. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Harry is the senior systems administrator in a large organisation. The computer network comprises several servers, each running a major system. His job requires that he and his team operate the computer systems quickly, to resolve (troubleshoot) any problem in the shortest possible time.<ol type=\"i\"><li>Identify ONE type of HCI that would be MOST appropriate for the job that Harry is required to do. Justify your response. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Explain what type of network Harry could use to remotely access the computer network. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Roles of users</b> (4 marks): Two roles, each explained (2 x 2).</li><li><b>(b) Definition of HCI</b> (1 marks): Correct definition.</li><li><b>(c) Three types of HCI</b> (6 marks): Three types each named and described (3 x 2).</li><li><b>(d)(i) Most appropriate HCI for Harry</b> (2 marks): Correct choice and justification.</li><li><b>(d)(ii) Remote access network</b> (2 marks): Network named and explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Users provide input:</b> they enter the data (via keyboards, scanners and other devices) on which the system depends, so the accuracy of their input determines the quality of the information produced. <b>Users receive and interpret output:</b> they read reports and screens and use them to make decisions; without a user to interpret the output, the information has no value. (Also: users monitor the system to detect problems and provide feedback.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> HCI is the part of a computer system (software and hardware) that defines how a user interacts with, and receives responses from, the computer.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Command line:</b> the user types instructions (commands) at a prompt in a console; there are no menus or icons to choose from, so commands must be remembered. <b>Menu-driven:</b> the system displays a list of options, similar to a restaurant menu, and the user selects one to activate a process, as on an ATM. <b>Graphical user interface (GUI):</b> uses windows, icons, menus and a pointer (mouse or touch) so the user communicates by pointing and clicking on graphics.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> A <b>command line</b> interface. It gives faster, direct access to each server's operating system, uses less memory and processing than a GUI, and commands can be combined in a batch file or script to run the same fix across several servers.</p><p><b>(d)(ii)</b> A <b>virtual private network (VPN)</b>. It creates a secure, encrypted connection (tunnel) over the Internet to the organisation's network, so Harry can log in from home as if he were on the internal LAN.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Mark scheme: identifying each HCI type earns 1 mark and its description 1 mark. Common errors: describing users only as \"people who use the computer\" (no role); naming two HCIs that are really the same (GUI and WIMP); choosing a GUI for Harry because it is \"easy\" (the scenario stresses speed for an expert user); answering \"the Internet\" or \"WAN\" for (d)(ii) without the secure connection that a VPN provides. The printed paper has a stray phrase, \"functions of an operating system\", after (a); it is a typesetting slip.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q4: star topology and security concepts","q":"<p>Computer networks help businesses organise their IT resources for maximum use and effective management.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>An organisation wishes to configure a network of five computers and one printer in the HR department.<ol type=\"i\"><li>Draw a diagram to represent a network topology that would allow the computers and printer to be connected in such a way that the failure of any one of the computers (or its connection) will not affect the function of the network as a whole. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Identify the topology drawn in (a)(i) and state why it would be able to prevent network failure in the event that one computer (or its connection) fails. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>A major concern facing individuals and companies is the security of their IT systems and data. Explain the following concepts.<ol type=\"i\"><li>Encryption <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Authentication <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Distinguish between 'logical access control' and 'physical access control' measures. Give ONE example of EACH. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p><p>Where a part asks you to draw, sketch it on paper first, then describe it here in words: name each symbol or device, its label, and what it connects to (for example \"Arrow labelled <i>hours worked</i> from Payroll Clerk to process 0\").</p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)(i) Network diagram</b> (5 marks): Star layout (2), central hub/switch labelled (1), computers and printer labelled (2).</li><li><b>(a)(ii) Topology and reason</b> (2 marks): Star identified (1) with a correct reason (1).</li><li><b>(b)(i) Encryption</b> (2 marks): Two points made.</li><li><b>(b)(ii) Authentication</b> (2 marks): Two points made.</li><li><b>(c) Logical versus physical access control</b> (4 marks): Both measures defined with an example each (1 + 1 + 1 + 1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)(i)</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Star topology: a switch in the centre with five PCs and a printer each connected by its own Ethernet cable.\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"110\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"190\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"110\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"30\"/></g><rect x=\"130\" y=\"96\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#F5C542\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"114\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1a1a1a\">Switch</text><g fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"><rect x=\"235\" y=\"98\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"185\" y=\"178\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"85\" y=\"178\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"35\" y=\"98\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/><rect x=\"85\" y=\"18\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\"/></g><rect x=\"185\" y=\"18\" width=\"50\" height=\"24\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\" stroke=\"#4FD18B\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"260\" y=\"114\">PC 1</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"194\">PC 2</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"194\">PC 3</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"114\">PC 4</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"34\">PC 5</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"34\">Printer</text></g></svg><figcaption>Star topology for the HR department: each device has its own cable to the central switch.</figcaption></figure><p>[Diagram described] A <b>switch</b> (or hub) is drawn in the centre and labelled. Six separate Ethernet cables run from it to PC 1, PC 2, PC 3, PC 4, PC 5 and the <b>printer</b>, each labelled. No device is connected to any other device except through the switch.</p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> This is a <b>star topology</b>. All data passes through the central switch, and each computer has its own cable to it, so the network does not depend on connections between computers: if PC 3 or its cable fails, only PC 3 is cut off and the others continue to communicate and print.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Encryption</b> is the coding or scrambling of data into an unreadable form (ciphertext) so that it can only be decoded and read by someone who has the correct decryption key; it is used, for example, to protect payroll files sent by e-mail.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> <b>Authentication</b> is the process of confirming that a user, device or account holder is who they claim to be before access is allowed, by comparing what they enter (username and password, PIN, security key or fingerprint) with the details stored in a database.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Physical access control</b> uses tangible measures or devices to stop unauthorised people from reaching the computer equipment itself, for example a swipe card on the HR office door or a locked server room. <b>Logical access control</b> uses software, procedures and security principles (limited privileges, segregation of duties) to restrict what a person can do once at the computer, for example a user ID and password or file permissions so only HR staff can open salary records.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: drawing a bus or ring (a break stops the whole network); omitting labels on the central device; putting the printer between two PCs; defining encryption as \"a password\"; confusing authentication with authorisation; giving \"a firewall\" without saying hardware (physical) or software (logical). The mark scheme labels part (c) as \"(d)\"; it is the same question.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q5: data flow diagrams and a payroll context diagram","q":"<ol type=\"a\"><li>A Data Flow Diagram (DFD) is an important software engineering tool.<ol type=\"i\"><li>Define the term 'data flow diagram'. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Outline ONE difference between a 'context level data flow diagram' and a 'Level 1 data flow diagram'. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Draw the symbols used in DFDs to represent EACH of the following: data flow; process; external entities; data stores. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>A payroll system involves the payroll clerk entering the hours worked by weekly paid employees. This results in the calculation of the net pay and the printing of payslips. The payslips are given to the employees and the payroll database is updated.<br>Draw a context diagram for the payroll system described above. <i>(8 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p><p>Where a part asks you to draw, sketch it on paper first, then describe it here in words: name each symbol or device, its label, and what it connects to (for example \"Arrow labelled <i>hours worked</i> from Payroll Clerk to process 0\").</p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)(i) Definition of DFD</b> (1 marks): Correct definition.</li><li><b>(a)(ii) Context versus Level 1</b> (2 marks): Both sides of the difference given (1 x 2).</li><li><b>(a)(iii) DFD symbols</b> (4 marks): Four correct symbols (4 x 1).</li><li><b>(b) Payroll context diagram</b> (8 marks): Entities (2), process (1), data store (1), flows (2) and accuracy (2).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)(i)</b> A data flow diagram is a <b>graphical representation</b> of the <b>flow of data</b> through an information system: where data comes from, how it is processed, where it is stored and where it goes.</p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> A <b>context level DFD</b> shows the entire system as a <b>single process</b> with little or no detail about its internal operations, whereas a <b>Level 1 DFD</b> breaks that process down into its individual <b>sub-processes</b> and shows the data stores and how data flows between them.</p><p><b>(a)(iii)</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Gane and Sarson DFD symbols: an arrow for a data flow, a rounded rectangle with an ID band for a process, a shaded square for an external entity, and an open-ended rectangle with an ID box for a data store.\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"55\"/><path d=\"M62 49 L72 55 L62 61\"/><rect x=\"95\" y=\"25\" width=\"70\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"42\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"42\"/><rect x=\"190\" y=\"25\" width=\"60\" height=\"60\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\"/><path d=\"M190 25 l-5 5 v60 h60 l5 -5\"/><path d=\"M345 40 H275 V70 H345\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"70\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"40\" y=\"100\">Data flow</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"36\">1.0</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"65\">Verb phrase</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"100\">Process</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"60\">Entity</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"100\">External entity</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"59\">D1</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"59\">Name</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"100\">Data store</text></g></svg><figcaption>Gane and Sarson symbols: data flow, process, external entity, data store.</figcaption></figure><p>Data flow: an <b>arrow</b> labelled with the data. Process: a <b>rounded rectangle</b> with an ID band at the top. External entity: a <b>square</b> with a shaded (double) edge. Data store: an <b>open-ended rectangle</b> with an ID box (D1) on the left.</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 230\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Context diagram: Payroll Clerk sends hours worked to process 0 Payroll System; the process sends payslips to Employee and payroll updates to the Payroll Database data store.\"><defs><marker id=\"pa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></marker></defs><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"60\" width=\"75\" height=\"40\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\"/><rect x=\"275\" y=\"60\" width=\"75\" height=\"40\" fill=\"rgba(255,255,255,.06)\"/><rect x=\"135\" y=\"45\" width=\"90\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><path d=\"M220 180 H140 V210 H220\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"210\"/></g><g stroke=\"currentColor\" marker-end=\"url(#pa)\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"133\" y2=\"80\"/><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"273\" y2=\"80\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"178\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"47\" y=\"84\">Payroll Clerk</text><text x=\"312\" y=\"84\">Employee</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"57\">0</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"85\">Payroll</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"98\">System</text><text x=\"109\" y=\"72\">Hours worked</text><text x=\"249\" y=\"72\">Payslip</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"150\">Updated payroll details</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"199\">D1</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"199\">Payroll DB</text></g></svg><figcaption>Context diagram for the payroll system (data store shown, as credited in the CXC mark scheme).</figcaption></figure><p>[Diagram described] External entity <b>Payroll Clerk</b> → data flow <i>hours worked</i> → process <b>0 Payroll System</b>. Process 0 → data flow <i>payslip</i> → external entity <b>Employee</b>. Process 0 → data flow <i>updated payroll details</i> → data store <b>D1 Payroll Database</b>. (The calculation of net pay happens inside process 0.)</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Mark scheme for (b): entities 2, process 1, data store 1, data flows 2, accuracy (including flow direction) 2. Note that the official example includes the payroll database on the context diagram; in general a context diagram shows only one process and the external entities, with stores appearing at Level 1, so follow the question. Common errors: mixing notations (Yourdon circles with Gane and Sarson squares); unlabelled arrows; flows labelled with verbs (\"calculate\") instead of data nouns; connecting the clerk directly to the database; adding several processes to a context diagram.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Specimen Q6: control structures, tracing and flowcharting an algorithm","q":"<ol type=\"a\"><li>Repetition is one of the control constructs that may be used in developing algorithms. Describe TWO other control structures that can be used in developing an algorithm. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Examine the algorithm shown below.<pre>sum = 0\ncounter = 0\nfor x = 1 to x = 20\n    if x mod 3 = 0\n        counter = counter + 1\n        sum = sum + x\n    x = x + 1\nz = sum / counter\nprint counter\nprint z</pre><ol type=\"i\"><li>Explain the function of the algorithm shown above. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>State the output of the algorithm. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Design a flowchart to represent the algorithm shown in 6 (b). <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>Total 15 marks</b></p><p>Where a part asks you to draw, sketch it on paper first, then describe it here in words: name each symbol or device, its label, and what it connects to (for example \"Arrow labelled <i>hours worked</i> from Payroll Clerk to process 0\").</p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Two other control structures</b> (4 marks): Two structures identified and described (2 x 2).</li><li><b>(b)(i) Function of the algorithm</b> (4 marks): Average (1), multiples of 3 (1), 1 to 20 (1), prints result (1).</li><li><b>(b)(ii) Output</b> (2 marks): Both output values correct.</li><li><b>(c) Flowchart</b> (5 marks): Symbols and decisions (2), counters and variables (1), looping (1), print counter and z (1).</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Sequence:</b> instructions are carried out one after another in the order in which they are written; each step leads to the next (for example, input two numbers, add them, output the total). <b>Selection:</b> a condition is tested and, depending on whether it is true or false, a choice is made between alternative paths (IF ... THEN ... ELSE), for example IF mark &gt;= 50 THEN print \"Pass\".</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> The algorithm calculates the <b>average</b> (mean) of all the <b>multiples of 3</b> between <b>1 and 20</b>. It examines each value of x from 1 to 20; when x mod 3 = 0 it adds 1 to the counter and adds x to the running sum. After the loop it divides the sum by the counter and <b>prints</b> how many multiples were found and their average.</p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> The multiples are 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 and 18, so <b>counter = 6</b> and sum = 63. The algorithm prints <b>6</b> and then <b>z = 63 / 6 = 10.5</b>.</p><p><b>(c)</b></p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 400\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flowchart: Start; set sum, counter to 0 and x to 1; decision x less than or equal to 20; if yes, decision x mod 3 equals 0; if yes add 1 to counter and add x to sum; then x equals x plus 1 and loop back. When x exceeds 20, z equals sum divided by counter, output counter and z, Stop.\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/></marker></defs><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"22\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"14\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\"/><path d=\"M120 95 L170 120 L120 145 L70 120 Z\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><path d=\"M120 165 L175 190 L120 215 L65 190 Z\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><rect x=\"55\" y=\"235\" width=\"130\" height=\"34\"/><rect x=\"70\" y=\"295\" width=\"100\" height=\"26\"/><rect x=\"235\" y=\"105\" width=\"115\" height=\"30\"/><path d=\"M250 160 H350 L340 190 H240 Z\" stroke=\"#4FD18B\"/><ellipse cx=\"295\" cy=\"225\" rx=\"35\" ry=\"14\"/></g><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"36\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"48\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"93\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"163\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"233\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"269\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"293\"/><path d=\"M175 190 H205 V308 H172\"/><path d=\"M70 308 H25 V120 H68\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"233\" y2=\"120\"/><line x1=\"292\" y1=\"135\" x2=\"292\" y2=\"158\"/><line x1=\"295\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"295\" y2=\"209\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"120\" y=\"26\">Start</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"69\">sum=0, counter=0, x=1</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\">x &lt;= 20?</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"194\">x mod 3 = 0?</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"250\">counter = counter + 1</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"263\">sum = sum + x</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"312\">x = x + 1</text><text x=\"292\" y=\"124\">z = sum / counter</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"179\">Output counter, z</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"229\">Stop</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"114\">No</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"157\">Yes</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"228\">Yes</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"184\">No</text></g></svg><figcaption>Flowchart for the specimen algorithm: terminals (ovals), processes (rectangles), decisions (diamonds), output (parallelogram) and a loop back to the x &lt;= 20 test.</figcaption></figure><p>[Flowchart described] Start (oval) → process: sum = 0, counter = 0, x = 1 → decision: x &lt;= 20? If Yes → decision: x mod 3 = 0? If Yes → process: counter = counter + 1; sum = sum + x → process: x = x + 1 → loop back to the x &lt;= 20 decision. If x mod 3 = 0 is No → go straight to x = x + 1. When x &lt;= 20 is No → process: z = sum / counter → output (parallelogram): print counter, z → Stop (oval).</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Two slips to know about: the mark scheme gives \"Recursive\" as a control structure, but the syllabus names only sequence, selection and repetition, so use <b>sequence</b> and <b>selection</b>. For (b)(ii) the scheme lists 6 and 63; 63 is the sum, while the value printed as z is 63 / 6 = <b>10.5</b>. The line \"x = x + 1\" is treated as the loop's own increment (if a FOR loop also added 1, x would skip values). Common errors: describing \"the algorithm adds numbers\" without \"average\" and \"multiples of 3\"; a flowchart with no loop back or with the output inside the loop.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_87g","title":"Paper 02 · Mock 1","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Paper 02 · Mock 1 (original practice paper)</b></p><ol><li>This paper consists of THREE sections and SIX questions: Section I (Module 1), Section II (Module 2) and Section III (Module 3).</li><li>Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 15 marks.</li><li>Time: 2 hours 30 minutes. Plan about 25 minutes per question.</li><li>Label every part (a), (b), (i), (ii) and use the command word: <i>state</i> and <i>identify</i> need a short answer; <i>describe</i>, <i>explain</i>, <i>distinguish</i> and <i>justify</i> need developed points.</li><li>Where a question asks for a diagram (network, DFD, flowchart or sketch), draw it on paper and describe it in your answer box: name each symbol or device, its label and what it connects to.</li></ol><p>Mock 1 examines the field and history of IT, data representation and IT tools, information systems and decision levels, the OSI and TCP/IP models, troubleshooting and wireless security, software development models and the SDLC, and pseudocode with programming.</p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q1: the field and history of Information Technology","q":"<p>Palmetto Point Secondary is planning an \"IT Careers and Heritage Week\". Form 6 students must prepare a display that explains what Information Technology covers, how it relates to other computing fields, and how computers and the Internet developed to the point where a student in Basseterre can video-call a cousin in Guyana. A visiting software developer from <b>Seaview Systems</b> in Sandy Point will judge the display.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe the scope of Information Technology by giving <b>TWO</b> uses and <b>ONE</b> limitation of IT in a Caribbean organisation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between <b>Computer Science</b> and <b>Software Engineering</b>, and state <b>ONE</b> commonality between them. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how membership of <b>ONE</b> named professional organisation could benefit the visiting software developer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Outline the history of computer hardware by describing <b>THREE</b> categories of computer, ordered by when they emerged, according to size and processing ability. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Outline <b>THREE</b> developments in the history of the Internet and telecommunications that make the video call possible. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Scope of IT: uses and limitation</b> (4 marks): Two uses and a genuine limitation, all in context.</li><li><b>(b) Computer Science versus Software Engineering</b> (3 marks): A clear distinction with a valid commonality.</li><li><b>(c) Professional organisation</b> (2 marks): Organisation named with a specific benefit.</li><li><b>(d) History of computer hardware</b> (3 marks): Three categories in order with size and processing ability.</li><li><b>(e) Internet and telecommunications history</b> (3 marks): Three accurate developments linked to the video call.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Information Technology is the use of computers, software and telecommunications to capture, store, process and communicate information. <b>Use 1:</b> hotels in Frigate Bay use online booking systems so tourists can reserve rooms and pay from abroad. <b>Use 2:</b> banks provide online and mobile banking so customers can transfer money without visiting a branch. <b>Limitation:</b> IT depends on electricity and communication links; after a hurricane such as Irma in 2017, power and network outages can stop systems completely, and IT cannot replace human judgement in decisions such as which families need help first.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Computer Science</b> focuses on the theory of computation: algorithms, data structures, programming languages and the mathematical foundations of what computers can do. <b>Software Engineering</b> applies engineering principles and a systematic process (requirements, design, testing, maintenance) to build large, reliable software products, usually in teams. <b>Commonality:</b> both disciplines involve designing algorithms and writing programs.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The <b>ACM</b> (Association for Computing Machinery): membership gives access to its digital library of journals and to conferences, so the developer can keep up to date with new techniques, and it publishes a code of ethics that guides professional conduct. (The IEEE Computer Society or BCS would also be acceptable.)</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Mainframes</b> (1950s–1960s): room-sized machines, first built with vacuum tubes and later transistors, used by governments and large firms for batch processing. <b>Minicomputers</b> (1960s–1970s): cabinet-sized, cheaper machines with less processing power, used by departments and smaller organisations. <b>Microcomputers</b> (1970s–1980s onward): desktop personal computers made possible by the microprocessor, cheap enough for homes and schools; these have since shrunk into laptops, tablets and smartphones with far greater processing ability.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>ARPANET (1969):</b> the first packet-switched network linking computers at different sites, the forerunner of the Internet. <b>TCP/IP (adopted in 1983):</b> a common set of protocols that allowed different networks to interconnect into one Internet. <b>The World Wide Web (1989–1991):</b> Tim Berners-Lee's system of hyperlinked pages viewed in a browser made the Internet easy to use. Undersea fibre-optic cables and mobile broadband now give the bandwidth needed for live video between St. Kitts and Guyana.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: giving uses with no Caribbean context; describing Software Engineering as \"writing software\" without the engineering process; inventing professional organisations; listing generations without saying what changed in size and processing ability; confusing the Internet (the network) with the World Wide Web (the hyperlinked documents on it). Dates are useful but explanation earns the marks.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q2: representing data and justifying IT tools","q":"<p><b>Salt Pond Sailing School</b> in Nanny Cay, Tortola, trains young sailors. Instructors teach basic distress signals, show tide graphs, and store student records on a computer. The school's office manager, Mr. Brooks, wants better communication with parents in Tortola, Virgin Gorda and Anegada, and needs to send large video files of races to a sponsor.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>analogue</b> and <b>digital</b> signals, giving <b>ONE</b> example of EACH from the sailing school. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><ol type=\"i\"><li>The character 'C' has the ASCII code 67. Write this code as an 8-bit binary number. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>The distress signal SOS is sent in Morse code as ··· ––– ···. Explain why Morse code is a suitable way to represent this message at sea. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Identify <b>ONE</b> special-purpose notation and <b>ONE</b> graphical representation the instructors could use, and state what information each would show. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Justify the use of <b>THREE</b> Internet tools by the school, giving <b>ONE</b> advantage and <b>ONE</b> disadvantage of EACH. Include a tool for sending the large video files. <i>(6 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Analogue versus digital</b> (3 marks): Both signals defined with relevant examples.</li><li><b>(b) ASCII, binary and Morse code</b> (3 marks): Correct binary and a valid reason for Morse.</li><li><b>(c) Notations and graphical representation</b> (3 marks): Both items identified with what they show.</li><li><b>(d) Justifying three Internet tools</b> (6 marks): Three tools each with an advantage and a disadvantage.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> An <b>analogue</b> signal varies continuously and can take any value within a range, such as the voice signal on the school's VHF marine radio or the needle on a barometer. A <b>digital</b> signal has discrete values, represented as 0s and 1s (on/off), such as the GPS position displayed on the boat's screen or the student records on the office computer.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> 67 = 64 + 2 + 1, so 'C' = <b>01000011</b>. <b>(ii)</b> Morse code represents each letter with only two signal elements (dots and dashes), so SOS can be sent by flashing a torch, sounding a horn or tapping on a radio when a voice cannot be heard clearly; the short, internationally recognised pattern is easy to recognise even when the signal is weak or noisy.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Special-purpose notation:</b> scientific or mathematical notation, for example a formula for calculating the boat's speed in knots from distance and time. <b>Graphical representation:</b> a line graph of tide height against time of day, which shows instructors when the water in Road Harbour is deep enough to sail.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Mailing list (e-mail):</b> advantage: one message reaches all parents in Tortola, Virgin Gorda and Anegada at once at almost no cost; disadvantage: messages may go to spam or be ignored. <b>Web-conferencing tool (e.g. Zoom or Teams):</b> advantage: instructors can hold a live parents' meeting across three islands without travel; disadvantage: it needs a stable, fast Internet connection, which may fail in bad weather. <b>FTP (file upload/download):</b> advantage: transfers very large race video files to the sponsor's server, which e-mail attachments cannot handle; disadvantage: plain FTP sends data unencrypted and requires the sponsor to set up server accounts.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: describing analogue as \"old\" and digital as \"new\"; binary of 67 written with 7 bits only (accept, but CAPE asks for 8 bits here) or with the bits reversed; calling a pie chart a \"special-purpose notation\"; tools without a reason, or advantages that are not linked to the school (distance between islands, file size). \"Justify\" requires a reason for every choice.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q3: information systems and decision-making levels","q":"<p><b>Sunrise Foods</b> runs supermarkets in Kingston, Spanish Town and Mandeville. Each checkout scans items and updates stock. Store managers receive weekly sales reports. The board is deciding whether to open a fourth store in Montego Bay or in Ocho Rios, and wants a system to model the costs and likely sales of each option.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe the <b>transaction processing system (TPS)</b> at a Sunrise Foods checkout, identifying its major input and output. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between a <b>management information system (MIS)</b> and a <b>decision support system (DSS)</b>, using an example of each from Sunrise Foods. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>For EACH of the following, describe the report and give an example that a Sunrise Foods manager could use: <ol type=\"i\"><li>summarised report <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>exception report <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Explain how the nature of information differs between the <b>strategic</b> and <b>operational</b> levels at Sunrise Foods, referring to <b>structured</b> and <b>unstructured</b> decisions. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Transaction processing system</b> (3 marks): TPS described with correct input and output.</li><li><b>(b) MIS versus DSS</b> (4 marks): Both systems defined with Sunrise Foods examples.</li><li><b>(c) Summarised and exception reports</b> (4 marks): Both reports described with examples.</li><li><b>(d) Strategic versus operational information</b> (4 marks): Both levels contrasted with decision types and examples.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> A <b>TPS</b> records and processes the routine, day-to-day transactions of the business. At a Sunrise Foods checkout the <b>input</b> is the scanned barcode of each item, the quantity and the customer's payment. The TPS looks up prices, totals the sale and reduces the stock level. Its <b>output</b> is a printed receipt and processed transactions (updated stock and sales records) that feed other systems.</p><p><b>(b)</b> An <b>MIS</b> takes data from the TPS and produces routine, scheduled reports for middle (tactical) managers to monitor and control operations, for example a weekly report comparing sales in the Kingston, Spanish Town and Mandeville stores. A <b>DSS</b> provides interactive models and data analysis to support semi-structured or unstructured decisions, allowing \"what-if\" questions; for example, the board can enter estimated rents, staffing and population figures to compare the likely profit of a store in Montego Bay with one in Ocho Rios.</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> A <b>summarised report</b> condenses detailed data into totals or key figures, giving an overview rather than every transaction; for example, total sales for each department in each store for the week. <b>(ii)</b> An <b>exception report</b> lists only the items that fall outside normal limits so managers can act on them; for example, products whose stock has fallen below the reorder level, or a store whose daily takings are much lower than usual.</p><p><b>(d)</b> At the <b>operational</b> level, supervisors need detailed, internal, current information to make <b>structured</b> decisions that are routine and follow clear rules, for example reordering bread when stock falls below 40 loaves. At the <b>strategic</b> level, the board needs summarised information, often from external sources (population, competitors, tourism trends), covering long periods and the future, to make <b>unstructured</b> decisions that have no set procedure and need judgement, such as where to open a fourth store.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: describing a TPS as \"a till\" (a device, not a system); saying an MIS \"makes decisions\"; confusing an exception report with an ad hoc (on-request) report; matching structured decisions to the strategic level. Use the syllabus terms <b>strategic, tactical, operational</b> and <b>structured, semi-structured, unstructured</b>.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q4: network models, troubleshooting and wireless security","q":"<p><b>Pelican Rest Guesthouse</b> in Portsmouth offers free Wi-Fi. A guest complains that her laptop shows \"Connected, no Internet\". The owner also discovers that the router still uses the default administrator password and that the guest network uses the old WEP setting.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><ol type=\"i\"><li>List the <b>SEVEN</b> layers of the OSI model in order, starting from Layer 1. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>For EACH of the following protocols, name the TCP/IP model layer at which it operates and state its function: HTTP, TCP, IP. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Describe <b>FOUR</b> steps the owner could take to troubleshoot the guest's \"Connected, no Internet\" problem, naming the command or check used in each step. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend <b>FOUR</b> wireless security measures the owner should configure, explaining how each protects the network. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)(i) OSI layers</b> (2 marks): All seven layers in order.</li><li><b>(a)(ii) TCP/IP layers and protocol functions</b> (3 marks): Correct layers and functions for all three.</li><li><b>(b) Troubleshooting steps</b> (6 marks): Four steps, each with a command or check and its purpose.</li><li><b>(c) Wireless security measures</b> (4 marks): Four specific measures, each explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)(i)</b> Layer 1 Physical, Layer 2 Data Link, Layer 3 Network, Layer 4 Transport, Layer 5 Session, Layer 6 Presentation, Layer 7 Application.</p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> <b>HTTP</b> operates at the <b>Application</b> layer; it requests and transfers web pages between browser and web server. <b>TCP</b> operates at the <b>Transport</b> layer; it breaks data into segments and provides reliable, ordered delivery by acknowledging and resending lost segments. <b>IP</b> operates at the <b>Internet</b> layer; it gives each packet source and destination IP addresses and routes it across networks.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Step 1 – physical connections and status lights:</b> check that the router and modem are powered and that the Internet/WAN light is on; if it is red or off, the fault is with the telephone line or modem, not the laptop, so restart them. <b>Step 2 – ipconfig:</b> run ipconfig on the laptop to view its IP address and default gateway; an address beginning 169.254 means the laptop did not receive an address from the router, so the router's DHCP should be checked or restarted. <b>Step 3 – ping:</b> ping the gateway (e.g. 192.168.1.1) and then a public IP address such as 8.8.8.8; replies from the gateway but not the public address show the LAN works but the Internet link is down. <b>Step 4 – nslookup:</b> run nslookup on a domain name; if the IP address pings but the name cannot be resolved, the DNS server setting is wrong and should be changed. (A speed test or checking firewall settings that block the laptop are also valid steps.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Replace WEP with WPA3 (or WPA2) using AES encryption:</b> WEP can be cracked in minutes, whereas WPA3 with AES encrypts traffic so eavesdroppers cannot read it. <b>Set a long, strong network key (passphrase):</b> mixed case, numbers and symbols make it hard to guess. <b>Change the default router password:</b> otherwise anyone who knows the factory default could log in and change settings. <b>Enable MAC filtering on the staff network and use a separate guest SSID:</b> only listed device MAC addresses can join the staff network, and guests are kept apart from the owner's booking PC.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: OSI layers out of order or \"Internet\" listed as an OSI layer; placing IP at the Transport layer; troubleshooting steps such as \"call the technician\" or \"check the Wi-Fi\" with no command named; describing ping without saying what the result shows; writing \"add a password\" instead of naming WPA2/WPA3, AES, network key and router password.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q5: development models, SDLC deliverables and fact-finding","q":"<p><b>Island Hopper Ferries</b> runs daily ferries from Kingstown to Bequia, Mustique and Union Island. Bookings are taken by phone and written in a ledger, leading to double bookings. The owner wants an online booking system within six months, but is unsure exactly what features customers want and expects to change requirements as the busy cruise season approaches.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between the <b>Waterfall</b> model and <b>Agile</b> software development. Recommend ONE for Island Hopper Ferries and justify your choice. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>For <b>THREE</b> stages of the systems development life cycle (SDLC), name the stage and state the deliverable produced at that stage. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Discuss <b>TWO</b> fact-finding tools the analyst could use during analysis, giving <b>ONE</b> advantage of EACH in this scenario. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Waterfall versus Agile with recommendation</b> (5 marks): Clear contrast and a justified recommendation.</li><li><b>(b) SDLC stages and deliverables</b> (6 marks): Three stages each with a correct deliverable.</li><li><b>(c) Fact-finding tools</b> (4 marks): Two tools discussed with scenario-linked advantages.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The <b>Waterfall</b> model is a linear, sequential approach: each stage (analysis, design, development, testing, implementation) is completed and signed off before the next begins, and requirements are fixed early. It is easy to manage and well documented but cannot easily accommodate changes. <b>Agile</b> development is iterative and incremental: the team delivers working parts of the system in short cycles (sprints), and the customer reviews each one, so requirements can change during development. <b>Recommendation: Agile.</b> The owner is unsure which features customers want and expects requirements to change before the cruise season; Agile lets a basic booking feature go live early to stop double bookings, then adds features based on feedback within the six months.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Feasibility study:</b> deliverable – a <b>system proposal</b> (feasibility report) recommending whether the project is technically, economically and operationally worth doing. <b>Design:</b> deliverable – <b>diagrams and charts</b> (DFDs, screen and database designs) and a design specification. <b>Implementation:</b> deliverable – a <b>conversion plan</b> (e.g. parallel running of the ledger and the new system) and <b>documentation</b> such as the user manual and technical manual. (Others: project plan from planning/analysis; test plans from development.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Questionnaires:</b> sent to regular passengers and travel agents. Advantage: they gather the views of many customers on St. Vincent and the other islands quickly and cheaply, which helps decide which booking features are needed. <b>Interviews:</b> with the owner and booking clerks. Advantage: the analyst can ask follow-up questions to understand in depth how bookings are taken and why double bookings happen. (Observation of clerks taking phone bookings or reviewing the ledger are also valid.)</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: describing Agile as \"faster\" without explaining iterations and feedback; recommending a model without referring to the scenario; giving deliverables from the wrong stage (e.g. \"user manual\" at feasibility); naming tools such as Gantt charts or DFDs when the question asks for <b>fact-finding</b> tools; advantages that could apply to any business.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 1 Q6: representing and developing an algorithm; algorithms and programming","q":"<p><b>Harbour Hop Water Taxi</b> carries passengers across St. John's harbour from the cruise pier to Redcliffe Quay. The fare is $5 for a child (under 12) and $10 for anyone aged 12 or over. At each trip the operator enters the age of each passenger in turn and enters −1 when boarding is complete.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Identify <b>THREE</b> ways of representing an algorithm and state <b>ONE</b> advantage of pseudocode over natural language. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Develop a pseudocode algorithm that reads passengers' ages until −1 is entered, counts the number of children and the number of adults, calculates the total fare, and outputs the two counts and the total fare. <i>(7 marks)</i></li><li>Outline the relationship between algorithms and programming, referring to the stages of program development and naming <b>ONE</b> programming paradigm with an example language. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Ways of representing algorithms</b> (4 marks): Three representations and a valid advantage.</li><li><b>(b) Pseudocode algorithm</b> (7 marks): Correct initialisation, sentinel loop, selection, processing and output.</li><li><b>(c) Algorithms and programming</b> (4 marks): Relationship explained, stages referred to, paradigm and language named.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Algorithms can be represented as (1) <b>natural language</b> (numbered steps in plain English), (2) <b>flowcharts</b> and (3) <b>pseudocode</b>. Pseudocode is more precise and less ambiguous than natural language because it uses a limited set of structured keywords (INPUT, IF, WHILE, OUTPUT) and indentation, so it can be translated directly into program code.</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre>START\n  children = 0\n  adults = 0\n  total = 0\n  INPUT age\n  WHILE age &lt;&gt; -1 DO\n     IF age &lt; 12 THEN\n        children = children + 1\n        total = total + 5\n     ELSE\n        adults = adults + 1\n        total = total + 10\n     ENDIF\n     INPUT age\n  ENDWHILE\n  OUTPUT \"Children: \", children\n  OUTPUT \"Adults: \", adults\n  OUTPUT \"Total fare: $\", total\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(c)</b> An algorithm is the <b>precursor</b> to a program: it is the step-by-step plan for solving the problem, designed and checked (for example with a trace table) before any code is written. A program is the algorithm translated into a programming language that a computer can execute. The stages of program development are: define and analyse the problem, design the algorithm, code the program, test and debug it, document it and maintain it, so the algorithm is produced at the design stage and drives the coding stage. Programs are written within a <b>programming paradigm</b>; for example, the <b>object-oriented</b> paradigm organises programs as objects with data and methods, and <b>Java</b> or Python are object-oriented languages (procedural: Pascal or C).</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: forgetting to initialise the counters and total; no INPUT inside the loop (an infinite loop) or the input only inside the loop so the first −1 is processed; using age &lt;= 12 for children (12-year-olds pay $10); counting the −1 as a passenger; outputting inside the loop. For (c), \"an algorithm is a set of steps\" alone does not explain the relationship.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_87n","title":"Paper 02 · Mock 2","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 · Paper 02 · Mock 2 (original practice paper)</b></p><ol><li>This paper consists of THREE sections and SIX questions: Section I (Module 1), Section II (Module 2) and Section III (Module 3).</li><li>Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 15 marks.</li><li>Time: 2 hours 30 minutes. Plan about 25 minutes per question.</li><li>Label every part (a), (b), (i), (ii) and use the command word: <i>state</i> and <i>identify</i> need a short answer; <i>describe</i>, <i>explain</i>, <i>distinguish</i> and <i>justify</i> need developed points.</li><li>Where a question asks for a diagram (network, DFD, flowchart or sketch), draw it on paper and describe it in your answer box: name each symbol or device, its label and what it connects to.</li></ol><p>Mock 2 examines information sources and selection criteria, data quality and manual versus automated processing, software types, users and HCI evaluation, the structure of the Web and HTML, categorising information, Level 1 DFDs and DFD errors, and algorithm characteristics, tracing and development.</p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q1: information sources and selection criteria","q":"<p>Keisha, a first-year student at Flamboyant Heights College, is researching how communities in the British Virgin Islands rebuilt after Hurricane Irma in 2017. She has found a government recovery report, a refereed journal article on Caribbean disaster recovery, a blog by a Road Town resident, a Wikipedia article, and many posts in a local Facebook group. She also plans to interview her grandmother, who lived in East End, Tortola, during the storm.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>primary</b> and <b>secondary</b> sources of information, giving <b>ONE</b> example of EACH from Keisha's research. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Discuss <b>ONE</b> advantage and <b>ONE</b> disadvantage of EACH of the following as information sources for Keisha: <ol type=\"i\"><li>a wiki <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>social media <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol></li><li>Identify <b>THREE</b> characteristics of information sources, and explain how EACH applies to the refereed journal article. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> criteria Keisha should use to decide whether to accept the resident's blog as a source. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Primary versus secondary</b> (4 marks): Both definitions with correctly matched examples.</li><li><b>(b) Wiki and social media</b> (4 marks): Balanced advantages and disadvantages for both.</li><li><b>(c) Characteristics of sources</b> (3 marks): Three characteristics applied to the article.</li><li><b>(d) Criteria for accepting the blog</b> (4 marks): Two criteria explained and applied to the blog.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> A <b>primary source</b> provides original, first-hand information collected by someone who experienced or observed the event, for example Keisha's interview with her grandmother about East End during Irma (or residents' own posts with photos). A <b>secondary source</b> interprets, summarises or analyses primary information produced by others, for example the Wikipedia article on Hurricane Irma or the journal article reviewing Caribbean recovery.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> <b>Wiki – advantage:</b> it is free, easy to search and broad, and its references can lead Keisha to other sources. <b>Disadvantage:</b> anyone can edit it, so content may be inaccurate or changed at any time and is not refereed. <b>(ii)</b> <b>Social media – advantage:</b> the Facebook group gives current, first-hand accounts and photographs from many residents. <b>Disadvantage:</b> posts are not verified, may be emotional or biased and can spread rumours, so they are unreliable as evidence.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Reliability:</b> the journal article is refereed (peer-reviewed by experts), so it is highly reliable. <b>Amount of detail (depth):</b> it provides detailed analysis with data and references rather than a brief overview. <b>Cost/availability:</b> it may sit behind a subscription, but Keisha may access it through the university library's electronic databases.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Bias:</b> the blog presents one resident's personal opinions and may reflect only his area's experience or a grievance against the government, so Keisha should recognise it as one viewpoint and balance it with other sources. <b>Refereed versus un-refereed (accuracy):</b> the blog is un-refereed, so no expert has checked its facts; Keisha should cross-check figures such as the number of homes repaired against the government report before accepting them. (Currency and cultural context are also acceptable.)</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: calling any Internet source \"secondary\" (a resident's eyewitness post is primary); disadvantages such as \"it is on the Internet\"; mixing up <b>characteristics</b> of sources (SO 1.5: availability, cost, currency, depth, breadth, reliability, format and medium) with <b>criteria</b> for selection (SO 1.6: bias, accuracy, cultural context, completeness, currency, refereed); naming a criterion without applying it to the blog.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q2: data quality, knowledge and manual versus automated processing","q":"<p><b>Harbour Gems Jewellers</b> in George Town records every sale on a carbon-copy receipt book and keeps stock on index cards. Each Friday the owner, Mrs. Richardson, adds up the week's sales by hand and asks staff what customers have been saying. She is moving to a computerised point-of-sale (POS) system with a barcode scanner and a stock database before the next cruise season.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>qualitative</b> and <b>quantitative</b> data, giving <b>ONE</b> example of EACH from the jewellery shop. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Explain how <b>knowledge</b> differs from <b>information</b>, using a decision Mrs. Richardson might make. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>THREE</b> characteristics of information that determine its value to Mrs. Richardson. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Compare manual and automated processing in the shop at <b>THREE</b> stages of the information processing cycle. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Explain the role of <b>feedback</b> in the information processing cycle. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Qualitative versus quantitative</b> (2 marks): Both terms defined with examples.</li><li><b>(b) Knowledge versus information</b> (3 marks): Clear difference with a decision example.</li><li><b>(c) Characteristics of information</b> (3 marks): Three characteristics explained in context.</li><li><b>(d) Manual versus automated processing</b> (6 marks): Three stages compared, each with the manual and automated method.</li><li><b>(e) Feedback</b> (1 marks): Correct role of feedback.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Qualitative</b> data is opinion-based and subjective, describing qualities, for example customers commenting that the new silver bracelets look \"elegant but overpriced\". <b>Quantitative</b> data is measurement-based and objective, for example \"14 gold rings sold on Tuesday\" or the price of each item.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Information</b> is data that has been processed so it has meaning, for example a report showing that ring sales double on days when three or more cruise ships are in port. <b>Knowledge</b> is the understanding gained from information, combined with experience, and its use in decision making: Mrs. Richardson knows from that report and past seasons that she should increase ring stock and schedule extra staff before the cruise calendar's busiest weeks.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Timeliness:</b> information is valuable only if it arrives in time to act; Friday hand totals are too late to restock popular items for Wednesday's ships. <b>Accuracy/reliability:</b> errors when adding receipts by hand give a false picture of sales, which can lead to over-ordering expensive stock. <b>Completeness:</b> if some receipt copies are missing, the totals are incomplete and decisions are based on part of the picture.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Input:</b> manually, the sales clerk handwrites each sale on a receipt book, which is slow and error-prone; with automation, the barcode scanner captures the item code and price instantly and accurately. <b>Processing:</b> manually, Mrs. Richardson collates and adds the week's receipts by hand; automated, the POS calculates totals, sorts sales by item and updates stock automatically after each sale. <b>Storage and retrieval:</b> manually, stock cards and receipt books are filed in a cabinet and searching takes time and they may be lost or damaged; automated, data is stored in a database that can be searched in seconds and backed up off-site.</p><p><b>(e)</b> <b>Feedback</b> is the evaluation of output (for example, a stock report that does not match a physical count), which loops back to the input stage so that data can be corrected or updated.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: describing qualitative data as \"quality data\"; defining knowledge as \"more information\"; naming characteristics without linking them to Mrs. Richardson's decisions; comparing only the automated system (each stage needs <b>both</b> sides to compare); omitting that feedback loops back to <b>input</b>.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q3: software, users and evaluating an accessible kiosk","q":"<p>The <b>Seaside Community Health Centre</b> in Willemstad has installed a self-service check-in kiosk with a touch screen. The kiosk runs an operating system and a check-in application; a separate embedded system controls the queue-number display. Many patients are elderly, some are visually impaired, and visitors speak English, Dutch, Spanish or Papiamentu. Patients report that the buttons are small, the grey text on a white background is hard to read, the \"Back\" button moves position on each screen and there is no way to return to the start.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>application software</b> and an <b>embedded system</b>, using examples from the kiosk. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Explain the roles of an <b>IT professional</b> and of an <b>end user</b> in the kiosk system, and distinguish between <b>expert</b> and <b>novice</b> users. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe <b>THREE</b> ways the kiosk interface could be adapted to the characteristics of its users. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Evaluate the current kiosk interface using <b>FOUR</b> HCI principles. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Application software versus embedded system</b> (4 marks): Both defined with kiosk examples.</li><li><b>(b) Roles of users</b> (4 marks): Both roles and the expert/novice contrast are clear.</li><li><b>(c) Adapting the interface</b> (3 marks): Three adaptations linked to user characteristics.</li><li><b>(d) Evaluating the interface</b> (4 marks): Four principles applied with judgements.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Application software</b> is a program designed to carry out a specific task for the user; the check-in application lets patients enter their name or date of birth and confirm their appointment. An <b>embedded system</b> is a dedicated computer system built into a device to monitor or control one function; the controller inside the queue-number display receives the next number and shows it on the screen. The user does not install or run it as a separate program, and it cannot be used for other tasks.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The <b>IT professional</b> (for example the health centre's technician or the software supplier) installs, configures, maintains and secures the kiosk and fixes faults. The <b>end user</b> (patients and reception staff) uses the system to complete tasks such as checking in and does not need technical knowledge. An <b>expert user</b> is experienced and confident, uses the system often and may use shortcuts (a receptionist overriding a check-in), whereas a <b>novice user</b> has little experience, such as a first-time patient, and needs clear prompts, simple steps and help.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Age:</b> larger buttons and text, with fewer options per screen, for elderly patients with reduced eyesight or dexterity. <b>Disability:</b> a non-visual interface for visually impaired users, such as speech output through a headphone socket and tactile or Braille keys, plus a high-contrast mode. <b>Cultural differences:</b> a language choice (English, Dutch, Spanish, Papiamento) on the first screen and universally understood icons.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Ease of use:</b> poor, because the small buttons are hard to press accurately, especially for elderly patients. <b>Ease of navigation:</b> poor, because there is no way to return to the start, so a patient who makes a mistake is stuck. <b>Alignment:</b> poor, because the Back button moves position on each screen, so users cannot predict where controls are; it should be fixed in the same place. <b>Use of colour:</b> poor, because grey text on white has low contrast and is hard to read; dark text on a light background with a strong accent colour for buttons would be better. Overall the interface is not suitable for its users and should be redesigned.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: calling the operating system \"application software\"; describing an embedded system as \"software inside the computer\"; saying novices are \"not smart\"; adaptations not linked to a characteristic; \"evaluate\" answers that only describe. Each evaluation point should name the principle, give the evidence and make a judgement.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q4: the structure of the Web and developing a page in HTML","q":"<p><b>Calypso Café</b> on Broad Street wants a simple website. Its address will be <b>https://www.calypsocafe.bb/menu.html</b>. The owner, Mr. Lake, asks how a customer's browser finds the page and wants a menu page he can update himself.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Distinguish between <b>HTML</b> and <b>XML</b>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>For the URL https://www.calypsocafe.bb/menu.html, identify the <b>protocol</b>, the <b>domain name</b> and the <b>file</b> requested, and state the purpose of the protocol. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how <b>domain name resolution</b> enables a customer's browser to load the page. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Write the HTML for the menu page. It must include: the document structure with the title \"Calypso Café Menu\"; a level-1 heading; a paragraph with the word \"fresh\" in bold; an unordered list of three dishes; an image file <i>johnnycake.jpg</i> with alternative text; a hyperlink to <i>booking.html</i>; a comment; and inline styling that makes the heading dark blue. <i>(7 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) HTML versus XML</b> (2 marks): Both sides of the distinction.</li><li><b>(b) Parts of the URL</b> (3 marks): All three parts correct with the purpose of HTTPS.</li><li><b>(c) Domain name resolution</b> (3 marks): Complete sequence from name to page request.</li><li><b>(d) HTML menu page</b> (7 marks): A complete, correctly nested page meeting every requirement.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>HTML</b> (HyperText Markup Language) uses a fixed set of predefined tags to structure and display content such as headings, images and links in a web browser. <b>XML</b> (eXtensible Markup Language) uses tags that the author defines to describe, store and transport data (for example &lt;dish&gt;&lt;price&gt;), and does not specify how the data is displayed.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Protocol:</b> https. <b>Domain name:</b> calypsocafe.bb (www is a prefix). <b>File:</b> menu.html. HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure) is the set of rules for transferring web pages between the web server and the browser, with the data encrypted so that it cannot be read in transit.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Computers locate each other by numeric IP addresses, not names. When the customer enters the URL, the browser sends the domain name www.calypsocafe.bb to a <b>DNS server</b>. The DNS server looks up the name (asking other DNS servers if necessary) and <b>returns the matching IP address</b>. The browser then connects to the web server at that IP address and sends an HTTPS request for menu.html, which the server returns for the browser to display.</p><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Calypso Café Menu&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Menu page: update dishes below each week --&gt;\n  &lt;h1 style=\"color: darkblue;\"&gt;Calypso Café Menu&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;All our dishes are made with &lt;b&gt;fresh&lt;/b&gt; local ingredients.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Johnnycakes and saltfish&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Conch and dumplings&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;Passion fruit cheesecake&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;img src=\"johnnycake.jpg\" alt=\"Golden johnnycakes on a plate\" width=\"300\"&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=\"booking.html\"&gt;Book a table&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: saying XML \"replaces\" HTML; giving \"calypsocafe\" without the top-level domain; describing DNS as \"the server that hosts the website\"; placing &lt;title&gt; in the body; using &lt;ol&gt; when an unordered list is asked for; &lt;img&gt; without alt; writing &lt;a&gt;booking.html&lt;/a&gt; without href; inline style written as style=color:darkblue (missing quotes) or as a separate CSS block (the syllabus asks for inline styling with the style attribute); improper nesting such as &lt;b&gt;&lt;p&gt;…&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q5: categorising information, DFD errors and a Level 1 DFD","q":"<p><b>Palm Beach Scooter Rentals</b> is replacing its paper rental book. In the new system a customer submits a rental request; the system checks the Scooters data store for an available scooter, records the rental in the Rentals data store and issues a rental agreement to the customer. When a scooter is returned, the system calculates the charge from the Rentals data store, sends an invoice to the customer and marks the scooter as available. A mechanic sends service reports that update the Scooters data store.</p><p>A student's draft Level 1 DFD for this system contains the following: [Diagram described: (1) a data flow arrow from the external entity Customer goes directly into the data store D2 Rentals; (2) a process labelled \"3 Monthly Report\" receives data from D2 Rentals but has no output flows; (3) a process labelled \"4 Invoices\" sends an invoice to Customer but has no input flows.]</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Categorise EACH of the following pieces of information as <b>essential</b>, <b>desirable</b>, <b>extraneous</b> or <b>cosmetic</b> for designing the rental system, giving a reason: (i) the daily rental rate for each scooter; (ii) the number of tourists who visited Aruba last year; (iii) a friend's assurance that tourists always return scooters undamaged, given instead of any damage records; (iv) customers' preferred scooter colour. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Identify <b>TWO</b> of the errors in the student's DFD and explain how EACH should be corrected. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Draw a <b>Level 1 DFD</b> (Gane and Sarson) for the rental system with <b>THREE</b> processes. Describe your diagram in words. <i>(7 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Categorising information</b> (4 marks): All four items correctly categorised with reasons.</li><li><b>(b) DFD errors and corrections</b> (4 marks): Two errors named and correctly fixed.</li><li><b>(c) Level 1 DFD</b> (7 marks): Correct Level 1 DFD with three processes, stores and labelled flows.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> (i) The daily rental rate is <b>essential</b>: the system cannot calculate the charge without it. (ii) Last year's tourist numbers are <b>extraneous</b>: they are not needed to design the rental system. (iii) The friend's assurance about undamaged scooters is <b>cosmetic</b>: it looks like useful information but masks the missing damage records, so it cannot guide the design. (iv) Customers' preferred scooter colour is <b>desirable</b>: it could improve service but the system works without it.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Error 1 – entity connected directly to a data store:</b> the flow from Customer goes straight into D2 Rentals. Data cannot move between an entity and a store without a process; correct it by sending the rental request to process 1 \"Process rental request\", which then writes the rental details to D2 Rentals. <b>Error 2 – black hole:</b> process 3 \"Monthly Report\" has input but no output, so data disappears. Add an output flow (e.g. <i>monthly rental report</i> to a Manager entity) and relabel the process with a verb phrase, \"Produce monthly report\". (Error 3 – <b>miracle</b>: process 4 \"Invoices\" produces an invoice with no input; add an input flow of <i>rental details</i> from D2 Rentals and relabel it \"Calculate invoice\".)</p><p><b>(c)</b> [Diagram described, Gane and Sarson] External entities (squares): <b>Customer</b>, <b>Mechanic</b>. Data stores (open-ended rectangles): <b>D1 Scooters</b>, <b>D2 Rentals</b>. Processes (rounded rectangles):</p><ul><li><b>1 Process rental request:</b> Customer → <i>rental request</i> → 1. D1 Scooters → <i>available scooter details</i> → 1. 1 → <i>rental details</i> → D2 Rentals. 1 → <i>scooter status (rented)</i> → D1 Scooters. 1 → <i>rental agreement</i> → Customer.</li><li><b>2 Process scooter return:</b> Customer → <i>returned scooter details</i> → 2. D2 Rentals → <i>rental record</i> → 2. 2 → <i>invoice</i> → Customer. 2 → <i>scooter status (available)</i> → D1 Scooters.</li><li><b>3 Update service record:</b> Mechanic → <i>service report</i> → 3. 3 → <i>updated service details</i> → D1 Scooters.</li></ul><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: mixing up desirable (useful) and cosmetic (looks useful but masks missing information); saying \"the DFD is wrong\" without naming the error type; corrections that remove the process instead of adding the missing flow; Level 1 diagrams with processes named as nouns (\"Rentals\"), unlabelled flows, entities connected to stores, or a store that is written to but never read. Use one notation (Gane and Sarson) throughout.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Mock 2 Q6: well-designed algorithms, trace tables and developing an algorithm","q":"<p>A weather club at a school in Dominica records daily rainfall (mm) at Canefield. Its algorithm is shown below. The value −1 ends the input.</p><pre>total = 0\ndays = 0\nwet = 0\nINPUT rain\nWHILE rain &lt;&gt; -1 DO\n   total = total + rain\n   days = days + 1\n   IF rain &gt; 10 THEN\n      wet = wet + 1\n   ENDIF\n   INPUT rain\nENDWHILE\navg = total / days\nOUTPUT days, wet, avg</pre><ol type=\"a\"><li>Explain <b>THREE</b> characteristics of a well-designed algorithm. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Complete a trace table for the algorithm using the inputs 4, 12, 0, 25, 9, −1, and state the output. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Develop a pseudocode algorithm, using a <b>FOR</b> loop, that reads the marks of the 25 students in a class, outputs \"Pass\" or \"Fail\" for each student (the pass mark is 50), and finally outputs the highest mark. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Distinguish between <b>natural language</b> and a <b>flowchart</b> as ways of representing an algorithm. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","points":15,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Characteristics of a well-designed algorithm</b> (3 marks): Three characteristics explained.</li><li><b>(b) Trace table and output</b> (5 marks): Correct trace with output 5, 2, 10.</li><li><b>(c) FOR-loop algorithm</b> (5 marks): Correct loop, selection, highest-mark logic and output.</li><li><b>(d) Natural language versus flowchart</b> (2 marks): Both representations contrasted.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Finite number of steps:</b> the algorithm must end after a finite number of steps; here the loop stops when −1 is entered. <b>Clearly defined and unambiguous:</b> each step has only one meaning, so any person or computer following it produces the same result. <b>Flow of control:</b> control passes logically from one process to the next in a defined order, with clear entry to and exit from each loop or decision. (Also: it is a <b>general solution</b> that works for any valid set of inputs, not just one example.)</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><div><table><thead><tr><th>rain</th><th>total</th><th>days</th><th>wet</th><th>avg</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>–</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>16</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>16</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>41</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>50</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>−1</td><td>50</td><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Output: <b>5, 2, 10</b> (5 days recorded, 2 wet days over 10 mm, average 10 mm).</p><p><b>(c)</b></p><pre>START\n  highest = 0\n  FOR count = 1 TO 25 DO\n     INPUT mark\n     IF mark &gt;= 50 THEN\n        OUTPUT \"Pass\"\n     ELSE\n        OUTPUT \"Fail\"\n     ENDIF\n     IF mark &gt; highest THEN\n        highest = mark\n     ENDIF\n  ENDFOR\n  OUTPUT \"Highest mark: \", highest\nSTOP</pre><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Natural language</b> expresses the steps in ordinary written sentences, which are easy for anyone to read but can be ambiguous and lengthy. A <b>flowchart</b> represents the steps graphically using standard symbols (ovals, rectangles, diamonds, parallelograms) joined by arrows, so the flow of control, decisions and loops can be seen at a glance.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p>Common errors: counting 10 as a wet day (the test is &gt; 10, not &gt;= 10); processing −1 as a reading; omitting the initial row of the trace table; using a WHILE loop when a FOR loop is asked for; initialising highest inside the loop; placing OUTPUT highest inside the loop; one IF statement doing both jobs so the highest mark is only updated for passes. In (a), \"it should be easy\" is not a syllabus characteristic.</p>"}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 03 (Paper 032) Practice Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_87r","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2012 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: stock control at the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet, St. Lucia.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2012), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to an information technology problem that you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss the MAIN reason why you researched this problem. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss TWO causes of the problem identified in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Write a problem statement that may form the basis of a solution to the problem you have researched. <i>[3 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> A more detailed analysis of the problem may require the collection of data through direct observation. Outline FOUR guidelines that should be followed when conducting the observation. <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> After the observation, you are still unclear about the way a small group of users work with the existing system. Identify ONE other data gathering technique, clearly explaining how the technique will enhance the research process. <i>[3 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Main reason for researching the problem</b> (2 marks): A clear main reason linked to the effect on the organisation.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) Problem statement</b> (3 marks): Who is affected, how, and what will happen if it is not fixed.</li><li><b>(d) FOUR observation guidelines outlined</b> (8 marks): Four distinct guidelines, each with a reason.</li><li><b>(e) Other technique and how it enhances the research</b> (3 marks): A suitable technique for a SMALL group and how it fills the gap.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The main reason I researched this problem is that the Seaside Pharmacy in Gros Islet was losing sales and putting patients at risk because of poor stock control. Fast-moving medicines such as insulin and children's antibiotics regularly ran out, so customers were sent to other pharmacies, while slow-moving items expired on the shelves. The owner estimated that about EC$4,900 of expired stock was written off in one quarter. Because the problem affected both the pharmacy's income and patients' access to essential medication, it was worth investigating for an IT-based solution.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause of the problem is that stock is recorded manually on paper stock cards that are updated only at closing time. Sales made during the day are not deducted until the evening, and on busy days some sales are never recorded, so the quantity on the card is higher than what is actually on the shelf. As a result, the pharmacist reorders too late and items run out.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for tracking expiry dates or reorder levels. Staff do not apply \"first expiry, first out\" because the cards do not show expiry dates, and no one is alerted when an item falls below its minimum level. This leads to older stock being left at the back of the shelf until it expires, while popular items are not reordered in time.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The pharmacists and counter staff at Seaside Pharmacy cannot tell accurately how much of each medicine is in stock or when it will expire, because stock is recorded by hand on cards that are updated only once a day. If this is not corrected, the pharmacy will continue to lose sales to competitors, write off expired medicine and leave patients without essential medication.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Guideline 1: Obtain permission and inform the staff.</b> I should get written permission from the owner before observing and tell the staff when and why I will be there. This is ethical and prevents staff from feeling they are being spied on.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 2: Plan the objectives and use a checklist.</b> Before observing, I should decide exactly what to look for, such as how deliveries are checked in and when stock cards are updated, and prepare a checklist or record form. This keeps the observation focused and makes the notes easy to analyse.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 3: Observe at different times, including peak periods.</b> I should observe on a quiet weekday morning and on a busy Saturday and delivery day. This shows how the system works under pressure, when most recording errors happen, rather than only on an easy day.</p>\n<p><b>Guideline 4: Be unobtrusive and keep information confidential.</b> I should stand where I do not interfere with serving customers and should not interrupt staff during sales. Any patient or prescription details I see must be kept confidential and left out of my notes, because pharmacy records are private health information.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I would use a structured interview with the three staff members who receive deliveries and update the stock cards. Observation showed me what they do, but not why they skip updating the cards on busy days or how they decide when to reorder. In an interview I can ask them to explain each step, ask probing follow-up questions and clarify anything I did not understand. This enhances the research by filling the gaps left by observation and confirming whether what I saw is normal practice.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), candidates restate the problem instead of the REASON for researching it. In (c), a problem statement without its three parts (who, how, what if not fixed) earns 1 mark. In (d), \"outline\" needs a guideline plus a short reason; four bare phrases earn only 4 of 8. Repeating one guideline in different words (\"be quiet\", \"do not disturb\") counts once. In (e), a questionnaire is a weak choice for a small group; an interview lets you probe.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p><b>(a)</b> Based on the problem statement in Question 1 (c), outline THREE possible solutions. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss THREE criteria that may be used to evaluate the solutions given in 2 (a) above. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Outline TWO reasons why it is the usual practice to identify more than one alternative solution. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Discuss TWO ways in which an enhanced information technology solution may impact positively on users. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions outlined</b> (6 marks): Three different IT solutions, each with a brief description.</li><li><b>(b) THREE evaluation criteria discussed</b> (6 marks): Technical, economic and operational criteria, each explained.</li><li><b>(c) TWO reasons for several alternatives</b> (4 marks): Comparison for the best choice and a fall-back.</li><li><b>(d) TWO positive impacts on users</b> (4 marks): Two impacts on users, each explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A custom-built stock database.</b> A local developer would build a Microsoft Access database with tables for products, suppliers and deliveries. Staff would enter sales and deliveries during the day, and the database would produce a reorder report and an expiry report each morning.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial off-the-shelf pharmacy point-of-sale (POS) and inventory package.</b> The pharmacy would purchase a pharmacy POS package with barcode scanners. Each sale scanned at the counter is deducted from stock immediately, expiry dates are stored for each batch, and the system alerts staff when items fall below reorder level.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A cloud-based inventory subscription (SaaS).</b> The pharmacy would subscribe to an online inventory service accessed through a web browser on a tablet and the counter PC. Stock levels are stored online, and the service emails the pharmacist and suppliers when items need to be reordered.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Criterion 1: Technical feasibility.</b> This asks whether the hardware, software and technical skills needed for a solution are available. For example, the cloud solution needs a reliable Internet connection, while the POS package needs barcode scanners and a counter PC. A solution that the pharmacy cannot technically support should be rejected.</p>\n<p><b>Criterion 2: Economic feasibility.</b> This compares the cost of a solution (purchase, licences, hardware, training, maintenance) with the benefits it brings, such as fewer expired items written off and fewer lost sales. A solution is economically feasible if its benefits outweigh its costs within a reasonable time.</p>\n<p><b>Criterion 3: Operational feasibility.</b> This asks whether the staff will accept and use the solution in their daily work. A system that slows down serving at the counter, or that staff find difficult, will be bypassed and will fail, even if it is technically excellent.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, identifying more than one solution allows the solutions to be compared, so that the optimal, best-value solution is chosen rather than the first idea. Second, it provides a fall-back: if the chosen solution becomes impossible, for example because the vendor stops supporting the product or funds are reduced, a second evaluated alternative is ready.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> One positive impact is that staff save time and work under less pressure. Stock is deducted automatically when each item is scanned, so staff no longer spend the last hour of the day updating cards and counting shelves, and can spend more time advising patients.</p>\n<p>A second positive impact is more accurate information for decisions. The pharmacist receives an accurate reorder report and expiry alerts, so the right medicines are ordered on time, customers are not turned away and patients receive better service.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> Three solutions that are really one (three brands of the same software) earn one set of marks. In (b), criteria must be named and explained: \"cost\" alone earns 1 mark; \"economic feasibility: whether the benefits outweigh development and running costs\" earns 2. In (d), keep the focus on users: staff time, accuracy, less stress, better service.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>You would have selected one of the solutions as the optimal or MOST feasible.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE reasons why you have chosen this solution. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Outline TWO ways in which cost may increase by implementing a new solution. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You are required to present a professional report on the problem statement, analysis and possible solutions. State the MOST appropriate software application that may be used to prepare this report. Justify your choice. <i>[3 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Outline ONE software and ONE non-software approach that may be used to ensure confidentiality of the documents you have prepared. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE reasons for the chosen solution discussed</b> (9 marks): Three distinct reasons, each developed with evidence from the case.</li><li><b>(b) TWO ways costs may increase</b> (4 marks): Two cost increases, each outlined.</li><li><b>(c) Word processing software named and justified</b> (3 marks): Word processing with features that make a professional report.</li><li><b>(d) ONE software and ONE non-software approach</b> (4 marks): One software and one physical/procedural measure, each outlined.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> I chose Solution 2, the commercial off-the-shelf pharmacy POS and inventory package with barcode scanners.</p>\n<p><b>Reason 1: It is technically proven and can be implemented quickly.</b> The package is already used by many pharmacies, so it has been tested and its features (batch expiry dates, reorder levels, barcode sales) match our requirements. It runs on a standard PC with scanners, which the pharmacy can install in weeks, unlike a custom database that would take months to develop and test.</p>\n<p><b>Reason 2: It is economically feasible.</b> The package has a one-off licence cost plus an annual support fee, which is lower than paying a developer to build and maintain a custom system. The owner estimates that cutting expired stock and lost sales will save more than the annual cost within the first year, so the benefits outweigh the costs.</p>\n<p><b>Reason 3: It is operationally suitable and supported.</b> Staff simply scan items at the counter as they already do with a till, so the new system fits their routine and is easy to accept. The vendor provides training and a help desk, and the package works on the local PC, so sales can continue during Internet outages, which the cloud option could not guarantee.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> First, new hardware must be bought: barcode scanners, a receipt printer, a more powerful counter PC and an uninterruptible power supply (UPS) to protect the system during power cuts. Second, there are training and conversion costs: staff time is needed to attend training and to enter the existing stock, batch numbers and expiry dates into the new system, and the annual licence and support fee must be paid every year.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The most appropriate software is word processing software, such as Microsoft Word. A report on the problem statement, analysis and solutions is a text document, and a word processor provides heading styles, an automatic table of contents, tables for comparing solutions, headers and footers with page numbers and a spelling and grammar check, so the report looks professional and is easy to read.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Software approach:</b> password-protect and encrypt the report file, so that only the owner and I, who know the password, can open it, even if the file is copied to another computer. <b>Non-software approach:</b> keep printed copies in a locked filing cabinet in the owner's office, give copies only to people who need to know, and shred draft copies after use.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), three reasons that all say \"it is cheap\" in different words count once; cover cost, technical fit and users. In (c), this is a written REPORT, so word processing is correct here (unlike the presentation questions in later years). In (d), candidates give two software measures; one must be non-software (locked cabinet, controlled copies, shredding).</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_87v","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2013 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: the cash-only canteen at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Speightstown, Barbados.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2013), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss any TWO causes of the problem described in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identification of possible solutions are two steps in the problem solving process. Explain ONE other step in the problem solving process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH scenario below, identify the most appropriate data gathering instrument. Justify your choice of EACH instrument.</p><p><b>(i)</b> You require feedback from five managers on the limitations of the current manual system. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> You need the answers to 15 questions, intended to gather quantitative data from 100 users in a single company. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> You wish to see how users interact with the current system on a day-to-day basis. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> For EACH of the three instruments identified in (d) above, outline ONE limitation which you should consider. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other problem-solving step</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Instrument chosen and justified for each scenario</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each instrument</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The canteen at Seagrape Bay Secondary School in Speightstown sells lunch to about 600 students using cash only, and each sale is written by hand on a tally sheet. This has resulted in long lunchtime queues, cash shortages at the end of the day, food waste and inaccurate sales reports for the school's bursar.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause of the problem is that every sale is processed manually in cash. The cashier must count change and write each item on the tally sheet, which takes about a minute per student, so queues build up and some students give up and go hungry. Handling cash by hand also leads to counting errors and shortages that cannot be traced.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the canteen keeps no reliable record of what sells each day. Because tally sheets are incomplete and are not totalled by item, the manager guesses how much food to prepare. This leads to popular items such as johnny cakes selling out early while other dishes are thrown away.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step is evaluating the possible solutions and selecting the most feasible one. This involves comparing each solution against criteria such as technical, economic and operational feasibility, so that the solution that best fits the canteen's budget, equipment and staff is chosen for implementation.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because there are only five managers, so I can meet each one face to face, ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and ask follow-up questions to probe and clarify their answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because 100 users can answer the same 15 closed questions at the same time, and their answers produce quantitative data that can be tallied and analysed quickly in a spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> This is the most appropriate instrument because watching the cashier and students during lunch shows how the system is actually used day to day, including problems that users may not mention or may not notice.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that interviews are time-consuming to arrange, conduct and write up, and the managers' answers may be biased, for example if they avoid criticising procedures they introduced.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate, since many users may not return the questionnaire, and closed questions may be misinterpreted with no chance for me to clarify, so the data may be incomplete or inaccurate.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is the Hawthorne effect: the cashier may work faster or more carefully because she knows she is being watched, so the behaviour observed is not typical. Observation also cannot show what users think about the system.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>You have proposed several solutions to the problem you described in Question 1 (a), from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE possible solutions for the problem you described in Question 1 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in 2 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A prepaid student meal card with a commercial canteen point-of-sale package.</b> The school would purchase an off-the-shelf cashless canteen package. Each student receives a barcode ID card linked to a prepaid account that parents top up at the bursary or online. At lunch the cashier scans the card and taps the items on a touch screen, and the cost is deducted. This removes cash from the queue, speeds up each sale and records every item sold, which addresses both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A custom online pre-ordering web app.</b> A local developer would build a web app where students or parents order and pay for lunch before 10 a.m. The kitchen receives a printed list of orders and students collect labelled meals. This reduces the queue and lets the kitchen prepare exactly what has been ordered, reducing food waste.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A spreadsheet-based sales system on a laptop.</b> The canteen would use a laptop with a Microsoft Excel sales template with buttons for each menu item. Cash is still taken, but each sale is entered in the spreadsheet, which totals sales and cash expected per day and produces a weekly chart of item sales for planning. This improves records and cash control at low cost, although payment is still in cash.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1 (prepaid card POS).</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs a touch-screen POS terminal, a barcode scanner and a card printer, all standard equipment that the vendor installs and supports. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the package and hardware cost about BBD$8,000 plus an annual fee, but this is offset by eliminating cash shortages and reducing waste. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; scanning a card is faster than counting change, and students and parents in Barbados are used to card payments.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2 (custom pre-ordering app).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on a developer and on students having Internet access in the morning, and online payments require a merchant account. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; custom development and hosting are expensive for one canteen, and there is ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> weak; students often decide what to eat at lunch time and may forget to pre-order, so a cash queue would remain.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3 (spreadsheet system).</b> <i>Technical:</i> highly feasible; the school already owns laptops and Microsoft 365, and the IT teacher can build the template. <i>Economic:</i> highly feasible; it costs almost nothing. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; staff are familiar with Excel, but entering each sale while handling cash may slow the queue further.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions lets the school compare their costs, benefits and risks, so that it chooses the optimal solution rather than the first idea. It also means a second evaluated solution is available if the preferred one cannot be implemented.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in Question 2 (b). <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies being recommended in the solution stated in 3 (a). <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the remaining alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the remaining solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify the selection of the alternative. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in 3 (a) and specify a means of compensating. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Your teacher has asked you to make a presentation to the class of your entire problem solving process. State the MOST appropriate type of application software to accomplish this. Justify your choice of software. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the prepaid student meal card with a commercial cashless canteen POS package.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Purchasing a commercial off-the-shelf package.</b> This is justified because the package is already tested in other schools, so it is reliable and can be installed during a school holiday, whereas custom development would take months. The vendor provides technical support and updates, reducing maintenance work for the school.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Issuing barcode meal cards linked to prepaid accounts.</b> This removes cash from the lunch queue, which directly solves the cause of slow service and cash shortages. Each scan takes seconds, and because money is paid into the account at the bursary, cash is handled once, in a secure office, and every top-up has a receipt.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Recording every item sold on the POS terminal.</b> Every transaction is stored, so the manager receives daily and weekly sales reports by item. This solves the second cause by allowing food preparation to be planned on real data, reducing waste. The reports also give the bursar accurate figures for auditing.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Training staff and introducing the system in phases.</b> The cashiers and bursary clerk will receive vendor training, and the system will first be used for Form 1 and 2 students before the whole school. This makes the solution operationally feasible because staff gain confidence and problems are fixed early. Finally, accounts are password-protected and backed up daily, so the data is secure.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 3, the spreadsheet-based sales system on a laptop.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the custom pre-ordering app because it can be implemented immediately at almost no cost, using laptops and Microsoft 365 licences the school already owns. Canteen staff are already familiar with Excel, so little training is needed, whereas the app would require a developer, online payments and students remembering to order. The spreadsheet still solves the second cause by recording sales by item for planning and cash checking.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that the spreadsheet system is still cash-based, so the long queues and the risk of cash errors remain, unlike the card system where no cash changes hands at the counter. To compensate, the bursary could sell prepaid paper meal vouchers in the morning, so that most students pay in advance and the cashier only collects vouchers at lunch, and a second till point could be opened during peak lunch time.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint or Google Slides. It allows me to show each stage of the problem-solving process on clear slides with bullet points, charts of the questionnaire results and images of the proposed system, so the class can follow the presentation easily.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_87z","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2014 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: bookings at Blue Reef Divers dive shop in Kralendijk, Bonaire.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2014), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss TWO causes of the problem identified in (a) above. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> For EACH data gathering tool below, explain ONE situation where it could be appropriately used for gathering data.</p><p><b>(i)</b> Interviews</p><p><b>(ii)</b> Questionnaires</p><p><b>(iii)</b> Observation</p><p><i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Imagine that some of the users that you need to gather data from are located in various countries. For EACH of the three tools mentioned in (c) above, discuss ONE way in which it can be implemented using the Internet. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed in depth</b> (6 marks): Two causes, each identified, explained and linked to its effect.</li><li><b>(c) A suitable situation for each tool</b> (6 marks): A realistic situation for each tool from your own problem.</li><li><b>(d) Each tool implemented over the Internet</b> (6 marks): An Internet method for each tool, with how it works.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Blue Reef Divers in Kralendijk takes dive bookings by telephone, email and WhatsApp and writes them by hand in a paper diary. This has resulted in double-booked boat seats and instructors, lost deposits and customers, many of them visitors from the USA, Canada and the Netherlands, arriving to find there is no space for them.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Cause 1: Several uncoordinated booking channels feed a single paper diary.</b> Bookings arrive by phone at the counter, by email to the owner and by WhatsApp to the instructors. Each person writes bookings in the diary only when they return to the shop, sometimes hours later. As a result, two staff members can sell the last seat on the same boat before either has written it down, causing double-bookings and angry customers.</p>\n<p><b>Cause 2: There is no system for recording deposits and confirmations.</b> Deposits are paid in many ways (cash, bank transfer, PayPal) and are noted on loose slips of paper rather than linked to the booking. Staff therefore cannot easily tell which bookings are confirmed. The effect is that unpaid bookings hold seats that could have been sold, while some paid customers are cancelled by mistake, damaging the shop's reputation on review sites.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> <b>Interviews</b> would be appropriate when I need detailed information from the owner and the two senior instructors about how bookings are currently taken and recorded, because there are only a few of them and I need to ask follow-up questions about the problems they experience.</p>\n<p><b>(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaires</b> would be appropriate when gathering the views of about 200 past customers from the shop's email list on how they prefer to book and pay, because many people can answer the same closed questions and the results can be analysed statistically.</p>\n<p><b>(iii)</b> <b>Observation</b> would be appropriate on a busy cruise-ship day, when I can watch the front-desk staff handle phone, walk-in and WhatsApp bookings at the same time and see exactly where the diary falls out of date.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Interviews:</b> I could interview overseas partners, such as travel agents in Toronto and Amsterdam, by video conference using Zoom, Microsoft Teams or a WhatsApp video call. This gives a face-to-face conversation without travel costs, and with permission the call can be recorded for accurate notes.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaires:</b> I could create an online form with Google Forms or Microsoft Forms and send the link by email to past customers in different countries. Customers complete it at a convenient time in their own time zone, and responses are collected automatically into a spreadsheet for analysis.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> I could observe an overseas agent remotely using a screen-sharing session on Zoom or Teams while they book a dive package, or ask them to send a screen recording. This lets me see the steps they actually follow and where delays occur, without being physically present.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (b), 6 marks means each cause must be developed (identify, explain, effect); a two-line list earns 2. In (c), candidates define the tools instead of giving a SITUATION where each fits. In (d), \"use the Internet\" is not a method: name the technology (video conference, online form, screen-sharing) and say how it reaches users in other countries.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>Based on your problem statement in Question 1, you have proposed several solutions, from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> For the problem statement given in Question 1, discuss THREE possible solutions. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in (a) above. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: Subscribe to a cloud-based dive booking system (SaaS).</b> The shop would subscribe to an online booking service designed for dive operators. Customers book and pay a deposit through a booking button on the shop's website and Facebook page, and staff log in from the counter PC or a tablet to add phone and walk-in bookings. Every booking goes into one live calendar that stops selling seats once a boat is full, which removes double-bookings and links each deposit to its booking.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A custom booking module added to the shop's website.</b> A local web developer would build a booking form and database for the existing website, connected to a shared online calendar. It would be designed exactly around the shop's boats, dive sites and instructor schedules, and would email confirmations to customers.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: Commercial off-the-shelf reservation software installed on the shop PC.</b> The shop would purchase a desktop reservation package and install it on the counter PC, with staff entering all bookings received by phone, email and WhatsApp. This replaces the paper diary with one central electronic record, but customers would still have to contact the shop to book.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a browser and Internet access, which the shop has through its fibre connection with a mobile data back-up, and the vendor maintains the software. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; the monthly subscription of about US$100 is small compared with the deposits lost through double-bookings, and no server is needed. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; overseas customers are used to booking online, and staff need only a short training session.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on finding a skilled developer and on securing online payments. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; development may cost several thousand dollars plus ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> feasible once finished, because it is built around the shop's own procedures, but development and testing would take months.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on the existing PC. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; a one-off licence is affordable. <i>Operational:</i> weak; staff on the boats cannot see or update bookings while away from the PC, and customers still cannot book themselves, so the root cause of multiple channels remains.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions before choosing allows the owner to compare costs, benefits and risks and select the optimal solution. It also leaves a tested alternative available if the first choice cannot be implemented.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in Question 2(b). <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies being recommended in the solution stated in (a) above. <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the remaining alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the remaining solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify the selection of the alternative. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in (a) above. Specify a means of compensating for the change. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> You wish to put together a discussion of all the work you have done. State the MOST appropriate type of application software that you would use to accomplish this. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (10 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Appropriate application software</b> (1 marks): A suitable software type named.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1, subscribing to the cloud-based dive booking system, because it is the most affordable and solves both causes of the problem.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: An online booking button on the website and Facebook page.</b> This is justified because most customers are overseas visitors who plan before they travel; letting them book directly removes most phone and WhatsApp bookings, which was the main cause of the diary being out of date.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Secure online deposits linked to each booking.</b> Card deposits are taken by the booking service's secure payment gateway, so the shop does not store card details. Each booking shows whether it is paid, solving the second cause and reducing no-shows.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: One live calendar with seat limits per boat and instructor.</b> The system stops selling once a boat is full, so double-booking becomes technically impossible. Staff on the boat can check the calendar on a tablet, which the paper diary never allowed.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Staff training, data migration and back-up procedures.</b> All staff will receive a two-hour training session from the vendor's online tutorials, and future bookings in the paper diary will be entered by the receptionist before the go-live date. The vendor backs up data daily and access is protected by individual passwords, so the system is reliable and secure. The subscription can be increased if the shop adds a second boat, so the solution can grow with the business.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the custom booking module on the shop's website.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the desktop reservation software because it still allows customers in other countries to book and pay online, which removes the root cause of bookings arriving through many channels. It is designed around the shop's exact boats and dive sites, and staff can reach it from any device with Internet access, whereas the desktop package is tied to one PC.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that the custom module takes much longer to develop and test than subscribing to a ready-made service, and the shop depends on one developer for fixes. To compensate, the shop could use a shared Google Calendar for all staff as an interim measure, require the developer to deliver the system in phases, and include in the contract full documentation and ownership of the source code so another developer can maintain it.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Word processing software, such as Microsoft Word.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), name one solution, not two. In (b), justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data, security), not the solution in general. In (c)(iii), the compensating measure is worth 2 of the 4 marks and is often left out. In (d), a \"discussion of all the work\" is a written document, so word processing is expected; naming a brand alone (e.g. \"Word\") without the type risks the mark.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_883","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2015 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: appointments at the Windwardside Community Clinic on Saba.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2015), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss any TWO causes of the problem described in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identifying possible solutions are two steps in the problem-solving process. Explain ONE other step in the problem-solving process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH scenario below, identify the most appropriate data gathering instrument. Justify your choice of EACH instrument.</p><p><b>(i)</b> You require feedback from five managers on the limitations of the current manual system. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> You need the answers to 15 questions, intended to gather quantitative data from 100 users in a single company. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> You wish to see how users interact with the current system on a day-to-day basis. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> For EACH of the three instruments identified in (d), outline ONE limitation which you should consider. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other problem-solving step</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Instrument chosen and justified for each scenario</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each instrument</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The Windwardside Community Clinic on Saba books patient appointments in a paper appointment book kept at the reception desk. This has resulted in double-booked appointments, patients waiting up to two hours, missed appointments that are never followed up and visiting specialists who fly in from Sint Maarten finding half-empty clinic days.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that appointments are recorded manually in a single paper book. Only the receptionist can see or change it, so when nurses book follow-up visits by phone they write them on notes that are later copied into the book. Copying errors and lost notes lead to two patients being given the same time slot.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for reminding patients of their appointments. The clinic does not record mobile numbers in a usable way and staff have no time to phone every patient, so many patients, especially the elderly, forget their appointments. This leads to wasted slots, particularly on days when a visiting specialist is on the island.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is analysing the problem by gathering data. This involves using tools such as interviews, questionnaires and observation to collect facts about how the current system works and what users need, so that the real causes are confirmed before solutions are designed.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> With only five managers, I can hold a face-to-face interview with each one, ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and use follow-up questions to probe for detail and clarify their answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is most appropriate because 15 identical closed questions can be given to all 100 users quickly and cheaply, and the answers form quantitative data that can be totalled and graphed.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because it lets me watch the receptionist and nurses at work over several days and see how the system is really used, rather than relying on what they say they do.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that it is time-consuming, since each interview must be scheduled around the managers' duties and then transcribed, and the interviewer may unintentionally bias answers through leading questions.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate: some of the 100 users may not complete or return it, and because it uses closed questions, I cannot probe or clarify an answer that has been misinterpreted.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is that staff may change their behaviour while being watched (the Hawthorne effect), so I may not see the usual shortcuts and errors. Observing patients at reception also raises privacy concerns that limit what I can record.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>You have proposed several solutions to the problem you described in Question 1 (a), from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE possible solutions for the problem you described in Question 1 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in 2 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A commercial off-the-shelf clinic management package on a local server.</b> The clinic would purchase a clinic management package and install it on a small server in the clinic, networked to computers at reception and in the consulting rooms. Appointments, patient contact details and visit history are stored in one database, so every staff member sees the same live diary and double-bookings are prevented. The package can print daily patient lists and send SMS reminders through a local SMS service.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A cloud-based appointment scheduling service (SaaS).</b> The clinic would subscribe to an online scheduling service accessed through a web browser. Staff book appointments online from any computer, patients receive automatic SMS and email reminders, and visiting specialists in Sint Maarten can view their Saba clinic list before they fly in.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom-built appointments database.</b> A developer would build a database designed around the clinic's own procedures, with forms for booking, a daily list for each doctor and a reminder list that the receptionist uses to telephone patients the day before. This replaces the paper book but relies on staff making reminder calls.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1 (commercial package, local server).</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on standard PCs and a small server and does not depend on the Internet, which is important on Saba where connections can be slow and are sometimes interrupted after storms. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the server and licence cost about US$6,000, but this is a one-off cost with a modest annual support fee. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; the vendor provides training and the screens are designed for receptionists, so staff should accept it.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2 (cloud scheduling).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it requires a reliable Internet connection at all times, and if the link fails reception cannot see the diary. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; there is no server to buy, only a monthly subscription. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; it is simple to use, but patient data is stored with an overseas provider, so the clinic must check that this meets health privacy requirements.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3 (custom database).</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; there is no developer on Saba, so development and support would be done remotely or by flying a developer in. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; custom development is costly for a small clinic. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it fits the clinic's procedures, but staff still have to phone patients, which they say they have no time to do.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions allows the clinic to compare them against the same criteria and choose the optimal one, reducing the risk of a costly mistake. It also provides an alternative if the first choice cannot be implemented.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in Question 2 (b). <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies to be recommended for the solution stated in 3 (a). <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the remaining alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the remaining solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify your selection of the alternative. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in 3 (a) and specify a means of compensating. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Your teacher has asked you to make a presentation to the class of your entire problem-solving process. State the MOST appropriate type of application software to accomplish this. Justify your choice of software. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the commercial off-the-shelf clinic management package on a local server.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Purchasing a tested commercial package.</b> This is justified because it is reliable and already used by other clinics, and it can be installed in weeks rather than the months needed for custom development. The vendor provides updates and remote technical support, which matters on an island with no local developers.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Installing it on a local server.</b> Because the data is stored inside the clinic, the diary keeps working when the Internet link is down, which makes the solution technically suitable for Saba. Patient records stay on premises, which helps the clinic protect confidential health data.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Networking reception and consulting rooms to one database.</b> Nurses and doctors can book follow-up visits directly, so appointments no longer pass through paper notes. This removes the first cause of the problem, double-booking caused by copying errors.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Automatic SMS reminders, training, security and back-up.</b> Reminders sent the day before tackle the second cause, missed appointments, without extra staff time. Staff will receive two days of vendor training before go-live, and each user has a password with access limited to their role. A nightly back-up to an encrypted external drive, stored off-site, protects the data in a hurricane. The one-off cost is affordable through the clinic's equipment budget, and more users can be added later.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the cloud-based appointment scheduling service.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the custom database because it can be set up within days with no developer, which Saba does not have, and because it sends automatic reminders, solving the missed-appointment cause that the custom database leaves to staff. Its monthly subscription is affordable and requires no server, and visiting specialists can view their lists from Sint Maarten.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that, unlike the local server package, the cloud service depends completely on the Internet connection; if the link fails, reception cannot see who is booked. To compensate, the clinic could install a 4G mobile data router as a back-up connection and print the next day's appointment list every afternoon, so the clinic can continue to see patients during an outage.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of application software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint. It lets me present each stage of the problem-solving process on clear slides, with charts of the questionnaire results and a diagram of the new appointment process, which helps the class follow and remember the main points.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_887","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2017 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: catch records at Old Road Fishermen's Co-operative in St. Kitts.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2017), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss any TWO causes of the problem described in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identifying possible solutions are two steps in the problem-solving process. Explain ONE other step in the problem-solving process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH scenario below, identify the most appropriate data gathering instrument. Justify your choice of EACH instrument.</p><p><b>(i)</b> You require feedback from five managers on the limitations of the current manual system. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> You need the answers to 15 questions intended to gather quantitative data from 100 users in a single company. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> You wish to see how users interact with the current system on a day-to-day basis. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> For EACH of the three instruments identified in (d), outline ONE limitation which you should consider. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other problem-solving step</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Instrument chosen and justified for each scenario</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each instrument</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Old Road Fishermen's Co-operative in St. Kitts records the weight and type of fish landed by its 45 members in handwritten notebooks at the landing site. This has resulted in disputes over payments to fishers, lost records when notebooks get wet or go missing, and late, inaccurate catch reports for the co-operative's board.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that catch data is written by hand at a busy, wet landing site. When several boats land at once, the clerk rushes, figures are written unclearly or in the wrong column, and pages are damaged by water. This leads to wrong weights being used to calculate payments.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the notebook data is copied manually into a ledger at the office before payments and reports are prepared. Copying the same data twice introduces further errors, and it takes the clerk about two days at the end of each month. As a result, fishers are paid late and the board does not have up-to-date catch figures.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is implementing the chosen solution. This involves installing the hardware and software, converting existing data, training users and changing over from the old system to the new one, so that the solution is actually put to work.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> An interview is most appropriate because there are only five managers, so I can speak to each one in depth, ask follow-up questions and clarify exactly which limitations of the manual system affect them.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is most appropriate because the same 15 questions can be answered by all 100 users, producing quantitative data that is easy to collect and analyse statistically.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because I can watch the clerk recording catches at the landing site over several mornings and see the actual process and where mistakes happen, instead of relying on what users say.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation is that it is time-consuming to schedule and conduct each interview, and the answers may be biased because managers may not want to admit weaknesses in procedures they are responsible for.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is that some users may not return it, giving a low response rate, and those who do may misinterpret a question, which I cannot clarify because I am not present.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is that the clerk may behave differently because she knows she is being observed (the Hawthorne effect). Observation is also time-consuming, as I may need to attend many landings before seeing the busiest conditions.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>You have proposed several solutions to the problem you described in Question 1 (a), from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE possible solutions for the problem you described in Question 1 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in 2 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: Mobile data capture with online forms and a cloud spreadsheet.</b> The clerk would record each landing on a rugged, waterproof tablet using an online form (for example Google Forms) with drop-down lists for boat, fisher and species. Each entry goes straight into a cloud spreadsheet that totals each fisher's catch and payment automatically. This removes both handwriting errors and copying into the ledger.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial co-operative accounting and inventory package.</b> The co-operative would purchase an off-the-shelf package installed on the office PC, with modules for members, purchases of fish, sales to hotels and payments. The clerk would still write catches at the landing site but would key them into the package once a day, which then calculates payments and produces board reports.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom mobile app linked to an electronic scale.</b> A developer would build an app that reads the weight directly from a digital scale by Bluetooth, records the fisher and species, and uploads the data to a database at the office, producing payments and reports.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a tablet with mobile data and free online tools, and the forms can work offline and upload when a signal returns. <i>Economic:</i> highly feasible; a rugged tablet costs about US$500 and the software is free. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; the clerk already uses a smartphone, and drop-down lists are quicker than writing, though she needs training.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on the existing office PC. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; the licence and annual support cost more than Solution 1. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it improves payments and reports, but catches are still written by hand first, so the first cause of errors remains.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> less feasible; Bluetooth scales and a custom app need specialist skills not available locally. <i>Economic:</i> not feasible at present; development costs several thousand dollars. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible once built, because weights are captured automatically.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several possible solutions allows the co-operative to compare them and select the optimal one within its budget. It also reduces risk, because an evaluated alternative is ready if the first choice fails.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in Question 2 (a). <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies to be recommended for the solution stated in 3 (a). <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the remaining alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the remaining solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify your selection of the alternative. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in 3 (a) and specify a means of compensating. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Your teacher has asked you to make a presentation to the class of your entire problem-solving process. State the MOST appropriate type of application software to accomplish this. Justify your choice of software. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: mobile data capture with online forms and a cloud spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Recording catches on a rugged tablet at the landing site.</b> This is justified because it removes handwriting at the wet, busy landing site, the first cause of the problem. A waterproof case protects the device, and drop-down lists mean the clerk taps instead of writing, which is faster and more accurate.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Using online forms that feed a cloud spreadsheet.</b> Each entry is stored once and appears immediately in the spreadsheet, so the double copying into the ledger is eliminated. Formulas calculate each fisher's weekly payment, so fishers can be paid on time, and the board can see up-to-date totals and charts.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Low cost using free software and existing skills.</b> The only purchase is a tablet and a mobile data plan, so the solution is economically feasible for a small co-operative. The clerk and treasurer already use spreadsheets, so training is short and user acceptance is likely.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Security and back-up.</b> The account is protected by a strong password with two-step verification, and only the clerk and treasurer can edit the spreadsheet; board members get view-only access. Data is stored online, so it is not lost if the tablet is damaged, and a monthly copy is downloaded to the office PC as a back-up. More boats and members can be added without extra cost.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the commercial co-operative accounting and inventory package.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than the custom scale app because it is available now at a known price, needs no specialist developer and runs on the office PC the co-operative already owns. It solves the second cause, double copying and slow month-end payments, by calculating payments and reports automatically, and the vendor provides training and support.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that catches must still be written on paper at the landing site and keyed in later, so handwriting errors are not removed, unlike the tablet solution. To compensate, the co-operative could introduce printed, pre-numbered landing slips with boxes for each species, have each fisher sign the slip to confirm the weight, and have the clerk key in slips the same day while they are fresh.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of software is presentation software, such as Google Slides or Microsoft PowerPoint, because it allows me to summarise each stage of the problem-solving process on slides with charts, photos of the landing site and a diagram of the new process, making the presentation clear and engaging for the class.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_88b","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2018 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: fleet management at the Punda Car Rentals in Willemstad, Curaçao.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2018), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, complete the following tasks.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss any TWO causes of the problem described in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identifying possible solutions are two steps in the problem-solving process. Explain ONE other step in the problem-solving process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH scenario below, identify the most appropriate data gathering instrument. Justify your choice of EACH instrument.</p><p><b>(i)</b> You require feedback from five managers on the limitations of the current manual system. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> You need the answers to 15 questions intended to gather quantitative data from 100 users in a single company. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> You wish to see how users interact with the current system on a day-to-day basis. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> For EACH of the THREE instruments identified in (d), outline ONE limitation which you should consider. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other problem-solving step</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Instrument chosen and justified for each scenario</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each instrument</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The Punda Car Rentals in Willemstad, Curaçao, tracks its 60 vehicles on a whiteboard and a basic spreadsheet. This has resulted in cars being double-rented, vehicles missing scheduled servicing, and disputes with customers over damage and fuel because check-out records are incomplete.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that the status of each car is kept on a whiteboard that staff at the airport desk and the town office update separately. Because the two locations do not share one record, a car returned at the airport may still show as rented in town, or a car may be promised to two customers. This leads to double-renting and lost bookings.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that there is no system for recording mileage, service dates and vehicle condition. Check-out sheets are filled in by hand and are often incomplete, so no one is alerted when a car is due for service, and the company cannot prove what damage existed before a rental. As a result, cars break down and damage claims are lost.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step is evaluating the possible solutions and selecting the optimal one. In this step each solution is assessed for technical, economic and operational feasibility and compared using criteria such as cost, time and benefits, so that the most feasible solution is chosen before implementation begins.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> This is most appropriate because there are only five managers, so each can be interviewed face to face, and I can ask probing follow-up questions to clarify the limitations they experience with the manual system.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> This is most appropriate because 15 standard closed questions can be distributed to 100 users at once, producing quantitative data that can be analysed quickly in a spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> This is most appropriate because watching staff at the rental desk as they check cars in and out shows how the system is actually used day to day, including shortcuts that users would not report.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> it is time-consuming to arrange and conduct interviews with busy managers, and responses may be biased by the interviewer's questions or by managers defending their own procedures.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> the response rate may be low because some users do not complete it, and closed questions may be misinterpreted, with no chance to clarify or probe the answers.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> staff may behave differently while being observed (the Hawthorne effect), and observation cannot reveal why users act as they do or what they think of the system.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>You have proposed several solutions to the problem you described in Question 1 (a), from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE possible solutions for the problem you described in Question 1 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in 2 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A cloud-based commercial car rental management system.</b> The company would subscribe to an off-the-shelf rental management package hosted online. Both the airport desk and the town office would log in to one shared fleet calendar, so every car's status is updated live. The system records mileage and fuel at each check-out, schedules servicing automatically and stores digital inspection photos taken on a tablet, which addresses both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: GPS trackers with a fleet monitoring dashboard.</b> A GPS tracking unit would be fitted to each car, reporting its location and mileage to an online dashboard. Managers would see where every car is and receive alerts when a car reaches its service mileage. Bookings would still be managed in the existing spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom web-based reservation and fleet database.</b> A local developer would build a web application with a database of cars, customers and rentals, designed exactly around the company's rates and procedures, with check-out forms and service reminders.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it requires only browsers, tablets and Internet access at both locations, and the vendor maintains the software. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; the monthly subscription is predictable and is less than the income lost from a few double-rentals and breakdowns. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; it is designed for rental desks, so staff can learn it quickly, and both offices share one record.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; GPS units are standard, but each must be installed in 60 cars. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; units and monthly data fees for 60 cars are costly. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; it solves service tracking but not double-renting, because bookings stay in the spreadsheet.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on a skilled developer and on secure hosting. <i>Economic:</i> less feasible; high development cost and ongoing maintenance. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible once completed, because it matches the company's own procedures, but it would take months to deliver.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several possible solutions lets management compare their costs, benefits and risks and choose the optimal solution, and it provides an evaluated fall-back if the first choice cannot go ahead.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in Question 2 (a). <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies to be recommended for the solution stated in 3 (a). <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the remaining solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify your selection of the alternative. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in 3 (a), and specify a means of compensating. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Your teacher has asked you to make a presentation to the class on your entire problem-solving process. State the MOST appropriate type of application software to accomplish this. Justify your choice of software. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the cloud-based commercial car rental management system.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: One shared online fleet calendar.</b> Both offices see the same live status for every car, so a car returned at the airport is immediately available in town. This removes the first cause, separate whiteboards, and makes double-renting technically impossible.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Digital check-out with tablet inspection photos.</b> Staff record mileage and fuel and photograph each car at check-out and return. This gives clear evidence of existing damage, so disputes with customers can be resolved fairly, and the records are complete and legible.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Automatic service scheduling.</b> The system flags cars due for service by date or mileage and blocks them from being rented, which reduces breakdowns and keeps the fleet reliable and safe.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Subscription, training and security.</b> A monthly subscription avoids buying a server and spreads the cost, making it economically feasible. Staff at both offices will receive vendor training before go-live, and the old spreadsheet data will be imported. Each user has a password with role-based access, the vendor backs up data daily, and the subscription can grow as the fleet grows.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 3, the custom web-based reservation and fleet database.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative is better than GPS tracking because it addresses both causes of the problem: it provides a single shared booking record that prevents double-renting, and it records mileage, condition and service dates. GPS tracking only tackles servicing and location. The custom system also fits Punda's own rates and procedures exactly and can be accessed from both offices.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that a custom system takes several months to develop and test, so problems continue longer than with a ready-made subscription, and the company depends on one developer. To compensate, the company could use a shared online spreadsheet between the two offices as a temporary booking record, require the system to be delivered in phases with bookings first, and include documentation and source-code ownership in the developer's contract.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint, because slides allow me to present each stage of the problem-solving process with bullet points, charts of my questionnaire results and screenshots of the proposed system, which makes the presentation clear for the class.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_88f","title":"Paper 03 practice · May/June 2019 (past Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: book lending at the Hopewell Community Library in Orange Walk, Belize.</p><p>This is a past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 paper (May/June 2019), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem which you have researched, complete the following tasks.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a description of the problem you have researched. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss any TWO causes of the problem described in (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identifying possible solutions are two steps in the problem-solving process. Describe ONE other step in the problem-solving process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH scenario below, identify the most appropriate data gathering instrument. Justify your choice of EACH instrument.</p><p><b>(i)</b> You require feedback from five managers on the limitations of the current manual system. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> You need the answers to 15 questions intended to gather quantitative data from 100 users in a single company. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> You wish to see how users interact with the current system on a day-to-day basis. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> For EACH of the THREE instruments identified in (d), outline ONE limitation which should be considered. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other problem-solving step</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Instrument chosen and justified for each scenario</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each instrument</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The Hopewell Community Library in Orange Walk, Belize, records book loans on paper cards kept in a box at the front desk. This has resulted in overdue books not being followed up, about 300 books lost in two years, and staff being unable to tell members whether a book is available without searching the shelves and the card box.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that loans are recorded manually on index cards filed by book title. To find which books are overdue, a librarian must go through every card by hand, which is never done during busy periods. As a result, overdue books are not chased and many are never returned.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that the library has no catalogue linked to the loan records. The book list is an old printed register that does not show whether a book is on the shelf or on loan, so members and staff cannot check availability. This leads to wasted time and frustrated members who stop using the library.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another step in the problem-solving process is reviewing and monitoring the solution after it is implemented. This involves checking whether the new system meets its objectives, for example whether overdue books and losses have fallen, gathering feedback from users, and making adjustments or maintenance changes so that the solution continues to work well.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> With only five managers, an interview with each one lets me ask open questions about the limitations of the manual system and follow up with probing questions to clarify and obtain detailed answers.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> A questionnaire is the best choice because the same 15 closed questions can be given to all 100 users quickly and cheaply, producing quantitative data that is easy to tally and analyse.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Observation is most appropriate because watching staff lend and receive books at the desk shows how the system is actually used each day, including delays and errors users may not mention.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> one limitation which should be considered is that interviews are time-consuming to arrange and write up, and answers can be biased by the way the interviewer phrases questions.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> one limitation is a low response rate, since some users will not complete it, and questions may be misunderstood with no opportunity for the researcher to clarify them.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> one limitation is the Hawthorne effect, where staff change their behaviour because they know they are being observed, so what is seen may not be typical. It also cannot show users' opinions.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>You have proposed several solutions to the problem you described in 1 (a), from which you will select an optimal solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss THREE possible solutions for the problem you described in 1 (a). <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution proposed in 2 (a). Include technical, financial or economical, and operational feasibility of EACH solution. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain the benefit of identifying several possible solutions prior to selecting your final solution. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: An open-source integrated library system with barcode scanning.</b> The library would install a free, open-source library management system (such as Koha) on a PC at the desk. Each book and membership card receives a barcode, and loans and returns are scanned. The system shows each book's status, lists overdue items automatically and can email reminders, addressing both causes.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A cloud-based library management subscription.</b> The library would subscribe to an online library service accessed through a web browser. It provides the same features (barcode loans, catalogue, overdue lists) plus an online catalogue members can search from home, with the vendor hosting and maintaining the system.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom Microsoft Access database.</b> A volunteer developer would build a database with tables for books, members and loans, with forms for lending and returning and a query that lists overdue loans. It would replace the card box but would not offer an online catalogue.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Solution 1.</b> <i>Technical feasibility:</i> feasible; it runs on a standard PC with a barcode scanner, but installation needs some technical skill, which the library can obtain from a local IT volunteer. <i>Economic (financial) feasibility:</i> highly feasible; the software is free, and only barcode labels, a scanner and a PC upgrade (about BZ$1,800) are needed. <i>Operational feasibility:</i> feasible; scanning is quick and staff can learn it in a short training session.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it needs only a browser and Internet access, and the vendor handles maintenance. <i>Economic:</i> partly feasible; the annual subscription is a continuing cost for a library that depends on donations. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; it is easy to use and members can search the catalogue online.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it depends on one volunteer's skill, and support may disappear if the volunteer leaves. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; Access is already available, so costs are low. <i>Operational:</i> partly feasible; the forms are basic and there are no automatic reminders, so staff must still chase overdue books manually.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Identifying several solutions allows the library to compare their costs, benefits and risks so that the optimal solution is selected, and it means an alternative is already evaluated if the first choice cannot proceed.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, you need to select the optimal or most feasible one.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Select the MOST feasible of the three solutions presented in 2 (a). <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your selection, making reference to ALL of the strategies to be recommended for the solution stated in 3 (a). <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You were unable to implement the most feasible solution due to some unforeseen circumstances and must select one of the alternative solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which of the alternative solutions you would select. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify your selection of the alternative solution stated in (c) (i). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of using this alternative rather than the solution selected in 3 (a), and discuss a means of compensating. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Your teacher has asked you to make a presentation to the class on your entire problem-solving process. State the MOST appropriate type of application software to accomplish this task. Justify your choice of software. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The most feasible solution is Solution 1: the open-source integrated library system with barcode scanning.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Strategy 1: Using free, open-source library software.</b> This is justified because there is no licence or subscription cost, which suits a library funded by donations. The software is widely used by libraries, so it is reliable, and it is regularly updated by its user community.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 2: Barcoding every book and membership card.</b> Scanning removes handwritten cards, the first cause of the problem. Each loan is recorded accurately in seconds, and the system knows exactly who has each book.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 3: Automatic overdue lists and email reminders.</b> The system produces a daily overdue list and emails members when books are due, so overdue books are chased without extra staff time, reducing losses. Its catalogue shows whether each book is available, solving the second cause.</p>\n<p><b>Strategy 4: Training, data entry, security and back-up.</b> Two volunteers will be trained to install and run the system, and books will be barcoded and entered section by section over the summer holiday. Staff log in with individual passwords, and the database is backed up weekly to an external drive kept off-site. The system can later be placed online so members can search from home, so it supports future growth.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would select Solution 2, the cloud-based library management subscription.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> This alternative solution is better than the custom Access database because it provides all the needed features immediately, including automatic overdue reminders and a live catalogue, whereas the Access database still leaves overdue books to be chased by hand. The vendor maintains and backs up the system, so the library does not depend on one volunteer's skills, and it needs no server.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage of the cloud subscription, compared with the open-source system, is the continuing annual fee, which a donation-funded library may struggle to pay every year. To compensate, the library could seek sponsorship from a local business in return for acknowledgement on the online catalogue, introduce a small annual membership fee, and choose the vendor's smallest plan, upgrading only when the collection grows.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The most appropriate type of application software is presentation software, such as Microsoft PowerPoint or Google Slides, because it allows me to present my entire problem-solving process to the class as a sequence of slides with key points, charts of my findings and images of the proposed system, which is clear and engaging for an audience.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_88j","title":"Paper 03 practice · 2020 specimen (Paper 032)","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>All three questions are based on an information technology problem that <b>you have researched</b>. Choose ONE problem before you start and use it consistently: Question 1 asks you to define and analyse the problem, Question 2 to propose and evaluate solutions, and Question 3 to select and justify the optimal solution and choose suitable software. Answers that switch problems between questions lose marks.</p><p>The model answers in this paper all follow one problem: parcel tracking at QuickDrop Couriers in Chaguanas, Trinidad and Tobago.</p><p>This is the CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032 specimen paper (2020), used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The questions are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p>With reference to a problem that you have researched, answer the following questions.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a problem statement that can be the basis of developing an Information Technology based solution. <i>[3 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Briefly discuss TWO causes of the problem identified in 1 (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Other than surveys, describe TWO tools or instruments that you can use for data gathering in order to properly analyze the problem. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Choose ONE of the instruments described in 1 (c) and discuss TWO advantages and ONE disadvantage of this instrument when compared with surveys. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Problem statement: who, how, result</b> (3 marks): All three elements: who is affected, how, and the result if not fixed.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes briefly discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) TWO tools other than surveys described</b> (4 marks): Two tools, each fully described.</li><li><b>(d) TWO advantages and ONE disadvantage compared with surveys</b> (9 marks): Two advantages and one disadvantage, each stated and discussed against surveys.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> The customers and dispatch staff of QuickDrop Couriers in Chaguanas, Trinidad and Tobago (who) cannot find out where a parcel is or whether it has been delivered, because parcels are logged by hand in a book and drivers record deliveries on paper slips that return to the office only at the end of the day (how). If this is not rectified, parcels will continue to be lost, customers will keep phoning the office for updates, and the company will lose business customers to competitors that offer online tracking (result).</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is that parcels are recorded manually in a logbook with no unique tracking number. Staff search the book line by line when a customer calls, and parcels with similar names are confused, which leads to lost and misdelivered parcels.</p>\n<p>A second cause is that drivers have no way of updating delivery status while on the road. Proof of delivery is a paper slip handed in at night, so the office cannot confirm deliveries during the day, and slips that are lost leave no record that a parcel was delivered.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Interviews:</b> an interview is a planned face-to-face (or video) conversation in which the researcher asks the owner, dispatcher or drivers prepared questions, follows up with further questions and records the answers. It gathers detailed information about how the current system works and its problems.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> observation involves the researcher watching users carry out their normal work, for example a dispatcher logging parcels and a driver completing deliveries, and recording what happens on a checklist. It shows the actual process, delays and errors as they occur.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I have chosen the interview.</p>\n<p><b>Advantage 1: Answers can be clarified and probed.</b> Unlike a survey, where a respondent's vague or misunderstood answer cannot be checked, in an interview I can ask follow-up questions on the spot. For example, if the dispatcher says \"parcels go missing\", I can ask when and how, which validates the data immediately and saves time later.</p>\n<p><b>Advantage 2: It produces more detailed, in-depth information.</b> A survey usually uses closed questions that limit answers to fixed options. An interview lets the owner explain the whole process, including problems I had not thought of, so I gain a deeper understanding of the causes of lost parcels.</p>\n<p><b>Disadvantage: It is more time-consuming and costly for large numbers of people.</b> A survey can be sent to all 25 drivers and many customers at once and analysed quickly, but each interview must be scheduled, conducted and written up individually. This takes much more time, so only a few people can be interviewed, and their views may not represent everyone.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), a problem statement missing the \"what if not rectified\" element loses a mark. In (c), surveys are excluded, so naming questionnaires earns nothing. In (d), the comparison with surveys is the point: \"interviews are detailed\" earns 1; \"unlike a survey, an interview lets me ask follow-up questions, so vague answers can be clarified on the spot\" earns 3.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p><b>(a)</b> Based on the problem statement in 1 (a), discuss THREE possible solutions. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> State FIVE factors that you should consider to determine an optimal solution. <i>[5 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> For any THREE of the factors stated in 2 (b), discuss the implication/impact of EACH for one of your possible solutions. <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) FIVE factors stated</b> (5 marks): Five distinct factors.</li><li><b>(c) Implications of THREE factors for one solution</b> (6 marks): Three factors applied to one named solution, each with its implication.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: A cloud-based courier management system with a driver smartphone app.</b> QuickDrop would subscribe to an online courier management service. Every parcel receives a barcode tracking number when it is logged; drivers scan parcels and capture a signature and photo on a smartphone app at delivery; and customers track parcels on a web page. This removes both the logbook and the paper slips.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: A commercial off-the-shelf parcel tracking package on an office server with handheld scanners.</b> The company would purchase a package installed on its own server. Parcels are barcoded and scanned in and out at the office, and drivers use handheld scanners that are synchronised when they return. This creates an accurate electronic record but updates delivery status only at the end of the route.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: A custom-built web tracking system.</b> A local developer would build a web database and a simple mobile web page for drivers, designed around QuickDrop's own routes and customers, with SMS updates to customers when parcels are delivered.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> 1. Time needed to develop and implement the solution. 2. Human impact, such as possible redundancies of clerical staff. 3. Infrastructure required, such as equipment, network and Internet access. 4. Staffing with highly skilled employees to run and support the system. 5. Training required for staff to use the system.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> For Solution 1, the cloud-based system:</p>\n<p><b>Time:</b> because it is a ready-made subscription, it can be in use within about two weeks, so the company starts reducing lost parcels almost immediately, unlike a custom system that would take months.</p>\n<p><b>Infrastructure:</b> each of the 25 drivers needs a smartphone with a mobile data plan, and the office needs reliable Internet. This means an extra monthly data cost, and deliveries in areas with weak signal will only update when the phone reconnects.</p>\n<p><b>Training:</b> drivers and dispatchers must be trained to scan parcels and capture signatures. This means sessions must be held early in the morning before routes start, so deliveries may be slightly slower during the first week while staff gain confidence.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), three solutions that are really one (buy a PC, buy a printer, buy software) earn marks once. In (b), \"state\" needs only the factor, so do not waste time explaining. In (c), the mark scheme credits ONLY implications: say what the factor will mean for that solution (cost, delay, staff impact), not what the factor is.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p>Once the solutions have been evaluated, one should be selected as the optimal or MOST feasible solution.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Describe the approach that you would take in order to select the most feasible solution. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> It is said that “the selection of the optimal solution is based on a prevailing factor such as cost at the given point in time”. Discuss this statement. <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> You are required to produce a report which includes the problem statement and analysis of the possible solutions.</p><p><b>(i)</b> Which software application would be MOST appropriate? Justify your response. <i>[3 marks]</i></p><p><b>(ii)</b> Discuss THREE word processing features that would enhance the organisation and structure in the presentation of the report. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Selection approach described with an example</b> (4 marks): A clear method (e.g. weighted scoring) with a worked example.</li><li><b>(b) Statement discussed</b> (4 marks): Both sides discussed with a conclusion.</li><li><b>(c)(i) Word processor named and justified</b> (3 marks): A named word processor with reasons.</li><li><b>(c)(ii) THREE word processing features discussed</b> (9 marks): Three features, each with its use and placement in the report.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> I would use a weighted scoring matrix. First, I list the criteria that matter, such as cost, time to implement, ease of use and security, and give each a weight according to its importance (for example, cost 30%, time 25%, ease of use 25%, security 20%). Then each solution is scored out of 10 on each criterion, each score is multiplied by its weight, and the results are added. For example, the cloud system scored 8 × 0.30 for cost, 9 × 0.25 for time, 8 × 0.25 for ease of use and 7 × 0.20 for security, a total of 8.05, which was higher than the other two solutions, so it was selected as most feasible.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> This statement is partly true. At a given point in time one factor, often cost, can decide the choice: after a hurricane or a severe flood, for example, many small Caribbean businesses have very limited budgets, so the cheapest workable solution is often selected even if it is not the best long-term option. However, cost should not be the only factor. A cheap solution that staff will not use, or that is insecure, may cost more in the long term through lost parcels and customers. Also, the prevailing factor changes: when a company has funds, time to implement or long-term benefits may matter more. Therefore, the optimal solution depends on the circumstances at that time, but a good decision balances several factors rather than cost alone.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> The most appropriate application is a word processor such as Microsoft Word. A report is a text-based document, and a word processor is designed for writing and editing long text, with features such as heading styles, tables, spelling and grammar checking and page layout tools that produce a professional, well-formatted report.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> <b>Feature 1: Automatic table of contents.</b> By applying heading styles to each section (problem statement, analysis, solutions, recommendation), I can insert a table of contents after the cover page. It lists every section with its page number and updates automatically, so readers can find and navigate to any section quickly.</p>\n<p><b>Feature 2: Headers and footers with page numbers.</b> A header containing the report title and company name, and a footer with automatic page numbers, appear on every page after the cover. This gives the report a consistent, professional look and makes pages easy to reference in discussion.</p>\n<p><b>Feature 3: Section breaks and a cover page.</b> A cover page with the title, author and date is placed at the start, and section breaks separate the main parts of the report, so each major section starts on a new page and can have its own layout, for example a landscape page for the feasibility comparison table. This organises the report into clear, structured parts.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost.</b> In (a), a description with no example caps you at 2–3 marks. In (b), agreeing in one line (\"yes, cost is most important\") is not a discussion. In (c)(i), this is a REPORT, so a word processor is right; presentation software earns nothing here. In (c)(ii), naming bold, italic and underline is weak; choose features that organise the report (table of contents, headers/footers, section breaks) and say where they are placed.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_88o","title":"Paper 03 practice · Practice Examination 1","desc":"<p><b>1 hour 30 minutes · 60 marks.</b> This paper consists of THREE questions. Answer ALL questions. Each question is worth 20 marks.</p><p>This practice paper is built around the case of Hummingbird Ridge High School in the central stimulus. Read it first, then answer every question about that case, using the same facts throughout: Question 1 asks you to describe and analyse the problem and choose fact-finding techniques; Question 2 asks you to discuss and evaluate three given solutions; Question 3 asks you to select, justify and present a solution.</p><p>The model answers use the Hummingbird Ridge High School case given in the paper.</p><p>Practice Examination 1 is an original practice paper written in the format of the past CXC CAPE Information Technology Unit 1 Paper 032, used here as practice for Paper 03 (Module 3, problem-solving) and transcribed for class practice. The Paper 032 format and question style are copyright of the Caribbean Examinations Council (CXC). Mark guidance and model answers were prepared for this course by Kester Small.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"text","title":"Stimulus","q":"<p><b>School Management Information System</b></p><p>Hummingbird Ridge High School currently maintains student attendance, grades, discipline records and teacher reports manually. Teachers record information in paper registers while office staff use separate spreadsheets to prepare reports.</p><p>The school has identified several problems:</p><ul><li>Student records are duplicated.</li><li>Attendance reports are often inaccurate.</li><li>Parents receive report cards late.</li><li>Records are sometimes misplaced.</li><li>Preparing reports requires excessive staff time.</li></ul><p>The principal has decided that a computerised School Management Information System should be developed.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Defining and analysing the problem","q":"<p><b>(a)</b> Describe the problem that the school is experiencing. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Discuss TWO causes of the problem identified in part (a). <i>[4 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Defining the problem and identifying possible solutions are two stages of the systems analysis process. Explain ONE other stage in the systems analysis process. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> For EACH situation below, identify the most appropriate fact-finding technique and justify your choice:</p><p><b>(i)</b> The principal needs to explain the school's reporting procedures.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> The analyst wishes to obtain opinions from all 900 students concerning the school's reporting system.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> The analyst wants to observe how teachers currently record attendance during lessons.</p><p><i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>(e)</b> State ONE limitation of EACH fact-finding technique selected in Question 1(d). <i>[6 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Description of the problem</b> (2 marks): You named the organisation and described what goes wrong and its effect.</li><li><b>(b) TWO causes discussed</b> (4 marks): Two separate causes, each with an explanation of how it creates the problem.</li><li><b>(c) ONE other systems analysis stage</b> (2 marks): You named a valid step and explained what happens in it.</li><li><b>(d) Fact-finding technique chosen and justified</b> (6 marks): Interview, questionnaire and observation, each with a reason tied to the scenario.</li><li><b>(e) ONE limitation of each technique</b> (6 marks): One clear limitation for each of the three instruments, explained.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> Hummingbird Ridge High School keeps student attendance, grades, discipline records and teacher reports manually in paper registers, while office staff use separate spreadsheets to prepare reports. This has resulted in duplicated student records, inaccurate attendance reports, misplaced records, report cards reaching parents late and excessive staff time spent preparing reports.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One cause is the continued reliance on paper registers. Teachers record attendance and grades by hand, and the registers must be collected and read by office staff, which increases transcription errors and the risk of documents being misplaced. This leads to inaccurate attendance reports and lost records.</p>\n<p>A second cause is the lack of an integrated information system. Each office clerk keeps a separate spreadsheet, so the same student is entered several times with slightly different details. This causes duplicate records and inconsistent reports, and staff spend extra time combining data, which delays report cards.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Another stage is requirements analysis (analysing the current system). This involves gathering detailed information from users such as teachers, office staff and the principal to determine the functions, inputs, outputs and constraints the new system must have, so that the solution meets the school's real needs.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(i)</b> <b>Interview.</b> The principal is one person who needs to explain procedures in detail, so a face-to-face interview allows the analyst to ask probing questions and clarify each step of the reporting procedures.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(ii)</b> <b>Questionnaire.</b> With 900 students, interviews are impractical; an online questionnaire with mainly closed questions can be sent to all students at once, because it is quick, cheap and produces data that is easy to analyse.</p>\n<p><b>(d)(iii)</b> <b>Observation.</b> Watching teachers during lessons shows exactly how attendance is recorded in practice, how long it takes and where errors occur, which teachers might not describe accurately.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> <b>Interview:</b> it is time-consuming, and the principal's answers may be biased towards how procedures are supposed to work rather than how they actually work.</p>\n<p><b>Questionnaire:</b> the response rate may be low, since many students may not complete it, and some may misinterpret questions or answer carelessly, which cannot be clarified.</p>\n<p><b>Observation:</b> teachers may change their behaviour because they know they are being watched (the Hawthorne effect), and the analyst's presence may disrupt the lesson.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 1.</b> In (a), candidates describe the organisation but never say what goes wrong or its effect. In (b), they list symptoms (\"files are lost\", \"reports are late\") instead of causes, or give two causes with no explanation: \"discuss\" needs the cause AND how it produces the problem. In (c), repeating \"define the problem\" earns nothing. In (d), the scenarios are fixed by CXC: five managers = interview, 100 users and quantitative data = questionnaire, day-to-day interaction = observation. A bare instrument name earns only 1 of 2 marks; the reason must use the scenario's numbers or purpose. In (e), give one limitation for EACH instrument, not three limitations of one.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Possible solutions and their evaluation","q":"<p>Following the investigation, three possible solutions have been identified:</p><ul><li>Develop a custom School Management Information System.</li><li>Purchase a commercial School Management Information System.</li><li>Subscribe to a cloud-based School Management Information System.</li></ul><p><b>(a)</b> Discuss the THREE proposed solutions. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Evaluate the feasibility of EACH solution using technical, economic and operational feasibility. <i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Explain why organizations should evaluate several possible solutions before selecting one for implementation. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a) THREE distinct solutions discussed</b> (9 marks): Three genuinely different IT solutions, each described and linked to the problem.</li><li><b>(b) Feasibility of EACH solution</b> (9 marks): Each solution judged on technical, economic and operational feasibility.</li><li><b>(c) Benefit of identifying several solutions</b> (2 marks): You explained comparison leading to a better, lower-risk decision.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> <b>Solution 1: Develop a custom School Management Information System.</b> A developer would build a system designed specifically around Sunrise's procedures, with one central student database for attendance, grades, discipline and reports. It would remove duplicate records and produce report cards automatically. It offers maximum flexibility but requires the longest development time and specialist skills.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 2: Purchase a commercial School Management Information System.</b> The school would purchase an off-the-shelf package already used by other schools, installed on a server on the school network. Teachers would enter attendance and grades on networked computers, and reports would be generated from the single database. It can be deployed faster than a custom system, although some procedures may need to change or the package may need customisation.</p>\n<p><b>Solution 3: Subscribe to a cloud-based School Management Information System.</b> The school would pay an annual subscription for a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) system accessed through a web browser. Teachers could record attendance on laptops or phones in class, and parents could view reports online. There is no server to buy, but the school depends on reliable Internet connectivity.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Custom system.</b> <i>Technical:</i> partly feasible; it needs a skilled developer and a server, and the school has no in-house developer. <i>Economic:</i> least feasible; development, testing and maintenance are expensive. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible once complete, because it matches the school's procedures, but implementation would take a year or more.</p>\n<p><b>Commercial system.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible; it runs on a standard server and the school's existing network, with vendor support. <i>Economic:</i> moderately feasible; there is a large one-off licence and server cost, plus annual support fees. <i>Operational:</i> feasible; vendor training is provided, and the system is proven in schools, though staff may need to adjust some procedures.</p>\n<p><b>Cloud-based system.</b> <i>Technical:</i> feasible if Internet access is reliable; the vendor maintains the software and backups. <i>Economic:</i> feasible; costs are spread as a yearly subscription with no server purchase. <i>Operational:</i> highly feasible; teachers can record attendance from any classroom, and parents can view reports online, but staff must be trained and the school must accept that data is stored by a third party.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Organisations should evaluate several solutions so that they can compare costs, benefits and risks and select the optimal solution, rather than committing to the first idea. It also gives them a fall-back option if the preferred solution cannot be implemented.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 2.</b> The commonest loss is three \"solutions\" that are really one (buy a computer, buy a printer, buy software). Each solution must be a different IT approach, described and linked to a cause from Question 1. Listing earns identification marks only; \"discuss\" needs how it works and why it helps. In (b), feasibility must be judged for EACH solution and must name <b>technical</b>, <b>economic</b> and <b>operational</b> feasibility; a sentence such as \"this is feasible because it is cheap\" earns 1 mark at most. In (c), say comparison leads to the optimal choice and gives a fall-back.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Selecting and presenting the optimal solution","q":"<p><b>(a)</b> Select the solution you believe is the most suitable. <i>[1 mark]</i></p><p><b>(b)</b> Justify your recommendation. Include cost, ease of implementation, security, reliability, scalability, user acceptance, maintenance and long-term benefits. <i>[8 marks]</i></p><p><b>(c)</b> Assume your preferred solution cannot be implemented because funding was reduced.</p><p><b>(i)</b> State which remaining solution you would recommend.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> Justify your recommendation.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> Explain ONE disadvantage of the alternative solution and suggest ONE method of reducing its impact.</p><p><i>[9 marks]</i></p><p><b>(d)</b> Recommend the most appropriate application software for preparing a presentation of your investigation. Justify your choice. <i>[2 marks]</i></p><p><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Marking guide</h4><ul><li><b>(a)–(b) Most feasible solution selected and justified</b> (9 marks): You named the solution and justified every strategy in it with criteria.</li><li><b>(c)(i)–(ii) Alternative selected and justified</b> (5 marks): You named the alternative and gave reasons it beats the remaining option.</li><li><b>(c)(iii) Disadvantage and compensating measure</b> (4 marks): One clear disadvantage and a practical measure that offsets it.</li><li><b>(d) Presentation software named and justified</b> (2 marks): Presentation software with a reason tied to presenting to an audience.</li></ul><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>(a)</b> I recommend Solution 2: purchase a commercial School Management Information System.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Cost:</b> although the one-off licence and server cost is significant, there are no development costs, and the school owns the licence, so long-term costs are lower than an endless subscription. <b>Ease of implementation:</b> the package is ready-made and proven, so it can be installed and configured during the summer holiday. <b>Security:</b> student data is stored on the school's own server, protected by user passwords and role-based access, so teachers see only their classes and confidential discipline records are restricted. <b>Reliability:</b> the system runs on the local network, so registers and grades can still be entered when the Internet is down, with nightly back-ups to an off-site drive. <b>Scalability:</b> more users, classes and modules can be added as enrolment grows. <b>User acceptance:</b> the vendor provides training, and the interfaces are designed for teachers, so staff are likely to accept it. <b>Maintenance:</b> the vendor supplies updates and a help desk under the annual support agreement. <b>Long-term benefits:</b> one integrated database removes duplicate records, produces accurate attendance reports and allows report cards to reach parents on time.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(i)</b> I would recommend Solution 3, the cloud-based School Management Information System.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(ii)</b> With reduced funding, the cloud system is the most affordable remaining option because it requires no server purchase and spreads the cost as an annual subscription, whereas the custom system would be the most expensive and slowest. It can be implemented quickly, the vendor handles maintenance and back-ups, and teachers can record attendance in class on existing laptops, which solves the problems of duplicate and inaccurate records.</p>\n<p><b>(c)(iii)</b> One disadvantage is that the cloud system depends on reliable Internet access; if the school's connection fails, teachers cannot record attendance or enter grades. To reduce its impact, the school could install a back-up mobile data (4G) router that switches on automatically during outages, and keep a printable attendance sheet for each class to be entered later the same day.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Presentation software such as Microsoft PowerPoint or Google Slides is most appropriate, because it communicates the investigation clearly to an audience using slides, bullet points, charts of the questionnaire results and diagrams of the proposed system.</p><h4>Teacher tip</h4><p><b>How marks are lost in Question 3.</b> In (a), candidates hedge between two solutions: name one. In (b), \"it is the best and cheapest\" is a list, not a justification; justify EACH strategy inside the solution (hardware, software, training, data conversion, security) with criteria. In (c)(iii), most candidates give the disadvantage but forget the <b>compensating measure</b>, losing 2 marks. In (d), the task is a <b>presentation</b>, so word processing or desktop publishing earns no marks.</p>"}]}]}];
